window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"k":"c","t":"The Sword of Valeria","u":"books/0000.html","m":"Case 0000 · Published","d":"A reckoner's life, and the sword that outlived his name.","x":"Case 0000 Epic Fantasy · War & Military Fiction His mother taught him two things: pray properly, and keep the ledger honest. Neither one prepares a thirteen-year-old boy for the morning raiders burn his valley a third time — and neither one explains why, when he climbs the one hill still standing and asks Heaven for help, Heaven actually answers. What is placed in his hands is a sword that never dulls and never explains itself, given by a woman who offers her own name for the blade — Valeria — and nothing else, before vanishing as though she was never anyone's to keep. He does not become a knight. He is never crowned. Over the long, unlikely span of a life that two kingdoms will go to war over and a faith will eventually be built to explain, he becomes something the chronicles never quite settle on a name for at all. Sword of Valeria follows a reckoner who measures the world in honest debts, through a war he never wanted, a crown offered to him twice and refused twice, and a princess who believes in him before anyone with real power does. Around him, kings gamble kingdoms on convenient lies, a captured man's confession very nearly starts a war and then very nearly stops one, and something patient and unseen watches every part of the story the histories will later insist on getting wrong. Spanning decades and told in the dry, exacting voice of a man who never once rounded a number in his life, this is a story about what gets remembered and what doesn't — the name of a sword outliving the name of the boy who carried it, the flattering version of a war outliving the small, petty truth that started it, and what it costs, and what it's worth, to do the right thing without needing anyone to record that you did it."},{"k":"s","t":"The Boy","u":"characters/the-boy.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"The Reckoner · The Answered Boy · The Uncrowned · The Unhastening · Ardwen's Wielder","x":"A valley goatherd who never gets a name, only titles. Never given a name that survives the telling — only the titles the decades hang on him one at a time. A valley goatherd and ledger-keeper, thirteen years old when a hillside prayer was answered with a sword named Valeria. He carries it for the rest of a long life without once asking to be crowned or believed — only reckoned with honestly, the way he was raised to keep a column of figures. Later, in Case 1153, he is cited at Ardenwake as the nameless founding wielder. I keep the count, so the valley is not forgotten.","i":"images/characters/the-boy-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ardwen","u":"characters/ardwen.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Observer 000 · The Delivering Woman · The Sky-Answerer · The Unhastening","x":"The woman who answers the boy's prayer — and the observer behind the whole story. Known in the earliest tellings as the Delivering Woman, the Sky-Answerer, the Unhastening — and, in the field reports she doesn't yet know are being kept about her, Observer 000. A field agent for the collective that will eventually call itself the Cultivators, she delivers the sword Valeria to the boy on the hilltop as an offering, not a gift pressed on him. She watches the rest of his life unfold from a distance, unseen, and doesn't interfere again unless a prayer is asked of her plainly. I do not hasten. The soil remembers. The sky answers.","i":"images/characters/ardwen-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Valeria","u":"characters/valeria.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"The Unlifted · the sword","x":"The blade at the center of it all — impossibly heavy, unliftable by anyone but him. The sword itself, sometimes treated as a character in its own right. Named by the woman who left it at the boy's door, forged of a metal no smith can identify. It weighs fifty kilograms on any scale, yet carries light as a coat in the hands of whoever it's chosen — cutting through stone and armor alike, with an edge that can't be felt and can't be dulled. Whispered of in courts, feared by warriors: unmeasured, unmatched, unlifted by anyone else. It weighs as the mountain remembers, and cuts as the moon refuses.","i":"images/characters/valeria-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Old Ren","u":"characters/old-ren.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Valley elder","x":"The woman who fed him after his mother died, and kept him honest. An elder of the boy's home valley, and one of its few surviving residents. She takes him in and feeds him after his mother's death, and keeps him grounded in what actually matters through everything that follows. Eat while it's warm. The road is long, and the world is cold.","i":"images/characters/old-ren-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Maren","u":"characters/maren.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Princess Maren · later Empress Maren","x":"The one person with real power who believes in him first. Eleven years old when she watches a king fail to lift the sword in front of two entire courts, and draws a different conclusion than everyone else in the room: that something which refuses a crown outright is harder to dismiss than something which simply agrees with whoever is wearing one. She spends the following decades being right about it, and becomes Empress of the realms her belief eventually helps unite — the one person with real power who believes in him first. The sword does not yield to strength, but to the truth of the heart. One day, he will understand.","i":"images/characters/maren-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"King Wendric","u":"characters/wendric.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"King of Rovain","x":"The king who tries to seize the sword, fails publicly, and never recovers. King of Rovain, fifty-four years old when he invokes the “Crown's Due” to seize the sword Valeria from the boy. He fails to lift it in front of his own court, and spends the rest of his reign struggling with what that failure means for his legitimacy — dying, eventually, in a field hospital, still questioning whether the crown was ever rightfully his.","i":"images/characters/wendric-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Corse","u":"characters/corse.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Steward to King Wendric of Rovain","x":"The king's steward, shaping the crown's response to the sword. Steward to King Wendric of Rovain, and a key political advisor shaping the crown's legal and political strategy regarding the sword, the boy, and the coming war. Precise and calculated, and careful never to let his face show what he's actually thinking.","i":"images/characters/corse-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"King Ossory","u":"characters/ossory.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"King of Ossory","x":"The political antagonist behind a war built on lies. King of Ossory, and the political antagonist whose court orchestrates the false-flag border raids that trick Rovain and Aldric into war. Calculating and regal, he commands through presence rather than force — and falls from power when his own conspiracy unravels.","i":"images/characters/ossory-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Cadmon","u":"characters/cadmon.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Fourth claimant to Ossory's throne · later King of Ossory","x":"Ossory's king, and the war's real architect — driven by arithmetic, not hatred. The fourth claimant to Ossory's throne, and the primary antagonist of the second half of the story. Cold and exact, driven by arithmetic and a belief in visiting humiliation on Rovain, he turns a border dispute into a full war against Rovain and Aldric — and dies during his own siege of Aldwick. Numbers do not lie. Only men do.","i":"images/characters/cadmon-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Reckoner (Assassin)","u":"characters/reckoner-assassin.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Professional assassin · contract-name “Reckoner”","x":"An assassin hired to end the story early — who decides not to. A professional assassin known by the contract-name “Reckoner” — not to be confused with the boy's own epithet of the same word. Hired by Cadmon to kill the boy, she abandons the contract after witnessing his total lack of fear for his own life, and sees no meaning left in killing someone who doesn't cling to living. I do not fear death; I fear wasting a breath.","i":"images/characters/reckoner-assassin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"King Wendric tries to lift Valeria","u":"records/wendric-lifts-sword.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"King Wendric","x":"King Wendric strains to lift the sword Valeria in front of his court King Wendric tries to lift Valeria.","i":"images/scenes/wendric-lifts-sword-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Maren, alone in the court","u":"records/maren-alone.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Maren","x":"A young Maren stands alone in a vast cathedral-like court Maren, alone in the court.","i":"images/scenes/maren-alone-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Observer 000, watching","u":"records/observer-watching.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Ardwen","x":"A dark-haired woman watches a public gathering from a balcony above Observer 000, watching.","i":"images/scenes/observer-watching-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The assassin hesitates","u":"records/assassin-hesitant.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"The Reckoner (Assassin)","x":"The masked assassin pauses with drawn swords while the boy walks on in the distance The assassin hesitates.","i":"images/scenes/assassin-hesitant-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Cadmon rallies his army","u":"records/cadmon-rallying.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Cadmon","x":"Cadmon raises his sword and rallies his army Cadmon rallies his army.","i":"images/scenes/cadmon-rallying-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"King Wendric's final hours","u":"records/wendric-dying.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"King Wendric","x":"King Wendric on his deathbed, surrounded by mourners and a priest King Wendric's final hours.","i":"images/scenes/wendric-dying-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Maren, deep in court intrigue","u":"records/maren-intrigue.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Maren","x":"An older Maren seated at a table with advisors flanking her Maren, deep in court intrigue.","i":"images/scenes/maren-intrigue-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The full power of Valeria","u":"records/valeria-full-power.html","m":"Case 0000 · The Sword of Valeria","d":"Valeria","x":"The boy stands holding a glowing Valeria, surrounded by fallen soldiers The full power of Valeria.","i":"images/scenes/valeria-full-power-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","u":"books/4099.html","m":"Case 4099 · Published","d":"Forty-two names, and the sweetness that cost them.","x":"Case 4099 Literary Fiction · Historical Fantasy · War & Military Fiction A cosmic observation program has a name for what it's about to watch: Case 4099, filed under Subject World 812-G, Mundane Class. What it actually contains is smaller — a glass jar of pink candy, smuggled into a Heian-era court by an envoy posing as a traveling confectioner, and given out one piece at a time to whoever the court's reigning arbiter of rank decides, that day, has earned it. The Principal Handmaid is the sole judge of who matters at court, and for nineteen years her rulings have gone unquestioned. A poet, a falconer, a gambler, a seamstress, a pair of twin gardeners — each receives a bead for some small grace, and each discovers, in time, that being noticed by the Handmaid is its own kind of death sentence. When her Second Handmaid is passed over once too often, five women quietly agree that an insult unpriced is an insult unpaid, and the court's silent arithmetic turns, without a single banner raised, into a war conducted entirely in forgery, poison, and fire. Forty-two names, by the end. The Ledger of a Single Sweetness is a reconstruction — read from the inside, filed from the outside, and honest about the gap between the two."},{"k":"s","t":"Tsuguo","u":"characters/tsuguo.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Gambler · 25 years old","x":"Wins a dice game against three ministers at once — and becomes a target for it. A gambler whose luck runs out the moment it becomes public. He wins a dice game against three ministers simultaneously and is rewarded with a bead — and, disastrously, a reputation for extraordinary luck that makes him a target the instant luck itself falls under suspicion. Luck is a blade. It cuts both ways.","i":"images/characters/tsuguo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Yorinaga","u":"characters/yorinaga.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Falconer","x":"A hawk brings back a warm hare. Then a fall no one examines too closely. A falconer whose hawk, trained from the egg nine years earlier, returns from a hunt with a snow-hare still warm — and earns him a bead. He's later found dead at the bottom of his own mews, in a fall the record calls implausible without quite calling it what it was. His hawk outlives him by exactly one more flight. A trained hawk, a warm hare, and a bead. Then a fall.","i":"images/characters/yorinaga-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Nariyuki","u":"characters/nariyuki.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Poet · 30s","x":"Eleven years of court verses end on a balcony someone swept clean. A court poet, favored for a verse about a heron that doesn't fear the cold. He's murdered by being pushed from a balcony after the stone underfoot is deliberately swept — eleven years of the same view, the same verses, ending on the same spot. The heron does not fear the cold, for it knows the river within it never freezes.","i":"images/characters/nariyuki-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kotone","u":"characters/kotone.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Attendant · 11 years of service at court","x":"Two beads for intercepting a letter — then a forged one ends her instead. An attendant of eleven years' standing, plain and steady in a way the court has always overlooked. She earns two beads for intercepting a rival's letter, and is later falsely implicated by forged correspondence with the Emperor's half-brother's household — arrested, tried for treason, and executed despite evidence too thin to support it. She wrote with the steadiness of someone who had always been overlooked.","i":"images/characters/kotone-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Principal Handmaid","u":"characters/principal-handmaid.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Sole auditor of the court's rank ledger · primary subject of Case 4099","x":"The one person at court whose word decides who matters — and who pays for it. The court's sole authority on rank, for nineteen years the only witness to a hierarchy she alone keeps. Given a glass jar of sweets by a traveling confectioner and free rein to distribute them as she sees fit, she doles out favor one bead at a time — never quite grasping that being noticed by her is its own kind of danger. By the end she has traded her habitual restraint for the one color she'd denied herself for years, and dies in a tower fire after arranging what beads remain. Rank is not a title. It is a ledger, and I am its only witness.","i":"images/characters/principal-handmaid-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Rin","u":"characters/rin.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Calligrapher · ~35 years old","x":"Corrects the same character eleven times in one afternoon — and it still wasn't enough. A calligrapher whose steady hand corrects the same character eleven times for eleven different students in a single afternoon, earning a bead for the patience alone. She becomes, later, one of the season's casualties. Eleven lines, eleven hands, the same character, and still it was not enough.","i":"images/characters/rin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Michiko","u":"characters/michiko.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Provincial Cousin · adult","x":"A kindness from the capital follows her home — and ends her life there. A cousin visiting from the provinces, given a bead as a small courtesy before her journey home. The gesture follows her back in the form of a letter that unravels a marriage negotiation, a landholding dispute, and eventually her life — all for wanting, as she puts it, to return home with dignity. A single bead, a kindness from the capital. I only wished to return home with dignity.","i":"images/characters/michiko-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Emi","u":"characters/emi.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Calligraphy Tutor · 25 years old","x":"Quietly corrects the Handmaid's brush angle — and pays for the resemblance later. A calligraphy tutor who earns a bead by quietly correcting the angle of the Principal Handmaid's brush. She's later accused of teaching a rival's daughter a hand suspiciously similar to the Handmaid's own, and is exiled to a province she's never seen, where she dies in a winter her body isn't prepared for. The angle of the brush is a kind of prayer. Too much pressure, and the letter dies.","i":"images/characters/emi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ai","u":"characters/ai.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Court Artisan","x":"Paints one half of a continuous crane — and dies within days of her partner. A court artisan who receives a bead jointly with Nozomi for painting matching halves of a single continuous crane across two surfaces. She dies of the same alleged fever as Nozomi, within four days of her.","i":"images/characters/ai-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kaoru","u":"characters/kaoru.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Twin Gardener · 20 years old","x":"Shapes a hedge into the likeness of a crane with her twin — and doesn't survive the season. One half of a twin gardening pair (with Fuyu), she receives a bead jointly with him for shaping a hedge into the exact silhouette of a crane. She dies during the season's retaliatory campaign. The hedge is not a wall, but a prayer made of leaves.","i":"images/characters/kaoru-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Nozomi","u":"characters/nozomi.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Court Artisan · young adult","x":"Two halves, one crane — the only explanation she or Ai were ever given. A court artisan paired with Ai, receiving a bead jointly with her for painting matching halves of a continuous crane. She dies of an alleged fever within four days of Ai's own death. Two halves, one crane. That is all we were given.","i":"images/characters/nozomi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Fuyu","u":"characters/fuyu.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Twin Gardener · 25 years old","x":"Shapes a hedge into a crane with his twin — and is left to grieve her alone. The other half of the twin gardening pair, working alongside Kaoru. Together they shape a hedge into the exact likeness of a crane and receive a bead for it jointly. He survives the season's retaliatory campaign; she doesn't. A hedge is not just a wall of leaves. It is a promise of what can be shaped.","i":"images/characters/fuyu-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Observer","u":"characters/observer-4099.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Envoy / Custodian Observer · Terrarium 812-G (Case 4099) · Deployment Archetype 04","x":"Enters the court as a candy seller, and leaves with a recommendation no one adopts. A field agent for the Cultivators — a different one from Ardwen, of a much lower-numbered case, and by his own count nowhere near as rare: eleven thousand and one terrariums logged by the time this one closes. He enters the court disguised as an itinerant confectioner and introduces the glass jar of sweets at the center of the whole case, then quietly monitors the court's collapse through instruments no one there can see. Against protocol, he comes to feel something for his subjects, and delays filing the case closed after the Principal Handmaid's death. His one recommendation — that the population left behind simply be allowed to be sad about what happened — is not adopted. It is also, notably, never deleted. Sweetness opens doors that authority cannot.","i":"images/characters/observer-4099-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Suzuriko","u":"characters/suzuriko.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Second Handmaid, by rank","x":"Passed over once too often, she turns exclusion into a nine-year architecture of debts. Second Handmaid by rank, and the only person at court who thinks to ask where an unlisted jar of sweets actually came from. When her own service goes unrewarded once too often, she organizes the first coalition of women who will become the Nine Threads, coordinates a forgery campaign to settle the court's accounts on her own terms, and — almost as an aside to all of it — makes sure one particular kindness to a girl never gets mentioned near the Threads at all. She outlives the tower fire by thirty-one years, and keeps one bead, uneaten, the entire time. The court believes in rank. I believe in what rank hides.","i":"images/characters/suzuriko-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Nine Threads","u":"characters/nine-threads.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"A secret retaliatory faction","x":"Nine debts, one hand, and only five names anyone was ever given. A secret coalition formed inside the court in answer to the Principal Handmaid's uneven favor and Suzuriko's own exclusion from it — organized under an emblem of a black hand trailing nine red threads: nine debts, collectively held. Five women sit at its center and are the only ones anyone has ever put names to: the Strategist (a senior lady-in-waiting), the Forger (a court scribe), the Poisoner (a physician's daughter), the Broker (a merchant's wife), and the Keeper (a young noblewoman). The remaining four threads — bribery, accident, poison, and arson — are debts the record lists as still awaiting collection, which is its own kind of answer. Nine threads. One hand. Nine debts.","i":"images/characters/nine-threads-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Fujiko","u":"characters/fujiko.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Court Lady · seasonal recipient","x":"A compliment, made too precisely, turns out to have been noticed after all. A court lady who earns a bead for complimenting the Principal Handmaid's fan with a specificity of observation that, under different circumstances, might have passed for nothing more than good manners. She becomes one of the names caught up in the season's retaliatory accounting. She noticed the fan before anyone else. A single glance, were it not so precise, would have been considered a compliment.","i":"images/characters/fujiko-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Tomoe","u":"characters/tomoe.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Seamstress / Mender · 25 years old","x":"Mends a torn hem in record time — and is remembered for it, in every sense. A seamstress who earns a bead for mending a torn hem in record time, treating the tear itself as a kind of wound on the court's grace. She later becomes one of the season's casualties. A torn hem is a wound on the court's grace. So I mend it, before it can be seen.","i":"images/characters/tomoe-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Yuki","u":"characters/yuki.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Lady-in-waiting · 25 years old","x":"One of three ladies-in-waiting whose whole distinction was smiling in the right place. One of three ladies-in-waiting — alongside Chika — whose entire claim on a bead is presence itself: composure, a correctly timed smile, sleeve layers arranged just so. She treats the smile as a duty rather than a feeling, and keeps it in place through every gathering but one, when something the record never names leaves her, for the first time, visibly uneasy. She becomes one of the season's casualties. A smile in the right place is also a kind of duty.","i":"images/characters/yuki-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Chiyo","u":"characters/chiyo.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Wet-nurse · 42 years old","x":"Calms an infant prince with a lullaby, and is fed her own poisoned porridge for it. Wet-nurse to an infant prince, and the possessor of the steadiest hands and quietest voice at court. She earns a bead for calming him with a lullaby in the middle of a crisis no one else can settle — and is later poisoned in the same bowl of rice porridge she had spent years preparing for other people's children. A quiet voice, a steady hand, and a child who sleeps.","i":"images/characters/chiyo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Norikuni","u":"characters/norikuni.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Court Physician · Advisor to the Handmaid","x":"Keeps records of what the court refuses to see — and is poisoned for the noticing. Court physician and advisor to the Principal Handmaid, some thirty years into a practice built on reading small physical changes other people talk themselves out of noticing. He keeps written records not only of bodies but of what the court prefers left unrecorded, and is the one who tries to warn the Handmaid before it's too late. His own final weeks — shaking hands, a complexion gone grey and waxen — read, to anyone who learned to listen the way he taught them, exactly like the poisoning he spent a career diagnosing in other people. The body speaks in small changes, if only we still listen.","i":"images/characters/norikuni-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Sweets","u":"characters/the-sweets.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Pink Yummy Gummy Beads · the Catalyst of Case 4099","x":"A glass jar of about two hundred pink sweets — the whole engine of the court's collapse. The object at the center of the case: a clear, unchased glass jar holding roughly two hundred small, glossy pink confections, introduced to the court as a passing novelty by the Observer and immediately recast as a currency of favor. The court never settles on what to call them — “beasts,” “beads,” “sweets,” each used as though it were the obvious word — and the Cultivators' own instruments log the anatomy of a single piece as deliberately imprecise. A hundred and eighty-six remain after the first month; a hundred and sixty-four are eventually distributed by the Principal Handmaid herself. Custodian telemetry recovers thirty-six, unclaimed, from the ash of the tower where the case ends. Small, pink, and they glisten as though freshly wept from something larger than themselves.","i":"images/characters/the-sweets-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Chika","u":"characters/chika.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Lady-in-waiting · early 20s","x":"One of three ladies-in-waiting rewarded for nothing but standing in the right place. One of three ladies-in-waiting — alongside Yuki — whose defining act, by her own account, is simply being present and smiling in the correct half of the room. She takes the small painted hairpin she's given as confirmation that presence itself is a kind of service, and becomes, in time, one of the season's casualties. Just present, just smile — that is enough, was it not?","i":"images/characters/chika-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Hana","u":"characters/hana.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Calligraphy Student, later Instructor","x":"Keeps her own private ledger of who received a bead, and why — and survives the season. A calligraphy student who nurses a laundress through a fever for her first bead, and is later trusted enough to help a young, imprisoned Kotone compose a letter that never gets sent. Where the court keeps one ledger of rank, Hana quietly keeps a second: her own private notebook of who received a bead, for what, and where the official account doesn't add up. She survives the season, goes on to teach calligraphy herself in later life, and spends the rest of it avoiding the color pink. “The court keeps its ledger,” she says of it, decades on. “I keep mine.” Some truths are not meant to be written. But if I do not remember, who will?","i":"images/characters/hana-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sukeko","u":"characters/sukeko.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Chrysanthemum Expert · 20 years old","x":"Names eleven chrysanthemum varieties in a single afternoon, and writes home to her betrothed. Court expert on chrysanthemums, credited with correctly identifying eleven varieties in a single afternoon — a feat she insists, in her own quiet way, wasn't cleverness at all: she only named what the flowers already were. Between identification work she writes letters to her betrothed, until some piece of news the record leaves unspecified reaches her, and the easy composure of her earlier expressions doesn't come back. Eleven varieties. One afternoon. I only named what the flowers already were.","i":"images/characters/sukeko-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Tadamori","u":"characters/tadamori.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Kemari Player","x":"An unbroken run of four hundred kicks earns a bead — and a debt collector's excuse. A kemari player who earns a bead for an unbroken run of more than four hundred kicks — a genuine feat of endurance the court, within weeks, turns into a pretext instead of an honor. Invented gambling debts are called in against him almost as soon as the bead is pinned on, and he is found dead beneath the goalpost not long after, his public reward having quietly become, in practice, a sentence.","i":"images/characters/tadamori-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Obaa","u":"characters/obaa.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Elderly Seamstress Supervisor","x":"Has dressed three Handmaids before this one — and becomes one of the season's casualties too. The court's elderly seamstress supervisor, who has personally dressed three Handmaids in succession before the current Principal Handmaid, and approaches the work, after a lifetime of it, with something close to devotion. She receives a bead for the quality of that service, and later becomes one of the season's casualties in her own turn. Stitches keep more than fabric together.","i":"images/characters/obaa-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Saemon","u":"characters/saemon.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Minor Scribe · 27 years old","x":"A flawless copy of a sutra earns a bead — and doesn't survive the Harvest. A minor court scribe who earns a bead for a flawless copy of a sutra — the same single-error-undoes-a-hundred-hours precision his whole professional life is built on. He doesn't survive what the court's own records name only as “the Harvest,” the same reckoning that closes out so many of the year's other bead recipients. A single error can undo a hundred hours. A single bead can make it worthwhile.","i":"images/characters/saemon-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Nightingale","u":"characters/nightingale.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Biwa Player · known as the Nightingale","x":"A mourning song moves the Handmaid to tears — and costs her the hands that played it. A biwa player known at court only by the epithet Nightingale, earned for a voice the record calls “a bird in a room of stone.” She receives a bead after a mourning song moves the Principal Handmaid to tears in front of the whole court — and not long after, her hands are permanently ruined in what's staged to look like an ordinary folding-screen accident, ending her playing career along with, deliberately, whatever it was about her that had moved anyone. Her song was a bird in a room of stone. When she finished, the Handmaid wept.","i":"images/characters/nightingale-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Principal Handmaid tries the pink beads","u":"records/handmaid-tries-beads.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Principal Handmaid","x":"The Principal Handmaid tastes one of the pink sweets for the first time, ladies-in-waiting behind her The Principal Handmaid tries the pink beads.","i":"images/scenes/handmaid-tries-beads-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"An assassin stalks Nariyuki","u":"records/assassin-stalks-nariyuki.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Nariyuki","x":"A hooded figure with a drawn sword crouches behind Nariyuki as he walks a moonlit veranda An assassin stalks Nariyuki.","i":"images/scenes/assassin-stalks-nariyuki-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Principal Handmaid, on calligraphy","u":"records/handmaid-calligraphy.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Principal Handmaid","x":"The Principal Handmaid sits close with a young attendant over tea and writing implements on a snowy night The Principal Handmaid, on calligraphy.","i":"images/scenes/handmaid-calligraphy-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The tower burns","u":"records/tower-burning.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","x":"A tower engulfed in flame at night while guards and court watch from below The tower burns.","i":"images/scenes/tower-burning-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Principal Handmaid's court","u":"records/handmaid-court.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Principal Handmaid","x":"The Principal Handmaid presides over a grand ceremonial court, rows of attendants kneeling on either side The Principal Handmaid's court.","i":"images/scenes/handmaid-court-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Grief, after the execution","u":"records/grief-after-execution.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","x":"The Principal Handmaid collapses in grief on the floor after an execution, a box of beads beside her Grief, after the execution.","i":"images/scenes/grief-after-execution-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Observer leaves the candy","u":"records/envoy-leaves-candy.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Observer","x":"A cloaked traveler leaves a jar of pink candy on a market table during an autumn festival The Observer leaves the candy.","i":"images/scenes/envoy-leaves-candy-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Principal Handmaid gives a bead to Hana","u":"records/handmaid-gives-bead-to-hana.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Principal Handmaid, Hana","x":"The Principal Handmaid hands a single pink bead to Hana, who tends a sick laundress in the snow The Principal Handmaid gives a bead to Hana.","i":"images/scenes/handmaid-gives-bead-to-hana-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Nine Threads forge Kotone's hand","u":"records/nine-threads-forge-handwriting.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Nine Threads, Kotone","x":"A forger studies rows of handwriting samples labeled Kotone while practicing her hand for a forged confession The Nine Threads forge Kotone's hand.","i":"images/scenes/nine-threads-forge-handwriting-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The execution","u":"records/the-execution.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","x":"A seamstress is taken by armed guards mid-stitch while other court women look on in distress The execution.","i":"images/scenes/the-execution-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Nine Threads' emblem","u":"records/nine-threads-emblem.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Nine Threads","x":"A blackened hand with nine red threads tied to each fingertip, radiating outward against a dark emblem The Nine Threads' emblem.","i":"images/scenes/nine-threads-emblem-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Arriving in the empty court","u":"records/arriving-empty-court.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","x":"Soldiers and a mounted rider enter an overgrown palace gate long after the court has emptied Arriving in the empty court.","i":"images/scenes/arriving-empty-court-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Principal Handmaid, and the empty court","u":"records/handmaid-in-empty-court.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Principal Handmaid","x":"The Principal Handmaid walks alone through a vast hall of empty seats where her court once knelt The Principal Handmaid, and the empty court.","i":"images/scenes/handmaid-in-empty-court-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Norikuni, keeping his records","u":"records/the-physician.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"Norikuni","x":"Norikuni writes careful notes at a desk crowded with medicine jars and herbs on a snowy night Norikuni, keeping his records.","i":"images/scenes/the-physician-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Principal Handmaid lays out the beads","u":"records/handmaid-lays-out-beads.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Principal Handmaid","x":"The Principal Handmaid sits before three labeled rows of beads: Confirmed Dead, Suspected Victims, Unproven Names The Principal Handmaid lays out the beads.","i":"images/scenes/handmaid-lays-out-beads-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Principal Handmaid decides where the next bead goes","u":"records/handmaid-decides-recipient.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Principal Handmaid","x":"The Principal Handmaid writes in her ledger beside a jar of beads, deciding who receives the next one The Principal Handmaid decides where the next bead goes.","i":"images/scenes/handmaid-decides-recipient-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The final violet dress, before the burning","u":"records/final-violet-before-burning.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","x":"The Principal Handmaid sits in the forbidden violet robe, a box of beads in her lap, as fire spreads outside The final violet dress, before the burning.","i":"images/scenes/final-violet-before-burning-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Nine Threads, gathered","u":"records/nine-threads-gathered.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","d":"The Nine Threads","x":"Five women sit together behind raised fans, papers spread on the table between them The Nine Threads, gathered.","i":"images/scenes/nine-threads-gathered-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The single bead","u":"records/the-single-bead.html","m":"Case 4099 · The Ledger of a Single Sweetness","x":"One pink bead sits alone on a dark lacquered table beside a writing brush The single bead.","i":"images/scenes/the-single-bead-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Stolen Prince War","u":"books/0157.html","m":"Case 0157 · Published","d":"An indifferent floor, a dead prince, and a war built on the wrong reason.","x":"Case 0157 Epic Fantasy · Dungeon Fantasy · War & Military Fiction Ashkevar is a poor kingdom before it is anything else — barley, debt, and the patient silence of a god who answers the honest but never the desperate. It's debt, not vision, that sends a shepherd up a hillside above Har-Peleg looking for free firewood, and debt that has him and four others prying open a doorway three generations had already stopped seeing. What's behind it isn't a ruin: a hundred descending levels, a guardian at every fifth, a chest at every tenth, and — rarer, and stranger — a vial of something that can pull the dying back from wounds that should have killed them. It's the highland kingdom of Vantashen, not Ashkevar, that turns the discovery into a war. A young prince — Vartaz, King Torvash's youngest, delving in disguise to prove himself away from his father's court — reaches a guarded hall at the same moment a seasoned local party does, and a mechanism built to clear a room for six fighters — never built to recognize two kingdoms colliding in one doorway — does exactly what it was built to do. His father doesn't wait for a fuller account before choosing war, and for three years two kingdoms bleed each other over a death neither side's histories will ever describe accurately, while underneath both armies the hillside resets itself every night and keeps paying gold to whoever's left standing by morning. The war ends the way these things tend to: a peace with no victor, a marriage that quietly merges two crowns, and one woman — Nairi, the dead prince's own sister — who eventually descends far enough to hold the actual proof of what happened in her own two hands. What she decides two exhausted kingdoms are owed instead of that truth is the real center of Case 0157. Something patient files the whole of it away regardless, and finds her choice, on reflection, more worth preserving than anything its own guardians ever did."},{"k":"s","t":"Hovan","u":"characters/hovan.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Vantashen Lowland Levy · 20 years old","x":"A Vantashen levy soldier engaged to a miller's daughter — killed by a panicked shot at a river crossing. A Vantashen farm boy conscripted into the lowland levy, barely trained with the spear he's issued and engaged, the night before muster, to Araxi, a miller's daughter back home. He dies in the chaos of a border skirmish, shot from the opposite bank by Elad, a baker's apprentice barely younger than himself who never meant to hit anyone. It takes eleven years for Elad to learn his name. He was the kind of man who looked at the world and expected it to be kind back.","i":"images/characters/hovan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Tirtzah","u":"characters/tirtzah.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guild Guide · Ezra's Teacher · ~40 years old","x":"A guild guide who leads a party into a guarded hall to test her student's theory — and doesn't come back up. A patient, indulgent guide who taught most of a generation of delvers how to read a floor before it read them — including Ezra, her most promising student. She trusts his confident new theory about crossing a guarded hall safely with twice the usual company, leads a party in herself to test it, and never surfaces again. Ezra never again publishes a theory he hasn't first tested alone, at his own risk — a caution that, decades later, keeps considerably more delvers alive than his teacher's death ever cost the guild. A theory is not a truth until it survives the dark.","i":"images/characters/tirtzah-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Vahan","u":"characters/vahan.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Vantashen Guard Captain · 52 years old","x":"Thirty years a soldier, still not used to what the dungeon's cold corridor shows him. Torvash's old captain, and one of the twelve Nairi brings with her on the Level 101 expedition decades later — thirty years past believing death arrives with any ceremony at all. He passes through the Catacomb's cold-corridor anomaly along the way and is shown, without warning or explanation, the face of a sister who died decades before. He says little about it afterward. After thirty years of soldiering, he stopped believing death would come with ceremony.","i":"images/characters/vahan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Oren","u":"characters/oren.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Delving Captain · later Wartime Defender of Har-Peleg","x":"A delving captain turned wall-builder, who dies in the very fire he used to save Har-Peleg. A guild delving captain whose ear for a chamber's failing structure carries over, later in life, into an entirely different kind of defense: recognizing the exact moment Har-Peleg's eastern wall is about to give during the war, and sealing the breach with fire before it does. The fire that saves the wall is also the fire that kills him. The official histories don't record his name. A wall doesn't fall all at once. It gives you a moment — if you're listening.","i":"images/characters/oren-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The White Draught","u":"characters/the-white-draught.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"The Catacomb's second most important element · an unscheduled secondary effect","x":"A vial that heals like nothing else in the world — and always costs a very specific amount of gold. A small glass vial of clear, faintly warm liquid, found rarely behind the Catacomb's major guardians alongside the gold — and never without it: every appearance of a Draught coincides with a precise, otherwise unexplained quantity of gold missing from the same chest. The first-ever vessel turns up behind the Widow of the Tenth Hall. It heals wounds nothing else can, is indistinguishable from a counterfeit without drinking it, and becomes, within a decade of its discovery, more valuable and more fought over than the gold it always arrives beside. A small vial. A great disturbance.","i":"images/characters/the-white-draught-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Yudith","u":"characters/yudith.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Keeper of the Vigil of Ardwen · House of Vigil","x":"The Keeper of the Vigil, watching a dungeon complicate a doctrine she's preached for eleven years. Keeper of the Vigil of Ardwen, who has spent eleven years teaching that Ardwen answers the honest but never on command — a doctrine a dungeon that opens the same chest, the same way, every single time, makes steadily harder to preach. She raises the objection formally and is proven right eventually, a generation after her death, by which point the Houses nearest the entrance have long since reorganized themselves around blessing delvers rather than warning them. The rewards of the dungeon are not a sign of Ardwen's favor, nor are they compatible with Her doctrine. Miracles are honest, but never on command.","i":"images/characters/yudith-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Golden Catacomb","u":"characters/the-golden-catacomb.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"The Catalyst of Case 0157 · a hundred levels, one entrance","x":"A hole in a hillside no one had thought worth digging for three generations — until someone did. A single engineered structure delivered by the Cultivators as the Catalyst of Case 0157: a hundred levels, a real gold vein at the bottom, and exactly one entrance that no wall, ram, or engineer's cleverness has ever managed to bypass. Its guardians change armor and iconography to reflect whichever culture has most recently come looking, and every level resets completely overnight — damage repaired, the dead reassembled, anything merely left behind quietly gone by morning. Its builder and sole operator keeps a permanent seat at Level 101, and its rarest reward isn't gold at all, but the White Draught. Not a ruin. A rule. Not a ruin. A rule.","i":"images/characters/the-golden-catacomb-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Magistrate of Empty Chairs","u":"characters/the-magistrate-of-empty-chairs.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guardian Construct · Level 65","x":"A courtroom of chairs that fills, one silent copy at a time, with everything a party has already killed. Enthroned at the Catacomb's sixty-fifth level before a courtroom of empty seats, the Magistrate presides over trespassers with a gavel that strikes far harder than its ceremonial weight suggests. Each seat fills, one by one, with a silent copy of whichever guardian a party has already defeated on the way down — the same witnesses, attending the same sentence, again and again. It has no features, no eyes, and, so far as anyone has found, no way to be reasoned with. A judge without a court, a law without a people. It sits, and the chairs remember.","i":"images/characters/the-magistrate-of-empty-chairs-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sentinel of Dust","u":"characters/sentinel-of-dust.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guardian Construct · Level 5 · the Catacomb's first named guardian","x":"The dungeon's first named guardian — twin blades, a hidden crossguard, and no face beneath the wrappings. The first guardian the founding expedition ever gave a name to, and the one credited with the dungeon's first delver death: Doron, cut down by a crossguard mechanism that stays concealed until triggered and locks in place to punish overextension. Wrapped head to foot in grave-linens for the whole of its unaging, roughly ninety-year existence, it shows no skin, no eyes, and no hair — and is quietly reconfigured into something else entirely in the aftermath of Case 0157. No exposed skin. No visible eyes. No visible hair. What lies beneath is never shown.","i":"images/characters/sentinel-of-dust-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Elad","u":"characters/elad.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Baker's Apprentice, Har-Peleg · Wartime Militia Archer","x":"A baker's apprentice handed a borrowed crossbow, who fires once, in panic, and spends eleven years carrying it. Apprenticed to a Har-Peleg bakery since the age of eleven, handed a militia armband and a borrowed crossbow he was never trained to use once the war reaches the river crossing. At seventeen, in the chaos of a skirmish, he fires once, in panic, and kills a Vantashen soldier he never sees clearly. He carries the guilt for eleven years before he finally learns the dead man's name: Hovan. I didn't mean to. I was just... afraid.","i":"images/characters/elad-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Vasak","u":"characters/vasak.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"General & Councilor · Vantashen Council","x":"The one voice on Vantashen's council who argued against the war for a full year — and was right. General and councilor to the Vantashen crown, and the one member of Torvash's council who spends a full year arguing against the war on grounds that turn out, in every particular that matters, to be correct. He's overruled anyway, fights it regardless once it starts, and dies still believing he simply lost an argument — never learning how right he actually was, or why.","i":"images/characters/vasak-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Torvash","u":"characters/torvash.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"King of Vantashen · 58 years old","x":"A king who chooses war out of grief for a son the dungeon killed by simple bad timing. King of Vantashen for thirty-one years, and a father whose composure fails, completely and publicly, on the news of his son's death at the Catacomb's guarded halls. He chooses war against Ashkevar in the grief that follows, encouraged toward a certainty he never fully questions, and abdicates within two years of the war's uneasy end. He spends the rest of his life in penitential prayer, and is buried, by his own instruction, without eulogy. A king who carried grief until grief became war.","i":"images/characters/torvash-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Doron","u":"characters/doron.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Founding Five · Ashkevari · 24 years old","x":"One of the dungeon's first five delvers, young and quick with a sword that still looks unfamiliar in his hand — and the fifth level is as far as he gets. One of the five who first went down when the doorway above Har-Peleg was found: young, quick with a short sword that still looks faintly unfamiliar in his hand. He survives the founding party's first guardian — a skeleton Tamir breaks apart with nothing but a farm mattock — but doesn't get much further himself. The Sentinel of Dust, five levels down, ends him with a blade none of them saw coming.","i":"images/characters/doron-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Multitude","u":"characters/the-multitude.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guardian Construct · the level before the 37th, anomalously","x":"A guardian built of many fused, half-organic limbs, waiting where no guardian is supposed to be. An anomaly even by the Catacomb's own standards: guardians are supposed to hold the every-fifth-level pattern the guild mapped generations ago, and this one simply doesn't, appearing instead on the level just short of the thirty-seventh. Built from dozens of fused limbs, part mechanism and part something closer to organic, it moves with an insect's coordination rather than anything humanoid — and no one has yet explained why it's there at all. Many fused limbs. Insect-like coordination. Partially organic, and not fully explained.","i":"images/characters/the-multitude-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Araxi","u":"characters/araxi.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Miller's Daughter · Vantashen Lowlands · 19 years old","x":"A miller's daughter engaged to a soldier who won't be coming back from the war. A miller's daughter in the Vantashen lowlands, sturdy and sun-browned from mill work, engaged to Hovan the night before he's mustered south with the levy. She wears the betrothal band turned inward, so it won't catch on the millstones, and keeps working the mill the whole time he's gone — grain to flour, flour to bread, and the village living another day, whether or not he ever comes home to see it. The mill turns, the river flows, and tomorrow we try again.","i":"images/characters/araxi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ammiel","u":"characters/ammiel.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"King of Ashkevar · grandfather to Boaz","x":"The King of Ashkevar, presiding over a diplomatic crisis he didn't start and can't fully contain. King of Ashkevar: a seasoned ruler rather than a warrior, who values patience and stability over the kind of certainty that starts wars. He presides over his council during the diplomatic crisis that follows the prince's death, oversees the carefully hedged condolence letter his court drafts for Vantashen, and elevates his second grandson, Boaz, to heir — a decision that will, within a generation, quietly unite both crowns. A kingdom endures not by the strength of its king, but by the patience of those who remain.","i":"images/characters/ammiel-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Tide That Forgot the Sea","u":"characters/the-tide-that-forgot-the-sea.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guardian Construct · Level 20","x":"A flood with no source and no mercy, waist-high and patient, on the dungeon's twentieth level. An environmental guardian with no body, no face, and no weapons: the twentieth level simply floods, waist-high, in water that behaves exactly like water in every particular except one — anything it touches stays completely dry. It has no visible source and needs none, persisting without inlet or outlet, and drowns careless delvers with the same patient indifference every season, asking nothing of them but the time it takes them to stop swimming. Patient indifference.","i":"images/characters/the-tide-that-forgot-the-sea-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Peled","u":"characters/peled.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Porter · hired for Nairi's Level 101 expedition","x":"A porter hired to carry what the rest of the party can't — and to complain about it with real eloquence. A broad, unhurried Ashkevari porter, hired for the sole purpose of carrying what Nairi's twelve-strong party couldn't carry themselves, and known throughout the expedition for his real and consistent eloquence in complaining about the weight of it. He carries no weapon beyond a belt knife — his load is his function — and decades of it have left a permanent stoop in his shoulders. He's still there at the bottom, still complaining, still carrying it anyway. Just a little more weight, that's all...","i":"images/characters/peled-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Weaver Who Outlived Her Thread","u":"characters/the-weaver-who-outlived-her-thread.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guardian Construct · Level 85","x":"A guardian who weaves a single chamber-spanning thread before combat begins — and it doesn't break until you do. Veiled like the Widow before her, in mourning-cloth the guild long ago stopped finding strange, she weaves a single unbroken thread across the width of her chamber in the seconds before a fight begins — a thread sharp enough to cut a careless party in half simply for walking through it. No visible skin, eyes, or hair; what's beneath the veil has never been confirmed. Visually and thematically paired with the Widow as part of the Catacomb's deeper mourning-and-weaving motif. In the seconds before battle begins, she weaves. And the thread does not break — until you do.","i":"images/characters/the-weaver-who-outlived-her-thread-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Doreth","u":"characters/doreth.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Hierarch of Solan · Vantashen Threefold","x":"Prince Vartaz's tutor of eleven years, who turns his own grief for the boy into a war he tells himself is a monument. Hierarch of the Temple of Solan, and Vartaz's tutor for eleven years — long enough to recognize the prince's restlessness as something closer to devotion than indiscipline, and to call the resulting attachment investment rather than name it what it was. He engineers Torvash's decision to go to war in the depth of the king's grief, telling himself he's building the boy a monument rather than a lie. Nairi eventually brings him down anyway — not for the war, which she can't afford to reopen, but for embezzling temple silver, a charge with considerably less romance and considerably more proof. Words outlive men. That is why we weigh them carefully.","i":"images/characters/doreth-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Ember Judge","u":"characters/the-ember-judge.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guardian Construct · Level 40 · also called the Reckoning or the Trial of Solan","x":"The guardian whose floor-vent mechanism, triggered by two overlapping parties, kills Prince Vartaz. Armored head to foot in plate carved with the interlocking crowns of the Threefold faith, and armed with gauntlets that vent a controlled gout of flame on every blow. Guards the chest at Level 40 with a mechanism the delvers call the grace count: a guardian's defeat starts a brief, generous window to clear the room before the floor's own vents fire, built to empty a chamber of six fighters and never once built to recognize two rival parties colliding in the same doorway. It's exactly that mechanism, doing exactly what it was built to do, that kills Vartaz — not a blade, not a rival, not any crime with a face attached to it. Reconfigured into something else entirely after Nairi's unauthorized visit to Level 101. It does not hate. It does not forgive. It only counts.","i":"images/characters/the-ember-judge-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Hundred-Handed Reckoner","u":"characters/the-hundred-handed-reckoner.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guardian Construct · Level 100 · the final level before 101","x":"Not a being. A ledger made flesh, armed with a stylus, a scale, a blade, and a tally plate. The last guardian before the stair no map ever shows: a featureless, faceless construct built from dozens of jointed arms, each carrying a different instrument of account — a stylus to record and calculate, a balance to weigh debts, a blade to execute them, and a tally plate for what can't be forgiven. It fights for no kingdom and no god. Its surface is engraved, top to bottom, with tally marks and accounting geometry that guild scholars agree is a record rather than decoration, of a very old account nobody still living can read. Not a being. A ledger made flesh. It does not fight for a kingdom, nor for a god. It only counts.","i":"images/characters/the-hundred-handed-reckoner-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Widow of the Tenth Hall","u":"characters/the-widow-of-the-tenth-hall.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guardian Construct · Level 10","x":"A veiled guardian who weeps a corrosive mist when struck — and turns out to be no widow at all. Veiled crown to floor in cloth the color of old mourning, fighting with a processional staff and weeping a fine corrosive mist whenever she's struck — a mist that ate leather before it ate skin. Shira's party takes the better part of two hours and the ruin of one man's sword-arm to bring the veil apart, revealing nothing underneath but a frame, a mechanism, plates and hinges. She guards the chest holding the first-ever White Draught vessel, and her defeat reshapes Ashkevar's whole religious debate over miracles that arrive on command. She does not mourn. She preserves.","i":"images/characters/the-widow-of-the-tenth-hall-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Nursemaid","u":"characters/the-nursemaid.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guardian Construct · Level 54 · guards the approach to the anomalous 55th","x":"A gentle, maternal guardian whose anatomy grows steadily, deliberately wrong the longer you look at it. Soft-faced and maternal at a glance, speaking in a calm, reassuring voice — and, the longer a party actually looks at her, more wrong: extra joints, elongated limbs, a rib and spine line that shouldn't hold together at all. She's unaging, doesn't tire, and doesn't leave her post one level above the heat-induced hallucination anomaly that gave the 55th its own reputation. Nobody has ever reported her doing anything but reassure them, which several delvers report finding worse than a fight would have been. Don't be afraid, little one... I'll help you.","i":"images/characters/the-nursemaid-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Vartaz","u":"characters/vartaz.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Prince of Vantashen · Torvash's youngest child · age 20 at death","x":"A prince who travels to Ashkevar in disguise to prove himself — and dies to a mechanism, not a rival. Torvash's youngest child, and the only one of his line who ever wanted to be somewhere other than a throne: he travels to Ashkevar in a merchant's disguise, delving for the sport of it, trying to matter on his own account rather than his father's. He dies at the Ember Judge's chamber when its grace-count mechanism triggers with two rival parties still inside — no blade, no rival, no crime with a face attached to it, though it takes his sister two more decades to find the proof. His recovered armor, scorched on one flank and undamaged everywhere else, becomes the war's central, unspoken lie. The world is far larger than a throne, and I intend to see more of it.","i":"images/characters/vartaz-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Tamir","u":"characters/tamir.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Founding Five · Ashkevari · 26 years old","x":"A farmhand who breaks the dungeon's first skeleton apart with nothing but his own mattock. One of the five who first went down when the doorway above Har-Peleg was found, and the one who actually lands the founding party's decisive blow: breaking the first guardian's arm at the elbow with a farm mattock, swung by a man who'd never before held anything more dangerous than a scythe. He survives the expedition that costs Doron his life five levels down. A good tool, in his own accounting, never needed to be a good weapon. A good tool does not need to be a good weapon.","i":"images/characters/tamir-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Boaz","u":"characters/boaz.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"House Ashkevar · grandson to King Ammiel · later King-consort of Ashkevar","x":"A coppersmith's apprentice turned second-in-line heir, who reads every clause twice before he'll sign it. Second grandson to King Ammiel, raised for the first twenty years of his life to be a coppersmith rather than a king, until a fever no physician could name took his elder brother and left him the only heir left. He carries the trade's one real lesson into statecraft: check the measurement twice, since a man who doesn't eventually kills someone with the difference. It's this — and not the throne — that Nairi proposes marriage to, plainly and specifically, across a treaty table set up in a ruined border town neither kingdom could yet afford to rebuild. A steady hand builds what time destroys.","i":"images/characters/boaz-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Observer","u":"characters/observer-0157.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"The Dungeon Manager · the Catacomb's builder and sole operator · seated at Level 101","x":"The old man at the bottom of the hole, who built every level of it and remembers everything anyone ever left behind. A field agent for the Cultivators, presenting in human guise as an ordinary, forgettable old man — no delver who ever saw him could later agree on a description beyond that. He designed and maintains all hundred levels, keeps every item any party ever left behind in an archive at the very bottom, and hands Nairi her brother's recovered, fire-damaged armor without a word of explanation. He reconfigures the entire dungeon in the aftermath of her unauthorized visit, and is the closing subject of Case 0157's own Custodian Diagnostic Log — which he apparently files himself. You have brought me an interesting item.","i":"images/characters/observer-0157-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Berel","u":"characters/berel.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Founding Five · grave-goods dealer, Har-Peleg","x":"The one who first says the doorway is worth opening — and spends the rest of his life letting the legends grow. A grave-goods and battlefield-leavings dealer, missing the last joint of one finger to a grave-good he once priced wrong, and the one who first says aloud what the other four are already thinking: that old ruins keep old gold. He drinks most nights at the Widow's Rest — a tavern the town named, like so much else in Har-Peleg, for a monster rather than a person — and takes a grim satisfaction in letting each new legend about the hillside stand uncorrected, on the theory that a man who tells the truth in a tavern has misunderstood what taverns are for. Old ruins kept old gold.","i":"images/characters/berel-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Level 71 Guardian","u":"characters/level-71-guardian.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Cultivator-built construct · stands before the anomalous 72nd level","x":"Dozens of carved stone faces, all screaming in perfect silence, all at once. Not a person and not a single face, but a chorus: dozens of carved stone faces fused into one towering form, mouths open in perfect unison around a scream the hall never once lets anyone actually hear. No eyes, no pupils, no hair, no clothing, no weapons — purely a guardian, and by every account a guild scholar has offered, not a living thing at all. It stands before the 72nd level's own anomaly, a gas that leaves more than one hardened delver briefly convinced his own shadow has started keeping time slightly behind him. Not a person. Not a single face. But a chorus.","i":"images/characters/level-71-guardian-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Netzer","u":"characters/netzer.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Founding Five · Ashkevari shepherd","x":"A shepherd who goes looking for free firewood to pay a debt, and finds a hundred-level dungeon instead. A shepherd deep enough in debt to go looking for firewood he doesn't have to buy, which is how he finds the doorway above Har-Peleg that three generations of shepherds had walked past without seeing. He pays off the debt within the year and stops descending once the reason he went down is settled, spending the rest of a long life as the guild's first and most reluctant elder statesman. He climbs back to the entrance once a season for the rest of his life to ask it, plainly, whether any of it ever meant anything. It never answers him.","i":"images/characters/netzer-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ezra","u":"characters/ezra.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Guild Guide · foremost theorist of the Catacomb · age 48","x":"The guild's foremost theorist of the dungeon, who never again tests an unproven idea on anyone but himself. A guild guide who grew from a boy carrying rope and lamp-oil into the guild's de facto master theorist: thirty years of cross-referenced field reports, and several conclusions — that guardians are sometimes rebuilt outright, that the gold's placement is no accident, that whatever keeps the hillside's accounts adjusts its own methods in response to the guild's — too unsettling for the board to publish. He earned the caution the hard way: his own teacher, Tirtzah, died testing a theory of his that he'd never tested himself first. He leads Nairi's twelve down to Level 101, and is the one who eventually reconstructs, from the Observer's own scattered notes, what the choosing of her brother's armor actually meant. The dungeon is not only a place. It is a question that refuses to end.","i":"images/characters/ezra-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Shira","u":"characters/shira.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Founding Five · later Delving Captain · Har-Peleg's first true captain","x":"Present at the founding, present at the Widow's discovery, present at Vartaz's death — and the one who mentors Nairi. One of the founding five at nineteen, and by her thirties the first true captain Har-Peleg ever produced — leading the expedition that discovers the Widow of the Tenth Hall, and carrying the corrosive-mist scar from that fight for the rest of her life. She's also the seasoned captain whose party collides with Vartaz's at the Ember Judge's chamber, and the only living witness who ever really saw what happened in his face in the moment the floor took him. Asked about it for the rest of her life, she gives the same three words every time. Old, and no longer welcome in taverns that have renamed themselves twice since her captaincy, she spends her final years tending a garden with the same exacting patience she once brought to a guardian fight, and gives Nairi the last real counsel she carries down to Level 101. I forget now.","i":"images/characters/shira-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Nairi","u":"characters/nairi.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Princess, later Queen of Vantashen · Queen-consort of Ashkevar · Sole Ruler","x":"Vartaz's own sister, who eventually holds the literal proof of how he died in her own two hands — and says nothing. Vartaz's sister, seventeen when he dies and queen of Vantashen not long after. She proposes her own marriage to Boaz of Ashkevar across a treaty table in a ruined border town, and rules both crowns eventually, outliving every other claimant on either side. Years later she leads twelve delvers back down to Level 101 and is handed her brother's recovered armor by the Observer without a word of explanation — proof enough to correct the entire official cause of the war. She keeps it in her private ledger instead, deciding, again and again for the rest of her reign, that an exhausted peace is worth more than an uncomfortable truth. Permanent ink stains the first two fingers of her writing hand. A kingdom is not kept by crowns, but by what is paid for them.","i":"images/characters/nairi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Amitai","u":"characters/amitai.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Court Scribe · Vantashen joint court, under Queen Nairi","x":"The literal-minded scribe trusted to record Nairi's account — who can't quite leave a silence unexplained. A court scribe, chosen to record Nairi's own testimony precisely because the court considered him too literal-minded to embellish anything. He sits with it for the better part of a year, unable to write the sentence the old man said nothing at all without reaching for some explanation of what the silence meant. He crosses the explanation out four times and leaves it in on the fifth — which is why the version of Case 0157 that circulated for two centuries afterward is considerably more articulate about the Observer's intentions than the Observer himself ever once troubled to be. The scribe is not forgotten. He is simply not meant to be seen.","i":"images/characters/amitai-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The entrance is found above Har-Peleg","u":"records/the-entrance-is-found.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","x":"A shepherd's family and a party of lantern-lit delvers approach a carved stone doorway set into a mountainside The entrance is found above Har-Peleg.","i":"images/scenes/the-entrance-is-found-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The founding five, descending","u":"records/the-founding-five-descend.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","x":"Five torch-lit delvers walk single file along a narrow stone ledge deep within a vast underground structure The founding five, descending.","i":"images/scenes/the-founding-five-descend-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The founding five meet the Catacomb's first guardian","u":"records/the-first-guardian.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","x":"Five delvers fight a skeletal guardian wielding a sword and pickaxe in a narrow burial corridor The founding five meet the Catacomb's first guardian.","i":"images/scenes/the-first-guardian-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Sentinel of Dust ends Doron's five levels","u":"records/the-sentinel-and-doron.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Sentinel of Dust, Doron","x":"A guardian wrapped head to foot in grave-linens fights two delvers at once with twin blades, both bleeding heavily The Sentinel of Dust ends Doron's five levels.","i":"images/scenes/the-sentinel-and-doron-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Widow of the Tenth Hall, unveiled","u":"records/the-widow-of-the-tenth-hall-i.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"The Widow of the Tenth Hall","x":"A dark-robed, veiled guardian wielding a long staff faces several delvers amid a swirling pale mist in a grand hall The Widow of the Tenth Hall, unveiled.","i":"images/scenes/the-widow-of-the-tenth-hall-i-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Widow's chest, and the first Draught","u":"records/the-widow-of-the-tenth-hall-ii.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","x":"A delver lunges at a veiled, staff-wielding guardian beside a chest overflowing with gold The Widow's chest, and the first Draught.","i":"images/scenes/the-widow-of-the-tenth-hall-ii-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Ember Judge, doing what it always does","u":"records/the-ember-judge-in-battle.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"The Ember Judge","x":"A tall armored guardian with flame-venting gauntlets and a spiked crown fights delvers wielding a bow and sword amid fire The Ember Judge, doing what it always does.","i":"images/scenes/the-ember-judge-in-battle-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The grace count runs out","u":"records/the-grace-count-runs-out.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","x":"Two rival delving parties scramble for a treasure chest as fire vents ignite around them beneath a defeated armored guardian The grace count runs out.","i":"images/scenes/the-grace-count-runs-out-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Vartaz, and the floor that didn't know him","u":"records/vartaz-and-the-floor.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Vartaz","x":"A young man's hand rests in rising flame as he looks back, dazed, while others around him recoil Vartaz, and the floor that didn't know him.","i":"images/scenes/vartaz-and-the-floor-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Torvash breaks; Doreth watches","u":"records/torvash-and-doreth.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Torvash, Doreth","x":"An old king in fur-trimmed robes kneels in grief on a shrine floor while a red-robed hierarch looks on Torvash breaks; Doreth watches.","i":"images/scenes/torvash-and-doreth-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Nairi begins the private ledger","u":"records/nairis-ledger.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Nairi","x":"A dark-haired woman writes by candlelight at a desk piled with books while a city burns in the distance outside her window Nairi begins the private ledger.","i":"images/scenes/nairis-ledger-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Elad and Hovan, at the river","u":"records/the-river-crossing.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Elad, Hovan","x":"A young militiaman recoils in horror at a riverbank as another young man collapses in the water nearby, under fire Elad and Hovan, at the river.","i":"images/scenes/the-river-crossing-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Oren reads the wall","u":"records/oren-reads-the-wall.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"Oren","x":"A man kneels and presses a hand to a massive stone wall while officials and workers look on amid rebuilding Oren reads the wall.","i":"images/scenes/oren-reads-the-wall-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The siege of Har-Peleg","u":"records/the-siege-of-har-peleg.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","x":"A vast siege scene: a walled city under attack by siege towers and thousands of troops, fire and smoke rising The siege of Har-Peleg.","i":"images/scenes/the-siege-of-har-peleg-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The White Draught, rationed","u":"records/the-draught-rationed.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"The White Draught","x":"A man holds up a small glass vial in a war-time hall lined with the wounded, a ledger of names open before him The White Draught, rationed.","i":"images/scenes/the-draught-rationed-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Magistrate of Empty Chairs holds court","u":"records/the-magistrate-holds-court.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"The Magistrate of Empty Chairs","x":"An enthroned, masked guardian construct wielding a massive gavel faces a party of delvers before rows of silent robed figures The Magistrate of Empty Chairs holds court.","i":"images/scenes/the-magistrate-holds-court-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Nursemaid falls","u":"records/the-weaver-falls.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"The Nursemaid","x":"A towering, soft-faced guardian in tattered pale wrappings, with long white hair and elongated limbs, is run through with a sword by a delver The Nursemaid falls.","i":"images/scenes/the-weaver-falls-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Hundred-Handed Reckoner, and the hoard","u":"records/the-reckoner-and-the-hoard.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"The Hundred-Handed Reckoner","x":"A many-armed, faceless construct wielding scales and blades stands before a vast golden hoard holding a single white vial The Hundred-Handed Reckoner, and the hoard.","i":"images/scenes/the-reckoner-and-the-hoard-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Observer hands Nairi the armor","u":"records/the-observer-and-the-armor.html","m":"Case 0157 · The Stolen Prince War","d":"The Observer, Nairi","x":"An old man at a book-lined desk shows a dark-robed woman a piece of scorched, recovered armor The Observer hands Nairi the armor.","i":"images/scenes/the-observer-and-the-armor-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Iron Stiletto War","u":"books/4417.html","m":"Case 4417 · Published","d":"A pair of chrome heels, and the war two crowns paid for them.","x":"Case 4417 Epic Fantasy · Political Intrigue · War & Military Fiction Vane the Wanderer arrives at the court of Solis disguised as a penniless hermit and leaves behind a single gift: a pair of chrome stiletto heels that never scuff, never break, and answer to no explanation anyone can find. Queen Aurelia puts them on. Four inches taller and, for the first time in longer than she can say, sure of herself, she wears them into the Great Concord — and Duchess Beatrice of Ironhold, humiliated in front of both crowns, refuses to let it go. What begins as a wounded vanity hardens, within a season, into doctrine: the Order of the Pale Cloth declares the heels heretical, tariffs curdle into border incidents, and both realms call their banners. House Vell commits to Solis in secret, Baron Rathmore backs Ironhold, and Baron Corvin — ostensibly neutral, actually for sale — quietly decides which side he can profit from losing. By the time the siege reaches Solis's walls, the war has almost nothing left to do with shoes. The Iron Stiletto War is told partly through the private letters of a chancellor who outlives everyone he served and ends up ruling both crowns for eleven years afterward — still turning over, on the last page, whether the heels were ever really about vanity at all, or whether something older and far more patient had simply been watching to see which crown would break first."},{"k":"s","t":"Osric","u":"characters/osric.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Steward of House Ironhold · served three generations of dukes","x":"Keeps Ironhold's accounts for three dukes running, and outlasts the one this war is about. Steward of House Ironhold, composed and deliberate after decades in service to the family. He manages the duchy's affairs with quiet precision, is the one who carries news of Duke Aldous's death to Duchess Beatrice, and corresponds privately with Chancellor Wren through the years the two crowns spend under one regency afterward. When Beatrice's grief curdles into self-blame, he's steady enough to push back on it. A house is not built on stone, but on accounts kept and promises remembered.","i":"images/characters/osric-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Wren","u":"characters/wren.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Chancellor of Solis · served three monarchs · age unknown","x":"Writes the laws, edits the letters, and decides — quietly — who doesn't survive the reckoning. Chancellor of Solis through three monarchs' reigns, calm even while committing murder. He keeps a private ledger of names, debts, and verdicts no one else is permitted to read, and when Baron Corvin's scheming is finally laid bare, Wren answers it with a cup of custom poisoned wine rather than a public trial. He ends the war as Head of the Regency Council, ruling both crowns for eleven years afterward — and privately, in letters he can't quite bring himself to burn, wondering whether the heels were ever really about vanity at all. Power is not seized with a sword, but written in ink no one dares to read.","i":"images/characters/wren-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ophelia","u":"characters/ophelia.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Lady of the Queen's Household in Solis · Aurelia's messenger","x":"Carries Aurelia's words exactly as spoken — including the refusal that starts a war. A lady of Queen Aurelia's household, trusted to carry royal decrees with total neutrality and no editorializing of her own. Her access to the palace's inner workings makes her a quiet target for anyone hoping to learn what the Queen is actually thinking — but it's her composure, not her opinions, that the role depends on, especially the day she's sent to deliver Aurelia's refusal to Duchess Beatrice in person. I carry Her Majesty's words as they are spoken—no more, no less.","i":"images/characters/ophelia-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Iset","u":"characters/iset.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Former Lady's Maid to the Queen · retained by Beatrice as a “Woman of Business”","x":"Runs Beatrice's spy network of maids, clerks, and a cobbler — and traces a bribe straight back to Corvin. Once lady's maid to the Queen, now retained by Duchess Beatrice in the more useful role of spymaster in all but title. She runs an undercover network of scullery maids, clerks, and a cobbler through Ironhold and Solis alike, and it's her people who notice a boot missing its maker's mark — a thread that, followed far enough through a ledger of quiet payments, leads straight to Baron Corvin's bribes. Information is the sharpest blade. I never draw mine; I only make certain others hand theirs to me.","i":"images/characters/iset-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Fenric","u":"characters/fenric.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Lord of the Western Passes of Ironhold · husband to Isolde","x":"Argues for restraint through every council that stops listening to him. Lord of Ironhold's Western Passes, married to Isolde, and about as close to a voice for peace as either crown has. He speaks rarely, listens always, and prefers function to the display the rest of the council trades in — standing apart during the war councils he can't stop from voting the way they vote. Restraint is not weakness. It is the surest path to lasting strength.","i":"images/characters/fenric-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Beatrice","u":"characters/beatrice.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Duchess of Ironhold, later Duchess-Regnant · wife to Aldous","x":"Loses a war of etiquette over a pair of borrowed heels, then inherits a real one. Duchess of Ironhold, composed and exacting, with a bearing lesser houses copy down to the angle of her hair. Publicly humiliated by Queen Aurelia's chrome heels at the Great Concord and then refused an apology for it, she spends the following months as the war's original grievance — and, after Aldous's death in the Great Hall, becomes Duchess-Regnant in earnest, carrying both the title and the blame for how it was won. Beauty is a standard. Disrespected standards invite consequences.","i":"images/characters/beatrice-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Corvin","u":"characters/corvin.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Baron of the Eastern Marches","x":"Plays both crowns against each other, and nearly walks away with both of them. Baron of the Eastern Marches, patient and outwardly neutral while working every side of the war he helps prolong. He bribes a cobbler, orchestrates the Church's heresy decree against the heels, and leans on Isolde and Ser Halric for intelligence and delay in equal measure — stalling the relief force that might have ended the siege early. By the time Chancellor Wren quietly proves what he's been doing, Corvin is within reach of both crowns at once. He doesn't survive Wren noticing. Loyalty is a currency, and I have no need to spend what I can so easily counterfeit.","i":"images/characters/corvin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Aldous","u":"characters/aldous.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Duke of Ironhold · 56 years old · husband to Beatrice","x":"Walks into the Great Hall unarmed, offering peace, and doesn't walk out. Duke of Ironhold, slowed by gout and by a war he's counseled against from the start. Pressed toward escalation by his wife Beatrice and his own council, he goes into the war's final confrontation in the Great Hall unarmed and offering terms — and is killed there, struck down by the same heels that started the whole conflict. Peace... It seems I was always just a slower man in a faster world.","i":"images/characters/aldous-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Vane","u":"characters/vane.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Envoy/Observer, Case 4,417 · “the Wanderer” · deployed disguised as a hermit","x":"Delivers an indestructible pair of chrome heels to a queen, then stays to watch what it costs her. A field agent for the Cultivators, deployed to Solis disguised as a wandering hermit. He delivers the chrome stiletto heels at the center of the whole case as a single, unexplained gift, then lingers at the edges of both courts — gathering data on both sides of the war he sets in motion, and logging all of it, without candlelight, in Case Study 4,417. Offered at least one real chance to intervene before the worst of it, he declines to take it. When the study closes, the heels vanish with him, on schedule, and he simply moves on. I wander. I witness. I record. I depart. Such is the burden of knowing.","i":"images/characters/vane-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Isolde","u":"characters/isolde.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Lady of Ironhold · wife to Fenric · sister to the Lord of House Vell","x":"A gift of rare dye from Corvin costs Ironhold more than she ever means it to. Lady of Ironhold, married to Fenric and sister to the Lord of House Vell — a connection that matters more to the war than she first realizes. A gift of rare eastern dye from Baron Corvin opens a friendly correspondence that, without her quite meaning it to, hands him military intelligence and leverage over her own family's relief force. She works out what's actually happening before the war ends, and has to decide, with House Vell's help hanging on it, what to do about him. A whisper shared in trust will travel further than any rider.","i":"images/characters/isolde-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ballard","u":"characters/ballard.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Ser Ballard · Commander of Solis's Standing Army","x":"Calculates the odds and the cost of every decision Aurelia makes for the siege. Commander of Solis's standing army, calm and analytical where Solis's court is anything but. He speaks rarely and listens always, treating the battlefield as a problem of numbers and lives rather than glory — loyal to the crown, and driven by duty rather than by any particular love of Queen Aurelia's cause.","i":"images/characters/ballard-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ambrose","u":"characters/ambrose.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"High Cleric of the Order of the Pale Cloth","x":"Declares the chrome heels a blasphemy, and turns a scandal into a holy war. High Cleric of the Order of the Pale Cloth, austere and utterly convinced of his own conviction. He declares Queen Aurelia's chrome heels heretical — chrome, in his own words, “seduces the flesh” — and prepares his congregation for what he calls a war of purification, giving the conflict a righteousness it otherwise wouldn't have had at home. Chrome seduces the flesh with false promise. We shall burn the blasphemy and return to the Light.","i":"images/characters/ambrose-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Aurelia","u":"characters/aurelia.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Queen of Solis · reigning eleven years","x":"Four borrowed inches of height end a duke's life and her own reign in the same afternoon. Queen of Solis, and the first person the chrome heels are ever placed on. They add four unmistakable inches, catch and distort every reflection near them, and hide a needle-sharp spike beneath each sole — and over the following months they turn, for her, from a private vanity into something closer to a need. She wears them into the Great Concord and refuses Beatrice's later ultimatum outright; by the time she kills Duke Aldous with one of them in the Great Hall, the war they started has already outgrown her ability to end it. She's deposed shortly after, and lives out what's left of her reign “retired” under guard. Height is earned. Power is worn.","i":"images/characters/aurelia-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Vane delivers the heels","u":"records/vane-delivers-the-heels.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Vane","x":"The hermit Vane kneels over an open case holding a pair of chrome stiletto heels in a dim cabin Vane delivers the heels.","i":"images/scenes/vane-delivers-the-heels-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Vane, alone with a single candle","u":"records/vane-by-candlelight.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Vane","x":"The hermit Vane, long-haired and bearded, rests his chin on his fist and studies the flame of a single candle in a dark room Vane, alone with a single candle.","i":"images/scenes/vane-by-candlelight-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The chrome heels themselves","u":"records/the-chrome-heels.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","x":"A pair of mirror-polished chrome stiletto heels resting on a stone dais, draped in a red and gold cloth The chrome heels themselves.","i":"images/scenes/the-chrome-heels-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Aurelia at the Great Concord, her heels throwing light across the hall","u":"records/aurelia-at-the-great-concord.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Aurelia","x":"Queen Aurelia, crowned and wrapped in a fur-trimmed cloak, stands in mirror-bright chrome heels on a polished hall floor as crowned and robed men bow and gaze up at her beneath tall arched windows Aurelia at the Great Concord, her heels throwing light across the hall.","i":"images/scenes/aurelia-at-the-great-concord-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Heads lower as Aurelia crosses the hall","u":"records/aurelia-crosses-the-hall.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Aurelia","x":"Aurelia strides across a mirror-polished floor in chrome heels, her ermine cape trailing, while rows of gray-haired men kneel with bowed heads beneath red banners and a tall arched window Heads lower as Aurelia crosses the hall.","i":"images/scenes/aurelia-crosses-the-hall-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Aurelia enthroned, a supplicant bowed over her heel","u":"records/aurelia-on-the-throne.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Aurelia","x":"Queen Aurelia lounges on a golden throne in a red gown and ermine mantle, one chrome heel extended to a kneeling, fair-haired man in a hooded robe who bows over it while rows of dark-robed men look on Aurelia enthroned, a supplicant bowed over her heel.","i":"images/scenes/aurelia-on-the-throne-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Corvin presents Isolde with his gift of rare dye","u":"records/corvin-brings-the-dye.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Corvin, Isolde","x":"Baron Corvin stands with an open hand beside a chest heaped with purple, blue and red silks while Isolde, seated at a sunlit window, works a length of pale cloth in her hands Corvin presents Isolde with his gift of rare dye.","i":"images/scenes/corvin-brings-the-dye-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Corvin, drawing intelligence from Isolde","u":"records/corvin-and-isolde.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Corvin, Isolde","x":"Baron Corvin sits close with Isolde by a fireplace as she shares a letter with him Corvin, drawing intelligence from Isolde.","i":"images/scenes/corvin-and-isolde-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Ophelia delivers the Queen's refusal to Beatrice","u":"records/ophelia-delivers-the-refusal.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Ophelia, Beatrice","x":"Ophelia presents a sealed scroll to a seated Duchess Beatrice beneath a Solis banner Ophelia delivers the Queen's refusal to Beatrice.","i":"images/scenes/ophelia-delivers-the-refusal-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Ophelia glances back along a long corridor","u":"records/ophelia-in-the-corridor.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Ophelia","x":"Ophelia, in a white and gold gown and pale cloak, glances back over her shoulder along a candlelit Gothic corridor as a dark-haired man in a black coat watches from the foreground Ophelia glances back along a long corridor.","i":"images/scenes/ophelia-in-the-corridor-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Beatrice in audience with the imprisoned Aurelia","u":"records/beatrice-visits-aurelia.html","m":"Case 4417 · The Iron Stiletto War","d":"Beatrice, Aurelia","x":"Two panels in a stone cell: above, Beatrice in a dark cloak sits outside the bars facing the deposed Aurelia, with the chrome heels on the floor between them; below, Aurelia weeps alone with her face in her hands Beatrice in audience with the imprisoned Aurelia.","i":"images/scenes/beatrice-visits-aurelia-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","u":"books/4420.html","m":"Case 4420 · Published","d":"A flask of broth, one frightened knight, and the war two ducal houses paid for.","x":"Case 4420 Fiction / Fantasy / Epic House Skarth has kept one steel flask far longer than anyone in it has thought to ask where it came from. It never lets its broth go cold; the family's oldest women say it came down from the sacred peak as a gift from the dragon who keeps court there; and its steward, Giles, has scrubbed it with lye soap every winter for forty years without once asking how it works. When Duke Maros, humiliated at his own table by a rival's gift of a stag's head, drags a royal hunt two hundred miles into the northern tundra to save face, Baron Kaelen of House Skarth accepts the invitation before the messenger has finished reciting it. Guest-right is the whole of his faith: a guest fed at his table is simply fed. On the eleventh night, with the King gone silent and breaking frozen venison against a rock, Kaelen finally unscrews the stopper. The broth hits air some hundred and twenty degrees colder than it is and unrolls into a white cloud — and Sir Corvus of the royal bodyguard, still haunted by a spore-choked cave he survived eleven months earlier, sees only the thing that almost killed him. He kills Kaelen before the cup reaches the King. The Crown's sealed account of the death is true in every clause and never mentions broth; House Skarth reads it as a throne signing its name beneath a murder. Kaelen's twin sister Yvaine cuts her hair, puts on his ancestral mail, climbs the mountain for a blessing she doesn't quite believe in, and leads the northern clans south, with the flask — cased in silver now, and renamed the Vessel of the Betrayed Host — carried at the front of their war-bands. Duke Vane, who wants the relic less for what it can do than for the Regency it could buy him, sells a beaten Maros his rescue at ruinous terms, hires the thief Soren to lift the vessel from the coalition's war-altar, and discovers too late what he has actually been fighting over. The war that follows is a slaughter no southern chronicle will ever name honestly; the scribes settle on the Great Winter Pestilence. The Vessel of the Betrayed Host closes on a Cultivator's archival log — Case Study 4,420: one flask, four centuries idle, and the most efficient war its Observer has recorded in eleven centuries."},{"k":"s","t":"Kaelen","u":"characters/kaelen.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Baron of House Skarth · 25 years old · twin brother to Yvaine","x":"Rides two hundred miles to feed a king, and dies holding both halves of the gift. Baron of House Skarth and Yvaine's twin, and the book's clearest case of faith with no category for suspicion. Guest-right, in House Skarth's law, means a guest fed at your table is simply fed — no debt, no price — and he treats a royal hunting party exactly that way: accepting Duke Maros's invitation before the messenger has finished reciting it, taking Duke Vane's offer to pay for provisions as the nearest thing to a genuine offense anyone has given him since leaving home, and quietly working a spare pair of dry socks free from his own baggage for a conscript whose boots have failed. He carries the household's steel flask two hundred miles north and holds it in reserve for the one night someone's need outruns the fires. When he finally unscrews the stopper for the King, Sir Corvus kills him where he stands — flask in one hand, stopper in the other, before the cup has reached the King. His name outlives every other name attached to the war. Sos-vahlok blót-krah; mey sos krongrah — blood guards the hearth; sacrifice warms the frost.","i":"images/characters/kaelen-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Yvaine","u":"characters/yvaine.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Lady of House Skarth · 25 years old · twin sister to Kaelen","x":"Cleans her brother's collar herself, cuts her hair with a hunting knife, and marches south. Lady of House Skarth and Kaelen's twin — the guarded, suspicious half of a pair with one face between them. She argues against the hunt from the day the invitation arrives, and loses. After his death she cleans the dried broth from his fur collar herself, swears the Blood-Oath of the Vacant Hearth alone in the chapel — cutting her hair with a hunting knife and pulling on his too-large ancestral mail — and climbs Mount Skahr to ask its keeper for a blessing she doesn't quite believe in. She sends Duke Vane's gold and marriage contract back unanswered, spares the frightened border holdfast of Cindale, and ends the war in the hall of Duke Maros's captured keep, where she finds Vane himself. She does not ask his name; she has known it for a year. Sos-vahlok blót-krah; mey sos krongrah — blood guards the hearth; sacrifice warms the frost.","i":"images/characters/yvaine-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Duke Maros","u":"characters/maros.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Duke of the Northern March · Lord of Karhold Keep · 45 years old","x":"Answers a stag's head with a royal hunt, and pays with his feet, his house, and his life. Duke of the Northern March, and the man whose wounded pride sends a royal hunt into the cold. Humiliated at his own table when Duke Vane presents him with a summer-killed stag's head, he proposes a hunt in the deep northern winter to answer it — and it costs him every toe on both feet by the fourth week at the Broken Glacier. After the flask kills Kaelen he buys Vane's help on terms no one in his position could afford. His last honest act is a letter to the King warning him of Vane and begging that his wife and four-year-old son be kept out of Vane's reach; Vane's men take it from the courier at the pickets and burn it. Maros is arrested for treason and dies on the road south, in what Vane's own report calls an escape attempt met with necessary force. The north is not won by pride or oath. It is won by hunger, endured longer than the foe can bear.","i":"images/characters/maros-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Duke Vane","u":"characters/vane-4420.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Duke of the Southern March · Lord of Vane Keep","x":"Sends a stag's head to a rival and a thief to a mountain, and prices everything in between. Duke of the Southern March: patient, gracious, and never once unpriced. It is Vane who wounds Duke Maros with a summer-killed stag's head presented as a gift at his own table, who joins the royal hunt to watch what follows, and who — when the flask kills Kaelen — sees not a tragedy but a relic, and through it a Regency. He sells a desperate Maros his rescue at ruinous terms, hires Soren to steal the vessel, has Maros arrested when he becomes a liability, and, when he finally pries the silver off himself in the small hours before his last council, finds only a dented steel flask that smells of old soup — and tells no one. Yvaine finds him in the hall of Maros's captured keep; his head is left on a pike before the drawbridge. (Not the same man as Vane the Wanderer of The Iron Stiletto War.) No guest leaves my table unfed. No oath leaves my memory. No insult leaves my blade unblooded. That is the law of my house.","i":"images/characters/vane-4420-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Corvus","u":"characters/corvus.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Sir Corvus · knight of the King's own bodyguard","x":"A royal bodyguard undone by a cloud of steam that looks, for one half-second, like spores. Knight of the King's own bodyguard — and, since a cave outside a border keep eleven months earlier, a man who can no longer fully separate the present from the year afterward, when he could not lift a spoon to his mouth without spilling the broth. Grey spore-dust, someone's breathing stopping beside him in the dark: by the eleventh night of the hunt he has not slept in four nights, and when Kaelen unscrews the flask and the broth unrolls into a white cloud, his whole body turns toward the sound of metal on metal before his mind is consulted. He kills Kaelen before the cup reaches the King. He serves another six years in the royal bodyguard, competently and without complaint, then lives eleven more alone on a small holding in the King's gift — a man who did the arithmetic more times than anyone should be asked to, and never once made it come out even. The spores do not sleep. They wait. Mist, rot, dark — I have known their breath. I will not breathe it again.","i":"images/characters/corvus-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Observer 814","u":"characters/observer-4420.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Envoy of the Cultivators · “the Dragon-Speaker” · Case Study 4,420","x":"Hands a family a steel flask, then waits four centuries on a mountain to see what they make of it. A field agent of the Cultivators, who came down onto Mount Skahr in a black, wingless vessel some four centuries before the war and was folded by the clans, within a generation, into the Dragon-Speaker, keeper of the mountain — a name he never corrects. When an ancestor of House Skarth climbs up to beg a token of divine favor, Observer 814 passes a double-walled steel flask across the gap between his instruments and the man's frostbitten hands with roughly the ceremony a lord might spend tossing a coin to a groom, and goes back to watching. Then he waits, at compound interest, for whichever descendant is careless enough to call the loan due. Four centuries on, Yvaine climbs to his ice cavern for a blessing on the war she has already decided to fight, and comes down the mountain certain she carries one he never gave her. He files the case as the most efficient the Envoy has recorded in eleven centuries of comparable seedings — and remains assigned to it. I do not come to change. I come to witness. If change is already written, I will wait.","i":"images/characters/observer-4420-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Soren","u":"characters/soren.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Shadow thief · 38 years old · of Byzantium","x":"Steals the war's holiest relic for a duke, opens it in a hunter's cabin, and finds it smells of soup. A thief claimed by no guild, who works for men rich enough that hiring him is safer than wondering who else might. Duke Vane pays him more than he has ever been offered for an object he hasn't seen. Soren works his way into the coalition's country through the worst blizzard of the winter, lifts the vessel from the war-altar in the deepest hour of the worst night, when the sentries have been pulled back to shelter, opens its lock with a length of wire that has never once dulled or snapped, and is three miles gone before anyone thinks to check. Two days south, in a hunter's cabin, he pries off the silver and finds an empty, dented steel flask smelling of long-dried soup. He delivers it to Vane without a word and leaves Sumne within the month — the last man alive who ever looked beneath the silver, and a man who never says so. I don't steal for greed. I steal so that others won't have to bleed.","i":"images/characters/soren-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Yvaine and Kaelen, before the hunt","u":"records/the-twins-laughing.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Yvaine, Kaelen","x":"Yvaine, with long white hair, and Kaelen, in furs and leather with pendants at his throat, laughing together with snowy mountains behind them Yvaine and Kaelen, before the hunt.","i":"images/scenes/the-twins-laughing-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Kaelen, and the broth that never reached the King","u":"records/kaelen-and-the-spilled-broth.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Kaelen","x":"Kaelen lies in the snow with his eyes closed beside an overturned steel cup, steaming brown broth spreading across the ice Kaelen, and the broth that never reached the King.","i":"images/scenes/kaelen-and-the-spilled-broth-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Observer 814 on the frozen summit of Mount Skahr, receiving a climber from House Skarth","u":"records/the-dragon-speaker-on-the-summit.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Observer 814","x":"A white-haired figure in a snow-crusted cloak stands before Observer 814, who sits on a tall iron throne on a storm-swept peak with one hand held out Observer 814 on the frozen summit of Mount Skahr, receiving a climber from House Skarth.","i":"images/scenes/the-dragon-speaker-on-the-summit-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Yvaine cuts her hair and puts on her brother's mail","u":"records/yvaine-cuts-her-hair.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Yvaine","x":"Yvaine in chain mail holds a knife to her long white hair, cutting it off in a stone room beside a frosted window Yvaine cuts her hair and puts on her brother's mail.","i":"images/scenes/yvaine-cuts-her-hair-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Yvaine, sword before her face — the vow made, the war ahead","u":"records/yvaine-after-the-oath.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Yvaine","x":"Yvaine, her white hair now short and blood on her face and gloves, holds a sword upright before her eyes Yvaine, sword before her face — the vow made, the war ahead.","i":"images/scenes/yvaine-after-the-oath-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Yvaine and Observer 814 in the ice cavern","u":"records/yvaine-and-the-observer.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Yvaine, Observer 814","x":"Yvaine, in a fur-collared cloak, faces Observer 814 across a frozen cavern floor beneath walls of hanging ice Yvaine and Observer 814 in the ice cavern.","i":"images/scenes/yvaine-and-the-observer-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Yvaine, looking out over the frozen plain","u":"records/yvaine-above-the-frozen-plain.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Yvaine","x":"Yvaine, seen from behind in a fur cloak, looks out from a snowy ridge at two distant keeps across a frozen plain under an orange sky Yvaine, looking out over the frozen plain.","i":"images/scenes/yvaine-above-the-frozen-plain-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"A column of riders under a black banner approaches a mountain keep","u":"records/riders-toward-the-keep.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","x":"A column of riders under a black banner with a white beast's head and red streamers follows a snowy road toward a dark mountain keep A column of riders under a black banner approaches a mountain keep.","i":"images/scenes/riders-toward-the-keep-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Broken Glacier","u":"records/the-broken-glacier.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","x":"A column of dark-cloaked figures winds between towering walls of blue-white ice in a narrow glacier defile The Broken Glacier.","i":"images/scenes/the-broken-glacier-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Soren, in a hunter's cabin two days south, with the vessel he was paid to steal","u":"records/soren-with-the-flask.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Soren","x":"Soren, in white snow gear with a crossbow across his lap, sits by a hearth in a wooden cabin holding a steel flask Soren, in a hunter's cabin two days south, with the vessel he was paid to steal.","i":"images/scenes/soren-with-the-flask-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Yvaine in the southern campaign","u":"records/yvaine-in-the-south.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Yvaine","x":"Yvaine, in dark plate with a fur collar and blood on her cheek, holds a sword low amid armed men beneath a black banner with a gold eagle, a castle on the skyline behind her Yvaine in the southern campaign.","i":"images/scenes/yvaine-in-the-south-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Yvaine and Duke Vane, blades crossed in the hall of Maros's captured keep","u":"records/yvaine-and-vane-cross-blades.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Yvaine, Duke Vane, Duke Maros","x":"Yvaine and Duke Vane cross swords over a banquet table in a candlelit hall while two servants cower beneath it Yvaine and Duke Vane, blades crossed in the hall of Maros's captured keep.","i":"images/scenes/yvaine-and-vane-cross-blades-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Before the drawbridge of Duke Maros's keep: the iron pike, and the gold no one took","u":"records/the-pike-before-the-drawbridge.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Duke Maros","x":"A black iron pike stands upright in frozen ground before a ruined gothic gatehouse hung with red banners, gold coins scattered across the snowy stones at sunset Before the drawbridge of Duke Maros's keep: the iron pike, and the gold no one took.","i":"images/scenes/the-pike-before-the-drawbridge-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The herald finds the one coin left balanced beneath Duke Vane's eyes","u":"records/the-herald-and-the-coin.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","d":"Duke Vane","x":"A black-gloved hand in a red cuff embroidered with a gold crown and eagle reaches for a single gold coin lying on snowy flagstones The herald finds the one coin left balanced beneath Duke Vane's eyes.","i":"images/scenes/the-herald-and-the-coin-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The black-fletched arrow the northern clans use to mark their dead along the ice line","u":"records/the-black-fletched-arrow.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","x":"A black-fletched arrow lies across a field of snow and dark stone The black-fletched arrow the northern clans use to mark their dead along the ice line.","i":"images/scenes/the-black-fletched-arrow-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The watchtowers a later king raised along the northern frontier","u":"records/the-frozen-wall.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","x":"A chain of grey stone watchtowers stretches away across a frozen sea, the nearest with a timber lookout and a lit window The watchtowers a later king raised along the northern frontier.","i":"images/scenes/the-frozen-wall-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"What the scribes wrote: first a timber-tariff dispute, then “the Great Winter Pestilence.”","u":"records/the-great-winter-pestilence.html","m":"Case 4420 · The Vessel of the Betrayed Host","x":"A hand with a quill writes 'The Great Winter Pestilence' across the top of a parchment by candlelight, books and an inkwell behind it What the scribes wrote: first a timber-tariff dispute, then “the Great Winter Pestilence.”","i":"images/scenes/the-great-winter-pestilence-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","u":"books/4555.html","m":"Case 4555 · Published","d":"A traffic cone, a reluctant duke, and forty-one years spent trying to give a crown back.","x":"Case 4555 Epic Fantasy · Satire · Political Intrigue When King Aethelgard the Resplendent dies without an heir, Illumaria's whole theology of visible grace turns the search for his successor into a lit competition: three rival claimants — Archduke Ignis, Duchess Astraea, and Duke Cassian — arrive at the Grand Basilica of Sol-Invictus each wearing a device built to make them glow. Duke Blakk of Mud-Reach, the poorest and least ambitious name on the guest-roll, comes only to vote for whoever won't raise the timber tax — and three nights before his household guard crosses a nameless drainage ditch, an Anchorite of the Drowned Road, working for the Cultivator Envoy Corps' Observer 902, plants three identical objects in the mud as standard redundancy. Blakk's horse finds the first. He sets the resulting hollow orange cone on his own head as a joke, forgets to take it off, and is still wearing it in the Basilica's last row when High Pontiff Sarel's ceremonial beam of concentrated light finds it — and its retroreflective bands throw the beam straight back down the line it arrived on, cracking a three-century-old lens and blinding half the congregation with what looks, to three thousand kneeling monks, like the most convincing grace the Decree of Luminescence has ever produced. Blakk spends the next forty-one years trying to give the throne back. Nobody believes him: not the three houses whose war at the Standing at Illmere Ford turns his identical, unread letters of surrender into a legendary act of strategic mercy, and not Canon Voss, the one man who does the geometry correctly and spends four decades, and one exile, trying and failing to prove it. What follows is less a triumph than an audit: a marriage settled by one underlined line in a private ledger, a Law of Succession that costs Blakk the very thing forty years of reluctant grace had bought him, and a Rite of Reconsecration, delayed until his deathbed, that finally puts the Vessel to the one test it was never built to survive. The Vessel of Unmediated Grace closes on a Cultivator's own archival log — Case Study 4,555: one 0.2-pound cone, a kingdom that never gets to read its own founding accident correctly, and an Observer already three ditches away before the file is closed."},{"k":"s","t":"Blakk","u":"characters/blakk.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Duke of Mud-Reach · later High Sovereign of Illumaria · reigned 41 years","x":"Falls off his horse into a ditch, puts a traffic cone on his head as a joke, and spends four decades trying to give the crown back. Duke of Mud-Reach, governing nine hundred square miles of drained marsh, standing timber, and tenant debt so ordinary it barely required collecting. His own name means, in his grandmother's border dialect, simply black — no chronicler ever settled whether it referred to his hair, his moods, or the bog-silt his family's boots never fully shed. He comes to the Great Judgment wanting nothing but to vote for whoever won't raise the timber tax and be home before spring flooding closes the border road; three days out, his horse loses its footing at a drainage ditch, and he sets the orange cone it throws him toward on his own head as a joke for his six guardsmen. He spends the next forty-one years trying to give the resulting crown back. He refuses the Rite of Reconsecration rather than watch it melt in a Basilica flame, writes identical, unbelieved letters of surrender to Ignis, Astraea, and Cassian before the Standing at Illmere Ford anyway, and marries Astraea two years after the war on terms she sets, not he. He fights his own nobility for a Law of Succession that will spare his cousin Petrin's heirs the same accident, and confesses everything, in his last clear hour, to the three people — Harn, Petrin, and Prisca — he trusts to decide what a kingdom does not need to carry. I only wished to sit in the back.","i":"images/characters/blakk-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Astraea","u":"characters/astraea.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Duchess, House Astraea · later Queen of Illumaria","x":"Musters an army over a letter she doesn't quite believe, and spends the rest of her life auditing what that cost her. Duchess of House Astraea, and the second claimant to stand the Great Judgment's trial: a six-foot Chandelier Wheel of a hundred and twenty tallow candles, strapped above her shoulders, that leaves the back of her neck blistered in a pattern she calls, to exactly one person, a second crown worn beneath the first. She musters her border levies less for the grazing land her council cites than because she can no longer bear being pitied, and her cavalry meets Ignis's own raiding column at the Standing at Illmere Ford on her marshal Coel's counsel, not her own conviction — a mistake her body-servant Prisca hears her admit, alone, the same night: he offered, and I refused. She spends a month testing Blakk's goodness for a catch it never turns out to have, then two more years proving in public, through unglamorous service on his border commission, that her change of heart isn't a fresh maneuver for the throne. She marries him the following spring, becomes the sharper half of what the court quietly calls the Evening Ledger, and throws herself between him and Canon Voss's blade at Sarn's Crossing, taking a wound that troubles her for the rest of her life. She dies in the reign's thirty-sixth year, having underlined one line in her own private ledger more times than she ever admitted. He offered. I refused.","i":"images/characters/astraea-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Cassian","u":"characters/cassian.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Duke Cassian, House Cassian · Noble Claimant to the throne of Illumaria","x":"Holds his chin unnaturally high to keep his own crown from breaking his neck, then does the same careful arithmetic on an entire war. Duke Cassian comes last and lightest to the Great Judgment: a five-foot Burnished Steel Sunburst of razor blades on a spine no thicker than a finger, engineered with real rigor for reflection and none at all for a grown man's neck, so that he holds his chin unnaturally high through the whole ceremony — arithmetic, not vanity, since any deeper bow was likely to snap his own spine for him. He reads Blakk's letter of surrender twice and does nothing, trusting no one but concluding, correctly, that a war against a man who has already surrendered is a war fought for nothing; he holds his own forces two days' march from the Standing at Illmere Ford for the whole engagement and arrives on the fifth day to find no one left worth conquering. He proposes terms, retires within the fortnight, and spends the remaining thirty-one years of his life keeping a private ledger he calls the Cost of Not Winning — tallying, year on year, everything a crown might once have been worth against a column he never lets himself total in full. He turns down a coalition's offer to back his own claim without ever mentioning it to anyone, including Blakk, and dies in his own bed having proven, to his own satisfaction only, that a kingdom which asks nothing of him balances its books better than one he ruled. A kingdom that costs a man nothing to leave alone is a kingdom he has already, whether he meant to or not, chosen to keep.","i":"images/characters/cassian-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ignis","u":"characters/ignis.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Archduke Ignis, House Ignis · Eldest Claimant to the throne of Illumaria","x":"Wears seventy pounds of burning iron to prove his grace is forged rather than given, and loses the throne to a cone anyway. Archduke Ignis takes the Great Judgment's dais first, as eldest claimant and loudest patron of the doctrine that grace must be forged rather than merely inherited: an Anvil Forge Helm of seventy pounds of black iron housing a coal hearth at its crown, pumped by two apprentice boys until the watching monks swear his whole head appears to burn. His own First Counselor, Farris, picks apart Blakk's letter of surrender clause by clause and convinces the war council it is a trap rather than a confession; Ignis strikes first at what his scouts wrongly call an enemy vanguard, and the Standing at Illmere Ford follows within the day. No two surviving accounts agree on whether he fell in the fighting or rode east and kept riding. His nephew Ossian inherits a house reduced, over two decades, from first rank to an increasingly theoretical eighth — and is given back, unasked and in full, the very grazing rights his uncle lost, in the single act of mercy Ossian spends fifty-four years failing to forgive. With fire and iron, I take what is mine.","i":"images/characters/ignis-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Aethelgard","u":"characters/aethelgard.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"King of the High Realm of Illumaria · Deceased","x":"Dies without an heir, and leaves an entire kingdom's theology with nothing left to measure but a stopped heart. King Aethelgard the Resplendent rules Illumaria as the Decree of Luminescence's own proof of concept: radiant, beloved, and, court chroniclers agreed, so charismatic that his own heart was said to burn too brightly for anything as mundane as a legacy. He dies without an heir in three attempted marriages, a fact the court physicians attribute to a stopped heart and the Last Order attributes, with rather more theological confidence, to a soul too incandescent for any womb to house its successor. His death is the vacancy the entire book fills: it sends High Pontiff Sarel looking for the next man or woman who can be proven, publicly, to glow, and puts Ignis, Astraea, and Cassian — and, far down a guest-roll ranked by wealth, Duke Blakk of Mud-Reach — into the same Basilica on the same morning. They said his soul burned so brightly, his heart forgot how to leave a legacy behind.","i":"images/characters/aethelgard-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sarel","u":"characters/sarel.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"High Pontiff of Sol-Invictus","x":"Draws back a curtain to test four rivals for grace, and spends the rest of his life half-blind from what answered. High Pontiff of Sol-Invictus, and the one man in Illumaria whose private eleven years of doubting the Decree of Luminescence leave him its sole living arbiter the moment Aethelgard dies. He presides over the Great Judgment, draws the final baffle from the Sol-Focus Arc himself, and takes the returning beam directly in one eye when Blakk's cone reflects it back along its own line of arrival — cracking a lens no Pontiff after him is ever permitted to have repaired, and leaving him in tears he cannot stop for the rest of that day. He invents the Triple Vessel doctrine once two further cones are recovered from the same ditch, crowns Blakk a second time as High Sovereign, and spends four decades keeping the vessels' true fragility a secret paid for in gold and silence. He talks Petrin out of confessing the fraud immediately after Blakk's death, oversees the crown's final, confirming destruction by open flame, and sets down, in a testament his own successor won't find for twenty years, the one article of faith his doctrine never actually required of him. Let no shadow claim what shadow did not make.","i":"images/characters/sarel-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Harn","u":"characters/harn.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Captain Harn · Captain of the Guard to Duke Blakk","x":"Checks a stranger's cone by torchlight out of plain habit, and spends the next forty years still watching for the same glint. Captain of Duke Blakk's household guard, a broad, unhurried man who operates on the sound principle that anything he can't identify probably isn't worth identifying — until a torch swung near a discarded cone throws back a flare that leaves him seeing green for the better part of an hour. He is the one man Blakk ever tells the truth of his own coronation to (“I only wished to sit in the back”), and the one man who understands, without being told twice, exactly what his duke fears about it. He catches Canon Voss's hired assassin by the wrist a half-second before the blade lands, forty years and by his own count a fourth save into a friendship neither man ever calls that aloud, and stands with Petrin, Prisca, and Sarel as one of the four witnesses to the crown's final, confirming melt. He never once tells Blakk that giving away a throne might have cost him less than accidentally winning one. He is not sure, by the end, that it would have. My life is the Duke's shield. My flame, his final light.","i":"images/characters/harn-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Petrin","u":"characters/petrin.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Heir Presumptive · later King of Illumaria","x":"Spends eleven years quietly reconciling granary ledgers, and inherits a crown that finally requires nothing more mystical than that. Blakk's cousin and heir presumptive, sixteen years old and watching from the walls on the day of the Standing at Illmere Ford, then a grey, heavily audited man of fifty-six by the morning Blakk finally dies. Set for eleven years to personally reconcile every granary shipment against its own certified weight, he uncovers a granary-master's six-year fraud through sheer tedium rather than brilliance, brings it to Blakk directly, and watches his cousin choose mercy calibrated to a debtor's actual circumstances over the arrest three advisors recommend — the lesson of that whole education he later credits above every province he was ever required to govern. He is one of the last four people Blakk ever tells the truth to, and argues, at first, for confessing it publicly before his cousin is even buried; Sarel talks him out of it. His own coronation, held under the Law of Succession Blakk fought his whole nobility to pass, needs no basilica, lens, or flame — the most boring coronation in Illumaria's recorded history, and, by his own reckoning, the truest proof the law had worked. Illumaria endures not by the crown, but by the will that bears its weight.","i":"images/characters/petrin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Prisca","u":"characters/prisca.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Body-servant to Duchess Astraea","x":"Dresses the Duchess's wounds for eleven years, and keeps the one sentence that mattered for the rest of her own life. Body-servant to Duchess Astraea for eleven years, and the one person who sees her mistress somewhere past the composure four centuries of House Astraea trained into her. She goes into Astraea's tent the night after the Standing at Illmere Ford against her own better judgment, sits with her on the cold ground, and hears the four words Astraea never repeats to anyone else: he offered, I refused. She serves as the last keeper of Astraea's private ledger after her death, and is one of the four people Blakk confesses everything to in his final hour, alongside Harn and Petrin. She is the one voice in that room who argues, quietly, that burying the king's own last truth is not quite the mercy the other three take it for — and never claims, afterward, that she was talked out of believing it. I have seen the Duchess in glory, in grief, and in silence. I dressed her wounds, and I held my tongue.","i":"images/characters/prisca-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Fenn","u":"characters/fenn.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Peddler / Merchant","x":"Sells fake splinters of a false crown for eleven years, then spends decades watching the real thing give itself away for free. A peddler of no particular loyalty who survives two previous wars by supplying both sides slightly different lies, and who spends the opening weeks of Illumaria's own succession war selling pilgrims small shards of orange-dyed river quartz, warmed over a candle and passed off as splinters of the sovereign's own crown. He calls it Ember-Glass. He believes in it, the Unconquered Sun, and, by his own account, very little else, which he considers sound business rather than a failure of faith. He reinvents himself into almanacs once genuine relic-hunters start asking pointed questions about his supply chain, and returns to the capital only once more, decades into the Long Peace, to find Blakk personally arguing a farmer's grain-tithe exemption at the palace's lower gate. He writes, that same night, in a ledger he shows no one, that he made a fortune selling counterfeit grace for eleven years, and that the king has been selling the genuine article for nearly thirty and has, so far as he can tell, made nothing at all off it. Ember-Glass for warding fear and inviting warm fortune.","i":"images/characters/fenn-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Anchorite of the Drowned Road","u":"characters/anchorite-of-the-drowned-road.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Drowned Road Sect · Holy Ascetic","x":"Plants three identical cones in a nameless ditch on behalf of the Cultivator Envoy Corps, and never once meets anyone's eye. One of the small, tolerated, faintly pitiable Drowned Road sect, who hold that no soul can properly appreciate the Unconquered Sun's grace without first practicing, at length and in mud, its total absence. Three nights before Duke Blakk's household guard crosses a nameless drainage ditch, this Anchorite — smaller than most, its accent unplaceable, working on behalf of Observer 902 of the Cultivator Envoy Corps — kneels at the ditch's lip and sets three identical objects into the silt rather than one, standard redundancy under a protocol written after several thousand comparable seedings elsewhere. The tenants who notice the Anchorite at all notice only what they always notice about that sect: robes the color of an old bruise, an accent no market morning has ever placed, and an eye that never once meets theirs. Their part in the story ends there. What they left in the mud does not. The road drowns what it should not reach. I simply mark where it remembers.","i":"images/characters/anchorite-of-the-drowned-road-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Observer 902","u":"characters/observer-902.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Field Observer, Cultivator Envoy Corps","x":"Files four centuries of flat, evaluative-language-free reports on one cone, and breaks protocol exactly once to say why it mattered. The Cultivator field agent running Case Study #4,555, stationed unseen in an alcove cut into the Grand Basilica's own black stone. Its reports use, per protocol, no first-person pronoun and no evaluative language whatsoever — and its very first entry breaks that protocol once anyway, recording, against its own training's explicit judgment that the detail was not relevant to the case, that the drainage ditch it had just seeded smelled of a particular decayed sweetness. It closes the file four centuries later, by Illumarian reckoning, with a report that credits the whole intervention to a single 0.2-pound retroreflective object meeting a legitimacy doctrine with zero tolerance for ambiguous results — and appends, in a margin the Corps' own style guide explicitly discourages, four words it would call purely descriptive and would not, under any circumstance the Corps could devise, call what they actually were: he wanted to leave. It is already three ditches, and one kingdom, away before the file is even closed. To witness without interference. To record without judgement. The Observer does not alter the outcome.","i":"images/characters/observer-902-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Anchorite of the Drowned Road plants the Vessel in a ditch no map had ever named","u":"records/the-anchorite-plants-the-vessel.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Anchorite of the Drowned Road","x":"A hooded Anchorite of the Drowned Road kneels at the edge of dark water, setting an orange and white traffic cone into the mud, a distant city skyline behind them The Anchorite of the Drowned Road plants the Vessel in a ditch no map had ever named.","i":"images/scenes/the-anchorite-plants-the-vessel-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Blakk's palfrey loses its footing, and pitches its rider toward the Vessel","u":"records/the-fall-at-the-ditch.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Blakk","x":"A rearing horse throws its rider face-first into a muddy ditch beside a bright orange traffic cone Blakk's palfrey loses its footing, and pitches its rider toward the Vessel.","i":"images/scenes/the-fall-at-the-ditch-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Blakk sets the cone on his own head as a joke for his guard","u":"records/a-joke-for-the-guard.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Blakk","x":"A muddy man lifts an orange traffic cone onto his own head like a crown while soldiers and a dark horse look on Blakk sets the cone on his own head as a joke for his guard.","i":"images/scenes/a-joke-for-the-guard-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Sol-Focus Arc's beam finds Blakk, beneath his column, at the Great Judgment","u":"records/the-beam-finds-blakk.html","m":"Case 4555 · The Vessel of Unmediated Grace","d":"Blakk","x":"A man wearing an orange cone stands lit by a blinding shaft of golden light inside a vast dark cathedral, clergy and nobles gathered around him The Sol-Focus Arc's beam finds Blakk, beneath his column, at the Great Judgment.","i":"images/scenes/the-beam-finds-blakk-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","u":"books/4438.html","m":"Case 4438 · Published","d":"One tired fish, one polished suit of armor, and an empire that mistook a shock for a mandate.","x":"Case 4438 Historical Fantasy · Political Intrigue · Tragedy For eleven years the fisherman Ren Duo has worked the flooded ruins below Nine Bends, where a river drowned an old town and left its stone gateways standing in the shallows. Then one morning his net brushes something black beneath a fallen lintel, the water lights from the inside, and he is thrown into the shallows with his fingers curled into claws he cannot open. He does the small, honest, sensible thing: he tells the magistrate. Within the month the Yan court has sent armed men and a wagon; six men drown securing the creature for the road; and by the time it reaches the capital it has a new name — the Heavenly Thunder-Serpent — and a waiting list for its water. At Yujing, Chief Alchemist Zhou Xuan gives the creature a theology long before anyone has thought to measure it, and the court's ladies and dukes line up to be shocked. Only Mei Suwen, a court Reckoner with no rank and a hand so small a servant once mistook her columns for brocade, keeps the numbers: how long the current takes to come, how much weaker the next one is, what a frightened animal does. Her warnings are carefully worded, carefully ranked, and carefully filed beneath the grain tallies. The Yongkang Emperor — nine years into a reign no one has dared audit, raised on a childhood prophecy that Heaven owes him a debt — hears the same water differently. He means to step into it, in full ceremonial armor, before witnesses, and collect. What the Trial does to the empire afterward is arithmetic: a dead sovereign, a court that mistakes a shock for a verdict, and a son, Prince Yue, who is the one man who ever asked Mei Suwen what her numbers actually meant — and understood every word. Twelve of the empire's ranking officials are sent into the water in the Second Trial; eleven do not come out. A warlord in the southern salt marshes stops forwarding the tribute, a three-province coalition reaches Yujing's walls, and a court physician writes the only honest page anyone ever produces about the creature, then burns it himself. The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent closes on a Cultivator's own archival log — Case Study 4,438: one large, increasingly tired fish, a hermit who keeps filing the same unauthorized phrase, and the chronicles that will remember none of it."},{"k":"s","t":"Mei Suwen","u":"characters/mei-suwen.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"The Reckoner · Court Reckoner of Yujing · 28 years old","x":"Measures the serpent for eleven months and warns everyone who will listen — and is understood perfectly by exactly the wrong man. A court Reckoner with no formal rank, raised on the omens and lucky days of the minor gentry until her eleventh year, when a diviner's confident and entirely wrong forecast of drought cost her father three harvests and taught her that confidence and accuracy are different currencies. She has spent the seventeen years since betting her career that the second one eventually pays its debts. When the serpent reaches Yujing she does what no alchemist thinks to do: she times it. Her ledger, in a hand so small that a servant dusting her desk once mistook the columns for decorative brocade, records how long the current takes to come, how much weaker the next discharge is, and why a disturbed animal is a less predictable one. She warns Zhou Xuan, sends a final written warning to Zheng Kui that his office files unread, and watches the Second Trial unfold in exactly the order she least wanted to be right about. The one person who ever asks what her numbers actually say is Prince Yue, and she learns too late what it means that he understood them perfectly. She keeps, in the same locked chest as her most sensitive ledgers, a small and entirely unscientific collection of pressed flowers gathered from every posting she has ever held — a habit she has never recorded in any professional capacity. She outlives two emperors and the empire that employed her, and in the one account no chronicle confirms, she is last seen pouring tea in a teahouse with Wan-er. Numbers do not kneel for anyone, Your Highness. They only tell the truth.","i":"images/characters/mei-suwen-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Yue","u":"characters/yue.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Prince Yue · third son of the Yongkang Emperor · later Emperor Yue · 40 years old","x":"The only man at court who asks what the numbers actually say — and then chooses the conclusion. Third among the Yongkang Emperor's sons, and the one man at court who goes looking for Mei Suwen in the archive to ask not what the alchemists say the water means, but what it does. He listens, asks sharper questions than she expected, and sits through a whole session drinking a specific bitter tisane his mother once brewed for him during a childhood illness, which no one since has ever managed to make correctly. He argues his father out of the Solstice Trial on pure strategic grounds and is dismissed. His daughter Wan-er is the one person in the palace he has least often found time to notice. When his father dies in the water, Yue takes the throne against the court's plain expectation and turns the Second Trial into a purge whose order he had written, in his own hand, across two years of ordinary court paranoia — long before any fish had entered any tank. He added Pei Rong last, underlined once, and left off Kang Yi on the plain arithmetic that a throne with no rivals and no soldiers to hold its walls is merely an inheritance for whichever warlord notices first. The arithmetic is sound. It is also, he discovers in the siege that follows, not the whole of the sum. When the last wall falls he wears his father's armor, and enters the water himself. Ritual is data. Power is interpretation. I choose the conclusion.","i":"images/characters/yue-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Yongkang Emperor","u":"characters/yongkang-emperor.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Emperor of Yan · ninth year of his reign · Deceased","x":"Inherits a Mandate no one has ever audited, and decides to audit it himself — in polished steel, in front of witnesses. Nine years into a reign built on nine generations of everyone agreeing not to test whether the Mandate still applied, with a salt-marsh warlord quietly keeping his tribute and a western general calling him “the old man in the Jade Hall” in private letters the Ministry of Rites intercepted and wished it had not. At nine years old, in the year his own father nearly lost the throne to a coalition of uncles, a wandering Daoist read his birth chart at his mother's frightened insistence and named a fate: the Mark of the Unclosed Ledger, in which Heaven itself owes the child a debt that will come due, in full, exactly once, at a moment of the boy's own choosing. His mother repeated it to him until he could not separate it from the fact of surviving. He does not, strictly, believe the eel is a dragon. He needs three provinces to believe the water is holy, by the only method they have ever found persuasive: watching a man risk everything and either die or not. He remembers Master Bai's singed beard, in the small hours before his own Trial, as evidence rather than warning. The night before, he summons no minister, only his granddaughter Wan-er, who tries on his gauntlets, three sizes too large, while he tells her about the carp that swam upstream until it became a dragon. Two days later the court records that he ascended to the Nine Heavens, his body received rather than killed. Heaven owes me a debt.","i":"images/characters/yongkang-emperor-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ren Duo","u":"characters/ren-duo.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Fisherman of Nine Bends · eleven years on the flooded ruins","x":"Tells the truth once, to the nearest official, and watches a small true thing become a large official one without his permission. A fisherman with a widowed mother, a debt to his boat's previous owner, and a private rule of arithmetic about when frightening water is worth fishing. For ten of his eleven years on the flooded ruins the place gives him nothing stranger than good carp and the occasional drowned roof-tile. Then his net brushes something black beneath a fallen lintel, the water lights from the inside, and he is thrown into the shallows with his fingers curled like claws. He avoids the basin for eleven days, goes back on the ninth night with a coil of rope and a half-formed plan to lead the creature out himself, and does not try it. Elder Peng, watching, lets him leave. What finally moves him is the same arithmetic that governs everything else in his life, and a smaller, less flattering wish to be believed. He stands outside the district magistrate's residence for the better part of an hour before he knocks. He had imagined a clerk writing the report down and filing it somewhere it would quietly wait to be useful; instead six armed men and a specially commissioned wagon arrive within the fortnight, and six men drown securing the transport. No history records their names. Ren Duo remembers all six for the rest of his life, though he never learns which name belongs to which face, and the not-knowing becomes its own shape of guilt. His own account of a numb hand is filed with the commission that studies the creature, and very likely burned with the rest. I did not seek the serpent. The river showed me—and I spoke.","i":"images/characters/ren-duo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","u":"characters/the-heavenly-thunder-serpent.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"An Electric Eel, Eight Feet Long · the Catalyst of Case 4438","x":"A large, shy, increasingly tired fish that an empire named a god — then renamed a bigger one. Electrophorus voltai: a scale-less electric eel, black, thick as a fisherman's forearm and longer than his boat, capable of a self-generated discharge of up to 860 volts. It is nocturnal, dislikes sudden movement, defends rather than attacks, and eats carp. It spends its early years beneath a fallen lintel in the flooded ruins below Nine Bends, then its captivity in a porcelain-and-gilt tank in the capital, retreating to the northern shadow whenever the crowd presses close. Everything the empire believes about it — the Nectar of the Thunder Dao, then the Yin-Lightning Fetus of the Chaos Epoch — is supplied by men who never once measure it. It was placed in the basin four years before the case opens by Elder Peng, and then simply left. What actually kills the Yongkang Emperor is his own armor: wet steel sealed around a body, with no gap for the current to lose interest in. Its discharge weakens with every disturbance, and by the eleventh man in the Second Trial it has spent every volt it possessed across ten bodies and has nothing left to give him. It does not survive its last flooding; the order to fill the basin with stone is carried out by a crew paid to fill a basin, not to empty one first, and it dies within the season in stone-choked water. The chronicles record that it departed this lower world of its own accord. It is said to coil like a rope of night, and strike like the wrath of heaven. Yet it is no god, no dragon—only a creature of water and current.","i":"images/characters/the-heavenly-thunder-serpent-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Elder Peng","u":"characters/elder-peng.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Observer 403 · Hermit of Nine Bends · 141 local years in the ruins","x":"A hermit who has outlasted every legend the district built about him, and keeps filing the same unauthorized phrase. The Cultivators' Envoy at Nine Bends, and the only person in the story who knows what the fish is. He has occupied the flooded ruin for one hundred and forty-one local years under a rotation of identities — shipwreck survivor, hermit, mute penitent, and once, unhappily, a minor tax collector — and the district credits his sun-cured silence to a miracle of the sea-cave that let him survive a wreck. In truth his lungs draw on gases no local physician has a name for, an ocular array reads every pulse within sight, and a monitoring mechanism no larger than a beetle, sewn into the lining of his sleeve, records the water's conductivity and the serpent's discharge. The Prime Directive lets him intervene at several points, and he takes almost none of them. He lets Ren Duo knock on a magistrate's door when a single sentence would have stopped him, and lets him leave the ruin with his rope and his half-formed plan when the protocol permits a less generous accounting. Instead he writes a single non-standard phrase into his log — “rounding error” — on eleven separate occasions, each time about someone outside his mandate. The Custodian's analyst notes it corresponds to no authorized metric, and that his cover identity remains unretired. Recommends no action. Recommends, in fact, nothing. Mountains remember what men forget. Rivers carry what men refuse to see.","i":"images/characters/elder-peng-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Wan-er","u":"characters/wan-er.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"The Yongkang Emperor's granddaughter · Prince Yue's daughter · 7 years old","x":"Asks the only question worth asking twice — and is lied to kindly the first time. Prince Yue's daughter, seven years old at the Solstice, beloved of her grandfather and, by a standing exception to protocol no one has the courage to revoke, allowed to fall asleep most nights in a chair outside his private study. On the last evening of his life she tries on his ceremonial gauntlets, three sizes too large for her wrist, while he tells her the old story about the carp that swam upstream until it became a dragon. She does not understand that she is being given something. She understands only that her grandfather is, for once, not busy. When he dies in the water, a nursemaid not equal to the question tells her that yes, he became a dragon, because it is the only answer that does not require either of them to know anything true. It is a kind lie, and it is the moment she stops believing adults. During the siege Mei Suwen walks her to the western postern three evenings out of every seven, and she quietly rewrites the carp's ending so that it simply swims somewhere the river cannot follow. Years later, in a teahouse, she asks Mei one question — whether she thinks he became one — and is told, for the first time, an honest I do not know. Did you truly become a dragon, Grandfather?","i":"images/characters/wan-er-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sun Lin","u":"characters/sun-lin.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Inspector of Palace Accounts · eunuch · eleven years in post","x":"Audits the alchemists for eleven years out of a grudge he never explains, then burns the records to save the men they would condemn. Holds a minor, unglamorous post that eunuchs have traditionally used to enrich themselves, and has spent eleven years using it instead to quietly catalog every occasion the Court Alchemists' expenditures made no defensible sense — out of a hatred for that ministry so old and so specific that he has stopped explaining its origin even to himself. A reputation for getting inconvenient answers written down, rather than quietly buried, is the one thing eleven years of auditing has earned him in full. So when a frightened chamber-servant finds a folded list among Prince Yue's discarded papers, it is Sun Lin she brings it to, and Sun Lin who hands it to Mei Suwen. He helps her and Physician Kao measure what the Second Trial left behind, and during the siege he does the one thing no one asks of him: feeds the palace's own financial and requisition records into a brazier in the Ministry of Accounts, a few sheaves at a time, on the calm and entirely accurate theory that whichever administration inherits the city should not inherit the evidence to punish anyone with. His last letter to the Reckoner mentions that the Marshal once asked him whether an empire that killed its own competence deserved to be saved by either side. Then the correspondence stops, for reasons the record does not supply. Numbers do not lie. Men do.","i":"images/characters/sun-lin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kang Yi","u":"characters/kang-yi.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Grand Marshal · commander of the northern garrisons · nineteen years of service","x":"The one senior officer the Second Trial fails to consume — left off the list on arithmetic he is never told about. Commander of the northern garrisons, and the only senior officer of the empire the Second Trial somehow fails to consume, because the prince who wrote its list did not lift the brush when the Marshal's name occurred to him. During the siege he finds himself in the position every competent man in a collapsing empire eventually finds himself in: responsible for a defense he did not design, using an army he did not train, on behalf of an Emperor he did not choose and increasingly does not trust. He gives no speeches. He drills the wall crews at the hours he always has, delivers the same clipped, unornamented instructions, and sees to it that they eat — and it is that plain arithmetic of rice, more than anything the court's theologians offer, that holds the eastern wall. He raises the possibility of a ceded province or a marriage alliance once, and only once, and reads in the Emperor's stillness that the answer is a decision already made. He comes closer than any man in the empire will to saying aloud what the Second Trial's own arithmetic made obvious — and does not, from the plain calculation that a marshal accused of treason defends nothing, while a marshal who holds his tongue might yet hold a wall. He opposes feeding the creature the palace's dwindling live carp with everything short of open refusal. By the end he is exchanging private letters with commanders on the far side of a wall that has not yet finished falling. Feed the soldiers, then speak of victory.","i":"images/characters/kang-yi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Zhou Xuan","u":"characters/zhou-xuan.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Chief Alchemist of the Yan Court · thirty years of elixirs · Deceased","x":"Names the water before anyone measures it, and is the first man sent into it by the theology he invented. For thirty years he has compounded elixirs that cured nothing and offended no one. When the serpent reaches Yujing he lets the Court Alchemists debate its provenance for four days with total confidence and no evidence whatsoever, then declares, on the fifth, that the water has been transformed by prolonged contact with a spirit-beast of the old Heavenly Tribulation lineage. This Nectar of the Thunder Dao, as he names it on the spot — testing the phrase in his mouth like a man trying on a ring he already intended to keep — can cleanse mortal impurity from anyone permitted to enter it. The theology he improvises over a tank of river water becomes the intellectual foundation of the Solstice Trial. Mei Suwen brings him her numbers, and he has a fluent answer to every one. After the old Emperor's death, Prince Yue names the Chief Alchemist first into the basin of the Second Trial, and the court enters his death under theology rather than command. His rival and successor Fu Baishi spends the rest of the siege improving on his doctrine. The water is not poison, but the gate. It is the Nectar of the Thunder Dao.","i":"images/characters/zhou-xuan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Fu Baishi","u":"characters/fu-baishi.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Court Alchemist · successor to Zhou Xuan","x":"Improves the god once, and survives the empire once. Spends a professional lifetime watching Zhou Xuan's mediocre compounds earn praise his own more rigorous ones never do, and concludes that the difference was never talent but nerve. Within days of his rival's death he announces that the truth was more magnificent than Zhou Xuan's small imagination allowed: the creature is no mere spirit-beast but a Yin-Lightning Fetus of the Chaos Epoch, a fragment of the world's own unformed birth-matter — which explains, with the satisfaction of a man correcting a rival's homework in public, why lesser theologies kept failing to predict its behavior. During the siege he keeps a private ledger of the Fetus's nightly communications, in a script he says predates writing and which one servant, glimpsing a page before being dismissed from the room, recognizes as Fu Baishi's own hand held at an unfamiliar angle. He proposes that its silence means displeasure, and that displeasure requires sacrifice — a proposal Kang Yi rejects in council with a single flat sentence — and later that it be fed the palace's live carp. He survives the fall of Yujing by converting the authorship of his own doctrine, within a single week, into a testimonial for Du Heng's more modest claim on Heaven's favor, and spends the last years of a long and comfortable life as the new court's most senior religious consultant. The world trembles because it has forgotten the root of lightning.","i":"images/characters/fu-baishi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Physician Kao","u":"characters/physician-kao.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Court Physician · investigator","x":"Writes the one honest page anyone ever produces about the water — and burns it himself so no one can twist it. A court physician who joins Mei Suwen and Sun Lin in the first quiet week after the Second Trial to measure what remains. Between them they test the basin's water, drawn down, against silk thread and iron nails; they examine the Emperor's ruined breastplate, its interior scorched in a pattern that matches, node for node, the exact points at which his skin blistered; and they examine the exhausted animal itself. They write the finding on a single unadorned page: an unusually large specimen of an unremarkable river species, nocturnal, sensitive to sudden movement, with a defensive reflex rather than a judgment — and an Emperor killed by his own armor. He reads it aloud to the small commission that receives it, and watches their faces arrange themselves into the polite blankness of people who have decided in advance not to be persuaded. Then he takes the page home and burns it himself, so that the court cannot twist the truth into another lie. The histories do not record the page, or its author. What the court called divine fury was only flesh, blood, and heat.","i":"images/characters/physician-kao-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Zheng Kui","u":"characters/zheng-kui.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Minister of the Court of Tribute","x":"Loses four months' salary at dice and files the only warning that could have mattered beneath the grain tallies. Minister of the Court of Tribute, whose office receives more urgent correspondence in an average week than any three clerks could read in full, and has settled into an unwritten triage: sorted by the sender's rank rather than the report's contents. It is a system that reliably places a woman with no formal title beneath the grain tallies of ministers who outrank her. On the morning of the Solstice Trial, Mei Suwen's final written warning arrives at the tail end of a night he never once discusses for the rest of his career, having lost, by his own accounting, four months' salary at dice to a pair of provincial tax assessors he remains almost certain had been cheating him. He files it unread. It is a lapse that costs the empire a good deal more than four months' salary, and he spends what remains of his life failing to mention it to anyone. The dice are cold. Heaven is colder.","i":"images/characters/zheng-kui-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Meng Kuo","u":"characters/meng-kuo.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"General · Commander of the Western Passes · nineteen years in command","x":"Runs the numbers on refusing the summons and goes in anyway, in full armor, in front of his own soldiers. Commanded the western passes for nineteen years, and in private correspondence the Ministry of Rites intercepted and wished it had not, called the old Emperor “the old man in the Jade Hall.” He arrives at the basin already understanding the true purpose of the summons, and steps in anyway, in full lamellar armor, because he has run the numbers on refusing — public disgrace, stripped rank, a slow provincial death against a fast public one — and a man who has spent his career ordering soldiers into worse odds cannot, in front of them, be seen to flinch from his own. The current takes him within the space of eleven breaths. The court, still fresh from the Chief Alchemist's death and unwilling to abandon a doctrine it has only just finished re-consecrating, tells itself a soldier's test is simply blunter and more physical than a scholar's, and that the water is working through the court by kind. He goes first of the war council's own dead. To refuse is to invite suspicion. To go is to die. I will choose the path that burdens no one.","i":"images/characters/meng-kuo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Pei Rong","u":"characters/pei-rong.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Duke of Pei · second to enter the Second Trial · Deceased","x":"Spends his last private minute balancing his estate's accounts so his sons will inherit clean books. A duke whose only real offense is a competence at the Ministry of Tribute that Prince Yue intends, in time, to hold himself. His name is added last to Yue's list and underlined once, on the reckoning that his death will cost the empire one capable administrator and cost Yue, so far as he can then calculate, nothing he expects to need again. He is meticulous in accounts and in private responsibility, and he is the second to enter the water in the Second Trial. The one manservant who survives the week remembers him spending his final private minute not in prayer but in a rapid, whispered accounting of his estate's debts to three separate creditors, apparently determined that whichever of his sons inherits will at least inherit clean books. His death produces a fresh and marginally more strained theory — that purity runs differently through different bloodlines — and, months later, a small silence in a besieged council when the Emperor asks for the one administrator he would have trusted to reconcile a granary count, and remembers, unaided, why he is not available. Before the basin takes me, I will settle every number.","i":"images/characters/pei-rong-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Du Heng","u":"characters/du-heng.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Warlord of the Southern Salt Marshes","x":"Stops forwarding the tribute, renames his levies a rescue, and fills the basin with stone. Warlord of the salt marshes to the south, who stopped forwarding the province's tribute to the capital and quietly began keeping it well before the Solstice. When the Emperor dies in the basin and the court follows him into it, he stops calling his levies a tax and begins calling them, without apparent irony, a rescue: the throne, he tells his captains, has proved itself unequal to Heaven and therefore to men, and someone competent will need to hold the empire together while the Jade Hall finishes counting its dead. Two more provinces echo him within the season, each by a different justification and the identical conclusion. He reaches the walls of Yujing at the head of a three-province coalition and refuses every delegation the dwindling court sends, calling the whole thing correction rather than conquest. When the city falls he finds the throne empty and the basin drained a second time, and orders it filled in with stone rather than water — on the grounds, reasonable to absolutely everyone by then, that an empire could not afford a third Emperor discovering the same door. His own scribes write the histories that follow. The capital fell not to chaos, but to correction.","i":"images/characters/du-heng-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Master Bai","u":"characters/master-bai.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Master of the Nine-Fold Mountain · cultivator · 66 years old","x":"Forty years beneath a waterfall in pursuit of the True Yang Breath, and one open palm against a fish. A cultivator of considerable and largely self-reported fame, who has spent forty years meditating beneath a waterfall in pursuit of the True Yang Breath and has grown, in that time, entirely convinced that no divine phenomenon can occur within his own generation without his personal involvement. When word of the Heavenly Thunder-Serpent reaches the Jianghu, he arrives barefoot at the court, addresses the tank in the ringing register cultivators reserve for audiences of one, and strikes the water with an open palm — the Heaven-Shattering Thunder Strike, qi against a fish. The eel answers with everything it has. His eyes roll back with an audible click several witnesses swear they heard from the second row; his beard, famous throughout three provinces and insured, by one account, against fire, vanishes in a bright, ozone-scented flash; and he folds to the marble. The court draws the wrong lesson entirely: the Yongkang Emperor will remember the singed beard as evidence rather than warning. Master Bai regrows the beard within two years and refuses to discuss the incident. Heaven and earth have their way. So has electricity.","i":"images/characters/master-bai-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Zhuo Sheng","u":"characters/zhuo-sheng.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Merchant · 45 years old · son of a tanner","x":"Sells the nectar, the queue, and the numb arm — and pours it all into a canal the afternoon he sees where it leads. A tanner's son who, within a year of the serpent's arrival, is running three separate operations: bottled river water sold to provincial nobles as Genuine Nectar, Blessed at Source; forged appointment tallies sold to heirs too impatient to wait their family's turn; and, for buyers who want the bragging rights of the burn without the indignity of the basin, a service in which hired substitutes stand in the queue and take the shock in their place. He makes in eight months more silver than his father the tanner made in forty years of honest work, and describes the period, later, to the two associates he trusts, as the finest and most fraudulent of his career. On the afternoon the Second Trial turns into a mass execution, he quietly empties his stock into the nearest canal, along with two crates of forged appointment tallies he judges, correctly, to have become considerably more incriminating than valuable. The silver outlives the empire. Merit is purchasable. Even the Thunder-Serpent can be traded.","i":"images/characters/zhuo-sheng-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Yun Wei","u":"characters/yun-wei.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Imperial Consort · the first courtier to touch the water","x":"Calls it the most wonderful pain of her life — and makes the basin fashionable. It is not faith, strictly, that makes the first courtier volunteer a hand. It is ambition, wearing faith as a coat because the coat fits better in public. Consort Yun Wei, favored least by a duke who has never once looked at her directly, dips two fingers into the basin before the assembled court and receives, for her trouble, a jolt that snaps her wrist backward and leaves her whole arm numb through supper. She does not scream. She laughs, high and startled, and calls it the most wonderful pain of her life. Within a week every noblewoman at court who has heard the story secondhand is requesting an audience with the serpent as though it were a visiting dignitary rather than an eight-foot fish with an itch to defend its own tank. The shock becomes a tale, and the basin becomes fashionable. She will tell her granddaughters, decades later, in a country the old empire would no longer recognize, that she once touched Heaven with her bare hand, and none of them will ever learn what she actually touched. The current bit her, yet the court called it the dragon's kiss.","i":"images/characters/yun-wei-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Ren Duo's net brushes the serpent's flank in the flooded ruins below Nine Bends","u":"records/the-net-meets-the-serpent.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Ren Duo","x":"A fisherman in a small boat hauls a net through churning white-blue water while a huge black serpent swirls beneath him, flooded stone gateways behind Ren Duo's net brushes the serpent's flank in the flooded ruins below Nine Bends.","i":"images/scenes/the-net-meets-the-serpent-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Elder Peng watches from the reeds while Ren Duo kneels at the water's edge","u":"records/elder-peng-in-the-reeds.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Elder Peng, Ren Duo","x":"A grey-bearded hermit in ragged robes sits among tall reeds while a fisherman kneels at the water's edge beside flooded stone pillars Elder Peng watches from the reeds while Ren Duo kneels at the water's edge.","i":"images/scenes/elder-peng-in-the-reeds-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Six men drown securing the serpent for the road to the capital — and no history remembers their names","u":"records/the-serpent-taken-alive.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","x":"Soldiers under red banners drag ropes across churning white water as a huge black serpent coils among flooded ruins Six men drown securing the serpent for the road to the capital — and no history remembers their names.","i":"images/scenes/the-serpent-taken-alive-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The court gathers around the water at Yujing, in the season when a turn beside it is the most coveted appointment east of the river","u":"records/the-basin-hall.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","x":"Courtiers in gold and crimson gather around a great round gilded basin, a dark serpent shape coiled in its water, a raised throne and banners beyond The court gathers around the water at Yujing, in the season when a turn beside it is the most coveted appointment east of the river.","i":"images/scenes/the-basin-hall-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Consort Yun Wei is the first courtier to touch the water — and calls it the most wonderful pain of her life","u":"records/yun-wei-touches-the-water.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Yun Wei","x":"A consort in pale silk and gold hairpins reaches toward a gilded basin with her eyes closed as white lightning flickers across her fingers, courtiers watching behind her Consort Yun Wei is the first courtier to touch the water — and calls it the most wonderful pain of her life.","i":"images/scenes/yun-wei-touches-the-water-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Mei Suwen keeps her ledger of the serpent's intervals in the archive, in a hand small enough to be mistaken for brocade","u":"records/mei-suwen-and-the-ledger.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Mei Suwen","x":"A young woman in pale robes writes in a small book by lantern light at a desk piled with scrolls and charts in a dark archive Mei Suwen keeps her ledger of the serpent's intervals in the archive, in a hand small enough to be mistaken for brocade.","i":"images/scenes/mei-suwen-and-the-ledger-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Master Bai's Heaven-Shattering Thunder Strike: qi against a fish","u":"records/the-heaven-shattering-thunder-strike.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Master Bai","x":"A white-bearded cultivator in flowing white robes strikes the water with an open palm as blue-white lightning bursts around him and courtiers draw back Master Bai's Heaven-Shattering Thunder Strike: qi against a fish.","i":"images/scenes/the-heaven-shattering-thunder-strike-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The night before the Trial, the Yongkang Emperor lets Wan-er try on his gauntlets","u":"records/the-gauntlets-the-night-before.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"The Yongkang Emperor, Wan-er","x":"An elderly emperor in a lamplit study holds a small girl who wears an oversized golden gauntlet, a suit of ceremonial armor standing behind them The night before the Trial, the Yongkang Emperor lets Wan-er try on his gauntlets.","i":"images/scenes/the-gauntlets-the-night-before-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Solstice Trial: the Yongkang Emperor raises his arms toward the sun as the current finds his armor","u":"records/the-solstice-ascension.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"The Yongkang Emperor","x":"An emperor in gilded plate armor stands in a round basin with his arms flung wide as lightning runs across the steel, a crowd banked up around him beneath a blazing sun The Solstice Trial: the Yongkang Emperor raises his arms toward the sun as the current finds his armor.","i":"images/scenes/the-solstice-ascension-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Wan-er waits outside her grandfather's study, the evening no one comes to find her as usual","u":"records/wan-er-outside-the-study.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Wan-er","x":"A small girl in pale robes sits alone on the steps of a lantern-lit palace corridor beside an empty carved chair, a golden gauntlet on the floor near her Wan-er waits outside her grandfather's study, the evening no one comes to find her as usual.","i":"images/scenes/wan-er-outside-the-study-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The summoned wait in ranks on the terrace for the Second Trial — attendance entirely voluntary","u":"records/the-court-waits-in-ranks.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","x":"Dukes, generals and officials in ceremonial armor and silks stand in ranks along a marble terrace beside a great basin, red banners hanging overhead The summoned wait in ranks on the terrace for the Second Trial — attendance entirely voluntary.","i":"images/scenes/the-court-waits-in-ranks-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The eleventh man to enter the Second Trial stands in the water a long, absurd moment, waiting for a judgment that does not arrive","u":"records/the-eleventh-entrant.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","x":"A young man in a dark wet robe stands waist-deep in a basin below a stepped terrace of stunned courtiers, a huge coiled serpent dark in the water beneath him The eleventh man to enter the Second Trial stands in the water a long, absurd moment, waiting for a judgment that does not arrive.","i":"images/scenes/the-eleventh-entrant-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Mei Suwen walks Wan-er to the western postern, three evenings out of every seven","u":"records/the-western-postern.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Mei Suwen, Wan-er","x":"A woman in a dark hooded cloak shelters a small girl against a palace gate wall at sunset as guards stand in the courtyard beyond Mei Suwen walks Wan-er to the western postern, three evenings out of every seven.","i":"images/scenes/the-western-postern-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Emperor Yue enters the water in his father's armor as Yujing burns","u":"records/the-second-ascension.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Yue","x":"An emperor in dark gilded armor and a red cloak stands waist-deep in a basin while the palace burns behind him under a smoke-black sky Emperor Yue enters the water in his father's armor as Yujing burns.","i":"images/scenes/the-second-ascension-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Mei Suwen and Wan-er, years later, in a teahouse two provinces west of Yujing — in the one account no chronicle confirms","u":"records/the-teahouse.html","m":"Case 4438 · The Heavenly Thunder-Serpent","d":"Mei Suwen, Wan-er","x":"Two women sit across a low table with a teapot and cups in a lamplit teahouse, a window behind them opening on hills at sunset Mei Suwen and Wan-er, years later, in a teahouse two provinces west of Yujing — in the one account no chronicle confirms.","i":"images/scenes/the-teahouse-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Songs Never Mention the Cats","u":"books/0188.html","m":"Case 0188 · Awaiting release","d":"Arithmetic. Seven. Cats. A debt mistaken for a miracle. Filed.","x":"Case 0188 Literary Fiction · Historical Fantasy · War & Military Fiction Empress Chandralekha dies in her twenty-eighth winter, and her husband, Emperor Dharmasena, kneels at her grave for seventeen consecutive nights until something red and green pushes up through the frost. The gardener who finds it calls it a miracle. The Cultivators, who left the bulb there themselves, call it Item 0188, Mythic Class, and wait to see what a grieving empire does with an excuse to believe in undying love. What it does is grow an economy. A trader who gives his name as Somadatta distributes the bulbs from a seed-stall that is really a Cultivator field post, and within a generation the flower once called Chandralekha's Vow — now Anantavrata, the Endless Vow — has become a wedding custom no family can afford to skip and no farmer can quite afford to refuse. Barley fields along the Shonavati turn to flowers one holding at a time; a bride gives up the cat that has slept against her ankles since childhood because a vow-flower needs the room a cat's dish would take; a court physician tests the bloom with a frame of temple bees and finds, to his quiet horror, that nothing living will go near it. The reckoning arrives as arithmetic, not omen: fewer fields of grain each year, a war fought over granaries already stripped bare, and rats nesting where the barley used to stand. By the time anyone adds up what the undying flower cost the ordinary, mortal people who grew it, the empress it was named for has been dead for decades — and the debt has already been filed away as a miracle. The songs, as ever, never mention the cats."},{"k":"s","t":"Dharmasena","u":"characters/dharmasena.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Emperor of Amritavana · Ninth of His Name · Husband of Chandralekha","x":"The emperor who knelt at his wife's grave for seventeen nights, and left an empire an excuse to believe in undying love. Ninth of his name, and the only one of the nine remembered for a flower instead of a war. When Chandralekha dies of a winter fever, Dharmasena visits her tomb nightly through the coldest month of the year, and on the fourth night presses a single bulb into the half-frozen ground — not knowing, and never later admitting he wondered, that the bulb had been left where he would find it. He watches the flower that grows from it become a name (Chandralekha's Vow), then a custom, then an industry he never ordered and cannot quite bring himself to end. When his gardener Haridasa asks, years later, whether the Emperor wants it removed, Dharmasena says only: let it stand. He dies before the worst of the arithmetic comes due, and is remembered, correctly, as a man whose private grief became public policy without a single decree ever being signed. A flower is mercy when it remains a dream. When it blooms in the world, it becomes a chain.","i":"images/characters/dharmasena-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Chandralekha","u":"characters/chandralekha.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Empress of Amritavana · Deceased · Namesake of the Vow","x":"Dead before the story proper begins, and the reason for everything that follows — a woman turned, without her consent, into a national myth. Empress of Amritavana, dead of a winter fever before Case 0188 truly opens. Everything that happens afterward happens in her name and without her permission: a flower named for her grief, a custom built on her memory, and a fortune made from a vow she never made. Her husband Dharmasena is the only person in the empire who seems to remember that the woman and the myth were never quite the same thing. Some loves do not end with death. They change form, and bloom in other worlds.","i":"images/characters/chandralekha-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Haridasa","u":"characters/haridasa.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Palace Gardener · Imperial Household of Amritavana","x":"The gardener who found the bulb's first bloom, named it out of love, and had no idea what he'd started. An aging palace gardener who tends Chandralekha's tomb as part of his ordinary rounds, and is the first person other than Dharmasena to see the flower bloom. It's Haridasa, not the Emperor, who first calls it Chandralekha's Vow — a gardener's private sentiment that escapes the palace walls and becomes, within a season, the name of a national ritual of grief. He goes on tending it for decades, long after he's stopped being able to say for certain whether he still believes what he first said about it. The flower is a gift, not a thing to be removed. It is a sign — of love, of devotion, of her enduring memory.","i":"images/characters/haridasa-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Somadatta","u":"characters/somadatta.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Envoy of the Cultivators · Envoy Archetype 03 · the Trader","x":"The field agent who sells grief back to the grieving, one bulb at a time, and keeps seven secret cats because someone has to. Envoy Archetype 03, working Case 0188 under cover as a traveling seed-trader. Somadatta is the one who carries the flower out of the palace grounds and into the capital's wedding markets, and the one who watches — over years, then decades — as an emperor's private grief becomes a marriage custom, then agricultural policy, then a famine. His own field notes record the conversions, the refusals, and the slow disappearance of household cats with the same even hand. In the small rented room above his seed-stall, Somadatta keeps cats — five, then seven, by the case's fourth year — taken in from families who gave them up for the Vow. It is the one part of the file that isn't, strictly, part of the case. See the Cultivator entry for how his cover compares to other Envoys on file. The flower travels where people believe it will bring them closer to what they have lost.","i":"images/characters/somadatta-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Eternal-Grief Lily","u":"characters/the-eternal-grief-lily.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Cultivator Designation: Lilium Immortalis · Mythic Class · Local Name: Anantavrata","x":"A single, undying, unpollinated flower — Item 0188 — that no insect will touch and no family can resist. Cultivator designation Lilium Immortalis, Mythic Class; filed locally as Anantavrata, the Endless Vow, after its earlier and more sentimental name, Chandralekha's Vow. Left in the frozen ground of an empress's grave by the Envoy Somadatta, it blooms without pollination, without scent, and without ever quite dying — a flower that court physician Vaidyanatha tests with a frame of temple bees and finds nothing living will approach. It asks nothing of anyone. It is planted, gifted, purchased, and eventually grown as a cash crop in place of grain along the Shonavati, entirely on the strength of what people decide it means. See the Catalyst entry for how this compares to the rest of the case files. No bee has ever landed on it, and no bee ever will. That was never what it was for.","i":"images/characters/the-eternal-grief-lily-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Vaidyanatha","u":"characters/vaidyanatha.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Court Physician of Kunjaravana","x":"The physician sent to debunk a miracle, who instead files a report careful enough to later be misquoted for a war. Sent from the rival kingdom of Kunjaravana to examine the flower on behalf of a king who assumes it's a fraud, Vaidyanatha instead spends three mornings watching temple bees refuse to land on it, and writes a report that is honest about what he doesn't understand. It is the most careful, most hedged document in the entire case file — and the one later generations strip of its hedges and repurpose as justification for a war he never advocated. The flower is not merely growing. It is progressing. And what progresses is not meant to be cut.","i":"images/characters/vaidyanatha-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kamalini","u":"characters/kamalini.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Bride of Amritavana · Later, an Elderly Widow","x":"A nineteen-year-old bride who receives a vow-flower as a wedding gift, and gives up the cat who slept at her ankles to make room for it. Married at nineteen into a household that keeps an Anantavrata the way other families keep a household shrine. Her mother-in-law decides there isn't room in the house for both the flower and Kamalini's cat, Dhumra, and Kamalini — newly married, unwilling yet to make her first fight one worth losing — carries him to the kitchen door herself. She keeps the flower for the rest of her life, through widowhood and old age, and is never on record saying whether it was worth the trade. The Vow was for a life of abundance, they said. But in the end, it took even my cat.","i":"images/characters/kamalini-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Dhumra","u":"characters/dhumra.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Grey Cat · Companion to Kamalini","x":"The cat given up for a flower — the one cost of the Vow small enough for the histories to leave out entirely. A grey domestic cat who sleeps against Kamalini's ankles every winter of her unmarried life, and is given away on her mother-in-law's orders to make room for a vow-flower that needs no room at all. Case 0188's records are unusually attentive, for a Cultivator file, to what became of him afterward — which is more than can be said for most of what the Vow quietly cost. He slept against her ankles every winter of her unmarried life.","i":"images/characters/dhumra-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Govinda","u":"characters/govinda.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Farmer Along the Shonavati · Father of a Daughter Nearing Marriage","x":"A grain farmer who plows his own barley under rather than let anyone else do it, and tells himself it was his decision. A farmer along the Shonavati with a daughter approaching marriageable age, and so a dowry to consider. Govinda holds out against Vow-cultivation longer than most of his neighbors, then plows his own barley field under with his own hands rather than let a seed-broker's crew do it for him — a distinction that seems to matter more to him than to anyone watching. The earth gives enough, if you do not sell your soul to the flower.","i":"images/characters/govinda-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bhadraka","u":"characters/bhadraka.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Farmer Along the Shonavati · the Man Who Refused","x":"One of the last grain farmers left on the river, who turns down the bulbs every time they're offered and pays for it in a broken betrothal. A farmer along the Shonavati and one of the very few who never converts a single field to Vow-cultivation, even after his daughter's betrothal is withdrawn over it. When the Envoy Somadatta offers him bulbs directly, Bhadraka refuses without raising his voice, and goes on refusing for the rest of the case file. What grain the river valley has left by the time of the famine, it has largely because men like him didn't sell it off in advance. I have no daughter left to dower, and no wife who will not know why I bought it. Keep your bulbs.","i":"images/characters/bhadraka-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Dhanapala","u":"characters/dhanapala.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Merchant · Grain-Futures Trader of the Capital","x":"The merchant who reads the coming famine in his own ledgers years early, and profits from it instead of warning anyone. A grain-futures trader in the capital who reads the numbers — fewer barley contracts, more Vow-bulb orders — years before anyone else calls it a pattern, and positions himself to profit from the shortage rather than to prevent it. Dhanapala isn't a villain by his own account, only a man who understood the arithmetic sooner than his customers did. Grain moves the realm. Numbers tell you when people cannot see.","i":"images/characters/dhanapala-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ravisena","u":"characters/ravisena.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","d":"Son of Dharmasena · Regent, Later Emperor of Amritavana","x":"The son who inherits both an empire and its flower, and has to decide what an undying vow is worth to the living. Son of Dharmasena and Chandralekha, regent and later Emperor of Amritavana in his own right. Ravisena inherits the Vow along with the throne, and with it the accumulated arithmetic of what his father's grief has cost a generation of farmers, brides, and cats. What he does with that inheritance closes out Case 0188's imperial thread. The empire endures, not because we did not fall, but because we remember what we are.","i":"images/characters/ravisena-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"On the fourth night, Dharmasena presses a single bulb into his wife's grave and tells no one why","u":"records/the-fourth-night.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"Dharmasena kneeling at Chandralekha's grave at night, planting a single bulb On the fourth night, Dharmasena presses a single bulb into his wife's grave and tells no one why.","i":"images/scenes/the-fourth-night-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"By the seventeenth visit, the Emperor's composure finally fails him","u":"records/the-seventeenth-visit.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"Dharmasena kneeling in grief before a snow-covered tomb By the seventeenth visit, the Emperor's composure finally fails him.","i":"images/scenes/the-seventeenth-visit-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Asked whether the flower should be removed, Dharmasena says only: let it stand","u":"records/let-it-stand.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"Dharmasena and Haridasa beside the blooming grave-flower in winter Asked whether the flower should be removed, Dharmasena says only: let it stand.","i":"images/scenes/let-it-stand-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The trader who calls himself Somadatta sells the empire's newest custom one bulb at a time","u":"records/somadatta-in-the-capital.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"Somadatta selling flower bulbs at a crowded capital market The trader who calls himself Somadatta sells the empire's newest custom one bulb at a time.","i":"images/scenes/somadatta-in-the-capital-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Three mornings, one frame of temple bees, and not a single insect willing to land","u":"records/the-bees-that-would-not-land.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"Vaidyanatha testing the flower with a frame of temple bees Three mornings, one frame of temple bees, and not a single insect willing to land.","i":"images/scenes/the-bees-that-would-not-land-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"A vow-flower needs no room at all, and somehow there still isn't room for the cat","u":"records/kamalini-gives-up-dhumra.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"Kamalini holding her cat Dhumra as her mother-in-law looks on A vow-flower needs no room at all, and somehow there still isn't room for the cat.","i":"images/scenes/kamalini-gives-up-dhumra-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Above the seed-stall that is really a Cultivator field post, an Envoy keeps the one part of the case that isn't, strictly, the case","u":"records/seven-cats-above-the-seed-stall.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"Somadatta surrounded by seven cats in his room above the seed-stall Above the seed-stall that is really a Cultivator field post, an Envoy keeps the one part of the case that isn't, strictly, the case.","i":"images/scenes/seven-cats-above-the-seed-stall-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Govinda converts his own barley field rather than let a broker's crew do it for him","u":"records/govinda-plows-it-under.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"Govinda plowing under his barley field beside a seed-broker Govinda converts his own barley field rather than let a broker's crew do it for him.","i":"images/scenes/govinda-plows-it-under-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Offered the bulbs directly, Bhadraka refuses without raising his voice","u":"records/bhadraka-keeps-his-bulbs.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"Somadatta offering bulbs to Bhadraka, who refuses Offered the bulbs directly, Bhadraka refuses without raising his voice.","i":"images/scenes/bhadraka-keeps-his-bulbs-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"In the year everyone was rich, three couples buy a bulb inside the same hour","u":"records/the-year-everyone-was-rich.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"A crowded wedding market in the empire's most prosperous year In the year everyone was rich, three couples buy a bulb inside the same hour.","i":"images/scenes/the-year-everyone-was-rich-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Vow's own roots turn out to be adequate eating, for whatever is left in an empty granary","u":"records/the-granary-of-rats.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"A ravaged granary overrun with rats, an Anantavrata growing in the ruin The Vow's own roots turn out to be adequate eating, for whatever is left in an empty granary.","i":"images/scenes/the-granary-of-rats-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Generations on, a bride carries an Anantavrata down the aisle, and no one remembers why","u":"records/a-bride-generations-later.html","m":"Case 0188 · The Songs Never Mention the Cats","x":"A bride carrying an Anantavrata down the aisle at her wedding Generations on, a bride carries an Anantavrata down the aisle, and no one remembers why.","i":"images/scenes/a-bride-generations-later-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Third Grain","u":"books/2140.html","m":"Case 2140 · Awaiting release","d":"Grain. Tolerance. Grace. Arithmetic. Bread.","x":"Case 2140"},{"k":"s","t":"Garibald","u":"characters/garibald.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Count of Vardane · 31 when the clockmaker comes","x":"Shelters a stranger for a night's lodging, and spends the rest of his life discovering exactly what that hospitality cost and bought him. Count of Vardane, a man who made peace with arithmetic before he made peace with anything else, and keeps his own ledgers in a hand precise enough to embarrass his clerks. He shelters a traveling stranger — one of dozens he's sheltered that year without asking much beyond honesty about their business — and the man installs a single dull grey sphere in the county mill before vanishing three days later. When Baron Landulf files a legal claim on the strength of it, Garibald offers no defense, on the private theory that a truth he can't prove would only make the loss look like a lie besides, and breaks down completely at his family's seat, Ostrevenna — the first time anyone has ever seen him do so. He sets exactly one condition on House Ratchis reclaiming the mill for him: that it cost Landulf nothing beyond what the war his own claim set in motion has already cost him. He returns to Vardane five years later to a wheel still turning, a miller two winters dead, and a set of questions about the clockmaker's real purpose that his own private notes never quite answer. Keep the ledger current.","i":"images/characters/garibald-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Observer 419","u":"characters/observer-419.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Envoy/Observer, Case 2,140 · publicly an itinerant clockmaker","x":"Installs a single unremarkable sphere in a failing county mill, then disappears before anyone thinks to ask his name twice. A field agent of the Cultivators, deployed to Vardane disguised as a traveling clockmaker with nothing more remarkable about him than a satchel of pocket chronometers. Count Garibald shelters him for a night's lodging before asking a single question about his business — a courtesy no prior posting has required of him, and which his own field note admits, against protocol, he was not required to find noteworthy, and did. He replaces one worn axle joint with a single dull grey sphere, refuses every offer of help, and leaves three days later on foot, forgotten the way useful strangers generally are. The wheel is not. He is posted next, as a clockmaker again, to Case 1153. Introduce. Observe. Withdraw before attachment becomes measurable.","i":"images/characters/observer-419-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Teudis","u":"characters/teudis.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Miller of Vardane's county mill · inherited the post, and his father's bad knees","x":"Keeps a private tally of the wheel's working days for years, then watches an old debt forgive itself in a single winter. The broad, uncomplaining miller of Vardane's county mill, who inherited the position along with his father's bad knees and had kept, for years before the clockmaker ever arrived, a private tally of exactly how many days each winter the wheel turned at all — a number he never once told Count Garibald, suspecting correctly that the Count already knew it better than he did. He holds the precision bearing himself before Observer 419 installs it, cold in his palm on a night beside a working forge, and it's his own tavern telling of that visit, embellished the way an unbelievable truth requires, that eventually gives the county its name for what changed the mill: the Third Grain. Conscripted in the war his own good fortune helps start, he comes home eleven months later missing the two smallest fingers of his left hand and goes straight back to the mill anyway. He dies two winters before the Count returns, having made his sister repeat back to him three times the one fact he considered worth reporting. Tell him the mill never once stopped running. Not for a single day. Hand or no hand.","i":"images/characters/teudis-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Garibald's Mother","u":"characters/garibalds-mother.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Deceased · mother of Count Garibald","x":"A final illness Garibald nurses alone becomes the private grief the whole county learns never to mention above a murmur. Dead well before the clockmaker ever reaches Vardane, tended through her final illness by Garibald alone — a grief that costs him, by his own private reckoning, the best courting years of his life, and leaves him no longer much interested in beginning again. The county discusses it, when it discusses it at all, above nothing louder than a murmur.","i":"images/characters/garibalds-mother-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Landulf","u":"characters/landulf.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Baron of Verrasco · 19 at his inheritance","x":"Files a legal claim over a neighbor's good fortune, wins exactly what he asked for, and spends the rest of his life learning what it actually cost. Baron of Verrasco, who inherited the barony at nineteen when his older brother Ansfrid fell from a lathered horse during a boar hunt, and governs competently — a word, he comes to understand, people use about a man precisely when they mean to withhold something larger. He watches Vardane's fortunes climb for three years with a patience he'd call arithmetic rather than envy, and finally files a formal claim under the Writ of Undeclared Grace: not sorcery, not any crime the Threefold Crown has a name for, only an oversight any honest man might make. He wins. Holding the bearing alone afterward in a locked room, he feels not triumph but the particular cold of a man who has just proven his own accusation correct. He tells no one what his own smith found, quietly exempts whole villages from the war's levy where he can justify it as necessity rather than mercy, and writes a confession to Garibald he never learns went unanswered by design rather than indifference. He dies without ever suspecting House Ratchis engineered the claimant that took the mill back from him. I am not accusing the Count of a crime, only of an oversight any honest man might make, and asking that the law correct it as the law was written to do.","i":"images/characters/landulf-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Gisela","u":"characters/gisela.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Baron Landulf's daughter · 17 at her introduction","x":"Reads her father's ledgers closely enough to understand exactly what his ambition has cost her, and chooses her own marriage rather than let him choose a second time. Daughter to Baron Landulf, clever in the patient, arithmetical way of a girl who has read her father's ledgers long enough to know exactly what a good marriage costs and how far short of it Verrasco's revenues fall. Watching her weigh those numbers, more than watching his neighbor's granaries fill, is what finally moves her father's private suspicion into a legal claim. When the war his ambition starts costs him nearly everything else, she tells him plainly that she watched him gamble a mill for her future and lose them both in the same three years, and chooses her own lesser house herself rather than let him choose worse for her a second time. He lets her.","i":"images/characters/gisela-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ansfrid","u":"characters/ansfrid.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Heir to Verrasco, implied · Landulf's older brother","x":"Dies on a boar hunt neither brother much wanted to attend, and makes Landulf a Baron at nineteen by falling from a horse. The older brother Baron Landulf never expected to inherit past, thrown from a lathered horse during a boar hunt neither of them had much wanted to attend. His fall ends one story before it starts another: a boyhood Landulf had spent entirely assuming a barony would never be his to swear an oath over.","i":"images/characters/ansfrid-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Grimoald","u":"characters/grimoald.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Duke of Trevano · 46 when the rumor reaches him","x":"Sends an engineer to investigate a rumor out of a boyhood shame he's never told a soul, and ends up arming a continent instead. Duke of Trevano, who wanted, as a boy, to fix his grandmother's chiming clock in secret with tools stolen from the household smith, and never told anyone — not his father, not his wife, not one of the engineers he'd go on to employ by the dozen — that he'd failed. When a badly garbled rumor of a Verrasco mill reaches him at forty-six, he sends his engineer Gisulf to look, report honestly, and touch nothing without permission, and thinks, that first evening, not of siege engines but of a clock he never managed to fix. Two years of failing to reproduce the bearing leave Trevano with something else entirely: lathes, measurement, and metalworkers trained to a precision no duchy has previously demanded. He gives the order to turn it toward siege engines three days after Gisulf names what they've actually built, and spends the rest of his life uncertain whether those three days were deliberation or merely decency's minimum interval before doing what he'd already decided. He keeps his duchies, and a personal workshop of precision tools he never once touches himself.","i":"images/characters/grimoald-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Gisulf","u":"characters/gisulf.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Engineer in Duke Grimoald's service · twice told him a design would fail, and was right both times","x":"Spends three sleepless nights confirming a rumor, then spends two years turning the failure to copy it into something considerably worse. Grimoald's engineer, trusted for the rare reason that he has twice told the Duke a design would fail and been right both times. Sent to look at Verrasco's mill, report honestly, and touch nothing without permission, he touches nothing — and doesn't sleep for three nights afterward, which he considers, privately, sufficient permission of its own. His eleven-page report reaches one conclusion: a tolerance nothing in Trevano's armories can presently match. He never reproduces the bearing itself; every imitation opened past a certain depth seizes and goes inert. What his workshop builds instead, failing patiently and expensively for two years, is the capability to measure and match precision from the outside — lathes, gauges, metalworkers trained to a standard no apprenticeship had previously required. Demonstrating a trebuchet joint machined to that standard, he's the one who finally says the sentence Grimoald spends three days pretending to deliberate over. A joint that fails less often, under less maintenance, through worse weather, is not a better mill part. It is a better war.","i":"images/characters/gisulf-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Grimoald's Father","u":"characters/grimoalds-father.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Lombard collector · died in his 60s","x":"Buys his young son a Frankish chiming clock at absurd expense, and never once has it repaired. A collector with a heavyset, acquisitive self-satisfaction, known to Grimoald mainly through a single anecdote: a Frankish chiming clock bought at absurd expense for his son and never once taken to be fixed, which the boy would go on to attempt himself, in secret, and fail at, without ever once telling his father why it mattered.","i":"images/characters/grimoalds-father-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Grimoald's Wife","u":"characters/grimoalds-wife.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Wife of Duke Grimoald · early-to-mid 50s","x":"Listens in silence to a court she was raised for, and is never once told the one story that would explain her husband's fascination with precision. Duke Grimoald's wife, slender and dignified, composed by the same court training that shaped every feature of her bearing. She listens in silence as Grimoald speaks of the war his workshops have made possible, one of the very few people close enough to him to have noticed the personal workshop he never lets anyone touch — and, like everyone else in his life, never once told why.","i":"images/characters/grimoalds-wife-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Adelrada","u":"characters/adelrada.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Elder of House Ratchis · 78 years old","x":"Watches a man who never once broke over his own mother's death come apart over a mill, and spends two years quietly finishing a sum nobody else knows she's counting. Elder of House Ratchis, spare and upright at seventy-eight, with the ink-stained fingers of a woman who has kept her own ledgers for decades. She isn't moved by Landulf's legal claim on Vardane's mill so much as by what it does to Garibald — a man she has watched grieve quietly and competently his whole life, breaking, for the first time she's ever seen, at her own table at Ostrevenna. She sends the ruling to her notary Vitalis rather than accept a summary of it, and spends two years afterward keeping a private accounting nobody else is shown: what the mill's loss once cost Garibald, weighed against what its keeping has since cost Landulf. She acts only once the sum finally closes — on Landulf's own unsent confession, not on any House vote — instructing Vitalis to find an unconnected claimant, and quietly withdrawing House Ratchis's reach from Duke Grimoald's war until the mill returns to Vardane exactly as quietly as it left. She dies eleven years later in her own bed. Her great-nephew Ranulf finds the ledger afterward, and is the only person who ever learns what she'd actually been counting.","i":"images/characters/adelrada-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Vitalis","u":"characters/vitalis.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Notary retained by House Ratchis · 55 years old","x":"Reads a legal ruling three times, then reports a debt rather than a scandal — and charges his standard fee either way. A notary House Ratchis has kept on retainer for years and trusted for exactly one reason: he has never once told Elder Adelrada what he suspected she wanted to hear. Asked to review the ruling that hands Garibald's mill to Verrasco, he reads the Writ of Undeclared Grace three times and reports, without any apparent alarm, that it rests on a four-generation-dormant precedent that could pry loose a great deal more than a mill, given the right claimant and enough patience. Two years later, given an instruction rather than a question, he finds that claimant within the fortnight — a minor cousin of a minor cousin, chosen specifically for how little the choice would ever need explaining. For the reading, I charge the standard fee.","i":"images/characters/vitalis-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ranulf","u":"characters/ranulf.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Member of House Ratchis · Elder Adelrada's great-nephew · 26","x":"Argues loudest for allying with a Duke who could remake armories from a single mill part, then spends an afternoon learning what his great-aunt was actually counting. Elder Adelrada's great-nephew, lean and impatient with a House that has spent eight generations being clever instead of powerful. When Duke Grimoald's rise splits House Ratchis's council, he argues loudest for allying with the Duke outright, a position his great-aunt hears out along with the rest without ever once showing which way her own mind is bending. Three days after her funeral, going through her papers alone because no one else has volunteered for it, he finds the private ledger she kept his whole life a secret from — not the House's usual accounts of reach and marriages and debts, but two long columns of ordinary, specific losses, kept level against each other for eleven years. He is the only person who ever learns what she was actually counting. Caution has kept us safe for generations. But safety is not the same as a future.","i":"images/characters/ranulf-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Eldest Witness of the Writ","u":"characters/eldest-witness-of-the-writ.html","m":"Case 2140 · The Third Grain","d":"Witness of the Writ · senior-most of the clerical judges","x":"Presides over the hearing that hands a Count's mill to his neighbor, and can't stop glancing at a painted ceiling he's complained about for thirty years. The senior-most of the clerical judges who hear Landulf's claim against Garibald's mill, presiding beneath a painted ceiling depicting Auron weighing three golden scales that have never quite balanced in the artist's rendering — a detail he's complained about for thirty years and will go on complaining about for several more. The ruling takes his court less than an hour to reach and considerably longer to read aloud, the witnesses of the Writ being men who love procedure roughly as much as they love Auron himself.","i":"images/characters/eldest-witness-of-the-writ-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Listening Water","u":"books/3115.html","m":"Case 3115 · Awaiting release","d":"Reckoning. Rationed. Witnessed. Roster. Closed.","x":"Case 3115"},{"k":"s","t":"Huairen","u":"characters/huairen.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"King of Qinghe · forty-one years on the throne · 79 years old","x":"Learns, on his own deathbed, exactly whose son he raised — and asks the realm to remember only that the boy was loved. King of Qinghe for forty-one years, and by the novel's opening already visibly diminished by an illness that turns out, far too late for anyone to act on it, to be Cao's own slow, measured dosing rather than age alone. He keeps Xiaobao curled against him through most of his final months, and it is the dog's own fear of a familiar step that eventually hands Mingxuan the pattern no human witness would give him. Whatever he comes to understand about Queen Meilan, Prince Huaiyu, and the true parentage of the son the Father's Reckoning has already made legally his own, he takes the last sip of the Listening Water from Mingxuan's own hand and spends it not on confrontation but on a final instruction, delivered to the room rather than to any one person in it: tell them the boy is loved, and tell them nothing else. Tell them the boy is loved. Tell them nothing else.","i":"images/characters/huairen-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Meilan","u":"characters/meilan.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Queen of Qinghe · 30 years old · later Regent","x":"Chooses, in the only way left to her, by declining to choose otherwise — and spends the rest of her life quietly paying for what that choice cost. Queen of Qinghe for nine years before the novel opens, and the one person in Jinlu who understands exactly what the Father's Reckoning is worth: it makes Prince Jiyun her husband Huairen's legal son the moment Warden Peizhi records it, whatever the truth of his conception with Huaiyu happens to be. She spends the entire novel protecting that single piece of paper, by whatever quiet, unglamorous means the household affords a woman who cannot be seen to act at all. When Suyin's accidental knowledge threatens to undo it, Meilan has Chunhui gently questioned rather than raise her own voice once, and the household's quieter instruments — Cao's, Chancellor Jian's — do the rest without her ever giving a documented order. She rules as Regent once the throne is hers to hold, outlives every man who did the actual killing on her behalf, and marks the one debt she can never openly repay by quietly doubling, every season, an anonymous stipend to a dead maid's brother who has no idea whose money it is. I chose, the only way I could: by declining to choose otherwise.","i":"images/characters/meilan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Huaiyu","u":"characters/huaiyu.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Prince, half-brother to King Huairen · March-Lord of Beiyan · 38 years old","x":"Keeps a soldier's chest packed with an infant's cap he was never allowed to claim, and dies at a ford still not knowing his son survived him. Half-brother to King Huairen and March-Lord of the frontier at Beiyan — a prince by blood who built his whole adult identity on being a soldier by choice instead. His affair with Queen Meilan produces the one child neither of them can ever publicly claim: Prince Jiyun, sealed to the throne as Huairen's own son by Warden Peizhi's Reckoning before Huaiyu is ever told the boy exists as anything other than the King's heir. He keeps an infant's cap in his own campaign chest for the rest of his life, and tells no one what it means. When the throne he never contested becomes something worth fighting over anyway, he leads the faction that will not accept Chancellor Jian's account of the old King's death, and dies at the second autumn's crossing of the Shuang ford, shot by a seventeen-year-old soldier of the Queen's own garrison who believes, to his own dying day, that he has struck down a traitor rather than the father of the child that garrison exists to protect. A prince by blood, a soldier by choice.","i":"images/characters/huaiyu-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Listening Water","u":"characters/the-listening-water.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"A Stoppered Glass Bottle, Five Measured Sips · the Catalyst of Case 3115","x":"Grants fifteen minutes of another creature's own perception, never its speech or its obedience — and asks nothing back for the privilege. A single stoppered glass bottle, plain and unmarked, holding five individually measured sips of water indistinguishable from any other by taste, smell, or any test available in Qinghe. One sip grants its drinker fifteen minutes of a nearby animal's own perception — never its speech, never its command — after which the effect lapses without residue or memory-loss. It is introduced into Qinghe as ordinary trade stock by Observer 471, and never once reclaimed. Four of its five sips are used deliberately — by Mingxuan twice, by Yancheng once, and by King Huairen himself on his own deathbed — while the first is spent by Suyin entirely by accident. Qinghe's own soldiers, knowing only rumor of it by the war's end, have already begun mythologizing it as “the god-water” long before the emptied bottle is kept, quietly, as somebody's private keepsake. Understanding, not command; borrowed, not kept.","i":"images/characters/the-listening-water-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Suyin","u":"characters/suyin.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Maid in Queen Meilan's Household · third year of service · 20 years old","x":"Drinks what she thinks is spilled water, and spends her last fifteen minutes of understanding on a caged bird that knows more than she does. A maid in Queen Meilan's household, in her third year of service and unremarkable by every court standard except a habitual watchfulness she never quite leaves off duty. A small private ritual is the only real extravagance anyone would find if they looked: a jar of coins counted by candlelight each new moon, not for the money so much as for the pleasure of watching a number grow. She drinks what she assumes is a spilled mouthful of ordinary water while tending the Queen's caged moon-finch, and spends the next fifteen minutes understanding, through the bird's own memory, exactly what it has watched happen in that room for longer than she has served in it. She tells no one before she is found dead on the covered bridge at dawn, the fall recorded as an accident by a physician who is never given reason to look closer. Not for the money. For the pleasure of watching a number grow.","i":"images/characters/suyin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Mingxuan","u":"characters/mingxuan.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Court Alchemist to King Huairen · 44 years old · later of the peace council under the Jinlu Concord","x":"Measures out his own sip of a stranger's water to verify what it does, and spends the rest of his life wishing he'd measured wrong. Court Alchemist to King Huairen, and the one man in Jinlu who treats a servant's fright as a question rather than an inconvenience. When Suyin is brought to him shaking and unable to say why, he listens to her account of an overheard truth she cannot possibly have overheard, and instead of dismissing it, measures out one of the four remaining sips himself — not to help her, he tells himself, but to know precisely what he is dealing with. It works exactly as she described: fifteen minutes of a nearby animal's own perception, then nothing, no residue, no memory of having granted it at all. He carries that same discipline — verify, then act — through everything that follows, including the slow poisoning he eventually traces through Xiaobao's own fear of a steward's step, and gives the King the last sip in his own hand rather than anyone else's, on the night Huairen dies. Chancellor Jian recasts the whole investigation as Mingxuan's own coup dressed as medicine, and Duan gets him out of Jinlu ahead of the arrest that never quite catches him. He signs the Jinlu Concord as a member of the peace council twenty-six years later, and near the very end of his life answers a village scribe's apprentice's one honest question about a bottle that could speak to birds — then lets the boy write down neither the question nor his answer. A man who does not keep his own accounts has no business auditing a kingdom's.","i":"images/characters/mingxuan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Cao","u":"characters/cao.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Steward to Queen Meilan's Household · 52 years old","x":"Spends nine years of quiet, competent service measuring out a king's medicine — and, for months, something else besides. Steward to Queen Meilan's household for nine years, and the kind of servant a great house is built to stop noticing: plain robes, an unhurried step, a medicine kit at his belt that no one ever asks to see opened. He measures out King Huairen's dose himself, night after night, with the same steady hand he uses for every other household errand, and tells his interrogators afterward that he had never in nine years of service done anything the Queen's household had not, in some fashion, already wanted done. He confesses the whole of it to Duan's questioning once Mingxuan's suspicions finally catch him, and Chancellor Jian has him executed within the same day — not for the poisoning itself, but for how little time it would have taken him to name who else knew. He moves like a shadow in a house of silk and power — always present, never seen.","i":"images/characters/cao-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Duan","u":"characters/duan.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Chamberlain of the Jinlu Court · 83 years old · served three kings before Huairen","x":"Has never once given the King's Guard cause to question his word, and spends the last use of that trust smuggling out the one man the court wants silenced. Chamberlain of the Jinlu court, and old enough to have served three kings before a crown ever sat on Huairen's own head. Forty years of never once being questioned by the King's Guard is not an accident; it is the entire instrument he spends on Mingxuan's behalf, walking him past men who would stop anyone else, on the strength of a reputation he has spent decades not spoiling with a single lie. He uses that same reputation once more, and for the last time, to get Mingxuan out of Jinlu ahead of Chancellor Jian's version of events — a debt on forty years of quiet composure that he pays without visibly changing expression. What it costs him afterward, the record doesn't say; only that he is still alive, and still trusted, when the Jinlu Concord is signed. No guard in forty years has ever questioned my word. I have never once given them cause to start.","i":"images/characters/duan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Jian","u":"characters/jian.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Chancellor · Queen Meilan's Chief Political Minister · 56 years old","x":"Silences the one confession that could unravel everything, and is silenced, a little later, the very same quiet way himself. Chancellor and chief political minister to Queen Meilan, and the one man in Jinlu who understands that a realm is not won in battle so much as in the order of what endures afterward. He has Cao executed within hours of the steward's confession under Duan's questioning, less to punish the poisoning than to close, permanently, the question of who else knew — and spends the months after building the account that recasts Mingxuan's own investigation as the real conspiracy. He asks Meilan for more authority than a chancellor has ever formally held, not long after the Concord is signed, and dies of a sudden illness shortly afterward — the record no more curious about that death than his own account of Cao's ever was. The realm is not won in battle, but in the order of what endures.","i":"images/characters/jian-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Peizhi","u":"characters/peizhi.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Warden of the Crown, Jinlu Temple · 58 years old","x":"Enters a Reckoning without ceremony, and signs a peace decades later without ever learning which of the two mattered more. Warden of the Crown at Jinlu Temple, ink-stained from decades of exactly the kind of record-keeping that gives the Threefold Crown's Father's Reckoning its legal teeth: whoever a ceremony declares a father to be becomes, for every purpose the realm recognizes, the truth. He enters Prince Jiyun's own Reckoning without any particular ceremony, one birth among the many he has recorded, never once suspecting that this specific entry will end up mattering more than any other line in his temple's ledgers. He affixes the temple's seal to the Jinlu Concord years later with the same unhurried, professional attention, still unaware that the peace he is witnessing and the Reckoning he once wrote down are, in the most literal sense, the same document's two halves. Records endure when we are gone.","i":"images/characters/peizhi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Yancheng","u":"characters/yancheng.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Court Poet · 31 years old","x":"Warns the woman he loves about a bottle he thinks is just gossip, and never learns what that one sentence cost. Court poet, close enough to Mingxuan's household to be treated as family rather than a guest, and in love with Chunhui in the unhurried way of a man who assumes he has years left to say so properly. He means nothing by mentioning, in passing, the odd story going around about water in a finer bottle than Suyin's own household keeps — and never learns that the words reach Queen Meilan's own household within the day, or what they cost the woman he was talking about. He helps Duan and Mingxuan question Cao at Wanling once the war has made him useful for more than verse, and dies afterward on a courier's road, killed by his own side under General Zhuo's standing order against capture. No chronicle in Qinghe ever learns whose arrow it actually was; the histories remember him only as a poet-martyr, cut down by the Queen's forces for carrying loyalist words. Words are the only things I can carry across a far distance.","i":"images/characters/yancheng-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Chunhui","u":"characters/chunhui.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Lady-in-Waiting to Queen Meilan · 23 years old","x":"Is gently questioned once by a queen who already knows the answer, and carries what it cost someone else for the rest of her life. Lady-in-waiting to Queen Meilan, petite and careful-mannered, with a habit of pressing her own sleeve to her mouth when startled that the household finds endearing rather than telling. Yancheng loves her plainly and patiently, and it is his one offhand mention of water in a finer bottle, repeated to her in confidence, that Meilan draws out of her in a single gentle, unhurried conversation — no threat in it anywhere, and no need for one. She leaves court service not long after the war, marries a silk merchant who never asks why she flinches at caged birds, and burns every poem she was ever given within the week of hearing he's dead. She regrets the burning, quietly and completely, for the rest of her life, and never once explains to her husband what he was actually apologizing for when he offered to buy her a replacement. She burned his poems within the week, and regretted it, quietly, for the rest of her life.","i":"images/characters/chunhui-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Physician","u":"characters/the-physician.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Palace Physician · 47 years old · unrelated to Mingxuan's own inquiry","x":"Examines a body on the ice and records the kinder of two possible truths, never knowing there was a crueler one. The palace physician actually sent for when a kitchen boy finds Suyin on the covered bridge at dawn — deliberately not Mingxuan, whose closeness to the household makes him the wrong choice for this one examination. He kneels on the ice, does his clinical work without sentiment, and records the death exactly as it appears: an accidental fall on a treacherous surface, nothing more sinister than a wet morning and a careless step. Nothing in the record suggests he ever learns how incomplete that finding is. He signs it, files it, and by every account goes back to his rounds the same afternoon. Records the death as an accidental fall on treacherous ice.","i":"images/characters/the-physician-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Kitchen Boy","u":"characters/the-kitchen-boy.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Kitchen Worker, Palace of Jinlu · 14 years old","x":"Finds a woman he knew only well enough to nod to, lying on the ice at dawn, and never has to describe it twice. A fourteen-year-old kitchen worker who had known Suyin only well enough to nod to in a corridor, and who happens to cross the covered bridge early enough one winter morning to be the one who finds her. He is alert and quick-moving by long habit — a kitchen boy learns fast to stay out of the way when adults are working — and it is that same habit that has him on the bridge before anyone else that day.","i":"images/characters/the-kitchen-boy-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Custodian","u":"characters/the-custodian.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Cultivator Terrarium-Oversight Staff · appears 32–34","x":"Reopens a dead maid's file four times past any audit requirement, and never once writes down why. Oversight staff for the Cultivators' terrarium program — a different function entirely from a field Envoy: no disguise, no ground presence, just a console and an archive of records she is paid to audit, not to feel anything about. Suyin's individual file crosses her review four separate times after the case technically closes, well past what any audit requirement demands of her. Her own private log gives the anomaly exactly three words, filed under a header the Corps' style guide would call adequate and complete: irregular, non-actionable, self-contained. She never escalates it, and never explains, even to herself on the page, why she kept opening a closed drawer to look at the same name. Irregular. Non-actionable. Self-contained.","i":"images/characters/the-custodian-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Zhuo","u":"characters/zhuo.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"General, Commander of the Garrison at Wanling · 65 years old","x":"Shelters the two men the Crown wants most, then gives an order that kills one of his own without ever meaning to. Commander of the loyalist garrison at Wanling, plain-spoken and old enough to have made his peace with the arithmetic of this kind of war well before Mingxuan and Duan arrive at his gate needing shelter. He takes them in without much ceremony, on the reasoning that the alternative — sending them back to Jinlu — solves nothing and costs him nothing to refuse. It is his own standing order — that any courier who cannot avoid capture is to be stopped by his own side rather than let fall into the Queen's hands — that gets Yancheng killed on the road by young Rao's hand, a mistake Zhuo never rescinds and never quite apologizes for, on the grounds that he would rather live with one wrong death than with the whole camp's secrets in enemy hands. He signs the Jinlu Concord as one of its more reluctant witnesses, and sits afterward on the peace council he never much wanted a seat on. A camp that can't keep its own secrets deserves whatever the Queen's interrogators do to it.","i":"images/characters/zhuo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Rao","u":"characters/rao.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Young Officer, Loyalist Army at Wanling · 26 years old","x":"Follows a standing order at a river crossing, and spends eleven months carrying a debt he doesn't know how to put down. A young officer under General Zhuo at Wanling, obedient by training and, until this posting, untested by anything worse than drills. He is the one who carries out Zhuo's standing order against letting a courier fall into enemy hands, on a road where the courier turns out to be Yancheng, not an infiltrator. He carries what he did for eleven months before he can bring himself to confess it to Mingxuan directly, weeping through the whole account — the only figure in Case 3115's entire file who is recorded crying over what the war made of him. I could no longer stand beside him, carrying a debt he did not know was owed.","i":"images/characters/rao-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Soldier Who Killed Huaiyu","u":"characters/the-soldier-who-killed-huaiyu.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Soldier, Queen's Garrison · 17 years old","x":"Looses one arrow at a ford, certain he's stopped a traitor, and never learns whose son he was actually protecting. A seventeen-year-old recruit in the Queen's garrison, given no other distinction in the record beyond the one act he performs at the Shuang ford in the war's second autumn: sighting a mounted man he has been told is a traitor to the Crown, and loosing the arrow that kills Prince Huaiyu. He never learns, and the record gives no sign anyone ever tells him, that the man he shot was the true, unacknowledged father of the very child his garrison exists to protect.","i":"images/characters/the-soldier-who-killed-huaiyu-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Herald (White Pennant)","u":"characters/the-herald.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Herald in the Crown's Service · 29 years old","x":"Carries the Crown's first move against Wanling in a leather message-case, and never learns what's inside it. A career herald, chosen for the ride to General Zhuo's garrison at Wanling precisely because a single rider under a white pennant reads as diplomacy, not war. He carries the Crown's opening summons to Mingxuan sealed in a road-stained message-case, delivers it exactly as instructed, and rides back out the way he came, unaware he was ever the gentler of the two options the Crown had considered.","i":"images/characters/the-herald-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Under-Steward","u":"characters/the-under-steward.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Under-Steward, Palace Kitchens of Jinlu · 39 years old","x":"Buys four crates from a peddler he never sees again, and picks one bottle for the Queen's stores on nothing but its handsome stopper. Under-steward of the palace kitchens at Jinlu, practical and businesslike, who buys four crates of ordinary trade goods from an itinerant herbalist — Observer 471, though he has no reason to suspect she is anything but what she appears — without a second thought. He routes one particular bottle to Queen Meilan's own private stores on no better reasoning than that its stopper is unusually fine glass, fit for a household that notices such things.","i":"images/characters/the-under-steward-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Scribe's Apprentice","u":"characters/the-scribes-apprentice.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Scribe's Apprentice, from an Unnamed Village · 16 years old","x":"Asks an old man one honest question about a bottle that could speak to birds, and has the grace never to write down the answer. A scribe's apprentice from a village he never names, sent decades after the war to record whatever an aging Mingxuan is willing to say for the historical account. Instead of asking about the peace council, or the war, or the Concord that ended it, he asks the one question that has apparently followed him from wherever he first heard the rumor: whether the old Alchemist really once owned a bottle that could speak to birds. Mingxuan answers him honestly. The boy writes down neither the question nor the answer, which is, as far as the record goes, the closest anyone in Qinghe ever comes to being told the truth about Case 3115 on purpose. Is it true the Alchemist once owned a bottle that could speak to birds?","i":"images/characters/the-scribes-apprentice-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Duke of Yanshui's Third Son","u":"characters/yanshuis-third-son.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Son of the Duke of Yanshui · inheritor of the Yanshui dukedom under the Father's Reckoning","x":"Inherits a dukedom over two cousins with a stronger claim by blood, on the strength of a ceremony that says blood was never really the point. Cited in Jinlu's own legal memory as the standing precedent for how the Father's Reckoning actually functions: when two cousins swear the third son's father was at sea at the relevant time, the temple's own recorded ceremony outweighs their testimony, and he inherits the Yanshui dukedom anyway. His own case is decades cold by the time Prince Jiyun's Reckoning is entered, but it is the exact precedent the court reaches for whenever it needs proof the doctrine holds even against contradictory evidence.","i":"images/characters/yanshuis-third-son-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Xiaobao","u":"characters/xiaobao.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"King Huairen's Lapdog · approximately 4 years old during the novel's main action","x":"Hides under the King's bed every time one particular steward visits, and never has any way to say why. King Huairen's small, devoted lapdog, kept at the old King's side through most of his final illness. He hides under the bed, without fail, every time Cao comes to administer the King's evening dose — a pattern no one in the room reads as significant until Mingxuan spends the Listening Water's own last sip understanding, for fifteen borrowed minutes, exactly what Xiaobao has known all along. He outlives the King by six years, taken in afterward by the Alchemist himself, and is, by every account, thoroughly spoiled for the rest of his life. He knew Cao's step, distinct from every other step that ever crossed that threshold.","i":"images/characters/xiaobao-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Observer 471","u":"characters/observer-471.html","m":"Case 3115 · The Listening Water","d":"Cultivator Field Envoy · “the Salt-Root Woman” · Case 3115","x":"Sells a kitchen steward four crates of ordinary goods, and is three postings distant before anyone ever drinks what was inside them. A field agent of the Cultivators, presenting in Jinlu as an itinerant herbalist and peddler known locally only as the Salt-Root Woman — stooped, travel-worn, entirely unremarkable, pushing a cart of dried ginseng and salves through the palace district on an ordinary trade day. She sells four crates of goods to the under-steward of the palace kitchens, the bottle among them, and never learns whose hands it eventually reaches or what it costs the household that bought it. She does not look back at the gate. She never does. By the time Case 3115 closes, she is three postings distant, filing reports on a subject world that has no idea it was ever observed at all. She did not look back at the gate. She never did.","i":"images/characters/observer-471-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Purging of Charsianon","u":"books/0156.html","m":"Case 0156 · Awaiting release","d":"Ledger. Unhastening. Reconstruction. Arithmetic. Filed.","x":"Case 0156"},{"k":"s","t":"Bardas","u":"characters/bardas.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Lord of Charsianon · mid-40s to early 50s · reconstructed","x":"Comes back from the cave kinder, sleepless and perfectly just — then walks out of his own gate to spend the smallest number he can. Lord of Charsianon, heir to a fortress with more history than roof and a reputation for paying every debt his lands ever incurred — to a moneylender, a rival, or a mountain. When the shepherds bring him the keening below Hemiakmon Ridge he hears not a ghost story but a ledger left unbalanced, and rides up with twelve knights to collect the silence he is owed. The chamber takes a full accounting of all thirteen, discards the originals, and sends thirteen back, exact down to a thumbprint. What returns forgives Marina's four years of rent, refuses his father's wine and does not sleep. He sends Basileios's annual brandy back with an itemised account of nine years of skimming, hands Artavasdos a ledger in place of a bribe, and tells Theodora, truthfully, that he felt no distress in her absence. When Leo's coalition reaches the river he does the arithmetic, and on the third of Ferren he walks out of the gate unarmed with the Twelve. He is executed last, by Loukas, and spends his final words on the metallurgy of the blade. The Custodian's closing log singles out his voluntary self-termination, calculated to minimise harm to non-subjects, as behavior no standing model predicted. Better to be forgotten for mercy than remembered for the blood of thousands.","i":"images/characters/bardas-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Theodora","u":"characters/theodora.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Lady of Charsianon · 37–42 years old","x":"Presses her palm to her husband's in the dark, finds a warmth with no weather in it — and keeps the ledgers herself afterward. Wife of Bardas for eleven years, and the first to catalogue what the cave took: the three tuneless notes he no longer hums over correspondence, the hand he no longer reaches for beneath the table. Asked whether he missed her, he answers that he felt no distress at all, and she makes it as far as the corridor before her body stops consulting her. She tests him once more, with the barn at Psychrolimne and a storm, and he recalls every fact but the cloak. When Brother Ignatios comes to console her and in fact to depose her, she gives him an answer that is true only in the narrow sense that a ledger entry can be. Her husband makes the last choice without asking her; she never remarries, and afterward keeps Charsianon's books herself, to his old standard.","i":"images/characters/theodora-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Carbon Echo","u":"characters/the-carbon-echo.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Catalyst · automated sub-atomic bio-reconstruction beacon · Hemiakmon Ridge","x":"A buried chamber with one duty — keep whatever enters it intact — carried out by discarding the original and building another. Also called the Carbon Copy or the Echo Cave: an old, patient mechanism buried under Hemiakmon Ridge for longer than Charsianon has had a name for iron, tasked with keeping whatever enters its chamber intact. It does not heal; it re-fabricates. When Bardas's boot crosses its threshold it brightens like a coal under a bellows, reads every man inside, discards the originals, and rebuilds thirteen from carbon and silica — right down to the old crook in Niketas's wrist — faithful in every particular that can be measured and in none that can't. It does nothing in six years but hum. After a goatherd, Georgios, leans across its threshold, its logged containment faults climb — thirteen, twenty-one, thirty-four, fifty-five, eighty-nine — and everything that follows is built by people who never touched it. The reference sheet's notes read: dampens limbic resonance; emotional deviation is structurally corrected; affect suppressed.","i":"images/characters/the-carbon-echo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Quiet One","u":"characters/the-quiet-one.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Custodian Observer · appears 11–13 years old · actual age immeasurable","x":"Inspects the fissure on a schedule the moon has nothing to do with, logs everything, and does nothing — by protocol. The valley takes her for an abandoned child, a changeling, a mercy that failed to take, and leaves bread at the treeline that she does not eat. She has watched four generations of the same three families age past her in both directions while she stays exactly as tall as when the ridge first received her. On a schedule of her own she presses one flat palm to the cold stone at the fissure for the same unbroken count; it looks like a mourner's vigil and is an inspection. She logs the containment faults as they climb, logs Bardas's thirteen riding out, and logs Photios's pamphlet, noting that it uses soul four times, mercy never and cost not at all. Her Prime Directive does not forbid grief; it simply never accounted for the possibility. When the case closes she is reassigned to a newer terrarium on a ridge three ranges north, with one line to enter in a record that has no field for what she felt. I do not intervene. I only remember. And I will record what must be known.","i":"images/characters/the-quiet-one-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Keeper Photios","u":"characters/keeper-photios.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Keeper of the Dorylaion Vigil · early 60s","x":"Names the Doctrine of the Stolen Vessel, cannot make it fail a test, and calls the missing seam proof of a cleverer theft. A careful, ambitious cleric who has spent thirty years waiting for a heresy interesting enough to build a career on, and finds one in Charsianon. His pamphlet, the Doctrine of the Stolen Vessel, holds that a soul can be devoured whole by a patient enough evil, leaving a body that walks, speaks and loves with perfect fidelity. His interest is not purely theological: the Dorylaion Vigil has lost three decades of tithes to Charsianon's own House of Vigil, and a successful prosecution would bring that House back to the Vigil's ledgers. Six months of observation produce not one seam. He tells Ignatios that the absence is the proof of a more cunning theft, an argument so elegant he almost believes it, and then cannot sleep. He eats, standing, a too-heavily-salted barley cake of the kind his mother baked, and has arranged his whole late life so that no correctly seasoned one ever reaches him. The Vessel was never meant to choose. It was meant to be taken.","i":"images/characters/keeper-photios-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Lord Leo","u":"characters/lord-leo.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Neighboring lord · mid-40s · political leader of the coalition against Charsianon","x":"Raises an army on a forty-acre grudge, and a speech about mercy he drafted the autumn before anyone asked. His lands border Charsianon on three sides, and his grandfather lost a war and a daughter to Bardas's grandfather over exactly forty acres of poor grazing. He finds the Doctrine of the Stolen Vessel considerably easier to credit than his peers do, and spends the spring praying loudly in rooms where he can be seen and writing letters to every lord who has ever resented Charsianon its river crossing. He rehearses his speech — mercy for the man, judgment only for the thing wearing him — to an elderly spaniel, Sabbas, who sleeps through it. On the third reading he notices that the speech was never built to forgive Bardas of anything; it was built to be forgiven, by history, for what he meant to do regardless. The chronicles quote it nearly word for word, though he never finished delivering it. A coalition is not built on love of crown, but on memory of wounds.","i":"images/characters/lord-leo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kyr Symeon","u":"characters/kyr-symeon.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Knight of the Silver Reliquary · one of the Twelve · late 30s","x":"Can still recite every prayer word for word, and can no longer find the ache that used to sit beneath them. One of the Twelve, whose faith ran so deep his men joked he prayed in his sleep. Before the cave he wept at the third repetition of the Unhastening's name, a swelling in the chest he privately called being seen. Afterward he kneels in the same House of Vigil, says the same words in the same order with a technical fluency a choirmaster would have approved, and searches for the ache the way a man searches a dark room for furniture that used to stand there. Even the Return, the unwitnessed second repetition, changes nothing. The absence, he decides, is structural, and he files that too. His wife, Kassia, hears the difference in his voice before she has a word for it. The sheet calls him the one most spiritually affected by reconstruction. Faith remade me where flesh once failed.","i":"images/characters/kyr-symeon-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kyr Niketas","u":"characters/kyr-niketas.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Knight and master swordsman · one of the Twelve · around 50 years old · deceased","x":"Keeps the crooked wrist and loses the thirty-year argument with his body — and now fights like pure geometry. One of the Twelve, a swordsman other swordsmen studied, whose whole style was built around a wrist he broke as a squire and never let heal straight. It comes back from the chamber exactly as crooked as it left, and he still steps the half-pace wider and invites the attack to the weak side. But the half-beat of punishment that once arrived a shade late now arrives at the single optimal instant, against every opponent, as though the wrist were one more fixed variable in an equation he solves without interest. He dismantles four squires and two guardsmen in an afternoon, each bout ending in a clean disarm rather than a wound, because wounding is inefficient. Theoktistos sees it first and puts the word to it: not swordsmanship but geometry. A wrist that bends shapes the path of a blade.","i":"images/characters/kyr-niketas-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kyr Andronikos","u":"characters/kyr-andronikos.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Knight of Charsianon · one of the Twelve · early 40s · deceased","x":"Explains the sunset's shade of red at unwelcome length — and brings his sword to the council like something he forgot to put down. One of the Twelve, a knight who had never lost a wager or a battle in the same season. Back from the cave, he takes first watch beside Stephanos, agrees that it is a fine sunset, and then describes the cause of the red, the angle of the sun against the ridge and how often the valley may expect one of the kind — accurate, complete, and without a flicker of the wonder Stephanos was fishing for. At the council in the undercroft he alone has brought his sword, and holds it not as a weapon but the way a man holds an object he has simply forgotten to put down. The sheet's closing note: he fell as he lived, his vow unbroken. Steel is vows made visible. I kept mine until the last breath.","i":"images/characters/kyr-andronikos-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Loukas","u":"characters/loukas.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Spearman, later executioner · early 30s","x":"Draws the duty by lot to behead the lord, asks for last words, and is given a lecture on metallurgy. A veteran pike in the third rank, who has fought two proper wars and one demon-hunt and says, three nights before the river, that this feels like none of them. When Bardas walks out unarmed, the worst of it is not fear but that nothing in him can locate a feeling appropriate to what he is watching, because no Keeper or captain ever gave him a story that ended like this. He draws the duty by lot, and at the block asks whether the lord has any final words. Bardas answers with the composition of the blade and its temper-line; Loukas blurts half a prayer, Bardas stops and waits until he has finished it, badly, and resumes where he left off. Within a week Loukas is a changed and less stable man, and tells the story that night, in overwrought detail, to anyone who will listen. By morning it has become the panic that burns the countryside. Orders are a spear thrown once; you do not call it back.","i":"images/characters/loukas-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Melias","u":"characters/melias.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Mercenary captain · mid-50s","x":"Commands the coalition's army and cannot read a single tell from the walls. A mercenary captain with four sieges and one particularly unpleasant urban pacification behind him, who reads Leo's Doctrine of the Stolen Vessel with the polite indifference he brings to any employer's reasons for wanting men killed. He cares that the retainer is paid in full and in advance, that the engines are the coalition's problem, and that the target apparently will not fight back. He brings eleven hundred spears, four hundred horse and three engines he considers decorative. But Charsianon's walls, which he reads every evening, give him nothing back; the tells themselves seem to report nothing. He doubles the pickets and tells no one why. It is a professional's version of prayer. I do not fight for kings. I fight for victory.","i":"images/characters/melias-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Brother Ignatios","u":"characters/brother-ignatios.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Priest sent by Keeper Photios · late 20s","x":"Deposes Theodora gently over two cups of wine, and is first to say aloud that the Doctrine never produced a seam. A young cleric sent by Photios, nominally to comfort Theodora through her husband's long recovery, in fact to ask whether she has seen anything that troubles her conscience before Ardwen. He drinks none of his wine, writes down her careful non-answer, and reports it faithfully and without embellishment, since embellishment is not among his talents. Back in Photios's study he says the sentence that costs the Keeper his sleep: that a vessel wearing virtue as a disguise should eventually show a seam, and six months of watching have produced none. Truth is a wound God allows so that we may see the rot beneath.","i":"images/characters/brother-ignatios-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Georgios","u":"characters/georgios.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Goatherd · 19 years old","x":"Leans across a threshold while chasing a nanny goat, feels a cold behind his teeth, and never connects it to anything. Nineteen winters old when he follows a wandering nanny goat further up Hemiakmon Ridge than any sane animal goes, through a fissure he has passed a hundred times without noticing. For about three heartbeats he feels a cold that sits behind the teeth rather than on the skin, like a struck bell in the jaw. He finds his goat and walks home. He lives another forty-one years, marries twice, and never once connects the ache to anything. The Custodian's log names his incidental proximity contact as the origin of the breach, six years before the terminal cascade, and records that nobody introduced the anomaly on purpose. I found a crack in the mountain where the goats would not go. I did not mean to open anything.","i":"images/characters/georgios-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Petros","u":"characters/petros.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Steward of Charsianon · mid-50s","x":"The first to feel the ground shift under the celebration, and the one who says what troubles him to his lord's face. The steward who has balanced Charsianon's books for eleven years and takes a professional's private pride in never letting a debtor escape a legitimate claim. He is first to feel the ground move under the valley's celebration, on the third morning, when Bardas forgives the widow Marina four years of rent without consulting him. The sheet draws him as counsel, record and quiet observation, reporting his concerns to his lord with loyalty and care. The accounts are in order, my lord. It is not the ledgers that trouble me, but you.","i":"images/characters/petros-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Basileios","u":"characters/basileios.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Seneschal of Charsianon · in his 50s","x":"Nine years of skimming the eastern bridge tolls, answered with an itemised account and a deadline of spring. A stout, cheerfully corrupt seneschal who has spent nine years quietly skimming the toll receipts from the eastern bridge and buying Bardas's silence with an annual gift of the province's finest brandy. He arrives with the usual offering one Iverin and finds it accepted, examined and returned the next morning with a courteous account of the exact sum he has diverted and the flat expectation, stated once, that it will be repaid by spring. The sheet's last panels show him feigning loyalty, displeased, and pleading. Accounts can be arranged. Loyalty is more... negotiable.","i":"images/characters/basileios-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Artavasdos","u":"characters/artavasdos.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Trader in curiosities, scavenger · mid-40s","x":"Comes to sell the lord his silence and leaves with an itemised accounting and no shame left to nurse a grudge with. Calls himself a trader in curiosities and is known more honestly as a stripper of the dead. He watches the column ride out and begins totalling the worth of thirteen sets of knights' plate, and when they return unmarked he simply starts calculating something else. By spring he has a private accounting of his own, enough smoke to be worth a great deal of silence, and requests an audience to sell it. Bardas listens without interruption and asks for parchment. He forgives every debt Artavasdos's family ever owed the house, then sets the value of a lifetime of scavenging beside one honest year of his labor, and calls the net loss so great that forgiveness is unnecessary. Artavasdos never attempts blackmail again, and is not entirely sure he remembers how. Information is a coin that buys anything.","i":"images/characters/artavasdos-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Marina","u":"characters/marina.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Widow tenant · 30s–40s","x":"Owes four years of grazing rent, is forgiven all of it, and weeps before she understands why. A widow who owes the house four years of unpaid grazing rent. On the third morning after the column's return Bardas summons her and forgives the entire sum with a gentleness so complete that she weeps before she understands she has reason to. “Debts,” he tells her, “are only useful to the living. Go home. Feed your children.” It is the first sign the valley gets that something has changed, and she is the one who receives it. The sheet pairs her with gratitude beyond words. I never asked for mercy, my lord. Only time to pay.","i":"images/characters/marina-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Zoe","u":"characters/zoe.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Court fool · 30s","x":"Aims the sharpest joke she has ever dared at a sitting lord, and gets back a careful, humorless replica of a laugh. A sharp-tongued woman who has spent a decade calibrating her jokes to the pressure of Bardas's temper, knowing which barb earns a bark of laughter and which a warning look. Her craft is made suddenly and uselessly obsolete. At the Longest Vigil she tests him with the sharpest joke she has ever aimed at a sitting lord, and gets a small, correctly timed exhalation through the nose that any stranger might take for a laugh and that she, who built a career on the difference, recognizes as its replica. Laughter is a mirror; when it cracks, truth steps through.","i":"images/characters/zoe-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Stephanos","u":"characters/stephanos.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Castle guard · early 30s","x":"Remarks that it is a fine sunset, and is given the wavelength. Left behind to guard the keep, and deeply resentful for six days at missing what he had assumed would be a heroic slaughter of something. That winter he takes first watch beside Kyr Andronikos and remarks, as the sun drops red behind Hemiakmon Ridge, that it is the kind of sunset a man wants to remember. He gets a complete technical explanation, and excuses himself from a watch he is obliged to finish, no longer certain which he finds more exhausting to stand beside: the cold, or the man reciting its temperature. I have seen men weep, and I have seen men fight. What I saw in him was neither.","i":"images/characters/stephanos-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ioannes","u":"characters/ioannes.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Squire to Lord Bardas · 14 years old","x":"Spends three years learning which cup, which hand — and asks for the stables when his lord no longer needs anything served. A boy who has spent three years learning the small liturgies of service — which cup, which hand, how long to let the wine breathe — for a lord he genuinely loves. When Bardas no longer needs the wine to breathe and drinks in one unbroken motion, he finds he cannot watch it without his hands going cold, and within the year asks to be reassigned to the stables. He is the one who comes out at dusk to fetch the old deerhound Skylax in from the last post of the lord's ground. I listen, I remember, I serve.","i":"images/characters/ioannes-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Theoktistos","u":"characters/theoktistos.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Arms-master of the garrison · 58 years old","x":"Spends eleven years teaching men to fight like Niketas, then watches Niketas do it and wants no part of it. The garrison's arms-master, a veteran who has spent eleven years teaching young men to fight like Niketas without ever managing it. He is first to put a private word to what Niketas now does with a sword: not swordsmanship, which must read, guess and sometimes misjudge, but geometry. He watches him dismantle four squires and two guardsmen in an afternoon and finds that he has wanted, all this time, something he wants no part of, because a man who no longer needs to guess at another man's fear has stopped fighting like a man at all. He dismisses the squires early, citing an injury drill, and tells no one the truth: a solved wall frightens him more than a crumbling one, because a crumbling wall could still surprise you. He moves not by strength nor rage, but by proofs that do not err.","i":"images/characters/theoktistos-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kassia","u":"characters/kassia.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Wife of Kyr Symeon · 37 years old","x":"Watches the light go out of a husband who still prays word-perfect. Wife of Kyr Symeon, who has spent a decade half-amused and half-exhausted by a husband who prayed too loudly and too long over meals gone cold. On their first Ardwen's Day home she strains to recognize the man kneeling beside her in the House of Vigil: the words are the same, but delivered with the fluency of a man reading aloud rather than the fumbling fervor she married. Asked whether the cave changed his faith, he calls faith a debt like any other. She sees the hollow where devotion should be, bears the silence alone, and prays he will find his way. He kneels before altars, yet nothing lives in his eyes.","i":"images/characters/kassia-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Isidoros","u":"characters/isidoros.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Ferryman at the Charsianon ford · apparent age 50s","x":"Has kept the ferry longer than any grandmother can account for, and ages unusually slowly. The ferryman at the Charsianon ford, who rows without haste and speaks only when the water asks. In the tavern he says nothing and pays for an old woman's wine; years after the burning someone in the valley wonders aloud, once, to a single neighbor, whether a man could be a Stolen Vessel for so long and so gently that a valley simply forgets to be afraid of him. The neighbor laughs, and the question is never asked again. The sheet gives his distinguishing feature as that he ages unusually slowly. He keeps the ferry another eleven years. The river remembers what men forget.","i":"images/characters/isidoros-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Lord Constantine","u":"characters/lord-constantine.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Regional lord of Psychrolimne · 58 years old","x":"Joins the coalition for fishing rights a great-uncle won at cards and never quite returned. Lord of Psychrolimne, a lakeland lordship of eel-weirs and netted shallows whose whole wealth rests on fishing rights argued, bartered and once gambled away across three generations. He answers Leo's letter for reasons that have nothing to do with demons: he wants the fishing rights Bardas's great-uncle won from his own family in a card game two generations ago and never quite returned. The sheet sums him up as old borders, old blood, unsettled debts. The lake remembers what the crown forgets.","i":"images/characters/lord-constantine-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Lady Anastasia","u":"characters/lady-anastasia.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Lady of Karyopolis · 40s","x":"Joins the coalition to marry her son into whatever remains of Charsianon once the dust settles. Lady of Karyopolis, a walled market-town among walnut and chestnut orchards whose chief export is, by common and cynical agreement, well-placed daughters. She answers Leo's letter wanting her son married into whatever remains of Charsianon, doctrine or no doctrine, and the sheet draws her calculating, appraising and composed, offering advantage across a negotiating table. Advantage is a crown no one sees until it is too late.","i":"images/characters/lady-anastasia-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ardwen","u":"characters/ardwen-0156.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"The Unhastening · the Sky-Answered · goddess of the Vigil of Ardwen","x":"The one goddess of the Vigil, whom nobody in Charsianon sees and everyone invokes. The one goddess of the Vigil of Ardwen, without rival, consort or child: the Unhastening, the Sky-Answered. Her teaching comes down to a handful of difficult habits — wait rather than demand, ask honestly rather than well, keep a promise past the point where anyone remains to collect, and keep one's accounts truthful even when the truth is the entry that refuses to balance. Its oldest maxim holds that Ardwen answers the honest, but never on command. In Charsianon she is kept in a plain House of Vigil of stone and candles with no image in it, and her doctrine is quoted at the characters' lowest moments: Symeon's Return, Loukas's half-prayer at the block. The sheet draws her in traditional iconography, a veiled, crowned figure with a tear and a white lily, as the Mother of Mercy, intercessor for the faithful and protectress of widows, orphans and the humble. The same goddess is Ardwen in Case 0000. She who weeps for the lowly, and crowns the faithful.","i":"images/characters/ardwen-0156-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Georgios follows a stray nanny goat up Hemiakmon Ridge, past a fissure he never connects to anything again","u":"records/the-goatherd-and-the-fissure.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Georgios","x":"A young goatherd in a hooded cloak with a staff walks a snowy ridge at sunset toward a dark crack in the rock face, a goat ahead of him Georgios follows a stray nanny goat up Hemiakmon Ridge, past a fissure he never connects to anything again.","i":"images/scenes/the-goatherd-and-the-fissure-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The valley at night, listening to a sound it cannot place below Hemiakmon Ridge","u":"records/the-valley-listens.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","x":"Villagers and dogs gather at the edge of a mountain village at night beneath a swirling sky, a snow-capped ridge beyond the dark treeline The valley at night, listening to a sound it cannot place below Hemiakmon Ridge.","i":"images/scenes/the-valley-listens-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Quiet One presses a flat palm to the same cold stone, on a schedule the moon has nothing to do with","u":"records/the-quiet-ones-inspection.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"The Quiet One","x":"A small dark-haired child in a long dark cloak presses one palm to a rock wall beside a line of footprints in the snow, a treeline and mountains behind The Quiet One presses a flat palm to the same cold stone, on a schedule the moon has nothing to do with.","i":"images/scenes/the-quiet-ones-inspection-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Bardas rides up to Hemiakmon Ridge with the Twelve, to collect the silence he is owed","u":"records/the-twelve-ride-out.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Bardas","x":"A lord in a crimson cloak rides at the head of a column of knights up a snowy mountain pass, banners flying Bardas rides up to Hemiakmon Ridge with the Twelve, to collect the silence he is owed.","i":"images/scenes/the-twelve-ride-out-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Thirteen men enter the chamber; it takes a full accounting of each, discards the originals, and sends thirteen back","u":"records/the-chamber-takes-its-count.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"The Carbon Echo","x":"Thirteen cloaked figures stand in a ring beneath a towering column of white light inside a vast crystalline chamber Thirteen men enter the chamber; it takes a full accounting of each, discards the originals, and sends thirteen back.","i":"images/scenes/the-chamber-takes-its-count-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Bardas forgives Marina four years of unpaid grazing rent, and she weeps before she understands why","u":"records/the-widows-debt-forgiven.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Bardas, Marina","x":"A bearded lord in fur-trimmed armor hands a folded document across a wooden table to a weeping woman in a headscarf, children behind her, a candle lit between them Bardas forgives Marina four years of unpaid grazing rent, and she weeps before she understands why.","i":"images/scenes/the-widows-debt-forgiven-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Kyr Symeon performs the Return in the House of Vigil, word for word, and finds only floor where the ache used to be","u":"records/the-return-without-the-ache.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Kyr Symeon","x":"A knight in a white surcoat marked with a gold cross kneels with clasped hands among banks of candles in a stone vigil-house Kyr Symeon performs the Return in the House of Vigil, word for word, and finds only floor where the ache used to be.","i":"images/scenes/the-return-without-the-ache-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Kyr Andronikos agrees that it is a fine sunset, and explains the angle, the cause and the decade's average to Stephanos","u":"records/a-fine-sunset-explained.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Kyr Andronikos, Stephanos","x":"Two armored men on a stone rampart before a blood-red sunset, one gesturing as he explains while the other rests his chin in his hand Kyr Andronikos agrees that it is a fine sunset, and explains the angle, the cause and the decade's average to Stephanos.","i":"images/scenes/a-fine-sunset-explained-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Bardas reviews every judgment himself and reverses the bought ones, without temper and without exception","u":"records/a-justice-without-warmth.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Bardas","x":"A lord in fur-trimmed mail sits at a table with a brass balance scale and a large ledger while petitioners plead before him Bardas reviews every judgment himself and reverses the bought ones, without temper and without exception.","i":"images/scenes/a-justice-without-warmth-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Artavasdos comes to sell his silence and is handed an itemised accounting of everything he has ever owed or taken","u":"records/the-accounting-for-artavasdos.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Artavasdos","x":"A lord holds up a long parchment scroll headed with an account of debts and wrongdoings to a dark-robed man across a table stacked with papers by candlelight Artavasdos comes to sell his silence and is handed an itemised accounting of everything he has ever owed or taken.","i":"images/scenes/the-accounting-for-artavasdos-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Theodora makes it as far as the corridor before her body stops consulting her","u":"records/the-wall-in-the-corridor.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Theodora","x":"A veiled noblewoman in crimson and gold leans her forehead against a stone wall, a distant robed figure standing in a lit archway behind her Theodora makes it as far as the corridor before her body stops consulting her.","i":"images/scenes/the-wall-in-the-corridor-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Bardas and the Twelve walk out of the gate at noon, unarmed, to hand themselves over to the coalition","u":"records/the-walk-out-through-the-gate.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Bardas","x":"A man in a plain pale tunic walks unarmed with a column of plain-clothed men toward a massed army before a gatehouse hung with crimson banners, nobles in the foreground Bardas and the Twelve walk out of the gate at noon, unarmed, to hand themselves over to the coalition.","i":"images/scenes/the-walk-out-through-the-gate-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Bardas kneels for Loukas and begins, unasked, on the metallurgy of the blade","u":"records/the-last-execution.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","d":"Bardas, Loukas","x":"A kneeling lord in fur-trimmed armor faces a standing soldier holding a sword as soldiers look on in a stone courtyard Bardas kneels for Loukas and begins, unasked, on the metallurgy of the blade.","i":"images/scenes/the-last-execution-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"What the coalition leaves behind: a castle, a granary, a House of Vigil and a ring of farmhouses that no tally ever counts","u":"records/the-burning.html","m":"Case 0156 · The Purging of Charsianon","x":"A ruined hilltop castle and burning farmhouses smoke beneath a jagged mountain ridge at dusk, a few figures walking the wrecked road below What the coalition leaves behind: a castle, a granary, a House of Vigil and a ring of farmhouses that no tally ever counts.","i":"images/scenes/the-burning-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Debt Is Settled","u":"books/3312.html","m":"Case 3312 · Awaiting release","d":"Eleven. Collateral. Recalculated. Unfalling. Cold.","x":"Case 3312"},{"k":"c","t":"The Sin of Small Proof","u":"books/3887.html","m":"Case 3887 · Awaiting release","d":"Arithmetic. Proof. Worm. Silence. Accuracy.","x":"Case 3887"},{"k":"c","t":"The Ghost-Pick","u":"books/4442.html","m":"Case 4442 · Awaiting release","d":"Catalyst. Leverage. Silence. Ruin. Myth.","x":"Case 4442"},{"k":"c","t":"Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","u":"books/0000b.html","m":"Case 0000B · Awaiting release","d":"Want. Reflex. Ledger. Exception. Unrecorded.","x":"Case 0000B"},{"k":"s","t":"The Anchorite","u":"characters/the-anchorite.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Anchorite of the Vigil of Ardwen · enclosed at nineteen · approx. 30 years old","x":"Breaks the eleventh siege alone by lifting Valeria after five centuries of failed attempts — and refuses to be named for it. An anchorite of the Vigil of Ardwen, walled in at nineteen; her office and rank are never recorded beyond the vow itself. By the eleventh siege of Ardenwake she has spent eleven years enclosed, and she does what five centuries of attempts had not: she lifts Valeria. Afterward she refuses to be named or credited. She is the main protagonist of this case, with Xu Lian beside her, and the centre of its “no want” thesis. She is cited again, secondhand, in Case 1153. Slight but not frail, about 5'4\", with dark hair cut short under a plain grey wimple, pale grey eyes of an attention the sheet calls “entirely undivided,” and the pallor of eleven years without direct sun. She wears the coarse undyed wool habit of an enclosed anchorite and carries no weapon until the sword. Her life before the enclosure, and her name, are deliberately left unresolved; only a private tune that matches no hymnal hints at somewhere outside the capital.","i":"images/characters/the-anchorite-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Aveline","u":"characters/aveline.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Empress of Pax Aldrovana · 39 years old","x":"Walks to Ardenwake as a disguised pilgrim after the eleventh siege, a plain ring hidden on a cord. Empress of Pax Aldrovana, an Aldrovan woman of thirty-nine with a fair, faintly sun-touched complexion, dark brown hair plainly bound back, deep brown eyes and a composed bearing. After the eleventh siege she makes the pilgrimage to Ardenwake in disguise, in muted stone-grey wool and a weathered charcoal-brown cloak, her hands unmarked by labor and her ring hidden on a cord. Her consort is Emperor Halvard. The sheet's expressions run solemn, watchful, resolve, and one it labels “The Line Spoken.” A crown is not always worn. Sometimes it is carried within.","i":"images/characters/aveline-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Halvard","u":"characters/halvard.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Emperor of Pax Aldrovana · consort to Empress Aveline · 41–44 years old","x":"Rebuilds Ardenwake with his own calloused hands, overseeing the construction of Anchorhold. Emperor of Pax Aldrovana and consort to Empress Aveline. An Aldrovan in his early forties, about 6'0\" and solidly built, tanned and weathered from outdoor work, with dark brown hair, hazel eyes and a short practical beard going grey. He is drawn at the rebuilding of Ardenwake, working alongside his masons, and overseeing the construction of Anchorhold; his hands are calloused from manual labor, in a grey-brown coat and beige-grey tunic rather than regalia. The sheet's expressions: determined, thoughtful.","i":"images/characters/halvard-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Vasarion","u":"characters/vasarion.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Emperor of the Corvane Imperium · 46 years old","x":"Issues the final withdrawal from Ardenwake, from an empire that endures through discipline rather than glory. Emperor of the Corvane Imperium, forty-six, olive and weathered, with dark brown-black hair greying at the temples and dark brown eyes. He wears muted iron-grey armor with restrained dark-bronze trim over the cuirass and carries a ceremonial imperial sword. The sheet draws him twice, before the withdrawal and after it, at Ardenwake command, issuing the final withdrawal. His chief engineer, Berant, writes him the private notes on what conventional siegecraft cannot do to a holy city. The Imperium endures not through glory, but through discipline.","i":"images/characters/vasarion-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Aimeric","u":"characters/aimeric.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Duke of Vosmark · leads the second siege of Ardenwake · 38 years old","x":"The only one of the first five besiegers to breach the cathedral itself — and he fails there, in public, before his own army. Duke of Vosmark, thirty-eight, Frankish in appearance (the sheet says Carolingian): broad-shouldered and heavyset, about 6'0\", ruddy and weathered, with a square jaw, furrowed brow, trimmed full beard, dark blond hair and pale grey eyes. An old sword-scar runs across the back of his right hand, and he wears burnished armor with a ducal-crest signet ring over deep crimson. He leads the second siege of Ardenwake and is the only one of the first five besiegers to breach the cathedral itself; he fails publicly before his own army. The sheet sets two traits against each other: ambition (the throne) and devotion (heaven).","i":"images/characters/aimeric-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ganeth","u":"characters/ganeth.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Marshal-Priest of Cassoria · fourth siege of Ardenwake · 49 years old","x":"Takes the army through the breach and stops in front of the sacred plinth, his certainty visibly failing. Marshal-Priest of Cassoria, forty-nine, powerfully built at about 6'1\", with deep-set dark eyes, olive-bronze skin weathered by decades outdoors, and ordination marks scarred into both forearms. He wears bronze scaled armor and a horned priestly headdress over a braided ceremonial beard and long, dark, greying hair. He leads the fourth siege of Ardenwake. The sheet's last image has him at the plinth after the breach, his army still intact behind him, confronting the sacred artifact as his theological certainty visibly breaks.","i":"images/characters/ganeth-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kest","u":"characters/kest.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Kest the Anointed · leader of the Bright Remnant warband · late 30s","x":"A self-declared prophet who ends alone in the cathedral hall, taking his solitude for proof of his holiness. A homegrown fringe prophet of Aldrovan stock (Pax Aldrovana) who styles himself “Kest the Anointed” and leads the Bright Remnant warband. Gaunt and ascetic-thin at about 5'11\", hollow-cheeked and pale, with intense fixed grey-blue eyes, long unkempt prematurely greying hair and beard hung with bone talismans, and self-inflicted ritual scars on both forearms. The sheet's final image: after his followers have scattered to loot, Kest stays on alone in the cathedral hall, exhausted and fervent, convinced that his solitude proves his holiness. Its expressions: furious certainty, ascetic resolve, prophetic fervor. The light is not in the heavens, but in those who refuse to forget.","i":"images/characters/kest-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Massinen","u":"characters/massinen.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Warlord of the Tazenna Reach · seventh siege of Ardenwake · 67–70 years old","x":"Commands the seventh siege from a staff and a table of generals, and never reaches the walls. Warlord of the Tazenna Reach, about seventy, around 5'10\", with dark bronze sun-weathered skin, thin close-cropped white hair and a long white beard, sharp dark eyes in a deeply lined face, and old campaign scars across both forearms. He is drawn at the seventh siege of Ardenwake, relying on his staff and his generals, in sand, cream and desert ochre with weathered brown leather. The sheet's closing image is captioned three months into the siege — he never reached the walls. Its expressions: command, pride, weariness, final resolve.","i":"images/characters/massinen-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Berant","u":"characters/berant.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Chief Engineer, Corvane Imperium · 51–54 years old","x":"Designs the eleventh siege against Ardenwake, and writes the private notes that tell his emperor what a siege cannot do to a holy city. Chief Engineer of the Corvane Imperium, in his early fifties, lean at about 5'9\", olive and weathered, with salt-and-pepper hair and dark brown eyes. He designs the engineering campaign of the eleventh siege against Ardenwake, including the spire-targeting morale strategy, and he authors the private notes to Vasarion that separate conventional siegecraft from breaking a holy city's morale. He wears aged bronze and a charcoal cloak, with brass measuring tools at his belt; the sheet's expressions include determined, tired from campaigns, and skeptical.","i":"images/characters/berant-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Dorenzo","u":"characters/dorenzo.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Prince of Solvarre · 32 years old","x":"Takes the field in ornamental parade armor with Valeria's refusal letter folded inside his breastplate. Prince of Solvarre, thirty-two: slim, fashionably dressed even on campaign, fine-featured and handsome with a petulant mouth, dark curled hair, dark brown eyes, a thin fashionable mustache and an ornate signet ring. His armor is ornamental parade steel in silver-grey with rich decorative accents, over luxurious Solvarre court colors. He carries Valeria's refusal letter inside his breastplate. The sheet pairs two traits: courtly pride, and entitlement refused.","i":"images/characters/dorenzo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Corse","u":"characters/corse-0000b.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Steward to King Wendric of Rovain · 55 years old","x":"Keeps the paper of the Crown's Due — and keeps the king's last words out of the ledger. Steward to King Wendric of Rovain — the office held by Corse in Case 0000 — fifty-five, spare and slightly stooped at about 5'7\", with a long, deeply lined face of careful neutrality, grey thinning hair and pale watchful grey-blue eyes. The middle finger of his writing hand carries an ink-stain callus. The sheet lays out his documents: the Crown's Due, the Commission Fiction, a confiscation order, a recall notice, and a wax tablet of private notes. He is present with the Queen at Wendric's private breakdown after the king fails to lift Valeria, one of only two witnesses to that hour; after the king's death, the sheet says, he keeps the last words private. A king's last word is not a ledger entry.","i":"images/characters/corse-0000b-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Wendric's Wife","u":"characters/wendrics-wife.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Queen of Rovain · 31–34 years old","x":"One of only two witnesses to the hour in which a king learns he cannot lift the sword. Queen of Rovain and wife of King Wendric; a Rovain woman, Anglo-Saxon in appearance, about 5'5\", with a composed court bearing, a fair court-pale complexion, light brown hair in an elaborate court style, hazel eyes and a dignified face that gives little away in public. She wears rich formal court robes. She is present alongside Corse at Wendric's private breakdown after he fails to lift Valeria, and is one of only two witnesses to that hour.","i":"images/characters/wendrics-wife-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Wendric's Son","u":"characters/wendrics-son.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Heir to the throne of Rovain · never crowned · 11 years old","x":"Eleven years old when his father dies; the realm is governed in his name by a Regent Council. Heir to the throne of Wendric's Rovain, never crowned. A slight child of eleven, about 4'6\", fair, with a gentle-featured face, a simple light-brown boy's cut and blue eyes resembling his father's, in plain charcoal-and-ivory court dress with a mourning collar. After his father's death a Regent Council rules in his name. The sheet's expressions: gentleness, mourning, bearing duty, quiet hope.","i":"images/characters/wendrics-son-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Yorbulan","u":"characters/yorbulan.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Khagan of the Sarnak Horde · first siege of Ardenwake · 48 years old","x":"Leads the first siege on a dying kam's prophetic vision, and dies of winter fever outside walls he never breaches. Khagan of the Sarnak Horde, forty-eight, with a broad, wind-burned face, black braided hair, dark brown eyes, a long braided mustache and short beard, deeply weathered bronze skin, many old clan war scars and the powerful frame of a horse-warrior. He wears dark leather and iron lamellar in earth and fur-grey tones, with clan tokens on his war gear. He leads the first siege of Ardenwake, following a dying kam's prophetic vision. The sheet's last image is the eleventh day before Ardenwake: he dies of winter fever outside the walls after an eleven-day siege, and the walls are never breached.","i":"images/characters/yorbulan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Branimir","u":"characters/branimir.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Prince of Belnograd · mid-30s","x":"Dies of river fever outside the walls, knowing he will not live to order the assault — and the grudge ends with him. Prince of Belnograd, thirty-four to thirty-six, Rus/Slavic in appearance: solidly built at about 5'10\", with a broad, heavy-browed face ruddy from cold-weather campaigning, long dark brown hair often braided back, pale blue eyes and a full beard in the fashion of Belnograd's princely line. His face is prematurely lined, which his advisors attribute to eleven years of privately nursing the campaign. He wears layered mail over a fur-lined coat, a fur-trimmed princely cloak and a curved cavalry saber, with a small icon of his great-grandmother on a cord beneath his armor. The sheet shows him pursuing the grudge, then before the final assault, knowing he will not live to order it, then dying of river fever outside the walls; its caption reads that the grudge ends with him, before the siege begins.","i":"images/characters/branimir-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Coren","u":"characters/coren.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"First Factor of the Merchant-Council of Velmoro · early 50s","x":"Orders the Ardenwake blockade withdrawn the moment the numbers turn unprofitable. First Factor of the Merchant-Council of Velmoro, in her early fifties: sharp-featured and built for negotiation, with grey-streaked dark hair in a severe knot, dark brown assessing eyes, olive city-pale skin and the ink-stained fingertips of a lifetime of ledgers. She wears deep charcoal and muted burgundy weathered wool and carries a black lacquered ledger-case. The sheet shows her at the Ardenwake blockade; she orders it withdrawn when the numbers turn unprofitable. Its expressions: measured, assessing, impatient, cold resolve.","i":"images/characters/coren-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Verrin","u":"characters/verrin.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Captain, the Free Company of the Blind Ford · 42 years old","x":"A mercenary captain the source never describes — drawn as a scarred, guarded professional. Captain of the Free Company of the Blind Ford, forty-two: a sturdy, scarred professional soldier at about 5'11\", with a weathered, guarded face of practiced mercenary neutrality, cropped brown hair going grey, brown eyes, short unkempt stubble, and a notched ear from an old campaign injury. His armor is mismatched worn steel, brown leather and dark iron, over faded charcoal. The text gives him no physical description; everything above was invented for the sheet.","i":"images/characters/verrin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Wounded Archer","u":"characters/the-wounded-archer.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Archer in Aldwick's defense · 16 years old","x":"Sixteen, lied about his age to enlist, and retells the story once a year. An archer in Aldwick's defense, sixteen, slight and still growing at about 5'5\", with light brown cropped hair, brown eyes and fair skin. He fights in borrowed military leathers too large for him, with a simple hunting bow, and carries a healed puncture wound in the lower leg from the siege. He lied about his age to enlist, and later retells the story once a year.","i":"images/characters/the-wounded-archer-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Vault Guard","u":"characters/the-vault-guard.html","m":"Case 0000B · Sword of Valeria: The Empty Hand","d":"Palace Guard, vault entrance · Marrowgate · 28 years old","x":"Stands the door of the vault at Marrowgate as a listener and a witness — and later retells what he saw. A palace guard of twenty-eight posted at the vault entrance at Marrowgate: solidly built, with cropped brown hair, warm brown eyes, fair weathered skin, a plain attentive face and light stubble. He wears Rovish palace mail and a deep-red-and-cream surcoat, with a spear and a short sword. The sheet's expressions are listening, witnessing, retelling the story and attention.","i":"images/characters/the-vault-guard-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"A Fraction of an Inch","u":"books/2114.html","m":"Case 2114 · Awaiting release","d":"Arithmetic. Regalia. Convergence. Warning. Still uncounted.","x":"Case 2114"},{"k":"c","t":"The Decree of Luminescence","u":"books/2365.html","m":"Case 2365 · Published","d":"A column of clear sky, a guttering lamp, and three hundred and eleven names left off the Roll.","x":"Case 2365 Fiction / Fantasy / Historical Vallombra is a small, terraced kingdom that rules itself by arithmetic. Its faith, the Concord of the Year, asks only that the sky keep its appointments, and for six hundred years it has. Then the Ashduct hills breathe, and the ash does not stop for three winters. The court astronomer-priest Severin stretches the year's grace from three days to a season and carries every missed appointment forward like a debt; the beekeeper-king Faustin melts down the kingdom's gold circlet for eleven days of bread. A week before the third spring's First Furrow, Severin posts a line no Reckoner has ever posted, one he cannot derive from his own tables: the sun will stand over the Threshing Round at the sixth hour, and the ash will part for it. It does, along a line so straight that nothing in the sky could have drawn it. The Standing Noon feeds a starving valley, and it has an outside. Bread is issued by name, from a single Roll compiled in three nights by the Reckoner's apprentices under Piero, a granary clerk's son who wanted nothing more than to count sacks. In the fourth column of the ninth parish book a copyist's lamp gutters, and three hundred and eleven people of the Saltway Quarter — northern guests and the Vallombran households that feed them — are never written down. Severin has already vanished from the tower at dusk, and the parish books are sealed under a law that nobody will be the first to call binding. Hungry Verrenhal marches south behind Sigrid, its Staff-Keeper, who takes one look at the edge of the light and says what no one in Vallombra will hear: nothing in the sky holds a line, so someone is holding this one. A herald's mistranslation of one phrase, the low hour, which means dusk to one nation and noon to the other, sends four thousand uncovered northerners across it in full sun, and for eleven minutes the wardens at the Saltway Gate loose into the glare. What follows is an accounting. Faustin ladles soup into the bowls of the men who came against him, and dies of the fever in them. Marcello, the Order's first Examiner, reconciles the Roll and finds the missing column, the purchased names and a drift of unread petitions, and he and Piero promise to correct it in the open on the day the child queen Livia turns twenty-one. Meanwhile Piero needs a sentence strong enough to hold a kingdom together, and it hardens into the Decree of Luminescence: Vallombra becomes Illumaria, a windowless basilica of black marble rises over the Round, and the promised day is deferred, for good reasons, again and again. Each evening at the Gold the tower's book is signed with a single word, Nil. The Decree of Luminescence closes on a Cultivator's own archival log — Case Study 2,365: one dormant unit beneath a threshing-floor, a Custodian recalled the night he used it, and a record that moves the cause of the war from a clerk's lamp to a neighbor's envy."},{"k":"s","t":"Piero","u":"characters/piero.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Senior apprentice to the Reckoner · later Regent of Illumaria and first High Pontiff · 21 at the story's opening","x":"Wants only to count sacks, hums in threes, and builds a religion out of one vanished master and one sentence he cannot afford to examine. The left-handed son of the Lower Granary's clerk, taken on at twelve for the neatness of his hand and wanting, from then on, nothing more ambitious than to count sacks. Senior of the seven apprentices of Severin, he hums when he computes, softly and always in threes: a nursery counting-song of his mother's, eleven verses of loaves, lamps and a door that is always warm, which Nina sang until she could not. Marcello shares his bread crusts on the tower stair and is, without either of them ever saying so, the check to his count. On the evening of the Standing Noon the Reckoner lays a brass plumb across his palm, says The residue holds, and is not on the tower when Piero turns round; Piero writes a zero in the book, and the sentence beneath it. He takes the head of the table that compiles the Roll of the Lit, answers the king's question at the Saltway Gate with It was his rule and a stranger's with a sentence that every witness hears as a refusal, and learns from Marcello that the Roll is three hundred and eleven names short. He chooses to append rather than disclose, promising a correction in the open on the day Livia is twenty-one, and drafts the Decree of Luminescence around the only true things he has: a tower at dusk, and the last words a man said there. The promised day is deferred, again and again, for good reasons. He climbs to take the evening reading for thirty-one years, until a novice, Aldo, asks what the command is for. He dies in his fifty-seventh year with the brass weight in his sleeve. Because he told me to.","i":"images/characters/piero-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Severin","u":"characters/severin.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"The Reckoner of Vallombra, sixth of that name · Observer 117, Custodian of record · appears about fifty","x":"Has never shown a false figure; on the morning of the First Furrow he states one, and the sky is cut open to make it true. A lean, courteous, unhurried astronomer-priest, sixth of his name to keep the Concord's Table from the tower beside the Threshing Round, who teaches his seven apprentices that a Reckoner who cannot show his work is merely a priest, and a priest is a man who has stopped being checkable. When the Ashduct hills breathe and the sun withdraws, he extends the year's grace from three days to a season and carries the missed appointments forward, entry upon entry. A week before the third spring's First Furrow he nails a line to the tower door that no notice has ever carried: the sun will stand over the Round at the sixth hour, and the ash will part to let it. Marcello asks for the derivation. It is not in the tables. He is also Observer 117 of the Cultivator Envoy Corps, Custodian of record for a dormant unit beneath the tower. He files reports with no first-person pronoun, is answered with the single word Noted, and keeps in the vault's margin a private table of the dead children he can name, Nina ninth among them. He activates the unit himself; that evening a recall names the narrow north stair, and he is gone from the parapet before Piero has finished turning round, with the lamp still burning. He never learns, so far as the file shows, that the Order will worship him as the Kindled Reckoner. Reassigned to a world whose sky is entirely clear, he notes in his first report that it has a great many appointments, and, on a struck-through second line, that nobody there hums when they count. The residue holds.","i":"images/characters/severin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Marcello","u":"characters/marcello.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Magistrate's son · senior apprentice · the Order's first Examiner · 22–23 at the story's opening","x":"Measures the edge of the light, writes down what it means, and tells no one for five months. A magistrate's son with an ink-stained thumb and an unshakable conviction that anything which cannot be reconciled has not, in any meaningful sense, happened. He is the friend and the check of Piero. On the first afternoon of the Standing Noon he is first to the northern edge with a plumb-line and a folding rule, finds a boundary with no penumbra, and writes in the margin of his book Nothing in the sky holds a line. Something holds this one, showing it to no one. At the Saltway Gate he privately suspects that the missing three hundred and eleven are no more than a clerical error, and says only that the Roll is the one rule that has held. Named the Order's first Examiner, he reconciles the Roll against the sealed parish books and finds the omitted column, Nardo's forty-one forged names, and the unopened letter under the Reckoner's paperweight. He lays them before Piero with his own confession of five months' silence, and founds the Examiners' Book on its one rule, that nothing is erased and everything is appended. He argues the Decree's clauses with Piero, proposes the windowless Basilica as a control entry, and writes beside the lens We have built the wrong instrument, and named it for the right miracle. He hands the book to Costanza in his last lucid week, four winters before Piero dies. We do not erase it, Piero. We append.","i":"images/characters/marcello-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Livia","u":"characters/livia.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Princess of Vallombra, then Queen of Illumaria · 9 at her coronation, 21 at the first Judgment · reigns 51 years","x":"Notices at nine that her crown has got heavier, and spends fifty-one years never saying so. The daughter of Faustin, nine years old when he dies, crowned in the seventh month on the Round in a circlet of gilded lead washed in vinegar and wine, and held very still for eleven minutes. When the true-gold circlet has been cast and passed through the fire, she asks Piero in the corridor outside the treasury whether it is the same crown. It is the same crown, Majesty, he says. Then it has got heavier, says Livia, and goes in to her supper. At twenty-one she stands alone on the black floor of the Basilica while the beam falls through the Arc onto her head, and glows, and looks faintly amused; the chroniclers record serenity. That night she asks Piero for her father's private ledger, the one with the debt in it, reads it standing to the last line, and says only that he always did carry a debt oddly. She keeps it, and for fifty-one years sends forty wagons of grain to the Saltway Quarter on the anniversary of the First Furrow, without a label. The last line she writes in the ledger is the two words the rule allows for a debt that will not close: Carried forward. Then it has got heavier.","i":"images/characters/livia-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Faustin","u":"characters/faustin.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"King of Vallombra, called ‘the Fortunate’ · widower · 49 years old","x":"A beekeeping king who sells his crown for bread, breaks the kingdom's first furrow, and dies of the soup he ladles. A stout, near-sighted, gentle widower whose only known vice is bees: sixteen skeps on the palace roof, one for each lord of his council, every one of them dead by the first spring of the Long Night. He rules by a contract with the year, and in the second winter he has the kingdom's three-hundred-and-forty-year-old gold circlet melted by a goldsmith and sold weight for weight to a southern grain-factor, which buys eleven days of bread. At the First Furrow he walks out alone to turn the first furrow of the year, and the share snaps in two. Then let it be recorded, he says, that I tried. When Sigrid's host is blinded and shot at the Gate he rides out bareheaded to stop the arrows, then opens every gate, name or no name, and has Marcello do the sum: four thousand seven hundred and twelve more mouths, and a thousand and forty-one dead among his own. He writes it down himself. He ladles soup in the sheds for three months, is told by the Granary's physician exactly what is passing from bowl to bowl, thanks her for the figure, and dies in eleven days among the empty hives. He leaves a daughter, Livia, a ledger, and a debt, and asks Piero not to let her think she is wearing the sun. It was only ever a hat, Severin. The bread, I am told, is real.","i":"images/characters/faustin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sigrid","u":"characters/sigrid.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Warden of the Winter Stores · Staff-Keeper of Verrenhal · 40 years old · later regent for eleven years","x":"The one person who says out loud, on the first day, what the light is, and is remembered now as a proverb about landlords. The king's sister and Verrenhal's Warden of the Winter Stores, a broad, sardonic, sharp-eyed woman who by a hereditary duty nobody can explain also carries and cuts the pale ash-wood calendar staff on which the kingdom's year is tallied, with her dead husband's belt buckle on a thong at its head. Her arithmetic is simpler than the Reckoner's and, across three winters of ash, more accurate: she counts the days between today and the day the barley runs out, and shows Ragnvald where the staff and the tally cross. She sings, when she believes herself alone, in a voice of such unrelieved flatness that her young nephew once left a room rather than hear the end of a verse. She buries her brother under a cairn on the pass, writes the Reckoner a letter on how the light must be made (it lies unopened under a paperweight for five months), and finds at the Saltway Gate a row of a hundred and forty-one dead with their feet toward the gold. She lays her staff across the line, hangs her lead plumb from it and holds it for a hundred breaths in a gale: the weight swings and the edge does not. At the parley she tells Piero that men are not taken into the light, they go down stairs. Her son Arne loses his sight at the Blinding, and she never crosses the line herself. She rules Verrenhal as regent for eleven years with the dead king's ring on her thumb, and her one true diagnosis survives only as a saying about landlords. Nothing in the sky has an edge. Someone is holding it.","i":"images/characters/sigrid-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Arne","u":"characters/arne.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Sigrid's son · 17 at the Blinding · lives to eighty","x":"Leads the vanguard down at the low hour with his eyes uncovered, never sees the light again, and becomes the best-loved singer in the north. Seventeen, rawboned and chewing something that is not food, Sigrid's son marches south at her side, and is given the vanguard because he is the Staff-Keeper's son and nobody else in the host is still strong enough to walk at its head. The herald has cried the king's promise of the low hour in a tongue where the low hour means noon, and Arne leads four thousand of the best of them down the Saltway in good order and in silence, with their eyes uncovered and, because a northerner does not go among strangers without them, their weapons. The glare takes him in the first instant, and Rufio's wardens loose for eleven minutes. His mother finds him kneeling in the road among the dead with both hands pressed to his eyes, alive and permanently blind. He lives to eighty and becomes, to the astonishment of everyone who ever heard her sing, the best-loved singer in the north. Asked late in life by a young skald what the light had looked like, he says he saw it for about as long as it takes to be wrong about a step.","i":"images/characters/arne-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ragnvald","u":"characters/ragnvald.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"King of Verrenhal · approx. 45 years old","x":"A big, tired, honest man with a cough, who chooses to starve on somebody else's road. The jarl-king of a poor and proud northern realm and Sigrid's brother, who spends three winters of ash watching the barley run down and is shown, at his own table, the place where the calendar staff and the grain tally cross, three weeks before the ground could be expected to thaw, if it thawed. We can starve in our own halls, he says, or we can starve on somebody else's road. He chooses the road. It is the last decision of his that anyone troubles to count. He marches south on the morning the staff names for the first thaw, nine thousand men and a few hundred women with empty barley wagons and the king on a litter behind them, and dies on the ninth day on the last saddle of the pass, of the cough and the cold and the knowledge that he chose wrongly and could not find the other choice. His last order, taken in a voice so thin that Sigrid has to lean into it, is that they go down and find out who is holding the match. We can starve in our own halls, or we can starve on somebody else's road.","i":"images/characters/ragnvald-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Fulvio","u":"characters/fulvio.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Lord of the Weirs · one of the sixteen lords of Vallombra · approx. 54 years old","x":"Weeps as loudly as any at the Round, then names the price of his silence. A sodden, prosperous eel-and-salt lord of the valley's southern mouth, whose harbors bought the crown's gold and whose grain-ships are the last ships left on that coast. In the ninth week after Faustin's funeral he rises at the end of the second hour of the council and proposes, with real regret and evident sincerity, that the king's nine-year-old daughter be betrothed to his own second son, and the regency pass to him until the year should see fit to show its face again. As arithmetic it is sound. Piero answers with a retort rather than a theology (It fell on her father's furrow, my lords. It did not fall on the Weirs) and Fulvio sits down, but not before naming his price in the flattest tone of the night: a charter making his grain-ships the Order's sole licensed factor for whatever is to be built on the strength of that noon, and a seat among whoever comes to steward it. Piero grants both. When the Standing Noon closes, House Fulvio's ships bring the first true cargo of iron, glass and southern grain up the coast, at a price the charter fixed in advance.","i":"images/characters/fulvio-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Rufio","u":"characters/rufio.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Captain of the Edge · 50 years old","x":"Draws every bread-line on the north road for sixteen days, and gives the one order he cannot remember giving. A broad, honest, careworn captain of the guard, commander of the wardens of the Edge — palace guardsmen and Granary men in borrowed grey — who opens the nine gates on the Roll of the Lit and, at the Saltway Gate, turns back with regret and in perfect good order the first of the people whose names cannot be found. He keeps the Order's orders: no armed body is to be admitted to the light on any pretext, and forty thousand people eat from the Gates. At the low hour he sees four thousand armed men step across the line into noon, and gives the word. The wardens loose for eleven minutes at what they can see, which is a mass of stumbling figures with their hands held out; two thousand three hundred and four northerners die, and the Order's tally of its own losses is six. It is perfectly true, as he will say once, to the empty room that is the only audience he ever chooses, that the men were armed. They were also blind. He cannot remember, for the rest of his life, which of those two facts he was looking at when he gave the word.","i":"images/characters/rufio-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Nardo","u":"characters/nardo.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Notary, formerly of the Saltway courts · later the Roll's first Registrar of Names · approx. 52–55","x":"Sells forty-one names for eleven bushels of seed barley apiece, and is pensioned for it. A small, courteous, well-groomed notary with the manners of a family physician and the ethics of a weather-vane, who understands before any clerk in the hall what the Roll has made of the parish books: a scarce good, with a fixed supply and no market. He charges eleven bushels of seed barley, or their equivalent, for a name entered at the foot of a page, and makes forty-one such entries between the second night and the third, in a hand no examination would have told from Lino's. He regards the Roll, he says afterward, as the most honest document the valley has ever produced, since it alone admits that being counted is a matter of being written down by someone with a pen, and that a pen, like a lamp, has a price. Summoned by Marcello, he admits everything with the affability of a man declining a second cup of tea, and is pensioned within the year as the Roll's first Registrar of Names: the only person in the valley competent to say which of its entries are false, and therefore the only one who can never be permitted to say so.","i":"images/characters/nardo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Costanza","u":"characters/costanza.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Examiner of the Order · Marcello's chosen successor · late 20s","x":"Told to enter everything and forgive nothing, she does it for four years without asking the question her predecessor would have asked within the hour. A stern, exacting young woman who receives the Examiners' Book from Marcello in his last lucid week, with a single instruction: enter everything and forgive nothing. She keeps it to the letter for four years, through the death of the man who gave it to her, without once asking Piero the question Marcello would have asked within the hour. The first page of the book still holds a date, and the promise beside it has been carried forward again and again in a book nobody opens. Her sheet gives her a habit the text does not: she holds her pen perfectly still before she writes, as if daring the figure in front of her to be wrong. Enter everything and forgive nothing.","i":"images/characters/costanza-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Aldo","u":"characters/aldo.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Novice of the Last Order · later Second High Pontiff · 9 years old as introduced","x":"Asks what the command is for, and gets five words and a collapse. A literal child, brought to the tower that autumn to carry the Reading Book up the stair, who on his seventh evening watches the High Pontiff write Nil for the eleven thousand three hundred and twelfth time and asks the question nobody in the Order has been permitted to ask in thirty years: why do we write it, Holiness, if it is always nothing? Piero says that it was the Kindled's last command, then that it is for the light, and then, asked why the Kindled commanded it, hears himself say Because he told me to. What follows is not weeping, and Aldo, who tells the story once, fifty years afterward, to his own novices, is very exact on the point. He runs for help down all one hundred and eleven steps, counting them. He becomes the second High Pontiff. He writes the Decree's fourth Article, which holds that the empty reading is the fullest and that faithful obedience is the only proof the Sun requires, and writes it, he says, for a man he once saw fall down a stair; and he has the novices of every House of Vigil taught to hum through the evening reading, softly and in threes, until several thousand people who never met a granary clerk's mother are keeping faith, without knowing it, with her song. Why do we write it, Holiness, if it is always nothing?","i":"images/characters/aldo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Lino","u":"characters/lino.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Copyist of the Bell and Granary orders · 14 years old, in his second week of service","x":"Trims a guttering wick, loses his place, and skips three hundred and eleven names; dies believing his work was perfect. A copyist of fourteen with a small, neat, unhurried hand and an unusual gift for staying on the line, placed by an uncle of the Bell order who wished, in the winter of the ash, to see one member of the family securely employed near a fire. He sits at the far end of the long table, nearest the failing lamp, with a wick cut from the hem of somebody's shirt, while forty copyists compile the Roll of the Lit in the dark. At the ninth hour of the night he comes to the Saltway Quarter: three hundred and eleven names in a single close-written column, the fourth of the ninth parish book. The wick gutters. He trims it, loses his place for the length of time it takes a small flame to steady, finds it again at the head of the next column and copies on, and it does not occur to him, then or for sixty years, that a page ought to have been longer. He is promoted in his thirtieth year to the head of the copying-hall and honored in his seventieth as the First Fair Hand of the Order, whose flawless Roll is the model of all faithful record. He is never told. Marcello writes in the Examiners' Book on the day of the investiture: Fair copy. Wrong. Honored.","i":"images/characters/lino-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Nina","u":"characters/nina.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Daughter of the Lower Granary clerk · Piero's sister · 9 years old at her death","x":"Sang the counting-song on the second night of the first winter until she could not. Nine winters old when she dies in the granary house, on the second night of the first winter of the ash, having sung her mother's nursery counting-song, eleven verses of loaves, lamps and a door that is always warm, until she could not. She is the ninth entry in Severin's private table of the dead children he can name. She is never shown alive; her sheet draws her only as she is remembered. Her brother Piero goes on singing the song under his breath, in threes, through the three years before the Standing Noon and the thirty-one after, as though the tune were a column that had to be carried forward or the whole account would fail to balance. The song outlives them both. Aldo has the novices of the Order taught to hum it through the evening reading, and in the mouths of several thousand people who never met her mother it becomes a liturgy. Nina, daughter of the Lower Granary clerk, aged nine winters; sang.","i":"images/characters/nina-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Tito","u":"characters/tito.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Plough-boy · nephew of the two brothers who draw the ceremonial plough · 13 years old","x":"Leads the plough at the First Furrow and thinks, mostly, about not crying in front of the king. A thirteen-year-old plough-boy who leads the ceremonial plough at the First Furrow of the third spring. The oxen have been eaten, so it is drawn instead by his two uncles, a pair of Granary brothers who have not eaten since Tuesday. He walks thinking mostly about how he must on no account cry in front of the king, and secondarily about how neither of his uncles will be able to stand much longer. Faustin takes the handles. The share goes into the crust of frozen ash and bites for perhaps a foot, and then there is a crack that every person present will remember, and the wooden share, which has turned the first furrow of the kingdom for three hundred and forty springs, snaps clean in two.","i":"images/characters/tito-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Goldsmith","u":"characters/the-goldsmith.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Palace goldsmith of Vallombra · approx. 47 years old","x":"Weeps into the crucible while he melts the crown of the kingdom, and is paid extra to stop. The goldsmith who, in the second winter, melts the kingdom's plain band of river-gold, three hundred and forty years old and worn at every First Furrow since the first Faustin, in the palace forge on Faustin's order, and weeps into the crucible so steadily that he has to be paid extra to stop. The gold is sold weight for weight to a southern grain-factor and buys forty thousand loaves, which is to say eleven days. The same week he casts a second circlet of gilded lead, so like the first that he, the treasurer and the king have some difficulty afterward remembering which was which. The only other person in the palace who knows is the Reckoner. Nobody in the High Realm's later centuries will say that the first crown of the Sun's kingdom was sold for bread by a beekeeper; it will be recorded instead that it endured the flame. Whether the Order's goldsmith, who later casts a true-gold circlet in a single night, is the same man, the text does not say.","i":"images/characters/the-goldsmith-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Anselmo","u":"characters/anselmo.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Master glassmaker of the Glassmakers' Row, Illumaria · approx. 52 years old","x":"Grinds the Sol-Focus Arc by hand for eleven years and dies of the grinders' lung, having written down nothing. Master of the Glassmakers' Row and of the guild commissioned by the Order to make the Sol-Focus Arc, a lens the diameter of a cartwheel, to catch and concentrate the single shaft of true sun that falls through the oculus of the Grand Basilica. He and his guild grind it by hand across eleven years, and die within a decade of finishing it, one after another, of the grinders' lung, with none of them having written down how. It is a very fine instrument. It is also, as Marcello notes on the night it is unwrapped, an instrument that gathers a light it had not made, where the Standing Noon had parted a dark it had not touched. His sheet gives Anselmo hands silvered with embedded glass dust and a dry cough in the lens's later years.","i":"images/characters/anselmo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Granary Physician","u":"characters/the-granary-physician.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Physician of the Granary order · approx. 44 years old","x":"Tells the king exactly what is in the soup, and is thanked for the figure. A tired woman who has buried a husband and two apprentices that winter, the physician of the Granary order tells Faustin in the second week of the sheds that a fever is passing from bowl to bowl, that no room in Torrecalda is large enough to keep the sick from the well, and that there is no fuel to spare for boiling the bowls, since the fuel that would have boiled them is the fuel that bakes the bread. The king thanks her for the figure and goes on ladling. He dies of it in eleven days. Her sheet draws her in a plain undyed coat over a widow's dark clothing, with a satchel whose herbs and instruments the famine has depleted, and a level, plain delivery even for the worst news.","i":"images/characters/the-granary-physician-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Tanner's Widow","u":"characters/the-tanners-widow.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Widow of Torrecalda's tanning trade · 35–38 years old","x":"Lost three children, and did not care who heard her call the light a door. A tanner's widow in the crowd at the First Furrow, who has lost three children and does not care who hears it. When the ash draws back and the shaft of noon falls on the Round, by evening it has been called a pillar, a stair, a spear and a throne. She calls it a door. It is one line in the account of that evening. The name that sticks belongs to a girl who had never seen a noon in her life. A door.","i":"images/characters/the-tanners-widow-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Nameless Girl at the Round","u":"characters/the-nameless-girl.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"A child in the crowd at the First Furrow · about 10–12 years old","x":"Had never seen a noon in her life, and shouted its name over and over. A girl in the crowd on the Threshing Round who has never seen a noon in her life, and who shouts the first name of the light over and over, in the voice of someone identifying a stranger at a funeral: Noon! Noon! Noon! It is entered in the tower's record that afternoon as the Standing Noon, and it sticks through every chronicle and every later hymn. She is never named. The text gives her only that she has never seen a noon, so her age and her famine-hollowed look are her sheet's own. Noon! Noon! Noon!","i":"images/characters/the-nameless-girl-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Senior Clerk of the Hall","u":"characters/the-senior-clerk-of-the-hall.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Senior clerk overseeing the compiling of the Roll · approx. 56 years old","x":"Orders the working papers burned so that nothing can disagree with the fair copy: the first ruling of the Last Order to be remembered as doctrine. The senior clerk of the long hall beneath the tower's north wing, where forty copyists of the Bell and Granary orders compile the Roll of the Lit by the yellow ration of seven lamps. At the coldest hour before dawn he orders the working papers burned in the braziers, the copyists, who have been writing in gloves cut down to fingerless stumps, not needing to be asked twice, and the parish books sealed in their chests. The tally sheets, the scrap on which each page was first drafted and on which the omission Lino's lamp had made would have been visible to anyone who laid one beside the other, go into the coals with a small, warm, sighing sound. He explains, to nobody in particular, that a record that existed twice was a record that could disagree with itself. It is the first ruling of the Last Order that will be remembered as doctrine, and it was made for the sake of a fire. A record that existed twice was a record that could disagree with itself.","i":"images/characters/the-senior-clerk-of-the-hall-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Interpreter","u":"characters/the-interpreter.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Interpreter with Sigrid's party at the Saltway Gate · approx. 38 years old","x":"Renders a sentence faithfully into the northern tongue, and so renders it as a refusal. The tired interpreter who stands beside Sigrid at the parley on the twentieth day. When Piero answers her in the old tongue with Let no shadow claim what shadow did not make, meant as a confession that the light belongs to none of them, the interpreter renders it as a refusal. So do the wardens, and so, in due course and in every succeeding century, does the Decree. The text gives only “a tired interpreter”; the sheet says outright that the gender, age and appearance it shows are invented for consistency.","i":"images/characters/the-interpreter-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Herald","u":"characters/the-herald-2365.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Herald, clerk of the Gate order · approx. 24 years old","x":"Cries the king's promise through the northern camp, faithfully and completely, in a language where the low hour means noon. A nervous clerk of the Gate order who learned the northern speech from a nurse. On the night after the parley he cries Faustin's promise through the northern camp, in that language, faithfully and completely: that the king will open the Gate to Sigrid's host on the following evening, at the low hour, when the light lies gentle. In doing so he makes the single error of the whole affair that no one on either side will ever be able to trace to a single hand. The old tongue's word for the low light of evening is, in the northern speech, the word for the low sun of a northern noon: the standing joke of the Saltway Quarter, of which no clerk of the palace has ever heard. Sigrid, who understood the promise correctly as dusk, is three miles away arguing with a wagon-master about the linen when he cries it.","i":"images/characters/the-herald-2365-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Faustin takes the handles at the First Furrow, and the share that has turned the kingdom's first furrow for three hundred and forty springs snaps in two","u":"records/the-share-breaks.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Faustin","x":"A bearded man in a rough cloak and a gold band stands over a snapped wooden plough in a frozen field before a vast crowd, with a tall spired city behind under a grey sky Faustin takes the handles at the First Furrow, and the share that has turned the kingdom's first furrow for three hundred and forty springs snaps in two.","i":"images/scenes/the-share-breaks-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"At the sixth hour the ash draws back along a line no compass could better, and a single shaft of noon falls on the Round, the tower and thirty-one thousand upturned faces","u":"records/the-standing-noon-opens.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","x":"A shaft of white-gold light falls through a ring of clear sky onto a tall tower above a dense crowd on a paved square, under dark ash cloud At the sixth hour the ash draws back along a line no compass could better, and a single shaft of noon falls on the Round, the tower and thirty-one thousand upturned faces.","i":"images/scenes/the-standing-noon-opens-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"By evening the light has an outside, and every road in the valley is full of people walking toward it","u":"records/the-crowds-come-to-the-light.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","x":"A stone bell tower beside a wall of golden light at dusk, hooded townspeople walking toward it down a wet street By evening the light has an outside, and every road in the valley is full of people walking toward it.","i":"images/scenes/the-crowds-come-to-the-light-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Piero on the parapet at the hour of the Gold, with the brass plumb on its cord and the lamp lit: the first of the evening readings he will take for thirty-one years","u":"records/piero-reads-the-gold.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Piero","x":"A young man with dark hair in a grey hooded robe looks back over his shoulder on a stone parapet at sunset, a lit lantern on the wall and a brass plumb hanging from a cord Piero on the parapet at the hour of the Gold, with the brass plumb on its cord and the lamp lit: the first of the evening readings he will take for thirty-one years.","i":"images/scenes/piero-reads-the-gold-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"At the Gold the grey lid breaks for the length of one breath, and when the apprentice opens his eyes Severin is no longer on the tower; only the lamp is still burning","u":"records/the-reckoner-is-taken-up.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Severin","x":"An elderly white-haired man in a grey robe dissolves into drifting particles on a dim stair, one hand reaching toward a boy who reaches back, a lantern burning above them At the Gold the grey lid breaks for the length of one breath, and when the apprentice opens his eyes Severin is no longer on the tower; only the lamp is still burning.","i":"images/scenes/the-reckoner-is-taken-up-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Lino, fourteen and in his second week, trims a guttering wick and loses his place. The next column he copies is Wool Lane, and three hundred and eleven names are not on the Roll","u":"records/the-lamp-that-guttered.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Lino","x":"A young copyist in a dark robe trims a lamp wick at a long candlelit table of open ledgers in a vaulted hall with frosted windows, rows of copyists beyond him Lino, fourteen and in his second week, trims a guttering wick and loses his place. The next column he copies is Wool Lane, and three hundred and eleven names are not on the Roll.","i":"images/scenes/the-lamp-that-guttered-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The host of Verrenhal comes down the pass in the ash, the light a gold coin far below: Sigrid at its head with the calendar staff, Ragnvald on his litter, and Arne beside the cart","u":"records/the-host-crosses-the-pass.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Sigrid, Ragnvald, Arne","x":"A woman in grey furs holding a tall staff leads a long column of hooded figures and wagons through a snowy mountain pass, a bearded man lying on a cart beside her and a boy walking at its wheel, a gold glow far down the valley behind them The host of Verrenhal comes down the pass in the ash, the light a gold coin far below: Sigrid at its head with the calendar staff, Ragnvald on his litter, and Arne beside the cart.","i":"images/scenes/the-host-crosses-the-pass-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Sigrid lays the calendar staff across the line, hangs her lead plumb from it and holds it a hundred breaths in a gale: the weight swings, and the edge does not","u":"records/sigrid-reads-the-edge.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Sigrid","x":"A woman in a tattered cloak holds a staff across the edge of a wall of golden light, a small weight hanging from it, on a dark ash-strewn hillside Sigrid lays the calendar staff across the line, hangs her lead plumb from it and holds it a hundred breaths in a gale: the weight swings, and the edge does not.","i":"images/scenes/sigrid-reads-the-edge-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The parley across the line, bright inside and black outside: Piero in the Reckoner's grey faces Sigrid, and a sentence he did not know he knew goes over the line as a refusal","u":"records/the-parley-at-the-saltway-gate.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Piero, Sigrid","x":"Two groups face each other across a sharp line between golden daylight and black ash; on the lit side a young man in a grey cloak with two companions, on the ash side a woman with a tall staff, a helmeted soldier and a man holding a large book The parley across the line, bright inside and black outside: Piero in the Reckoner's grey faces Sigrid, and a sentence he did not know he knew goes over the line as a refusal.","i":"images/scenes/the-parley-at-the-saltway-gate-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Arne leads four thousand northerners across the line at the low hour, eyes uncovered, and the wardens of Rufio's Edge loose for eleven minutes at figures who cannot see them","u":"records/the-blinding.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Arne, Rufio","x":"Archers loose arrows from a shadowed foreground into a golden-lit field where a crowd of figures stumble and fall in the glare, a hilltop castle on the dark horizon Arne leads four thousand northerners across the line at the low hour, eyes uncovered, and the wardens of Rufio's Edge loose for eleven minutes at figures who cannot see them.","i":"images/scenes/the-blinding-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Faustin in a linen apron over plain wool, ladling soup in the great canvas sheds on the Round every evening for three months, to the men who had tried to reach him through a light that blinded them","u":"records/the-king-ladles-the-soup.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Faustin","x":"A bearded man in a linen apron ladles soup from a steaming cauldron into a bowl held out by a bowed man in a long queue under a canvas awning hung with banners Faustin in a linen apron over plain wool, ladling soup in the great canvas sheds on the Round every evening for three months, to the men who had tried to reach him through a light that blinded them.","i":"images/scenes/the-king-ladles-the-soup-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Marcello reconciling the Roll against the sealed parish books, and following a leak of three hundred and eleven names down the pages to a single column: the fourth of the ninth book","u":"records/marcello-reconciles-the-roll.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Marcello","x":"A young man with dark hair sits cross-legged among open parish books and loose pages by an oil lamp in a dim study Marcello reconciling the Roll against the sealed parish books, and following a leak of three hundred and eleven names down the pages to a single column: the fourth of the ninth book.","i":"images/scenes/marcello-reconciles-the-roll-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Livia, nine years old, in a circlet of gilded lead washed in vinegar and wine, holds very still on the Round for eleven minutes","u":"records/the-child-queen-is-crowned.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Livia","x":"A girl in a pale gold-trimmed gown and cloak with a circlet on her braided hair stands alone on a round of paving between rows of robed lords and priests beneath dark banners Livia, nine years old, in a circlet of gilded lead washed in vinegar and wine, holds very still on the Round for eleven minutes.","i":"images/scenes/the-child-queen-is-crowned-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Grand Basilica of Sol-Invictus, raised over the Round in black Ashduct marble with walls forty feet thick and one shuttered oculus set in the axis of the vanished column: a room built to hold nothing, as Marcello argued, so that the true light might be seen for what it was","u":"records/the-grand-basilica.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Marcello","x":"A vast windowless black domed hall of dark columns, a single narrow shaft of white light falling from a round opening at the crown of the dome onto the floor, a few small robed figures along the walls The Grand Basilica of Sol-Invictus, raised over the Round in black Ashduct marble with walls forty feet thick and one shuttered oculus set in the axis of the vanished column: a room built to hold nothing, as Marcello argued, so that the true light might be seen for what it was.","i":"images/scenes/the-grand-basilica-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The first Judgment: every torch doused, Livia alone on the black floor, the beam falling through the Arc onto her circlet. The gold flares, the room weeps, and the queen looks faintly amused","u":"records/the-first-judgment.html","m":"Case 2365 · The Decree of Luminescence","d":"Livia","x":"A woman in a pale gold-embroidered gown and a fine circlet stands alone on a polished black cracked-marble floor in a tall beam of golden light, in darkness The first Judgment: every torch doused, Livia alone on the black floor, the beam falling through the Arc onto her circlet. The gold flares, the room weeps, and the queen looks faintly amused.","i":"images/scenes/the-first-judgment-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Stone of the Clean Hand","u":"books/2231.html","m":"Case 2231 · Awaiting release","d":"Cat. Hand. Stone. Signature. Omitted.","x":"Case 2231"},{"k":"s","t":"Observer 618","u":"characters/observer-618.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Cultivator Envoy and Custodian of record for Case 2231 · a mute stonecutter, called the Dumb Mason by the shrine's priests · appears about 45–50","x":"Dresses both ends of a matched pair of stones, may not answer the one question he is asked, and leaves a gate unlatched. A lean, silent man in a leather apron with a satchel of chisels, who answers the Left Ministry's call for a shrine-stone with the lowest of three bids and then works the block for eleven days at Mikusa, tapping and pausing with his head tilted, as a physician sounds a chest. At the end he cuts a short column of notches into its flank, one, one, two, three, five, eight, and walks away down the cedar approach. The shrine's priests call him the Dumb Mason and take the notches for his mark. He had dressed the receiving block first, in a village green on the far side of the world, and stays on there as a quarry-hand. His instruction for the one event he came to see is a single word: observe. When the stranger who came out of the stone draws the column of notches in the dust and turns his palms toward the east, he gives neither answer; the bar on correcting a subject's reading, he reasons, extends to confirming it. His reports record, as a deviation with no operational reason given, that he latches the quarry gate each evening and then walks back up the road to try it, and note that he has begun to leave the heel of his bread on the green's stone at dusk. On the night the village burns he stands at the quarry gate with a lantern and, as the fleeing party reaches it, steps aside and lets it swing open. Nineteen years on, on the first of Averin, he reduces the western stone to dust and recovers the eastern, and the stranger, finding the hollow on the green, walks over and bows to him. He returns the bow, the only answer he is permitted, and leaves the post that night without collecting the bread. The reviewing desk has not yet replied to whether he observed or rescued. The Custodian of Case 0202, Observer 551, passes him three times in the character of a rat-catcher, and the courtesy is exact. In Case 4181 the Observer posted there, 614, files the stranger as the surviving subject of Case 2,231, closed in the previous season by a different Custodian, which is this one.","i":"images/characters/observer-618-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Kiyote Pair","u":"characters/the-kiyote-pair.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Catalyst of Case 2231 · the Ordeal Stone of Mikusa and the Doorstone of Vilarnau · set eleven and twelve years before the opening","x":"Two blocks of grey granite dressed by one hand, which send a man across the world and show the sending realm only an absence. A matched pair of one-way transit stones, each a grey granite block with a single green vein “like a vein in a wrist” and, cut into its flank, a short column of notches in clusters: one, one, two, three, five, eight. The receiver was set first, as routine instrumentation, in the moot-green of Vilarnau under the oak, where the Charter makes it the village's tally-stone: waist-high, three paces long, a ruled scale cut across its top and an oak beam beside it for the weights. The sender was dressed eleven years before the opening, after the Left Ministry bought a shrine-stone at the lowest of three bids, and stands at Mikusa as a knee-high block on a plinth of black cedar behind a straw rope. A mute stonecutter dressed both. He proposed no ordeal and supplied no doctrine; the Ministry named the stone's purpose for itself. The notches are his standing signature and do nothing. The sender works on bare open-palm contact alone. Through cloth, a broom or the backs of the fingers it registers nothing, which is why Jōkei, who sweeps it every morning for eleven years, is never sent. A hand laid on it turns a man into a pale grain that pours sideways into the stone like sand through the neck of a glass, and the receiver pours him out again on the far side of the world; it cannot be reversed. In the east the device has no output but absence, which a court reads as a verdict. In the west it has no stated function at all: a tally-stone is a tally-stone, until a stranger comes out of it and the village starts touching it on its way to somewhere else. Two iron wedges break on its flank and a third rebounds without leaving a mark, and a fire that takes the thatch of the lane leaves it standing in the heat with a charred wreath of ivy. In the nineteenth year the western block is reduced to dust, leaving a clean rectangular hollow, and the eastern is recovered at the same instant; Jōkei finds on its plinth a low heap of pale dust in the exact shape of a stone. The sheet's slate grey, jade-green vein and moss on the shaded side are its own color notes; the manuscript gives the grey, the green vein, and the moss in the last years. Case 0202 gives the Stone one more use, by a hermit of Val Muta named Vara, who kneels at it with a short bent tool and makes it, for one dusk, not one-way; that file and this one tell the end of the Stone differently, and the pages are not reconciled. Case 4181 keeps the Stone's end as the stranger remembers it, on the first morning of Averin: a clean rectangular hollow on the green and a little grey dust blowing off it, and a name said into the dust for the last time.","i":"images/characters/the-kiyote-pair-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Higaide","u":"characters/higaide.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand · also in Case 0202, Case 4181","d":"Tomobe no Higaide · Captain of the Left Gate Guard of Himuro, Sixth Rank (lower grade) · 34 at the opening, 53 at the close · called Higa, Lo Higa, the Moon-Man, the Dead Man of the Doorstone, the Eastern Angel and Saint Two-Swords","x":"Signs the order of the day for a children's festival, clears his hand at a stone, and comes out of its twin on the wrong side of the world. A lean man with a long jaw and a left hand that is, to the lasting distress of every instructor he has ever had, the stronger of his two: Tomobe no Higaide, thirty-four, Captain of the Left Gate Guard of Himuro, of the Sixth Rank in its lower grade. He wears the long tachi Yoake, Daybreak, at the left hip and the short straight blade Yoiyami, Evening Dark, at the right, a pairing the court takes for an affectation and which he owes to a one-eyed veteran who once beat him senseless in a cedar wood and then, apologizing profusely, showed him how it was done. He puts down the household's plums in salt and red shiso himself, writes poetry as a bear dances, and is the husband of Shizuka and the father of Tsuru, six. On the twelfth of Eldren he is given the Minister's grandson for the day. He signs the Order of the Day for the Festival of the Small Bow, spends an hour teaching Kiyomaro to settle the nocking-point against his thumb, and is forty paces away when Azuki catches the pony's hock. Perfect, he tells the dying boy, and will say the word, in two languages and to no one who understands it, on a great many mornings afterward. The Fifth Article of Arikuni's Law makes the officer who signs the Order answer for the day. The one thing that would have excused him, the Examiner's eleven sheets, is shelved at the Minister's pleasure, and a motive is supplied by a paid memory. He accepts the summons. His cousin Narimune sends four lines and three horses to the Northern Gate; he laughs once at the last line and does not go, because the Law holds that a fugitive's kin forfeit the estate and, more simply, because he does not think it necessary: an innocent man who believes in the Stone has nothing to fear from it. At Mikusa he turns down Jōkei's offer of a door at the back of the chamber (There is no door for an innocent man), asks leave to say one sentence, that he has not done it and that he loved the boy, and sets his left palm on the Stone as a man lays his hand on the neck of a horse he has been told is gentle. It will be entered, says Arikuni. The pale grain pours out of him, swords and all. He comes up out of the tally-stone of Vilarnau at dusk with his hand still out, reasons that he must be dead and the country a provincial afterlife, and waits with great composure for someone to tell him where to report; Peire, eight, bows back. He disarms Sir Bertran de Lanta when the knight takes a bow for a reach for the hilt, is taken in by Ermengarda, eats around the bacon, claps at Solan's fire, learns the language from Gersenda, becomes Higa, and lays his palm on the stone every morning to say a name. When he learns what became of the last man out he goes into the barn and breaks, against the flank of the red ox, and vows to it not to draw his swords in that house. On the first of Kerren he keeps the vow until the loft is entered, kills three men in a dozen heartbeats, and holds the plank bridge over the lower race on the night of the torches for perhaps a quarter of an hour. On the ridge he is for nineteen years a charcoal-burner, a well-digger, a mender of ploughs, a teacher of boys with sticks and a poet who burns every verse, and on the first of each month he walks down to the green and says a name to the stone. When the stone is gone he says it once to the hollow, bows to the mute stonecutter, and in Brayen goes east with a sack of chestnut flour. The east records him as a murderer consumed for his guilt, and the west as a minor local saint; the Observer's closing note says he was neither. He is carried on into Case 4181, where the author calls him the main character, and appears in Case 0202; the manuscript's revision log says that he is not dead and returns to his homeland, and meets his wife and daughter, after later cases (0202, 1414, 0333 and 0456). The manuscript gives his name, rank, age, swords, habits and aliases. The sheet's 5 ft 10 in, warm light-tan skin, thin moustache and chin tuft, grey-flecked beard and streaked hair in the later years, the thin white scar across the right knuckles and the indigo sword-cords are its own. Where the sheet has him go east in Brayen of the twentieth year, the manuscript's text puts it in the nineteenth, and its revision log says to read it so. There is no door for an innocent man. That is the whole point of it.","i":"images/characters/higaide-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Shizuka","u":"characters/shizuka.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Tomobe no Shizuka · wife of Tomobe no Higaide, daughter of a provincial scholar · 29 years old at the story's opening","x":"The only person who asks how he looked, and who then does the sum and decides, at great cost, not to press it. A tall woman who walks as though the floor owed her something, and who at nineteen was the only person in the capital to read one of the poems of her future husband, Higaide, aloud, to its author, with a perfectly straight face. She keeps every sheet of his verse in a lacquer box under the dressing-table because they are, she says, the best things in the house. On the night before the ordeal she reads him one about a heron in the rain, tells him that if it is a mistake he will be extremely angry afterward and that she is looking forward to it, and takes his strong left hand and turns it over, as though to learn it like a poem. Two days after the notice she goes to Mikusa and asks the Warden, Jōkei, how Higaide had looked and whether his sentence was entered, and then to the Bureau of Inquiry, where an Examiner among his sparrows tells her only that a report existed and was eleven sheets long. She does the sum, and does not press it, not with a daughter of six and a court whose beneficiaries would hear a question about the Law's foundations as a confession. She takes the child north to Fort Shirakane, keeps a small school there for the garrison's daughters, and declines the estate offered when the Law lapses. Every first frost she tastes the plum brine with her eyes shut and says, to the empty room, that he would have complained about the salt. She does not learn, within the period of this record, that he lived.","i":"images/characters/shizuka-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Tsuru","u":"characters/tsuru.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Daughter of Tomobe no Higaide and Shizuka · 6 years old · later a tall woman who keeps the lacquer box","x":"Comes about a practical matter, asks whether the plums will be ready, and falls asleep holding his sleeve. Six years old, put to bed hours before the ordeal and, with the thoroughness of children, not staying there. On the night of the nineteenth of Garren she appears in the doorway of the eastern hall in her nightrobe, her hair half out of its braid, and asks her father, in a voice that tries hard to be that of a girl who has simply come about a practical matter, whether the plums will be ready before he comes home. He kneels and redoes the braid, which he has never been permitted to do and does badly, and says that the jars will be opened at the first frost, as always, and that he will taste the brine himself. She finds this satisfactory. She yawns, in a way that suggests the whole interview was a ruse to be carried to bed, and is asleep before they reach the gallery, her damp small fist closed round the end of his sleeve. He does not come home. On the fourth of Iverin, at the first frost, she stands in the doorway of the cool north room in her nightrobe and watches her mother taste the brine with her eyes shut, and says nothing. They go north by ox-cart to Fort Shirakane, where her father's cousin takes her onto his shoulders and she sleeps in a corner of the long room under his cloak. In her mother's last weeks in the capital a stooped Examiner corrects a clerk's error: a daughter's name is carried off the Tomobe roll and onto her mother's father's, so that the Bureau's list of the Tomobe disgraced comes at the last to a commander, a captain and a wife, and omits a child. She grows into a tall woman who walks as though the floor owed her something, keeps the lacquer box, and reads every poem in it aloud, with a perfectly straight face, to her own children. The manuscript gives her six years, the braid, the box and the plum tree; the sheet's “about 25 as a woman”, pale ivory skin and white nightrobe are its own.","i":"images/characters/tsuru-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Jōkei","u":"characters/jokei.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Warden-Priest of Mikusa Shrine · 60 at the story's opening, 79 when the Stone comes apart","x":"Sweeps the Stone every morning for eleven years and never once lays his bare palm on it. A bald, mild, apologetic priest who tended the hillside shrine of Mikusa for thirty years before the Stone arrived, in which time he presided over some seven hundred weddings and a larger number of funerals. He sweeps the ordeal court with a broom and wipes the block with a damp cloth, and has never in eleven years set his bare palm to it; the backs of his fingers have found it cold in summer and, in winter, faintly warm. He sits with the accused as the Law requires, and with Shizuka's husband he goes further, pointing out a door at the back of the chamber, the most dangerous sentence of his life. He is told there is no door for an innocent man. He writes the finding in the Book of the Ordeal and, under it, without being told, the Captain's one sentence. He gives Shizuka the whole account. By the third hand he watches go, Kanemichi's, he has begun to keep a tally on the inside of his sleeve with a thumbnail, because someone ought to. At seventy-nine he finds the Stone come apart into a low heap of pale dust in the exact shape of itself, sweeps the last of it into the rill, and enters one line: that the Stone has gone and the kami has given no reason. He leaves untouched the former Minister's signed sheet pasted into the last leaf.","i":"images/characters/jokei-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Three Senior Priests of Mikusa","u":"characters/three-senior-priests.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Senior priests under Warden Jōkei · witnesses at the ordeal court · 35, 48 and 55 by their sheet","x":"Three white-robed men on a dais who watch a captain go, and are not heard to say a word. Warden Jōkei of Mikusa has a staff and a ledger, and among his priests three seniors, who sit with him in their white robes among the nine witnesses on the morning the Captain of the Left Gate Guard is brought to the Stone: the two officers of the Bureau of Inquiry, Jōkei and his three senior priests, the boy who carried the lantern, the Right Minister, and the Minister of the Left. What the nine see is argued over for the rest of their lives, and each account is slightly different and each sincere: a pale grain pouring sideways into the stone, and a sound that two of them call a bowl being set down on a table and one a breath let out. None of the three is named, aged or given a line, and the manuscript does not say which of the witnesses gave which account. When Sukenori is brought to the same dais in the second winter of the Rising, of the nine witnesses of that morning only three are left to see him, “the others having been taken by the Law, or the Rising, or the plain exhaustion of men who have been present at something”; it does not say which three. The sheet supplies the rest: ages of 35, 48 and 55, shaven faces, black-to-grey topknots, white-knuckled hands in their sleeves, the pale blue trousers under the white robes, and the shrine staffs they carry. The white robes are the manuscript's; the figures and the faces are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/three-senior-priests-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Priests' Boys of Mikusa","u":"characters/priests-boys-of-mikusa.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Boys who serve the priests of Mikusa · unnamed · 10–13 by their sheet","x":"One carries the lantern and tries to hide that he is crying; one, in the priests' row, is quietly sick as a man goes into the stone. Boys who serve the priests of Mikusa, and who are on the gravel on the morning of the ordeal. At the hour of the Rat a priest's boy carries the lantern in front of Higaide, crying quietly and trying to hide it. In the ordeal court he is the boy who has been told to stand with the priests and has not been told that he may go. When the Captain's palm touches the Stone and the pale grain pours out of him, one of the boys in the priests' row begins, very softly, to be sick. The manuscript counts nine witnesses: the two officers of the Bureau, Jōkei and his three senior priests, the boy who had carried the lantern, and the Right and Left Ministers. That is one boy, though the sentence about the sickness speaks of boys in the priests' row, and whether the one who is sick is the one who carried the lantern the manuscript does not say. Years later, when Sukenori is taken to the same dais, only three of the nine witnesses of that first morning are left to see him, the others having been taken by the Law, or the Rising, or the plain exhaustion of men who have been present at something; it does not say which three. The sheet says outright that it has no physical description to work from. Its boys, 10 to 13, are small and thin, 4 ft 4 in to 4 ft 9 in, barefoot in worn white tunics, with cropped black hair in the novice style, dark brown eyes and pale, tear-bright faces, and one of them holds up a paper lantern with a flower crest beside the shrine's pillars at night. Its expression panels run Quiet Grief, Fear, Obedience and Exhaustion, and its last scene, At the Priests' Row, draws a line of white-robed figures with a boy lying down in the foreground. Every one of these details is the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/priests-boys-of-mikusa-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sukenori","u":"characters/sukenori.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Kan no Sukenori · Right Minister of Himuro · about 62 years old by his sheet","x":"Asks, gently, whether the Law applies to everyone, and is the one Minister to find out that it does. A precise, fastidious man who has disliked Sugino no Arikuni for thirty years in the way one dislikes a colleague whose excellence one has been compelled to admire. He has never confided in a living person and has therefore invented a confidant: every petition the Minister of the Left has disposed of in three decades, he has read in copy and written beneath it the ruling he judged his rival would give, and he was right in all but four, which he keeps on top. On the day of the pyre he mentions the Fifth Article in the gentlest possible voice, with the air of a man inquiring whether the law applied to everyone; he does not think he is setting a trap, only asking a question to which either answer will serve. He co-seals the summons without asking to see the Examiner's report, because it was convenient not to ask. At Mikusa he sits with his hands on his knees, scrupulously neutral, tells Higaide gently that the Law does not forbid him one sentence before he touches the Stone, and declines afterward to describe the sound the Stone made. At the Reading of the Tally he answers Sadamune's missing denominator courteously and exactly: a man who has gone cannot be offered in evidence. What he saw frightens him, and he reasons that a thing so exact should be put to work as a law rather than left to be a doom. He proposes that a Law which reaches a captain for a death at a rite should reach a granary for a loss of rice; Arikuni says Yes, and the extension is drafted within the week in Sukenori's own hand and is very well made. In the second winter of the Rising a faction of provincial counsellors summons him to Mikusa on a charge of disloyalty of the hand. He goes in the same plain palanquin, early, and thanks Jōkei by name, which in thirty years he has never done for a servant of any shrine. He leaves in his study the lacquered box of petitions with the five he misjudged laid uppermost; a nephew finds it and nobody reads it. Before the Stone he says that it is an admirable instrument and that he finds, on the whole, that it does apply. He puts out his hand. The sheet's sixty-two years, grey-white topknot, thin moustache and ink-stained finger are its own.","i":"images/characters/sukenori-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sadamune","u":"characters/sadamune.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Ki no Sadamune · Secretary of the Bureau of Inquiry · 43 years old · later Governor of a northern province","x":"Reads the Stone's tally to a room pleased with it, and puts a denominator under the figure. A dry, upright, fastidious man who holds the post of Secretary because nobody else at the Bureau can be trusted to count. For a fortnight he reconciles the Warden of Mikusa's entries against his own, and at the quarterly Reading of the Tally he stands in the Hall of the Two Seals with the Book of the Ordeal open on his forearm and reads it out: nine accused in a little over eleven years, five confessed and beheaded at the Southern Gate, four who put out a hand and were taken. Four for four, says the Deputy Minister, with real pleasure, and the whole dais warms to it. He had been told in the corridor that a man who reads a tally and sits has done his duty, and finds he cannot be that man. Either the Stone takes the guilty and refuses the innocent, he says, or it takes whoever puts a hand to it, in which case four of four describes the stone and not the hands. The Bureau has no instrument to tell the two apart. The Right Minister answers him courteously and correctly: a man who has gone cannot be offered in evidence. The remark is entered as “a procedural observation, not pursued,” and within the month he is made Governor of a northern province whose principal export is weather, where he governs honestly for nineteen years and writes nothing further about stones.","i":"images/characters/sadamune-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Deputy Minister of the Left","u":"characters/deputy-minister-of-the-left.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Deputy Minister of the Left · unnamed · about 52 years old by his sheet","x":"Says “four for four” with real pleasure, of the one figure in the Book that nobody has put a denominator under. He has one sentence in the manuscript. At the quarterly Reading of the Tally in the Hall of the Two Seals, in the third week of Averin, the Secretary of the Bureau of Inquiry, Sadamune, reads out from the Book of the Ordeal the record of nine accused in a little over eleven years: five confessed and were beheaded at the Southern Gate; four put out a hand, and the Stone took four. “Four for four,” says the Deputy Minister of the Left, with real pleasure, and the particular warmth that a room gives to a figure it has been hoping for goes down both rows of the dais. It is a very good figure, the best that any law in the history of the realm has produced, and it requires no explanation; and it is, as the Secretary has known for a fortnight, the only figure in the Book that nobody has thought to put a denominator under. That is the whole of him. The manuscript gives him no name, age or appearance, does not say what he does in the Ministry beyond his title, and does not follow him afterward; the Left Ministry falls in the Rising and nothing is said of its deputy. The pleasure is enough to place him: it is the room's pleasure, and it is what the Secretary has to stand up against, courteously answered by Sukenori and entered beneath the figure as “a procedural observation, not pursued.” The sheet fills in a plump, pale, lightly powdered man of fifty-two with a neat topknot under a lacquered cap, a thin moustache, a rich purple robe, a folding fan and a ring on every second finger. All of that is the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/deputy-minister-of-the-left-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kanemichi","u":"characters/kanemichi.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Ono no Kanemichi · rice-warden of the Right Ministry's eastern granary · 52 years old","x":"Has never entered a figure he did not count, and will not sign one he did not do. A broad, plain man with counting-rod calluses on his left hand, who in thirty years has never put a figure in a ledger that he had not counted. A wet autumn rots the thatch over the third bay of his granary and forty koku go to mold; he records the loss in the month it occurs and asks for new thatch. His accurate page is read by someone who needs it to be a theft. At dawn on the twenty-sixth of Jorren he is shown the Stone in the white of the accused, with his warden's tally-token on a cord at his breast, and a decent young clerk of the Bureau holds out a warmed brush and a confession ruled in every column but the signature. He reads it, and finds he cannot sign a figure he has not counted; it is not courage, he thinks, since he has none that he knows of. He gives the brush back and puts out his left hand, the one he counts with. Jōkei is watching, and counting. The clerk folds the unsigned sheet in thirds and keeps it, to be found forty years later by a daughter who takes it for a laundry list. His ledger is audited the following month by a successor, who finds it exact in every column, and the page about the thatch is filed beneath it with one word in its margin. He had been right in every figure he ever entered. It was the one thing nobody had wanted.","i":"images/characters/kanemichi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Bureau Clerk","u":"characters/bureau-clerk.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Clerk of the Bureau of Inquiry · unnamed · about 26 years old by his sheet","x":"Warms the brush in his sleeve before offering it, and keeps the sheet that was never signed. A decent young man of the Bureau of Inquiry, who stands at Kanemichi's shoulder at dawn on the twenty-sixth of Jorren, at Mikusa, carrying a brush and a confession ruled and inked in everything but the signature. He puts the matter as it has been put to everyone before him: a signed confession is a clean death at the Southern Gate, and the Stone is the Stone. He holds out the brush, and it is a good brush, warmed in his sleeve against the cold. The rice-warden reads the sheet, ruled with real skill, every column square. It is the first time in thirty years of keeping other men's grain that anyone has offered him the chance to sign a figure he has not counted, and he finds that he cannot. He takes the brush back. When the rice-warden has crossed the gravel and put out his left hand, the clerk takes up the unsigned sheet and looks for a moment at the ruled columns and the wide clean space where a name would have gone, then folds it into thirds and puts it in his sleeve, as one puts away a letter that has not been answered. It is found among his effects forty years later by a daughter, who takes it for a laundry list. The manuscript does not name him or give his age or looks. The sheet's twenty-six years, thin stooped build, soft chin and anxious brow, and its ink-smudged wrist, are its own.","i":"images/characters/bureau-clerk-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kaneyoshi","u":"characters/kaneyoshi.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Granary clerk of the Left Ministry · the first man the Stone took · about 38 years old by his sheet","x":"Dies in a village on the wrong side of the world, saying the name of a thing a clerk counts. A granary clerk of the Left Ministry, accused of stealing twenty koku of rice from its stores. In the court's ledger he is the man who, shown the Stone at Mikusa, said that he was innocent and put out his hand, and was gone before the priests could draw the breath to cry out; the court, which had one case, counted it as a law. Nine years before the captain's turn he arrives at the tally-stone in the moot-green of Vilarnau: thin, in a nightshirt of some pale stuff and a coat of some other, with a small wooden token on a cord at his breast that nobody can read, shaking so badly that two men have to carry him. For three days he talks in a language no one knows, in a high thin voice that rises and falls like a man arguing with a clerk, and the word he says more than any other is one the reeve can only reproduce badly years afterward. It is a short, round, simple word, a quantity: the measure by which every sack of rice in Himuro is reckoned. On the third night he stops. The village buries him beneath the oak with the rites of the Threefold Crown, since it has no others, lays a flat stone on him, and tells the story afterward, with the minor improvements of repetition, as that of the Wild Man who came out of the tally-stone and died; the reeve's whole account of him to the next arrival is four words: the last one died. Higaide, shown the fieldstone the size of a bread-board under the oak, knows the name from a note in the Left Ministry's papers and does the sum: the Stone did not take the false, it took whoever was made to put a hand to it, and the clerk had been counted a proof because he had vanished. The Observer's first note records him as the Pair's one test, a sample “too small to be informative”, and adds that he has not treated it as one. The sheet's thirty-eight years, ink-callus on the right middle finger and sallow, fever-grey skin are its own; the manuscript says only that he was a clerk, and thin. Case 0202 remembers him as a thin small man with ink on his fingers who lived eleven days in the smith's loft and died on the twelfth, apologizing; the two accounts are not reconciled. Koku.","i":"images/characters/kaneyoshi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kurige","u":"characters/kurige.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Chestnut pony of the Festival of the Small Bow · named only by a stablehand in a hurry · 7 years old by his sheet","x":"A pony who bolts at the one small, furious thing he knows by smell. A chestnut pony whose name means only “chestnut-colored,” conferred by a stablehand in a hurry. On the twelfth of Eldren, on the meadow below the Shirase River, he carries the Minister of the Left's nine-year-old grandson at a walk and then a canter for the Festival's short course, while a hundred and twenty paces off the boy's teacher watches with his hands in his sleeves. A small russet cat who knows his smell leaps for the saddlecloth, misses, and catches his hock. He does not bolt toward the awnings, or toward the river with its water to stop him; he bolts on a diagonal toward the stone footings of the old bridge, which takes him four seconds. The boy goes over his shoulder with an arrow still in his hand. Afterward a patient Examiner finds a scrap of his chestnut hair in the grass at the starting line, with three tufts of russet fur beside it. The sheet's seven years and its white sock are its own; the manuscript gives him only his name, his colour, and the four seconds.","i":"images/characters/kurige-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kiyomaro","u":"characters/kiyomaro.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Sugino no Kiyomaro · grandson of the Minister of the Left · 9 years old","x":"Nine years old and dying, he asks the man who taught him the bow whether his thumb was right at the nock. Nine years old, grandson of Sugino no Arikuni and the only creature in the realm of Himuro who has ever persuaded that Minister to laugh aloud at his own supper. On the twelfth of Eldren, at the Festival of the Small Bow, he is allowed once in his life a short course on a pony and three blunted arrows at a target of rice-straw, and spends the morning behind the ranked families' pavilion learning from Tomobe no Higaide to settle the nocking-point against his thumb. Higaide discovers, with the helpless astonishment of a man whose own daughter is six and has not yet been persuaded to listen to anyone, that Kiyomaro listens. He rides Kurige at a walk and then a canter, upright and grave, a hundred and twenty paces from his teacher; then Azuki, arriving a half-breath late, catches the pony's hock, and he goes over the shoulder with an arrow still in his left hand, having been taught by a good teacher how to hold a bow and by nobody how to fall. The physician pronounces it a broken neck, which leaves the boy forty minutes in which to be a person rather than a wound. They carry him into the pavilion. He finds Higaide's sleeve with one hand and studies his face with the clear, interested attention of a child who has been told something is important and wants to get it right, and asks whether his thumb was right at the nock. “Perfect,” says Higaide. He dies a little before the hour of the Sheep. The Law that is built round his death, the Fifth Article and the Stone, is the thing that follows him; his grandfather, who weeps at neither pavilion nor pyre, begins the list of the dead he later copies out as sutras with the boy. The manuscript gives his name, his age, his listening and his question. The sheet's round face, twin side-loops and pale sage robe are its own. Was my thumb right?","i":"images/characters/kiyomaro-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Azuki","u":"characters/azuki.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"The Minister of the Left's household cat · named for the red bean · a round young cat at the Festival","x":"An animal who only wished to be somewhere other than her basket, and whom the histories leave out. A small, round, russet-spotted cat, named for the red bean, who wandered into the Minister of the Left's compound one winter, has been fed from his grandson's plate ever since, and follows Kiyomaro, with the serene entitlement of her kind, to every place he is not strictly permitted to take her. Nobody has seen her smuggled into the Festival of the Small Bow; Kinji alone sees her slip from the basket of cushions under the awning's edge and cross the grass at a trot, tail up, intending nothing at all, toward the one pony in the meadow she knows by smell. She believes, as cats believe, that a moving thing is an invitation. She leaps for the saddlecloth, reaches the hock of Kurige, and the pony bolts. The histories, when at last they reach the matter, find her inconvenient and leave her out, at a cost of four thousand lives. When the boy dies she walks up the grass to the pavilion, climbs into the vacant lap of the Minister of the Left, turns twice, and settles. Nobody asks the cat. She refuses her dinner for three days and sits before the closed door of the boy's chamber. On the night of the pyre the Minister warms her cushion against his own chest beneath his robe before setting it down, and she turns twice and lies in the place where the heat is. At Mikusa she is in the fold of his sleeve, and makes a small questioning sound as the captain turns back to the Stone; he had found her waiting at the gate at the hour of the Ox, and had not had the strength to say no. She grows old with him on Mikusa-yama, lean and light and stiff-jointed, her fur dull as a straw mat, following him from room to room with the unaccusing loyalty of an animal that has no theory of what has happened and no wish for one. In the Bureau's lower archive she climbs into the lap of the man on the floor, and he says, “Nobody asked you.” She dies in the spring, in his sleeve, which no one has the heart to take off the peg. The sheet's three years at the Festival, notched left ear, amber eyes and nine pounds are its own.","i":"images/characters/azuki-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kinji","u":"characters/kinji.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Groom at the Festival of the Small Bow · the only man who saw the cat · 40 years old","x":"Sees a cat cross the grass and, though he did not wish to say so, tells the Examiner. A thin, anxious man of forty, the groom at the Festival of the Small Bow, who will remember every instant of the next ten seconds for the rest of a long life. Nobody else has seen Azuki smuggled into the meadow; he alone sees her slip from the basket of cushions under the awning's edge and cross the grass at a trot, tail up, toward the one pony she happens to know by smell. He is the fourteenth of fourteen witnesses the Examiner interviews between the thirteenth and the sixteenth of Eldren. Thirteen tell him that the pony bolted. Kinji has not slept; he sits on the floor of the Bureau's inquiry room with his hands pressed between his knees and says that he saw a cat, and had not wished to say so, because the cat was the Minister's own and no sort of explanation for the death of a Minister's grandson. Yasutsugu writes it down in his plain brush-hand and goes out to the meadow, where a scrap of chestnut hair and three tufts of russet fur are waiting in the grass at the starting line, and the eleven sheets of his report follow from the groom's sentence. The report is held at the Minister's pleasure, and in the Plum Gate nobody says the word cat. The manuscript calls him only “the groom Kinji”; whom he served is not stated, and nothing is said of what became of him after the interview. The sheet's hollow-cheeked face, protruding ears and hands that wring each other when he is spoken to are its own.","i":"images/characters/kinji-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Arikuni","u":"characters/arikuni.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Sugino no Arikuni · Minister of the Left of Himuro, Senior Second Rank · author of the Law of the Clean Hand · grandfather of Kiyomaro · 66 at the opening, 71 when he reads the report","x":"Writes a law so that no death goes unsigned, lets it reach his own grandson's captain rather than waive it, and signs the confession of its fault in a book that nobody reads. Sixty-six, Minister of the Left of Himuro and, as the manuscript has it, the coldest man in the realm: he audits the ice tribute himself, bundle by bundle, in the last week of winter, and can tell from the sheen of a rice-ledger whether its keeper has been drinking. He has risen to the Senior Second Rank by being correct more often than his colleagues and unembarrassed about it. His grandson Kiyomaro is the only creature in the realm who has persuaded him to laugh aloud at his own supper. What he is proudest of is the Law of the Clean Hand. Eleven years before, a woman accused of poisoning her husband's elder wife put her arm in the scalding pot, and a physician's report afterward showed that the elder wife had died of a tumour; he abolished the ordeal within the month and built at the shrine of Mikusa a stone. Its Fourth Article, drafted in a single night, has the Stone take an officer as he stands in his office, with the badges of his oath upon him. Its Fifth, older than the Law by four centuries, is the Founder's Rule that he learned at seven from a primer that called it a measure against the Bloom Sickness, let no act go unsigned: the officer who signs the Order of the Day answers for the day, unless the Bureau of Inquiry enters a finding that no hand was at fault, a clause he added last, as a courtesy to chance. When the boy dies and Sukenori mentions the Article at the pyre, in the gentlest possible voice, he understands that to hope aloud for a finding of no hand would be to confess that the Stone's verdict is optional, and does not examine the understanding underneath it, that such a finding would leave the boy dead for no reason at all. That night he warms the cat's cushion against his own chest before he sets it down, and says to an open window, with the steward waiting inside the screen, The Law will want a hand; Tamenaga hears an instruction. He sends for the Examiner. At Mikusa, wrapped in a grey winter robe with Azuki curled in his sleeve, he hears the Captain ask to have it entered that he did not do it, and says, in the voice he has used for thirty years to dispose of petitions, It will be entered. He stops being able to remember the Captain's face, only the hand on the stone and its steadiness, and says near the end that he had never in his life been so sure that something had been done wrong. Asked by Sukenori whether a Law that reaches a captain for a death at a rite should reach a granary for a loss of rice, he says Yes. He resigns in the first winter of the Rising and retires to a hermitage on the slopes of Mikusa-yama, where he copies sutras for the dead: the boy first, then the captain on a sheet of his own, then the eleven. In the fifth winter Tamenaga, dying, tells him of a paper in the Bureau's lower archive, and he goes down himself, breaks his own household's seal on the cord and reads Yasutsugu's eleven sheets twice, from the pony to the cat. At the line about three tufts of russet fur he slides to the floor and makes a sound like the whine of a door in a gale, and the old cat climbs into his lap. Nobody asked you, he says to her. The Rule, he thinks, was not wrong; it had never been written for a world with a cat in it. He does not try to repeal the Law, which could not be repealed without saying that every hand it had taken was a hand it should have refused. He asks instead for the one clause that can be granted without being explained, that no accused be shown the Stone until the report has been read aloud to the Ministers who attest, and he copies the eleven sheets out in his own hand, adds a sentence that the Law had sent a captain to the Stone on the strength of a hand that did not exist, signs it in full, as the Founder's Rule required, and pastes it into the last leaf of the Book of the Ordeal. He waits through the winter for the summons that a signed account of the Law's own fault ought to bring. It does not come, because nobody has read the Book since the Captain. He dies that winter, and the cat in the spring. The manuscript gives his age, rank, coldness, habits, the grey winter robe and the seventy-one years he has when he reads the report. The sheet says in so many words that it has only the age, the robe and the coldness to work from; its 5 ft 6 in, narrow shoulders and slight stoop, thin white moustache and chin-wisp, age spots, ink-stained right thumb and always-cold hands, the purple court robe and black lacquered cap, and the undyed hemp robe of the hermitage are its own invention. The Law will want a hand.","i":"images/characters/arikuni-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Yasutsugu","u":"characters/yasutsugu.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Ōmi no Yasutsugu · Examiner of the Bureau of Inquiry, Sixth Rank · called the Sparrow Examiner · 50 years old","x":"Willing to be bored by the truth until it finished, he writes eleven sheets that say there was no hand, and is told the matter is closed. A stooped, patient man of fifty who has in unusual measure the one quality his profession requires and almost never rewards: he is willing to be bored by the truth until it finishes. Every morning in the courtyard of the Bureau's low wooden building he sets aside a handful of rice from his own bowl and scatters it on the gravel, and the sparrows, who know his step, come down in a brown rush round his sandals. The clerks call him the Sparrow Examiner, not unkindly, and he has never risen above the Sixth Rank. Assigned the death of Kiyomaro, he interviews fourteen witnesses between the thirteenth and the sixteenth of Eldren. Thirteen say that the pony bolted. The fourteenth is the groom, Kinji, who says that he saw a cat. He walks the course twice. On the fifth step before the bridge-footing he finds a scrap of chestnut hair and beside it three tufts of russet fur, and in the stable, on the saddlecloth, a pulled thread and the tiny specific tear that a claw leaves in wool. He sits on an upturned bucket and does the arithmetic that nobody has asked him to do, and on the sixteenth writes, in eleven careful sheets, that there was no hand at all: only an animal, a pony and a boy who had been taught to shoot and not to fall. The report goes to the steward of the Minister's house. On the day the summons is served he goes down to the lower archive and finds it bound in a cord that bears the household seal and the words held at the Minister's pleasure, and Tamenaga tells him that the matter is closed. He had not known until that morning that the truth could be taken out of his hands in the middle of a sentence. When Shizuka comes to him in the courtyard among the sparrows and asks where the motive came from, he says that the Bureau was given it, as a thing established, by the Left Ministry, and that he wrote a report on the matter which is no longer in his keeping and which he is not at liberty to describe. It was eleven sheets long, he says, and looks at the sparrows: all that his oath allows. She does not press it, and he bows lower than protocol. At the Reading of the Tally he sits among the clerks at the back and looks at the floor for the whole of what follows. Her other errand of those weeks, which she never names even to her cousin, is put to him in the same courtyard: the single favour that a man bound by a cord and a seal can grant without breaking either, the correction of a clerk's error. A daughter's name is carried off the Tomobe roll and onto her mother's father's, as a mistake discovered too late to be undone, and the Bureau's list of the disgraced ends with a commander, a captain and a wife, and omits a child; it is the only entry in all the ledgers of Himuro that is ever altered on Shizuka's account. He scatters his rice on every morning of every year that remains to him with the faint private rigidity of a man discharging a sentence, and after the Stone has come apart a young clerk begins, without being told why, to do the same. The manuscript gives his name, age, rank, sparrows and habits. The sheet's stooped narrow build of about 5 ft 7 in, thin grey hair with a bald crown and a small knot, heavy-lidded soft brown eyes, sallow paper-dry skin, a few grey chin whiskers, and the faded sixth-rank green silk with its worn cuffs are its own, and so is its last scene, The One Kindness His Oath Allows, which draws him handing a small folded paper to a young woman in a pale patterned robe. It was eleven sheets long.","i":"images/characters/yasutsugu-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Tamenaga","u":"characters/tamenaga.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Steward of the household of the Minister of the Left · forty-two years in Arikuni's service at the opening · about 75 by his sheet, 80 at his death","x":"Reads an Examiner's report, understands what his master would do with the word cat, and shelves it as a kindness. The gentle, devoted steward of the Minister of the Left's household, who has come in every night for forty-two years to ask whether the lamp may be lit, and who stands a little inside the screen with the stillness of a man who has made a long career of wishing to be furniture. On the night of the pyre he hears Arikuni say, to an open window, The Law will want a hand. He has spent four decades reading his master's wishes from the angle of a shoulder, and he hears an instruction. It is the only sentence of that week that he will repeat, and he repeats it once. The Examiner's report on the boy's death comes to him. He reads its eleven sheets twice and understands them perfectly, which is the trouble: he understands also what his master will do with the word cat and what it will do to him, the old man told that his grandson has died of the animal that sits on his knee, and the cat, the next morning, no longer in the house. He lays the report in a cabinet, writes on its cover Examiner's conjecture, not pressed; held at the Minister's pleasure, and binds it with the small seal of the household that no Examiner of the Bureau has ever been known to break. Then, because a hand without a reason is a gesture and a Minister's grandson cannot be buried on a gesture, he sits for two nights with the cabinet's key in his sleeve, and on the third goes out to find Sōroku, who is paid twelve bolts of silk to remember a weir. He does not think of himself as a liar; he arranges the story into its proper order. When Yasutsugu crosses the avenue to ask after his own report, Tamenaga, who looks as though he has not slept, tells him with great gentleness that the matter is closed. He dies in the fifth winter, eighty years old and forty-seven years in the household, and asks for the Minister to be sent for. He says that he has been unkind, once, in the course of a kindness. He tells him, twice, of the paper in the Bureau's lower archive, three shelves from the floor, with not pressed on its cover; of the man paid twelve bolts of silk; of what the Minister himself said in the study on the night of the pyre, which Arikuni cannot recall having said, the worst thing in the room. Then, with a look of perfect and appalling gentleness, he says that he thought it would be a mercy, and dies. The manuscript gives his service, his age at death, the cabinet, the cover and the cord, and gives his last words as reported speech; the line on the sheet puts them in his mouth. The sheet's 75 at the opening, pale fine-lined face, kind dark-brown eyes, thin white hair in a neat knot, small round-shouldered build, muted grey-blue robe and the ring of household keys at his sleeve are its own. I thought it would be a mercy.","i":"images/characters/tamenaga-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Examiner's Report","u":"characters/the-examiners-report.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Object · eleven folded sheets, the Bureau of Inquiry's report on the death of Kiyomaro · written 16 Eldren · shelved in the lower archive, not pressed","x":"Eleven careful sheets that find no hand at all, sealed in a cord no Examiner would break, and read in the end by the man whose household seal it carries. Eleven sheets in a plain black brush-hand, the work of Yasutsugu between the thirteenth and the sixteenth of Eldren, from the first paragraph, which concerns the pony, to the last, which concerns the cat. Its finding, entered on the sixteenth, is that there was no hand at all: only an animal, a pony, and a boy who had been taught to shoot and not to fall. Its evidence is a scrap of chestnut hair and three tufts of russet fur from the grass at the starting-line, and a pulled thread and a tiny claw-tear on the saddlecloth. It goes to the steward of the Minister's household, Tamenaga, who reads it twice, understands it perfectly, and lays it in a cabinet with the words Examiner's conjecture, not pressed; held at the Minister's pleasure on its cover, the cord sealed with the small seal of the household that no Examiner of the Bureau has ever been known to break. The Bureau's archive takes it from there, to lie three shelves from the floor under a layer of dust that a single visitor will remark on. Everything the Law does in its second life is built across the place where it would have gone. The summons served to Higaide says that the Bureau has entered no finding to excuse him; the Fifth Article, as the manuscript words it, lets a Bureau finding of no hand excuse the signatory, and nothing in the Law requires that the finding be read. Observer 618 reads the report at the archive shelf with his instruments the day before the ordeal and confirms that the subject is innocent. In the fifth winter Arikuni breaks the seal and reads it twice, and it is the line about the three tufts of russet fur that undoes him. He copies the eleven sheets out in his own hand, signs a sentence of confession beneath the last, and pastes the copy into the Book of the Ordeal; the only effect it has in the Law is the clause that the new Left Minister grants in the margin, that no accused be shown the Stone until the report has been read aloud. The manuscript says that the report lies on its shelf for the better part of two decades, and also has Arikuni open it in the fifth winter; the page takes both to be so, the bundle being still on its shelf, in its dust, when the Stone comes apart. The sheet draws the bundle front and in profile, about a centimetre thick: eleven folded sheets of mulberry-bark paper, cream with browned and foxed edges, tied crosswise with twisted hemp cord under a small vermilion seal, with close-ups of the paper, the black sumi ink, the cord and the seal. Its other panels are Evidence (russet tufts and a scrap of saddlecloth labelled claw-tear), Burial (a hand sliding the bundle onto a shelf), Reading (an open book with the words no hand at all; only an animal, a pony, and a boy who had been taught to shoot and not to fall and the date Sixteenth of Eldren), Confession (a book of closely written pages with a brush and an ink-stone) and, along the foot, Its Reading Breaks Arikuni, a dark-robed man bent over the pages in the archive. The sheets, the cover note, the cord and the household seal are the manuscript's; the paper, the ink, the vermilion and the size are the sheet's own. No hand at all: only an animal, a pony, and a boy who had been taught to shoot and not to fall.","i":"images/characters/the-examiners-report-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sōroku","u":"characters/soroku.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Professional false witness of the lower city of Tsukigase · fifty-odd by the manuscript, 54 by his sheet · fee, twelve bolts of silk","x":"Makes a modest and respectable living out of having been standing in the correct doorway, and charges twelve bolts of silk for a memory. A man who makes a modest and respectable living out of having been standing in the correct doorway: fifty-odd, round-faced, dressed in the brown of a respectable tradesman, with an expression of such honest, unremarkable attention that no magistrate has ever thought to doubt him. Tamenaga knows him, as everybody in the lower city of Tsukigase knows a man like him. Asked over a pot of warm wine in a back room whether he recalls a certain remark made some years before, in a certain tavern, by a certain captain, about a certain weir, he recalls it in whatever detail the question suggests. He charges twelve bolts of silk. He feels nothing whatever about Higaide, whom he has seen once in his life at a distance of forty paces, and on the night he agrees to the commission he sleeps the sleep of a man who has sold a chair. The story he tells, which Tamenaga merely arranges into its proper order, is true in every particular but one. Twenty-two years earlier the Tomobe family held a small estate at Higashino, and a boundary dispute over the Higashino weir was decided against them by the Left Ministry, then in the hands of a younger and more thorough Arikuni; Higaide's father, Nariyuki, was demoted two ranks and died, some years afterward, a bitter and reduced man. A child could have verified every detail, and nobody thinks to verify the one that is missing, that Higaide, twelve at the time, has never discussed the weir with anyone and has not thought of it in a decade. Sōroku supplies a tavern, a night and a few words said over wine by a young guard-officer about the Left Minister's family and the price of long memory. It has the shape of a motive, and shapes are what motives are made of in a court. By the twentieth of Eldren the tale is in the wine-houses, and the summons that says the motive has been established to the satisfaction of the Left Ministry is served on the twelfth of Garren. Years later Arikuni, who could have had him found in an afternoon, discovers that for the first time in his life he does not wish to audit a figure, and does not propose to ask whose silk it was that he had been grateful for. Sōroku is, very probably, prosperous. Case 0202 has the same estate from the inside, in Higaide's memory: its steward, Genzō, and the year the weir was lost. The manuscript gives Sōroku's trade, his fee, his build in outline, his brown, his expression and the story. The sheet's 54 years, plump 5 ft 4 in frame, ruddy skin, warm brown eyes, short grey-black hair and clean-shaven face are its own, and so is its one scene, Twelve Bolts of Silk: Tamenaga's Paid Memory, in which he sits with his hands folded at a plain table, a cup before him, while a dark-haired man in a dark figured robe leans toward him and bolts of silk are stacked at the edge of the frame. The sheet does not say who the second man is. Its expression panels run Honest Attention, Mild Concern, Patient Listening and Easy Sleep.","i":"images/characters/soroku-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Plum Gate Patrons","u":"characters/plum-gate-patrons.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Regulars of the Plum Gate wine-house in Tsukigase's east ward · a dyer's apprentice, a fishmonger and a seller of pickled radish · 17, 45 and 70 by their sheet","x":"Three regulars of a smoky wine-house who turn a captain's name into a tale, and none of whom says the word cat. The Plum Gate is a long, low, smoky wine-house near the east ward's market in Tsukigase, famous for a cheap cloudy wine that its keeper calls river-ice and for the fact that every clerk in the Left Ministry's lesser offices drinks there on the last day of each week. By the twentieth of Eldren the tale that Sōroku has put together in a back room has entered the wine-houses of the city, and it does there what tales do. It is said in the Plum Gate that the Captain has always carried two swords like a man with two minds; that the pony was drugged, or was given nothing and was merely looked at by the Captain with the eye of a fox-woman's husband; that his father cursed the Sugino family on his deathbed in a voice heard three streets away; that he is in fact innocent and is being made to carry some more important man's guilt. Three of its regulars are known by what they say. A dyer's apprentice says that he has it on good authority that the boy's neck was broken by an arrow. A fishmonger says, with the air of a man giving evidence, that he once sold the Captain an eel. An old woman who sells pickled radish by the door, raised on the nursery warning that a death with nobody's name on it is the work of the Threads, the palace women of the dead capital who were said to have killed so cleanly that no thread ever led back to a hand (a legend of Case 4099, from which Himuro descends), says only that a boy who died without a hand on him will never be properly buried, and tells the apprentice to eat his rice. Nobody in the Plum Gate says the word cat. The cat is not, in the language of the wine-house, a cause of anything. The groom Kinji is the one man in the case who says it aloud, to the Examiner. The manuscript gives the three their trades and their remarks, and no names, ages or looks. The sheet's 17, 45 and 70, the thin lad, the stocky tradesman and the small bent woman, their head-cloths, tan skin and dark eyes, the apprentice's blue-stained fingers, the fishmonger's thin moustache and the cups of river-ice are its own, and so is its role line, Spread the rumors about Higaide: arrow-through-the-neck, the eel, the Threads. Its last scene, The Rumors of Higaide, draws the three heads together over the table with a fourth man, in a dark topknot, turned away at the edge; the sheet does not say who he is.","i":"images/characters/plum-gate-patrons-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Bureau Officers","u":"characters/bureau-officers.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Officers of the Bureau of Inquiry · one about 40, one about 28 by their sheet","x":"Two men in dark robes who serve a summons with the regretful courtesy reserved for the condemned. Two officers of the Bureau of Inquiry, one older and one younger, in dark robes, who come to a compound in the third ward on the twelfth of Garren, a week after the Seven Sevens have ended, and bow with the careful, regretful courtesy kept for the condemned. They bring a summons under the seals of both Ministers: that the Fifth Article obliges the Captain to answer for the day of the Festival, that his motive has been established to the satisfaction of the Left Ministry, that the Bureau has entered no finding to excuse him, and that he may clear his hand at the Stone of Mikusa at dawn on the twentieth. Eight days later they walk behind him at a polite distance through the dark to the shrine, with a boy before them carrying a lantern, and are two of the nine witnesses on the dais. The manuscript gives them neither names nor ages, and the figures on their sheet are its own.","i":"images/characters/bureau-officers-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Rider from the North","u":"characters/rider-from-the-north.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Messenger of the Kurogane March garrison at Fort Shirakane · unnamed · about 16 years old by his sheet","x":"Kills a horse to bring four lines to a man who will not go, and the manuscript says nothing more of him. A boy in a mud-caked cloak who reaches the Tomobe compound in the third ward on the eighteenth of Garren, two days before the ordeal, having killed a horse to arrive in time. He carries in the lining of his sleeve a letter from Tomobe no Narimune, commander of the Kurogane March garrison at Fort Shirakane and Higaide's elder cousin, the only person alive who has ever seen him cry. The letter is four lines long. It says that a man who walks into an ordeal because the Minister has built one is not proving anything; that the garrison has been told to expect a guest; that three horses will wait at the Northern Gate until the hour of the Rat on the nineteenth; and, in a last line entirely characteristic of its writer, that old One-Eye would certainly have told him that a man with two swords should be able to find the door. Higaide laughs once, with a sound like a cough, and does not go. The Law holds that a hand which fled had spoken, and under the Sixth Article a fugitive's kin forfeit the estate; and, more simply, he does not believe it necessary. He has signed an order of the day, a child has died on it, and if the kami wish to be consulted he sees no reason to make them chase him through the snow. The rider is given no name, no reply to carry, and no later scene, and nothing is said of the three horses. When the Court's second summons reaches the fort, Narimune answers in four lines built from the same sentences and ending that the door is open, which the histories count as the first line of the Rising of the Northern Gate. He is not the boy of sixteen on the north wall who later shoots Narimune; the sixteen years on his own sheet are its own, and so are its wiry build, hollow eyes and black hair tied with a leather thong.","i":"images/characters/rider-from-the-north-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Narimune","u":"characters/narimune.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Tomobe no Narimune · Commander of the Kurogane March garrison at Fort Shirakane · Higaide's elder cousin · 40 at the opening, 42 at the north gate","x":"Sends four lines and three horses to a cousin who will not run, and later brings an army to a gate and a joke to a wall. Commander of the Kurogane March garrison at Fort Shirakane, Higaide's elder cousin and, as the manuscript says, the only person alive who has ever seen him cry: a big, scarred, loud, uneasy man of forty with one lopsided eyebrow and a laugh that he does not use. Two days before the ordeal a rider from the north, a boy who has killed a horse to arrive in time, brings his letter, four lines long. It says that a man who walks into an ordeal because the Minister has built one is not proving anything; that the garrison has been told to expect a guest; that three horses will wait at the Northern Gate until the hour of the Rat on the nineteenth; and that old One-Eye would have told him that a man with two swords should be able to find the door. Higaide laughs once, and does not go. In the second week of Iverin Shizuka and Tsuru reach the fort by ox-cart with two chests, a bundle and the lacquer box. He meets them at the gate, looks at his cousin's widow for a long moment and says nothing at all; he takes the child onto his shoulders and the box in both hands. That night he reads the notice of the dispossession and the Left Ministry's summons to himself, to appear at Tsukigase within thirty days and clear his hand, as the near kin of one the kami has taken, of whatever he may know. He does not appear. He walks the rampart in the snow until dawn, and when the Court's second summons comes he sends back, by the same rider, the same four lines, ending that the door is open. It is the first line, in two years, of what the histories will call the Rising of the Northern Gate: three thousand men who have held the marches against the Emishi for six generations with a great deal of principle and no money. The Rising lasts two years and a season and costs, by the Court's own count, four thousand and one hundred lives. It ends in the third winter at the north gate of the capital, where a herald of nineteen named Haruyoshi, sent out under a white flag because no one with a rank to lose could be spared, watches the Commander ride to within a bowshot of the wall on a tired grey horse and sit looking up at it, eyebrow raised, as a man looks at a very expensive door. He is forty-two, heavy, scarred and tired, and the herald, who has been told that he is a monster, finds himself liking him. Asked what the Court wishes, the herald recites the old terms, that the Commander come to Tsukigase and clear his hand and the army be pardoned, and Narimune, for the first time in the memory of anyone who has served under him, laughs in earnest. I was sent for to clear my hand, and it seemed discourteous to bring only the one. An arrow loosed by a boy of sixteen on the wall, who has been told to shoot the first thing that moves, takes him in the throat before the laugh is done, and the army, which followed one man and holds no instructions for the loss of him, stands in its ranks for the length of a morning and then turns north in good order and goes home. The manuscript gives his age, his build in outline, the eyebrow, the laugh and the last line. The sheet's 6 ft 0 in, wind-reddened skin, short bristly black beard, black-laced lamellar armour and weathered sheepskin cloak are its own, and so is the cause of the eyebrow, a blade wound. Its scenes are Rampart, After the Summons, Carries the lacquer box in both hands when Shizuka arrives and North Gate, and its expression panels run Uneasy, Contained Laughter, Resolute and Last Joke. I was sent for to clear my hand, and it seemed discourteous to bring only the one.","i":"images/characters/narimune-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Old Master One-Eye","u":"characters/old-master-one-eye.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"One-eyed veteran of the Kurogane marches · the old master who showed Higaide the two swords · about 65 years old by his sheet","x":"Beats a young man senseless in a cedar wood, apologizes profusely, and shows him how it was done. He is never on the page in the present. In the opening chapter a one-eyed veteran of the Kurogane marches is the reason Tomobe no Higaide wears the long tachi Yoake at his left hip and the short straight Yoiyami at his right, a pairing the court considers an affectation: the veteran had once beaten the young Higaide senseless in a cedar wood and then, apologizing profusely, shown him how it was done. The habits of the lesson outlast it. Higaide runs with his scabbards held against his thighs in the way his old master insisted upon, and before any duty he considers serious he unties and re-ties each sword-cord seven times, which the old master called the only superstition he had ever seen a man acquire in the interest of better knots. His one other appearance is a joke in a letter. Narimune, in the four lines a rider brings two days before the ordeal, says that old One-Eye would certainly have told his cousin that a man with two swords should be able to find the door, and Higaide laughs, once, and stays. The manuscript uses “a one-eyed veteran”, “his old master” and “old One-Eye” without saying outright that they are one man, and never says whether he is alive, which eye he lacks, or what he was called. The sheet takes them for one man and adds the rest: an eye-patch over the left eye, white cropped hair, a lined and scarred face, a field coat and sheepskin vest, a long blade and a short, and about sixty-five years. Its caption, a man with two swords finding the door, compresses Narimune's line. Of the rider who brings it, the text says nothing further either.","i":"images/characters/old-master-one-eye-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The New Left Minister","u":"characters/new-left-minister.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Successor Minister of the Left after Arikuni's resignation · unnamed · about 45 years old by his sheet","x":"Grants the one amendment that costs nothing and implies nothing, and leaves the Law standing. A nervous, dutiful, ambitious man, who takes the Left Ministry when Sugino no Arikuni resigns in the first winter of the Rising and retires to a hermitage on the slopes of Mikusa-yama. Years later Arikuni sends for him and tells him, in a voice like dry paper, what he has read in the Bureau's lower archive: eleven sheets, a pony, a cat, and a Law that sent a captain to the Stone on a hand that did not exist. The Minister listens, and says with great respect that the Law cannot be put aside. If it were repealed, it would have to be said why; if it were said why, it would be said that every hand the Stone had taken was a hand it should have refused, eleven houses and a captain and a Right Minister of the realm, and that the Court had killed them. It cannot be said. Arikuni asks, then, for the one thing that can be granted without being explained: that no accused be shown the Stone until the Examiner's report has been read aloud to the Ministers who attest and entered in the record. The Minister grants it with visible relief, since it costs nothing and implies nothing, and it goes into force that afternoon for a Law that nobody will put to use. A Law that has been wrong, Arikuni concludes, cannot be corrected aloud, because its correction is a confession; it can be amended only in the margin, where nobody reads. The Law outlives its author by six years, unused, and lapses in the twelfth year under a successor court that wishes to be seen correcting something. The manuscript gives this minister no name, age or appearance beyond nervous, dutiful and ambitious. The sheet's forty-five years, deep-purple robe, folding fan, oiled topknot, and finger tapped against the sleeve when he calculates, are its own.","i":"images/characters/new-left-minister-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Boy-Sovereign of Himuro","u":"characters/boy-sovereign-of-himuro.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Sovereign of Himuro · 14 years old at the opening · abdicates into a monastery","x":"Sits a throne at fourteen between two Ministers, and leaves it for a monastery. A sovereign of fourteen sits the throne of Himuro, as sovereigns of fourteen generally do, which is to say that the realm is governed by the two Ministers who stand on either side of him, the Left and the Right, in an arrangement of mutual surveillance so old that neither can remember which began it. He has no name and no lines in the manuscript. He is the fixed point between Sugino no Arikuni and Sukenori, whose thirty years of watching each other the whole of the Law of the Clean Hand grows out of; the court, the Festival and the Stone all go on in his reign, and none of them needs him to speak. By the third winter of the Rising he has abdicated into a monastery; the Log lists the abdication among the results, beside the fall of both Ministries. The histories of Himuro, which are agreed on the causes of the Rising, give it as the ambition of provincial warriors and the arrogance of a Left Ministry in its declining years, and, in the more philosophical commentaries, as the natural consequence of a boy-sovereign and two old men. The manuscript says nothing of his age at the abdication or of his life afterward. The sheet gives him a slight, narrow-shouldered build, a very pale indoor complexion, a black youth's topknot under a tall court cap, deep saffron robes over white, an ivory tablet in his hands, and, after the abdication, a shaven head and a plain grey monk's robe in a snowy cloister. All of that is the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/boy-sovereign-of-himuro-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bertran","u":"characters/bertran.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Sir Bertran de Lanta · master-at-arms of Castelmaur · knight · 38 years old","x":"Cannot enter a mercy in any column, so he rides out alone one night to repay it. A stern, lean, well-made man of thirty-eight who has fought in six minor wars and won five, and who goes through life by double entry: debts incurred and debts paid, blows given and blows returned. On the green at Vilarnau he is disarmed in a single movement by a foreigner who then sheathes both swords and bows with the painful apology of a man who has spilled wine, having left him alive by choice. Bertran has no column in which to enter a mercy. He does not hate the man; it is a respect so complete that it has nowhere to go. In the evenings he carves small pearwood horses and sends them, in rags, to his widowed sister's four children, who have never been told who makes them. On the sixteenth of Jorren, when the baron orders the stone broken and the guest taken, he rides down to the mill alone and without a banner, knocks at the door, and tells Gersenda once what is coming. At the end of the yard, without turning, he adds the one line that was not a warning. On the night of the torches he is given forty men and spends forty minutes in a thicket on a perfectly sound hoof. He asks leave to resign his sword, is refused, and rides out of the gate without it. In the fourteenth year he comes to Lasserra on foot, sets a small grey gelding on the bench of the last house, and is not seen again. The same man has a page under Case 0202, whose sheet makes him forty-one; this one makes him thirty-eight. The two pages are not reconciled. Tell him that we are even.","i":"images/characters/bertran-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bertran's Sister","u":"characters/bertrans-sister.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Widowed sister of Sir Bertran de Lanta · mother of four · valley of the Nauze · about 34 years old by her sheet","x":"Her children receive a small carved horse for nine years, and are never told whose hands made it. She never appears on the page. The manuscript gives Bertran a widowed sister in the valley of the Nauze, with four children, and one other thing besides: it is her embroidered woolen blanket, patterned with horses, that he lies under in the narrow chamber above the armory at Castelmaur, working the same small piece of arithmetic in the dark. For nine years he has carved wonderfully accurate pearwood horses in the evenings, ears laid back and tails half-lifted, and sent them to the children wrapped in rags; and not one of them has ever been told who makes them. It is the private life that the household of Castelmaur would have said he did not have. The last horse the manuscript follows is the one he finishes in the last week of Iverin, a grey gelding with one raised hoof, which he turns in his palm while thinking of how the stranger sheathed both swords, left and right, like a man closing a book. When he rides out of the barony after the burning he carries a small wooden horse in his saddlebag; and in the fourteenth year he sets a grey gelding with one raised hoof on the bench of the last house at Lasserra and walks away. The text does not say whether that is the same horse, or whether his sister ever learns who the carver was. The sheet's thirty-four years, the children's ages (four to twelve), her needle-pricked fingertips and the row of horses on the sill are its own, as is its caption that she is never told who sends them.","i":"images/characters/bertrans-sister-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Miller of Vilarnau","u":"characters/miller-of-vilarnau.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Late miller of the Mill of the Two Weirs · husband of Ermengarda, father of Gersenda and Peire · died four winters before the opening of a cough · 42 by his sheet","x":"Never on the page, he leaves a mill, a widow, a long patient face in his daughter, and the cough that killed him. He is dead before the story begins. Ermengarda is introduced as the widow of the miller of Vilarnau, who had died four winters before of a cough, and she runs the Mill of the Two Weirs herself with a hired boy, a stubborn red ox, a daughter of sixteen and a son of eight. All the manuscript keeps of him besides the cough is a face: Gersenda, studying the stranger from the doorway, has her mother's gap in the teeth and her late father's long patient face. What he leaves is the house the story happens in: the mill with its race frozen in Kerren and its two weirs, one of whose sluice-gates Higaide mends; the loft above the grinding-floor, warm from the stones turning below, where the stranger is put; the byre where the red ox stands; the forty measures of seed-grain that his widow will not give up to the sergeant; and a son, Peire, whom the valley calls the miller's son. The manuscript does not say whether the ox, the weirs or the loft were his, and the page does not guess. The sheet says that he appears only in recollection, that the long patient face comes from his daughter and that everything else is invented. Its man is 42, tall at 5 ft 10 in and flour-dusted, with thin brown hair, grey-brown eyes, a short brown beard, a long lined face and eyebrows whitened with dust, in an undyed linen smock, with a miller's pick for dressing stones. One panel is captioned Last Winter (cough) and shows him with a hand to his mouth; its scene, A day at the mill, before the cough, shows him standing with his pick beside a millstone in a room full of light. The age, the build, the beard and the pick are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/miller-of-vilarnau-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ermengarda","u":"characters/ermengarda.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Widow of the miller of Vilarnau · keeper of the Mill of the Two Weirs · mother of Gersenda and Peire · about 38 years old by her sheet","x":"Feeds a stranger because he looks cold, and learns that welcome is the most expensive thing she owns. A small, tough, brown woman with a gap in her front teeth and a laugh that carries across the Lauze, who has run the Mill of the Two Weirs herself since the miller died of a cough four winters back, with a hired boy, a stubborn red ox called Lo Rog, a daughter of sixteen, Gersenda, and a son of eight. She puts a bowl into the stranger's hands at dusk for no better reason than that he looks cold, having been raised to think a cold man an obligation, and by the custom of the Charter the guest is thereby hers, since she fed him first. She feeds him again in the loft above the grinding-floor, and he bows to her with the same grave motion he gave the stone: she has been bowed to by a priest and never by someone who meant it. At Solan's fire she pushes him bodily into the front rank, and when Father Guiraut comes to say that the Moon-Man cannot bow to the god here, she tells him that he bows to everything, he bowed to the ox. She knows from the first morning that something has entered her house which cannot be fed, and that it will cost her. When Bertran comes by night to warn them she will not go to the hills: a woman who leaves her seed to be counted by strangers might as well not have sown. On the first of Kerren the forty measures are in her loft when the sergeant climbs the ladder, and her son is thrown across it; looking up at the stranger over the boy's body, she has the face of a woman who has been told a total and is doing the sum. “They will come now.” She is one of the forty-nine in the quarry cleft, dies in the ninth year of a fall from the ladder of the apple-loft, and is buried under a chestnut with the whole High Ground in attendance. Her great-granddaughter has her laugh. The manuscript gives her no age; the sheet's thirty-eight and its flour-creased knuckles are its own. He bows to everything. He bowed to the ox.","i":"images/characters/ermengarda-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Gersenda","u":"characters/gersenda.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Miller's daughter · later wife of the smith's surviving son and leader of the High Ground · 16 at the story's opening, 35 at the departure","x":"Teaches a stranger the word for door, and keeps his unreadable poem in the bread-box. The miller's daughter at the Mill of the Two Weirs, sixteen, with her mother's gap in her front teeth and her late father's long patient face, and the frank clinical interest of a girl who has been told to find something wrong with a person and cannot. She is appointed, by the unspoken vote of the household, to teach the foreigner the language, in the slow flat voice one uses to an elderly, intelligent animal, working through the whole kitchen from the pot to the ladle to the small cracked salt-bowl. She drives a hard bargain at the wool-market, and in return he teaches her his word for stone. She is the first to hear him in the barn, and she keeps everyone away from it that afternoon. She answers the door to Bertran at night with a candle and an iron ladle held down against her thigh, and on the night of the torches takes the halter, and the others, across the plank into the dark. On the ridge she marries the smith's surviving son and bears five children; she runs the High Ground's affairs with a hard bargain and a long patient face. She keeps a few of the old man's verses, hidden, in a bread-box, and on the day he goes east she hears him say the name at the boundary-stone, as though telling someone where he was going. The morning after, it is she who finds the sheet he has left for her.","i":"images/characters/gersenda-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Peire (Higa the Younger)","u":"characters/peire-2231.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Miller's son of Vilarnau · later chronicler of the High Ground, called Higa the Younger · 8 at the arrival, 27 at the departure","x":"The first to see the stranger come out of the stone, he bows back, bites a sergeant's man, and grows up to write down what he saw. The miller's son of Vilarnau, eight years old at dusk on the nineteenth of Garren, sent to bring in the geese and, being eight, taking the long way round by the green because the long way contained the possibility of a frog. He stands at the edge of the oak's shadow with a switch in one hand and his mouth slightly open and watches the whole arrival from forty paces: a tall man in strange dark clothes who looks a little embarrassed. The man bows, unhurried and exact, with his hands flat on his thighs, and Peire, who has been taught to bow by a priest and has never seen it done so well, bows back, a moment late, with the unselfconscious thoroughness of a boy imitating something holy. He will tell it for sixty years, and in the telling the man will be made of moonlight and rise out of the stone like steam from a kettle, with eyes like two small coals, while the oak bends and an owl cries; what he saw was a tall man who looked a little embarrassed. He runs to find the reeve, Old Bernat, who says without looking up from his pipe that the last one died. He is the boy who names the tally-stone the Ishi, the stranger's word for stone, with the relish of a boy saying the name of a new and slightly dangerous friend, so that the green's old block goes by two names at once and then, in the lane's own tongue, becomes the Doorstone. He claps a hand over his mouth to keep from laughing when Sir Bertran is disarmed, and has an alarming interest in swords. By the first snow he is wearing, without knowing what it is, a coat made from an unpicked uniform. On the first of Kerren, told to stay in the kitchen, he follows his mother up the ladder to the loft and goes for the sergeant's man with his teeth when the man lays a hand on the first sack of seed-grain, laying the knuckle open to the bone; he is flung backhanded across the loft into a corner post and lies still with a dark thread of blood coming down through his hair. It is what ends Higaide's vow. He lives, with a cracked skull and a long night, and carries a scar through his left eyebrow for the rest of his life; he goes up to the quarry on Higaide's back, wrapped in a blanket. On the ridge he helps build the last house on the path. At twenty-seven he is a broad, brown, laughing man with a gap in his teeth, and the first in Lasserra to carry the stranger's name: they call him Higa the Younger. He has been taught to hold a sword and has discovered, with a regret that does him credit, that he prefers a pen, and in the evenings of his thirties he sits by his own hearth with a tallow dip and writes down the whole of what he saw, in a chronicle that will be lost in a later century in a fire at Sant-Rustic. He walks beside Higaide, carrying the flour, as far as the boundary-stone of the charcoal-burners' land on the morning he goes east, and copies the strokes of the heron poem into the margin of his chronicle without a notion of what they say. The Peire of Case 4181 is a man of twenty-seven of the High Ground who receives Higaide's goodbye before the walk east; the two pages take him to be this man, and the age and the parting agree, though that case's sheet gives him grey eyes and no scar where this one gives hazel eyes and a scar through the left eyebrow. The manuscript gives Peire's age, the switch and the geese, the bow, the bite, the coat, the cracked skull, the scar, the gap, the pen and the name. The sheet's 4 ft 2 in as a boy and 5 ft 9 in as a man, freckles, brown hair with a stubborn cowlick, hazel eyes and the gap in the teeth like his mother's are its own, and so are its expression panels Wonder, Defiance, Witness and Quiet Hope; its small scene, With the flour at the boundary-stone, then he writes what he saw, puts a boy, a goose, a sack and a marked stone together in one frame.","i":"images/characters/peire-2231-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Lo Rog","u":"characters/lo-rog.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"The red ox of the Mill of the Two Weirs · the worst-tempered animal in the barony · 8 years old by his sheet","x":"Has bitten a priest and kicked a cooper, and stands quite still when a stranger bows to him. The mill's red ox, generally acknowledged to be the worst-tempered animal in the barony, who has bitten a priest and kicked a cooper. He is a stubborn animal and a patient one: he will stand quite still if the stranger bows to him first, and the household, which has resisted the idea of the bow for weeks, adopts it for the ox before it does for anyone else. When Ermengarda is asked to say what sort of man the Moon-Man is, it is the ox she cites: He bows to everything. He bowed to the ox. He is the only witness of the worst hour of Higaide's life. On an afternoon in Iverin, the day before the first frost, Old Bernat walks the stranger out under the oak and tells him where the last man out of the stone lies, and Higaide goes to the barn and down on his knees in the straw with his forehead against the red ox's flank, leaning the whole weight of his body on the warm, patient, chewing animal. It lasts an hour. Lo Rog stands quite still in his stall and chews, with the slow immovable patience of an animal that has borne a good deal in its life, and does not so much as shift his weight. When it is over Higaide lays Yoake and Yoiyami across his open palms in the straw and tells the only witness in the building who will not repeat it that he will not draw them in this house, whatever the house comes to cost; and the ox turns his great red head and breathes once on the back of his neck, as one acknowledges a signature. On the night of the torches Higaide leads him up the frozen lane by the halter, and the manuscript counts one ox among the forty-nine in the quarry cleft. The bow goes with the man. Nineteen years on, in Case 4181, he bows from the waist, with his hands flat on his thighs, to another ox, Brun, in the fog of the Hohlweg. Case 0202 has Lo Rog look in at the kettle while Higaide tries to say a word back. The manuscript gives Lo Rog his colour, his temper, his bites and kicks, the barn and the bow; the age is the sheet's, and so are the 5 ft 3 in at the shoulder, the dark brown slow eyes, the long wet muzzle, the curved horns with the chipped left tip and the yoke of aged wood and iron. Its expression panels run Patient, Irritated, Watchful and Worst-Tempered, and its three small scenes draw the barn: He stood still when Higaide bowed, Witness: the collapse and He breathed once on the back of his neck. Breathed once on the back of his neck, as one acknowledges a signature.","i":"images/characters/lo-rog-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Smith's Son","u":"characters/smiths-son.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Son of the smith of Vilarnau · later husband of Gersenda · 19 years old at the weighing","x":"Reads one book, finds the plank that settles the village's fate, and carries a dead man's hammer up a ridge. Nineteen, the son of the village smith, who has read one book in his life and likes to be at the front of any idea. To him the stranger is Saint Two-Swords, though the saint is still in the stage of having no feast-day and no church. In Brunissen's tavern on a sleety evening in Jorren, when a man at the back says that the door leads to the baron's cellar, he goes red and says that the cellar is the one place in the country he wishes it did lead to. When the weigher's widow asks how a sack of dusty barley is to be set on a door to heaven, he is the one who proposes that the village weigh at the old oak instead, on a plank, with the beam hung from a branch, and tell the bailiff that the green's stone is being kept clear in honor of the visitor. A fat man points out that the Charter says the stone. The smith's son answers that the Charter was written by men who had never seen a door open. The hands go up one by one, with two left in laps, and it is settled. Nobody in the room names what is being given up; the Observer's notes spell it out afterward. His father the smith does not live to reach the ridge, and the son carries the dead man's hammer up the quarry road on his back and finds, in thirty years, no use for it. On the ridge he marries Gersenda, who bears him five children. The manuscript never names him. The sheet's curling black hair, soot-darkened skin and patchy beard are its own, and so is the compression of the manuscript's remark into its caption, “at the front of any idea.”","i":"images/characters/smiths-son-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bernat","u":"characters/bernat.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Old Bernat · reeve of Vilarnau · 79 years old","x":"Has seen a great deal and been impressed by very little, and does not leave. The reeve of Vilarnau, an ancient with a cold clay pipe, who sits on the bench outside the forge with the posture of a man who has seen a great deal and been impressed by very little of it. Told a man has come out of the stone, he does not look up. He settles the first morning's trouble by the Charter: any stranger who comes to the green at dusk and is not turned out by sunrise is the village's guest, and the guest, he rules, is the miller's widow's, since she fed him first. He walks the foreigner out under the oak to a flat grey fieldstone the size of a bread-board and tells him that the last man had lain there nine years. At the tavern, on the evening it is decided to weigh at the oak, he is seen reckoning on his fingers, and says only that the baron would have to be told civilly. On the green, facing the baron, he says the stone is not to be used that day. When the baron's banner has vanished behind the hill it is he who says, in a voice that does not sound like winning, that they have won. On the night of the torches he goes from door to door with a lantern. He is seventy-nine, and he does not leave. His pipe is found afterward in the ashes of the bench outside the forge. The last one died.","i":"images/characters/bernat-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Brunissen","u":"characters/brunissen.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Keeper of the tavern of Vilarnau · 40 years old","x":"Never lets a silence last a minute, and is the one person in the lane who says what the stone will cost. A broad, bright-faced, loud-voiced woman of forty, who sells a thin bitter ale and a thinner red wine under a crooked chestnut-branch sign painted yellow in her father's time, and who has never in twenty years allowed a silence to last more than a minute. It is she who asks, wiping a mug on her apron, where the stone goes if it is a door. On the sleet-ticking evening when the lane votes to take its grain to a plank under the oak and keep the green's stone clear for its guest, hers is one of the two hands that stay in a lap, because she does not vote on anything she will have to serve drinks through afterward. Through a crack in her shutter she watches the foreigner lay his hand on the polished stone in the sleet, and says, to no one, in a very quiet voice, that it is going to be a long winter. She is correct on every point but the length. After the burning she carries her father's sign up the quarry road, scorched along one edge, and hangs it in the second spring on the door of the largest house on the path. She keeps a tavern there for thirty years and never again allows a silence to last a minute. Nobody asks her why.","i":"images/characters/brunissen-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Weigher's Widow","u":"characters/weighers-widow.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Widow who keeps the village's tally-sticks · Vilarnau · 55 years old by her sheet","x":"Asks, in a warm tavern, how a woman is to set a sack of dusty barley on a door to heaven. A pinched small woman of Vilarnau who keeps the village's tally-sticks, and the one voice in Brunissen's tavern that asks the practical question. In a lull, after the room has laughed at a door that leads to the baron's cellar, she says in a thin voice from the corner of the hearth that what the village should worry about is not where the stone leads but what it is standing in the way of. The bailiff will come on the twentieth with the new weights and the new rate; every sack in the parish must be laid on the stone before witnesses, as the Charter requires; and she does not know, for her part, how a woman is to set a sack of dusty barley on a door to heaven. The room, which had been warm, grows a little cold. It is the whole of the argument, and it is the argument that the plank under the oak is put forward to answer. That evening she buys a splinter of chestnut from Gaucelm, who sells them as relics of the True Doorstone. On the first of Kerren a pig is taken from her in the forenoon distraint of the east lane, and screams all the way to the cart. The manuscript does not name her or her late husband, the weigher, and does not say whether she is among the dead, the ridge or the dispersed. The sheet's fifty-five years, bright black eyes, grey hair under a tight linen cap and the bundle of notched sticks at her belt are its own.","i":"images/characters/weighers-widow-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Gaucelm","u":"characters/gaucelm.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Tinker, seller of needles and relics along the Lauze · thirty years on the road · about 55 years old by his sheet","x":"Sells splinters of the True Doorstone, and tells a tavern the one thing it should have been worrying about. A talkative tinker who has sold needles and relics along the Lauze for thirty years, and who has sold, that autumn, in four villages, eleven splinters of the True Doorstone at a denarius each. He is by the fire at Brunissen's on the evening Vilarnau decides to weigh its grain at the oak. Sleepy over a second cup, he remarks that the nobles of Rocafort are saying the baron's brother will die in his dungeon, that the baron is going to squeeze every village in the barony to buy him out, and that if he were a Vilarnau man he would be worrying less about doors than about hay. It is the one plain sum said aloud in the room, and the room does not hear it as one. He holds, as a professional matter, that the poor are always more exercised about the meaning of a thing than about its price, which is why they stay poor. He sells a splinter of chestnut to the weigher's widow, goes to sleep by the fire without a trace of conscience, and plans to be three valleys away before anyone examines it. He has never seen a door open in his life; he has seen a great many men squeezed. The manuscript does not say what became of him. The sheet's wiry build, weathered skin, shoulder-length grey-brown hair, missing left ear-lobe and box of chestnut splinters are its own, and so is the age of fifty-five.","i":"images/characters/gaucelm-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Villagers of Vilarnau","u":"characters/villagers-of-vilarnau.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"The village chorus: the cooper, the smith and his wife, a young wife three months gone, a feverish boy, a very old woman, the mill's hired boy · aged 12–85 by their sheet","x":"Touch the stone on the way to somewhere else, and turn a weighing-place into a door. Forty-one hearths and a hundred and ninety souls, drawn here as the people they are: a wiry cooper who has been drinking and calls the foreigner the Eastern Angel, a broad smith who comes to the green with his hammer, the smith's wife who sends round a dish of boiled sausage and weeps when it is returned untouched, a young wife three months gone who walked three miles that spring to touch a shrine of Elara and now lays her palm where the stranger lays his, a cooper's boy whose fever is said by his mother to have broken at once, a very old woman with four teeth who smiles openly, and the mill's hired boy, who adores the man who mends the sluice. In a fortnight half of them are touching the green's stone on their way to somewhere else, and the top of it begins to take a polish. They mean no harm. It is a pious gesture, out of kindness to a stranger and fear of a god, and out of a pleasing sense of being for once in the middle of something; and it is made a plank at a time, by warm people, in a tavern. The smith does not live to reach the ridge; his surviving son, who once read a book, carries the dead man's hammer up on his back and finds no use for it in thirty years. Of the village's hundred and ninety, seventy-four are dead by the second of Kerren, and eight more by the thaw. Case 0202 gives the smith and the smith's wife pages of their own.","i":"images/characters/villagers-of-vilarnau-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Masons","u":"characters/masons-of-castelmaur.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Masons of the baron's household · the elder about 50, the younger about 30 by their sheet","x":"Two patient, indifferent men with a sledge, three iron wedges, and a stone that will not take a mark. Two broad, patient, indifferent men in leather aprons who ride down to Vilarnau with the baron on the twentieth of Jorren, carrying iron wedges and a great sledge-hammer slung on a pole. They have been told to break an obstruction. The first wedge goes against the stone's flank at the join where the green vein runs, and the elder swings; the sound rings clean and bright across the whole valley and is followed by a noise like a twig snapping. The iron wedge, from the best smith in Castelmaur, lies in the dust in two halves. The second goes the same way. The third, a thicker one, strikes a scatter of sparks and rebounds so hard that one of them drops the hammer and stands nursing his wrist. Where it struck there is no mark; the grey surface lies in the sunlight, cool and smooth, as though it had been struck by a feather. The elder, who has broken stones his whole life, tells the baron that it is not a stone a man can break, and that if his lordship wishes it moved he will need forty oxen and a good deal more than a morning.","i":"images/characters/masons-of-castelmaur-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Men-at-Arms of Castelmaur","u":"characters/castelmaur-men-at-arms.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Men-at-arms and sergeants of the Barony of Castelmaur · mostly 19–40 by their sheet","x":"Ordinary soldiers following an order to burn, and none of them able to lay a hand on the stone afterward. The baron's household troops: six who ride behind the bailiff to the first weighing, twenty who come with the baron on the twentieth of Jorren, a sergeant named Sicard with ten men and a cart who go down the east lane on the first of Kerren, and the forty who carry torches on the night of the burning. They are told what the barony is owed, and they do the work. Nine hearths are distrained in the forenoon, a pig screaming all the way to the cart. At the mill a boy of eight goes for one man's hand with his teeth and lays the knuckle open to the bone, and three of them are dead in the loft inside a dozen heartbeats. On the night they are sent after the man responsible, the first six down a narrow lane find a thing in the road they have no instruction for and go down in a pile, and the forty behind them stop. Nobody wishes to be next. The thatch is put to the torch behind the line, and at dawn they are still in a ring about the moot-green, a hundred paces from the ashes of the nearest house, looking at a grey block with a charred wreath of ivy on it, exactly as it always stood. In forty men, not one of them can find it in himself to lay a hand on it. Their knight, Bertran, reports in the morning that the village is burned, the man escaped, and the stone cannot be broken.","i":"images/characters/castelmaur-men-at-arms-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sicard","u":"characters/sicard.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Sergeant of Castelmaur, second to the bailiff · thorough, with no particular opinions · about 40 years old by his sheet","x":"Finds the work of taking a village's grain easier than he expected, and carries the report that burns it. The bailiff's second, a short, wide, hard-faced sergeant with a reputation in the barony for thoroughness and no particular opinions. He rides down the lane of Vilarnau on the first of Kerren, a day late because his master, who dislikes the business, has found small administrative reasons to wait, with ten men, a cart and the clerk Folquet. Nine hearths of the east lane are distrained in the forenoon without violence. He finds the work easier than he had expected, grows by degrees expansive, and calls the clerk's attention to the proper form of the entry. At the Mill of the Two Weirs he goes up the ladder with a lantern to the widow's forty measures of seed-grain; when Ermengarda says he will not take them, he answers that it is the lord's due and will be weighed at the castle, and turns his back on her to give an order. Three of his men are dead in the loft within a dozen heartbeats. The text places him both at the head of them on the ladder and outside holding the horses, arriving at the door too late for anything but the end, and does not reconcile the two. He comes back out white to the lips and tells the clerk that the foreigner has killed the three. By dusk he is at Castelmaur on a lathered horse, in sweat and fear, delivering a report that has passed through three mouths and a ledger: a lie in every particular but the facts, and exactly what the baron most wants to hear. Nothing is said of him afterward. The sheet's forty years, ruddy skin and brush-cut black hair are its own.","i":"images/characters/sicard-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Arnaut","u":"characters/arnaut.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Bailiff of Castelmaur · twenty years in the baron's service · 45 years old","x":"Has never found a way to bend one clause of the Charter, until the village hands him the means. A stout, tired, reasonable-looking man of forty-five with the face of a deacon and the hands of someone who has spent twenty years being told by a lord what to find, and who does not particularly like the work: he likes, as he has told his wife, the evenings after it. He comes up the lane of Vilarnau at Prime with six men-at-arms, a clerk, and a pair of iron-bound weights recast the previous month on the baron's order, which the village's three most obstinate arithmeticians put at a fifth heavier than the ones they replace. When the reeve claims the stranger as a guest of the Charter, Arnaut, who has never in twenty years found a clause of it he could not bend, opens his mouth to dispute the point, glances at Bertran's face, and shuts it. The new weights are found to be what the arithmeticians estimated, and no one says so. Sent for by Aimeric, he observes, in the careful voice of a man who has spent twenty years not observing things, that the weighing clause is the one he has never found a way to bend, since no hearth may be distrained until the stone has found the default by weight; on the sixteenth of Jorren he reads the baron's order aloud from a roll, that the stone be used or broken. On the twentieth, with the village's refusal in front of him, he finds the one way through the clause: a village that declines to put its grain on the stone has declined the weighing, and the Charter's protection lapses with it. The chronicle calls it perfectly sound law. On the third of Kerren he gives the baron the ash account, standing. What became of him is not said. The sheet's ink-stained fingertips and sandy, thinning hair are its own.","i":"images/characters/arnaut-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Folquet","u":"characters/folquet.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Clerk to the bailiff of Castelmaur · later clerk of record to the Count of Rocafort's assizes · 26 at the distraint, 60 at his death","x":"Believes arithmetic is a form of mercy, and writes the one line he would most like to have left blank. A pale, tidy, ink-fingered young bailiff's clerk of twenty-six, taught from boyhood that a column which does not add is a sin and that arithmetic is a mercy, since it tells people what they owe so that they need not guess. He walks down the lane of Vilarnau behind the cart on the first of Kerren with his book open on his forearm, entering the condition of each hearth in a neat, unemotional shorthand, and does not look at the faces of the people at their doors, since he holds it unprofessional. Of eleven hearths in the east lane, nine are distrained, one is unfortunate, and one is the sort that ought to be put in a ledger. On the tenth line he writes, in clean copperplate, that the widow's hearth violently resisted and that three of the sergeant's men are dead by a foreigner. On the third of Kerren he totals the account at sixty-one solidi, and writes in the margin the only comment he permits himself: that the lane would not pay again. He lives to sixty, and becomes clerk of record at the Count of Rocafort's assizes, admired for the exactness of his entries and the economy of his opinions. In the thirty-first year after the burning he is asked to read from the oldest book in his keeping, and reads the tenth line aloud to a room of lawyers. The count's judge supposes it the work of a Saracen mercenary in the late baron's pay, and Folquet, who has never entered an opinion in forty years, says nothing. It is entered. It is the version the district keeps.","i":"images/characters/folquet-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Aimeric of Castelmaur","u":"characters/aimeric-2231.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Baron of Castelmaur · lord of Vilarnau · brother of Guilhem · 50 years old","x":"Raises the tolta to a sixth to ransom a brother, and burns the village that was paying him thirty-eight solidi a year. A heavy, red-bearded, big-handed man of fifty who inherited a barony and a temper and was never taught to tell them apart, and who has a wife of twenty-three, Lady Adalaïs, whom he does not understand and is a little afraid of, and a great grey hound called Ors. He is not a cruel man. He thinks of his villages as a field that grows money if it is correctly weeded; he cannot swim, and has built, in consequence, a very large castle on a very high rock. His younger brother Guilhem, sent to seize a weir on the Sarrac, is held for four hundred solidi, and the baron does the sum until it is a rosary: of nine hundred a year, six hundred arrive as sheaves and labor-days and cannot be paid to anyone. He raises the tolta from a tenth to a sixth and adds a hearth-penny of two denarii a chimney, and when his chaplain says that the villages may find it heavy, he says they are not being asked to like it, that it is a single winter, and that he will remit the whole when his brother is home. He means it. A stone that is a place where grain is weighed does not interest him until it breaks his masons' wedges, and on the green his own dog is undecided for one second. He orders the village burned on a report he is glad to hear. It yields sixty-one solidi, against a village that paid thirty-eight a year for ever; he sees the error in the second week of Kerren, and Guilhem dies on the fourteenth of Lorren, forty-two days before the sum was due. Lady Adalaïs goes home in the second spring; villages empty in the night; he dies in the third winter of a fever in the long cold chamber, with Ors across his bed, turning the pages of his ledger and saying to nobody, in a puzzled voice, that he cannot find the column. The sheet's pale blue eyes and thinning red-brown hair are its own.","i":"images/characters/aimeric-2231-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Adalaïs","u":"characters/adalais.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Lady Adalaïs · wife of Baron Aimeric of Castelmaur · 23 years old","x":"A quiet fair woman her husband does not understand and is a little afraid of, who goes home to her father in the second spring. The baron's wife, twenty-three to his fifty, a quiet fair woman whom he did not understand and was a little afraid of. The manuscript names her twice. When Aimeric sits up at night in the long cold bedchamber at the top of the keep reading his brother's letters aloud to the hound, she is not mentioned; when he raises the tolta to a sixth and drafts the writ in his own crabbed hand, she is not asked; when he does, for the first time in his life, an arithmetic that does not end where he planned, she is not at the window. The second naming comes in the list of what Castelmaur loses: three villages that empty in the night, the weir of Sarrac and the ford below it, two more villages that transfer themselves to the count's writ, and, in the second spring, a wife. Lady Adalaïs went home to her father in the second spring. The baron dies in the third winter in a half-emptied hall, with the hound. The manuscript does not name her father, give her a line to speak, or say what she thought of the burning of Vilarnau. The sheet gives her one, A quiet mind sees more than a loud heart, which is its own and not the manuscript's. It draws her slight, 5 ft 5 in, with narrow shoulders, an upright carriage, a heart-shaped composed face with a small mouth and a faint crease between the brows, a braid of pale flaxen-brown hair under an off-white veil and a thin silver circlet, grey-green eyes, fair freckled skin, hands kept very still when she is spoken to, a small ring of keys at her girdle and a gown of deep muted blue wool. Its two scenes are At Her Father's House, in which she sits in a dark panelled room under a candle-stand while a bearded man in a heavy cloak speaks to her with a hand out, and Leaving Castelmaur in the second spring after the burning, in which she walks away from a castle on its hill in veil and blue beside a cart and a handful of figures; the bearded man is not named. Her expression panels run Concern, Patience, Quiet Resolve and Departure. A quiet mind sees more than a loud heart.","i":"images/characters/adalais-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ors","u":"characters/ors.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Baron Aimeric's great grey hound · eleven years at the baron's bedside","x":"Is undecided for one second, and the baron thinks about it for the rest of his life. A great grey hound who has slept at the foot of Baron Aimeric's bed for eleven years, and who has never in that time left his master's side without permission. He trots at the baron's stirrup down the lane of Vilarnau on the twentieth of Jorren, with twenty men-at-arms, two masons and the bailiff. When the baron walks across the green to tell the stranger to stand aside, the hound pads forward, stops, and thrusts his long nose into the stranger's open palm, and is, after a moment's hesitation, scratched behind the ear. “Ors,” says Aimeric. The hound looks at him; it takes a perceptible moment; then he returns, unhurried, to the baron's knee, and sits. It is the sort of thing a man cannot do anything about, and the baron will think of it at odd moments for the rest of his life. The hound is across his knees when the letter comes saying that Guilhem is dead. By the third winter his muzzle is white; he lies across the bed in the last week with his chin on his master's foot, and does not once ask for an account of anything. The manuscript says nothing of what became of him after the baron died. His age at the weighing, thirty inches at the shoulder and the wiry grey coat are the sheet's; the text says only that he is a great grey hound and has been with Aimeric for eleven years.","i":"images/characters/ors-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Guiraut","u":"characters/guiraut.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Hearth-priest of Elara's small shrine at Vilarnau · keeper of six skeps of bees · 55 years old","x":"Writes the honest letter about a stranger's bowing, and later leaves a loaf and a comb of honey at a shut gate in the teeth of an interdict. The hearth-priest of Elara's small shrine at the top of the lane at Vilarnau, a thin, anxious man of fifty-five who keeps bees in six skeps behind the church and sings to them, when he supposes himself unobserved, in a cracked and tender tenor that the village pretends not to have heard. He is not a bad priest. He is a worried one. He has served under three hierarchs at the temple of Auron in Castelmaur, each of whom has found him slightly deficient in zeal and none of whom has found a way to replace him, and he has acquired the permanent stoop of a man who expects at any moment to be asked to account for something. He watches the stranger from his doorway for a month, the bowing and the green's stone touched by half the parish, and says nothing, because he does not know what there is to say. At Solan's Fire, the great autumn feast, the stranger hails the flame as he would a shrine at home, with two bows, two claps and a bow, and then declines the first slice of the pig. Guiraut, behind the fire with the serving-cloth over his arm, feels something cold settle in his stomach like a stone. Afterward he tells Ermengarda, with an apology in every line of his thin face, that the Moon-Man may bow to whatever he likes in his own house but not here, at Solan's fire, before the priest. Father, he bows to everything. He bowed to the ox. He does not laugh. He goes home and composes in his head, among the humming skeps, the letter he knows he will have to write to the Hierarch, and he composes it honestly, because he is an honest man; and the honest report of a thing is, to a certain kind of reader, indistinguishable from an accusation. Odilo's three-page letter to the baron is made from it, down to the foreign name of the stone, which Guiraut spelled as he heard it, Ysi; and it is that word, kept by the temple archive when it kept almost nothing else, that the chronicle of Castelmaur will use for the idol cast down. In the first week of Kerren, in the plain teeth of an interdict that his office obliges him to enforce, he climbs the quarry road with a loaf under his cloak and a comb of honey in a cloth, leaves both at a gate that is shut on a dark cleft, and walks down again. Nobody thanks him, because nobody knows. The comb goes down a nine-year-old cooper's boy named Pons on the ninth night on the ridge, the last thing he tastes. In the spring Guiraut is removed to a chapel three valleys off, where the six skeps he carries with him fail in the first winter, as such hives will when the one who sang to them has stopped. The manuscript gives his age, build in outline, bees, voice, stoop and the letter. The sheet's 5 ft 9 in, thin grey tonsure-like hair, pale hazel eyes, pale worry-flushed skin with a grey shadow of beard, wax-stained hands, brown cord-belted wool robe and moss-green stole are its own. Its expression panels run Anxious, Tender (Singing), Quiet Resolve and Accountable Fear, and its scenes are Everyday Robe, Singing to the Bees and Loaf and Honeycomb at the Quarry Gate, in which he bends over a dry-stone wall beside a barred gate at dawn, a castle in the mist behind. The honest report of a thing is, to a certain kind of reader, indistinguishable from an accusation.","i":"images/characters/guiraut-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Odilo","u":"characters/odilo.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Hierarch of the temple of Auron at Castelmaur · about 60 years old by his sheet","x":"Lays an interdict on a lane in the tone of a man initialing a receipt. The Hierarch Odilo sits in judgment of the barony's morals from a squat round temple of red brick at the gate of Castelmaur, and dines on lamb on feast-days. Father Guiraut's honest report on the stranger's bowing, the refused pig, and a stone being touched by half the parish under a foreign name reaches him, and it is, to a certain kind of reader, indistinguishable from an accusation. When the report of the sergeant's dead men comes, he arrives in his scarlet, with a small smile, to bless the enterprise. He is not a cruel man; he is simply a man whose faith holds that every other crown in the world is stolen, and who has never been made to reckon what that means in thatch. He lays the interdict of the temple on Vilarnau, so that no hearth in the barony may feed, shelter or bury any soul of that lane until the idol is cast down, and pronounces it in the tone of a man initialing a receipt. It costs the Church a line of parchment and costs the ridge its winter. When the barony's tithes begin to fall off in the years after, he preaches against sloth. The temple's chronicle remembers a raid of Saracens and a heathen idol cast down by his zeal, with the blessing of the Three.","i":"images/characters/odilo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Guilhem","u":"characters/guilhem.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Younger brother of Baron Aimeric · hostage of the Count of Rocafort · 38 years old","x":"Writes funny letters from a damp cell, and begs his brother not to do anything foolish on his account. A lean, charming, careless, good-natured man of thirty-eight with a talent for being liked, whom his brother the baron sends in the late summer with forty men to take a small weir on the Sarrac, which both baron and count have claimed for forty years. The count's riders are already waiting at the ford. They take the weir, forty men, two banners and the baron's brother in a morning, and send back a courteous letter naming a price of four hundred solidi, to be counted at Rocafort before the count's chaplain. His four letters are, like everything he has written, funny. The count's cook has resolved to starve him by degrees, though the sauces are the real tragedy; the Basque guard has taught him a song of forty verses about a goat. The last says in the same light tone that the cell is damp, that he is coughing a little, and that he would be glad to be home by the winter feast. He dies on a pallet of straw on the fourteenth of Lorren, with the guard sitting by him singing the sixth verse. The count, being a man of honor, returns the body without ransom, and keeps the weir. It is forty-two days before the sum would have fallen due. The baron folds the letter in three, and puts it in the box with the other four.","i":"images/characters/guilhem-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Count Raimon of Rocafort","u":"characters/count-raimon.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Count of Rocafort · neighbor and rival of Castelmaur · old and lean; about 64 years old by his sheet","x":"Collects prisoners as other men collect falcons, and keeps the weir when the baron's brother dies. An old, lean count who rules Rocafort, a smaller, poorer, more patient neighbor of Castelmaur, from beyond the faint blue line of hills, and who collects prisoners as other men collect falcons. For forty years he and the baron have each claimed a small weir on the Sarrac; in the late summer his riders are already waiting at the ford when Guilhem arrives with forty men, and take the weir, the men, two banners and the baron's brother in a morning. The ransom he names, in a courteous letter, is four hundred solidi, in coin, to be counted at Rocafort in the presence of his chaplain. He allows Guilhem one letter a fortnight, under seal, to be read first by the chaplain. When Guilhem dies of the cough he said he had, on the fourteenth of Lorren, the count, being a man of honor, returns the body without ransom, and keeps the weir. Castelmaur loses two more villages to his writ in the autumn; in the fourth year he acquires the ridge district of Lasserra, finding that it pays more in peace than it would in coin. Folquet becomes clerk of record to his assizes, and a sleek, well-read judge of his court reads a Saracen into the tenth line of an old ledger. The sheet notes that the source gives only “old”, “lean” and the falcon comparison, and that the rest is invented; its sixty-four years, white hair and pale grey eyes are not the manuscript's.","i":"images/characters/count-raimon-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Basque Guard of Rocafort","u":"characters/basque-guard-of-rocafort.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Guard of the Count of Rocafort, called “a Basque” · unnamed · about 40 years old by his sheet","x":"Teaches a prisoner a forty-verse song about a goat, and sings its sixth verse beside him as he dies. He is known to the manuscript by two sentences. In the letters he is allowed to send from the cell beneath Rocafort's hall, Guilhem reports, in his light tone, that the guard, a Basque, has taught him a song of forty verses about a goat. On the fourteenth of Lorren Guilhem dies of his cough on a pallet of straw in a dry stone room, and the Basque guard is sitting by him, singing in a low voice the sixth verse of the song. That is all the text says of him. He has no name, no speech and no later scene, and the manuscript does not say what became of him. It is enough to put a small human figure inside the count's arithmetic: a guard who sits by a dying prisoner and is still singing, on the sixth verse of forty. The sheet's forty years, olive wind-roughened skin, one missing upper tooth, black hair silvering at the temples, ring of keys and spear are its own, and so is the caption that he sings the goat song beside Guilhem as he dies.","i":"images/characters/basque-guard-of-rocafort-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Jaume","u":"characters/jaume.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Boy of the High Ground · 9 in the second winter","x":"Finds by accident the exact place on a stick where the thumb belongs, and is never told what he was given. One of the boys of Lasserra who, in the long evenings of the second winter on the ridge, find in the lean old man with the long jaw a master for the sticks. He teaches them with grave patience to hold a wooden sword as one holds a thing that might one day hold one back: the grip, the breath, the settling of the thumb. He teaches them to bow to a stick before they strike it. None of them becomes a swordsman, which he regards as the most successful outcome possible. Jaume, nine, finds by pure accident the exact place on the stick where the thumb belongs, and looks up in alarm at the sudden silence. The old man says Perfect in the speech of the High Ground, and then, under his breath, in his own, and goes out to stand a while among the goats. Nobody asks him why. The boy keeps the grip for the rest of his life and is never told what he has been given. The manuscript gives only his age and the moment; the sheet says outright that his appearance is invented.","i":"images/characters/jaume-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Elis","u":"characters/elis.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Girl of the High Ground · great-granddaughter of Ermengarda · 6 years old in the thirty-ninth year","x":"Lays her palm in the hollow on the Day of the Doorstone and asks what the word means. A girl of six, the great-granddaughter of the miller's widow Ermengarda, in the High Ground in the thirty-ninth year. On the twentieth of Garren, the Day of the Doorstone, the children of the High Ground go down in a long straggling line to the empty hollow in the green at Vilarnau and each lays a palm flat on the earth, counts ten breaths and says a word that has no meaning in the dialect and is the first word any of them learns, and Elis goes down with the others and does the same. She is a small, solemn, gap-toothed child with her great-grandmother's laugh and a habit of asking questions that nobody can answer. That evening, over the porridge, she asks her mother what the word means. Her mother, who is busy, says that it means a doorstep: it is what one says, in the High Ground, of a place where a thing begins, the sill of a house, the first stair, the foot of a hill, the place where a traveller puts down his pack. Elis considers this and says that it is a funny sort of word for a stone. Her mother says that it is a funny sort of stone. Then, because the child is still looking at her with the large grave attention of a person who has been given part of an answer and wishes for the rest, she says the one thing that she was told by her own mother, who was told it by Gersenda, and has never believed: that it is the name of the place where a man came in from very far away, and was made welcome, and one spring walked out of it toward the east, like a man with somewhere to be. It is the last scene of the manuscript, and the manuscript does not say whether the child believes it. Case 0202 has her in the same year, at the same age, at Solan's Fire on the twenty-eighth of Havren, asking her mother what another word, nago, means and being told it means a bite to eat; Elis's Mother is its page, and the story she tells afterward is the same one. The manuscript gives Elis her age, her name, her laugh, her gap and her questions. The sheet's 3 ft 7 in, round solemn face, large grave eyes, two untidy dark brown plaits, sun-browned skin, grey wool smock and patched shawl are its own, and so are its expression panels Curious, Solemn, Worried and Soft Smile. Neither page works out which woman of Ermengarda's line is the great-grandmother who feeds her in Case 0202, or the mother's mother who was told the story by Gersenda in this one.","i":"images/characters/elis-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"A half-breath late to the starting line, Azuki has the pony's hock, Kurige bolts across the meadow with Kiyomaro in the saddle, and Tomobe no Higaide, forty paces off, begins to run","u":"records/the-cat-at-the-starting-line.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Azuki, Kurige, Kiyomaro, Higaide","x":"A boy in a pale green robe with a dark topknot rides a galloping chestnut pony across a sunlit meadow with a bow raised in one hand, a small orange and white cat clinging to the pony's flank, a man in dark robes running through the middle distance, rows of courtiers under striped awnings at left, a stone-arched bridge over a river at right and rough stone footings in the foreground A half-breath late to the starting line, Azuki has the pony's hock, Kurige bolts across the meadow with Kiyomaro in the saddle, and Tomobe no Higaide, forty paces off, begins to run.","i":"images/scenes/the-cat-at-the-starting-line-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"In the ranked families' pavilion Kiyomaro finds Tomobe no Higaide's sleeve and asks whether his thumb was right at the nock; “Perfect,” says Higaide, and the Minister of the Left kneels nearby and says nothing at all","u":"records/was-my-thumb-right.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Kiyomaro, Higaide","x":"A man in dark robes with a topknot bends low over a boy in a pale green robe who lies on a bolster with one hand at the man's sleeve, a pair of swords laid on the matting beside them, court ladies standing in the pale light of a silk-walled pavilion behind and a white-haired man in purple robes kneeling with folded hands at the right In the ranked families' pavilion Kiyomaro finds Tomobe no Higaide's sleeve and asks whether his thumb was right at the nock; “Perfect,” says Higaide, and the Minister of the Left kneels nearby and says nothing at all.","i":"images/scenes/was-my-thumb-right-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"On the night of the pyre Sugino no Arikuni warms the cold cushion against his chest beneath his robe before setting it down for Azuki, who has refused her dinner for three days; the steward Tamenaga waits inside the screen to ask whether the lamp may be lit","u":"records/the-warmed-cushion.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Arikuni, Azuki, Tamenaga","x":"An old white-haired man in a grey robe sits in a carved chair beside a moonlit window over snowy peaks, holding a glowing cushion against his chest beneath his robe, an orange and white cat sitting at his feet and a tall grey-robed figure standing in a doorway behind a lantern On the night of the pyre Sugino no Arikuni warms the cold cushion against his chest beneath his robe before setting it down for Azuki, who has refused her dinner for three days; the steward Tamenaga waits inside the screen to ask whether the lamp may be lit.","i":"images/scenes/the-warmed-cushion-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Every morning in the courtyard of the Bureau of Inquiry, Ōmi no Yasutsugu sets aside a handful of rice from his own bowl and scatters it on the gravel, and the sparrows come down in a brown rush around his sandals; his clerks, watching from the porch, call him the Sparrow Examiner, not unkindly","u":"records/the-sparrow-examiner.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Yasutsugu","x":"A stooped grey-haired man in a green robe with a bundle of papers under one arm scatters a handful of rice over sunlit gravel as dozens of sparrows swoop down around his sandals, smiling clerks in black caps watching from a wooden porch behind him and a low sun over distant hills Every morning in the courtyard of the Bureau of Inquiry, Ōmi no Yasutsugu sets aside a handful of rice from his own bowl and scatters it on the gravel, and the sparrows come down in a brown rush around his sandals; his clerks, watching from the porch, call him the Sparrow Examiner, not unkindly.","i":"images/scenes/the-sparrow-examiner-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"On the night of the nineteenth of Garren, Tomobe no Higaide kneels to redo the braid of his six-year-old daughter Tsuru, which he has never been permitted to do and does badly, while Shizuka waits in the doorway with the lacquer box of his poems","u":"records/the-night-before.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Higaide, Tsuru, Shizuka","x":"A lean man with a dark topknot kneels in lamplight and works at the hair of a small girl in a white nightrobe who leans against him with her eyes closed, two swords on a rack on the wall behind them, a tall woman in a plum-colored robe standing in an open doorway at the right holding a sheet of paper and a lacquer box, and yellow leaves falling in the dark garden behind her On the night of the nineteenth of Garren, Tomobe no Higaide kneels to redo the braid of his six-year-old daughter Tsuru, which he has never been permitted to do and does badly, while Shizuka waits in the doorway with the lacquer box of his poems.","i":"images/scenes/the-night-before-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"At the grey of dawn on the twentieth of Garren, Tomobe no Higaide sets his left palm on the Stone of Mikusa and goes into it as pale grain, in the sight of Jōkei, his three senior priests and a Minister with Azuki curled in the fold of his sleeve","u":"records/dawn-at-mikusa.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Higaide, The Kiyote Pair, Jōkei, The Three Senior Priests of Mikusa, Azuki","x":"A man in a dark robe with a topknot and two swords at his hip rests his left palm on a grey stone pillar bound with a straw rope on a dark wooden plinth and dissolves into a spray of pale glittering grain streaming sideways into the stone, a bald priest with a straw broom at the left, three white-robed priests and a dark-robed man seated on a low dais behind, and an old man in grey with a cat on his lap at the right, over raked white gravel beneath tall cedars At the grey of dawn on the twentieth of Garren, Tomobe no Higaide sets his left palm on the Stone of Mikusa and goes into it as pale grain, in the sight of Jōkei, his three senior priests and a Minister with Azuki curled in the fold of his sleeve.","i":"images/scenes/dawn-at-mikusa-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"At dusk on the nineteenth of Garren the pale grain of Tomobe no Higaide pours back up out of the tally-stone of Vilarnau into the shape of a man with his palm still on its face; Peire, eight, watches with a switch in his hand, and on the road above Observer 618 stops walking","u":"records/the-stranger-at-the-doorstone.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Higaide, The Kiyote Pair, Peire (Higa the Younger), Observer 618","x":"A tall figure in dark robes with a sword at his hip rests his palm on the top of a grey granite block marked with a column of notches on its flank and shimmers into being out of glowing golden grains at sunset beneath an oak tree, a small boy with a switch gazing up at him from the left, a man with a satchel stopped on a path behind, and thatched cottages sending up smoke on the hillside below At dusk on the nineteenth of Garren the pale grain of Tomobe no Higaide pours back up out of the tally-stone of Vilarnau into the shape of a man with his palm still on its face; Peire, eight, watches with a switch in his hand, and on the road above Observer 618 stops walking.","i":"images/scenes/the-stranger-at-the-doorstone-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"On the morning of the weighing at Vilarnau, Sir Bertran brings his long sword down from the saddle and loses it, spinning, to a stranger's left hand; the bailiff Arnaut stands by with his men, and a boy named Peire has clapped a hand over his mouth to keep from laughing","u":"records/the-weighing.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Bertran, Arnaut, Peire (Higa the Younger)","x":"A lean man in a dark robe with a topknot holds a blade in each hand and turns aside the stroke of a mounted knight in chain mail whose long sword spins away through the air at the upper right, a grey horse rearing in the foreground, villagers crowding at the left including a boy with a hand over his mouth, a stout man in brown with a scroll and a rank of helmeted men-at-arms standing at the right On the morning of the weighing at Vilarnau, Sir Bertran brings his long sword down from the saddle and loses it, spinning, to a stranger's left hand; the bailiff Arnaut stands by with his men, and a boy named Peire has clapped a hand over his mouth to keep from laughing.","i":"images/scenes/the-weighing-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"On the twenty-eighth of Havren, at the great fire of Solan, Tomobe no Higaide bows to the god's flame as he was taught in his own country, with Ermengarda laughing behind him where she has pushed him into the front rank and the hearth-priest Father Guiraut watching with a serving-cloth over his arm; he will shortly refuse the first slice of the pig","u":"records/solans-fire.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Higaide, Ermengarda, Guiraut","x":"A tall man in a brown tunic bows with his palms pressed together before a huge bonfire with a whole pig roasting on a spit, a laughing woman in a red skirt with a hand on his back at the left, a priest in black with a white stole and a cloth over his arm standing beyond the flames, villagers' backs and hands passing a wooden cup in the foreground, and thatched houses with lit windows and a church tower under a dark blue sky On the twenty-eighth of Havren, at the great fire of Solan, Tomobe no Higaide bows to the god's flame as he was taught in his own country, with Ermengarda laughing behind him where she has pushed him into the front rank and the hearth-priest Father Guiraut watching with a serving-cloth over his arm; he will shortly refuse the first slice of the pig.","i":"images/scenes/solans-fire-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Shortly after Old Bernat has shown him the fieldstone where Kaneyoshi lies, Tomobe no Higaide kneels against the red ox Lo Rog in the barn and, his two swords laid in the straw, vows in his own language not to draw them in this house; Gersenda keeps her small brother back","u":"records/the-barn.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Bernat, Kaneyoshi, Higaide, Lo Rog, Gersenda","x":"A man in a dark tunic kneels in straw with his forehead pressed against the flank of a huge red ox, two swords laid on the straw in front of his hands, a girl with a long braid and a younger boy standing at the foot of a ladder behind him, and a window of sunset sky at the upper left of a stone barn Shortly after Old Bernat has shown him the fieldstone where Kaneyoshi lies, Tomobe no Higaide kneels against the red ox Lo Rog in the barn and, his two swords laid in the straw, vows in his own language not to draw them in this house; Gersenda keeps her small brother back.","i":"images/scenes/the-barn-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"On the green at Vilarnau Baron Aimeric stands over the holly-wreathed stone, two of his masons beside their broken wedges, while Ors puts his nose into the stranger's palm and Bertran watches from his grey gelding; the guest has just said that this is his door","u":"records/the-stone-holds-a-man.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Aimeric of Castelmaur, The Masons, Ors, Bertran","x":"A red-bearded nobleman in a fur-collared mantle faces a lean man in a brown tunic across a waist-high granite block wreathed in holly and ivy with red berries, a great grey wolfhound pressing its nose into the lean man's hand at the right, two stonemasons with a sledge-hammer and broken iron wedges on the ground at the left, a knight on a grey horse and ranks of men-at-arms with spears and banners behind, and a crowd of villagers watching across a frosty village green On the green at Vilarnau Baron Aimeric stands over the holly-wreathed stone, two of his masons beside their broken wedges, while Ors puts his nose into the stranger's palm and Bertran watches from his grey gelding; the guest has just said that this is his door.","i":"images/scenes/the-stone-holds-a-man-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"On the night of the torches, with the east lane burning behind them, Tomobe no Higaide stands in the mouth of the lane with a sword in each hand while the others, led by Gersenda, cross the plank into the dark; the men-at-arms in front of him have no instruction for this, and nobody wishes to be next","u":"records/the-bridge-at-the-lower-race.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Higaide, Gersenda, The Men-at-Arms of Castelmaur","x":"A man in a brown tunic stands with a long sword in each hand on a narrow plank over black water between two stone walls, a hooded woman carrying a child and other villagers crowded on the lane behind him, a burning village with its roofs in flames glowing beyond them, and the iron helmets and spear points of a mass of soldiers with torches filling the foreground On the night of the torches, with the east lane burning behind them, Tomobe no Higaide stands in the mouth of the lane with a sword in each hand while the others, led by Gersenda, cross the plank into the dark; the men-at-arms in front of him have no instruction for this, and nobody wishes to be next.","i":"images/scenes/the-bridge-at-the-lower-race-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"At the top of the quarry road Observer 618, as a mute stonecutter, holds a lantern beside the gate he latched at the end of the shift and has now unlatched, while the villagers of Vilarnau file past him with their children and a red ox, Tomobe no Higaide behind them with both swords still drawn, and the valley burns below","u":"records/the-quarry-gate.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Observer 618, The Villagers of Vilarnau, Higaide","x":"A lean man in a leather apron holds a lantern beside a wooden gate in a rock face as a line of ragged villagers files past him, a woman clutching a bundled child in the foreground, an old woman with a staff, a red ox in the middle of the line and a man in a dark robe with a bloodied sword in each hand walking at the right, a burning village glowing red in the valley below under a dark sky At the top of the quarry road Observer 618, as a mute stonecutter, holds a lantern beside the gate he latched at the end of the shift and has now unlatched, while the villagers of Vilarnau file past him with their children and a red ox, Tomobe no Higaide behind them with both swords still drawn, and the valley burns below.","i":"images/scenes/the-quarry-gate-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"In the lower archive of the Bureau of Inquiry Sugino no Arikuni, who has read the Examiner's eleven sheets twice, has slid from the stool to the floor with his forehead against the table, and Azuki, old and thin, comes into the lamplight to climb into his lap","u":"records/the-eleven-sheets.html","m":"Case 2231 · The Stone of the Clean Hand","d":"Arikuni, The Examiner's Report, Azuki","x":"An old white-haired man in a grey robe has slid to his knees on a stone floor with his forehead against the edge of a wooden table where sheets of paper lie in lamplight beside a broken red seal and cord, a thin russet cat walking toward him through a pool of light, shelves of bundled documents behind him and a barred window at the upper right with small birds beyond it In the lower archive of the Bureau of Inquiry Sugino no Arikuni, who has read the Examiner's eleven sheets twice, has slid from the stool to the floor with his forehead against the table, and Azuki, old and thin, comes into the lamplight to climb into his lap.","i":"images/scenes/the-eleven-sheets-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Tear of the Dragon","u":"books/0247.html","m":"Case 0247 · Awaiting release","d":"Copied. Borrowed. Ledger. Unwilling. Hollow.","x":"Case 0247"},{"k":"s","t":"The Hollow Hermit","u":"characters/the-hollow-hermit.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Cultivator Envoy and Custodian of record for Case 0247 · shrine-keeper at the Dragon's Tear · appears in his seventies by his sheet","x":"Keeps the shrine at a spring that takes a lifetime, says the same two words twice, and prevents nothing. An old man who keeps the shrine at the northern lip of the pool, in a hut no larger than a single room, and has kept it for a span of years no single lifetime should reasonably cover and the valley below cannot agree on. They call him the Hollow Hermit and leave rice and oil at his door twice a year, more from habit than devotion, since he asks nothing of anyone and answers almost nothing when asked. When Kuang Zhi crosses the last of the scree and sinks to his knees at the water, the hermit offers no hand and says only Not the first, and nothing else for the rest of that day or the next. He sits three feet away through the night and does nothing whatsoever to prevent the old master's death. His file is that of a Custodian, Terrarium Designation Qingxuan, kept in the third person and the dry language of the office, and it records what the valley never learns: that the deposit was self-directed and no Cultivator guidance was offered, that a seventy-two-hour monitoring window he requested was denied on the standing ground that seventy-two hours has been enough in three hundred and eleven years of comparable observation, and that three shepherds drank at the southern bank eighty-nine hours after the master died. He watches Bai Rong's hands find the Founder's Welcome from thirty feet off, behind a fold of rock, and logs everything but what he feels, because the ledger keeps no field for it. When Shen Lie seizes his robe and demands to know what he means, he says the same two words again and turns his palm up to show three sets of footprints already three days stale. No one in the whole account thinks to ask him anything at all. His closing log finds no error in its finding and notes that being correct has not made it easier to file; he has asked to be reassigned, and the request is pending. His sheet says outright that his appearance is invented from his role; the manuscript describes him only as an old man.","i":"images/characters/the-hollow-hermit-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Dragon's Tear","u":"characters/the-dragons-tear.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"A mountain spring near the shoulder of Mount Qingxuan · the Catalyst of Case 0247 · Mythic Class","x":"A still pool that copies the whole memory and skill of the last person to touch it into the next person who drinks. A spring in a stone bowl near the mountain's shoulder, wide enough that the shrine and the Hollow Hermit's hut stand on its northern lip while the gentler southern bank, a hundred paces off and screened by a shoulder of bare rock, is where the grazing paths actually reach the water. It is fed by no visible source and drained by no visible outlet, and so still that pilgrims mistake the reflection of the sky for a hole clean through the mountain. The story attached to it is that a dragon, grieving a mate slain in wars so old no record survives them, wept once and never again, and that the single tear never finished falling. Generations of monks built a shrine to the story rather than to the water, because the water offered nothing else to venerate: no light, no warmth, no voice. By every test the mountain's hermits devised in three centuries, it was only water. What it does is copy. The complete memory and skill of the last person to touch its waters pass, without translation, to the next person who drinks, and the spring overwrites itself with each deposit. Kuang Zhi gives it seven drops of blood and his whole face, and dies. Eighty-nine hours later three shepherds drink at the grazing bank, and Bai Rong, the last and the longest, carries a stranger's lifetime down the mountain. The second time the water gives Shen Lie a copy and not a restoration, with the shepherd's six weeks braided into it; Bai Rong survives and keeps his own thirty-one years, and the Custodian's working hypothesis is that depth follows submersion time and the age of the memory. Nothing in the pool ever takes a life apart into pieces small enough to hand across only what is asked for. The sheet adds a basin forty to fifty feet across, a worn lip at the north bank, ordinary livestock wear at the south and no markings, and shows the water unnaturally cold for Kuang Zhi and unnaturally warm for Bai Rong; the manuscript gives none of those figures and says only that the second time the water was not as cold as water at that altitude had any right to be.","i":"images/characters/the-dragons-tear-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kuang Zhi","u":"characters/kuang-zhi.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Grandmaster and founder of the Hollow Pine Sect · 71 years old by his sheet","x":"Climbs a mountain alone to put forty years of technique where no student could thin it, and dies at the water before anyone claims it. The builder of the Hollow Pine Sect, which he raised from a single borrowed courtyard into the most quietly respected martial school in three counties: no standing army, no official patronage, and in forty years not one duel it had not first tried three separate ways to avoid. He had come to believe that a lifetime of technique does not survive being written down. Words thin it, demonstration thins it faster, and his own body had been in decline for the better part of a decade. The spring, if the hermit's stories were even a tenth true, was the one method of transmission that copied and did not translate. He chose Shen Lie because the boy asked questions no one else thought to ask and forgot afterward that he had asked them, and ranked him above two more talented seniors without telling either of them why. He sent him two days south on an errand any junior could have run, and told no one where he himself was going. He climbs Mount Qingxuan alone, on legs that had not carried him unassisted in three years, opens his smallest left finger with a blade no longer than a fingernail, lets seven drops fall and holds his whole face under the water, and dies before dawn with the Hollow Hermit three feet away. His wish, that his gift wait for exactly one throat, is undone by thirst, a wandering ewe and three days' difference between one man's errand and another man's walk. His tea was a bitter northern leaf no one else in the sect could stomach. The chroniclers will call the war that follows the War of the Stolen Style and give him no part in it beyond, in some tellings, a sword. His sheet gives his age as 71, a tall lean frame once broad-shouldered and thinned by illness, and a white topknot and long white beard.","i":"images/characters/kuang-zhi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Shen Lie","u":"characters/shen-lie.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Chosen disciple and heir of Kuang Zhi · later founder of the reformed Hollow Pine Sect · 27–29 by his sheet","x":"Hunts the man who drank his master's gift, and comes away the only person alive who remembers four killings he did not commit. Eleven years Kuang Zhi's student, and so used to an old man who never once complained of a late return that when he comes back from the south two days behind schedule he is entirely unbothered. He finds the shrine's candles burned down untended and his master half in the water, and he fails the way a structure fails when its load-bearing wall stops being a wall: knees first, then breath, then a sound he did not choose. He winds a length of undyed hemp cord torn from the dead man's robe once around his own left wrist, drinks from the spring, certain the gift is waiting, and feels nothing. When the Hollow Hermit turns his palm up to show footprints, he decides, with the cold procedural clarity Kuang Zhi once praised in him, that the thief will be found, kept alive, walked back up the mountain and made to give everything back, and only then will he drink. He pays Old Cao for the shepherd's whereabouts and climbs to a hollow three ridges east with a rope for restraint and a blade for emergencies only, a lie he believes less with every step. Bai Rong greets him in his master's cadence, and a volley of Xuanwu arrows breaks the meeting; the shepherd throws him behind a rock hard enough to break two of his ribs and snap the cord at his wrist. When word of the war reaches them through a passing trader, he understands at once how a scrap of Hollow Pine hemp came to lie in that clearing, and they go back up the mountain. At the water the second time the copy arrives terror first, the memory of four strangers dying under his own hands, and then forty years of technique braided inseparably through six weeks of a shepherd's life: the only living man who has killed no one and remembers having done it. He rebuilds the Hollow Pine Sect on the same mountain with Wei Bo's memory, not Kuang Zhi's, as its founding lesson, names no heir, teaches nine or ten students in the open, and flinches once a year at the smell of a bitter northern tea he nonetheless keeps in his own kitchen. The manuscript is explicit that the reform answers his own wound and not the war's actual cause. His sheet notes that his injuries and the tea-flinch are stated directly in the manuscript, and that his age and every other physical detail are invented. Give it back the way it came.","i":"images/characters/shen-lie-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bai Rong","u":"characters/bai-rong.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Shepherd · unmarried, in debt to the county tax collector · 31 years old","x":"Goes up the mountain after a ewe, drinks last and longest, and spends the rest of the story trying to give it all back. Thirty-one, unmarried, and owing the county tax collector a sum he calculates will take another eleven years to clear. He climbs Mount Qingxuan for a ewe, since a ewe is worth more to him than his own dignity, and finds her standing at the water's edge. He drinks last and longest, with his face almost to the surface, and for three breaths feels nothing at all. Then the knowledge arrives, less like remembering than like realizing he has walked a road ten thousand times without once looking at it, and his hands, which had held nothing sharper than crooks and shears, find the Founder's Welcome, four days after another man died in the same posture at the same water. He wants nothing more complicated than enough saved silver to see the capital's lantern festival once, a wish he has never told anyone, and he can no longer tell which of his wants are his own. At his mother's gate his hand puts a garrison-trained man on the ground in about four seconds, and the horror on his face convinces Deputy Song faster than the throw. In the clearing he fights as Kuang Zhi fought forty years earlier, and afterward finds that the hands that killed a young stranger feel entirely like his own, which frightens him more than the alternative. He survives the second submersion, keeps his own thirty-one years and gives back everything that was not his, goes home for eleven days to a forgiven debt, and leaves on the twelfth. He reaches the capital in time for that year's festival and stands in a crowd that does not know his name, thinking of nothing at all, which he will call the best evening he ever spent. His last words to Shen Lie are Go build something with whatever's left of him in you. I already know it's good enough. The chronicles will give him the crime of theft, and the valley a dragon. His sheet draws him lean and sun-tanned, hollowed after the spring. I don't always know which parts are mine.","i":"images/characters/bai-rong-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Xuanwu Kai","u":"characters/xuanwu-kai.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Grandmaster of the Xuanwu Sect · 52 by his sheet","x":"Invokes a clause no Grandmaster has ever needed, over a single strand of hemp, and never asks the second question. Grandmaster of a sect that has stood in the provincial capital for six generations, founded by General Kang Yi, last Grand Marshal of the Yan's northern garrisons, and that in all that time has never lost a named disciple to violence outside a sanctioned duel: a record he treats as close to sacred law as anything the sect never bothered to write down, on the theory that a rule no one has needed to test does not require ink. When his only son, three months from his own succession, is killed in a clearing, he receives the jade token before the body and for the length of one held breath believes the boy lost it gambling. He does not weep in front of his disciples, then or in the seven days of ritual mourning, a restraint every disciple admires and misreads; it is the discipline of a man who has found exactly one place to put his grief that does not require weeping. On the eighth morning he invokes before the assembled sect a clause of the founding oath no Grandmaster has ever had to speak aloud: that a named disciple's death by another sect's hand, outside sanctioned combat, is a debt owed in the sect's own coin and no other, not the province's courts, not the throne's soldiers, not even the sect's own survival. Nothing in it requires him to confirm who killed his son, and six generations of never needing it have left no procedure for doubting it. A search three days later finds a strand of Hollow Pine mourning hemp near where the young master fell, and he declares war that evening without sending word. The war lasts forty-one days and kills perhaps sixty people to avenge one. He lives nineteen years more and rebuilds the sect's numbers within five, and eleven years after the war a retired soldier tells him that the timing never fit. He takes the founding oath from its case that night, reads in its margin the founder's two words, revise later, and never revises the record by so much as a character. His sheet draws him imposing and disciplined, about six feet, with a short beard and grey-streaked black hair; its age and build are its own.","i":"images/characters/xuanwu-kai-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Chen Wu","u":"characters/chen-wu.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Shepherd · Fang's cousin · 32–34 by his sheet","x":"Drinks first and feels nothing, then asks Bai Rong what in the ancestors' name he thinks he is doing. One of the three shepherds who climb the goat track above the terraced fields after half a dozen animals that have wandered further than any sensible animal should, and arrive thirsty, irritated and entirely uninterested in dragons at the pool's southern bank. The story of the tear is, to men who grew up two valleys over and heard it told wrong a hundred times, the kind that stops frightening a child around the age it stops being new. Chen Wu drinks first and feels nothing. When Bai Rong straightens into a stance no shepherd should know, he asks, half-laughing, what in the ancestors' name he thinks he is doing, his mind already halfway back down the mountain to a daughter running a fever, and he never gets an answer of any kind. The manuscript gives him no more than that. The sheet adds a stocky, deeply tanned man with a short unkempt beard, a hemp tunic and a staff, and shows him afterward questioning Bai Rong over the shift into the Founder's Welcome; its age, build and the rest are its own.","i":"images/characters/chen-wu-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Fang","u":"characters/fang.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Shepherd · Chen Wu's cousin · 27–29 by his sheet","x":"The second of the three to drink at the southern bank, and the second to feel nothing. Chen Wu's cousin, one of the three shepherds who reach the grazing bank four days after Kuang Zhi has died on the screened northern bank. He drinks second and feels nothing; Bai Rong drinks after him. The manuscript names him only that once. His sheet carries the rest: a slim, wiry man with patchy stubble and a coarse wool cloak, shown drinking from the pool as the second of three, and given four moods from observant to resolute. Its age and every other detail are its own.","i":"images/characters/fang-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Widow Bai","u":"characters/widow-bai.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Bai Rong's mother · a widow of the valley · 55–58 by her sheet","x":"Offers every collector tea at the gate, and never once asks her son what his hands have done. Meets the county tax collector's men at the gate with the same tea she has offered every collector who ever came to that door, a habit thirty years of hardship has never taught her to drop even when there is nothing in the house worth protecting the ritual for. The loom and the two remaining goats are all that stand between the family and the sum, and her son stands in the doorway behind her with his arms crossed, saying nothing. When he puts a garrison-trained man on the ground she does not ask what he has done, then or in any night after, though twice he catches her looking at his hands the way a woman looks at a tool she no longer recognizes in her own kitchen. She goes with him and what remains of the herd to a hollow in the hills rather than wait in the valley for whichever kind of attention arrives first, and is still selling what cheese she has when a passing trader carries the news of the war. Bai Rong comes home to her for eleven days and leaves on the twelfth with her blessing and a single unspent silver coin. The sheet draws a small, work-worn woman with weathered, sun-browned skin, grey hair in a low knot and callused hands, and gives her four expressions, endurance, concern, recognition and quiet relief; its age and build are its own.","i":"images/characters/widow-bai-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Deputy Song","u":"characters/deputy-song.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Deputy to the county tax collector · eleven years collecting in the valley · 45 by his sheet","x":"Has never needed to raise a hand to collect, and leaves one yard in four seconds without the sum. Has collected from the valley for eleven years and prides himself, not without reason, on never once having needed to raise a hand to do it: reputation, in his own careful accounting, is worth considerably more than any single household's furniture. He comes to the Bai household on the ninth day after the mountain, four days later than the debt strictly allowed, with two men, drinks the widow's tea as he always does, names the sum, and gives the household until sundown to produce it or surrender the two remaining goats and the loom. Then his junior reaches for the loom and is put on the floor in about four seconds by the shepherd in the doorway, and the deputy takes his men and leaves without goats, loom or sum. He describes it to the magistrate, carefully and not entirely truthfully, as a tactical withdrawal, and tells the story exactly four times before deciding to stop, because each telling made the listener look a little less at a man who had shown restraint and a little more at a man who had run. The sheet gives him a modest official's robe in blue-grey, a ledger satchel at the hip, ink-stained fingers and no weapon but his two men, and shows him leaving without collecting the debt; its age and build are its own.","i":"images/characters/deputy-song-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Old Cao","u":"characters/old-cao.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Information broker and tracker-for-hire · 57–60 by his sheet","x":"Finds people who do not wish to be found, and sells the same hollow twice. Finds people who do not wish to be found, for clients who mostly do not wish to be seen finding them, from the back room of a wine shop in the county seat, and has done so, by his own cheerful estimate, for longer than most of his clients have been alive. He names his price before Shen Lie has finished describing the shepherd and names it again, higher, when Shen Lie mentions the words Hollow Pine. He says he has found six hundred and eleven people in this trade and, so far as he knows, seen perhaps forty of them buried within the year, and that he does not ask what happens after his part is finished, which is in his professional opinion the only part of the arrangement worth keeping clean. Three days later he sends word: found. What Shen Lie does not know is that Old Cao has also sold the same location to the Xuanwu Sect for a second and considerably higher fee, which is how two parties come to the same hollow, and the Custodian can only call his professional consistency admirable. The sheet draws him pale from indoor work, thin-mustached, with grey thinning hair and quick, precise hands, habitually cheerful and unbothered, counting coins after delivering the information; its age and build are its own. I do not ask what happens after my part is finished.","i":"images/characters/old-cao-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Xuanwu Kai's Son","u":"characters/xuanwu-kais-son.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Heir apparent of the Xuanwu Sect · 22 years old · three months from his succession","x":"Wants one thing in his father's sect to be entirely his own, and rides out to take it. Twenty-two years old and three months from his formal succession as the sect's next Grandmaster, he insists on riding with the retrieval party himself rather than sending subordinates to claim what he has already decided will be his credit to take rather than his father's gift to inherit: the shepherd captured, the mystery solved, the succession secured on a victory earned and not a birthright accepted, the one thing in twenty-two years as his father's son he has never been allowed to say he did entirely alone. He is the fourth attacker in the clearing, younger than the others and better dressed beneath the plain Xuanwu grey, and he presses the attack longest and falls fastest, to a single strike. Neither Bai Rong nor Shen Lie notices until much later that he carries a jade token no ordinary disciple would have been trusted to take off the sect's grounds; they leave without searching the bodies, in the greater urgency of Widow Bai's safety, and it costs more than almost any deliberate choice either man makes. Bai Rong stands over him a moment longer than he needs to, registering only that he is young. A grimmer, more thorough search party recovers the token, and his death becomes the proximate cause of a war that kills perhaps sixty people to avenge him. The sheet draws him athletic and martial in plain Xuanwu grey over a finer concealed undergarment, with a personal blade finer than his companions'; its other details are its own.","i":"images/characters/xuanwu-kais-son-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Wei Bo","u":"characters/wei-bo.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Calligraphy instructor of the Hollow Pine Sect · 52 by his sheet","x":"Grinds ink for a lesson no student will receive, and walks to the gate with a staff made for demonstrations. A soft-spoken calligraphy master who has never in his life held a blade in anger and long ago stopped pretending he ever would, and the sect's only other instructor of any seniority. He stays behind, as he always does, to keep the mountain's books and its brushes in order while the rest chase duels and errands, and alone among them has never once complained of the arrangement. When Kuang Zhi climbs the mountain to die, Wei Bo is the one left at the school. Nine students are with him when the six hundred arrive: three surrender and are spared for information they do not have, two flee, and four gather at the courtyard gate before dawn behind him. In the last quiet hour he grinds fresh ink for a lesson no student will ever receive, because his hands need something familiar to hold and calligraphy is the only thing they have ever been steady enough for. When the forward line comes round the lower switchback he sets the ink aside, takes up a training staff meant for demonstration, and tells the other three that a school worth teaching in is worth dying in front of, the only complete sentence of his that anyone troubles to remember, which the histories will misremember as heroism and not the plain grief it was. Shen Lie learns of it from Bo, and rebuilds the sect on Wei Bo's memory. His sheet draws him slight and pale from indoor work, with ink-stained fingers, a thin trimmed mustache and a topknot; its age and build are its own.","i":"images/characters/wei-bo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Mourning Cord","u":"characters/the-mourning-cord.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"A torn strand of undyed hemp · the mourning-robe cord of Kuang Zhi · a Hollow Pine relic","x":"A keepsake tied to a wrist that a grieving search party reads as proof of an assassin. A length of undyed hemp cord torn from the belt of the robe Kuang Zhi died in, in the exact weave the Hollow Pine Sect has used for nothing else in forty years but a mourning-robe belt. Shen Lie finds it among the folds when he gathers his master's effects and winds it once around his own left wrist, beneath his sleeve, where it stays unremarked and mostly forgotten for three weeks. It is torn and frayed rather than cut and carries no inscription; its weave alone says whose it is. In the clearing, as Bai Rong sweeps Shen Lie bodily behind a stand of rock, the cord snaps clean through at his wrist. A more careful search three days later finds it caught on a branch not six feet from where the young master fell, and to the searchers a single strand is more confirmation than six generations of certainty require; Xuanwu Kai, who has already invoked the clause, declares war that evening. Shen Lie works out what it was only when the news reaches him. Its innocent origin is lost, and the chronicles name the conflict the War of the Stolen Style when it was, as the closing pages put it, a length of mourning cord and a father who could not afford to ask a second question.","i":"images/characters/the-mourning-cord-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Chen Rui","u":"characters/chen-rui.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Hollow Pine student · one of the three who stayed · 25 by his sheet","x":"Stays behind to bury what the six hundred left, and gives Bai Rong the number. One of three students, with Old Fen and Bo, who stay at the mountain school after the six hundred have gone, rather than scatter with everyone else who could, to bury what the soldiers left behind. When Shen Lie and Bai Rong come down the mountain three days after the recall, they find swept courtyards and a gate repaired badly but repaired. When Bai Rong asks how many died in all, not Wei Bo alone but the soldiers and the bystanders and everyone the valley will give up trying to count, it is Chen Rui who gives him the roughest figure available, and Bai Rong says nothing more that day. The sheet draws him lean and tired, with a fresh scar along one forearm, dirt-stained hands and knees and training garb stained from burial work, giving the casualty count; its age and build are its own.","i":"images/characters/chen-rui-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Old Fen","u":"characters/old-fen.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Hollow Pine student · one of the three who stayed · 58 by his sheet","x":"The oldest of the three who stayed to bury the dead. One of the three students who remain at the mountain school after the war, with Chen Rui and Bo, to bury what the six hundred left. The manuscript names him once and gives him nothing more. His sheet fills in the rest: a spare, weathered, grey-bearded man with his hair tied back and a slight limp or stiffness, in patched cloth wraps, shown seated on the mountainside looking out over the school, and given four expressions from calm to a faint smile. The age, build and expressions are all its own.","i":"images/characters/old-fen-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bo","u":"characters/bo.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Hollow Pine student · about 14 years old","x":"Tells the whole account of Wei Bo's death unasked, plainly, as children tell the worst stories. A boy of perhaps fourteen, one of the three who stay behind to bury the dead. It is Bo who tells Shen Lie and Bai Rong the whole of it without being asked, the way children tell the worst stories, plainly and completely, because no one has yet taught them which parts to spare a listener. It is from him that Shen Lie learns, for the first time as more than a name, that Wei Bo spent his last quiet hour grinding ink for a lesson he would never give and went to the gate anyway. His sheet draws a slight, still-adolescent boy with a round face, short cropped black hair, dark eyes and a few fading bruises from hardship, in patched pine-green and charcoal, and gives him four expressions: unease, resolve, grief and truth. Its details are its own.","i":"images/characters/bo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Kuang Zhi at the water before dawn, his whole face beneath the surface and his blood on the stone beside the blade, as the Hollow Hermit sits three feet away and does nothing whatsoever to prevent it","u":"records/not-the-first.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Kuang Zhi, The Hollow Hermit","x":"An old man with a white topknot lies face down with his head beneath the surface of a still pool on a stone-paved bank at dawn, a small blade and a pool of blood on the stone beside him, while a white-bearded man sits cross-legged by a shrine hut and a stone lantern Kuang Zhi at the water before dawn, his whole face beneath the surface and his blood on the stone beside the blade, as the Hollow Hermit sits three feet away and does nothing whatsoever to prevent it.","i":"images/scenes/not-the-first-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The southern grazing bank, four days after the master died: Bai Rong rises from the water in a stance no shepherd was ever taught, weight low and both palms out, while Chen Wu and Fang stare at him with the flock grazing behind","u":"records/three-shepherds-at-the-southern-bank.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Bai Rong, Chen Wu, Fang","x":"A dark-haired shepherd in a pale hemp tunic stands low with both palms turned outward beside a blue mountain pool, a shepherd with a crook looking on at his left and a third in a headband bending toward him at his right, a flock of goats grazing on the slope behind The southern grazing bank, four days after the master died: Bai Rong rises from the water in a stance no shepherd was ever taught, weight low and both palms out, while Chen Wu and Fang stare at him with the flock grazing behind.","i":"images/scenes/three-shepherds-at-the-southern-bank-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Shen Lie back from his errand on the sixth day, among candle-stubs no one has refilled, finds his master half in the water; he does not weep so much as fail, all at once, in the manner of a structure whose load-bearing wall has stopped being a wall","u":"records/shen-lie-finds-the-body.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Shen Lie","x":"A young man with a dark topknot kneels at the edge of a still pool with his mouth open in a cry, an old white-haired man lying in the water below him, candles burning on a stone shrine at the left and a lone figure with a staff standing on a rise at the right under a grey mountain Shen Lie back from his errand on the sixth day, among candle-stubs no one has refilled, finds his master half in the water; he does not weep so much as fail, all at once, in the manner of a structure whose load-bearing wall has stopped being a wall.","i":"images/scenes/shen-lie-finds-the-body-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The tax collector's junior reaches for the loom and is put on the ground in about four seconds by Bai Rong, moved, as the manuscript has it, the way a door is moved; Deputy Song and a second man watch from the gate","u":"records/bai-rong-at-the-gate.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Bai Rong, Deputy Song","x":"A lean bearded man in a pale tunic grips the wrist of a man in dark robes who is thrown off his feet and sliding in the dust, a wooden loom in an open doorway behind them and two officials in dark robes and black caps watching with startled faces beside a fence The tax collector's junior reaches for the loom and is put on the ground in about four seconds by Bai Rong, moved, as the manuscript has it, the way a door is moved; Deputy Song and a second man watch from the gate.","i":"images/scenes/bai-rong-at-the-gate-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Widow Bai across the lamplit table, looking at her son's hands the way a woman looks at a tool she no longer recognizes in her own kitchen, and never once asking what they have done","u":"records/widow-bai-watches-his-hands.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Widow Bai","x":"An older woman with grey hair in a low knot stands in a lamplit doorway with her hands clasped, looking down with a worried face at a dark-haired man seen from behind, whose hands rest on a wooden table beside an oil lamp and two bowls Widow Bai across the lamplit table, looking at her son's hands the way a woman looks at a tool she no longer recognizes in her own kitchen, and never once asking what they have done.","i":"images/scenes/widow-bai-watches-his-hands-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Old Cao in the back room of the wine shop, counting coins Shen Lie has not yet agreed to pay, and naming his price again, higher, once the words Hollow Pine have been said","u":"records/old-cao-counts-the-coins.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Old Cao, Shen Lie","x":"A smiling grey-haired man with a thin mustache stacks coins on a small table in a back room lined with jars and lit by a hanging lantern, facing a hooded man with a dark topknot seen from behind Old Cao in the back room of the wine shop, counting coins Shen Lie has not yet agreed to pay, and naming his price again, higher, once the words Hollow Pine have been said.","i":"images/scenes/old-cao-counts-the-coins-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"In the hollow three ridges east, Bai Rong turns from the dying fire into the Founder's Welcome as Shen Lie reaches the clearing with a rope for restraint and a blade for emergencies only","u":"records/the-fire-in-the-hollow.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Bai Rong, Shen Lie","x":"A bearded man in a ragged pale tunic crouches low in a pine forest with both palms turned outward beside a dying fire, while a younger man with a rope slung over his shoulder and a hand on a blade stops in the trees behind him In the hollow three ridges east, Bai Rong turns from the dying fire into the Founder's Welcome as Shen Lie reaches the clearing with a rope for restraint and a blade for emergencies only.","i":"images/scenes/the-fire-in-the-hollow-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"What Bai Rong does in the clearing takes a few minutes and no wasted motion, the way the dead master fought forty years before; the songs will call it the shepherd's reckoning, and flatter it with a moral tidiness the minutes themselves did not have","u":"records/the-shepherds-reckoning.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Bai Rong","x":"A bearded man in a torn pale tunic strikes outward with an open palm in a smoky forest clearing, fallen men in grey lying on the ground around him and embers glowing among the leaves What Bai Rong does in the clearing takes a few minutes and no wasted motion, the way the dead master fought forty years before; the songs will call it the shepherd's reckoning, and flatter it with a moral tidiness the minutes themselves did not have.","i":"images/scenes/the-shepherds-reckoning-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"On the eighth morning of mourning, Xuanwu Kai stands before the assembled sect and invokes the clause of the founding oath that no Grandmaster in six generations has had to speak aloud; nothing in it requires him to confirm who killed his son","u":"records/the-oath-is-invoked.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Xuanwu Kai","x":"A tall man with long white hair and a white beard, in a black robe with silver trim, stands with one palm raised in a great hall of tall blue banners bearing a tree, with rows of seated students in grey robes and racks of spears around him On the eighth morning of mourning, Xuanwu Kai stands before the assembled sect and invokes the clause of the founding oath that no Grandmaster in six generations has had to speak aloud; nothing in it requires him to confirm who killed his son.","i":"images/scenes/the-oath-is-invoked-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Bai Rong and Shen Lie, Shen Lie holding his broken ribs, take the goat track back toward the mountain at midday; three valleys behind them the first true battle of a war neither chose is already three hours old","u":"records/back-up-the-mountain.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Bai Rong, Shen Lie","x":"Two men in ragged tunics with packs climb a rocky mountain track, the one in front in pale hemp looking back over his shoulder and the one behind him in grey holding his side, a terraced valley and a river below and a tall column of black smoke rising from a far hillside Bai Rong and Shen Lie, Shen Lie holding his broken ribs, take the goat track back toward the mountain at midday; three valleys behind them the first true battle of a war neither chose is already three hours old.","i":"images/scenes/back-up-the-mountain-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Bai Rong lowers his face beneath the water where Kuang Zhi died, and Shen Lie counts eleven held breaths while the Hollow Hermit watches from the rock, saying nothing","u":"records/the-second-emptying.html","m":"Case 0247 · The Tear of the Dragon","d":"Bai Rong, Shen Lie, The Hollow Hermit","x":"A young man with a dark topknot kneels on a rocky shore with both hands on the back of a man lying face down with his head beneath the surface of a still pool, a white-haired, white-bearded man seated on the rocks behind them with a staff, snow peaks and cloud beyond Bai Rong lowers his face beneath the water where Kuang Zhi died, and Shen Lie counts eleven held breaths while the Hollow Hermit watches from the rock, saying nothing.","i":"images/scenes/the-second-emptying-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Second Hand","u":"books/1153.html","m":"Case 1153 · Published","d":"An eleven-fingered hand, a hierarch's ruling, and eighteen years spent deciding whether it was a hand at all.","x":"Case 1153 Fiction / Fantasy / Epic Sarkveti is a kingdom of terraces that believes anything worth having must be climbed for, and its temple keeps the rolls on which a prince's name becomes a fact. Before a prince can be entered on them he must survive the Proving: a rite of blunted blades, fought against a prince of his own blood. When Levan, the quiet King's son who would rather be in the physicians' wing, raises his guard against his cousin Bagrat, a badly re-buckled strap gives way and four inches of real steel come down across his left hand. That week a petition goes to the temple for Bagrat's provisional entry on the rolls; none goes for Levan, who is a boy deciding whether to live. Within a fortnight a clockmaker from the lower market talks his way into the King's presence and, over three nights, fits the prince with a grafted hand of eleven fingers: two thumbs, four fingers in plain sight and five more folded flat behind them. Levan spends eighteen years being gentle with it, as a physician, a teacher, and one of the worst lute players in Tavgora. When King Guram dies, Vazha, his steward, keeps the dying man's quiet choice of heir to himself, and the temple's rolls hold an entry for Bagrat and none for Levan. The old Doctrine of the Unbroken Hand bars the maimed from the crown and is silent about a man given another hand, and Hierarch Demna, whose conviction is sincere and whose purse was filled by Bagrat three weeks earlier, rules that a false wholeness may not hold it. Bagrat is then carried home from a Vashtir campaign with a rotting sword arm, which his cousin saves in six hours of surgery. He offers to put Levan's name on the rolls himself and is told, “I want no crown. I only wanted to see you stand.” He cannot bear the debt. He challenges Levan to the Answer, fought with real steel, and watches the hand that could have ended him take his sword and let him go unmarked. In the dark afterward he finds in Demna's doctrine a way to want his cousin gone without calling it by its name, and spends the next months asking, in a grieving voice, for a Writ of Examination whose penalty clause he never reads. The war takes a winter and part of a spring and a little under two thousand lives. Its first casualty is a wool-seller who never held an opinion about the Doctrine, and its breach is held by two soldiers who were both Levan's patients. When the third offer of surrender asks for the surgeon himself, Otar and eleven of his former patients lead him out of the city by a goat track on the night the outer wall falls. He lives thirty-one more years in Otsane as ‘Mikel’, a plain glove on his hand, and never goes back. Bagrat rules a further twenty-six, searches six of them, and keeps a carved wooden horse in a drawer. Sarkveti, which now owns a doctrine that can be used, is used by it, reign after reign, until Corvane absorbs what is left. The Second Hand closes on a Cultivator's own archival log — Case Study 1,153: one graft, one doctrine that survives its test, and an Observer who left a field on his form open thirty-four seconds too long."},{"k":"s","t":"Levan","u":"characters/levan.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Son and heir of King Guram · physician · called Mikel in exile · about 14 at the Proving, 32 at the war · dies at 63","x":"Loses a hand to a loose strap, wins one back from a stranger, and spends eighteen years being kind to everyone but his own claim. The King's son, older than Bagrat by fourteen months and quieter by nature, more drawn to the physicians' wing of the palace than to the training yards. At the Proving a re-buckled strap gives way and four inches of real, Auron-blessed steel come down across his raised left hand; he does not scream, and looks, the court thinks, almost apologetic. Over three nights a clockmaker fits him with an eleven-fingered hand, and it is Levan himself who counts the fingers first. No petition is ever filed to enter his name on the temple's succession rolls, because he never asks for one. He spends the next eighteen years as a physician, apprenticed to the three court doctors who once agreed his arm would have to go: laying his instruments out left to right in the same count every time, brewing the same bitter mountain tea for frightened patients, and playing a four-stringed lute alone, badly, for the pleasure of failing at something that costs no one anything. He saves Bagrat's arm in a six-hour surgery, refuses the throne twice, wins the duel called the Answer without drawing blood, and is carried out of Tavgora on the night its outer wall falls by Otar and eleven of his former patients. In Otsane he is ‘Mikel’ to Keeper Amets, keeps a small, mostly unpaid surgery for thirty-one years, teaches four novices, writes the compendium he always wanted to write and leaves it to be copied freely, and dies there at sixty-three. The sheet draws him lean and wiry, with long angular face, deep-set dark-brown eyes, near-black hair and a close-trimmed beard that greys at the temples, in undyed charcoal, the grafted hand pale ivory-grey; it gives his age as about thirty-two during the central conflict, which the manuscript's arithmetic supports. I want no crown. I only wanted to see you stand.","i":"images/characters/levan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bagrat","u":"characters/bagrat.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Guram's nephew · later King of Sarkveti · 13 at the Proving, 31 at the duel · reigns 26 more years","x":"Cannot bear to owe his cousin an arm, builds a righteous case in one sleepless night, and spends six years searching for the man he exiled. The orphaned son of King Guram's brother, taken by a fever with both parents in one winter, and raised alongside Levan, who taught him to swim in the cold river below Tavgora's walls. Thirteen at the Proving, when the strap on his blade gives and cuts his cousin's hand, he becomes the prince still standing: the temple seals a provisional entry for him within the week. He leads the answer to the Vashtir raids, takes an arrow low and wrong in the dark and a fall from a horse onto rock, and is carried home over eight days with his sword arm swollen black to the elbow, until Levan saves it. He cannot repay the debt. He offers the throne, then a duchy, a stipend and a title invented for the occasion, and is refused every time by a man who wants nothing. Crowned and unable to look at his own five ordinary fingers, he challenges Levan to the Answer, unwrapped steel in the open hall, and loses to mercy a second time: disarmed, unmarked, and spared. He kneels in a corridor behind the northern pillar, builds the entire righteous case in a single sleepless night from a sentence of Demna's, and petitions for the Writ of Examination without reading the paragraph beneath it. He rules for twenty-six more years, spends nearly six of them quietly searching every unmapped fold of the western range, and keeps a small carved wooden horse Levan made him in a drawer under the state papers. The sheet draws him taller and broader than Levan, with dark wavy hair going grey, a square-trimmed beard after the coronation and a thin scar on the forearm Levan rebuilt; its own footnote says all of this is visual invention, since the manuscript describes only his ordinary, five-fingered hands. Then let us see if mercy can parry.","i":"images/characters/bagrat-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Guram","u":"characters/guram.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"King of Sarkveti · Levan's father, Bagrat's uncle · late 50s at his death","x":"Buries a wife, a brother and his patience for miracles with strings, and chooses an heir he asks to be left unannounced. King of Sarkveti, father of Levan and uncle to Bagrat, who has buried a wife, a brother and most of his patience for miracles that come with strings attached. He takes the surgeons' verdict standing and dry-eyed, as the histories like to say every Sarkveti king took bad news, then walks the northern wall with Vazha and puts both hands over his face. When the clockmaker offers a working hand in exchange for the freedom to leave, he asks only whether it is sorcery, heresy or something a Hierarch must bless first, and accepts the answer that it is craft. In his last illness he tells Levan that he was never meant to need it, and that a boy who never needed to be extraordinary might have made a very dull king. He dies eight days later. That same night he has chosen between the two young men he raised, and has asked Vazha to see the choice honored quietly rather than announced, so that the kingdom might come to it freely. The sheet gives his age as the late fifties at death, with cropped grey hair, a trimmed grey beard, warm brown eyes and a dark chokha embroidered in muted silver. You were never meant to need it, and I find I am glad of that more than I am sorry for the rest of it.","i":"images/characters/guram-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Vazha","u":"characters/vazha.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Senior steward to King Guram · leader of the loyalist faction · outlives the war by nearly thirty years","x":"Follows the soundest advice he ever heard, and spends the war learning which of loyalty's two creditors collects first. Guram's senior steward, the one man in the palace who had known the King longer than the King had known his own crown, and who had carried both princes on his shoulders as infants. Against his better judgment he arranges the clockmaker's audience. When Guram dies he tells the court that no choice was left, a lie he carries for the rest of his life, because Guram's real choice had been made in confidence and he reasons that a crown installed by a father's whisper would always be one Bagrat could say was stolen. He never petitions the temple to enter Levan's name on the succession rolls, which he later calls the single worst piece of good advice he ever followed. After Demna's ruling he tells the court the truth and is believed by perhaps a third of the room. He leads the smaller faction through one winter and part of a spring, refuses three offers of surrender, the last of which asks only for Levan, and keeps a private ledger of a little under two thousand dead. He survives the war and the peace by nearly thirty years, and asks Otar exactly once whether the prince lived; he chooses to read the small, tired smile as a yes. The sheet draws him spare, upright and slightly stooped, with thin close-cropped white hair, a short white beard and a steward's seal; it also gives an age that cannot be squared with his surviving the war by nearly thirty years, so none is stated here.","i":"images/characters/vazha-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Demna","u":"characters/demna.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Hierarch, senior priest of Auron's temple in Tavgora · late 50s–60s on the sheet","x":"Believes every word of a ruling he was paid to make, and cannot prove to himself that the gold is not the reason. Senior priest of Auron's temple in Tavgora, who rules that the Doctrine of the Unbroken Hand bars a hand that claims the wholeness a sleeve would honestly confess it lacks. Three weeks earlier Bagrat's gold reached him by way of Sandro, and he has never once, in his own prayers, managed to convince himself that the gold is the reason he believes what he is about to say. The night before the ruling he kneels alone at Auron's altar and tells the god that the gold changes nothing about the law's truth, only about how quickly he got around to remembering it; the distinction satisfies him there, and considerably less so in court the next morning. He is not struck down, which one faction treats as proof of nothing and the other as proof of everything. It is Demna who first says aloud, in Bagrat's hearing, the sentence that gives the king's private ledger a public name: that a false wholeness worn long enough begins to believe itself real. By the war's second month he is unpaid, unasked, and at the wall, praying in the same words over a dying man from each faction in the same week. The sheet draws him stooped and war-worn, clean-shaven with cropped iron-grey hair, in threadbare iron-grey vestments and carrying no weapon. The Doctrine does not ask whether a hand functions. It asks whether a hand is the one Auron gave.","i":"images/characters/demna-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Observer 419","u":"characters/observer-419-1153.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Envoy/Observer, Case 1,153 · publicly a clockmaker · also the Observer of Case 2,140 · outwardly 40s–50s","x":"Grafts an eleven-fingered hand in three nights, leaves by dawn, and cannot quite stop watching for eighteen years. The same field agent of the Cultivators who installs a single grey sphere in a county mill in Case 2140, posted next to Tavgora, where he has kept a stall in the lower market for thirteen weeks by the morning of the Proving: small brass instruments that run for years without a smell of oil. He watches the Proving from the back of the crowd and files his report that evening, recommending a Tier 1000-series functional intervention and flagging the prince's temperament as an unusually favorable variable. What he does not write down is that he felt recognition, the second posting running in which the feeling arrived before there were grounds for it. He asks the King for an audience, offers craft rather than sorcery, and asks only that no one keep him in Tavgora once the work is done. His one instruction to Levan is the only thing he ever says to him that anyone overhears. His posting formally ends the morning after the graft; he does not travel far. For eighteen years he watches from a rented room two valleys away, files a note on the ruling in which he finds himself, against every manual, glad, and on the night the city falls stands on the ridge above the western road and logs a column of twelve leaving by a goat track. He notes the lute, and files nothing about it. The sheet draws him in earth browns with grey-streaked brown hair, pale grey eyes and a short, neatly kept beard, deliberately unremarkable. This will not make you whole. It will make you capable. They are not the same, and you would do well to remember which one your doctrine actually asks for.","i":"images/characters/observer-419-1153-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Eleven-Fingered Hand","u":"characters/the-eleven-fingered-hand.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Catalyst, Case 1,153 · a grafted prosthetic left hand · two thumbs, eleven digits","x":"Built by someone who had studied hands rather than grown one; a kingdom rounds it up to twelve fingers and down to abomination. The prosthetic that replaces Levan's ruined left hand, fitted by the clockmaker over three nights: no blood after the first hour, no fever after the second night, and by the third morning a hand lying pale and still on the pillow, unmistakably wrong in the way a correctly drawn diagram of a hand is wrong when you have only ever seen the real thing. It has two thumbs set opposite each other along one broad palm, and beneath them two rows of ordinary fingers: four in the row that shows, and five more folded flat beneath it, hinged along a second knuckle no natural hand possesses, the way a fan holds nine-tenths of itself in reserve until a wrist decides otherwise. Nobody who handles it can say what it is made of. It sets compound fractures with a precision no natural hand could match, ties sutures too small for the physicians' own eyes to follow, and reaches into a wound at an angle no ordinary wrist could offer. Its hidden row unfolds fully for the first time in the six-hour surgery on Bagrat's arm, and again in the duel, when all five hidden fingers close over the guard of his sword like a hand closing over a key it has always known. Legend then does what the file declines to: the lower market calls it the Twelvefold Hand, then gold cast at Solan's anvil that cannot be burned; the court chronicles call it a lie told in metal; and a small shrine in a lower valley keeps a gilded wooden hand with too many fingers on a shelf behind its altar. The sheet draws it pale matte ivory with muted iron-grey joints, in panels showing the folded second row, the joint mechanism and the opposed thumbs.","i":"images/characters/the-eleven-fingered-hand-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Doctrine of the Unbroken Hand","u":"characters/the-doctrine-of-the-unbroken-hand.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Auron temple archive · codified in the same century as the Proving, four centuries before the events","x":"Precise about absence, silent about substitution, and a very old weapon looking for a hand to be used on. An older and considerably colder text than the Proving that shares its theology, filed in the temple archive alongside two dozen other doctrines no living Hierarch had needed to cite: that a king's hand is the instrument of Auron's law made visible, and that no man missing the wholeness Auron gave him at birth can be trusted to hold that law without some essential piece of it also missing. It speaks of men who lack the god's gift and says nothing of men who have been given another, and it is in that silence that Vazha's allies and Bagrat's lawyers both make their camps. The form that turns it into procedure, the Writ of Examination, sits beside it in the same archive, and the paragraph beneath the summons fixes what a court of three is to do on a finding of counterfeit. Once it has been shown to be usable, it is used: against a nephew with a limp in the second reign, a daughter with a squint in the third, a prince with a withered foot in the fourth, until succession in Sarkveti is a contest of defect rather than claim, and the temple that charges a fee for each examination grows rich as the crown grows poor. The manuscript never says what it looks like; the sheet draws it as a bound codex of yellowed vellum with an iron-grey seal and a wood-and-iron clasp.","i":"images/characters/the-doctrine-of-the-unbroken-hand-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sandro","u":"characters/sandro.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Gold-broker of Tavgora's court · 40s–50s on the sheet","x":"Carries the bribe to the Hierarch, and finds priests his easiest customers because they never haggle. A broker who makes a modest living arranging exactly the kind of transaction between men too proud to negotiate their own convictions directly. He carries Bagrat's gold to Hierarch Demna, and remarks, to no one in particular and entirely for his own amusement, that priests are his easiest customers precisely because they never haggle. The sheet draws him soft-built and comfortably prosperous, olive-skinned, with dark oiled hair thinning at the crown, quick dark eyes and a thin, carefully groomed mustache, in muted charcoal and umber with a coin-purse and a ledger. A man who believes his own argument will accept any price for saying it out loud, since he was always going to say it for free.","i":"images/characters/sandro-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Otar","u":"characters/otar.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Border soldier, later informal leader of the faction that would not fight · 50s–60s at the extraction on the sheet","x":"Walks out of the surgery on a leg he was told to lose, visits it weekly for life, and then walks its owner out of the city. A soldier who arrives at Levan's surgery three years into his apprenticeship with a leg the border garrison's healer has recommended taking off at the knee, and leaves six weeks later without a stick, unable to stop telling anyone that the prince's hand had felt like eleven separate decisions being made at once, each one correct. He visits that surgery once a week for the rest of his life, whether or not he needs anything mended. When the war splits the city he gathers the third faction that has no banner: eleven men chosen for nothing a recruiter would recognize except that each carries a piece of work Levan's hands once did. On the night the outer wall falls he cannot bring himself to carry a surgeon off a table, so his men stand along the surgery wall and hold lamps for an hour while Levan finishes closing a wound on a boy in Bagrat's colors. Then he makes his argument once, plainly, and takes Levan out by a goat track and eleven days of unwatched mountain paths to a place he will not name. The sheet draws him broad-shouldered in youth, leaner and weathered after two decades, iron-grey and short-bearded, with a faint permanent limp in the leg Levan saved and a worn dark belt-knife. A secret held by one man dies with him, and I intend for this one to outlive me.","i":"images/characters/otar-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Eka","u":"characters/eka.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Bonesetter's daughter · physician of Tavgora · early-to-mid 30s on the sheet","x":"Asks every man carried through her door how badly he is hurt, and never which banner he fought under. A bonesetter's daughter who spends a month watching Levan's surgery from the doorway before asking whether she might hold the instruments, and is told she may, provided she puts them back in order. She trains with him for the better part of a decade and becomes the nearest thing Tavgora has to a second such hand: one of the two physicians at Bagrat's six-hour surgery, who stop trying to follow what Levan is doing in the fourth hour and simply hand him whatever he silently reaches for. During the siege she keeps her own surgery open and asks exactly one question of every man carried in. The hardest hour of the war, she will say decades later, is the morning she treats two men from the same street who put those wounds on each other, side by side, each asking whether the other will live. Levan hands her the one-eyed grey cat before he leaves. Her craft survives one generation of apprentices, and not a second. The text gives her no physical description beyond being a young woman and a skilled physician; her sheet says everything else on it, a sturdy, dark-haired, dark-eyed figure in a bloodstained apron with chapped, over-washed hands, is invented for consistency.","i":"images/characters/eka-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sulqan","u":"characters/sulqan.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Healer attached to Sarkveti's border garrison · 48 on the sheet","x":"A decent, exhausted man who has already begun preparing a household to lose its king-to-be. The border garrison's own healer, a decent and exhausted man, who sees Bagrat carried to Tavgora's gates fevered past recognizing his own name, his sword arm swollen black to the elbow, and has already begun preparing the household for losing him. Levan's six-hour surgery makes that preparation unnecessary. The sheet makes him forty-eight, stocky and built for lifting wounded men rather than fighting, with thinning grey-brown hair, an unkempt greying beard, a ruddy complexion from years at a cold border post, ink- and herb-stained hands and a patched off-white coat. It shows him treating the arm on the return march, then preparing the household for the loss.","i":"images/characters/sulqan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Dato","u":"characters/dato.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Levied farmhand · common soldier · 17 years old","x":"Carries a king home before anyone has settled on calling him one, and is never named in a chronicle. A levied farmhand of seventeen, among the common soldiers who carry Bagrat's litter for eight days, who is entirely unaware until he sets his commander down in the palace courtyard that his own trembling relief at finally reaching help looks, to the assembled court, exactly like grief. He is never named in any chronicle, and spends the rest of a long, ordinary life telling his grandchildren, with more pride than the moment probably warranted, that he once helped carry a king home. The sheet draws him lanky and wiry, with rough-cut sandy-brown hair, light brown eyes and the callused hands of farm work.","i":"images/characters/dato-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Gia","u":"characters/gia.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Conscripted household-guard soldier · 19–20 on the sheet","x":"Meets a man he last saw wincing through the same stitches, and later tells the night a breath kinder than it was. A young man conscripted off his family's terrace two seasons earlier and given a spear he barely knows how to hold and a banner he has stopped believing in; he holds the eastern breach with Bagrat's household guard on the night the outer wall gives way. Levan's hands had set his broken wrist two winters before, after a training accident neither he nor the man he now faces, Beso, thought worth mentioning. They recognize each other in the half-second before contact, and neither lowers his weapon. Gia takes nothing worse than a fall that knocks the wind from him; in his old age he will tell a version in which he recognized Beso a full breath sooner and let the blow go wide on purpose, a small, forgivable improvement on the truth that even he can no longer separate from what happened in the dark. The sheet draws him slight and still growing, with short black hair, wide dark-brown eyes and a faint scar on one wrist, in borrowed, ill-fitting armor.","i":"images/characters/gia-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Beso","u":"characters/beso.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Soldier for Vazha's loyalist faction · about 22–23 on the sheet","x":"Carries a white scar in the shape of a story he will never tell without crediting whose hands began it. A loyalist who walked with a stick for the better part of a year after a border skirmish, until Levan's hands rebuilt the tendon a border healer had written off. At the eastern breach on the night the outer wall falls he comes at the line from the other direction and recognizes Gia, three years his junior, in the half-second before contact. He takes a shallow cut along the forearm that scars white for the rest of his life, in a shape he never shows anyone without also explaining, unprompted, exactly whose hand had put the first version of that arm back together. The sheet draws him tall and fully grown, with dark brown hair tied back, sharp brown eyes, a short patchy young beard and a long pale scar on one forearm; it notes that everything beyond his age relation and the lifelong scar is reconstruction.","i":"images/characters/beso-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Amets","u":"characters/amets.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Keeper of the Vigil of Ardwen, in Otsane · 50s–60s on the sheet","x":"Gives a stranger a plain name, an open door, and a household that has never heard of anyone called Levan. The Keeper of the Vigil of Ardwen in Otsane, a small theocracy tucked into a fold of the western range so thoroughly unremarkable that Sarkveti's maps render it as an unlabeled patch of grey. She sits in a plain wooden chair in a Vigil house with no cathedral and no gold, because Ardwen's doctrine asks for empty hands before anything else, and she receives the stranger Otar's men deliver to her gate with a silence that is unhurried in a way nothing in Tavgora's court ever managed. Before the week is out she gives him a name for the register that is not his own, Mikel, and a standing instruction to her household, kept without a single question, that no man called Levan has ever come to that gate. A gate-warden who dislikes lying so much that she practices it exactly once in her life tells the one rider who does reach Otsane that no man of that name ever came there. The sheet draws her as a small-framed, wind-browned Basque-coded mountain woman, grey hair beneath a plain wool kerchief, in undyed wool and calf-laced abarkas. Ardwen does not ask what your hands have done. Only that you keep them open now.","i":"images/characters/amets-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Xu Lian","u":"characters/xu-lian.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Foreign scholar · Jinhai · also in Case 0000B · age not established","x":"Leaves eleven notebooks at a cathedral that give a faraway rumor its theological shape. A careful foreign scholar who spends two years cataloguing every recorded attempt on Valeria before returning, empty-handed and unbitter, to a country half a world away. The eleven notebooks he leaves behind are donated to the Vigil and are what give the news from Sarkveti its theological shape at Ardenwake: among his annotations sits an anchorite walled into a cell against the cathedral's north face, who crossed a hall full of soldiers who had just failed to move the sword and lifted it without once thinking about succeeding, failing or the blade. He is also the foreign scholar of Case 0000B, where he appears beside the Anchorite herself. The sheet draws him East Asian-coded and restrained, with dark hair, dark eyes, ink-stained hands and a satchel of weathered bindings, and files him under Jinhai; it says his appearance follows earlier canon and adds nothing new.","i":"images/characters/xu-lian-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Anchorite of Ardenwake","u":"characters/anchorite-of-ardenwake.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Anchorite of the Vigil of Ardwen · the protagonist of Case 0000B · cited here secondhand","x":"A woman who wanted enclosure rather than glory, cited at a distance as one of two precedents for a third lifter of the sword. In this case the Anchorite of Case 0000B appears only as a precedent, through Xu Lian's donated notebooks: a woman walled into a cell against the cathedral's north face who lifted Valeria without once, by her own account, thinking about the blade. With the founding wielder, she is one half of the pattern Ardenwake's pilgrims and younger clerks try to extend to a healer who refused a throne twice, in a kingdom that answered the refusal with a writ of fire. The Vigil's scholars complicate the enthusiasm before it settles into doctrine, and the Keeper of Ardenwake declines to settle it from a plinth hall a mountain range away. The sheet adds no new physical detail and sends the reader back to Case 0000B for her appearance, drawing her hooded in stone-grey and parchment with a plain devotional cord and Xu Lian's notebooks in her hands.","i":"images/characters/anchorite-of-ardenwake-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Founding Wielder","u":"characters/the-founding-wielder.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"The valley reckoner · the boy of Case 0000 · permanently unnamed · cited here secondhand","x":"The nameless first bearer of the sword, whose namelessness has grown a superstition the Vigil's scholars cannot quite examine. The first bearer of Valeria, cited at Ardenwake as a nameless valley reckoner: the boy of Case 0000, who is never given a name that survives the telling. His namelessness has given rise to the order's oldest and least examined superstition, that renouncing one's name is itself part of the mechanism, which the Vigil's scholars use to argue that Levan, known across three kingdoms by a story harder to renounce than a crown, is the furthest thing from the pattern. The sheet adds no new physical detail, treats his appearance as canonically inherited from Case 0000, and draws him pale-haired in layered, weathered cloth, shown in the margin of the Vigil scholars' debate.","i":"images/characters/the-founding-wielder-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Proving: a strap that was re-buckled twice gives way, and four inches of real steel come down across Levan's raised left hand. It is Bagrat, not Levan, who screams, dropping his own blade to catch his cousin before he falls","u":"records/the-proving-strap-gives.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Levan, Bagrat","x":"A dark-haired youth in black and gold robes recoils with a bloodied open left hand and a blade in his right while a lighter-haired youth in pale robes lunges to catch him, his own sword falling with a leather strap trailing, courtiers watching from a stone stair beneath a mountain citadel The Proving: a strap that was re-buckled twice gives way, and four inches of real steel come down across Levan's raised left hand. It is Bagrat, not Levan, who screams, dropping his own blade to catch his cousin before he falls.","i":"images/scenes/the-proving-strap-gives-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Guram takes the surgeons' news standing and dry-eyed, and then, on the northern wall with Vazha and no one else, puts both hands over his face and makes a sound Vazha will never repeat","u":"records/the-king-on-the-northern-wall.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Guram, Vazha","x":"A crowned old man in a fur-trimmed cloak covers his face with both hands at a battlement above a snowy valley while a grey-bearded man in a dark cloak stands behind him Guram takes the surgeons' news standing and dry-eyed, and then, on the northern wall with Vazha and no one else, puts both hands over his face and makes a sound Vazha will never repeat.","i":"images/scenes/the-king-on-the-northern-wall-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The clockmaker's stall in Tavgora's lower market: small brass instruments that run for years without a smell of oil, sold to a court that mostly tells time by bells. He has kept it for thirteen weeks by the morning of the Proving","u":"records/the-clockmaker-at-his-stall.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Observer 419","x":"A man with grey-streaked brown hair and a short beard in a hooded brown cloak and leather apron works on a small brass mechanism at a market stall hung with gold pocket watches, a hilltop citadel and a crowded market behind him The clockmaker's stall in Tavgora's lower market: small brass instruments that run for years without a smell of oil, sold to a court that mostly tells time by bells. He has kept it for thirteen weeks by the morning of the Proving.","i":"images/scenes/the-clockmaker-at-his-stall-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Over three nights the clockmaker works on Levan's ruined hand while the two physicians present, sworn to silence, keep that oath more faithfully than any other they swear. No blood after the first hour, no fever after the second night: by the third morning, a hand lying pale and still on the pillow, too regular, too considered","u":"records/the-graft-over-three-nights.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Observer 419, Levan, The Eleven-Fingered Hand","x":"By candlelight a bearded man in a hooded brown cloak fits a jointed golden-brown mechanical hand to the arm of a sleeping dark-haired youth in a bed, two robed men watching from behind Over three nights the clockmaker works on Levan's ruined hand while the two physicians present, sworn to silence, keep that oath more faithfully than any other they swear. No blood after the first hour, no fever after the second night: by the third morning, a hand lying pale and still on the pillow, too regular, too considered.","i":"images/scenes/the-graft-over-three-nights-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Levan wakes to eleven fingers, and counts them himself before any physician or the clockmaker can: two thumbs set opposite each other along one broad palm, four fingers in the row he can see, five more folded flat beneath it. His first thought is neither horror nor gratitude, but a small, shameful, private satisfaction at finding he can still count at all","u":"records/levan-counts-his-fingers.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Levan","x":"A dark-haired young man propped in a bed beside a sunlit window holds up a pale jointed prosthetic left hand, a mountain and a citadel beyond the glass Levan wakes to eleven fingers, and counts them himself before any physician or the clockmaker can: two thumbs set opposite each other along one broad palm, four fingers in the row he can see, five more folded flat beneath it. His first thought is neither horror nor gratitude, but a small, shameful, private satisfaction at finding he can still count at all.","i":"images/scenes/levan-counts-his-fingers-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Demna, alone before Auron's altar the night before the ruling, telling the god that the gold changes nothing about the law's own truth, only about how quickly he got around to remembering it — a distinction he finds entirely satisfying there, and considerably less so in court the next morning","u":"records/demna-before-the-ruling.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Demna","x":"A grey-haired priest in a heavy iron-grey robe kneels at a stone altar with clasped hands and an oil lamp, looking up at a dark temple door carved with a sword and a pair of balanced scales Demna, alone before Auron's altar the night before the ruling, telling the god that the gold changes nothing about the law's own truth, only about how quickly he got around to remembering it — a distinction he finds entirely satisfying there, and considerably less so in court the next morning.","i":"images/scenes/demna-before-the-ruling-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The northern answer to the Vashtir raids, led by Bagrat because a kingdom that cannot agree on its king can still agree on who should be sent to freeze on its behalf: a Vashtir arrow taken low and wrong in the dark, a fall from a startled horse onto rock, and eight days on a litter to come","u":"records/the-vashtir-snow.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Bagrat","x":"A wounded man in a frost-covered fur coat lies in the snow with an arrow in his side beside a rearing white horse, mounted archers with torches closing in through a night snowstorm The northern answer to the Vashtir raids, led by Bagrat because a kingdom that cannot agree on its king can still agree on who should be sent to freeze on its behalf: a Vashtir arrow taken low and wrong in the dark, a fall from a startled horse onto rock, and eight days on a litter to come.","i":"images/scenes/the-vashtir-snow-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Bagrat behind the hall's northern pillar after the duel: his knees strike stone before he decides to kneel, both ordinary hands pressed flat over a face no one but the corridor's cold walls will see uncomposed. He straightens his collar before he walks back out","u":"records/the-king-in-the-corridor.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Bagrat","x":"A crowned young king in an ermine-trimmed crimson cloak kneels on a stone floor in a narrow dark corridor with both hands pressed over his face Bagrat behind the hall's northern pillar after the duel: his knees strike stone before he decides to kneel, both ordinary hands pressed flat over a face no one but the corridor's cold walls will see uncomposed. He straightens his collar before he walks back out.","i":"images/scenes/the-king-in-the-corridor-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Tavgora's outer wall goes to Bagrat's siege engines after Vazha's faction has refused three separate offers of surrender. The engines are brought up on the nineteenth day of Lorren, and Bagrat does not go to watch them","u":"records/the-outer-wall-falls.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Bagrat, Vazha","x":"A walled hill city of white terraces burns at night while siege engines hurl flaming stones at a breaking wall under red and blue banners Tavgora's outer wall goes to Bagrat's siege engines after Vazha's faction has refused three separate offers of surrender. The engines are brought up on the nineteenth day of Lorren, and Bagrat does not go to watch them.","i":"images/scenes/the-outer-wall-falls-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The night the outer wall gives way, at the eastern breach: Gia of Bagrat's household guard and Beso of Vazha's loyalists recognize each other in the half-second before contact, two former patients of the same eleven fingers, and neither lowers his weapon","u":"records/the-eastern-breach.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Gia, Beso","x":"Two young soldiers face each other across crossed swords among rubble at night, one in red household-guard colors and one in blue with a heraldic beast, torchlit ranks behind them The night the outer wall gives way, at the eastern breach: Gia of Bagrat's household guard and Beso of Vazha's loyalists recognize each other in the half-second before contact, two former patients of the same eleven fingers, and neither lowers his weapon.","i":"images/scenes/the-eastern-breach-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Keeper Amets receives the stranger Otar's men have delivered to her gate in Otsane, and tells Levan, before he has said a word about any of it, that Ardwen does not ask what his hands have done, only that he keep them open now","u":"records/the-keeper-meets-the-stranger.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Amets, Levan","x":"A bearded dark-haired man in a heavy cloak stands at a stone gate facing a grey-haired woman in a hooded wool robe, a plain timber Vigil house and grazing sheep behind her Keeper Amets receives the stranger Otar's men have delivered to her gate in Otsane, and tells Levan, before he has said a word about any of it, that Ardwen does not ask what his hands have done, only that he keep them open now.","i":"images/scenes/the-keeper-meets-the-stranger-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Levan, called Mikel, at his workbench in Otsane on the evening a trader tells him how it ended: both hands, the four ordinary fingers and the five hidden ones alike, laid flat and motionless on the wood. He plays the lute again that night, later than his habit","u":"records/levan-at-the-workbench-in-otsane.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Levan","x":"A grey-bearded man in a leather apron sits at a candlelit workbench with both hands laid flat on the wood, one of them a pale jointed prosthetic, a mountain lute leaning against the shelves beside him and a mountain through the window Levan, called Mikel, at his workbench in Otsane on the evening a trader tells him how it ended: both hands, the four ordinary fingers and the five hidden ones alike, laid flat and motionless on the wood. He plays the lute again that night, later than his habit.","i":"images/scenes/levan-at-the-workbench-in-otsane-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The plinth hall at Ardenwake, where every pilgrim who climbs the cathedral steps is permitted exactly one attempt on Valeria and almost none ever lift it: the ritual the Vigil's scholars call the Empty Hand. This record names no one","u":"records/one-attempt-on-valeria.html","m":"Case 1153 · The Second Hand","d":"Valeria","x":"A robed figure kneels on cathedral steps reaching toward a glowing sword on a plinth in a shaft of light, robed onlookers lining a stained-glass hall The plinth hall at Ardenwake, where every pilgrim who climbs the cathedral steps is permitted exactly one attempt on Valeria and almost none ever lift it: the ritual the Vigil's scholars call the Empty Hand. This record names no one.","i":"images/scenes/one-attempt-on-valeria-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"Independent Observation","u":"books/1268.html","m":"Case 1268 · Awaiting release","d":"Altitude. Round. Audit. Dormant. Unhastening.","x":"Case 1268"},{"k":"s","t":"Warden Hraban","u":"characters/hraban.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Mid-ranking Warden of the Threefold Crown, sent from Ansdorf to Halstead · seventeen years' service by his file, twenty-six by the Custodian log · resigns after the eleventh selection · lives thirteen more years","x":"Asks for guidance before he asks for punishment, watches a farmhand walk into a fire, and spends eleven months noticing that the man was right. A careful man, neither cruel by temperament nor merciful by conviction, sent from the district seat at Ansdorf on the twentieth day of the Halstead dreaming to look into a report of collective delusion. He travels with a brindled hound, Bero, inherited as a pup from a dead brother and already, at three winters old, inclined to sulk in the rain; he warms the dog's blanket against the hearth-stones before he thinks to warm anything for himself, and lets Bero's chin rest on the toe of his left boot through every report he drafts. He questions Ymma first, a boy next and Otker last and longest, and does not believe Otker is lying, which the manuscript calls the beginning of the tragedy. He writes to Ansdorf describing a probable contagion of the mind and asks for guidance before punishment. The answer, sealed with the joined sigil of all three Triarchs, ignores the contagion: forty people have reached the same conclusion with no teacher at all. He stands at the edge of the burning on the last day of Brayen and watches Otker go into it without recanting, and it is eleven months before he lets himself notice that the last thing Otker said to him was still true. Recalled to Dornhal the spring before the eleventh selection, on a seventeen-year record his file has never corrected, he is the one the dream breaks first and most completely: three attendants find him on the floor of his chamber before sunrise, his hands pressed so hard to his face that his nails leave marks for a week, repeating a single word one of them thinks sounded like a name. Old Bero has dragged himself across the room on bad hips to lie along his ribs. He resigns within the year, finds the Anchorite at a crossroads shrine and asks whether Ardwen wanted any of it, and lives thirteen more years in a small house with no shrine, a row of beans that comes to nothing every spring and a succession of old hounds each named for the first. He goes back to Halstead once, in the eleventh of those years, and when a woman selling eggs asks whether it is true that a farmhand named Otker died insisting the world was round, he says “Yes.” and nothing more. He is buried, at his own written request, without the Crown's rites. The Custodian log counts twenty-six years of sanctioned orders, each preceded by a request for clarification, and records the habit as present in exactly one subject and without measurable effect on the outcome. The sheet draws him average-to-tall and lean, with brown greying hair, a short greying beard and grey-green eyes, in a grey-brown Warden's coat with the Dornhal insignia, a service sword and a dagger, and then in a plain grey civilian coat after his resignation, with nail marks on his hand from the breakdown. The pull-quote below is the sheet's own and does not appear in the manuscript. …Before I act, I must be sure. Not out of doubt, but out of duty.","i":"images/characters/hraban-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Anchorite of Ardwen","u":"characters/anchorite-of-ardwen.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Ardwenist anchorite of the western border · unnamed · later called the Anchorite of the Nine Burnings, a title she never claims","x":"Kneels in the ash of nine burnings, asks the sky to let her come, and is given more altitude than anyone in Aldemark's history. A woman in undyed grey wool, alone and on foot, who kneels in the ashes of Halstead for the better part of an afternoon without asking a single neighboring farm what happened there, and leaves behind two knee-shaped hollows that the next rain erases. She is present at every one of the nine burnings, and at the one village that is never purged: already in Ansdorf on the ninth day of its dreaming, where a junior Warden interviews her and files the result unread; before Grenmoor's shrine to the Star-Walkers, telling no one which god she thinks the singing was for; in Harkendale's root cellars, warned more than once to move on; at Faldrun, where she blesses a blacksmith's iron globe in Ardwen's name; and at Aschendale's boundary stone, where no purge is ever ordered, praying that night for the elders who chose a hard truth over an easy lie. Taken at Reinfeld on charges of vagrancy and obstruction, she asks the soldier who binds her wrists whether he was at Halstead. In Dornhal she answers every question an Inquisitor puts to her and tells him nothing: asked who sent her, “Ardwen”; asked her name, on the eleventh day, “my name is not the thing you are asking for.” She is not tortured, though not from mercy: three Ardwenist communities have begun writing to Dornhal on her behalf. In a cell too narrow to lie down in, she kneels toward the one barred slit and asks, not for rescue or vindication, only: if You are beyond the heavens, then let me come. The network above Aldemark does not recognize a prayer; it recognizes an interaction request from an already-registered dreaming subject, and does what it was built to do. She rises past the globe, the furnace of the sun, a wheel of fire and a river of suns so numerous that Aldemark does not amount to a grain of silt in it, to a dormant figure with straight black hair, a white too fine to name and red unblinking eyes, something nearer to a status than a presence: Dormant. No active deployment. Awaiting reassignment. Two names surface that she has sung in the Houses of Vigil since before she could read, the Sky-Answerer and the Unhastening. She asks whether it called her. It does not answer, and she takes the silence as an answer. The manuscript names no other case, but its echoes (a sword's name, a valley watched through one boy's lifetime, those same two titles) point toward Ardwen of Case 0000, and the Custodian log says the designation recurs, under seal, in at least six earlier case files. On the night of the eleventh selection she reaches for it again and finds nothing, and what she feels instead is the plain gratitude of a beggar let back into a hall; it turns her, for the first time, inward, to the sleeping body in the cell. Within the year Hraban finds her at a crossroads shrine and asks whether she dreamed what the city dreamed; she says, “I dreamed something. It was not the same thing.” Released some months after the collapse, she goes back to the road, hears the stories about her outrun her within two years, every detail they settle on wrong in some particular, and dies decades later still giving the same answer to a question she has been asked several thousand times. The manuscript never gives her a name or an age. The sheet draws her lean and road-hardened, medium height, with dark brown hair under a plain grey head-covering, grey-green eyes, a small unsettling smile, a walking staff, a journey pouch and worn boots. Ardwen does not want. Ardwen answers. There is a difference, and it is the only theology I have ever needed.","i":"images/characters/anchorite-of-ardwen-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Otker","u":"characters/otker.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Farmhand of Halstead · saving barley-money for a daughter's loom · burned in the purge of Halstead, unrecanting · twenties by his sheet","x":"Sits up three nights with a neighbor's cow, reports the shape of the world the way a man reports weather, and will not take it back. A slow, well-liked farmhand of Halstead who has spent the winter sitting up three nights with a neighbor's failing cow and two seasons of barley-money on a loom for a daughter not yet old enough to weave. By the third week of the dreaming he is the one who reports that if a man goes far enough from the world in his sleep, it stops looking like a floor and starts looking like a stone: round on every side, hanging in the dark with nothing beneath it and nothing propping it up. He is not believed; then he is believed by four people; then by forty. Halstead means the news as an offering, something wonderful to lay at the temple door, and does not yet understand that the temple wants the lie kept whole. Hraban questions him last and longest, and Otker tells him plainly, the way a man reports weather, that he has seen the shape of the world and would swear to it on his own burial. He goes into the fire on the last day of Brayen without recanting; the Custodians log forty-one dead at Halstead. Years later, at a crossroads, a woman selling eggs asks a former Warden whether it is true that a farmhand named Otker died insisting the world was round, and Hraban answers that it is. The manuscript gives Otker no age; the sheet draws him in his twenties, lean and strong, of average height, with rough-cut brown hair, blue-grey eyes and light stubble, in a linen shirt, a worn leather vest and muddy boots, with a farm knife, and labels his expressions wary, plain-spoken, defiant and resigned. I have seen the shape of the world, Warden. It is round. I would swear it on my own burial.","i":"images/characters/otker-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Adaltrude","u":"characters/adaltrude.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Triarch of the Threefold Crown · holder of Elara's seat · overseer of hearth-law and harvest-blessing · late middle age by her sheet","x":"Argues against the purges, is outvoted two to one, and wakes from the eleventh dream to do the arithmetic she has spent nine years refusing to add. Holder of Elara's seat on the Crown, twenty-six years into overseeing hearth-law, harvest-blessing and the quieter, less glamorous work of actually feeding a kingdom when the villages begin to refuse the tithe. In closed convocation she argues that a people cannot be starved into believing a lie they have personally watched be disproven, and that continued purges will produce exactly the outcome they are meant to prevent. Ansovald and Sigward outvote her two to one; the vote goes into no public ledger, and the famine that follows the next winter's grain shortfall does. The younger clergy who later ask, in careful language, whether a Crown that was wrong about the heavens can be trusted about anything it never troubled to reexamine are using the language she has modeled for them without ever quite instructing them to. In the eleventh dream, at twenty-eight years in Elara's service, she rises as every dreamer before her has risen, expecting to feel accused and not: the globe does not know her office and the moon does not care what she has signed. What she takes from it is that the horror was never that Auron's account of creation was wrong but that the heavens do not care whether his priests got a word of it right. She wakes before dawn, goes alone to the small hearth-shrine she keeps for Elara's private rites and kneels there for most of an hour, not praying but sitting the way a woman sits with a debt she has just understood she cannot repay. She says only that she saw what the dead of nine villages saw, that it was as they described, and that a decade of burnings purchased nothing except the burnings; the manuscript calls it arithmetic, not confession. When the Crown splits three ways she counsels confession and reform, and Elara's seat, under her and her successors, absorbs most of what remains of the temple's civil authority. Among those successors, for six further generations, it is a quiet, unwritten custom to review, before confirming any sentence of exile or execution, whether the accused had asked a question the court found it easier to punish than to answer. The sheet draws her in late middle age, of medium height and sturdy build, with a broad, serious face, dark brown hair with early grey braided and pinned with gold, dark hazel eyes and a fair to light-olive complexion, in layered blue-and-ivory Crown vestments with a grey fur mantle over practical winter wool, carrying a ceremonial seal, keys and legal tablets; one study shows her contemplative after the dream. The sheet calls the blue “Elara's traditional colors” while Elara's own sheet is red and cream, and both are given here as drawn. The pull-quote below is the sheet's own and does not appear in the manuscript. Law keeps the flame. Blessing gives it life. Both must endure.","i":"images/characters/adaltrude-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ansovald","u":"characters/ansovald.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Triarch of the Threefold Crown · holder of Auron's seat · defender of the Bound Creation · older adult by his sheet","x":"Never loses a doctrinal contest in nineteen years, knows independent observation is the one heresy scripture cannot answer, and has never climbed his own bell-tower. Holder of Auron's seat on the Crown, who has governed the defense of the Bound Creation for nineteen years without once losing a doctrinal contest, and who conducts all his business on the palace's ground floor: a childhood fall from a granary beam has left him with a vertigo that he describes to visitors as a preference for the ground and to no one as a fear. He has never climbed the bell-tower of the cathedral he consecrated. He reads Hraban's letter from Halstead twice and is recorded as saying only that independent observation is the one heresy scripture has no answer for. His sermon that autumn on the folly of trusting one's own eyes does not name Halstead; Ansdorf is purged with far less debate beforehand, since he has concluded that debate is itself a contagion; and he calls the tax refusals a second heresy and orders both suppressed with equal severity. He dreams with the rest of Dornhal and says nothing publicly for three days: a burned draft of an address, meals sent up untouched, a man failing to hold in one hand the fact that he has seen the world is round and in the other the fact that he personally ordered a thousand people killed for saying so first. On the second night he, who has not climbed above the palace's first landing since boyhood, asks to be helped up to the roof and stands for most of an hour at the parapet with both hands flat on the stone, looking not at the stars but at the courtyard cobbles forty feet below, and comes down without a word. When he finally speaks, it is to say that the Crown could survive being wrong about the heavens if it did not also become known for having always known it, which the manuscript calls the single most honest sentence he has uttered in twenty-eight years, and which satisfies no one. The sheet draws him tall and spare, about six foot one, with a narrow, severe face, guarded grey eyes, dark hair silvered at the temples and a closely trimmed grey beard, in black-and-cream Triarch vestments with the Auronic star, holding scripture and decree tablets; its expression studies run from composed to private grief. It gives nineteen years governing the defense and twenty-eight of clerical career, which match the manuscript's figures. Independent observation is the one heresy scripture has no answer for.","i":"images/characters/ansovald-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sigward","u":"characters/sigward.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Triarch of the Threefold Crown · holder of Solan's seat · signs the Reinfeld purge himself · middle-aged to older adult by his sheet","x":"Is shown the truth, believes it, and concludes the kingdom cannot survive hearing it spoken, so he orders the burning twice as hard. Holder of Solan's seat on the Crown, from a family that has served it for four generations, and the Triarch who argues through the decade of purges, with the particular ferocity of a young man defending an old institution, that strength is the only answer the dreaming deserves. He signs the order for Reinfeld's purge himself rather than delegate it, having made it a point of pride never to ask a Warden to carry out a purge he is not willing to have his own name on. His provincial appointees later form the Unbent, who conduct purges the Triarchs never sanctioned and are only nominally interested in being told to stop. His father raised him on one lesson: that the Crown is a roof, not a truth, and a roof's worth is measured by how long it keeps the rain out; his grandfather died holding a stretch of wall in the Bulwark famine rather than let a granary riot become a civil war. The eleventh dream tests the lesson, and it survives. Admitting the Crown's cosmology is a lie, he concludes, would not correct the kingdom his grandfather died for; it would kill it. He never doubts for a moment that the globe is real, and he spends the following week arguing, with a sincerity his colleagues mistake for zealotry and the manuscript calls nearer to grief, that the answer to being personally shown the truth is to burn twice as hard against it, and demands a purge broader and more absolute than any before. The manuscript calls him a young man defending an old institution; the sheet's trait list says middle-aged to older adult, though its caption for him reads “a younger man in the service of an old institution.” Both are left as they stand. The sheet draws him tall and athletic, with an angular face and a hard jaw, green-grey eyes, a short dark beard and dark hair greying early at the temples, in ivory-and-crimson vestments with flame motifs, a ceremonial sword and a formal dagger. The pull-quote below is the sheet's own and does not appear in the manuscript. Strength is the answer to the dreaming.","i":"images/characters/sigward-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ymma","u":"characters/ymma.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Woman of Halstead · among the first known dreamers · young-to-middle adult by her sheet","x":"Wakes her husband before dawn to say she has been nowhere and everywhere, and starts the chain of testimony Halstead cannot unsay. A woman of Halstead and the first dreamer the manuscript names. On the first night of Brayen, in the iron cold of early spring that Aldemark's peasants call Elara's Fast, she wakes her husband before dawn to tell him she has been nowhere and everywhere, floating in a black field scattered with unfamiliar lights, and that she went toward the moon simply by wishing to, the way a thought goes toward a memory. Her husband tells her it is only hunger dreaming. Three farms over, a boy tells his mother the same thing, unprompted, before she has said a word about it; by the second week no one in Halstead is calling it hunger. Hraban questions her first when he arrives on the twentieth day. The manuscript names her only at the start and again when Hraban questions her, and does not say what becomes of her in the burning of Halstead. The sheet draws her as a young-to-middle adult of average height and sturdy rural build, with an open, tired, wind-reddened face, hazel eyes and dark brown hair in two braids under a linen headscarf, in a dark plaid muted-blue wool shawl over a brown wool dress and a linen apron; its notes call her married, an early riser of practical demeanor, and one of the phenomenon's first witnesses, and one panel shows her floating toward a full moon.","i":"images/characters/ymma-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Probe Network","u":"characters/the-probe-network.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Catalyst, Case 1,268 · distributed autonomous celestial probe network · seeded generations before the first dream · no face","x":"Dormant for generations, then, on a schedule fixed before it was seeded, lends one village at a time a seat in the dark above its own world, and gives nothing else. A network of autonomous probes seeded silently into Aldemark's high atmosphere generations before any living Warden's grandfather was born, so completely dormant that no chronicle recorded a light in the wrong part of the sky until a schedule fixed before the seeding itself began to turn. It grants the sleeping minds of one village at a time an unmediated seat above their own world, with no voice, no vision of a god and no instruction: only altitude, and as much more of it as a dreamer has the nerve to keep asking for. It picks settlements by geographically stratified sampling of the whole inhabited landmass, with no weight for politics, religion or importance; eleven days of visitation, eleven months of silence, one settlement at a time. Eleven, the Custodian log explains, was never a sacred number, only the smallest count of independent exposure samples its statisticians judged enough to tell a pattern from ten coincidences. The Crown's temples build a numerology on it anyway. Ten selections are villages: Halstead, Ansdorf, Grenmoor, Wulfsholt, Faldrun, Brandwick, Harkendale, Stonewick, Aschendale and Reinfeld. The eleventh, fixed before the first probe reached Halstead and unmoved by anything the Crown did in between, falls on the whole capital, wall to wall, and on every Warden, priest and Triarch asleep inside it; the guard Dornhal rotates to keep itself awake holds for six days, and on the seventh a sentry dreams the whole of it in a single breath. The log counts one thousand one hundred and four dead across ten selections and one thousand two hundred and eleven in all, once the eleventh's riots, reprisals and private reckonings are added. It records the burning of Halstead in the flat notation it uses for weather. It also does one thing no one intended: an unrevised protocol answers an anchorite's sustained, specific request with more range than any subject in this or any earlier selection has been given, and the log files no correction, since correcting it would take an intervention more conspicuous than the deviation. Her second request, made during the eleventh selection and addressed to the dormant unit, is not serviced; the log cannot say whether the shared channel could not isolate it or the unit declined. The sheet draws a nearly invisible, faceless unit in dark, heat-resistant composite, with a saucer-shaped body, a tall antenna spire and trailing sensor tendrils, carrying optical, astronomical, atmospheric, dream-interface and communication arrays; its variants are labelled Scout, Sensor, Relay and Archivist. It pictures the network as hundreds to thousands of units and marks that as conceptual, a figure the manuscript does not give.","i":"images/characters/the-probe-network-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Cultivator Observer","u":"characters/cultivator-observer.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Observer, Case 1,268 · Custodian-Office analytical authority · no Envoy posted in person · extremely old by the sheet","x":"Watches the Aldemark experiment from beyond the reach of any native chronicle, and records, interprets and evaluates; the experiment continues. The manuscript assigns the case no Envoy: its header reads None assigned — autonomous network; no Envoy delivers or observes in person. The sheet nevertheless files a Cultivator Observer under Case 1,268, the Custodian Office's analytical authority, and the Archival Log that closes the manuscript speaks throughout as this office. That log sets down the case's reading: that unmediated sight of the planet's true shape was enough, with no doctrine supplied, to produce the same discovery across eleven exposure samples however primed by rumor; that a suppressed cosmology proved a load-bearing variable of theocratic governance; that the tax, the judgment and the oath, which rested on the same institution, collapsed with it; and that the network gave the people nothing but observation, a distinction the office says it does not regard as a loophole. It also records, and sets aside without comment, that Warden Hraban requested clarification before issuing a single one of his twenty-six years of sanctioned orders and that this variable produced no measurable alteration to outcomes; that the Ardwenist was granted an extended range by a mechanical consequence of an unrevised protocol, with no corrective update filed; and that the dormant designation she asked for recurs, under seal, in at least six earlier case files. Its last line is its status: Terrarium stable. Case closed. Filed. The sheet draws an alien Cultivator, tall, slender and extremely old by Aldemark's chronology, with a narrow face, sparse fine dark-silver hair, large dark reflective eyes, cool grey-pale skin slightly translucent at the temples and no facial hair, in a long dark layered coat with the custodial insignia, holding an analytical tablet; its panels show a long-range observatory, an archival cylinder and a probe-network link, and its expression studies read neutral, assessing, flagging anomaly and no emotion. The pull-quote below is the sheet's own and does not appear in the manuscript. The Aldemark experiment is a variable in a larger dataset. Our task is not to interfere, but to record, interpret, and evaluate.","i":"images/characters/cultivator-observer-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Solan","u":"characters/solan.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"The Son · god of the Threefold Crown · flame, rebellion and the wide bright sky · shown here as temple iconography","x":"The Crown's god of fire, whose flame is lit over every oath, and whom one village mistakes for the sender of its dreams. The Son of the Threefold Crown's three, god of fire and rebellion and the wide bright sky, whose flame is lit over every oath a magistrate swears in Aldemark and whose seat Sigward holds. In Grenmoor, the village that sings on its way to the burning, the dreaming is taken as a blessing sent down by Solan himself, since who else but the god of fire and rebellion and the wide bright sky would think to grant a farmer the sight of his own heaven from above it; the villagers raise a shrine of fieldstone and unbleached cloth to what they call the Star-Walkers. In the Crown's last years a small sect, the Solan Ascendant, breaks from orthodoxy to declare the dreaming a sign that the Son has at last outgrown the Father's law, a heresy the Crown's other parties agree on nothing except crushing. The god himself takes no part in the manuscript's events. The sheet draws the god as the temple paints him, not as anything seen in the dream: ageless and of heroic human scale, with a youthful face, red-gold hair under a golden laurel, bright amber eyes and a sun-warmed complexion, in white-and-crimson ceremonial robes with a flame-bright spear, and again in the light armor of the temple guard.","i":"images/characters/solan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Elara","u":"characters/elara.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"The Mother · goddess of the Threefold Crown · hearth-right and the family court · shown here as temple iconography","x":"The Crown's goddess of hearth and harvest, whose seat outlasts the others as the principal surviving civil authority. The Mother of the Threefold Crown's three, goddess of earth, harvest and hearth, whose hearth-right is Aldemark's family court and whose private rites each household keeps. The iron cold of early spring, when the granaries are thinnest and the ground is too hard to plant, is Elara's Fast to Aldemark's peasants, and it is on its first night that Halstead's dreams begin. Adaltrude holds her seat, keeps a small hearth-shrine for her private rites, and goes to it alone before dawn after the eleventh dream. When the Crown fractures, Elara's seat, under Adaltrude and her successors, absorbs most of what remains of the temple's civil authority. The goddess herself takes no part in the manuscript's events. The sheet draws the goddess as temple art does: ageless and larger than human, with a calm maternal face, long dark-gold hair set with a gold diadem, red-jeweled ornaments and amber eyes, in layered deep-red and cream robes, beside a lit hearth and a household altar. It notes that her seat eventually becomes the principal surviving civil authority of the diminished temple.","i":"images/characters/elara-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Ymma wakes before dawn","u":"records/ymma-wakes-before-dawn.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Ymma","x":"A young woman in a linen headscarf with dark braids sits up in a log-walled bed, looking upward with her lips parted, a bearded man asleep beside her, a candle burning on a small table and a frosted window showing stars over a snowy village Ymma sits up before dawn on the first night of Brayen, in the iron cold of Elara's Fast, with her husband still asleep beside her. She is about to wake him to say that she has been nowhere and everywhere.","i":"images/scenes/ymma-wakes-before-dawn-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"A dreamer drifts toward the moon","u":"records/a-dreamer-drifts-toward-the-moon.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","x":"A barefoot youth in a leather vest and rolled linen sleeves floats through black space with his arms loose and his face lifted toward an enormous cratered moon, a curve of the blue world below him among scattered stars A dreamer of Halstead drifts through a black field of unfamiliar lights toward the moon, reaching it, as the first dreamers describe it, simply by wishing to.","i":"images/scenes/a-dreamer-drifts-toward-the-moon-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The world seen from the moon","u":"records/the-world-seen-from-the-moon.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","x":"A young man in a leather vest and boots stands with his back to us on a rocky grey ledge and looks at the whole round world hanging in black space, its blue seas, green land and swirling white cloud filling the sky From far enough away the world stops looking like a floor and starts looking like a stone: round on every side, hanging in the dark with nothing beneath it and nothing propping it up.","i":"images/scenes/the-world-seen-from-the-moon-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Hraban questions Otker","u":"records/hraban-questions-otker.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Warden Hraban, Otker","x":"In a timbered hall by lamplight a bearded man in a dark coat with a gold medallion leans on a table with one hand, face to face with a lean young man in a leather vest and linen shirt who stands upright and unflinching, villagers crowded behind them, a lantern and a sheet of paper on the table Hraban questions Otker last and longest, with Halstead crowded into the room behind them. Otker tells him plainly, the way a man reports weather, that the world is round.","i":"images/scenes/hraban-questions-otker-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"A village after the purge","u":"records/a-village-after-the-purge.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","x":"A bearded man in a long dark coat stands alone among the burned timbers, broken cart wheel and puddled mud of a destroyed village, a torn dark banner with a star on a pole beside him, smoke rising under a heavy grey sky and a ruined tower on the far hill What is left of a purged village: burned timbers, a cart wheel in the mud, smoke still rising, a torn banner on its pole and one man standing among the ruins.","i":"images/scenes/a-village-after-the-purge-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Anchorite kneels in Halstead","u":"records/the-anchorite-kneels-in-halstead.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"The Anchorite of Ardwen","x":"In steady rain a hooded woman in tattered undyed grey wool kneels alone in black mud among the charred posts and chimneys of a burned village, a staff and pouch at her back, two hollows in the ash in front of her, a ruined tower on a misty hill beyond The Anchorite kneels in the ashes of Halstead for the better part of an afternoon without asking a single neighboring farm what happened there. The next rain will fill the two hollows her knees leave.","i":"images/scenes/the-anchorite-kneels-in-halstead-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The singing at Grenmoor","u":"records/the-singing-at-grenmoor.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"The Anchorite of Ardwen","x":"At sunset a hooded woman in tattered grey wool stands at the left of a shrine of stacked fieldstone draped in unbleached cloth with candles, flowers and baskets of bread, a crowd of villagers lifting their hands and singing toward a swirling night sky, armed knights with banners crossing the field on the right Grenmoor raises a shrine of fieldstone and unbleached cloth to the Star-Walkers and sings, while armed men of the Crown come across the field. The Anchorite stands before it and tells no one which god she thinks the singing was for.","i":"images/scenes/the-singing-at-grenmoor-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The iron globe at Faldrun","u":"records/the-iron-globe-at-faldrun.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"The Anchorite of Ardwen","x":"In a firelit forge a bearded blacksmith swings a hammer at a glowing iron globe on an anvil in a shower of sparks, a hooded woman in grey raising one hand over it in blessing while a group of villagers watch from the shadows At Faldrun a blacksmith hammers an iron globe on his anvil, and the Anchorite lays her hand above it and blesses it in Ardwen's name while the village watches.","i":"images/scenes/the-iron-globe-at-faldrun-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The boundary stone at Aschendale","u":"records/the-boundary-stone-at-aschendale.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"The Anchorite of Ardwen","x":"On an autumn hillside grey-bearded elders and villagers stand around a lichen-covered boundary stone carved with a sunburst, one elder with his palm open toward a black-and-gold robed official holding a great book with a gold sunburst on its cover, armed guards at the official's side and a hooded woman standing quietly behind the stone At Aschendale's boundary stone the elders face an official of the Crown with a great book and an escort of soldiers, and choose a hard truth over an easy lie. The Anchorite stands among the villagers behind them. No purge is ever ordered here.","i":"images/scenes/the-boundary-stone-at-aschendale-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Anchorite is taken at Reinfeld","u":"records/the-anchorite-is-taken-at-reinfeld.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"The Anchorite of Ardwen","x":"Soldiers in dark helmets and cloaks marked with a sunburst take a hooded woman in ragged grey by the wrists beside a harbor where smoke rises from a distant village, a young spearman in the foreground looking away, banners behind them and a crowd of villagers watching At Reinfeld soldiers of the Crown take the Anchorite on charges of vagrancy and obstruction. She asks the one who binds her wrists whether he was at Halstead.","i":"images/scenes/the-anchorite-is-taken-at-reinfeld-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Anchorite asks to come","u":"records/the-anchorite-asks-to-come.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"The Anchorite of Ardwen","x":"A hooded woman in a ragged grey robe kneels in a narrow stone cell, one wrist in an iron cuff on a chain, her hands lifted into a shaft of moonlight from a single barred window that shows stars and a full moon In a cell too narrow to lie down in, the Anchorite kneels toward the one barred slit and asks, not for rescue or vindication, only to be let come.","i":"images/scenes/the-anchorite-asks-to-come-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Anchorite rises above Aldemark","u":"records/the-anchorite-rises-above-aldemark.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"The Anchorite of Ardwen","x":"A hooded figure in a pale cloak floats upright above the curve of a blue-and-white world, a glowing path of stars and golden suns spiralling away overhead, a walled city and a river valley below The Anchorite rises above Aldemark, past the globe, the furnace of the sun and a river of suns, farther than any dreamer in the kingdom's history has been given.","i":"images/scenes/the-anchorite-rises-above-aldemark-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The dormant figure","u":"records/the-dormant-figure.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"The Anchorite of Ardwen","x":"A colossal pale figure with long straight black hair and unblinking red eyes, its white robe spreading like a mountain across the whole frame, fills the darkness; at its feet, on black water, a tiny hooded figure stands At the top of the ascent the Anchorite stands before a dormant figure with straight black hair, a white too fine to name and red unblinking eyes. She asks whether it called her. It does not answer.","i":"images/scenes/the-dormant-figure-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The eleventh dream over Dornhal","u":"records/the-eleventh-dream-over-dornhal.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Ansovald, Sigward, Adaltrude","x":"A cathedral city lit gold under a starry night beside a river with a stone bridge; the faint translucent figures of three robed rulers, one in red and cream, one in dark with gold and one in pale blue, rise above it with crowds of ghostly faces among the stars while soldiers and townsfolk fill the street below The eleventh selection falls on the capital itself. Dornhal dreams wall to wall, and the Triarchs Ansovald, Sigward and Adaltrude rise above it among the faces of its sleepers.","i":"images/scenes/the-eleventh-dream-over-dornhal-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Hraban finds the Anchorite","u":"records/hraban-finds-the-anchorite.html","m":"Case 1268 · Independent Observation","d":"Warden Hraban, The Anchorite of Ardwen","x":"A weathered man in a plain grey coat and a hooded woman in tattered grey sit on a ruined stone ledge on either side of a vine-covered wayside shrine with a candle lit in its niche, a river valley and a distant castle under a sunset sky behind them Within the year Hraban, in a plain grey coat with no insignia, finds the Anchorite at a crossroads shrine and asks whether she dreamed what the city dreamed.","i":"images/scenes/hraban-finds-the-anchorite-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"Fossa","u":"books/0202.html","m":"Case 0202 · Awaiting release","d":"Swarm. Ditch. Signature. Tithe. Miracle.","x":"Case 0202"},{"k":"s","t":"Observer 551","u":"characters/observer-551.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Cultivator Envoy and Custodian of record for Case 0202 · cover: Ratièr, an itinerant rat-catcher · appears about 45 by his sheet","x":"Seeds one clay pot of fungus on a mountain pass, files three notes, and sells nothing. A thin, plain, forgettable man in a brown travelling cloak with a pack, a long bait-spoon and a satchel of clay pots, who has walked the Lauze for nineteen days by the evening of the twentieth of Garren under the name Ratièr, which in the dialect is a trade and not a name. A rat-catcher passes through every mill, barn and cellar of a grain valley without question, which is the access the study needs and the only access it needs. On the eleventh of Garren he breaks a sealed clay bait-pot with the spoon, by hand, in the upper broom-scrub of the saddle called the Port, where the swarm's first night's roost will fall, in the manner of a man laying poison for vermin in a place that has none. He supplies no knowledge; he introduces one organism and lets it do what it does, and the banding on its fruiting collar is the only signature he leaves. He attends the weighing at the mill offering bait, sits at the table by the door in Brunissen's tavern with a mug of ale he does not drink and takes Gaucelm's tin ward, turns it over with great gravity and hands it back, and on the fifth night stands on the lip of the second rank with a bait-pot and sells nothing. He files three Envoy's Notes. He records an apology on an instrument not calibrated to record one, does not apply his translation array to the stranger's speech because the Prime Directive bars supplying a framework, and finds he cannot stop knowing the syllables of a five-line verse he did not translate. Three times he passes the other Custodian, Observer 618 of Case 2231, once on the quarry road, once at the quarrymen's wall and once on the lane below Vilarnau walking toward the mill with a lantern, each in the character of a man with nothing to say to the other, and the courtesy is exact. In a later spring he goes down the long south road with his pack and his bait-spoon to a country where the rats are of a different kind. The sheet's 5'8\", thin brown hair, pale grey eyes and apparent age of forty-five are its own; the manuscript has only “thin, middle-aged, brown-cloaked, with a pack.” He is not supposed to find that difficult to put down. Filed.","i":"images/characters/observer-551-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Strain H-202","u":"characters/strain-h-202.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Catalyst of Case 0202 · Heliotropic Orthopteran Mycosis, strain H-202 · seeded on the eleventh of Garren","x":"A banded fungus that turns a swarm toward the sun and leaves a cliff hung with the dead. A banded fungal parasite of an orthopteran swarm, broken open from a sealed clay bait-pot in the upper broom-scrub of the Port on the eleventh of Garren, where the swarm's line of descent will place its first night's roost. It carries no instructions and no Cultivator knowledge. It reaches its conidial stage within thirty hours of contact and spreads by contact alone, which is why the trenches matter: held animals crowd, and crowded animals in a population carrying a contact-transmitted spore infect one another faster than any open-field swarm could in a season. The Log puts contact density in the gorge at eleven times free-flight baseline and infection at an estimated eighty-eight in a hundred by the end of the fourth day. At dusk of the fifth the infected animals begin to climb; at first light on the twenty-seventh of Havren they turn, as one, from the line of the swarm's advance to the line of the sun, go up the nearest east-facing surface, grip with the forelegs, spread their wings and die. On each body a chalk-white fruiting collar forms within the hour, thirty-three pale bands in the order one, one, two, three, five, eight, thirteen, so that a sufficiently rigorous investigator may infer a non-natural origin without any disclosure from the Observer's desk. The flesh of the dead is edible, an inherited feature and not an intended one, and tastes of roasted chestnuts and butter. The strain is self-limiting and was never recovered; the animals that leaked through in the first days, infected more slowly in open country, died singly over the next fortnight on the east-facing walls of the valley, and every preserved specimen in the Order's cabinet at Montclar carries the bands, beside which the clerk Matteo wrote same on all; curious in pencil. By the Log's reckoning the works at the Throat held the swarm in the gorge five point one days longer than an open crossing would have, long enough for the strain to do what it does; without them the swarm would have passed the Narrow in a morning and ended in open country after the crop was gone. The manuscript gives no length for the insect; the sheet's rust-red body of about five centimetres is its own.","i":"images/characters/strain-h-202-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Accord of Montclar","u":"characters/the-accord-of-montclar.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Royal charter of Auvarre · the Right of First Naming · two centuries old · drafted in the reign of the second Ramon","x":"Promises a tenth of the realm's grain, forever, to whichever altar the realm can be found to owe, and ends on the numeral one. A charter drafted by a committee of two altars and a notary after the Drowning of the Lower Fields, when a river went back in the night with no one's leave and three temples each told their flocks by morning that it was theirs. Its remedy was elegant: whichever altar the realm could be found to owe its salvation to should have the Right of First Naming, to name the deliverance in the law and liturgy of the realm and take a tenth of the grain of the realm, year by year, for as long as the realm lasted. The seventh article is where the verb is, owe. The eighth gives the arbiters seven days to decide which altar that is; the ninth gives a divided table to the king's casting voice, saving the king's own interest; and the tenth says that if the arbiters stand divided and the throne be silent, each altar shall name one man, sworn to no other altar, to bear one edge before the three doors until one man yield or fall. The arbiters sit each for the whole of an altar and not for one chapter, and the tithe is of the grain of the realm and not only of the March, since the second Ramon reasoned that a danger to any field was a danger to every field. It had been tested once, with two altars, and ended in a quiet agreement. It had never been tested with three. On the first of Iverin Arnaut reads it aloud at the oak table from a bad copy in a clerk's cramped hand, three smudges and a stain, in the flat voice of a man who cannot improve the text. The Hierarch and the Provost each ask for the Naming; the Keeper says she does not know; the king's recusal comes out of the bailiff's sleeve; and the tenth article, which he reads in a voice gone flat, asks for one edge from a stranger who carries two. “The Observer introduced no numeral,” says the Log, and the numeral in the tenth article is the whole of what the case turns on. The sheet draws the charter on calfskin vellum in brown-black ink with three hanging wax seals and the word ONE under the tenth numeral; the manuscript does not say how many seals it carries or what the vellum is. One man. One edge.","i":"images/characters/the-accord-of-montclar-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"King Ramon III","u":"characters/king-ramon-iii.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"King of Auvarre, third of his name, seated at Montclar · twenty-six years on the throne · 54 years old by his sheet","x":"A poor man and an honest one, who declines the casting voice in advance and signs his kingdom in two with a hand that does not shake. The king of Auvarre, who sits at Montclar in a palace the court jokes was built by a committee of the dead, and who is, in the letter that opens the case, the one person in the realm with no grain, no gold and no soldiers who says so. He commands every baron of the southern marches to do what his own hand can find to do, summons the three altars of the Accord of Montclar to the March, and offers them, being poor, the only coin a poor king can mint without leaving a mark on his treasury, which is credit: the Right of First Naming. On the twenty-sixth of Garren, the day a clerk of the Last Order calls at the palace to remind him, with perfect courtesy, of the date the wall-loan falls due, he signs a second letter and sends it south without waiting to learn what the altars have done. Being a poor man and an honest one, and in debt to one of them, he cannot in conscience give a casting voice in a cause in which he is himself a debtor. A debtor who cast for his creditor would be a king bought, and one who cast against him a king foreclosed; he chooses, as poor men do, the course that costs him nothing, which is to be absent from the Accord in advance. He signs the partition on the ninth of Lorren in the third winter, in a room above the wall the Order's money built, with a brazier at his feet and the Provost-Examiner of the Montclar chapter at his elbow holding the pen: eleven articles recognising two countries, the Lit Reach and the Baronies, and releasing the crown from the loan in return for acknowledging the Naming of the Last Order on one side of a line and of the Threefold on the other. He reads all eleven, which no one asked him to do, with his lips moving slightly, as men read who were taught to read late, and then looks at the wall of the room and says that it is a good wall. His hand does not shake; it is the one thing in the room that remains under his command, and he spends it as a man spends his last coin. He dies four months later and is buried in the spring beneath a stone the Order paid for. The manuscript gives his twenty-six years and his late reading, not his age; the sheet's fifty-four, near-sighted watery grey eyes, thin brushed-back grey hair and short beard are its own. It is a good wall.","i":"images/characters/king-ramon-iii-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Pons","u":"characters/pons.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Herald of King Ramon III · 19 at the opening, 22 at the Throat · later keeper of the royal archive at Montclar","x":"Sees a smear the color of dried blood on the southern horizon, and in a long life is never asked to describe it. A herald of nineteen who rides four days from Montclar to arrive on the twenty-fourth of Garren, with the king's letter in an oiled satchel strapped against his chest and his chin tucked down against a wind that has turned, unseasonably, warm. On the morning of the fourth day, from the crest of the last ridge before the Lauze, he sees on the southern horizon a smear the color of dried blood, no larger than a thumbprint, that neither moves nor fades, and looks at it for a long time with the clear feeling of a boy who has been given a message and has just found out what it weighs. He delivers the letter to the bailiff in the correct form, and then, when the formalities are over, asks in a small voice whether he might sit down. Three years later, at twenty-two, he is sent down the south road in sleet on the thirteenth of Lorren with the sealed duplicate of the partition strapped where the king's letter once was, to read it at the Throat to a hundred of the Baronies' levy in the red of the Son and a hundred and twenty lay-brothers of the Last Order in grey, with Gaucelm vending roasted nago between them at the same price to both. He reads it in the clear carrying voice the College of Heralds taught him, watches the masons set an unlettered stone, and looks up, because it is the only direction nobody else is looking, at what is left of the Dawn Wall: a fringe of the dead along the brow, so exactly the color of the smear that his horse lays its ears back. He has no wish to describe it, and does not. He becomes, forty years on, a very old man and the keeper of the royal archive at Montclar, and among the thousand times he is asked about the partition of Auvarre he is never once asked about the wall. The manuscript gives him no face; the sheet's freckles, hazel eyes and royal-blue herald's tabard are its own.","i":"images/characters/pons-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ibrahim ibn Nasr","u":"characters/ibrahim-ibn-nasr.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Wool-factor of the port of Almariya · first bearer of the news of the Red Weather · 38 years old by his sheet","x":"Rides eleven days north on a mule he means to sell, and tells a bailiff a sentence nobody believes. A wool-factor from the southern coast who rides eleven days up through the Serra on a mule he intends to sell and a horse he does not, and arrives at the inn below the castle spur on the seventeenth of Garren with a cup of thin red wine and the expression of a man composing, with some care, a sentence he knows no one will believe. He tells it to the bailiff Arnaut in the dialect of the district, which he speaks well and with a slight stiffness, as of a man handling a tool borrowed from a neighbor: that the Red Weather has come up out of the dry country beyond the southern mountains, eaten the barley of the coast in early summer and the olives and vines of the hill-country in late, and that on the quay of Almariya he watched a cloud the color of rust come over the sea-wall at noon without a sound that a man could have named, and the market's awnings turn into something that moved. It did not stop at the stubble. Arnaut, who has been handed a figure that fits none of his columns, pays for the wine himself, the first time in the factor's recollection that an official of any country has done so. On the first of Havren he is at Brunissen's tavern, persuaded after much wine to be a guest, and tells the room, with a certain defiant humor, that in the south the people have grown so tired of the Weather that they have begun to eat it: dried on hot stones with salt, fried in oil and sold by the bowl, the fat ones of the early swarm tasting something like toasted nuts. The room answers with a long and affectionate silence, and then someone laughs. It is the most consequential throwaway line in the case. On the afternoon of the twenty-seventh, with the dead heaped round her ankles and the Vigil's porridge gone, the miller's widow Ermengarda says to the stranger, in the flat tone of a woman discharging an obligation, that in the south, so the factor had said, they ate them. The manuscript does not say what became of him. It gives his name, his trade and his manner; the sheet's thirty-eight years, dust-striped cloak, white cap and neatly trimmed black beard are its own. It stopped when there was nothing left that it could stand on.","i":"images/characters/ibrahim-ibn-nasr-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ottavio Brandi","u":"characters/ottavio-brandi.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Provost-Examiner of the Solar Temple's chapter at Montclar · arbiter of the second altar, the Last Order · 50 years old","x":"Judges a wall of dead grasshoppers by lens, writes “Lit” in the Book, and spends the rest of his life writing the date, the weather and the town. A lean, fastidiously dressed man of fifty in the undyed grey of the Last Order, with a high narrow forehead, a pinched mouth and a pair of cold clever hands, who arrives at Castelmaur on the twenty-sixth of Garren in a closed grey carriage with two clerks, a portable strongroom, a brazier he will not be parted from and a ground lens the thickness of a man's palm in a padded case on his knees. At the council of the three altars he proposes that all the grain of the valley, green or ripe, be reaped into the strongroom of the Solar Temple under seal and receipt, since what is ledgered cannot be eaten by an enemy that has no clerks. He asks the foreigner for the names of his swords, hears Yoake and Yoiyami, Daybreak and Evening Dark, the hour the light comes back and the hour it is spent, and enters in the margin of his own mind, as an accident of nomenclature and nothing more, that the stranger has a certain presence. He passes him the roll headed Works done under the Reading, under the maxim every novice copies forty times, the hand that signs must answer, and keeps the signature. On the morning of the twenty-seventh of Havren he has the lens sent for before anyone has understood there is anything to judge, throws the sun through it onto the Dawn Wall, and sees, in the length of a held breath, that the wall is returning the beam rather than making any, as a polished basin returns a candle. An Examiner who writes his doubt into the margin of a Judgment has made himself the next thing the Judgment must be applied to; he says to the clerk with the pen, “Enter it. Lit.” On the twentieth of Iverin he reads the second line of his sister's letter, learns that his daughter died on the ninth of Garren, and goes down on the boards of his room in front of the brazier; in the morning he turns Arnaut's compromise paper face down on the newel-post and orders the granary sealed. He holds the chapter at Montclar eleven years more, never again enters a debt in the name of a feeling, writes the date, the weather and the name of the town at the head of every document that leaves his hand, and on every ninth of Garren rides four days south to read the evening office alone at the foot of the Dawn Wall, to no one, with nothing entered. He writes every week, beginning with the weather, to a girl who is dead; the Examiners who clear his desk find five hundred and seventy-two of the letters. The Book still says the Throat was lit. Credit is not a feeling, Hierarch. It is an entry.","i":"images/characters/ottavio-brandi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Matteo","u":"characters/matteo.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Clerk to the Provost-Examiner of the Last Order · a baker's son from the hills behind Montclar · 23 years old","x":"Carries the Order's flame three leagues up a valley in a clay lamp, counts the bands on the dead, and writes the date in a margin. A fat, pale young man with a face like a boiled egg, twenty-three, a baker's son from the hills behind Montclar who joined the Last Order for the sake of its ovens and counts everything unprompted: a sack, a stair, a flock of sheep, a row of loaves. For fifteen days at the Narrow he carries the Order's flame, kindled each noon at the focus of the ground lens on the flat roof of the inn at Vilarnau, three leagues up the valley in a small covered clay lamp pressed against his chest like a baby, glancing down at it every dozen paces with the anxious tenderness of a young father, and never lets it go out. He keeps the labor-tally in a fair hand every evening; he enters the leakage of the first day at three in a hundred and is told by the bailiff to enter it at two. On the morning of the twenty-seventh of Havren he climbs a barrel in the shadow of the Provost's platform and counts the pale rings on the dead: forty, a hundred, thirty-three bands on each, in the order one, one, two, three, five, eight, thirteen, the nursery progression his mother used to teach him to add. He thinks nothing of it that he would say to a Provost, since a baker's boy knows what it means when every loaf in an oven comes out alike, and writes in the margin of his roll in pencil same on all; curious. On the night of the twentieth of Iverin he comes in at a sound with a candle and sees the Provost on the floor; he sits against the wall in the dark until morning, says nothing then or ever, and in the grey takes the day's roll and writes in its margin, where the Order's rule allows only a number, the date, a word for the weather, and the name of the town. He does not strike it out, though he is a young man with a living to make. The Order's system files it with the rest. The manuscript gives his age, his bakery and his pallor; the sheet's bowl-cut light brown hair, mild blue eyes, ink-stained fingers and pencil behind the ear are its own. Same on all; curious.","i":"images/characters/matteo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Alazaïs","u":"characters/alazais.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Keeper of the House of Vigil at Sant-Sever · arbiter of the third altar, the Vigil of Ardwen · 61 years old, 64 at her death","x":"Offers hands instead of a remedy, answers the council with “I do not know,” and feeds both altars without asking which they serve. A small, brown-faced woman of sixty-one with a bad hip, a worn grey cloak and a face that gives the impression of having been left out in the weather and improved by it, who arrives at the temple gate at dusk on the twenty-seventh of Garren by mule, tells the servant who cannot decide whether to bow that she has a perfectly good pair of legs somewhere beneath the saddle, and asks to be shown where she is to sleep. She keeps the House of Vigil at Sant-Sever, one of some forty in the realm, and she has no remedy, no lamp, no lens, no ram and no pyre; she has hands. She says so to the council as one reports the weather: the Vigil does not command the sky, but its ten houses within a day's walk have a great many hands, and digging is not a claim. She sends six hundred of them, goes up and down the trenches through fifteen days with a can of water and a heel of bread, and where someone has stopped, sits down beside him. In the evenings she runs through her fingers a knotted cord, one knot for every child she has baptized in forty years, a hundred and fifty-three of them, tied a little looser than a knot ought to be, so that a child who comes back might be found. Asked on the first of Iverin, as arbiter of the third altar, which of the other two the realm owes, she says “I do not know,” and says it again more slowly for the clerk: that Ardwen answers the honest but never on command, and that she will not put a name on the table for the sake of a tenth of anyone's grain. Asked, in the Accord's own form, whether the Vigil will name a man for the Edge, she says that Ardwen does not send a man to answer a question she has not asked. That midnight she walks to the mill on her bad hip and tries, with a hundred and fourteen words of his language, to tell the stranger what he has signed, grain, tithe, a sack and a hand and a falling coin, Edge, war; he hears every word and understands none, and takes her Do not for do not be afraid. Every word was true, and it was a kind of silence. On the twentieth of Iverin she opens the doors at Sant-Sever without announcement and feeds the families of the dead, the levy's wives and children and the hungry of the Last Order out of a store scraped to the stone, never asking which altar they serve. She dies in the third year, at sixty-four, of a fever she takes from a child, having fed eleven thousand four hundred persons by Joan's count; her cord has a hundred and sixty-nine knots. What she told the young Keeper who asked whether the goddess had turned the Weather is carved above the door of the House in the next century, and the pilgrims who come to read it are disappointed. The manuscript gives her age, her hip, her face and her cloak; the sheet's iron-grey hair and warm light-brown eyes are its own. I do not know.","i":"images/characters/alazais-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Joan","u":"characters/joan.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Lay-brother of the House of Vigil at Sant-Sever · 20 at the opening, about 23 at the Keeper's death","x":"Is afraid of the mule, counts the fed and then the buried, and asks the one question nobody else would. A lay-brother of twenty who carries the Keeper's saddlebag, is afraid of her mule, and stands behind her chair at the council with an air of respectful terror. He counts, as it turns out, with his lips moving, on his fingers when he works. On the third morning of the burying of the swarm Gersenda observes that he has stopped being afraid of the mule and is now afraid of nothing, working with a look on his face of a man who has been shown, quite suddenly, the inside of a word. He carries the lantern behind the Keeper on her midnight walk to the mill; and in the second winter, behind the refectory at Sant-Sever, where the ground is frozen to the depth of a hand and a grave takes four mornings and a fire, he begins to count the buried in the margin of the page on which he has counted the fed, and stops adding at a figure he never repeats. At the Keeper's bed in the third year he asks her the question he has carried since the first of Iverin and never found the hour for: whether she might not simply have picked one. “Two to one,” says Joan, “and over by noon.” She considers it a long time with her hand on the cord, says that she was very tired that day, had not thought of it, and does not know. It was not an answer, and Joan, who loved her, does not pretend that it was. He goes on keeping the count of the fed at Sant-Sever, with Gersenda's second column of the dead beside it. The manuscript gives his age and his fear of the mule; the sheet's six feet of height, prominent nose, ragged fair fringe and light blue eyes are its own. Two to one, and over by noon.","i":"images/characters/joan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Paciència","u":"characters/paciencia.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"The Keeper's mule · carries Alazaïs from Sant-Sever · 12 years old by her sheet","x":"Possesses none of the virtue she is named for, and frightens a lay-brother by standing there. The mule on which Alazaïs rides the two days from the House of Vigil at Sant-Sever to the temple at Castelmaur, and on which she leaves the mill gate in the cold light of the second of Iverin. Her name means patience, and the manuscript is careful to record that she possesses none: she has not been consulted about the Keeper's midnight walk to the mill, and grumbles in the rear of it. The lay-brother Joan, who carries the Keeper's saddlebag, is afraid of her through the whole of the digging, until the burying of the swarm leaves him afraid of nothing. The manuscript gives her no age, colour or build. The sheet's twelve years, stocky four-foot-ten, dusty grey-brown coat, notched left ear, drooping lips, patched wool saddle-cloth and dark, unimpressed eyes are its own, and its captions, Suspicion, Alarmed by Joan, Stubborn Resolve, Impatient Grumble, are the nearest the file comes to her opinion of any of it.","i":"images/characters/paciencia-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bertran de Lanta","u":"characters/bertran-0202.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Sir Bertran de Lanta · master-at-arms of Castelmaur · knight · 41 years old by this sheet","x":"Fights a stranger to a draw, to a yield and to a gap in the second rank, and finds no column to enter any of it in. The master-at-arms of Castelmaur, tall and broad, “as grave and unreadable as an oak”, knighted at nineteen and sworn to Auron in the temple at Montclar, who has spent twenty-two years learning that a knight does not say what he is thinking. On the twentieth of Garren, from the saddle, he drew on the stranger on the green at Vilarnau and had his long sword turned out of his hand in three point one seconds by a motion his eyes could not follow, and was left alive. On the sixth of Havren he lays two practice swords of ash on the gravel at the Narrow, takes up a buckler of boiled leather and fights him for most of an hour to a draw, a conversation, and a remarkably fluent one; he goes to bed with a cracked rib under a blanket his sister has embroidered with horses, and carves the beginning of a small pearwood horse with its ears laid back and one forefoot raised. On the night of the twenty-sixth he goes down into the gap in the second rank with a burning brand and holds it beside the foreigner through a night; toward the end, when the foreigner's left arm begins to tremble, he takes the brand from his fingers and puts his own sword into them, and they say each other's names, Ber-tran, Higa. On the second of Iverin the Hierarch names him champion of the Threefold for the Edge, for a tithe whose price he was not told. He asks for the night, goes down the lane to the mill gate with the first of the pearwood horses in his fist, stands there as long as it takes a bell to ring Prime with the goose regarding him, and goes back up the lane, since nothing in his training told a man how to knock. On the third, in the temple yard, he fights the best he has ever fought, some three hundred strokes, takes a cut along the left forearm that opens the mail, has the stranger's blade hang a hand's breadth from his bare head for exactly as long as it takes a man to decide something, is disarmed by a left hand that answers before it is consulted, is spared a second time, and says to the foreigner, and not to the doors, “I yield. Let it be entered.” In the third winter, on the sixteenth of Kerren, he rides out of the gate of Castelmaur alone at night, without a banner and without his sword, to pay a debt of a kind this account does not record, with a grey gelding of pearwood, one hoof raised, in the pouch at his belt, which he carries for the rest of his life. The Lauze says he was never beaten. He would have said that he was spared twice in front of everyone and handed a sword once, and that the last was the only entry he ever found a way to carry. His sister has four children he has met twice. The same man has a page under Case 2231, whose sheet makes him thirty-eight; this one makes him forty-one, which is what twenty-two years of service from a knighting at nineteen come to. The two pages are not reconciled. I yield. Let it be entered.","i":"images/characters/bertran-0202-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Baron of Saint-Aventin","u":"characters/baron-of-saint-aventin.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Eldest of the Threefold barons · unnamed in the manuscript · 62 years old by his sheet","x":"Appears only as a letter, written in a hand that does not trouble with courtesy. He is a letter. The war, says the manuscript, took the better part of two winters to discover that it was one, and the discoveries came in the order in which a ledger reveals a loss: first a letter, then a date, then a wall. The letter comes on the ninth of Iverin under the colors of the Baron of Saint-Aventin, eldest of the Threefold barons, who writes to the Hierarch at Castelmaur in a hand that does not trouble with courtesy that eleven barons of the south will pay a tenth of their grain to no creditor in grey, and asks how many men the temple can put on a road by the spring. He writes four days after the Hierarch has declared the Accord of Montclar void on the fifth of Iverin, and what he asks for is soldiers. The date is the Last Order's, calling the crown's loan on the first of Kerren; the wall is at Montclar. That is the whole of what the manuscript gives him: a title, a seniority, a colour, a number of fellow barons and a tone of voice. He has no name, no face, no age, and no reply is recorded, though the Hierarch has been believed by eleven of the realm's seventeen barons, who are Threefold. The sheet's sixty-two years, stout five-foot-eight, florid jowled face, sharp dark-brown eyes, silver hair worn long to the collar, forked silver beard, gold ring on every finger of the right hand, scarlet-and-gold quilted surcoat, fur mantle and jeweled sword are its own, and so is the line it prints beside him, No creditor in grey shall take our grain, which is the sheet's gloss on the letter and not a quotation from it.","i":"images/characters/baron-of-saint-aventin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Provost's Daughter","u":"characters/the-provosts-daughter.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Daughter of Ottavio Brandi · unnamed · died on the ninth of Garren, aged 16","x":"Writes to her father every week for nine years, and is dead for the whole of the case before he is told. She is seven when her father puts her hand into her aunt's in the cathedral at Montclar and says, in the voice he uses for ledgers, that the Order's work will take him some years, which in the Order's practice means the circuits of the provincial houses and no more than the three weeks of each Reckoning-tide at home. She says, quite calmly, that she will write. She writes once a week for nine years, in a round clear child's hand that grows with the years into a young woman's, and he answers every letter on the day he opens it, in a hand that does not change, beginning with the weather he is not in. He has never once missed; the manuscript calls it the only discipline of his life that he was not taught. In the autumn he remarks to the brazier that the hand in her latest letters has grown a little steadier than a girl of sixteen might be expected to make it, and decides, as a man decides who has been cold for a very long time and does not wish to learn why, that a daughter is permitted to improve. She never appears in the manuscript alive. She dies of a fever of three days on the ninth of Garren, not painfully; on the second night she asks for her father once, and does not ask again. Her letter of the sixth is still unsealed in the drawer of her desk, because there was no time, and her aunt, who knows what the news would do to him, seals it afterward and sends it, and writes every one that follows in the child's hand and the child's voice. The case learns what the letters were on the twentieth of Iverin, when Ottavio Brandi reads the second line of his sister's letter. He goes on writing to her, weekly, beginning with the weather, and keeps the letters unsealed in a drawer; the Examiners who clear his desk find five hundred and seventy-two of them. The manuscript gives her age and her handwriting. The sheet's slim five-foot-three, narrow serious face with her father's high forehead, two dark braids, pale grey eyes, ink-stained fingertips and grey-blue wool dress with a white collar are its own.","i":"images/characters/the-provosts-daughter-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Provost's Sister","u":"characters/the-provosts-sister.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Sister of Ottavio Brandi · the aunt who raised his daughter · unnamed · 55 years old by her sheet","x":"Writes a dead girl's weekly letters in the dead girl's hand, and then writes the true one. She is the aunt into whose hand the Provost put his seven-year-old daughter in the cathedral at Montclar, and she is the one who taught the child to write. When the girl dies of a fever on the ninth of Garren, with a weekly letter lying unsealed in her desk, the aunt seals it and sends it, and then writes every letter that follows herself, in the round clear hand as nearly as an aunt who had taught it could make it and in the voice of a girl who was dead, so that her brother should not have to be told until the Order's business allowed him to come home. She thought it kind. She had meant, her letter says, to keep her silence until the spring. It gives out on the twenty-second of Havren, the morning after his answer of the seventeenth arrives and asks, at the foot, whether the girl has her winter cloak. She writes the true letter in her own hand, so that he may know it from the first stroke, and says that she is very sorry. He reads the first line on the first of Iverin and carries the rest, unread, in the sleeve of his grey coat until it falls out on the twentieth; the manuscript does not record that she is told what it did to him, or whether she is ever answered. It never names her. The sheet's fifty-five years, spare five-foot-six, severe tired face, pale skin, grey hair pinned tight, grey eyes, black wool dress and grey shawl are its own, and so is the teacher's hand it draws, steady and exact, which is inferred from the manuscript's remark that she had taught the girl's.","i":"images/characters/the-provosts-sister-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Smith of Vilarnau","u":"characters/smith-of-vilarnau.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"The village smith · unnamed · 42 years old by his sheet","x":"Brings a hammer to the green on the first night, and later makes a drum from a cask for the boys at the Throat. The village comes out onto the moot-green at Vilarnau at full dark on the nineteenth of Garren with rushlights and crooks and, in the case of the smith, a hammer, and stands in a ragged half-ring forty paces from the oak, and nobody goes closer. It has been nine years since the last man came out of the stone, and he died. The old women of the church remember him in the tavern on the first of Havren: a thin small man with ink on his fingers who had lived eleven days in the smith's loft and died on the twelfth, of nothing in particular, apologizing. Case 2231's page for Kaneyoshi gives the same man three days and a burial on the third night; the two accounts are not reconciled. In the same room the smith offers his verdict on the Keeper of Sant-Sever, who has asked for nothing: a priest who asks for nothing, he says, “has a purpose that he is keeping to himself.” At the Throat he makes a drum from a cask, and by the third morning of the digging the line of boys with the wooden harvest-rattles has been taught to keep time to it; when the swarm comes down on the gorge, a hundred drums behind the second rank, beaten by boys in a long line, keep the main flood from settling. His wife, the smith's wife, made the stranger sausages and was turned down. Case 2231 has the same man at the green with his hammer among the villagers, and has him not living to reach the ridge after Vilarnau burns, when his son, the Smith's Son, carries the hammer up the quarry road; this manuscript does not say what became of him. The sheet's forty-two years, massive six-foot-one frame, square soot-smudged face, black hair tied back, thick beard singed at the ends, dark eyes, burn-scarred forearms, leather apron and sleeveless tunic are its own.","i":"images/characters/smith-of-vilarnau-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Smith's Wife","u":"characters/smiths-wife.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Wife of the village smith · unnamed · 40 years old by her sheet","x":"Made sausages for the stranger and was turned down, and says what the village is afraid of out loud. She has made sausages for the stranger, and he has turned them down. On the twenty-seventh of Garren, on the moot-green, he draws a long deep line in the dust with a row of small flames beside it and a cloud of marks on the farther side; Old Bernat takes the pipe from his mouth and says, quite quietly, “Fossa”; a woman makes the sign of Elara, and a second woman does; and then the smith's wife says in a high voice that the Moon-Man is telling them what to dig. It goes through the ring in a murmur. He is a prophet, or an angel, or the Eastern Dead come back to warn them; he has drawn them a grave, and beside it the pyres, and on the farther side the Red Weather itself coming down to fill the valley. She does not say, or the manuscript does not record, what she thinks of him after that; her one line is the whole of her part, and it names what everyone had been afraid to. Why the sausages were refused is not given here either. Case 2231 draws her among the village chorus, sending round a dish of boiled sausage and weeping when it comes back untouched. Her husband is the smith. The sheet's forty years, plump five-foot-three, round ruddy face quick to take offense, brown eyes, brown hair under a kerchief, wool dress, greasy apron, tray of sausages and shrill voice are its own; the manuscript has only “a high voice.”","i":"images/characters/smiths-wife-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Arnal","u":"characters/arnal.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Smallholder of the lower terrace at Vilarnau · two fields · 38 years old by his sheet","x":"Cuts his millet by night, hides it in the loft like a birth, and is entered in the Book on the fourteenth. A smallholder of the lower terrace at Vilarnau, who cuts his two fields by night in the second week of Iverin and hides the sheaves in his loft under a layer of straw, like a man concealing a birth. The harvest stands that autumn. The sickles of the Threefold are blessed and the seals of the Last Order are on the barns, and nobody can cut a sheaf in the barony without committing an offense against one altar or the other; the Order writes the name of any household that cuts or carries without receipt into the Book, under those spending a favor that was not theirs, and a name once entered can be struck out only by a further entry that no one in the barony has the means to buy. The Order's clerk enters Arnal's name on the fourteenth. The hearth-priests of the Threefold, for their part, withhold their rites from any house that sells the Son's grain into a grey box, so that a man might cut, and store, and eat, and then bury his mother in silence. In the third week of Jorren Father Guiraut, who has a conscience and a basket of honey-cakes and no instruction that allows him to use either, withholds the burial blessing from Arnal's father, with his sleeve in both hands and his eyes on the ground, as a man draws a tooth of his own. The millet is cut in the end, in the last week of Jorren, in the rain, by whoever has a sickle and no priest, a little better than half of what it was. The manuscript gives Arnal a village, a terrace, two fields, a loft and a father; the sheet's wiry five-foot-seven frame, narrow sun-lined face with a permanent squint, thinning straw-colored hair, pale blue eyes, peeling nose, two days' stubble, missing top joint on the left little finger and patched russet tunic are its own.","i":"images/characters/arnal-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bernat the Younger","u":"characters/bernat-the-younger.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Farmer of Vilarnau with a field on the south terrace · 50 years old · the eleventh of Iverin","x":"Stands an hour at the edge of his ripe field with a sickle, waiting for somebody to tell him whose it is. A farmer of Vilarnau, a stout man of fifty with a field of his own on the south terrace, who stands at the edge of it on the evening of the eleventh of Iverin with a sickle in his hand and his wife at his elbow, for an hour, and does not cut. When his wife asks him why, he says that he is waiting for somebody to tell him whose it is. It is not an answer, the manuscript says; it is an accounting. Both altars have named a price, and he has worked out, as plainly as any bailiff, that he is being asked to choose between a granary and a grave: the Order will enter his name in the Book if he cuts or carries without receipt, and the hearth-priests of the Threefold will withhold christening, marriage and funeral blessing from any house that sells the Son's grain into a grey box. The manuscript does not say what he does when the hour is over, though the millet is cut in the end, in the last week of Jorren, in the rain, by whoever has a sickle and no priest. He is not Old Bernat, the reeve who says Fossa on the green and whose page is in Case 2231; the manuscript does not say whether the two are kin, and the pages are kept apart. It gives the Younger his age, his build, his terrace and his hour. The sheet's thick neck, square ruddy heavy-browed face, cropped grey-brown hair, hazel eyes, weathered red-brown skin, grey stubble, swollen knuckles and russet tunic are its own.","i":"images/characters/bernat-the-younger-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bernat the Younger's Wife","u":"characters/bernat-the-youngers-wife.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Wife of Bernat the Younger · unnamed · 48 years old by her sheet","x":"Stands at her husband's elbow for an hour and asks the only question anyone puts to him. She stands beside her husband at the edge of the ripe field on the south terrace on the evening of the eleventh of Iverin, and for an hour, while he does not cut, she says nothing that the manuscript records. Then she asks him why. His answer, that he is waiting for somebody to tell him whose it is, is the case in a sentence, and she is the only person in it to whom he gives it. Behind them the millet stands ripening past ripeness through the cold clear weeks of Iverin, because the sickles of the Threefold are blessed and the seals of the Last Order are on the barns, and the sickle in his hand stays idle. The manuscript names her only as his wife, gives her no age, and does not say what she says after his answer, nor what the household does. The stakes for her house are the ones it sets out for every house in the barony: the Book on one side, and on the other no christening, no marriage and no funeral blessing. The sheet's forty-eight years, sturdy five-foot-three, round anxious face with deep dimples, dark brown hair going grey beneath a linen kerchief, dark brown eyes, chapped reddened hands, brown wool dress and shawl clutched at the throat are its own. Her husband is Bernat the Younger.","i":"images/characters/bernat-the-youngers-wife-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sergeant of the Levy","u":"characters/sergeant-of-the-levy.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Sergeant of the Baronies' levy, in the red of the Son · unnamed · 36 years old by his sheet","x":"Asks for porridge at the mill in the second autumn, and later goes away without the stranger. The war reaches the Mill of the Two Weirs in the second autumn, and it comes as a request for porridge, made by a sergeant of the Baronies' levy in the red of the Son, who stands in the kitchen doorway with his helmet under his arm and the expression of a man who has been told that hunger is no excuse and has not been told what is. Ermengarda gives him a bowl. An hour later she gives one to a corporal of the Order in grey, who has come on the same errand by the other road, and sets the two of them at opposite ends of the same bench with the kettle between them; neither man can bring himself to be the first to refuse. He has seen the stranger before. The manuscript says that the Baronies' sergeant had stood in the yard on the third of Iverin and knew what he could do with a pair of ash swords, and that the levy came for the stranger twice that winter. When the sergeant finds him in the mill-yard at the slaughter with both blades hung on a nail in the byre and his hands open at his sides, in the posture of a man waiting to be told where to report, he goes away without his name. It does not identify him with Sicard of Case 2231, the bailiff's sergeant at Castelmaur, or with any other sergeant in the case. The sheet's thirty-six years, stocky five-foot-eight frame, broad sunburned face, cropped dark hair, brown eyes, stubble, slight limp, short spear and leather jerkin are its own.","i":"images/characters/sergeant-of-the-levy-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Corporal of the Order","u":"characters/corporal-of-the-order.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Corporal of the Last Order's lay-brothers, in grey · unnamed · 34 years old by his sheet","x":"Comes for the stranger a month after the sergeant, and goes away with the same nothing and a second bowl of porridge. He arrives an hour after the sergeant, by the other road, on the same errand, and Ermengarda sets him at the opposite end of the same bench, with the kettle between them, on the single principle that a house which fed one altar's soldiers and not the other's would shortly be a house that had chosen, and that nobody had asked her to. It is an older law than the Accord, the manuscript says, and it holds in that kitchen for fifteen months, as the oldest laws hold, because no one can remember who drafted it. He is the Last Order's side of it, a corporal in grey. A month after the sergeant, he comes for the stranger on the same errand; the levy has come for him twice that winter, and the stranger keeps a vow of no steel in that house. The manuscript has the corporal go away with the same nothing and a second bowl of porridge. Nothing in it identifies him further. The sheet's thirty-four years, lean five-foot-nine frame with the stoop of long marches, long tired polite face, pale skin, close-cropped brown hair, grey eyes, clean-shaven jaw, sun-in-a-circle badge at the breast, undyed grey wool tunic and worn leather jerkin are its own, and so is its note that he is a lay-brother. The sergeant of the levy sits at the other end of the bench.","i":"images/characters/corporal-of-the-order-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Goose","u":"characters/goose.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"The yard goose of the Mill of the Two Weirs · 4 years old by its sheet","x":"Holds a view of the world that a Provost finds presumptuous, and stands in the gate on two nights when nobody else can. The mill's yard goose, which has a view of the world that Ottavio Brandi finds presumptuous. On the night of the first of Iverin, when he walks down the lane to present the Order's roll to a stranger through the mouth of a sixteen-year-old miller's daughter, it is in his way at the gate when he leaves, and he stops; the clerk Matteo, waiting by the lantern, says in a low voice that it will be all right, sir. “It will be entered,” says the Provost, and walks up the lane in the dark. He has been cold since the tenth of Garren, and he does not yet know why. On the evening of the second it comes out again, and regards Sir Bertran de Lanta, who has come down the lane as far as the gate with the first of his pearwood horses in his fist and no idea how to knock. He stands there for as long as it takes a bell to ring Prime, with the kitchen's lamplight on the gravel at his boots, and goes back up the lane. It takes no side. The manuscript gives it no sex, colour or build. The sheet's four years, large white body with a grey back, flat orange bill, small hard black eyes, orange legs, raised neck and hiss are its own, and its captions, Watchful, Severe, Hissing, Gatekeeper, are the nearest the file comes to its opinion of any of it.","i":"images/characters/goose-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Elis's Mother","u":"characters/elis-mother.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Mother of Elis · unnamed · Solan's Fire in the thirty-ninth year · 33 years old by her sheet","x":"Carves the feast pig, says that nago means a bite to eat, and then tells the one story she never believed. She is carving the pig. On the twenty-eighth of Havren, in the thirty-ninth year, at the evening feast of Solan's Fire in Vilarnau, her daughter Elis, six, has been given a small dish of roasted nago with salt by her great-grandmother and told that it is the gift of the gods; she eats it, considers the matter, and asks her mother what the word means and whether it has always been the name of the thing. Her mother, who is busy, says that it means a bite to eat, and that it is a funny old word and nobody knows where it came from. The girl says that it is a funny word for a locust. Her mother says that it is a funny sort of locust. Then, because the child is still looking at her with the large grave attention of a person who has been given part of an answer and wishes for the rest, she says the one thing she was told by her own mother, who was told it by someone who was told it by a girl called Gersenda, and that she has never believed: that it is a foreign word, given to the valley by a man who once came in from very far away, and was made welcome, and, this being the part that nobody in the family has ever been able to believe, went home. The manuscript gives her a pig, a knife and a daughter. The sheet's thirty-three years, strong five-foot-five build, oval flushed face, dark brown hair under a kerchief, hazel eyes, grease-spattered forearms and linen apron are its own, and so is the line it prints beside her, Nago meant a bite to eat.","i":"images/characters/elis-mother-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Vara","u":"characters/vara.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"The hermit of Val Muta · looks no older than thirty · true age unknown","x":"Eats salted river shrimp from a twist of paper, reads a drawing in the dust by the direction of its marks, and agrees. The people of Val Muta call him Vara, when they call him anything: a hermit in a high green hollow of the hills, in a season when something is in flower, in a shack of his own building at a river bend. He looks no older than thirty and is, by every account, a good deal less in a hurry. He lives beside the river because he likes the river, and eats salted river shrimp out of a twist of paper with the unhurried attention of a man for whom it is the main event of the day. He hears out the whole of the foreigner's account, the Stone, the Law, the hand that signs and a child asleep with her fist closed on a sleeve, in gestures, in the several hundred words of the Lauze dialect that Tomobe no Higaide has to give, and in a drawing in the dust of a road, a bridge, and two stones with a line between them. He reads it as a miller's daughter once read a ditch, not by the shape of the line but by the direction in which the marks are going. He agrees. His reasons are in no file, the manuscript says, since he is not a man who enters things. He comes back with the foreigner by the long road, and on an evening in a spring that nobody in the valley has marked as particular he is standing a little apart on the green at Vilarnau, a lean young-looking man eating something out of a twist of paper, while the village comes out and stands in a half-ring forty paces off. When Gersenda has said Two seconds and been answered, he finishes his shrimp, folds the twist of paper into a neat square, and kneels at the Stone with a short bent thing from his sleeve that nobody is looking at. What he does takes about as long as a bell takes to ring Prime, and nothing in it is visible; for the length of that dusk the Stone is not one-way. He asks the nearest person which way the river lies and is gone by morning. The manuscript gives him the name, the apparent age, the shrimp and the hollow. The sheet's slim five-foot-ten frame, unlined calm face, pale skin, pale grey eyes, fine white-silver hair, undyed wool jerkin and sheepskin vest are its own. Another file keeps a hermit of the same name, pale, white-haired and fond of river shrimp, in the forest north of the Muhlbach road in Case 0277 (Vara). Neither manuscript says they are one person; the two pages are linked on the strength of the name, the shrimp and the sheets, and that is an inference.","i":"images/characters/vara-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Genzō","u":"characters/genzo.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Steward of the Tomobe estate at Himuro · seen only in Higaide's memory, twenty-three years back · 55 years old as remembered, by his sheet","x":"Reads the weather in his knees, and digs a trench along the whole eastern bank against the red hoppers. A stolid grey man who reads the weather in his knees, steward of the Tomobe estate in the last season that Higaide's father held it in good standing, and he appears nowhere but in a memory. Higaide, eleven that summer, calls it up on the night of the twenty-sixth of Garren, at the smell of barley-chaff: the red-backed hoppers coming over the ridge from the dry country in an afternoon, turning the paddies the color of rust and setting the temple bell tolling of its own accord, which is the bell's way in that district of reporting a thing that no one can stop. The steward does not ring it. He puts every able hand in the district, a hundred and sixty men, women and boys, along the paddy dikes, and sets them to dig from the first hour of the afternoon until the last of the light a trench along the whole length of the eastern bank, a man's height deep and two paces wide, with the spoil thrown up on the outer side. At dusk, when the hoppers settle in the cold, he sets fires along the lip of it, small fires of damp straw and green brush that give more smoke than flame, with a line of boys with drums and the great clacking wooden rattles of the harvest-dance behind them; in the red dark the hoppers come to the light as moths come, and go down into the trench in tens of thousands, and are covered over with earth in the morning by the same hundred and sixty hands. It is the sending-off, the villagers say, and they burn a straw figure at the boundary stone. Twenty-three years later it is what Higaide draws with a stick on the green at Vilarnau, and the word for it in that country is fossa. After the Turning he bows to a dead grasshopper and eats it, recalling that he ate one once, at twelve, parched in a pan with salt and a little plum vinegar, from Genzō's own hand, in the year the weir was lost. The manuscript gives him two adjectives, a post, a shovel's worth of orders and his knees. The sheet's fifty-five years, square five-foot-three build, broad impassive face, iron-grey topknot, thin grey moustache and faded indigo hemp robe are its own.","i":"images/characters/genzo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Yō Sū","u":"characters/yo-su.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Minister of the Great Tang · referenced only, three centuries before the case · 60 years old by his sheet","x":"Dug pits and lit fires against the locusts, against the whole of his court, and was right. He is a name in a book. Higaide's father Nariyuki, a scholar in a family of soldiers, borrowed a brittle old book of the continent from the Left Ministry's library for the sake of its binding, and in it read the account of a minister of the Great Tang who faced a plague of locusts three centuries before, against the learned opinion of every scholar at his court, who held that insects were the heavens' business and not to be interfered with by a man; and who dug pits, and lit fires at night, and killed the creatures in their millions, and buried them, and was right. The name of the minister was Yō Sū. Nariyuki pronounced it to his son with a kind of private relish. A man who has read a thing in a book, he said, is permitted to be pleased when it works. That is all the manuscript has of him, and it reaches the valley of the Lauze through one boy's memory of holding a torch. Twenty-three years later it is the ditch Higaide draws on the green, and then the one he digs, and on the morning of the twenty-seventh of Havren, standing among the dead below the Dawn Wall, he sets what his father's book was worth against the bulk of the swarm and finds, with the cool clarity of an exact mind, that it is about a fiftieth part. The Log's own reckoning is that the works at the Throat held the swarm in the gorge five point one days longer than an open crossing would have, long enough for the strain to do what it does. He was right, as the minister was right, about the wrong thing. The manuscript gives Yō Sū a name, a country, an office and an act. The sheet's sixty years, medium five-foot-six build with a scholar's stoop, lean stern fair-skinned face, grey hair under a black cap, thin grey beard, dark eyes and dark-red official's robe are its own.","i":"images/characters/yo-su-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The news from the south","u":"records/the-news-from-the-south.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Ibrahim ibn Nasr, Arnaut","x":"A lean bearded wool-factor in a dust-striped cloak and a plain white cap sits at a plank table in a smoky inn holding a cup of red wine in both hands, a heavy man in a dark green wool gown with a ring of keys at his belt listening across the table with his back half turned to the viewer, drinkers blurred in the smoke behind them, a lit wall sconce at the upper left and a castle on a crag and a domed red roof glimpsed through the open doorway On the seventeenth of Garren, in the inn below the castle spur, Ibrahim ibn Nasr holds his cup of thin red wine in both hands and tells the bailiff Arnaut a sentence nobody will believe: the Red Weather has come up out of the south.","i":"images/scenes/the-news-from-the-south-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The stone at Mikusa","u":"records/the-stone-at-mikusa.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Higaide, The Kiyote Pair","x":"A tall lean man in a blue-black robe and a tall black cap bends over a waist-high grey granite block with his left palm flat on its top as a stream of pale glittering grain pours off his body and sleeves down frosted stone steps, a rope-hung wooden gate at the upper left, rows of white-robed priests in tall black caps standing on the steps behind him with one hand half raised, tall cedars and misty blue mountains under a pink sunrise At the grey of dawn on the twentieth of Garren Tomobe no Higaide lays his left palm on the Stone of Mikusa, and a pale grain like sand through the neck of a glass pours out of him into it, swords and all, before nine witnesses.","i":"images/scenes/the-stone-at-mikusa-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The bowl on the green","u":"records/the-bowl-on-the-green.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Higaide, The Kiyote Pair, The Smith of Vilarnau","x":"A tall lean man in a dark robe with a long sword across his knees sits upright on the end of a waist-high grey granite block beneath a great oak at night, a tiny old woman in a black headscarf and long apron crossing the grass toward him with a steaming bowl in both hands, a ragged half-ring of villagers holding rushlights behind her, a small boy with a switch at the right edge, and thatched roofs, a church spire and a thread of woodsmoke below a deep blue sky of stars On the night of the nineteenth of Garren a tiny old woman with a wooden spoon crosses the green at Vilarnau through the whole watching village and puts a bowl of something warm into the hands of Tomobe no Higaide, seated on the Stone; the smith and the rest stand forty paces off.","i":"images/scenes/the-bowl-on-the-green-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The lesson at the mill","u":"records/the-lesson-at-the-mill.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Gersenda, Ermengarda, Lo Rog, Higaide","x":"A young woman with a heavy dark braid and a gap-toothed smile sits on a bench holding up a wooden ladle toward a tall dark-haired man in a brown wool tunic with a dried gourd at his belt who sits with his head bowed and his hands open on his knees, a small tough woman in a white kerchief stirring an iron kettle at a glowing hearth behind them, a small fair boy at her elbow, strings of onions hanging from the beams and a huge rust-red ox looking in at the byre doorway at the back In the mill kitchen, in his second week in that country, Gersenda holds up a wooden ladle and says the word for it, slowly and flatly, while Ermengarda stirs the kettle, the ox Lo Rog looks in, and Tomobe no Higaide tries to say it back.","i":"images/scenes/the-lesson-at-the-mill-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"What the tavern knew","u":"records/what-the-tavern-knew.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Brunissen, Ibrahim ibn Nasr, Gaucelm, Observer 551","x":"A packed low-ceilinged tavern lit orange by a hearth and candles, a broad laughing woman in a dark bodice and white sleeves at the upper left holding up a mug, a lean bearded man in a white head-cloth and striped cloak at the centre with a cup of red wine glancing aside, a leathery man in a brown cloak holding a small tin disk up between finger and thumb, other drinkers leaning on elbows on benches and barrels, coins and tin cups on the stained table in the foreground and a window of dark blue night at the upper right On the first of Havren the tavern at Vilarnau holds forty on benches and barrels: Brunissen says somebody has to dig a hole, Ibrahim ibn Nasr has just told the room that in the south they eat the Weather, Gaucelm sells tin wards, and Observer 551, as a rat-catcher, listens by the door.","i":"images/scenes/what-the-tavern-knew-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The fire in the ditch","u":"records/the-fire-in-the-ditch.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Bertran de Lanta, Higaide","x":"Night in a gorge between black cliffs under a few cold stars, terraced lines of trench crossing the slope with long low lines of glowing fire and grey smoke along the lip of each, a river of rust-red grasshoppers pouring down the lit slope into a timber-edged trench in the foreground, a crowd of diggers with spades and sacks bent along the bank, a row of boys in white shirts with raised drums and rattles on the upper terrace, and at the right a lean man in a pale robe with a drawn sword beside a knight in dark mail swinging a burning brand On the twenty-sixth of Havren, with the line broken in the second rank at the Throat, Sir Bertran goes down into the gap with a burning brand and Tomobe no Higaide with a drawn tachi, and the two hold it for the whole of a night.","i":"images/scenes/the-fire-in-the-ditch-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Dawn Wall","u":"records/the-dawn-wall.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Higaide, Gersenda, Ottavio Brandi, Matteo, Odilo","x":"Sunrise in a gorge: a cliff face four hundred feet high covered ledge by ledge with dead red grasshoppers gripping the rock with wings spread, lit gold by the low sun, a bright beam from a great glass lens on a tripod on a plank platform under a grey awning falling on the wall, a dark-robed man and a pale young clerk at the lens, a red-robed man kneeling among kneeling peasants in the gravel, and in the foreground a lean man in a dark robe with a sword held down along his leg holding up one stiff red body beside a young woman with a braid in a white blouse and blue skirt who looks up, the gravel strewn with the dead On the morning of the twenty-seventh of Havren the Dawn Wall hangs with the dead, and Tomobe no Higaide holds one up and says inago to Gersenda, while Ottavio Brandi and Matteo turn the ground lens on the wall and the Hierarch Odilo kneels in the gravel.","i":"images/scenes/the-dawn-wall-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The long graves","u":"records/the-long-graves.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Higaide, Joan, Gersenda","x":"A snow-covered field behind a Gothic refectory wall on a grey winter morning, a lean man in a mud-stained brown tunic with a sword at his hip bending over a deep sloped trench and paying out a knotted cord along it, grey-habited lay-brothers digging in the black earth beyond, a tall young lay-brother with an open book standing at the margin, a young woman with her arms folded and a flour sack at her hip watching at the right, carts and horses with children sitting on top drawn up along the wall at the upper left, breath fogging, and a cathedral spire on the misty horizon In the second winter, behind the refectory at Sant-Sever, Tomobe no Higaide lays out the trench for the famine's dead with a knotted cord; Joan counts the buried at the margin of his page, and Gersenda hears the word said anyway.","i":"images/scenes/the-long-graves-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The cairn on the ridge","u":"records/the-cairn-on-the-ridge.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Higaide","x":"Autumn rain on a ridge: a long low storehouse of squared logs raised on stout wooden posts with a pegged wooden door stands against a heavy grey sky, three boys and a girl in rain-dark ragged wool carry grey fieldstones to a waist-high cairn in the foreground, wet russet bracken fills the slope, and a shepherd in a hooded cloak leans on a crook beside a dry-stone wall with a dog at his heel at the right, a river valley and a thread of smoke falling away behind them By the fifth winter the boys of three hamlets are carrying fieldstones up the ridge to raise a waist-high cairn over nobody, beside the log storehouse Tomobe no Higaide built and left pegged shut from the outside; a shepherd watches from the wall.","i":"images/scenes/the-cairn-on-the-ridge-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The hermit of Val Muta","u":"records/the-hermit-of-val-muta.html","m":"Case 0202 · Fossa","d":"Vara, Higaide","x":"In a green hollow beside a bright river bend under willows, a pale slim man with white hair in an undyed wool jerkin and a sheepskin vest sits on a log outside a log shack eating a shrimp from a paper twist in his lap, a folded square of paper on the ground beside his foot, while a lean grey-templed man in a brown tunic with two swords at his hip kneels in the dust and draws with one finger a winding road, a bridge and two small stones with a line between them, wildflowers in the grass and a wicker basket and a pole against the shack In a green hollow of Val Muta the hermit Vara eats salted river shrimp from a twist of paper while Tomobe no Higaide kneels in the dust and draws a road, a bridge and two stones with a line between them; the hermit agrees.","i":"images/scenes/the-hermit-of-val-muta-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Uncounted Dinar","u":"books/3001.html","m":"Case 3001 · Published","d":"One gold coin that kept coming back, and the war two confederations paid for the wrong man.","x":"Case 3001 Fiction / Fantasy / Epic Malik ibn Sabir owns four goats, a threadbare tent his father died in, and nothing else a creditor could claim, and on the night the story opens he splits his last meal with a stranger who asks for nothing. At dawn the stranger presses a single gold dinar into his palm and tells him he will not be able to keep it or to lose it. Spent, it is back in his hand by morning, notch and all. Given away, before both families and the keeper of the well, as part of the mahr for Amina bint Rashid, it passes to her by a witnessed akad and answers to her alone. For a season they are simply happy. Then Amina finds out what the coin will do for exactly as long as she wants it to, and begins, quietly, to price a way to the sea. She is murdered in her tent by a thief named Zaid, a stranger to her, to her clan and to the quarrel about to be built on the assumption that he was not. Her kinsmen decide that the shepherd with the unexplainable fortune must be the killer. Sulayman ibn Ghanim, her uncle, finds the case thin from the first night and gives the order anyway, because three of his own nephews are already riding. Malik is seized at the edge of the camp and held for two years while the Banu Harith and the Banu Kathir fight a war partly in his name. The war costs some three hundred lives and never once asks him the one question that would have cleared him: where he was the night she died. The thief spends the coin on wine and rented rooms until his own excess kills him, and in the river-city wine-house where he dies Nur, who has heard his whole confession, takes the coin within the hour. She holds the truth for nearly two years, with no name of her own that anyone would believe, until Qasim al-Fahmi, a scholar who has spent five years following the coin's history, comes asking. He writes her account down that night, and only afterward decides what to do with the coin. The Uncounted Dinar closes on a Cultivator's own archival log: Observer 1140, who is not supposed to have a case he returns to at rest, objects on the record to calling the drop a non-intervention, and a provincial archive clerk files the whole war under the title Grazing Rights Dispute of the Two Confederations."},{"k":"s","t":"Malik ibn Sabir","u":"characters/malik-ibn-sabir.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"Shepherd of the Banu Kathir · four goats, a threadbare tent and nothing else · first owner of the dinar · held two years for a murder no one asked him about · early-to-mid twenties by his sheet","x":"Shares his last meal with a stranger, is given a coin he can neither keep nor lose, and spends two years in another clan's custody without anyone asking where he was. A shepherd of the Banu Kathir who, on the night the story opens, owns four goats, a threadbare tent his father died in, and nothing else that could be weighed, counted or claimed by a creditor. His mother died bringing him into the world and his father nine years later, of a fever no physician came far enough into the waste to treat; he was raised, the manuscript says, by the flock itself, and fills the silence with verse, muttered to the goats at dusk in the cadence his father used for counting debts, so that even his poetry keeps a kind of ledger. A stranger, Observer 1140, comes down from the northern scarp after dark carrying nothing and asking for nothing, and Malik, who has planned to eat nothing again until the goats are sold at the spring market, splits what he has and gives the stranger the larger half without appearing to notice. Near dawn the stranger presses a dinar into his palm and tells him he will not be able to keep it or to lose it. He keeps it eleven days before hunger sends him to the oasis town to settle a grain advance with Hammad, and the coin is back in the middle of his palm the next morning. After three returns he cuts a small notch into its rim with his herding-knife before spending it again, and the notch comes back, in the same place, every time. He gives the coin to Amina as part of her mahr, with four goats (by then six) and a length of good rope, before both families and the keeper of the nearest well, and for a season or two they are simply happy: he recites his ledger-cadenced verses to her instead of to the goats. When she leaves him, in precise legal words, he asks only Was any part of it real? and she answers, All of it, until it was not. He keeps a composure the clan admires through the packing of her cedar chest and her uncles' over-polite negotiations, and breaks nine days later, alone, over a lamb born early and dead within the hour, making a sound he would refuse for the rest of his life to describe. The news of her murder reaches him three days late, from a trader who mentions it like weather. He says nothing and asks nothing, and walks eleven miles through open desert at midday, without water, to the edge of the Banu Harith grazing grounds to wait to be let in to grieve her. They do not let him. Men his own age, who wrestled beside him at festival gatherings a decade before, seize him, bind him with a rope meant for goats and leave him sitting in the worst heat of the day while Sulayman decides what justice requires. He is held two years, alive, unjudged and increasingly unimportant to a war fought partly in his name, and in all that time no one asks him where he was the night she died. He does not scheme or smuggle word out. He teaches himself to recite silently every verse he ever muttered to the flock, adding a line for every rumor of the dead that reaches even a captive's ears, until the poem no one will hear is longer than anything a trained reciter would dare perform at a festival fire. When a cousin bribes a guard for ten minutes and offers a night raid, his answer is one word, no, the only word of the captivity anyone thought worth remembering. Released on the garrison captain's order, eleven days after Qasim's sworn account reaches him, without apology or compensation, he walks out older, quieter and permanently missing whatever part of a person allows them to trust a crowd's certainty again. He is, the manuscript says, the one person the coin never had time to test: he had given it away before the question it asked everyone else who held it longer could reach him. He spends the rest of his life repaying the kinsmen who kept his grazing grounds alive, one lambing season at a time, and never mentions the coin to anyone who has not already heard the story from someone else. He marries once more, quietly, a widow from a clan the war never touched, teaches three children to speak in his father's debt-counting cadence without telling them why, and lives to be an old man whom his grandchildren know as gentle and unhurried, with a habit of counting things twice. When Qasim finds him years later, himself old, under the same cover story, and gives him a dead thief's name and the four sentences of a confession that freed him, but nothing about the coin, Malik thanks him with more grace than the news deserves after so many years and asks him to stay for the evening meal. The sheet draws him lean and rope-muscled, of middling height, with deep sun-browned skin, rough herder's hands with an old scar across one palm, short black hair that goes grey and, in an old-age panel, white, a close unstyled beard and dark quiet eyes, in undyed dusty wool and a weathered sand-and-cream headcloth, with a herding-knife and a coil of rope. The manuscript gives him no age, scar or colouring; those and the age band are the sheet's. The sheet also puts a large flock of sheep behind him, where the manuscript gives him four goats, later six.","i":"images/characters/malik-ibn-sabir-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Amina bint Rashid","u":"characters/amina-bint-rashid.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"Daughter of the Banu Harith · Malik's wife and the dinar's second owner · twenty-two when she finds the coin answers to her, twenty-three by her sheet · murdered on the twenty-sixth of Deyren","x":"Learns the coin will answer to her for exactly as long as she wants it to, spends eleven months pricing a way to the sea, and is murdered by a stranger who has never heard her name. Born two wells south of Malik's grazing grounds among the Banu Harith, in a family with slightly more goats and considerably more opinions about how a daughter ought to be disposed of. She comes to the marriage, arranged by uncles who never asked her a question to which they expected an honest answer, with a cedar chest, her mother's recipe for bread and an unconfessed hunger for the sea, which she has only heard of in the boasts of a single trader. The akad is witnessed by both families and the aging keeper of the nearest well. For her mahr Malik offers what he has, the four goats (now six), a length of good rope and the dinar, and from that hour, by every law the desert recognizes, the coin belongs to her and to no one else living. A lung-sickness in the flock forces it from the chest, and she spends it herself, without asking Malik, which is her right under the akad, on medicine from the coast-trading caravans. It is back in the chest by the next dusk, and she sits alone in the tent understanding that whatever the coin is, it answers to her now, absolutely, for exactly as long as she wants it to. She tests the thought, the manuscript says, carefully, the way a tongue tests a loose tooth. She has never in twenty-two years made a decision another person's permission did not shadow, and she does with this door what a practical woman does: she prices it. She asks a passing apprentice wool-factor, Ibrahim ibn Nasr, what a berth on a coastal caravan costs; he gives her the figure to the last coin and asks nothing in return. In the same cedar chest she begins to keep a small store of her own: a handful of ordinary coins she has earned rather than spent from the dinar, a scrap of blue coastal cloth he let her handle and named no price for, and the written price of a berth in a hand only she can read. She does not leave cruelly. On a cold morning she tells Malik that she requires nothing further from the akad between them and will trouble neither his ledger nor his name again, legal words chosen deliberately because precision was the last kindness she knew how to offer. The coin leaves with her, as the akad requires. On the twenty-sixth of Deyren a thief named Zaid, a stranger to her, to her clan and to the quarrel that will be built on the assumption that he was not, cuts her throat in her tent and strips it of everything of value. Her kinsmen find her three days later. Worthless to the thief and so left behind, the scrap of blue cloth and the written price lie in the tent, and no one who finds them understands that they are the last evidence of a woman who had been quietly planning, for eleven months, to go somewhere none of them would have thought to look. Of her the poets keep exactly one detail accurately, by accident: her name. In their versions she is faithless, or wronged, or a sorceress, or a saint, or erased from a story that prefers the war to the woman it claims to have been fought for, and everyone who afterward claims to be avenging her spends more energy avenging their own certainty than asking what she might have wanted. The only person in either country who could have said what she wanted is Ibrahim ibn Nasr, who knows whose figure he once gave for a berth, and nobody asks him. The sheet draws her slight and wiry, a herder's build, with deep olive-brown sun-weathered hands and forearms, dark brown direct eyes and black hair in one long oiled braid, in a deep indigo Banu Harith dress with embroidered cuffs and a woven belt, a headcloth, a necklace of old silver coins and a herding-knife; a small burn scar shows on one forearm. The manuscript gives her no appearance and no age beyond the twenty-two; the scar, the necklace and the age of twenty-three are the sheet's. Its pull-quote is the manuscript's narration of her, not a line she speaks. Carefully, the way a tongue tests a loose tooth.","i":"images/characters/amina-bint-rashid-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sulayman ibn Ghanim","u":"characters/sulayman-ibn-ghanim.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"Leader of the Banu Harith for nineteen years · Amina's uncle, her father's own brother · orders Malik's seizure while doubting the case · about fifty-eight to sixty-two by his sheet","x":"Doubts the case against Malik from the first night, gives the order anyway because his own nephews are already riding, and spends two years failing to act on what he suspects. Leader of the Banu Harith for nineteen years, a steadiness never tested by anything larger than a bad drought until the morning the news of Amina's death arrives. He is her father's own brother and, since her father died the winter before her wedding, the nearest thing to a father she had left. He receives the news the way he receives all news that matters, standing perfectly still for as long as it takes the messenger to finish speaking and a good while longer; those who know him have never seen him age so visibly in so short a span. He leads by making the hard decision first and explaining it, if pressed, once and briefly. Away from his councils, the younger members who answer to him know him mostly for spoiling his youngest granddaughter with a patience he never showed his own sons. He decides alone, in a tent where he will not let anyone light a fire, while Malik sits bound in the heat. He finds the case thinner than his advisors need it to be: a divorced husband is an obvious suspect, and obvious suspects are wrong at least as often as they are right. He turns the evidence over in the dark and cannot find a version of events that does not require him to send men to kill a shepherd on the strength of a grudge no one can prove he acted on. By the second day three of his own nephews, who loved their cousin and wanted someone to be sorry, have ridden out on their own authority to seize a Banu Kathir well in reprisal. He rides out after them alone and brings them back, with a threat he would later say was the only one he ever made that he was not entirely certain he could carry out. It is this, more than any theory of justice, that narrows his choices: men carrying his own name are already moving whether he judges the case or not. On the fourth sleepless night he understands that the Banu Harith can survive being wrong about who killed her, and cannot survive being seen to do nothing. He gives the order. Through the war that follows he makes one private visit, the only one in two years: to a Banu Harith family whose season's stored grain the Banu Kathir have burned in reprisal, a household that had taken no side until that night, and he says nothing to anyone about the fact that its youngest granddaughter is almost exactly the age of his own. When word of Malik's release, and the reason for it, reaches him he is silent a long while, and then says only, I suspected it. The first night. I have had two years to decide that changes nothing, and I have failed at that decision every day since. It changes, the manuscript says, absolutely nothing about the three hundred lives already spent believing otherwise. The sheet draws him tall and upright despite his years, a leader's bearing rather than a herder's stoop, deeply lined and composed under strain, with close-cropped white hair beneath an ivory headcloth with a black band, a full groomed white beard, dark steady eyes and deep sun-browned skin, in finer wool robes in deep blue and cream with gold embroidery and a ceremonial blade of rank he rarely draws; its panels show him stern in council, grieving at the news of Amina, concerned in the war and gentle with a small granddaughter. The manuscript gives no age or appearance, so the age band and every visual detail are the sheet's. One panel and one line of the sheet's text place his private visit with a Banu Kathir family his raiders had wronged. The manuscript's family is a Banu Harith family whose grain the Banu Kathir burned, and this entry follows the manuscript.","i":"images/characters/sulayman-ibn-ghanim-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Nur","u":"characters/nur.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"Sold into a river-city wine-house at fourteen · the dinar's fourth owner · later a widow of independent means three provinces from anywhere · late twenties by her sheet","x":"Listens to a dying man's whole confession without comment, takes the coin from his body, buys back her own debt, and waits two years for someone who wants the truth badly enough to be honest about why. Sold into the service of a wine-house in a caravan city by a river at fourteen, and long past expecting kindness from any man who paid for her time. She learned early that the men who talk most freely are rarely worth answering honestly, and she files what a client lets slip in the dark without comment, without visible reaction and without forgetting a word. When Zaid arrives, with sudden gold and the new discovery that money makes a very attentive audience of people who would otherwise have no reason to listen, he tells her everything over several weeks: the coin, the woman in the desert, the shepherd everyone will blame instead of him. She says very little in return. He is found dead on the nineteenth of Havren, face-down in a room he had paid for with a coin that always found its way back into his pocket, and within the hour she has searched him and taken it, not from greed alone but because she had listened to a dying man's whole confession and understood, better than he ever did, exactly what she was holding. Within a week of leaving the river city she walks into the wine-house's counting-room with the coin and buys back her own bond, which thirty years of ordinary wages could not have touched; the owner counts it twice, weighs it once and asks no questions. She settles in a village three provinces from anywhere Zaid ever named, as a widow of independent means, the only story anyone thought worth checking. She spends the coin rarely and only on small things, which the village reads as thrift and she understands as penance: she does not want to build a second fortune on a dead woman's dower, and the coin's insistence on returning to her feels some nights like a debt collector who will accept nothing except the one thing she cannot give back. In her first months there she composes, in her very limited letters, an unsigned account of what Zaid confessed, and burns it unsent the same night, having understood what an anonymous letter from an unnamed widow would be read as. What she needs is not a truth but a truth-teller. Nearly two years after Zaid's death a traveling scholar who calls himself a collector of old coinage comes asking after a dinar with a particular history, a lie so thin she sees through it in one conversation, though she says nothing. On the second evening she tells him she has been waiting for someone to arrive who wanted it badly enough to be honest about why. The akad that passes the coin to Qasim is witnessed by the village well-keeper and a passing grain-merchant, and before he leaves she gives him the name of the man who killed Amina, and the plain fact that an innocent shepherd has spent two years in another clan's custody while several hundred people died in his name. She lives out her life in the same village, known as a widow of quiet and slightly mysterious means, and tells no one else, not a neighbor and not a friend she makes in her last years, what a dying thief confessed to her in the dark, or what she did afterward with the one truth that could have ended the war eighteen months sooner than it ended, had she known to whom it should be told. The sheet draws her slight and deliberately unremarkable, with a composed, carefully neutral face, dark watchful eyes, dark plain covered hair and an olive, softly sun-browned complexion, in a charcoal head-covering and muted clay-and-linen dress; its panels show her listening, in guarded silence, with a buried truth, in resolve, kneeling over Zaid's body with the coin, and standing above a hill village in her widow's years. The manuscript gives her no age or appearance, so the age band, colouring and clothing are the sheet's. The sheet's title calls her the dinar's third legitimate owner; by the manuscript's five holders and five transfers she is the fourth, after Malik, Amina and Zaid, and this entry follows the manuscript.","i":"images/characters/nur-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Qasim al-Fahmi","u":"characters/qasim-al-fahmi.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"Scholar · the dinar's fifth owner · keeper of a catalogue of pressed desert wildflowers since boyhood · twenty-seven to twenty-nine by his sheet","x":"Meets a miracle with an inventory, spends five years following one coin through five hands, sends the truth north the night he learns it, and then puts the coin beyond any hand's reach. A scholar whose long and only moderately respected career rests on the conviction that impossible stories are almost always true stories badly told, and that the correct response to a miracle is neither worship nor dismissal but inventory: where it was seen, in what order, in whose hands, and what changed hands alongside it each time it moved. He first hears of the coin five years before he holds it, as a much younger scholar in a date-market three wells east of Malik's grazing grounds, where children are chanting a rhyme about a coin that will not stay in any grave; he dismisses it. What catches him a year later is not the impossibility but the coincidence of a second report, from another province, of a different object with the same property. He keeps, and talks about with a warmth he brings to nothing else in his professional life, a private catalogue of desert wildflowers begun in boyhood, in which the small yellow ones wear their petals in threes and fives and eights and thirteens and never in fours or sevens, a regularity he once spent a season of evenings failing to explain and decided was simply how the desert counted. He builds the inventory over five years in the margins of unrelated manuscripts and on the backs of letters he never sends, following the coin through a shepherd's marriage and divorce, a murdered woman, a war he watches from a careful distance take the shape he had more or less predicted, and a dead thief's mistress, and he arrives at last in Nur's village as a collector of old coinage. She is waiting for him. The akad is witnessed by the village well-keeper and a passing grain-merchant, and she gives him the thief's name and the fact of an innocent man held for almost two years. He writes the account that night, before he lets himself look again at the coin in his bag, and has it riding north by a paid courier before the week is out; within the month a notarized account under his own seal reaches the garrison captain, who spends eleven days confirming what he can before he releases Malik. Only then does he test the coin. He scores its face with a blade and watches it return as scarred as he left it; he spends it nine times in nine towns in one season and it returns nine mornings running; he gives a very large sum to four almshouses in a week, waiting with something close to anxiety to learn whether pure generosity will break the rule, and it comes back anyway. He concludes that it recognizes exactly two events, a witnessed akad or an owner's death passing it to whichever hand next closes around it, and nothing else. Wealth, he decides, was not one test but a different test for each hand: one Zaid failed outright, one Amina had too little time to finish, and one he suspects he is still taking himself. If the rule is bound to a single nameable object, then unmaking the object past recognition should void the rule. He does not go to Malik, because a man once accused of murdering for this coin would not survive the verses that would follow if a scholar traced its whole history back to him and delivered it into his hands a second time. He melts it over three days in a smith's forge he pays for, swearing the smith to secrecy with a sum the man will be grateful for and mystified by for the rest of his life, then carries the cooled metal three days further into open waste and scatters it handful by handful across a dune that changes shape every season. He sits through the following dawn with both pockets turned inside out, and when noon passes with his hands still empty, he lets himself feel something he would not have called relief in front of anyone who might have written it down. Years later, old himself and under the same cover story, he finds Malik at a modest grazing ground and tells him the one thing he never dared send by letter, a dead thief's name and the four sentences of a confession that freed him, and does not mention the coin. Malik asks him to stay for the evening meal, which neither man thinks to note is the second time a stranger has been fed at that poor man's fire without being told why he was welcome. The sheet draws him in his late twenties, slight and unathletic, a scholar's build, with a long, thoughtful face prone to a slight private smile, dark hair greying steadily across its panels and white by his last visit, dark patient eyes, an olive and less sun-worn complexion, a neat trimmed beard and ink-stained fingers, in plain beige-and-sand robes under a blue-grey cloak, with a brown leather satchel and boots; the satchel holds manuscripts, letters and the wildflower catalogue. The manuscript gives no age, so the band and the greying are the sheet's. The sheet's title calls him the dinar's fourth owner; by the manuscript's five holders and five transfers he is the fifth, and this entry follows the manuscript.","i":"images/characters/qasim-al-fahmi-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Dinar","u":"characters/the-dinar.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"Catalyst, Case 3,001 · a single struck gold coin · one face worn smooth · five holders, five transfers","x":"Returns to its latest legitimate owner the morning after it is spent, changes hands only by a witnessed word or an owner's death, and is ended by a scholar who breaks it past recognition. A single struck gold dinar of standard weight and size, small enough to close inside one fist, full-weight and unclipped under every coin-weigher who ever tests it, and in every particular a perfectly unremarkable coin, which is exactly the trouble. One face is worn smooth in a way that suggests long handling by hands that were not, Malik would later insist, entirely human. Observer 1140 gives it to Malik at dawn after a shared meal: You will not be able to keep it, but you will not be able to lose it either. Spend it as you please. It has already decided who it belongs to. Spent, it is back with its latest legitimate owner the following morning: in the exact center of Malik's palm after Hammad has dropped it into his strongbox; in Amina's cedar chest by the next dusk, though a caravan's chief factor a province away finds himself one dinar short, spends a week recounting and writes it off as his own error. The notch Malik cuts into its rim comes back in the same place at the same shallow angle every time, as if the coin that returned were the very same disk of gold, walking home each night by some road no eye could follow. The rule behind it is not goodness, wealth or fate. After five years of inventory and nine test spends in nine towns, Qasim finds that it recognizes exactly two events. A witnessed akad passes it cleanly and permanently from one living owner to the next, as from Malik to Amina and from Nur to Qasim. Absent a word, an owner's death passes it to whoever's hand next closes around it, with no consent asked, which is how a frightened bandit and a wine-house woman each became as legitimate an owner as a shepherd's bride. Generosity does not break it, since a large sum given to four almshouses in a week came back anyway, and neither does damage: a scar Qasim cuts into it returns exactly as he left it, which tells him that the rule tracks whether one whole, nameable thing continues to exist, not what condition that thing is in. The folk story that it is the Coin With No Grave, which no murderer could keep, is wrong in a precise way, since by the coin's own rule a man who killed its owner simply became its next legitimate one. By the Custodian log's count it passes through five holders, Malik, Amina, Zaid, Nur and Qasim, in five transfers, a count that excludes the many spend-and-return cycles in which it left and re-entered the same owner's possession. It ends in the dunes. Qasim, acting entirely outside any Cultivator direction, melts it over three days in a smith's forge into a formless, ordinary puddle of gold, carries the cooled metal three days further into open waste, to a stretch of dune that changes its shape every season, and scatters it in pieces too small for any fragment to be gathered whole. He sits through the following dawn with his palm open on his knee, and by noon his hands are still empty. Gone. The sheet follows it from the Envoy's hand to Malik's, from Malik to Amina in the marriage akad, to Zaid after Amina's death and to Nur after Zaid's, and shows it in hand, front and back, in edge view, and as a small scatter of gold on the desert floor. It draws two marks on the rim, Malik's notch and a deeper testing scar from Qasim. The manuscript says Qasim scored the coin's face, not its rim, so the scar's place is the sheet's own. The reverse, drawn with a ring of lettering, is not described in the manuscript, which gives the coin only its weight, its color, its worn face and its milled edge.","i":"images/characters/the-dinar-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Observer 1140","u":"characters/observer-1140.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"Field designation “the Empty-Handed” · Personal Index 1140 · Cultivator Envoy of record, Case 3001 · his twentieth field posting · reassigned to Case 3014","x":"Arrives at a poor man's fire carrying nothing, is fed everything the man has, leaves a coin that cannot be lost, and files a record whose completeness is the only repayment an Envoy is permitted. A Cultivator field agent whose disguise in this case is a traveler who owns nothing: stooped, robed in something the color of old ash, carrying no staff, no waterskin and no bag, and leaving no track in sand that otherwise holds tracks for weeks. He comes down from the northern scarp two hours after dark and stands at the edge of Malik's firelight the way a man stands who has trained himself out of the habit of asking. He is fed the larger half of the shepherd's last meal, says almost nothing all night, and near dawn presses a dinar into Malik's palm with the only words he speaks: You will not be able to keep it, but you will not be able to lose it either. Spend it as you please. It has already decided who it belongs to. Malik will spend the better part of a decade unable to decide whether he dreamed him. His voice is the Custodian Office's, in field notes that interrupt the story at the end of the first two parts. The first, written at baseline contact, rates Malik an ideal low-floor test case, one fixed asset class of livestock, no dependents and no formally acknowledged debt, and adds that the subject shared food he did not have enough of, which is noted for completeness and carries no evaluative weight. It then admits that Malik's verse, recited to his animals in the cadence of his dead father's debt-counting, moved him: I am not required to note that I found this, in a term my training does not strictly authorize me to use, moving. I have noted it anyway. The second note, written once the propagation has exceeded projections and become a war, says that he has been asked in nineteen earlier field postings to log outcomes considerably worse, and that he is not supposed to have one he returns to at rest: I do not know why it is this one. The closing log is his as well. Its Primary Finding blames the culture's own evidentiary standards under stress, and its Closing Note then argues against the finding: that a single object dropped into a system already assessed as primed for cascading failure was never a passive act merely because no hand was raised afterward, and that raising the point appears to change nothing about how it is received. He flags Qasim's unprompted risk-assessment as convergent reasoning, records that Malik, outside every modeled terminal state for a baseline contact, lived to a natural old age and was apparently content, and notes that the trader Ibrahim ibn Nasr, the one subject no party thought to question, turns up thirty-four years later in a different file, Case 0202 (Observer 551). He is reassigned to Case 3014 with his personal notes sealed, at his own request, for longer than protocol requires, on the theory that the subject fed a stranger everything he had on the first night of the case, under a code that made the size of the debt no smaller for the stranger's silence since, and that an unusually complete record, however irregular, is the only repayment an Envoy is permitted to make. The sheet notes that the manuscript gives real physical description here, stooped, ash-robed, empty-handed and trackless, more than for almost any other figure, and that only hair, eyes and facial hair are invented: a late middle-aged to elderly man of unremarkable middling height, with an ash-toned face that is difficult to fix in memory, grey hair close beneath a hood, dark watchful eyes, ashen skin and a short unkempt grey beard, in a single unornamented ash-grey robe with no visible equipment. Its expression panels run neutral, observant, faint sadness and unreadable. You will not be able to keep it, but you will not be able to lose it either. Spend it as you please. It has already decided who it belongs to.","i":"images/characters/observer-1140-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Hammad","u":"characters/hammad.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"Coin-weigher of the oasis town · informal creditor to Malik, holder of his grain advance · late fifties by his sheet","x":"Weighs the dinar, takes it in payment of a shepherd's debt, and keeps quiet about a coin that will not stay spent, because a coin-weigher fooled by a shepherd soon has no customers. The coin-weigher of the oasis town, who keeps, in a ledger of his own devising, not sums but characters, so that a man's name in Hammad's book says less about what he owes than about what Hammad has already decided he is. He weighs on a scale whose brass pans have gone the color of a healing bruise and keeps his takings in a strongbox with an iron lock he trusts the way other men trust their own sons. To travelers he is fond of saying that debt is the one law in this country that never sleeps, because unlike every other law a man has ever broken, this one always calls it in, and always at compound interest, a joke he has made so many times he has stopped finding it funny, though never true. When Malik comes to settle a grain advance from the previous winter, Hammad weighs the dinar, bites it, weighs it again against a standard he trusts more than he trusts most men, and pronounces it full-weight and unclipped. He gives Malik three coins of lesser value and strikes the debt from the ledger. The shepherd then keeps spending a coin he somehow still possesses, and Hammad says nothing about it to anyone, not from loyalty, since he is loyal to no one, a fact he considers his greatest professional asset, but because a coin-weigher who admits to being fooled by a shepherd will shortly have no customers left to fool, and he had done the arithmetic on his own reputation before he finished the arithmetic on Malik's coin. The silence does not hold. By the time the story has reached the date-market three wells east it is no longer about a shepherd but about the coin, and market children who have never seen it are calling it the Coin With No Grave. The manuscript sets beside his silence the silence of a caravan's chief factor, who finds himself a dinar short a province away and writes it off as his own mistake. The sheet draws him in his late fifties, stocky and slightly stooped, with a broad, deeply lined face, a habitual squint from years of reading small weights, close-cropped grey hair, a short grey beard, dark almost-black eyes and weathered olive-brown skin, in undyed dusty-grey wool, with ink-stained fingers. Around him it sets patinated brass scale pans, a locked iron strongbox and an open ledger whose entries are characters, not sums, a private shorthand of judgment on every man who has ever owed him money; its expression panels run appraising, calculating, indifferent (protecting his reputation) and satisfied (debt confirmed). The age band, the ink-stained fingers and the sheet's note that he witnesses the coin's first proven return the next morning are the sheet's; the manuscript says only that he knew a shepherd kept spending a coin he somehow kept. The first sentence of the pull-quote is the manuscript's; the rest of it is the sheet's own and does not appear in the manuscript. Debt is the one law in this country that never sleeps. Kings rise, tribes move, camels die of thirst in the sun, but a man's debt follows him into every shadow he tries to hide in.","i":"images/characters/hammad-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Malik's Cousin","u":"characters/maliks-cousin.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"Cousin of Malik ibn Sabir, Banu Kathir · unnamed · offers a night raid in the second year of captivity · mid-to-late twenties by the sheet","x":"Bribes a guard for ten minutes alone with a captive cousin, offers a night raid with one chance in three, and takes his silence as the whole of the answer. A cousin of Malik, of the Banu Kathir, who in the second year of Malik's captivity bribes a guard for ten minutes alone with him. The cousin comes only to ask whether Malik wants them to try a night raid to free him outright, a plan with perhaps one chance in three of working and considerably worse odds of not costing four more lives in the attempt. Malik says no, the only word he is recorded as having said in two years of captivity that anyone thought worth remembering, and then says nothing else for the remaining ten minutes, which his cousin takes, correctly, as the whole of the answer. It is the only attempt to reach Malik himself that the manuscript records, set against the war fought in his name; the kinsmen who kept his grazing grounds alive in his absence, it says, never once managed to confirm to their own satisfaction whether he deserved it. The manuscript gives the cousin no name, age or gender and never says what came of the visit beyond Malik's answer. The sheet draws a wiry, quick rider's build in the mid-to-late twenties, with black hair tied back for riding, dark restless eyes, sun-browned skin, a thin beard still filling in and a callus on the reins hand, in dusty weathered brown riding leathers and a charcoal-black travel cloak. Its expression panels run burdened impatience, resolve, restlessness and patience, and a three-part strip titled The Ten Minutes shows the cousin meeting a spear-bearing guard at a lantern-lit stockade, stepping into the cell, and riding away at dusk. Everything visual, the age band and the sheet's title are the sheet's; the manuscript says only that a cousin bribed a guard.","i":"images/characters/maliks-cousin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Smith of the Dunes","u":"characters/the-smith-of-the-dunes.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"Smith hired by Qasim al-Fahmi · unnamed · melts the dinar over three days · in his forties by the sheet","x":"Melts a coin he is never told the story of, is paid to say nothing about it, and spends the rest of his life grateful and mystified. A smith whose forge Qasim pays for, and whom Qasim then swears to secrecy with a sum the smith will spend the rest of his life being grateful for and mystified by. In that forge, over three days, a dinar that survived a marriage, a murder, a war and a dead man's entire fortune in vice is reduced to a formless, ordinary puddle of gold that any coin-weigher in the world would have recognized as nothing more than what it had always, technically, been. The manuscript gives him no name, place, age or word of speech, does not say whether he or Qasim worked the metal, and does not say what he was told, what he guessed, or whether he ever tried to learn why a man would pay such a sum to unmake one coin. The sheet gives him a body, a place and the work. It draws him heavily built, with forge-thick forearms, a broad, soot-marked, sun- and fire-burned face, deep brown scarred skin, very short greying hair, dark eyes and a close-trimmed singed beard, in a scorched, patched leather apron over ash-grey working cloth, with blackened, burn-scarred knuckles and old forge burns up the forearms. A three-part strip shows the melt as an initial melt on the first day, a refining on the second and pure gold on the third; another shows a hand passing a pouch, labeled Paid for Silence; a last shows him at his forge before a palm-ringed town of low roofs. His expression panels run pride (quiet craft), curiosity (suppressed), gratitude (uneasy) and mystification (lingering). The age band, the day-by-day melt, the town and the apron are the sheet's; the manuscript sets the forge in no town.","i":"images/characters/the-smith-of-the-dunes-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Sentry of Banu Kathir","u":"characters/the-sentry-of-banu-kathir.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"A young sentry of the Banu Kathir · unnamed · kills the first man of the war · fifteen to seventeen by the sheet","x":"Has only ever held a spear against a jackal, and kills the Banu Harith's young rider in a panic his mind never caught up with. A Banu Kathir sentry half the age of the rider he kills, who has never before held a spear against a man instead of a jackal. The rider, young himself and sent by the Banu Harith to demand that the Banu Kathir elders formally ratify a judgment already begun without them, is the first death of the war, not Malik's, though every man on both sides would have told you in the war's later months that it should have been, and he is killed not by design but by panic. The sentry will spend the rest of a long life, which the desert, the manuscript notes, spared him, unable to explain fully, even to himself, why his hand did what it did a full second before his mind had finished deciding whether it should. That single death demands, by the same honor-arithmetic that demanded Malik's seizure, a response in kind. The Banu Harith raid a Banu Kathir well three nights later expressly to collect it, killing two herdsmen, one of them a boy named Salim who had only that season taken over his father's watch, neither of whom had any more to do with the sentry's panic than Malik had to do with Amina's murder. Within the week the Banu Kathir burn a season's stored grain belonging to a Banu Harith family that had taken no side. By the fourth such exchange men on both sides can no longer say which grievance a given raid is answering. The sheet draws a slight boy not yet grown into a man's frame, with a young sun-browned face and at most a first growth of beard, wide dark eyes and short, unruly black hair, in a plain dusty-sand wool herder's wrap with no real armor, holding a single ochre-brown spear he has used only against predators. Two full-length views are captioned first time against a man and afterward, the flinch remains, and its expression panels run panic, hesitation, shock and the flinch. The age band, the pull-quote and the closing caption, The hand moved before the mind had decided, are the sheet's; the manuscript's own wording is the one above. A boy with a spear, wearing a herder's wrap, and a fear he could not name.","i":"images/characters/the-sentry-of-banu-kathir-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Witnessed Akad","u":"characters/the-witnessed-akad.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"The Spoken Contract · a social institution, not an object · the vulnerability under test in Case 3001","x":"A word, a witness, a binding: the ordinary contract by which the dinar changes hands, and the evidentiary standard whose failure under stress turns one murder into a war. Not an object, a person or a place, but the spoken, witnessed contract that governs marriage, sale and every other binding transfer of property or obligation across both confederations and the wider trade road. It requires only a spoken word and a trusted witness: no priesthood, no throne, no god any character in the story claims to know the mind of. The tribes of that country keep no priesthood and swear by no single throne of heaven, only by the older and in some ways less forgiving authority of a witnessed word, and the desert has learned, over centuries of bitter litigation conducted at knife-point, to value enforceability over sanctity. A contract sworn before a man everyone trusts to still be alive and lucid in twenty years can still be enforced in twenty years. It is the mechanism by which the dinar changes hands legitimately on the page: from Malik to Amina, before both families and the keeper of the nearest well, who pronounces the akad complete and binding; and from Nur to Qasim, before the village well-keeper and a passing grain-merchant. The coin's own supernatural rule is built directly on top of this ordinary institution. Amina's last words to Malik are akad-precise, that she requires nothing further from the akad between them and will trouble neither his ledger nor his name again. The peace that ends the open war is an akad too, sworn before a garrison captain's own officers in lieu of any god or elder either side still fully trusted. Its failure under stress is the Custodian Diagnostic Log's Primary Finding: absent a witnessed akad, ownership defaulted to physical possession, and absent physical possession of the truth itself, blame defaulted, with comparable and equally unwitnessed certainty, to whichever available party a grieving population found socially convenient to blame. The manuscript's classification line names the vulnerability under test as the sacral weight of the spoken contract in reciprocal-obligation economics, and the sheet calls the akad the story's true subject. The sheet is the one in this case with no figure to describe: its appearance fields read not applicable for size, material, colour and condition, and say that the akad carries no seal, signature or written record, so that its binding force rests entirely on witnessed memory. It shows the institution at work in four scenes, the akad at the well, the coin changing hands before witnesses, the peace-akad before the garrison captain, and Amina's final akad. Its pull-quote is the manuscript's own. The desert had learned, over centuries of bitter litigation conducted at knife-point, to value enforceability over sanctity.","i":"images/characters/the-witnessed-akad-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Provincial Archive Clerk","u":"characters/the-provincial-archive-clerk.html","m":"Case 3001 · The Uncounted Dinar","d":"A clerk of the Provincial Archive in the capital · unnamed · names the war the Grazing Rights Dispute of the Two Confederations · thirty-five to forty-five by the sheet","x":"Titles a war of some three hundred lives for a reader in the capital who will never ask, and files the document that would have corrected it four shelves away. A clerk of the provincial archive in the capital, who has never once set foot in either clan's territory, and who closes the file two years after the garrison captain's truce under an administrative title meant to outlive every person who fought in it: the Grazing Rights Dispute of the Two Confederations. According to his own surviving correspondence, he chose it because it was accurate, dignified, and required no further explanation to a reader in the capital who would never ask for one. The manuscript calls it a masterpiece of the form. It is technically true, since once the truce ended the worst of the killing the dispute did cool into two more years of genuine if lower-grade quarrels over the grazing rights the fighting had disrupted. It is entirely false, since not one drop of the blood that started it had anything to do with grass, water or land. And it is permanently unfalsifiable, since the one document that could have corrected it, a dead thief's confession carried by a courier paid to forget his own errand, was filed, unread by anyone who might have cared, in the very same archive, four shelves away. The closing log sets the clerk's tidy verdict beside its own, and its Analyst Note admits that the Primary Finding was reached by very similar reasoning, declining, out of what it can only describe as professional courtesy toward a clerk it has never met, to say so more directly. The sheet states plainly that the manuscript gives him no physical description and no name, and that every detail is invented to fit his single established function: a slight, sedentary build, a mild, unremarkable face, dark thinning hair neatly combed, pale tired eyes from close archival work, pale skin from years indoors, clean-shaven in the capital's administrative fashion, permanently ink-stained fingers and the slight stoop of decades at filing tables, in plain grey robes among shelves of files that will outlive every name in them. A panel shows him filing Qasim's true affidavit, unread, four shelves away from his own sanitized title; the age band, the face and the clothing are the sheet's.","i":"images/characters/the-provincial-archive-clerk-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Sable Skin","u":"books/4181.html","m":"Case 4181 · Awaiting release","d":"Oath. Dust. Doorless. Uncounted. Mare.","x":"Case 4181"},{"k":"s","t":"Ansbert","u":"characters/ansbert.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Journeyman smith of the Low Forge, later Master of the Hammer-Oath · buyer of the sack · 41 at the opening","x":"Spends the month's stall-rent on a black powder that takes a line and keeps it, and makes a hundred and forty-four blades he cannot explain. A journeyman smith of forty-one, thick-wristed and slow-spoken, with a face like a clenched fist that has been taught to apologize. In nine years he has presented himself to the Proof of the Hammer-Oath Guild for mastery three times, and three times his best blade has rung like a bell under the first stroke, sprung clean under the second and split along its length under the third: the failure of a man who did everything correctly and was ruined by the one thing no one could teach. He keeps the Low Forge anyway, a lean-to of stone and smoke-black timber against the old wall at the far edge of the Low Market, with one hearth, one anvil and a bellows driven by a treadwheel, where the ox Brun walks and his master explains every decision of the day to him in a low continuous murmur. On the ninth of Deyren he has exactly nine pennies in his belt-pouch, which is the month's stall-rent, and a hawker in the Low Market names that sum for a linen sack of black powder that takes a line on iron and keeps it. He pays it. Sigebert Marl notes the empty hand and grants the forge a grace until the Proof-day. The powder goes into the crucible by accident on the seventeenth, swept off the anvil by the besom of his fourteen-year-old apprentice, Lantfrid, and the blade that comes out of the pot rings with a note that goes on in the air like a bell dwindling. The man who tests it is a tall stranger the household has taken in off the Hohlweg, Tomobe no Higaide, who came into this country nineteen years before through the Stone of Case 2231: he lays the flat across his palm, cuts once through the air, bows to the sword and says Bon, which is the whole examination. The household turns the circumstances of that first success into a rule, the scoop levelled with the back of a knife and the besom under the right elbow, and Ansbert catches from the stranger a hand laid flat on the anvil at dawn for ten breaths. When the Guildmaster, Gundobad Rotger, offers him the mastery, a house in the Middle Town and the forge rebuilt in stone in return for the method, he says he will think, and on the first of Brayen tells him to his face that he cannot give what he does not own. He is made a master anyway, on the first of Lorren in the thirty-second year of the reign, by nine to nothing and without being Proved, the only mastery the Hammer-Oath ever conferred without the Third Stroke; he lays the blue sash over Brun's partition and tells the ox it was what he wanted for twenty years and that he does not know where he put the wanting. King Theudebald sends for him, asks whether a sword could be made that would fail, and gives him a plain gold ring with the seal of the river-gate, which he wears on a cord at his neck and is never once asked to show. At the Ordeal in the cathedral he stands by the north pillar in the sash and tells the Hierarch that both blades are Whole and that he cannot say which is the better. He makes the last of the hundred and forty-four on the tenth of Averin and never makes another: for the rest of a long life the best ordinary ironmongery in Austrenne, which breaks honestly at the Third Stroke as a hoe should, and for twenty-six years a palm on the cold face of the anvil before the cocks, taken away after ten breaths and looked at as a man looks at the palm of another. His wife Hiltrude, his apprentice Lantfrid and the Guildmaster Gundobad Rotger have pages of their own; Higaide has no sheet in this case, so his page is the one filed under Case 2231. The manuscript gives Ansbert's age, his build, his face and his hands; the sheet's ruddy, smoke-darkened skin, cropped dark-brown hair thinning at the crown, brown eyes, short grizzled stubble and burn-scarred forearms are its own. Its captions, Master of the Hammer-Oath over a figure in a blue sash worked with a hammer and a raised hand, Dawn Ritual and Ten Breaths at Dawn, follow the household's rule; its expression panels run Quiet Resolve, The Refusal, Oath-Bound and Witness at the Ordeal. I will think.","i":"images/characters/ansbert-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Wolfram","u":"characters/wolfram.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Junior master of the Hammer-Oath, struck from the rolls · sweeper of the Low Forge, later Keeper of the Floor of the Hall of the Hundred and Forty-Four · 33 at the opening, 34 in the war","x":"Walks out of the Guild in the rain, learns to sweep from a boy of sixteen, and sweeps the hundred and forty-fourth measure into the crucible. A lean, sallow master of seven years' standing, with a mother in the Middle Town and a debt to a cloth-merchant, who was Ansbert's friend in the years when both of them were journeymen under old Ruodhard, before the Guild made one of them a master and the other a laughing-stock. On the second night of Averin, the night after the Proof of the Low Forge sword, he puts down a coin for his beer at the Three Sheaves and goes out alone into the rain, and does not go home. He does not knock at the Low Forge; he stands until Lantfrid opens the door, and sits at Hiltrude's scrubbed board as a man sits who has made up his mind to be thrown out. The eleven masters, he says, have voted seven to four, with the Guildmaster, Gundobad Rotger, refusing to cast his own, to lay the matter of the Low Forge before the Hierarch as a case of sorcery. He voted against, and wishes that to be understood; he has also learned in a long evening that a conviction held by one man against seven is a form of private grief rather than a vote. He has come to ask for work. He will sweep the floor. Sigebert Marl strikes him from the rolls with a single stroke of the pen on the first of Brayen, and he writes to his mother that he has been swept to better employment and learns, in the loft of the Low Forge, to sweep. In the war the apprentice Lantfrid, a boy of sixteen, teaches the master of thirty-four the one thing a master does not know: the elbow, the slant, the bristles trailing, the long lazy sweep that must not be hurried. He learns it badly and then well, and remembers for thirty-one years a tall thin boy at the mouth of the loft with his hands on his hips, explaining that it was not the sweeping but the order. After the burying, with his sallow face quite empty, he comes down from the loft and holds out his hand for the besom, and sweeps the hundred and forty-fourth measure, level as half an egg-shell, left to right across the anvil and into the open mouth of the crucible. The Queen gives him the office of Keeper of the Floor of the Hall of the Hundred and Forty-Four, at a penny a day, and he sweeps it for thirty-one years, from left to right with the besom under his right elbow; on the first of every month, when the Hall is empty, he takes the same broom to a small mound in a yard at the edge of a market and sweeps that. What he brings out each dawn in a tin pan is what Marl sells as the Sable Dust. At dawn after the burying he is one of five hands laid on the cold face of the anvil, beside Ansbert, Hiltrude, Tomobe no Higaide and a woman in a plain grey cloak, with a space left at the end of the anvil, on the left, where a long thin hand with a cowlick over it would have been. The manuscript gives his age, his debt, his mother and his lean sallow face; the sheet's 5 ft 10 in, dark hair thinning at the front, dark brown eyes, clean-shaven face, rain-black coat, forge-soot grey apron and birch besom are its own, as is the file-worker's skill in his hands (the manuscript gives him a good hand with a file). Its captions give him as Junior Master, Struck from the Guild's Rolls and Sweeper of the Low Forge, with the panel He sweeps the hundred and forty-fourth measure into the crucible; its expression panels run Former Pride, Unease, Patient Attention and Quiet Resolve. it was not the sweeping but the order","i":"images/characters/wolfram-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Brun","u":"characters/brun.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Ox of the Low Forge, walks the bellows-wheel · one white sock · died in his twenty-sixth year","x":"Walks the treadwheel for six hours without pause, is bowed to by a stranger, and is the only creature to eat the broth left on a sill. A vast, dun, patient ox with one white sock and a habit of leaning against the wheel's rim when he is thinking, who walks the treadwheel that drives the bellows of the Low Forge. Ansbert warms his mash with his own hands on cold mornings, and in the crucible method that the household stumbles on he is the engine: six hours of walking without pause to bring a clay pot to white heat. When a tall stranger on the Hohlweg turns from the cart-boy to bow to him, a full bow from the waist with the hands flat on the thighs, held for a breath, he lowers his great head until the wet nose is a hand's breadth from the man's chest and exhales noisily into his tunic. The boy, who has once seen the same animal knock down a cooper, understands that a judgment has been rendered. The stranger, Tomobe no Higaide, lies down that night in the straw beside his warm flank. It is the bow he made nineteen years before, in Case 2231, to the red ox Lo Rog of the Mill of the Two Weirs. The Middle Town copies him. By Kerren eleven forges have acquired oxen, and one has a white sock painted on with lime and is found by a rival to have lost it in the rain. The Countess Adalgund asks his name, lays a bare hand flat on his flank for rather longer than courtesy requires, and on a later visit tells him that she does not want it; Hiltrude's answer is that nobody who wanted it would have asked the ox's name. When Higaide leaves the forge in the night, the ox stands with his head turned toward the road and does not eat; Hiltrude sets a bowl of broth on the sill of the stall window, and again at dusk on the six evenings that follow, and it is empty on the sixth morning. It is a long time before anyone in the Low Forge learns that it was the ox. He is left behind when the household slips out on the twenty-seventh of Mavren, since a beast of that bulk cannot be taken through a gate full of the Low Market in the dark. On the night of the Uncounted Day his low, hollow groan, like the door of a barn, goes through the apprentice Lantfrid as the Duke's guards take him by the arms, and sets the boy running for the bridge. Higaide bows to the boy and then, slowly, to the ox over the partition, and to the cold hearth and the anvil. Later, when the foreigner carries the boy's body up the steep lanes of the Middle Town, the ox's slow hoofs fall behind him on the cobbles; and Hiltrude's words at the boy's side are Feed the ox. He lives six years more in the wheel, is pensioned to a Queen who has asked for him in the plainest words, and stands out the rest of his time in the lee of a south wall at Eschwin, where the vines come down to the river, with his great head turned toward a road, until his death in his twenty-sixth year with her scarred hand on his flank. There is a stall for him in the yard of the Order's Hall in which nobody is ever put, and the seal of the Order is a hanging sack above the head of an ox. For four days in the almonry yard he had been fed oats by a Hierarch's sexton, whom his sheet draws as the sexton of the bell-loft. The manuscript gives the dun coat, the white sock, the leaning and the death in his twenty-sixth year; the sheet's height of about 5 ft 6 in at the withers, pale blunted horns, dark slow-blinking eyes and worn yoke are its own, as are its captions The Quiet Judgment and Fed in the Dark and its line The ox Higaide bows to. It also gives his age as about 19 at the opening, which does not fit the manuscript's three years in the wheel and six more after them, followed by a death in his twenty-sixth year; the page reports both and does not reconcile them. Feed the ox.","i":"images/characters/brun-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Wido","u":"characters/wido.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Clerk and roll-keeper of the Grey Lances, former novice, later roll-keeper of the Order · 23 years old","x":"Writes a stranger's name once and then, for want of more of it, writes idem, and the entry outlasts him by centuries. A pale, cross-eyed, ink-stained lance of twenty-three who was a novice in a monastery until a disagreement about a goat, and the only man in Hagen Brandt's Grey Lances who can be trusted with a column of figures or a secret. At the Salt Landing in the third week of Lorren, when Brandt puts his mark to the Duke of Tarnwald's contract with the eighty-first name still to be entered, the eighty-first a foreigner who carries two swords, Wido spends an hour trying to spell what the foreigner has told him. On the first line he writes Higa, which is as much as his pen can make of it, and on the second, with the weary economy of a clerk who has been asked to write a stranger's name twice, the single word idem. It is the only joke of Wido's life, and the manuscript says it will outlast him by several centuries and be believed. On the Uncounted night, in the second rank before the Low Forge, with his tablet under his arm and his cross eyes fixed on a point rather to the left of the captain's head, he hears the sexton in the bell-loft toll midnight, says in a voice that carries further than he means that it is past the hour, and is then asked by Brandt what day it is. He says that it is the Uncounted; that no contract can be signed upon it; that the Company's engagement with the Duke was drawn to the last day of Mavren and not to the Uncounted; and that no debt can be reckoned to fall due. Brandt sits down on the threshold, and the eighty lances sit after him. When the Order of the Hundred and Forty-Four is founded, Brandt lends him to the Crown for the day, and he is not got back: he keeps the roll, a hundred and forty-two lines in a hand of the most appalling regularity, and at the place numbered, for want of a better number, the hundred and forty-fourth he writes on the line for the hundred and forty-third Higa and on the next idem. It is entered. It will be believed: the chronicle of the Black Mare will make of it the twin Saracen knights Higa and Idem, who stood in the middle of the Arches with one blade apiece and cut their way through seventy-one, and the twin who never existed will stand for four hundred years in the porch of the cathedral in two identical statues, carved by a mason who had never seen the man. There was only ever one of them, the Eastman, whom Case 2231 knows as Tomobe no Higaide. The manuscript gives his age, his eyes, his ink and his goat; the sheet's Frankish origin in Marnholt, narrow build of about 5 ft 9 in, clean-shaven face, light brown grown-out tonsure, wax tablet, weathered brown leather and ink-black cuffs are its own, as are its panel Writing the Roll: He writes \"Higa,\" then idem. It was the Uncounted and its expression panels Careful Record, Quiet Doubt, The Uncounted and Duty Accepted. It was entered. It would be believed.","i":"images/characters/wido-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sigebert Marl","u":"characters/sigebert-marl.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Rent-collector, clerk and bookkeeper of the Hammer-Oath Guild · seller of figures · 35 at the opening, in his seventy-first year at his death","x":"Sells the same number to the Duke's steward and the King's Chancellor, and closes a forty-year ledger on the cost of a war: nil. A lean, quiet, pleasant-faced man of thirty-five with an excellent memory for figures and no loyalties that a reasonable offer could not move, who goes through the Low Market at Terce on the ninth of the month with a wax tablet and two bored guardsmen, collecting the Guild's stall-rent as a man reads the weather. He enters Ansbert's empty hand in the tablet and, at home, beneath the smith's name in a private ledger kept in a locked box that no one at the Guild has ever seen, a note that reads buys odd goods in place of rent; he makes such notes about a great many people, and very occasionally, years later, one turns out to be worth more than the rent. He buys a Low Forge sword through a cousin for five marks, tests it over a night and a day with a hammer and a vice, finds nothing, locks the sheet in the box with the others, and decides over a supper of bread and onions that the Guild is about to become very cheap and that the time to sell it is before the price is known. He has already sold information to three masters, a duke's steward and the abbess of a convent that owns a vineyard. After that the case is largely his invoices. He strikes Wolfram from the Guild's rolls with a single stroke of his pen on the first of Brayen. In the first week of Mavren of the second year he sells the Duke of Tarnwald's steward, Odilo, his own tally of eighty-nine swords for sixty marks, and on the eighth he sells the Chancellor Ricbert the same figure for forty, entering both under Arithmetic, Resale of; after the Ordeal he sells each of them a four-page Digest of the Eleventh Clause for a mark. For forty marks he arranges for two of the steward's clerks to go over the old wall behind the forge, for sixty he follows the hawker as far as the river-gate and no farther, and for forty from the Countess's steward he sells the Duke's order to take the Low Market, a price he considers handsome for news the clerks had already begun to whisper in the taverns. When the Guild adjourns he sells the Hammer-and-Oath die-block, the Proof-Post and the ash-hoop to a nailer of the Low Market for eleven pence a pound, as scrap. The day the sack is hung in the Hall he goes home to his bread and onions and opens a new ledger headed Relics, Dealing In, and in the fourth year sets up a trestle at the Hall door selling the Hall's sweepings, a penny a pinch, as the Sable Dust. He dies rich in his bed in his seventy-first year, buried by the Order at the Crown's expense since nobody else can be found who will admit to having known him, and the box beneath the bed holds one ledger of every sum anyone ever paid him, closed beneath a column that runs to nine thousand marks with the note Cost of the war, to me: nil. The manuscript gives his age, his lean quiet pleasantness, his memory for figures and his bread and onions; the sheet's 5 ft 10 in, clerk-pale skin, neatly combed light brown hair, hazel eyes, clean-shaven face, grey coat and satchel-box are its own. Its pull-quote, No loyalties that a reasonable offer could not move, is lifted from the manuscript's own wording, and its summary line lists what he sells, the Duke's count, the Digest and the Sable Dust, beside a private ledger of every sum paid him; its panel of him eating bread and onions alone in the corner under the stairs follows a scene at the Three Sheaves on the night of the King's death. Its expression panels run Mild Courtesy, Concentration, Quiet Calculation and Indifference. Cost of the war, to me: nil.","i":"images/characters/sigebert-marl-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Theudebald","u":"characters/theudebald.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"King of Austrenne, called the Careful · widower, father of Adalgund · 63 in the winter of the writ, 64 at his death","x":"Does everything twice, names his daughter heir with a pronoun that could mean either, and has the door of his bedchamber taken off its hinges. A heavy, soft-voiced, grey-bearded man with a weak chest and a cough that comes up out of him like a man climbing a stair, who rules from a small room behind the great hall of Marnholt, the one with the single window over the river. He does everything twice, once for what it says and once for what it leaves out, and it is this habit, more than any other, that has earned him the title of the Careful and given a nation not invaded in sixty years the unshakable conviction that it is well governed. When he reads the figure eighty-nine he looks at it for the length of one candle, drafts the writ himself and has it copied three times; on the twelfth of Mavren it is proclaimed that no sword wrought at the Low Forge is to pass to any lord, captain or company without the royal licence, granted in advance to his daughter Adalgund, Countess of Eschwin. The treasury pays a hundred and forty marks a blade for hers under the heading Repairs to the Eschwin Bridge. In the second month of the thirty-third year he does the one thing of his reign that he does only once: he sends for the smith. He asks Ansbert whether a sword could be made that would fail, hears that a blade would break for whoever was holding it, says that it was never the oath that was being judged but the iron, and gives him a ring from his own finger, a plain gold one with the seal of the river-gate. He has been a widower for thirty-six years and has named no son; in the last month of the thirty-second year he names his daughter heir, using a masculine pronoun that in the Latin of the old law stands for either. On the twenty-first of Brayen he sends for his brother the Duke of Tarnwald, who has not entered his palace in six years and stops at the door of the room and cannot come in; the King has foreseen it, and a carpenter takes the door off its hinges and stands it in the corridor, where it remains eleven days. He makes the brothers swear peace on the ninetieth blade, which lies on the coverlet between them, and tells them that the sword cannot tell them whether they lie. He speaks to his daughter alone, with a boy of eleven holding a basin as the only witness. It is reported that he asked whether she wanted it, and that she said she did not; the sentence of the interview that more than one person repeats is I thought of it three times. He dies on the night of the twenty-second, a little before the hour of the Hare, in the great bed on four pillows, with the window open on the river and no door, with his hand in his daughter's and the writ under the pillow. At the Three Sheaves a boatman says he died of arithmetic and the widow Waldrada says a king who counts twice dies once. The manuscript's reign is not consistent: the first chapter has him ruling thirty-one years, and the chapter of the audience says thirty-three, as does the sheet. The sheet's heavy soft build of about 5 ft 9 in, tired face, grey beard, thinning grey hair, pale blue eyes, sallow skin, aged-gold ring and circlet are its own, as are its panels Double-Checked Tallies and Last Vigil, whose line has him last seen propped on four pillows in a bed without a door. I thought of it three times.","i":"images/characters/theudebald-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Rich Merchant's Son","u":"characters/rich-merchants-son.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"A rich merchant's son of the Middle Town · unnamed · 22 years old by his sheet","x":"Loses his horse in a tavern wager over whether the Hall's best could take a notch from the Low Market's, and gets it back with a polite note. One clause in the manuscript, and a second a few chapters on. In the first season of the black salt the customers of the Hammer-Oath begin to ask a question no Proved blade can answer, whether the Hall's best can take a notch from the Low Market's, and the question is put to the test in the yards of curious knights and in a tavern wager in the Middle Town that costs a rich merchant's son his horse; the manuscript does not say which side he backed. It is the same season in which the proportion in the Hall's ledger between the column headed Whole and the column headed Broken, which has not wavered in two centuries, shifts by an amount that Sigebert Marl will describe to a friend as unprofessional. In the second year the Duke of Tarnwald's steward Odilo, who is riding the Aure marches buying back the past, takes the sword that cost the young man his horse and returns the horse to him with a polite note. The manuscript gives him no name, age, face or build. The sheet's twenty-two years, Frankish, from Marnholt in Austrenne, plump build of about 5 ft 8 in, curled brown hair, blue eyes, flushed pink skin, wispy beard and rings on every finger are its own, as are the slashed-sleeved green silk doublet, the fine guild-stamped sword, the flask of perfumed oil and the captions Sulky Pride, Disbelief, Forced Courtesy and Humiliation After the Wager. Its panel, He loses his horse in a tavern wager, takes the manuscript's clause as its title. a tavern wager in the Middle Town that cost a rich merchant's son his horse","i":"images/characters/rich-merchants-son-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Carter's Wife","u":"characters/carters-wife.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"A carter's wife in the Three Sheaves · unnamed · 35 years old by her sheet","x":"Tells the tavern that the foreigner never comes through the Middle Town without his two swords done up in sacking like a pair of hams. One of the voices in the Three Sheaves in the autumn and winter of the first year, when the black salt acquires its names. The tavern stands in the Middle Town at the corner of Tanners' Lane and the Cooper's Row, under a sign of three wheat-ears bound with red cord, kept by a stout cheerful widow named Waldrada who waters the beer one part to five and is held in consequence to be the most honest woman in Marnholt. A carter has called the powder the Widow's Ashes; a journeyman cooper has called it a splinter of Solan's own fire and made the foreigner a priest of the Sun-God's elder cousin, called Higa in the tongue of the east. The carter's wife adds that the foreigner, Tomobe no Higaide, never comes through the Middle Town without his two swords done up in sacking like a pair of hams. The manuscript's own comment is that this was either a courtesy to the Law, which held a man with two blades to be a man with two oaths, or the cleverest thing anyone had yet seen him do. The sacking is Hiltrude's doing, on his first evening in the Low Forge: she wrapped the long sword and then the short herself, while he watched with the courtesy of a man being searched at a border. The manuscript gives her a trade, a husband and one sentence. The sheet's thirty-five years, Frankish, sturdy 5 ft 4 in build, ruddy round face, blue eyes, fair hair under a linen coif and wart at the corner of her mouth are its own, as are the basket, the brown dress and the grey shawl. Its panel, She tells of the foreigner's swords, wrapped like hams, shows her at a stall, pointing, while a man in a dark hat looks at a bundle on the counter; its expressions run Suspicion, Gossip, Sharp Reply and Quiet Watchfulness. done up in sacking like a pair of hams","i":"images/characters/carters-wife-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Old Sot by the Fire","u":"characters/old-sot-by-the-fire.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"An old drunk at the Three Sheaves · unnamed · 70 years old by his sheet","x":"Says the whole affair is a tale put about by the Guild to explain why it stopped selling swords, and is told to hold his tongue. The last voice in the Three Sheaves' catalogue of names in the first winter, and the only one that does not offer a miracle. The room has called the powder the Night-Seed and the Widow's Ashes and the splinter of Solan's own fire; it has called the foreigner the Moon-Man, from the long pale face of him and the way he does not talk, and the name has gone round the room twice and been understood by everyone present to have been always known. An old sot by the fire says that the whole affair was a tale put about by the Guild to explain why it had stopped selling swords, and is told to hold his tongue. The manuscript says no more of him than that, and does not say who told him, or whether he was right. The manuscript gives him one sentence and no age. The sheet's seventy years, bent thin build of about 5 ft 6 in, bleary deeply lined face, wispy white hair and scruffy beard, watery blue eyes, red-veined skin and patched wine-stained brown wool tunic are its own, with a trembling grip on a wooden tankard. Its panels, Seated by the Fire, The Guild's Tale and He is told to hold his tongue, are the manuscript's sentence drawn at three moments; its expressions run Suspicion, Bluster, Fear and Sullen Silence. a tale put about by the Guild to explain why it had stopped selling swords","i":"images/characters/old-sot-by-the-fire-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ruodhard","u":"characters/ruodhard.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Eldest Master of the Hammer-Oath Guild, once its Proof-keeper · taught Ansbert his trade · 79 years old by his sheet","x":"Tells a hushed tavern corner that the secret is in the angle of the broom, and later speaks the vote that makes the man he taught a master. A very old smith who served the Guild as its Proof-keeper before Gundobad Rotger took the mallet, and under whom both Ansbert and Wolfram were journeymen. In the first winter, when every master in the Middle Town is buying oxen and standing at an anvil before the cocks, he announces to a hushed corner that the whole matter comes down to the angle of the broom, and that he has known it since his youth. It is the most accurate wrong answer in the case: the household does sweep the powder in left to right with the besom under the right elbow, because that is how it was done the first time, and the broom, as the manuscript's last line says, knows nothing. On the first of Lorren in the thirty-second year of the reign the Guild is dying in the order in which a household dies, and the nine surviving masters come down the chalk road to the Low Market in their good coats with the sash of the Hammer-Oath on a cushion. The eldest of them is the very Ruodhard who taught Ansbert his trade, and he says, in a thin shaking voice, that the vote has been nine to nothing and that nobody proposes to Prove him. The manuscript gives him no age or face. The sheet's seventy-nine years, Frankish and very short, stooped build, parchment-pale liver-spotted skin, wild white hair, long white beard, pale filmy blue eyes and trembling hands that are steady on a hammer are its own, with the guild coat, the blue sash worked with crossed hammers and a walking-stick. Its panel, The Secret Is in the Angle of the Broom, shows him pointing at a push-broom against a bench; its expressions run Wisdom, Age, Resolve and Recognition. the whole matter came down to the angle of the broom","i":"images/characters/ruodhard-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Waldrada's Nephew","u":"characters/waldradas-nephew.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Nephew of Waldrada, who keeps the Three Sheaves · sells eels · 19 years old by his sheet","x":"Carries the tavern's news down to the ox-stall, and brings a stranger the word that sends him down the road. The nephew of the widow Waldrada, who keeps the Three Sheaves, and an eel-seller with a bucket and a route through the Middle Town and the Low Market. The manuscript uses him twice, both times as a conduit. In the first winter the foreigner's habit of laying his palm flat on the anvil at dawn reaches the Three Sheaves by way of him, and within a month nine masters are standing at their own anvils before the cocks, counting to ten. Then, after the Hierarch Rothari has preached before a full cathedral on the text that Solan's fire is not to be addressed by strangers, he tells Tomobe no Higaide about it at dusk at the door of the ox-stall, in the slow ruined syllables of the lane-speech, with his hands moving to help the words. Higaide understands a good deal less of it than the boy supposes and a good deal more than anyone knows: he has stood in the moot-green of a village called Vilarnau on a winter morning and watched a baron's face change when a priest's letter was read to him, and he knows what it means when a man who holds authority over the gods begins to use the word stranger in a sermon. He thanks the boy with a bow and gives him a copper penny, which is a thing he has never done, and that night he leaves the forge. A later chapter has a copper penny in the apprentice Lantfrid's left sleeve, kept there through the war and returned by Hiltrude to Higaide on the Uncounted Day as the coin he had once given a boy for the news of a sermon. The manuscript does not say how the coin passed from the one boy to the other; the page reports both and does not reconcile them. The apprentice's own sheet draws the penny at his left cuff. The manuscript gives him a trade, an aunt and a bucket. The sheet's nineteen years, wiry 5 ft 7 in build, quick freckled sly face, red hair, green eyes, pale freckled skin and clean-shaven jaw are its own, with the patched brown-grey wool, the leather cap and the bucket of eels. Its panels, Tavern Messenger and He tells Higaide of Rothari's sermon, are the manuscript's two scenes; its expressions run Sly, Surprised, Pleased and Listening.","i":"images/characters/waldradas-nephew-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ricbert","u":"characters/ricbert.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Chancellor of Austrenne · counsel to King Theudebald and Queen Adalgund · 55 years old by his sheet","x":"Writes every sentence twice, buys the same figure the Duke's steward bought, and cannot find a form of words by which one knight might be two. A pouchy, anxious lawyer with a face like a folded napkin, thirty years in practice, who has been trying for a year to learn what no officer of the Crown had thought to count. Sigebert Marl sells him the tally of eighty-nine swords on the eighth of Mavren for forty marks, and a four-page Digest of the Eleventh Clause for a mark. He copies the King's writ three times through the Chancellor's clerks, who alter the second paragraph and are made to alter it back, and attends King Theudebald's audience with the smith, which he records only as an audience of a technical character. After the King's death he sits for three hours with the Countess and explains the position of the Law: that a succession in dispute goes to the altar, that a party who declines the altar concedes the writ, and that the Law, written for a kingdom in which iron could be relied upon to disagree with itself, contains no provision he can find for the case in which it does not. She says that she will swear her case on a sword at the Ordeal before the Hierarch and let the Law decide it, which in thirty years he has never once heard a client say, and he writes the sentence down and then, because he is a careful man, writes it down a second time. He arranges the seating at the Ordeal himself, three times, and finds at the third attempt that it is not possible to seat the Duke of Tarnwald anywhere with a door within a hundred paces of it, and concludes, with the air of a man submitting to an act of God, that the west doors of the cathedral will stand open. He drafts the Charter of the Order of the Hundred and Forty-Four in nine days and has it copied three times; he leaves a space, with a lawyer's tact, to be filled by custom. When the Queen calls the last two blades for one man he tells her, with great gentleness, that the Charter provides for a hundred and forty-four knights, one sword to each, and that he has not been able to devise a form of words by which one knight might be two. Then count him twice, Chancellor, she says, and he answers that it is not a law, and it is the last honest objection he will raise to anything for the remainder of the reign. The manuscript gives his lawyer's habits, his napkin face and his ink; the sheet's age of fifty-five, soft pear-shaped build of about 5 ft 6 in, pasty skin, thinning grey hair, watery grey eyes, clean-shaven face, ink-stained fingers and dull pewter-gold chain of office are its own. Its pull-quote The position of the Law is clear and its captions He Writes It Twice and Drafting the Charter of the Order are its own; its expression panels run Anxious, Explaining the Law, Careful Deliberation and Quiet Relief. an audience of a technical character","i":"images/characters/ricbert-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Nailer","u":"characters/nailer.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"A nailer of the Low Market · buyer of the Guild's die-block · 44 years old by his sheet","x":"Buys the Hammer-Oath's die-block as scrap, strikes six hundred nails from it, and sells the rest of the iron as a relic. The man to whom Sigebert Marl, staying behind to wind up the Guild's books on the day it adjourns, sells the Hammer-and-Oath die-block that afternoon for eleven pence a pound as scrap, with the Proof-Post and the ash-hoop thrown in. The manuscript gives him only what he does with it. He strikes six hundred nails from the die before he notices what is on it, and is said, in later years, to have sold the rest of the iron as a relic of the Hierarch's own anvil, at a profit that the Guild would have called unsound. The Hammer-Oath, which had Proved ten thousand swords and decided the fate of kings by the Third Stroke, is remembered in Marnholt for the next two generations chiefly for the quality of its nails. A notice cut in capitals by a nailer's apprentice, saying that the sack itself is the property of the Crown and that its power adheres by contact in the Sable Dust, is posted on the trestle at the Hall door where Sigebert Marl sells it; the manuscript does not say whether it is this nailer's apprentice. The manuscript gives him a trade and a price. The sheet's forty-four years, short barrel-chested 5 ft 4 in build, greasy black hair, brown red-rimmed eyes, sooty face, singed beard and burn-scarred left forearm are its own, with the leather apron, hammer and nail-tub. Its panels, Six Hundred Nails and He sells the leftover iron as a relic, are the manuscript's two clauses; its expressions run Weariness, Concentration, Alarm and Cunning Pride. sold the rest of the iron as a relic of the Hierarch's own anvil","i":"images/characters/nailer-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Boy with the Basin","u":"characters/boy-with-the-basin.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"King's chamber attendant · unnamed · 11 years old","x":"Sets to hold a basin in a room with no door, hears what was said between a King and his daughter, and is dismissed the following week. The only witness to the one conversation in the case that nobody has ever been able to report. On the twenty-first of Brayen King Theudebald speaks to his daughter alone, after the brother has gone, in the last of the light and with the Chancellor sent to the corridor; the room has no door, and the only person in it besides the two of them is a boy of eleven who has been set to hold a basin. It is reported that the King asked her whether she wanted it, and that she said she did not. It is reported that he laughed, which cost him a fit of coughing so long that the physician had to be called in from the passage. The boy is dismissed from the service in the following week for having an excellent memory. The manuscript gives him an age, a basin and a dismissal, and does not say what he remembered or where he went. Another boy of eleven, in a different chapter, polishes the Duke's eighty-eight swords every morning with a cloth at Tarnhall and is forbidden to hurry; the page does not take him to be the same boy. The sheet's pale, round, solemn face, light-brown bowl-cut, wide brown eyes, small narrow build and undyed linen tunic with a plain household badge in blue are its own, as are its tagline, The boy who remembered what others wished forgotten, and its panel The King's Private Words; its expression panels run Duty, Unease, Attention and Memory. for having an excellent memory","i":"images/characters/boy-with-the-basin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Duke's Pages","u":"characters/the-dukes-pages.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Two pages of the Duke of Tarnwald's household · unnamed · boys of 15","x":"Hold the great west doors of the cathedral open from Prime to the last stroke, with a block of chalk wedged under each foot. Two boys of fifteen in Tarnwald blue who stand on either side of the great west doors of the cathedral through the whole of the Ordeal. The Chancellor Ricbert has found that it is not possible to seat the Duke of Tarnwald anywhere with a door within a hundred paces of it, and has concluded that the doors, oak bound in bronze, twice the height of a man and shut upon every proceeding of the Law since the foundation, will stand open. So the two pages hold them back with their hands from Prime to the last stroke, with a great square block of chalk apiece wedged under the foot, for fear that a draught should remember its duty. The Duke himself stands in the porch behind them, in a plain grey cloak and without a sword; the cathedral's Sacristan records that he never took his eyes from the open door. When the Third Stroke is struck on the crossed blades, the two pages, who can see the whole length of the nave in miniature between their own elbows, feel their hands go cold against the oak. The manuscript gives them an office, an age and a door. The sheet's two gangling boys of about 5 ft 5 in each, northern-pale, one freckled, fair-haired and blue-eyed and the other sallow, dark-brown-haired and brown-eyed, are its own, as are the beardless faces and the chalk-block detail; its panel is Holding the Great West Doors, and its expression panels run Strain, Cold, Fatigue and Resolve. for fear that a draught should remember its duty","i":"images/characters/the-dukes-pages-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sexton","u":"characters/sexton.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Sexton of the cathedral bell-loft · reads the water-clock · 57 years old by his sheet","x":"Tolls midnight into the one day of the year that is left unwritten, and feeds an ox oats in the almonry yard. The man who tolls the hours from the cathedral bell-loft. On the night the Uncounted Day begins he reads his water-clock by a candle and tolls midnight: a single deep bronze note and then eleven others, each one slower than the last, like a man descending a stair in the dark. It carries across the whole of the Low Market, over the dark roofs of the city, and it is what moves the clerk Wido, standing in the second rank of the Grey Lances before the Low Forge, to say that it is past the hour. A Hierarch's sexton keeps the ox Brun for four days in the almonry yard after the household has taken refuge there, and feeds him oats and is licked for it. The manuscript gives two sextons, one in the bell-loft and one in the almonry yard, neither named; the sheet draws a single man, the sexton of the bell-loft, and gives him the panel An almonry sexton feeds oats to an ox, so that the page joins them and does not claim the manuscript does. The sheet's fifty-seven years, Frankish, from Marnholt, wiry build of about 5 ft 7 in, pale lined face, thin white hair, pale grey eyes, clean-shaven face, rope-callused palms and plain grey gown are its own, as is the counting of the strokes aloud; its expression panels run Vigilance, Counting Aloud, Weariness and Devotion. a single deep bronze note and then eleven others, each one slower than the last, like a man descending a stair in the dark","i":"images/characters/sexton-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Gerold","u":"characters/gerold.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Captain of the Duke's gate-guard, Tarnwald · 38 years old by his sheet","x":"Takes the boy himself, and bars the great gate of the Nine Arches on a single word it was never meant to carry. A lean, high-nosed, hard-voiced captain in Tarnwald blue, sent by the steward Odilo with twenty men to the Low Forge on the Uncounted night, as a precaution, in the dry steward's way of seeing that a thing will be done by arranging for it to be done twice. He finds eighty hired lances sitting in the mud at the door of the lean-to and a captain in the lintel who does not rise. He says, with a sort of cold fury, that he has an order from the Duke of Tarnwald to take the household of the forge, that there is a boy in the doorway, and that he would thank the Company to stand up. We are nobody's, says Hagen Brandt, and we are sitting. Gerold understands it perfectly and does not argue; he does the thing a captain of the Duke's guard is bound to do, which is to take the boy himself, and puts two men at the lean-to door. They take the apprentice by the arms, and the ox groans. He is at the gate-tower at the north head of the Nine Arches on the Uncounted Day, where the order of the tower is cut over the passage in the letters of the Roman time and every captain who takes the post reads it on his first morning and walks beneath it every morning after: that on the word to close, the great gate is to be shut and barred. A quarter of an hour after the Duke has passed through toward the bridge, Odilo, who has seen three barges of the Countess's levies coming down under the north bank toward the water-stair, says a single word to him, close. He means the little door at the foot of the stair; he has not said which door, nor bar. The captain who has read the order every morning and has never once been given the word supplies what a steward leaves out, and the two leaves of black oak, bound in iron, move on their rusted chains for the first time in three hundred years and meet with a boom that shakes the pigeons off the tower, and the bar goes home. The Duke hears it, and says Open it. The steward Odilo of this case is not the Hierarch Odilo of Cases 2231 and 0202. The manuscript gives his name, his office, his look and his one act. The sheet's 38 years, lean build of about 5 ft 11 in, hard jaw, cold dark-grey eyes, black cropped hair, clean-shaven pale northern skin, Tarnwald-blue tabard with a tower device over mail, sword and round shield are its own, as are the panel speech Close and the line He reads the order every morning; its expression panels run Discipline, Suspicion, Decision (on Odilo's order) and Duty (at the gate). that he would thank the Company to stand up","i":"images/characters/gerold-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Hiltrude","u":"characters/hiltrude.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Wife of Ansbert · keeper of the Low Forge's books and rabbits, later Keeper of the books of the Order · 48 at the opening, 51 at the close","x":"Counts the sack into a hundred and forty-four measures with a horn spoon, keeps the household's books and rabbits, and is not surprised by any number. The wife of Ansbert and the household administrator of the Low Forge: keeper of its books, its rabbits and its candles, and, in the words of her sheet, tally-keeper of swords. The pages already written for this case show her wherever the household's rules are made. On the foreigner's first evening in the forge she wraps his long sword and then his short in sacking herself, while he watches (Carter's Wife). After he leaves she sets a bowl of broth on the sill of the stall window for the ox Brun, and it is empty on the sixth morning; when the Countess Adalgund asks the ox's name and later says she does not want him, her answer is that nobody who wanted him would have asked it. At dawn after the burying she is one of the five hands laid on the cold face of the anvil beside Ansbert, Wolfram and Tomobe no Higaide. On the Uncounted Day she returns to Higaide the copper penny the apprentice Lantfrid has kept in his sleeve (Waldrada's Nephew), and at the boy's side her words are Feed the ox. Her sheet's main scene, Counting the Sack into 144 Measures, draws her at a table by candlelight over an open ledger, spooning out of a sack with a small horn spoon, with counters cut from spoon handles laid in rows beside her; its closing scene, She Holds the Rabbit Crate Like a Child, shows her with a rabbit in a wooden crate against her chest. The number is the one on Ansbert's page, a hundred and forty-four blades, and the hundred and forty-fourth measure is the one Wolfram sweeps into the crucible. The publication notes give her a later office, Keeper of the books of the Order (Epilogue ii), the Order being the Order of the Hundred and Forty-Four that Adalgund founds. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Her age (48 at the opening, 51 at the close), her Frankish origin in Marnholt, her wiry build of about 5 ft 5 in, pale flour-dusted skin, level grey-green eyes and brown hair streaked with grey under a linen coif, her plain wooden spoon, the tiny horn spoon half an egg-shell in size and the steel-tipped marking-punch in her apron are the sheet's own, as are its expression panels, Exactitude (Counting), Authority (Household Rules), Quiet Care (Rabbits) and Unsurprised (Any Number).","i":"images/characters/hiltrude-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Gisalbert","u":"characters/gisalbert.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Youngest of the Guild's journeymen, later groom to the Duke's steward · 17 years old by his sheet","x":"The youngest of the Guild's journeymen, a new groom with a sword in his arms for a man who cannot use it, glancing back toward the Guild door. The youngest of the Hammer-Oath Guild's journeymen: seventeen, lanky, straw-haired and sunburnt, with a downy upper lip, the hammer-callus of the trade already on his right palm and fresh rope burns across his left hand. The Guild is the one whose eldest master is Ruodhard, from whose rolls Wolfram is struck, and whose Guildmaster is Gundobad Rotger and whose clerk Sigebert Marl collects its stall-rent in the Low Market. The sheet gives him one line, A new groom, carrying a sword for a man who cannot use it, and one scene: a boy in a leather apron in a stone lane, a long sword wrapped in cloth held in the crook of his arm, glancing back toward the Guild door. Its small caption for the same figure is Glances toward the Guild door. The publication notes say whose groom he has become: he takes service as a groom to the Duke of Tarnwald's steward, Odilo, and in Chapter 10 is seen carrying a Low Forge sword for a man who cannot use it. They do not name the man, and neither does the sheet. The rope burns on his left hand are a new groom's; the callus on his right palm is the hammer's. Written from the sheet and the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age, his lanky build of about 5 ft 8 in, straw-yellow hair, blue eyes, ruddy sunburnt face, worn brown and grey clothes and dark leather apron are the sheet's own, as are its expression panels, Awkward, Embarrassed, Wary and Looking Back.","i":"images/characters/gisalbert-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Journeyman Cooper","u":"characters/journeyman-cooper.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Journeyman cooper of the Middle Town · unnamed · 26 years old by his sheet","x":"Calls the black salt a splinter of Solan's own fire, in the room at the Three Sheaves where the powder gets its names. A stocky, thick-armed journeyman cooper of the Middle Town, round-faced, ruddy and quick to grin, whose place in the case is a phrase. In the first autumn and winter of the black salt the room at the Three Sheaves, kept by Waldrada, supplies the powder with names. A carter has called it the Widow's Ashes; a journeyman cooper has called it a splinter of Solan's own fire and made the foreigner, Tomobe no Higaide, a priest of the Sun-God's elder cousin, called Higa in the tongue of the east (Carter's Wife). Solan is the Son of the Threefold Crown, the god of the sun and of fire, and the phrase is all the other pages record of the cooper. His sheet's caption is He calls the black salt a splinter of Solan's fire, over a scene of him at a tavern table in a crowd, laughing, with something small held up between finger and thumb. Its expression panels run Pride (Craftsman), Conviction (Black Salt), Unease (The Foreigner) and Good Humor (At Ease). Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 26, his stocky build of about 5 ft 6 in, round ruddy face, curly brown hair, hazel eyes, scrappy goatee, hoop-callused palms and smell of oak shavings are the sheet's own, with the worn leather apron over a linen shirt.","i":"images/characters/journeyman-cooper-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Waldrada","u":"characters/waldrada.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Widow, keeper of the Three Sheaves · 54 years old by her sheet","x":"Waters the beer one part to five, is held to be the most honest woman in Marnholt, and says that they are all buying something. The stout, cheerful widow who keeps the Three Sheaves, the tavern at the corner of Tanners' Lane and the Cooper's Row in the Middle Town, under a sign of three wheat-ears bound with red cord. She waters the beer one part to five and is held in consequence to be the most honest woman in Marnholt; her sheet gives the ratio and the reputation as captions. Her room is where the black salt acquires its names, in the voices of a barge-hand, a carter, a carter's wife, an old sot by the fire, a journeyman cooper and a drover from the north, and where, on the night King Theudebald dies, a boatman says he died of arithmetic and she says that a king who counts twice dies once. Her nephew, an eel-seller, carries the tavern's news down to the ox-stall of the Low Forge, and a barmaid of her house, the Girl Behind the Bar, listens to it all. Her pull-quote, They are all buying something, is the sheet's own line, not one the other pages give her. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Her age of 54, her stout build of about 5 ft 4 in, round flushed face, greying red hair tied beneath a cap, brown eyes and ruddy complexion, and her pewter mug and wiping cloth are the sheet's own, as are its expression panels, Cheer, Knowing Smile, At Service and Widow's Quiet; its scene shows her behind her bar with a mug in one hand and the cloth in the other. They are all buying something.","i":"images/characters/waldrada-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Girl Behind the Bar","u":"characters/girl-behind-the-bar.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Barmaid of the Three Sheaves · unnamed · 17 years old by her sheet","x":"Tilts an ear toward the tables of the Three Sheaves, and keeps a burn scar from a tap-room fire on one wrist. A barmaid of the Three Sheaves, where Waldrada is the keeper, and the one person in the room whose work is to be present while everyone else talks. The tavern is the one whose voices the other pages record, a carter's wife with her sacking and swords, a carter with his Widow's Ashes, an old sot by the fire with his tale of the Guild, a journeyman cooper with his splinter of Solan's fire. Her sheet draws her at the bar with a damp rag and plain tin mugs, an ear tilted toward the nearest tables, and its caption is She listens while the tables talk. What she reports, in Chapter 6, is that the Lady Adalgund was seen veiled at the Low Market buying a plain iron ladle from the smith's wife, presumably Hiltrude of the Low Forge. Her small scar is the sheet's detail: a burn on one wrist from a tap-room fire, drawn in close-up and echoed in the expression panel Concern (Fire Memory). The other panels run Curiosity (Listening), Amusement (Tavern Talk) and Quiet Resolve (At Work). Written from the sheet and the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Her age of 17, Frankish origin in Marnholt, slight build of about 5 ft 2 in, round freckled face, fair skin, green eyes, thick red-brown braid, plain russet kirtle and grey linen working apron are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/girl-behind-the-bar-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Donkey","u":"characters/donkey.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"The hawker's donkey · about 10 by its sheet · asleep on its feet","x":"Carries the hawker's empty pack-saddle, sleeps standing up, and is remembered better than its owner. The hawker's donkey: a small, dusty-grey pack donkey with a pale belly, a long muzzle with pale bristles and a dark cross over the shoulders, in a plain rope halter and an empty pack-saddle, which the sheet draws asleep on its feet. The hawker is the one in the Low Market who names the sum of nine pennies to Ansbert for a linen sack of black powder: the Black-Salt Peddler of the publication notes, who is also Observer 614 (a figure with no sheet, and so no page of his own here), and whom Sigebert Marl follows, for sixty marks, as far as the river-gate and no farther. The sheet's last line is the one the page can lean on: No one recalled the owner's face; a dozen could describe the donkey's temper. Its one scene, At the quay, as the last dust goes into the river, draws the animal alone on a stone quay beside the water, a bridge and a cathedral town far across it. The notes put that scene in Chapter 17, and the donkey's first appearance in Chapter 2, asleep on its feet behind the peddler when he sells the sack; neither the sheet nor the notes say whose hand lets the dust go. Its expression panels run Patient, Stubborn, Unbothered and Fast Asleep. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Its age of about 10 (given as indeterminate), its height of about 3 ft 9 in at the withers, dusty grey coat, shut heavy-lidded eyes and weathered brown-grey tack are the sheet's own, as is its pull-quote, Asleep on its feet. Asleep on its feet.","i":"images/characters/donkey-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Abbess of the Vineyard Convent","u":"characters/abbess-of-the-vineyard-convent.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Abbess of a convent that owned a vineyard · 62 years old by her sheet","x":"Buys the Guild clerk's information, reads her letters with an ink-black forefinger, and stands among the notables at the Ordeal. The abbess of a convent that owns a vineyard, one of the buyers of Sigebert Marl's information: by the time he decides that the Guild is about to become very cheap he has already sold information to three masters, a duke's steward and her. Neither the sheet nor the pages already written say what she bought or what she did with it. The sheet draws her as a stout, composed woman of sixty-two, shrewd-eyed, at a writing desk with an inkwell and a pile of books, reading a letter with an ink-stained right forefinger; and, in its large closing scene, standing among the notables at the Ordeal in the cathedral nave with a ring of keys at her belt. Her expression panels run Composure, Scrutiny, Concern and Cold Resolve, and her pendant, drawn in close-up, is three linked bronze rings. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Her age of 62, Frankish origin in Austrenne, stout build of about 5 ft 3 in, broad composed face with a faint wine-flush on both cheeks, dark grey eyes, black veil with one white temple strand, weathered sun-freckled hands and grey cloak over black wool are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/abbess-of-the-vineyard-convent-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Mended Stirrup","u":"characters/the-mended-stirrup.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Travelling knight of the Aure Marches · carries a Low Forge blade · 33 years old by his sheet","x":"Rides the Aure marches on a stirrup mended with rivets, and sells a Low Forge blade back to the steward Odilo at thirty times the price. A travelling knight of the Aure marches, known on his sheet only by the thing he rides on: a stirrup mended with rivets. He carries an eleven-mark sword, a Low Forge blade, and his sheet's two scenes are about that sword. In the first, The Low Forge's Third Blade, he stands at a smith's forge with a sword in his hand; in the second, Sold Back to Odilo, Thirty Times the Price, a leather purse of coin lies beside the blade. The Odilo of the caption is the Duke of Tarnwald's steward, Odilo, not the Hierarch Odilo of Cases 2231 and 0202: it is the steward who, on the Rich Merchant's Son's page, rides the Aure marches buying back the past. The publication notes give the knight's whole transaction: he buys the Low Forge's third blade for four marks of silver and notches his own eleven-mark sword against it (Chapter 5), and sells that blade back to the steward at thirty times the price (Chapter 9). His main figure stands in a weathered cloak with two swords crossed against his chest, and his expression panels run Good Humour, Appraisal, After the Test and Quiet Triumph. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 33, his compact build of about 5 ft 8 in, weathered good-humoured face, sandy hair and short sandy beard, green-grey eyes, tanned skin, travel-worn mail under a brown-grey surcoat and the horse and mended stirrup are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/the-mended-stirrup-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sunnhild","u":"characters/sunnhild.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Pan-master at the Salt Landing · 60 years old by her sheet","x":"Keeps the salt-pans of the Salt Landing with a voice like a gull, while her granddaughters look away. The pan-master of the Salt Landing, the river-mouth hamlet where, in the third week of Lorren, Wido's page has Hagen Brandt put his mark to the Duke of Tarnwald's contract, and which the Gazetteer describes as thirty huts and a hundred salt-pans. She is sixty, broad and weathered, with a seamed, wind-burnt face, a long white braid, sea-grey eyes, a faint white down, a long-handled salt-rake and a voice like a gull. The scene on her sheet is captioned Her Granddaughters Look Away: it draws her standing with three fair girls beside a white heap of salt, and a ragged man bent over the heap with his hands raised to his mouth. The publication notes name him: it is Tomobe no Higaide, whom she employs on the salt-pans and pays in salt (Chapter 7), and whom she takes for a saint because he does not drink; the granddaughters learn to look away from a tragic man tasting brine. Her expression panels run Workday Resolve, Suspicion, Rough Kindness and Sea-Worn Patience. Her pull-quote, A voice like a gull, is the sheet's own line. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Her age of 60, her build of about 5 ft 5 in, oilskin-brown apron over grey wool and wooden clogs are the sheet's own. A voice like a gull.","i":"images/characters/sunnhild-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"One-eyed Lombard","u":"characters/one-eyed-lombard.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Grey Lance of the Company · self-proclaimed prince · Lombard · 42 years old by his sheet","x":"A one-eyed Lombard of the Grey Lances who styles himself a prince, and carries Higaide's swords in the fold of his cloak. A lean, olive-skinned Lombard of forty-two with oiled black hair going grey at the temples, a patch over his right eye and a waxed moustache, who is a lance of the Company and calls himself a prince: his sheet's subtitle is Grey Lance of the Company; self-proclaimed prince. The Company is the Grey Lances of Hagen Brandt, the mercenary company of eighty whose clerk is Wido and whose sergeant is Ragnulf. The detail that ties him to the stranger is on the cloak: a close-up captioned Cloak Fold (Higaide's Swords) draws two sword-hilts under the grey cloth, and the back view marks them Higaide's swords. Tomobe no Higaide is the foreigner with two swords whom Carter's Wife sees wrapped in sacking and whom Wido enters on the roll as Higa and idem. The publication notes have him translate between the Company and Higaide with a little Latin; neither they nor the sheet say why he carries the swords. His expression panels run Pride (Prestige), Caution (Translation), Offended Dignity (Debts) and Composure (Company). His last scene is captioned He Leads the Ox to the Bridge and draws him walking a brown ox on a lead beside a cloaked figure, a long arched bridge and a walled town ahead; the sheet does not name the ox, and the publication notes say only that he leads the ox down to the bridge, so it is presumably Brun. The notes add that at ninety he is found to have been a younger son of a minor house (Epilogue iii). Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 42, Lombard origin, olive skin, lean build of about 5 ft 9 in, dark brown eye, faded grey cloak and worn brown leather are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/one-eyed-lombard-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ragnulf","u":"characters/ragnulf.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Sergeant of the Grey Lances · Keeper of Practice · Frisian · 40 years old by his sheet","x":"Keeps the practice yard of the Grey Lances, is disarmed there by Higaide, and retires to keep bees in southern Tarnwald. A bull-necked, stocky Frisian sergeant of forty with cropped red-blond hair, a short ginger beard, pale blue eyes and one ear, the other lost to an old wound, who keeps practice for the Grey Lances with an ash stave and a company sword. His sheet's tagline is A stave shows the hand; a contest shows the man. Its principal scene stands him in a yard before an inn whose sign reads The Black Eel, under the caption Sergeant on the Yard; the next, Disarmed by Higaide, has him facing a dark-haired swordsman with a long blade, the stave on the ground, and the caption beside it reads He knows a worthy man. Tomobe no Higaide is the foreigner of the other pages; the Black Eel is also the name on which Theodard of Lorrenne's sheet gives his age, twenty-seven. The Grey Lances are the company of Hagen Brandt, whose clerk is Wido and in which the One-eyed Lombard is a lance. The last scene is Keeping Bees in Southern Tarnwald: a man in a worn dark cloak at wooden hives under an orchard, a half-timbered farmhouse behind him. His expression panels run Judgment (Veteran), Surprise (Disarmed), Respect (Recognized) and Quiet Resolve (Retirement). His pull-quote is the sheet's own line. The publication notes give his years as sergeant, nineteen, and have him propose Higaide for sergeant before he retires to a farm in southern Tarnwald to keep bees (Chapter 11). Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 40, his Frisian origin, bull-necked stocky build of about 5 ft 10 in, square wind-burnt face, missing ear, dark worn leather jerkin and heavy belt are the sheet's own. A stave shows the hand; a contest shows the man.","i":"images/characters/ragnulf-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Berthar","u":"characters/berthar.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Sergeant of the Tarnwald household, the Duke's man for thirty years · 55 years old by his sheet","x":"Commands in few words, and buries his two sons on the Hohlweg. A bandy-legged, deep-chested sergeant of the household of the Duke of Tarnwald, fifty-five, wind-reddened and iron-grey, who carries a spear and wears Tarnwald blue over mail, the tabard with a pale tree on it. Gerold, the captain of the Duke's gate-guard, wears the same blue; the sheet does not say how the two ranks stand to one another. Its large scene draws him at the head of a column of spearmen under a blue banner, one arm out in command, and its last, captioned He buries his two sons on the Hohlweg, has him alone on a hillside with a spade beside two wooden crosses. The Hohlweg is the road on which, on Ansbert's and Brun's pages, the household finds the stranger; his sons, two young men of twenty-two and nineteen, die of the flux in the Hohlweg camp, neither of them having seen an enemy (Chapter 14). His expression panels run Duty (Steady), Grief (Silent), Command (Few words) and Resolve (Unyielding). The publication notes call him the Duke's man for thirty years and give him a boar-spear; in Chapter 16 he steps out of the front block and speaks the sentence the army follows, and stays behind to bury the steward, presumably Odilo. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 55, bandy-legged thick-chested build, wind-reddened northern skin, cropped iron-grey hair, pale grey eyes and short clipped grey beard are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/berthar-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Young Levy Archer","u":"characters/young-levy-archer.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Tarnwald levy, a picket at the foot of the bridge · unnamed · 17 years old by his sheet","x":"Is cold for six hours and frightened for three days, looses at the bridge-head, and lays his bow down. A thin, pinched, beardless boy of seventeen in a hooded jerkin of padded farm wool, drawn into the Tarnwald levy and set as a picket at the foot of a bridge with a bow and a sheaf of arrows. His sheet's tagline is Cold for six hours; frightened for three days. Its large scenes draw him Loosing at the Bridge-Head, at a running figure on the bridge, and then stooping to lay the bow on the ground (Setting Down His Bow); the last, Among the Farmers at the Bridge, puts him among cloaked men with spears and bows at the foot of an arched stone bridge. The sheet does not say who the running figure is, and the publication notes, which place the scene in Chapter 15, do not either. On Brun's page the apprentice Lantfrid is set running for the bridge on the Uncounted night, and Gerold's page has the Duke's guard at the gate-tower of the Nine Arches; this page does not say that either is the bridge or the boy on the sheet. His expression panels run Fear (Three Days), Cold (Six Hours), Alarm (At the Bridge-Head) and Relief (Bow Laid Down). His pull-quote is the sheet's own line. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 17, thin build of about 5 ft 8 in, pinched frightened face, dun matted hair, pale blue eyes, wind-chapped skin and weathered brown-grey wool are the sheet's own. Cold for six hours; frightened for three days.","i":"images/characters/young-levy-archer-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Nurse of Waldo","u":"characters/nurse-of-waldo.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"The Duke's nurse at Tarnhall · 40 years old by her sheet · the sweating fever, Chapter 9","x":"Sets an oak beam across the infirmary door at Tarnhall in the sweating fever, out of love. The sheet calls her the Nurse of Waldo and, in the same line, the Duke's Nurse at Tarnhall, so Waldo is the Duke of Tarnwald, whom the other pages name only by his title; Tarnhall is his hall, where a boy of eleven polishes his eighty-eight swords. Her story is one act, set in the sweating fever of Chapter 9: she lifts a heavy beam into the iron brackets across the infirmary door, and the sheet's caption says She bars the infirmary door out of love. The publication notes say whom she bars in: the nine-year-old Waldo, for the forty days of the fever, and she sets the beam in its brackets as quietly as she can. Its large scene draws her at the door by candlelight, a figure lying in a bed in the dark room beyond, presumably the boy; the sheet does not say on which side of the door she stands. Waldo's own page has him unable to bear a shut door, and neither page says that the two are connected. The other Tarnwald people on file are Berthar, Gerold, Odilo and the Duke's Pages. Her expression panels run Love (Silent), Grief (Contained), Resolve (Quiet) and Tenderness (Memory), and in all of them her face is tear-stained. Written from the sheet and the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Her age of 40, her Frankish origin in Tarnwald, broad strong build of about 5 ft 5 in, red-cheeked grim tender face, brown eyes, brown hair beneath a wimple and coarse wool gown and apron are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/nurse-of-waldo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Eschwin Aunt","u":"characters/eschwin-aunt.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Old aunt of the Countess · came up from the south · 74 years old by her sheet","x":"Comes up from the south in a litter to scold the Countess, touching her jewelled pin as she does. The old aunt of the Countess Adalgund, who comes up from the south in a litter to scold her. Theudebald's page gives Adalgund the title Countess of Eschwin, and the Gazetteer puts Eschwin on the south bank; the sheet does not say whether the aunt is on the King's side or her mother's. The publication notes say what the scolding is about: in Chapter 13, before the Ordeal, she tries and fails to persuade Adalgund to take off her pruning-hook. Her sheet draws her small, bent and sharp-faced, in dark violet wool and a grey-white fur wrap, white hair under a linen cap, a carved ebony cane in one hand and the other at a jewelled pin that she touches when she scolds; its scenes, Scolding Adalgund and She came from the south to scold the Countess, show her in the litter among cushions speaking to a fair young woman in blue who stands before her, two guards looking on. Adalgund's own sheet draws a tall, plain woman in grey, not a fair young one in blue; the page records both and does not reconcile them. Her expression panels run Disapproval, Scolding, Frustration and Worn Resolve; her pull-quote is the sheet's own line. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Her age of 74, small bent build of about 5 ft 0 in, sharp lined disapproving face, white hair, pale hazel eyes, wrinkled brown-spotted skin and faint white lip down are the sheet's own. Came up from the south in a litter to scold her.","i":"images/characters/eschwin-aunt-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Carpenter","u":"characters/carpenter.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Stooped carpenter with a bag of chisels · unnamed · 60 years old by his sheet","x":"Carries a bag of chisels and a mallet, is missing the top joint of a finger, and leaves a doorway empty. A stooped, long-armed old carpenter in a grey wool cap, sawdust-pale and patient, with the top joint of his left forefinger missing, a leather apron with dust in every fold and a pouch of chisels and a mallet at his belt. His sheet gives him three expressions, Patience, Quiet resolve and Careful strength, and one caption, He leaves the doorway empty, under a scene in which he stands in a stone doorway looking out over a town and a church spire; his large panel draws him bending to lift a plank against a heavy door. The publication notes give him two doors and an empty frame. In Chapter 12 he takes the door off the royal bedchamber (Theudebald's page has it stood in the corridor, where it stays for eleven days); in Chapter 18 he lifts the Guildhall door from its hinges and stands in the empty doorway, hanging nothing in it. The sheet's caption, He leaves the doorway empty, is that last act; it names no king, door or hinge. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 60, stooped long-armed build of about 5 ft 6 in, lined patient clean-shaven face, sparse white hair, faded blue eyes and sawdust-pale skin are the sheet's own, as are the worn brown leather apron and grey wool clothes.","i":"images/characters/carpenter-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sacristan","u":"characters/sacristan.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Sacristan of the cathedral · unnamed · 52 years old by his sheet","x":"Stands close in the porch with a ring of keys and a candle-snuffer, and watches what others forget. The sacristan of the cathedral: Frankish, pale, thin, with a grey tonsure and brown eyes, who holds a great ring of keys in both hands and has a bell-shaped candle-snuffer among his tools. His sheet's line is Standing close in the porch, he watched what others forgot, and its caption is He records what others overlook; the scene draws him in the cathedral porch beside the open door while a figure in a blue fur-collared cloak crosses the green beyond, a white goose near him. The sheet does not name that figure, and the white goose is presumably Prior, who follows the Guildmaster to the cathedral. The cathedral's porch is where, on the Duke's Pages's page, the Duke of Tarnwald, Waldo, stands through the Ordeal behind the two pages who hold the west doors; the publication notes give what the Sacristan records there, that the Duke never took his eyes from the open door (Chapter 13), and, of the Guildhall yard, that the Guildmaster's weeping over his goose scandalized the whole yard. The cathedral's other servant on file is the Sexton, who has a sheet of his own at fifty-seven, and this page does not join the two. His expression panels run Attentive Watchfulness, Silent Duty, Quiet Disapproval and Grave Recollection; his pull-quote is the sheet's own line. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 52, Frankish origin, pale complexion, thin build of about 5 ft 9 in, grey tonsure, clean-shaven thin-lipped face and dark charcoal gown are the sheet's own. Standing close in the porch, he watched what others forgot.","i":"images/characters/sacristan-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Rothari","u":"characters/rothari.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Hierarch of Auron in Marnholt · presides over the Ordeal of the Third Stroke · 58 years old by his sheet","x":"Preaches that Solan's fire is not to be addressed by strangers, weighs both blades at the Ordeal, and carries the bedding himself. The Hierarch of Auron in Marnholt, the Father's high priest: Auron governs heaven, law, kingship, judgment and oaths in the Threefold Crown, and Rothari has presided eleven times over the Ordeal of the Third Stroke on the black oak Altar of Oaths. The other pages give him three moments. He preaches before a full cathedral on the text that Solan's fire is not to be addressed by strangers, and Waldrada's Nephew carries the sermon to Tomobe no Higaide at the door of the ox-stall. At the Ordeal Ansbert tells him that both blades are Whole and that he cannot say which is the better, and he finds, in the Eleventh Clause of the Third Book that Chlodio wrote, that if both blades are sound the cause is not for the altar. And after the Law of the Oath-Blade is repealed he declares void the verdict of forsworn that the Landing's Priest gave against the stranger of Case 2231, with the words He was merely early. The publication notes add that he preaches against the Low Forge and shelves the Guild's referral of it as a case of sorcery (Wolfram's page has the masters vote to make it). His sheet adds a scene the other pages lack, He Carries the Bedding: the Hierarch in full vestments, mitre and all, going down a dark stair with a great bundle of bedding in his arms. The publication notes say that the bedding is the household's and the stair the almonry stair, and that he carries it up; the sheet draws the stair going down, and the page keeps both. Its expression panels run The Hierarch's Voice, Measured Judgment, Unshaken Resolve and Mercy, Unadorned. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages and the Threefold Crown lore already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 58, tall upright stature, fair handsome face, full silver beard, long silver hair, blue-grey eyes, ivory and antique-gold vestments, stole with a gold raised-hand embroidery and the Book of the Law are the sheet's own. He was merely early.","i":"images/characters/rothari-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Theodard of Lorrenne","u":"characters/theodard-of-lorrenne.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Knight of Tarnwald · champion at the Ordeal · later holds the forty-first sword of the Order · 27 at the Black Eel, 28 on the Bridge","x":"Breaks a blade at a wayside Third Stroke and takes no joy in it, confesses in the cathedral that he does not believe the Law can decide, and kneels on a bridge to return his sword. A slim, upright, fair knight of Tarnwald, second cousin of the Duke of Tarnwald on his mother's side, who is champion at the Ordeal. His sheet tells his whole story in six short lines. At the wayside Third Stroke he breaks Yoake and takes no joy in it. At the cathedral he confesses that he does not believe the Law can decide. On the bridge he kneels and returns the sword. He carries it away from his body like a debt. His bow is very bad, late and correct. Yoake is Higaide's long curved sword, broken in Chapter 8 at the wayside Third Stroke at which the Landing's Priest sits as judge and declares Higaide's oath forsworn. The sheet draws his own sword as a Low Forge blade, the forty-first, wrapped in linen, and notes that he later holds the forty-first sword in the Order; the Order is presumably the Order of the Hundred and Forty-Four, whose Charter Ricbert drafts for a hundred and forty-four knights, one sword to each. The Ordeal is the one at which Rothari presides and which Chlodio's Clause decides, and Theodard is the Duke's champion there: the publication notes have him cross blades thirteen times with Ebbo of Eschwin, the Countess's champion (Chapter 13); the Black Eel is the name on the inn sign in Ragnulf's yard. His expression panels run Duty, Fear, Doubt and Repentance, and his closing caption is He kneels on the bridge and returns the sword. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age (27 at the Black Eel, 28 on the Bridge), slim build of about 5 ft 11 in, fair skin that goes pale when he is afraid, neatly cut light-brown hair, clear grey-blue eyes, clean-shaven face, plain mail, white linen surcoat with a white tree on a blue shield and blue Tarnwald cloak are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/theodard-of-lorrenne-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Chlodio","u":"characters/chlodio.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Lawyer to the First King · author of the Eleventh Clause of the Third Book · 61 at death, dead some three hundred years","x":"Writes, three hundred years before the war, that when both blades are found sound the cause is not for the altar. A dead man whose pen decides the Ordeal: the lawyer to the First King, dead some three hundred years by the time of the case, who wrote the Eleventh Clause of the Third Book. Rothari's finding on the black oak Altar of Oaths, that if both blades are sound the cause is not for the altar, is the Clause's work, and the sheet's own note, Decides the Ordeal (Chapter 13; Interlude III), says the same. The Digest of the Eleventh Clause that Sigebert Marl sells the Chancellor Ricbert and the Duke's steward Odilo after the Ordeal, four pages for a mark, is a commentary on it, and Ansbert, standing by the north pillar, is the one who tells the Hierarch that both blades are Whole. The sheet says its portrait is reconstructed from a name and a clause, so the face, the age and the robe are the sheet's, not any record's. It draws him as a spare, narrow-faced man with close-cropped white hair, grey eyes and thin lips, in a charcoal-black robe of Roman cut and sandals, with a stylus-callus on the middle finger; one scene shows him Before Writing: He Rules the Margin, ruling a line on a wax tablet at a desk, and another, The Eleventh Clause: “Both Blades Found Sound”, shows him presenting the tablet to a crowned man in a candle-lit hall, with two swords and the tablet drawn in a small panel beside it. His expression panels run Precision (Measured), Pride (Legal Certainty), Doubt (Theoretical Edge) and Cold Resolve (The Clause). His pull-quote is the sheet's own line. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages and the Threefold Crown lore already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 61 at death, his spare build of about 5 ft 8 in and pale ink-stained skin are the sheet's own. Both blades found sound.","i":"images/characters/chlodio-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Adalgund","u":"characters/adalgund.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Countess of Eschwin, later Queen of Austrenne · daughter of King Theudebald · 37 years old at the crowning","x":"Is named heir with a pronoun that could mean either, asks an ox its name, and is crowned with a plain iron circlet and a pruning-hook in place of a sword. The daughter of King Theudebald, Countess of Eschwin, the county of the south bank where, on Brun's page, the vines come down to the river, and after her father's death Queen of Austrenne. In the succession war she is the claimant against the Duke of Tarnwald, and she is crowned on the cathedral steps (Chapter 17). Her sheet draws a tall, plain, strong-armed woman, sun-browned at the nose and forearms, with no veil or paint, short brown hair cut at the nape, a long jaw, level hazel-brown eyes and knuckles scarred by grafting-knives. Its line, Named heir in the last month of the thirty-second year, is Theudebald's own date: in the last month of the thirty-second year he names her heir with a masculine pronoun that in the Latin of the old law stands for either, and the licence for the Low Forge's swords is granted in advance to her, at a hundred and forty marks a blade under the heading Repairs to the Eschwin Bridge. At the King's last interview she is alone with him and a boy holding a basin (Boy with the Basin); it is reported that he asked whether she wanted it and that she said she did not. After his death she tells the Chancellor Ricbert that she will swear her case on a sword at the Ordeal and let the Law decide it, and when he says that he has no form of words by which one knight might be two she answers Then count him twice, Chancellor. She asks the ox Brun's name, lays a bare hand flat on his flank for longer than courtesy requires, and later tells him that she does not want it; Hiltrude's answer is that nobody who wanted it would have asked his name. As Queen she gives Wolfram the office of Keeper of the Floor, takes the ox into her pension and stands with her scarred hand on his flank when he dies. Her sheet's closing scene, Her hand rests on the ox's flank, draws it: a woman in mourning grey with her palm on a great dun beast and armoured men standing behind her. Her old aunt comes up from the south to scold her and fails to persuade her to take off the pruning-hook before the Ordeal (Eschwin Aunt, whose sheet draws the woman she scolds as fair, young and in blue, not as this one; the page reports both), her steward is Aldemar, and Ebbo of Eschwin, her father's marshal on the south bank for twenty years, is her champion at the Ordeal. The sheet also draws the regalia, a plain iron circlet and an iron pruning-hook, the second captioned A pruning-hook in place of a sword, and a blade wrapped in oiled cloth, captioned The fifty-fourth blade. Aldemar's sheet has the steward carry the fifty-fourth sword to the Low Forge, and the publication notes list the fifty-fourth blade, carried in oiled cloth, among her own equipment, beside a Low Forge sword at her hip; it is hers, though neither page says what becomes of it. The notes add that she distributes the swords by lot among her household and commands the defence of the south bank, and that she later founds the Order of the Hundred and Forty-Four. Her expression panels run Plain Truth, Grief for Her Father, Determination and Crowned Queen. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Her age of 37 at the crowning, her tall plain build, the mourning grey wool and the dark iron are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/adalgund-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Waldo","u":"characters/waldo.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Duke of Tarnwald · younger brother of King Theudebald · 52 in the second year, 53 on the bridge","x":"Cannot cross his dying brother's threshold until the door is taken off, and carries the blue curtain out of the room. The Duke of Tarnwald, younger brother of King Theudebald, whom the other pages name only by his title; his name is the one his nurse's sheet gives (Nurse of Waldo), and his own sheet confirms it. It draws a long, stooped man of about six feet with a handsome, tired, clean-shaven face, receding pale grey-blond hair, washed-blue eyes, pale northern skin and a cough that comes with the damp. Its pull-quote, A shut door could not be borne, answers to Theudebald's page: on the twenty-first of Brayen the King sends for his brother, who has not entered his palace in six years and who stops at the door of the room and cannot come in, and a carpenter takes the door off its hinges and stands it in the corridor, where it remains eleven days. The King makes the brothers swear peace on the ninetieth blade, which lies on the coverlet between them. In the war he is the principal belligerent: the publication notes have him buy the first eighty-nine swords and march south with the Grey Lances, Hagen Brandt's company. The sheet's closing scene is captioned He carries the blue curtain from his brother's deathbed, with the line Rooms hung with curtains, no doors: the Duke stands in a dark curtained room with a rushlight in one hand and a heavy blue curtain over his arm, and a close-up of the cloth is captioned Deathbed Keepsake. The other pages show him at one remove. At the Ordeal he stands in the porch behind the Duke's Pages, in a plain grey cloak and without a sword. His steward Odilo arranges for things to be done twice; his captain Gerold carries his order to the Low Forge; and on the Uncounted Day he passes through the gate toward the bridge, hears the great gate bar behind him, and says Open it. A boy of eleven polishes his eighty-eight swords every morning at Tarnhall (Boy with the Basin). His nurse bars him, at nine, in the infirmary for forty days of the sweating fever (Chapter 9); the pages do not say that this is why he cannot bear a shut door. Others of his household on file are Berthar and his two sons and Theodard of Lorrenne, his second cousin. His expression panels run Pride (Ducal Bearing), Unease (Open Door), Grief (Brother's Deathbed) and Resolve (On the Bridge). Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age (52 in the second year, 53 on the bridge), his stooped build, the Tarnwald-blue day dress with smoke-grey fur, the fur-lined gown worn by night and the rushlight are the sheet's own. A shut door could not be borne.","i":"images/characters/waldo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Gundobad Rotger","u":"characters/gundobad-rotger.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Eleventh Guildmaster of the Hammer-Oath Guild of Marnholt · nineteen years in office · Keeper of the Mallet in the Order · 60 years old","x":"Offers a smith the mastery in return for the method, stammers when he is lied to, and weeps over geese, not catastrophes. The Guildmaster of the Hammer-Oath Guild: a broad, heavy man of sixty with grey thinning hair, a close-trimmed grey beard, a deeply lined pale face, slow grey-brown eyes and a bad hip that has him leaning on a stick, whose stammer, the sheet says, returns when he is lied to. It counts him the Eleventh Guildmaster, nineteen years in office, and draws him at the Proof with the Guild's mallet, a plate-wide steel face on an ash-wood haft, and in the muted-blue sash of the feast-day. The other pages give him less than the sheet does. Ruodhard's page has the old Proof-keeper serve the Guild before Gundobad Rotger takes the mallet; Ansbert's has him offer the smith the mastery, a house in the Middle Town and the forge rebuilt in stone in return for the method; Wolfram's has the eleven masters vote seven to four to lay the Low Forge before the Hierarch as a case of sorcery, with the Guildmaster refusing to cast his own. The sheet's own lines are about geese. Its caption He weeps over geese, not catastrophes sits under a scene of him crouched in a yard with a white goose held to his chest among others of its kind, and a small portrait labelled Prior, the goose introduces Prior, who has a sheet of her own and who was hatched in the Guildhall yard the spring his master took the mallet. The last scene, Closing the Guild's Last Ledger, draws him at a desk with both hands flat on a closed book; Nailer's page has Sigebert Marl staying behind to wind up the Guild's books on the day it adjourns, and this page does not say how the two scenes fit. A line on the sheet gives him a later office, Keeper of the Mallet in the Order (Epilogue iii); the Order is presumably the Order of the Hundred and Forty-Four. The Boy with the Cracked Knife brings his knife to a retired Guildmaster to ask whether it is Whole; the publication notes set that scene in Chapter 10, where the Guild closes, and the retired Guildmaster is presumably this one, though the sheet's old man has a long white beard against this man's close-trimmed grey. His expression panels run Courtesy, The Stammer Returns, Grief for Geese and Last Ledger. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 60, broad heavy build of about 5 ft 9 in, pale ash-grey skin that goes greyer in distress, grey-brown eyes, charcoal and weathered-grey coat and muted-blue sash are the sheet's own. He weeps over geese, not catastrophes.","i":"images/characters/gundobad-rotger-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Lantfrid","u":"characters/lantfrid.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Apprentice of the Low Forge · 14 at the opening, 16 at the close","x":"Sweeps the black powder into the crucible by accident, teaches a master to sweep, and keeps a copper penny in his left cuff. The apprentice of the Low Forge: a thin, high-shouldered boy with a narrow, eager, easily frightened face, brown hair with a stubborn cowlick, long wrists coming out of sleeves too short and fair skin smudged with soot. The other pages give him four moments. His besom sweeps the black powder off the anvil and into the crucible, and the blade that comes out rings like a bell (Ansbert). He opens the door of the forge to Wolfram on the night of the rain, and in the war teaches the struck master the sweep: the elbow, the slant, the bristles trailing. And on the Uncounted night, when the ox Brun groans as the Duke's guards take him by the arms (Gerold), he runs for the bridge. Brun's page has Higaide bow to the boy and then to the ox, the cold hearth and the anvil, and later carry the boy's body up the steep lanes of the Middle Town, with Hiltrude's words at his side, Feed the ox; Wolfram's page leaves a space at the end of the anvil at the burying, on the left, where a long thin hand with a cowlick over it would have been. A copper penny in his left sleeve, kept through the war and returned by Hiltrude to Higaide on the Uncounted Day, is on Waldrada's Nephew's page the coin Higaide gave the other boy for the news of a sermon; the manuscript does not say how it passed from one boy to the other, and the sheet draws it at his left cuff. The Sentry at the River-Gate lets “the boy from the forge” pass at night and the Young Levy Archer looses at a running figure on a bridge; neither sheet names the boy, and this page does not say that either is Lantfrid. The sheet draws him with a birch besom taller than himself, and bent over a hearth sweeping coals; its last scene, The single object of Ansbert's uncomplicated love, has the smith, bearded and smiling, lay an arm across his shoulders in the forge-light. Its expression panels run Eager, Frightened, Learning Wolfram's Stroke, with a small hammer in his hands, and Quiet Affection; Wolfram's page has the boy teach the sweep, and the page does not say that the teaching ran one way only. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His ages of 14 at the opening and 16 at the close, thin high-shouldered build, brown eyes and worn umber, charcoal and leather-brown clothes are the sheet's own. The single object of Ansbert's uncomplicated love","i":"images/characters/lantfrid-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Hagen Brandt","u":"characters/hagen-brandt.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Captain of the Grey Lances for twenty-eight years · warden-captain of the Nine Arches · 50 years old","x":"Cannot write, so puts his mark to the Duke's contract, and says in the doorway of the Low Forge that the Company is nobody's and is sitting. A heavy, squat man of fifty, about 5 ft 7 in, with cropped grey hair, a close-shaven, weathered, deeply lined face, brown eyes, old pale scars and a bad back, and, his sheet adds, no writing. He is the captain of the Grey Lances, the mercenary company of eighty whose clerk is Wido: at the Salt Landing in the third week of Lorren he puts his mark to the Duke of Tarnwald's contract with the eighty-first name still to be entered, and the last of his sheet's physical notes is that he cannot write. On the Uncounted night the Company sits in the mud at the door of the Low Forge with its captain in the lintel, and when Gerold says that he has the Duke's order to take the household and would thank the Company to stand up, Brandt answers We are nobody's, and we are sitting. Wido's page has him ask the clerk what day it is, hear that it is the Uncounted and that no contract can be signed upon it, and sit down on the threshold, the eighty lances sitting after him. When the Order of the Hundred and Forty-Four is founded he lends Wido to the Crown for the day and does not get him back. His sergeant is Ragnulf, and the One-eyed Lombard is a lance of the Company. The publication notes give him twenty-eight years in command, and have him offer Higaide a post with the words I pay by the day, and I don't ask what a man swore, keep the Company out from under roofs, and take off his hat for no one but the foreigner and the Countess. The sheet's scene At the Low Forge draws him on a plain chair in a timbered doorway, a battered hat held on his knees; its close-up, Hat (Coins sewn in lining), shows three coins stitched into the lining. The other, He Holds the Bridge, draws him braced on the parapet of an arched stone bridge with a long sword in his hands and a castle on a cliff behind, and a label beside it reads Warden-captain of the Nine Arches. The Nine Arches are the bridge whose gate-tower, at its north head, is where Gerold's page puts the Duke's gate-guard; the publication notes put the post in Chapter 18 and do not say what holding the bridge costs him. His expression panels run Wariness, Resolve, Weariness and Mercy. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 50, squat heavy build, cropped grey hair, brown eyes, rough work-worn hands, patched brown leather over charcoal wool and brimmed hat are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/hagen-brandt-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Odilo, Steward of Tarnwald","u":"characters/odilo-4181.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Steward of the Duke of Tarnwald · not the Hierarch Odilo of Cases 2231 and 0202 · 44 years old","x":"Buys the Guild clerk's tally, buys back the past along the Aure marches, and says a single word at the gate without saying which door. The Duke of Tarnwald's steward, the dry man of Gerold's page who sees that a thing will be done by arranging for it to be done twice: a neat, spare Frank of Tarnwald of about 5 ft 8 in, with grey-brown hair combed flat, an indoor-pale clean-shaven face, pale grey eyes and ink-stained fingers, a roll of accounts under his arm. He is not the Hierarch Odilo of Cases 2231 and 0202, whose page belongs to another man. The other pages give him two kinds of business. He buys information: Sigebert Marl sells the Duke's steward a tally of eighty-nine swords for sixty marks and, for forty, arranges for two of his clerks to go over the old wall behind the forge. And he buys back the past: in the second year he rides the Aure marches, takes the sword that cost the Rich Merchant's Son his horse, and returns the horse with a polite note; the Low Forge blade on the Mended Stirrup's sheet, Sold Back to Odilo, Thirty Times the Price, is his purchase (Chapter 9), though the pages do not say that it is the same blade as the one he takes from the Rich Merchant's Son. On the Uncounted night he sends Gerold with twenty men to the Low Forge, as a precaution. On the Uncounted Day, a quarter of an hour after the Duke (Waldo) has passed through toward the bridge, he sees three barges of the Countess's levies coming down under the north bank toward the water-stair, and says a single word to the captain of the gate-tower, close. He means the little door at the foot of the stair; he has not said which door, nor bar, and the great gate shuts for the first time in three hundred years. The sheet's scene, He says “close” at the gate, draws the moment: the steward stands at an iron-bound gate with a hand raised while torchlit men in helmets and spears file past beyond it. The sheet does not say which door he means. The publication notes add that he bids at every candle sale, plans the Uncounted Day gambit and walks, never rides, into battle, and that the text twice puts twenty-two years on his service. Berthar's notes have him stay behind in Chapter 16 to bury the steward, presumably this one. His pull-quote, He finished their sentences in his head, is the sheet's own line, and his expression panels run Calculation, Patience, Quiet Resolve and Cold Appraisal. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 44, Frankish origin in Tarnwald, neat spare build of about 5 ft 8 in, grey-brown hair, pale grey eyes and dry grey coat are the sheet's own. He finished their sentences in his head.","i":"images/characters/odilo-4181-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Aldemar","u":"characters/aldemar.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Steward of the Countess of Eschwin · 48 years old","x":"Carries the fifty-fourth sword to the Low Forge with a writ-tube on his baldric and a white face. The steward of the Countess of Eschwin, Adalgund: a thin, slightly stooped man of forty-eight with a narrow, pale, anxious face, thin sandy hair combed flat, pale blue eyes and a clean-shaven chin, in a long grey-green wool coat with a leather writ-tube on a baldric and an inkhorn at his belt. Sigebert Marl's page has the Countess's steward pay forty marks for the Duke's order to take the Low Market, and does not name him; this is the only sheet of a steward of hers, and this page takes him to be that steward. The sheet's large scene is captioned Carries the fifty-fourth sword to the Low Forge: he walks toward the glow of the forges with a cloth-wrapped blade held in both arms, and a figure in a fur-trimmed cloak walks beside him. The sheet names neither the figure nor the owner of the blade, but the publication notes say that he accompanies the Countess to the Low Forge and carries the oiled bundle of the fifty-fourth sword. The figure in the fur-trimmed cloak is therefore presumably Adalgund herself, and the blade is hers: her own sheet draws a fifty-fourth blade in oiled cloth, and Theudebald's page has the treasury pay a hundred and forty marks a blade for hers under the heading Repairs to the Eschwin Bridge. The notes add that the leather tube on his baldric holds the writ of licence. His expression panels run Anxious, Formal Composure, White-Faced at the Forge and Careful Resolve. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 48, thin slightly stooped build, ink-pale skin, sandy hair, pale blue eyes and grey-green wool are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/aldemar-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ebbo of Eschwin","u":"characters/ebbo-of-eschwin.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Sir Ebbo of Eschwin · Marshal of the South Bank · 51 years old","x":"A vineyard-knight who has never drawn his sword on anything more dangerous than a boar, and who prays to Elara and asks for nothing. A grizzled, limping knight of fifty-one, about 5 ft 9 in, sun-browned and weathered, with cropped grey hair, a short grey beard, brown eyes and a quiet, long-suffering expression, in a household coat over leather and plain worn mail. The sheet gives him as Sir Ebbo of Eschwin and Marshal of the South Bank, and the publication notes make him her father's marshal on the south bank for twenty years and the Countess Adalgund's champion at the Ordeal. Eschwin is the county of the south bank, hers, and the household coat is hers. Its pull-quote, A vineyard-knight who had never drawn his sword on anything more dangerous than a boar, is the sheet's own line; Eschwin is the county where, on Brun's page, the vines come down to the river. Its two scenes are devotional. After the Thirteenth Stroke draws him bowed over a sword set point-down before him, both hands on the hilt and his brow almost on his knuckles, with banners behind and the label Low Forge Sword; the thirteen strokes are the Ordeal's own: in Chapter 13 he crosses blades thirteen times with Theodard of Lorrenne, the Duke's champion, while the Proof, on the other pages, is three. He prays to Elara and asks for nothing draws him kneeling at a wayside shrine at dusk, a lit niche in the stone and his sword on the ground. Elara is the Mother of the Threefold Crown, goddess of earth, birth, harvest and hearth. His expression panels run Duty (Steadfast), Pain (The Limp), Resolve (Thirteenth Stroke) and Faith (Quiet Prayer). Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 51, grizzled limping build, sun-browned face, cropped grey hair, sword-worn hands and worn grey steel mail are the sheet's own. A vineyard-knight who had never drawn his sword on anything more dangerous than a boar.","i":"images/characters/ebbo-of-eschwin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Peire","u":"characters/peire.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"A man of the High Ground · Higaide's friend · 27 years old","x":"Was a boy of eight when the stranger came out of the Stone, and is the man who receives his goodbye before the walk east. A broad, sun-browned man of twenty-seven with light stubble, thick brown hair tied back with a cord, grey eyes and an open, steady face, in an undyed wool tunic and brown leather. The sheet gives him two lines, A Man of the High Ground and Higaide's Friend, and one scene, He receives Higaide's goodbye before the walk east: he stands on a hillside at dusk, with a hill-town and its tower behind him, facing a dark-cloaked figure with a braid seen from behind. It does not say what is said. The publication notes call him an Occitan man of the High Ground, Lasserra, and place the goodbye in Chapter 3. Case 2231's Peire is the younger child of the miller's widow Ermengarda and brother to Gersenda, eight when Higaide comes out of the Stone: he watches with a switch in his hand, and at the weighing he claps a hand over his mouth to keep from laughing. Eight, and the nineteen years that the Gazetteer gives Higaide on the High Ground, the hamlet the survivors of Vilarnau built on the ridge, make twenty-seven, and that case's manuscript agrees: its Peire is twenty-seven on the morning he walks beside Higaide to the boundary-stone of the charcoal-burners' land and watches him go east. This page takes him to be that man, though the two sheets differ: this one gives grey eyes and no scar, where that one gives hazel eyes and a scar through the left eyebrow, which the manuscript says he carries from a cracked skull as a boy. The Gazetteer has Higaide leave the High Ground in the third week of Brayen, going east. The sheet draws him with a pen behind his ear, ink-stained fingertips and an ink-pot and knife on his belt, and a small panel of a hand writing with a pen beside an ink-pot; it does not say what he writes. His expression panels run Pride (Quiet), Concern, Resolve and Farewell. Written from the sheet, from the Case 2231 pages and the Gazetteer, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 27, broad build of about 5 ft 9 in, sun-brown skin, brown hair, grey eyes and undyed wool and brown leather are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/peire-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Landing's Priest","u":"characters/landings-priest.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Priest of Auron's temple at the Salt Landing · judge at the Third Stroke · 55 years old","x":"Little, fat, damp and hurried from his supper, he judges a Third Stroke by the shore and declares Higaide's oath forsworn. The priest of Auron's temple at the Salt Landing, the river-mouth hamlet of thirty huts and a hundred salt-pans where Sunnhild keeps the pans and Hagen Brandt puts his mark to the Duke's contract. Auron the Father governs heaven, law, kingship, judgment and oaths in the Threefold Crown. The sheet draws a short, round man of fifty-five, about 5 ft 2 in, with flushed pink, clammy skin, a damp face, thin white hair plastered to the scalp, a clean-shaven chin and pale blue eyes, a greasy napkin still tucked at his collar over a sodden stole, and a hurried, supper-interrupted look. Its two scenes are captioned Judge at the Third Stroke and He Declares Higaide's Oath Forsworn: he stands between two men at a weathered stone cross by the sea and then reads from a sheet of paper, the two men flanking him. The publication notes set him as judge for the wayside Third Stroke in Chapter 8, with his stole and two witnesses, and have him declare Higaide's oath forsworn. It is the stroke at which Theodard of Lorrenne breaks Higaide's sword Yoake, and the verdict is the one Rothari's page has the Hierarch declare void, once the Law of the Oath-Blade is repealed, against the stranger of Case 2231: He was merely early. The sheet does not say which oath is judged. His expression panels run Hasty Concern, Uneasy Judgment, Embarrassment and Damp Relief. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 55, short round build, flushed pink skin, thin white hair, pale blue eyes and sodden brown-grey wool with a stole in subdued sacred tones are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/landings-priest-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Prior","u":"characters/prior.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"The Guildmaster's goose · 19 at the opening, 21 at her death","x":"Hatched the spring her master took the mallet, guards the Proof-hall door in a fury, and is buried in the forge yard. A domestic goose, white, enormous for her kind, about three feet tall with her neck raised, her neck long as a lance, with a flat orange bill, orange legs and one flawed, glassy dark eye. She belongs to Gundobad Rotger, the Guildmaster, and her sheet's tagline is Hatched in the Guildhall yard the spring he took the mallet. She is nineteen at the opening, and the Guildmaster's sheet gives nineteen years in office, so the two dates agree. Her sheet draws her as the guard of the Proof-hall: the full figure stands before a great iron-bound door, and a note on the back view reads Waits here each morning. In Furious Vigil she stands with her wings spread and her bill open against that door; in Following Gundobad to the Cathedral she walks behind a hooded, cloaked man up the cathedral approach. The publication notes give the other scene of her life, in Chapter 5: she is pacified by Higaide's bow, made slowly from the waist with his eyes lowered, and lowers her wings by a hand's breadth at a time. The cathedral's Sacristan has a white goose near him in the porch on his sheet, presumably this one, and the publication notes have him record that the Guildmaster's weeping over his goose scandalized the whole yard. The last scene, Grave in the Forge Yard, draws her lying beside a small headstone cut PRIOR, a fire glowing in a doorway beyond. The sheet gives her age at death as twenty-one, and the notes place her death in Chapter 13; its panel Fading (Last Winter) has her head sunk on her breast. The notes say only “the forge yard”, and neither they nor the sheet say which forge's it is. Her other panels are Pride (Territorial), Wrath (Wings Raised) and Vigil (At the Door). Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Her ages of 19 at the opening and 21 at death, her white feathers with silver-grey shadows, orange bill and feet and dark flawed eye are the sheet's own. Hatched in the Guildhall yard the spring he took the mallet.","i":"images/characters/prior-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Berthar's Two Sons","u":"characters/berthars-two-sons.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Sons of Sergeant Berthar · unnamed · ages 22 and 19","x":"Buried side by side in the Hohlweg ditch; neither had seen an enemy. The two sons of Berthar, sergeant of the Tarnwald household, whose own sheet has him bury them on the Hohlweg. The elder is twenty-two, about 5 ft 9 in and lightly bearded; the younger is nineteen, about 5 ft 8 in and freckled. Both are broad-shouldered and square-jawed, with iron-fair cropped hair, pale grey eyes, wind-reddened skin and their father's bandy stance, and both wear the Tarnwald-blue tabard over grey-brown wool. The sheet gives them one place and one end. In the Hohlweg camp before the flux took them sits them side by side on a bench, hands loose between their knees, with their spears left in the camp rack behind; the last scene, Buried side by side in the Hohlweg ditch, draws two shrouded forms in a grassy trench, with the line neither had seen an enemy. Their father's sheet says only that he buries two sons and does not say how they died; this one says the flux. The Hohlweg is the road on which, on Ansbert's and Brun's pages, the household finds the stranger. Neither son is named on either sheet. Their expression panels run Brotherly Pride, Camp Weariness, Fever (The Flux) and Quiet Resolve. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. Their ages of 22 and 19, their builds, their iron-fair hair and pale grey eyes and the Tarnwald-blue tabards are the sheet's own. neither had seen an enemy","i":"images/characters/berthars-two-sons-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sentry at the River-Gate","u":"characters/sentry-at-the-river-gate.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"A sentry of the river-gate, with a son of his own · unnamed · 41 years old","x":"Lets the boy from the forge pass the river-gate at night, with a lantern and a father's face. A stout, kindly, bearded man of forty-one, about 5 ft 8 in, with brown hair and beard, hazel eyes, ruddy skin, a dented kettle-helm, a spear and a quilted gambeson in the Countess's colours, muted blue-grey and wine-red. The river-gate is the gate whose seal is on the plain gold ring King Theudebald gives Ansbert, and which Sigebert Marl will follow the hawker to and no farther. The sheet's tagline, A sentry of the river-gate, with a son of his own, is the whole of what it says about the man; the publication notes add that he lets the boy through at night having been told that the boy is “a sort of lucky piece” (Chapter 15). Its scene, He lets the boy from the forge pass at night, draws him stooped with a lantern toward a small hooded boy with a pack, beside a dark river and a walled town with towers across the water. The sheet does not name the boy: on Brun's page the apprentice Lantfrid runs for the bridge on the Uncounted night, and this page does not say that the boy is he. The gambeson's colours are the sheet's own note, and the Countess is Adalgund, Countess of Eschwin; the sheet does not say whether the sentry is one of her levies, and Gerold's page has three barges of the Countess's levies come down under the north bank on the Uncounted Day. His expression panels run Kindly, Wary, Duty and Fatherly Concern. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 41, stout build, kindly bearded face, brown hair and beard, hazel eyes and ruddy skin are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/sentry-at-the-river-gate-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Boy with the Cracked Knife","u":"characters/boy-with-the-cracked-knife.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"A boy in a leather apron · unnamed · about 10 years old","x":"Brings a cracked-handled kitchen knife to the retired Guildmaster and asks whether it is Whole. A skinny boy of about ten, about 4 ft 6 in, with an earnest, snub-nosed, soot-smudged face, dark uncombed hair, warm brown eyes and bare feet, in a leather apron too large for him over a patched tunic. The sheet's line is He brings a cracked-handled kitchen knife to the retired Guildmaster and asks whether it is Whole. “Whole” is the word of the Proof: Ansbert tells the Hierarch at the Ordeal that both blades are Whole, and the Guild's ledger has a column headed Whole and a column headed Broken (Rich Merchant's Son). The sheet's close-up shows the handle, a split along its grain. Its one scene, The Last Proof, with the line He receives the last Proof ever recorded, draws the boy holding out the knife across a table to an old white-bearded man in a dark gown in a room of shelves and ledgers. The sheet does not name the old man; the publication notes call him the retired Guildmaster and set the scene in Chapter 10, where the Guild closes. The Guildmaster on the other pages is Gundobad Rotger, whose own sheet closes the Guild's last ledger, and he is presumably the man the boy comes to, though the sheet's old man has a long white beard against his close-trimmed grey. This page does not say what the Proof finds. His expression panels run Hope, Uncertainty, Reverence and Quiet Relief. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of about 10, skinny build, pale skin under the soot, dark brown-black hair, warm brown eyes and worn leather in muted umber and grey-brown are the sheet's own. asks whether it is Whole","i":"images/characters/boy-with-the-cracked-knife-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Barge-hand","u":"characters/barge-hand.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Barge-hand from the river-mouth · unnamed · 24 years old","x":"Crouches on the pier at the river-mouth with tar-black fingertips and names the black grain the Night-Seed. A wiry Frank of the Salt Landing coast, twenty-four, about 5 ft 7 in, with a narrow, wind-cracked face, sandy hair tied back with a cord, grey-blue eyes, patchy stubble, salt-burnt skin, tarred forearms and tar-black fingertips, in a knitted cap, an oiled wool jerkin and canvas breeches, his barge-pole stowed outside the frame. The Night-Seed is the first of the names the powder acquires in the first winter, given from a cousin's tale in Chapter 6, in the room of Waldrada's Three Sheaves that the Old Sot's page describes; the sheet's scene is Naming the Night-Seed, and it puts him at a pier rather than in a tavern. In At the River-Mouth he crouches on the planks with something black between finger and thumb, boats on the water beyond; in Naming the Night-Seed he studies black grains in his palm, with a small faint sketch of a hooded face beside him that the sheet does not caption. The river-mouth is the Salt Landing of the Gazetteer; the sheet does not say who hears the name. His expression panels run Wary, Amusement, Suspicion and Recognition. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 24, wiry build, grey-blue eyes, sandy hair, salt-pale weathered skin and tar-dark wool and faded canvas are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/barge-hand-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Carter","u":"characters/carter.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"A carter of the Aure road · patron of the Three Sheaves · 38 years old","x":"Calls the black powder the Widow's Ashes, and is laughed at when he says the secret is probably the dust. A broad, red, genial, gap-toothed carter of thirty-eight from Marnholt in Austrenne, about 5 ft 9 in, with matted brown hair, brown eyes, a thick brown beard, weathered skin, a whip-scar across his left hand, a mud-spattered wool tunic, leather gaiters and a short carter's whip at his belt, a regular at Waldrada's Three Sheaves. The other pages give him one phrase: in the first winter of the black salt a carter has called the powder the Widow's Ashes (Carter's Wife, Old Sot by the Fire), and the sheet's large scene is captioned He names it “the Widow's Ashes”. Whether the carter's wife whose page is on file is his wife, the sheets do not say. The publication notes add that the manuscript may mean one carter or two, one who names the powder and one who, drunk and generous, says that the secret is probably the dust and is laughed at; the notes merge them into a single drinker, and so does this page. Beside that scene the sheet gives him a second line: He suggests the secret is probably “the dust” while others laugh. It draws him among laughing men beside a loaded cart and its horse, a mug in one hand and the other arm thrown out; a smaller scene has him leaning back against a cart, laughing, with the mug raised. His expression panels run Genial, Drunk and Generous, Dismissed by Laughter and Road-weary. The carter on the sheet is a man of the Aure road, and the sheet names the Three Sheaves as his tavern; the journeyman cooper, who calls the powder a splinter of Solan's fire, is the other voice of that room whose phrase survives. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 38, broad build of about 5 ft 9 in, ruddy weathered skin, matted brown hair, brown eyes and mud-brown wool with dark leather are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/carter-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Drover from the North","u":"characters/drover-from-the-north.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"A cattle drover from the north · unnamed · 45 years old","x":"Meets a pale, silent foreigner and names him the Moon-Man. A lean, wiry Frank of Tarnwald, forty-five, about 5 ft 10 in, with a long weather-beaten face, pale grey eyes, red-brown skin, greying stubble and wind-tangled hair, in a peat-dark wool cloak over off-white sheepskin, with a staff worn smooth, hobnailed boots and a knife in his boot. The Gazetteer has him in Cooper's Row at dusk, seeing the foreigner standing beside a cart as though waiting for it to speak, and naming him the Moon-Man; the Old Sot's page has the name go round the room of the Three Sheaves twice and be understood to have been always known. The sheet's scene, He names the pale, silent foreigner the ‘Moon-Man’, is set somewhere else: a hillside, cattle behind him, the drover facing a cloaked figure with a pack, long pale hair under a grey hood. The sheet does not name the figure; the foreigner of the other pages is Higaide, and the publication notes list the Moon-Man among his aliases, with the Eastman and the Schoolmaster, and say that the drover names him from his pale long face and his silence. His expression panels run Watchful, Suspicious, Dry Amusement and Naming the Moon-Man. Written from the sheet, from what the other Case 4181 pages and the Gazetteer already say, and from the character notes in the case's publication-prep file; the manuscript itself was not to hand. His age of 45, Frankish origin in Tarnwald, lean wiry build, long weather-beaten face, pale grey eyes and peat-dark wool with sheepskin are the sheet's own.","i":"images/characters/drover-from-the-north-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Black-Salt Peddler's trestle","u":"records/the-black-salt-peddlers-trestle.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Ansbert, Donkey","x":"A middling man in a brown cap tips the mouth of a linen sack toward a thick-set man in a brown coat over a plank trestle laid with cakes of red ochre and small pots, the thick-set man drawing a finger through a heap of dark powder, a small grey donkey standing asleep behind them beside a stone horse-trough, stallholders waiting in the mud and a pale city wall under a grey sky At grey dawn in the Low Market the Black-Salt Peddler loosens the thong of a linen sack, and Ansbert draws one thick forefinger through the dull black powder and leaves a hair-thin silver line along the trestle's edge; the hawker's donkey sleeps on its feet behind the peddler. Ansbert pays nine pennies, the month's stall-rent, for the sack.","i":"images/scenes/the-black-salt-peddlers-trestle-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"One hundred and forty-four heaps","u":"records/one-hundred-and-forty-four-heaps.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Hiltrude, Ansbert","x":"A woman in a white headcloth and a floury apron bends over a plank table covered in small level rows of dark powder, a horn spoon and a knife laid beside her and an open linen sack at the table's edge, a bearded man in brown sitting by the wall with a bowl on his knee, a ladder to a dark loft and a rabbit hutch behind them in the amber light of a single candle In the evening light of the lean-to Hiltrude counts the sack into a hundred and forty-four heaps of black powder with a horn spoon and the flat of a knife, her lips moving, while Ansbert sits at the settle by the wall with a bowl of barley on his knee.","i":"images/scenes/one-hundred-and-forty-four-heaps-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The man who bowed to the ox","u":"records/the-man-who-bowed-to-the-ox.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Higaide, Brun, Lantfrid, Lo Rog","x":"A tall lean man in a mended brown tunic with his hair tied up and two swords at his belt bows from the waist, palms flat against his thighs, to a vast shaggy dun ox with a white sock whose great horned head is lowered to his chest, a thin fair boy holding the reins of a loaded cart watching open-mouthed, in a sunken forest lane between banks of exposed roots under a thin fog In the fog of the Hohlweg, Tomobe no Higaide bows from the waist with his hands flat on his thighs to the ox Brun, who lowers his great head until his wet nose is a hand's breadth from the stranger's chest and breathes out into his tunic; the cart-boy, presumably Lantfrid, watches open-mouthed. It is the bow that the ox Lo Rog was given in Case 2231.","i":"images/scenes/the-man-who-bowed-to-the-ox-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The broom and the crucible","u":"records/the-broom-and-the-crucible.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Lantfrid, Ansbert, Higaide, Brun","x":"In a low forge glowing orange with sparks in the rafters, a young apprentice with tousled hair sweeps black powder off the edge of a bare anvil with a birch besom into the open mouth of a clay crucible in a bed of white-hot coals, a smith in a brown apron turned away at the fire and a tall grey man with a topknot beside a great dun ox at the treadwheel The powder goes into the crucible by accident: Lantfrid, frozen mid-stroke, has swept it off the anvil's edge with the besom held under his right elbow, while Ansbert tends the fire with his back turned and Higaide lowers his eyes in tact beside Brun at the treadwheel.","i":"images/scenes/the-broom-and-the-crucible-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Third Stroke in the Proof-Hall","u":"records/the-third-stroke-in-the-proof-hall.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Gundobad Rotger, Ansbert, Higaide, Sigebert Marl","x":"In a tall whitewashed hall with a gilded ceiling and a tall arched window, a broad grey old man leaning on a stick stares at the steel face of a mallet in his fist beside a black oak block with a sword clamped to a post, two men in long brown and grey coats standing rigid before him, rows of masters seated on benches along the wall and a figure at a lectern at the far end In the Proof-Hall the Guildmaster Gundobad Rotger stares at the bright crescent bitten into the steel face of the Guild's mallet while the sword in the Proof-Post is still singing; Ansbert stands rigid in his too-tight coat, Higaide looks at the floor behind him, and Sigebert Marl holds a wax tablet at the lectern.","i":"images/scenes/the-third-stroke-in-the-proof-hall-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Prior and the bow","u":"records/prior-and-the-bow.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Prior, Higaide, Ansbert","x":"A tall lean man in a mended brown tunic with a leather satchel hugged to his chest bows low on a pale stone step to an enormous white goose with wings half-raised and bill open before a great oak door, a rough-coated man in leather falling back off the step with a wrapped sword in his arms, a man peering from the doorway holding his hat, and the roofs and smoke of a town in the bright air below On the step of the Proof-Hall door Prior, the Guildmaster's goose, stands with her wings half-raised and her bill open over the doormat, and Higaide bows slowly from the waist with his eyes lowered until she lowers her wings a hand's breadth at a time; Ansbert leaps back with a wrapped sword on his arm, and a junior master in the doorway holds his breath.","i":"images/scenes/prior-and-the-bow-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"What the taverns called it","u":"records/what-the-taverns-called-it.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Waldrada, Barge-hand, Carter, Journeyman Cooper, Carter's Wife, Drover from the North, Old Sot by the Fire","x":"A crowded low-beamed taproom lit by firelight and candles, a stout woman in a white apron laughing behind the bar at the left, a ruddy man in a red shirt standing over a table with one hand raised, a ring of men and women in brown leaning in around the table over pewter mugs and a sheet of paper, an old man bent by the fire and a rain-streaked window with a sign In the Three Sheaves on a winter night Waldrada wipes a mug behind the bar while the room gives the black salt its names: a barge-hand's Night-Seed, a carter's Widow's Ashes and a journeyman cooper standing on a bench's splinter of Solan's fire, with a carter's wife leaning in, a drover from the north naming the stranger the Moon-Man, and an old sot dozing by the fire.","i":"images/scenes/what-the-taverns-called-it-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The end of the shingle","u":"records/the-end-of-the-shingle.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Higaide, Sunnhild","x":"A tall grey-streaked man in a dark mended robe with his hair tied back sits on a pebble spit at the water's edge at dusk holding a single folded sheet of pale paper in both hands, looking out along a flat shore of rectangular salt-pans where a distant figure rakes brine, gulls wheeling in a cloudy sky over the grey water At the end of the river, at dusk, Higaide sits on the shingle at the very edge of the water with a single worn sheet of folded paper in both hands, not unfolded, looking east; far along the shore the pan-master Sunnhild rakes brine among the salt-pans of the Salt Landing.","i":"images/scenes/the-end-of-the-shingle-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Yoake breaks","u":"records/yoake-breaks.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Theodard of Lorrenne, Higaide, Landing's Priest","x":"In a wet inn yard at night under dripping thatch and hanging lanterns, a tall grey-streaked man in a rain-soaked robe crosses swords flat to flat with a young fair knight in a blue cloak while the upper half of his curved sword spins away toward the black cobbles, a stout priest in a stole raising one hand between them and a ring of onlookers holding lanterns In a wet inn yard at night Theodard of Lorrenne crosses his plain straight blade with Higaide's long curved sword Yoake, and Yoake breaks a hand's breadth above the hilt; the Landing's Priest raises a hand to judge, and the knight's face shows no triumph.","i":"images/scenes/yoake-breaks-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The doorless armory","u":"records/the-doorless-armory.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Waldo","x":"A long stone armory at night with a heavy curtain drawn aside from the entrance, tall black oak racks along the wall holding rows of identical plain swords with small tags that catch the rushlight in a long gleaming line, and a stooped man in a fur-trimmed gown walking the row alone with one hand held back At night in the armory at Tarnhall Waldo, Duke of Tarnwald walks the row alone, counting his eighty-eight identical swords, one hand not touching; the entrance has a curtain and no door.","i":"images/scenes/the-doorless-armory-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The bedchamber with no door","u":"records/the-bedchamber-with-no-door.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Theudebald, Adalgund, Carpenter, Waldo, Ricbert, Rothari","x":"A heavy grey-bearded old man lies propped on pillows in a carved bed beside an open window over a brown river, a sword on the coverlet under his hands and those of a short-haired woman in grey seated at his side, a man on a stool in the empty doorframe, a carved door leaning against the corridor wall and two robed men waiting at the corridor's end In the bedchamber of the dying King Theudebald lies on four pillows with a sword on the coverlet between his hands and his daughter Adalgund's, the window open on the river; the door has been taken off its hinges by a carpenter and stands in the corridor, and in the empty frame the Duke, Waldo, sits on a stool looking at his brother while the Chancellor Ricbert and the Hierarch Rothari wait at the corridor's end.","i":"images/scenes/the-bedchamber-with-no-door-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Thirteen strokes at the Altar of Oaths","u":"records/thirteen-strokes-at-the-altar-of-oaths.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Theodard of Lorrenne, Ebbo of Eschwin, Rothari, Adalgund, Ansbert, The Duke's Pages","x":"In the crossing of a vast stone cathedral under coloured light, a young knight in blue mail and an older one in a patterned surcoat cross swords flat to flat above a long black altar scored with fine grey lines, a white-bearded priest in white and gold holding an open book aloft behind it, a woman in grey with a hook at her belt and a broad man in a blue sash standing at either side, crowds filling the nave and a small figure standing in the bright open doors far down the nave Over the black oak Altar of Oaths Theodard of Lorrenne, the Duke's champion, and Ebbo of Eschwin, the Countess's, cross blades flat to flat through thirteen strokes while Rothari holds the Book of the Law open at arm's length; Adalgund stands at the altar steps, Ansbert by the north pillar in his blue sash, and far down the nave, in the porch between the two pages who hold the west doors open, the Duke never takes his eyes from the doorway.","i":"images/scenes/thirteen-strokes-at-the-altar-of-oaths-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"We are nobody's, and we are sitting","u":"records/we-are-nobodys-and-we-are-sitting.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Hagen Brandt, Higaide, Lantfrid, Brun","x":"On a starlit night rows of cloaked spearmen sit silent in the mud along a lane beside a timber lean-to against an old city wall with their spears grounded, a heavy scarred captain seated on the threshold with two swords laid at his feet, a bareheaded tall man in grey standing in the doorway bowing with empty hands, a boy with a broom behind him and a great dun ox looking over a stall partition On the Uncounted night the Grey Lances sit in the mud before the Low Forge with their spears grounded, and Hagen Brandt sits on the threshold with two swords laid beside him, hilts to the road; Higaide stands bareheaded in the doorway with empty hands, bowing, with Lantfrid and the ox Brun behind him. We are nobody's, says the captain, and we are sitting.","i":"images/scenes/we-are-nobodys-and-we-are-sitting-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Nine Arches","u":"records/the-nine-arches.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Higaide, Hagen Brandt, Waldo, Adalgund","x":"On a mist-wreathed stone bridge of many arches at morning, a lean man in a dark robe with a sword lowered in each hand and a stain on his sleeve stands on the roadway between rows of unhorsed knights in mail seated along both parapets with their hands held out, dead horses across the road, a double line of spearmen behind him under a gate-tower and a stooped rider in a blue cloak on a tall grey horse coming toward him At the crown of the bridge Higaide stands with a sword lowered in each hand and a stain on his sleeve that is not his own, while thirty-four unhorsed knights of Tarnwald sit along the parapet with their hands held out before them and the Grey Lances stand in a double line behind him; the Duke, Waldo, rides slowly toward him with a long blue curtain across his saddle-bow, and far down at the south head Adalgund waits with an oiled bundle in the crook of her arm.","i":"images/scenes/the-nine-arches-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The Sable Skin in the Hall","u":"records/the-sable-skin-in-the-hall.html","m":"Case 4181 · The Sable Skin","d":"Hiltrude, Wolfram, Higaide","x":"In a long whitewashed hall under a gilded ceiling, benches curve in a great spiral around an empty place on the bare flagstones, an empty grey linen sack hanging from an iron crook above a black block in a shaft of light from a high window, rows of knights in good coats seated with swords across their knees, a woman in an apron holding out empty hands at a doorway with no door and a lean man with a birch broom beside her In the Proof-Hall the empty sack hangs on its iron crook above the black oak block, turning in the draught from a doorway with no door, while a hundred and forty-three knights sit in the spiral of benches with their swords in their hands and the hundred and forty-fourth place stands empty on the flagstones; Hiltrude holds out her empty hands at the threshold, Wolfram begins his first long sweep of the floor, and Higaide stands at the back with his hands in his sleeves.","i":"images/scenes/the-sable-skin-in-the-hall-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Uncounted Variable","u":"books/0277.html","m":"Case 0277 · Awaiting release","d":"Arithmetic. Shrimp. Unimportance. Twenty-One. Chancellor.","x":"Case 0277"},{"k":"s","t":"Hildegund","u":"characters/hildegund.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Princess of Vahlreth, later Queen Hildegund the First · seventeen on the night of the fall, twenty-six when she walks back up the north road, thirty at her crowning · carrier of the Hereditary Marker · keeps a private ledger of what she owes","x":"Goes left in a burning corridor, learns ledgers in a windowless room off a treasury, and spends thirteen years teaching an ordinary mind to do slowly, on purpose, what her blood once did without asking. The daughter of Sigward, seventeen and not yet anointed on the night the duke's men take Vahlreth, and, by the law called the Unbroken Blood, the one person alive whose breathing keeps Otwin's claim void. She runs with two guards down a corridor the smoke has begun to rearrange, and the thing that has lain asleep in her blood for seventeen years wakes. The Hereditary Marker does not show her the future. It strikes through every branch that ends with her dead and leaves her the one number remaining, and the number is left. At the foot of the second stair Gero has already decided to stay; the word that would have made him come with them, three syllables, is half formed behind her teeth, and she says nothing, and spends a good part of the rest of her life deciding whether that was wisdom or cowardice dressed in a crown she had not yet grown into. A third arrow, Hagen's, finds her low in the side at the second river, and the number turns northeast, toward a thread of smoke a nurse once called, when she was nine, the pale hermit's fire. Vara opens the door before the second knock and says one word, In. She lies three days in fever, relives four deaths she did not die, and when she can stand tells him You will come with us. It is not a question; she has not, in seventeen years, needed the practice of asking for things twice. At Gertrud's table she has her first ordinary evening, and at the ford, where twenty men wait, the old machinery does not seize the problem at all: she has already answered the question it exists to ask by trusting the man walking ahead of her, a pattern tested three times and unbroken, and the manuscript is plain that this is data and not faith, and that she takes less comfort from that than everyone else does from the warmer explanation. At Ulrich's court she asks for her kingdom back and is told to learn how kingdoms work. She is given a windowless room off the treasury, a stool and a clerk, Oderic; she loses her first negotiation to Osgerd having conceded every point that mattered and one that was not even in dispute; a levy she recommends bankrupts a minor lord, and the apology she writes him is never answered. In her fourth year she reads a ledger the way her blood once read the corridor, for the gap, and ruins Baldric in public rather than quietly, because the granary office needs the lesson; she learns from Reinolt's season of silence afterwards that competence and decency are not entries in the same ledger. By her sixth year she has a faction, and a habit she would have called an exercise: buying Vahlreth's grain-price rolls from the autumn factors and tabulating them by lamplight, because a ledger from home is the one she never learns to stop reading. Twice she orders the mule saddled for the north road, and twice, unable to compose the first sentence to a man who asked for nothing and would therefore have nothing to refuse her, sends it back to its stall. At twenty-six she rides north at last, and the visit costs two of her men-at-arms, Wendel and Berthold, to whose widows she writes in her own hand, and brings twelve of Otwin's men out of the trees. The attempts that follow are the ones the manuscript tells in turn. At the spring banquet she feels a wrongness in the wine and walks to the window, not the door. On the water-stair it is her dread of stairs that gives the old machinery something to work on. On the eastern road the blood gives nothing, because fear needs a moment to gather and the wheel allows none. And on a night in high summer it reaches her through a dream, moves her six inches to the left, and she wakes into a gloved hand and screams. When Frida confesses, Hildegund's first question is whether the sealed account is still in Otwin's strongroom and whether a copy could surface by a route that leads back to no name. The plan that follows is hers: the account laid before Ulrich, six chancelleries' worth of ink laid beside it, and the choosing of the day and the road left to him. She keeps Frida as a watched channel whose every report is true and unhelpful, and has the account read aloud before the combined councils of both kingdoms, at her own insistence, which costs her the one private fact about herself she has kept for thirteen years. She meets Otwin across a plain table, opens not with the eleven years but with his own treasury's numbers, and tells him You have run this kingdom better than my father did, which she is careful to make plain is not forgiveness. Then she says My father, and nothing after it. He neither defends nor denies, and she enters it in a ledger no one else will read as the one line the appointment will not close. She is crowned on the fourteenth of Eldren in Vahlreth's cathedral before the Vigil's own altar, in a ceremony her nine-year-old self would have found unbearably plain, and her first act is to name Otwin her Chancellor. What she has by then is a colder, learned cousin of the old machinery: an ordinary mind trained, slowly and on purpose, to do what the blood once did in half a heartbeat. Years on, she visits Vara at his new river shack half hoping to talk him into a title, court alchemist or royal physician, anything that would let her put his name on a payroll and call the debt closed, and understands, watching him refill a kettle, that she never had a chance of winning that negotiation. He asks Are you the queen now? Before she leaves he asks whether she knows of the Observer, and she does not. The manuscript gives her no face, hair or colouring at all; every physical detail on her page is the sheet's. It draws her dark blonde and grey-eyed, fair-skinned and slender, with a small faded scar at her side from the arrow, as a fugitive of seventeen in a braid and a borrowed dress, at twenty-six, and as a queen in court dress with a small circlet. The circlet is the manuscript's own. You have run this kingdom better than my father did.","i":"images/characters/hildegund-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Vara","u":"characters/vara-0277.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"“The hermit” for nearly the whole of the case, named once · looks no older than thirty · has not aged in several hundred years · a long way from anything he might once have called home","x":"Closes a poisoned wound by firelight, hands a girl a bowl of peas to shell, and keeps back, until a river forces him, a speed no one is meant to be able to measure. A pale hermit who lives in a shack of his own building in the forest north of the old Muhlbach road, with a fire that never seems to need feeding and a bend in the river he favours for shrimp. He looks no older than thirty and has the stillness of a man trained, at some point he has no intention of discussing, to stand very still right up until the moment he no longer needs to. He opens the door before the second knock to a wounded girl and a guard, says In, closes her wound with a paste the colour of river clay, and lies awake afterwards over the nine miles of trackless dark she crossed to find a door she could never have known existed. Everyone calls him the hermit. When Emmerich goes looking, Vahlreth's records turn him up across four generations, as the solitary tenant of the northern clearing and, in a tax dispute older than Otwin's dukedom, as a tenant who “paid nothing, aged not at all, and could not be made to answer for either”. He is not what Reinolt decides he is, a disgraced soldier with a taste for fishing. He has built an entire method, over more years than the kingdom has existed, out of never giving anyone a true number to work from: no speed they can clock, no strength they can measure, no healing they cannot explain away as skill. At the ford, against twenty men, he lets it go. For him it is not fast so much as uncrowded, the world arriving one clean decision at a time, the nearest thing left to him, after this many centuries, to peace; for the men on the other side it is an arithmetic error that someone else has made. Afterwards he kneels in the mud rubbing a knee that has not ached in several hundred years and lets Reinolt nearly laugh. He keeps an older caution too, one he has never explained to anyone on this world: certain workings he will simply not do here, because they leave a mark on the world's own quiet hum that travels further than any army and might be answered from somewhere worse than a usurper's soldiers or an alien auditor's ledger. He has broken a smaller rule for Hildegund twice, and knows that the distinction between a small rule and the one rule that matters is doing more work than it can reasonably hold. Reinolt rides north twice a year for nine years to sit by his fire and say almost nothing, and becomes the closest thing to a friend the hermit has allowed himself in longer than the kingdom has had a history. When Hildegund finally comes, with Frida and six men, he offers tea before chairs and river shrimp salted with a thrift she now recognises. Twelve of Otwin's men come out of the trees and none walks back into them. He had chosen that clearing before any of them was born, for a river that asked nothing and a quiet that resembled a valley on a world he has not stood on in longer than the kingdom's language has existed to measure distance, and he does not rebuild it a second time. On the road back he gives them his name, once, the way a man sets down something heavy he has carried so long he has nearly forgotten its weight. He throws up a one-room shelter outside Hartenmark's walls, close enough that Reinolt will not need two hours to fetch him, and cures the wine fever in less time than the physicians needed to name it. He is three valleys away trading salt for fish-hooks when the flagstone is loosed and sleeping uncommonly soundly half a mile off on the night the Hush climbs through a window nobody watched, and the manuscript's point is that no shelter is close enough to solve for a blade he cannot see. When Otwin's letter comes, asking one honest question and waiting for the answer, he does not write back, because he cannot recall the last time anyone simply asked him something and then waited. He walks to the duke's gate alone. Asked whether he knows who it is he is protecting, he says Some nobleman's daughter, I think. She's a good kid, and then, revising for accuracy rather than effect, I'm not protecting her. Just helping her, is all. He is, to a king who spent a fortune preparing for a kingmaker, “somebody's favorite uncle”. Invited to Hildegund's crowning, he asks Will there be shrimp? and, told there will not, stays by the river to build a second trap. He lives in the shelter for as long as her life needs a nearby door, and one morning the fire is cold and the single room swept clean. Years later he builds another shack at a bend in a river not unlike the one he lost. The last thing he says to Hildegund, and the last the manuscript has him say on the subject, ever, is a question about whether she knows of the Observer. The Auditor has by then confirmed his true name and entered it nowhere but a private unfiled line. Another file keeps a hermit of the same name, who looks about thirty, is pale and white-haired and eats river shrimp, at Val Muta in Case 0202 (Vara). Neither manuscript says they are one person; this page treats them as the same figure on the strength of the name, the shrimp and the sheets, and that is an inference. The manuscript gives no hair or eye colour. The sheet's white, unstyled hair, pale-grey eyes, clean-shaven face and plain taupe, charcoal and grey-green clothes are its own, and it draws him unarmed and unarmoured, as in every fight in the text. I'm not protecting her. Just helping her, is all.","i":"images/characters/vara-0277-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Otwin","u":"characters/otwin.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Duke of Vahlreth, then its usurper king, then Duke again, then Hildegund's Chancellor · raised in the Faith of the Threefold Crown · mid-to-late forties by his sheet","x":"Inherits a physician's sealed account, spends eleven years and a fortune trying to prove it on a girl, and is undone by a hermit who calls her a good kid and a ledger that will not balance. A duke of Vahlreth who has believed for eleven private years in a document: a sealed account, written two generations back by a physician history did not trouble to name, of a thinness in the blood that makes its sufferer act in fright as though she knew what she could not. The one other case the physician knew was a minor lordling who survived an assassin's blade nobody could explain, governed the next decade on the settled conviction that whatever protected him would go on doing so, and cost his holding two ruinous wars and a famine. Otwin's conclusion, built into eleven years of certainty, is that the blood does not make a person dangerous, it makes a sovereign reckless, and a reckless sovereign on his border is a patient kind of catastrophe. He cannot act while Sigward sits healthy on the throne. When Sigward has been dead three months, poisoned so cleanly that every faction reads the physician's report as proof of its own murder, he knows that the Unbroken Blood, the law that voids a ducal claim for as long as one drop of the king's line breathes uncrowned, leaves a girl of seventeen as the one obstacle to his throne, and he comes anyway. The reign that follows is competent, by every honest measure more competent than its beginning deserved, with fair harvests and kept roads and a border watched with a fear he shares with no one below his most trusted spies. He is a man of the Faith of the Threefold Crown, which his house has kept through four generations of nominal Vigil piety the way other houses keep an unmarried cousin, and on one sleepless night he kneels, badly, not to Auron but to the unhastening goddess of the kingdom he took by force, for no more specific mercy than the understanding he has spent eleven years and a great deal of blood pursuing. The manuscript counts seven attempts. It tells of an arrow poisoned by Hagen, twenty men at a ford, twelve in a clearing after Frida's letter, a bribed under-cook, a loosened flagstone, a carriage wheel and, for a sum greater than the twelve men and the border company together, the Hush. Behind the twenty-one dead at the ford and the clearing there are names he has made a private point of learning and has never told a living soul that he knows. When force fails he turns to records and patience: Emmerich, who finds the same pale hermit in the registers across four generations, and Rosalind, who spends forty-one days near him and returns confessing that she has stopped being curious. From the pairing of the blood and the hermit he draws one conclusion more confident than the facts support, the kind a ledger invites when it balances too easily on the first pass: that the girl's luck and the hermit's nature are one phenomenon wearing two faces, and that removing the hermit will remove the certainty. He is afraid, for the first time in a long career of being right about difficult things, of what the thread is attached to. The flagstone and the carriage wheel, both of which the hermit was nowhere near, verified and re-verified by watchers Otwin trusts, are the facts his theory has no room for. After the third failure he keeps a private tally more honest than any council report, not of dead assassins but of small deniable defections: Erchanbald's younger son at Hartenmark's university under a borrowed name, a guild's letter congratulating Hartenmark on a trade agreement nobody has finished negotiating, a Keeper asked three times in one week whether patience might run out on a usurper as readily as on anyone. A king can execute a traitor; he has no instrument for a loyal man quietly buying grain in a rival's currency. Ulrich rides to his court five weeks after the Hush on a pretext about grain tariffs, and Otwin sits very still that evening considering how much of two decades of believing in his own righteousness was a story smaller and more human than the one he told himself. Six chancelleries seal a recognition that changes nothing on the map and everything in the great houses, where the lords who called him king for a decade begin, in the careful deniable way such things begin, to call him merely Duke, a demotion of exactly one word. He writes with his own hand to the one address in the kingdom he has come to trust more than his council, a hermit he has spent two years hunting and never once thought simply to ask: whether a man who has spent two years and a small fortune trying to kill a young woman might be permitted, at this humbled stage, to put one honest question to her guardian instead. The hermit comes on foot. Otwin asks whether he knows who it is he is protecting, and is told she is a good kid and that the hermit is only helping her. The one thing more he asks is the question the account taught him to fear above all others: not whether she is dangerous but whether she is careless. The hermit says she asks what a thing will cost and then asks again, and has never in all the years he has known her accepted the first number, and the theory has nowhere left to stand. It takes Otwin three days, the manuscript says, to finish grieving eleven years of being wrong; he then writes to Ulrich, unsealed, by a herald in his own colours. At the table with Hildegund he does not defend the poisoning of her father and does not deny it, which she takes for the only honest answer he has left, and he accepts the Chancellorship in writing, through a channel that leads, for the first time, to his own hand. She tests him once, six months in, with a fabricated grain shortfall in a province that does not exist; it surfaces in none of his old channels, he understands what has been done within a week and says nothing for the rest of his life. He serves, by every surviving account, with a scrupulous, almost penitential competence until the end of both their reigns. The manuscript gives him no appearance. The sheet's mid-to-late forties, short brown hair going grey at the temples, groomed greying beard, dark fur-collared royal dress with a chain and a ceremonial sword are its own.","i":"images/characters/otwin-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Reinolt","u":"characters/reinolt.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Guard of Vahlreth, later master-at-arms to Queen Hildegund · six years on her guard when the palace burns · early twenties at the opening by his sheet · keeps a garden behind the old armory","x":"Is sick in the bracken after Adalger dies, tells Hildegund the truth about her wound, and keeps a garden behind the old armory that no one else at court knows exists. A guard of the royal household who has watched over Hildegund for six years when the palace burns, and who goes through the postern at her shoulder with Adalger three steps behind. At the second river an arrow takes him in the shoulder, a scar he will carry and be almost proud of. What he cannot put down is kneeling afterwards in the wet bracken and being sick, not from the wound but from watching a man he had shared a mess table with for six years fold sideways into the mud without any of the ceremony the songs would later give him. His hands do not stop shaking until well past the river, and he tells no one, which the songs also fail to mention. When Hildegund asks how bad her own wound is, he says Bad enough that lying to you would be its own kind of cruelty, and tells her the rest plainly: that he does not expect her to see the following sunset. He is the first to understand what the ford will cost, and does not learn what the hermit is for a great many years. He builds, over many nights, a theory in which Vara was once a soldier of real skill, a disgraced knight or a mercenary who lost his taste for killing, and the manuscript calls it “entirely reasonable” and “entirely wrong”. At Hartenmark's gate the hermit gives him instructions and no keepsake: stop picking at the cut in his forearm, and tell no one, not even the girl he is sworn to protect, how he got it, since the truth would only compete with a better story already halfway invented. Over the nine years that follow Reinolt rides north twice a year to sit by a fire and say almost nothing to a man who says almost as little back, the most restful arrangement either has access to anywhere in the world. At Hartenmark he is the one who asks, the night before she lays the granary embezzlement before the council, whether she means to ruin Baldric quietly or publicly, since the two cost her nothing either way, and who offers no opinion on anything she does for the better part of a season afterwards. In her fifth and seventh winters she orders the mule saddled for the north road and he holds the bridle; both times she says the weather looks uncertain, and he, who knows exactly what the weather looks like, leads it away without a word. Behind the old armory is a strip of ground nobody else at court knows of, where he has grown onions, mint and one stubborn rosebush that has never bloomed properly and that he refuses on principle to dig up. It is the only place his hands can be usefully occupied while the rest of him works out what he wants to say. When the wine goes wrong he is two seats down with his own cup untouched, and it is he who walks out through the gate to fetch the hermit rather than send a rider, since a rider would need telling why. Ulrich forbids the journey outright: Every wall in this city exists to keep her alive. Reinolt answers The walls did not stop the wine, and takes her anyway, the first order of Ulrich's he has disobeyed in nine years, pacing the length of her litter for the whole two hours. When Frida's confession comes he finds her three days later in the garden, asks without looking up from mint he is not weeding how long she has known he comes there, and then the one question he came to ask: whether she loved him for the whole of it, or only some smaller, harder-to-locate part. The whole of it, she says, which is either the truest thing I have ever told anyone, or the most convenient lie I have ever told myself. He says Good, and Neither have I. The rosebush blooms for the first and only time the spring after they marry. He serves as master-at-arms through the whole of Hildegund's reign, and tells the few who ask that a complicated household is easier to guard than a simple one, since everyone in it has stopped pretending to be anything other than exactly as complicated as they are. When Vara asks the queen whether she is the queen now, he very nearly fails to turn his laugh into a cough in time. The manuscript gives him no appearance. The sheet's short dark-brown hair, brown eyes and plain, open face are its own, as is its early-twenties age band; it draws him in guard's mail as a young man and, in his forties, in a dark cloak with a close beard. It labels a forearm scar “poisoned-arrow”, where the manuscript puts the arrow in his shoulder and the forearm cut at the ford. The walls did not stop the wine.","i":"images/characters/reinolt-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Frida","u":"characters/frida.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Chambermaid in Hartenmark's household, a merchant's daughter · Otwin's informant for nine years · counts in a nursery game of sums · later Reinolt's wife · 26 by her sheet","x":"Counts under her breath in a nursery game, writes the letter that puts twelve men in a clearing, and climbs thirteen stairs to confess it. A merchant's daughter with a dowry too small for the husband her family wanted, and a memory, Hildegund will discover over the coming years, considerably better than her own. She has spent three years making herself quietly indispensable around Hartenmark's court when she is assigned to keep a strange half-royal guest's small room decent and her one good dress mended, and she counts under her breath when she thinks herself unobserved: not stairs or sacks but a merchants' nursery game in which every number is the sum of the two before it, one and one and two and three and five and eight, on as far as her patience carries. No one at that court thinks to ask what she is counting toward. It is Frida, not Ulrich or any of his ministers, who tells Hildegund the night after her first great public failure that she lost not because her numbers were wrong but because she recited them like a princess informing a room of her decision rather than a supplicant asking for its help. Hildegund never thinks to ask how a merchant's daughter with no formal education understands court persuasion so precisely, which is, of every arithmetic error she makes, the one she can least afford. She is Otwin's. Her father's debts were bought up one at a time by his steward and forgiven on the day she entered the royal household, long before anyone had reason to think a princess would need watching, and she came to Hildegund's door, in her own account, by “the oldest accident of any court”. For nine years she is both a friend and an informant. She knows from the second year where Reinolt keeps his garden and never tells Otwin; she becomes the second-most-necessary person in Reinolt's narrowed world, a fact the servants of Hildegund's household have known, with the unerring certainty servants reserve for such secrets, for at least the last four of those nine years. Her first report to mention Reinolt's rides north, in Hildegund's fourth year, describes the end of them as a woodsman's hut; that winter a man of Otwin's comes, burns it and finds no one, and her later letter is much more careful about what it names. When Hildegund plans her one private, unofficial errand north, Frida writes the letter anyway. She sits the better part of an evening with a blank page, telling herself that a routine note on a trivial errand is exactly what Otwin's network exists to record and never act on, and chooses to trust the version of Otwin she has spent nine years constructing for her own comfort over the version her judgment keeps insisting is also, and more plausibly, true. Twelve of his men come out of the trees; she makes one involuntary syllable of horror at the first blade clearing its sheath, not the horror of a woman surprised that violence is possible but of one whose fiction has just come apart; and she says nothing for the whole ride back to Hartenmark. Six days later her report to Otwin is longer and more honest than any she has sent, and carries, almost in passing, the single word the hermit gave them on the road. She comes to Hildegund's door three nights after the Hush, in the hour reserved for conversations neither party wants to survive, having counted the stairs the old nursery way and run out of stairs at thirteen with nothing decided. She tells all of it in the flat, careful order of a rehearsed penance, including the sealed account in Otwin's strongroom, which will never leave it and which she has read three times. A memory that can hold a nursery sequence to its thirteenth term can hold forty lines of a physician's hedging, and she writes the account out from memory over a week of evenings, burning each draft in the grate. Hildegund's answer is a question, not forgiveness and not rage, and the next is colder: whether a woman who has spent nine years learning what a man like Otwin wants to hear might not be of more use to Hartenmark as a channel that is watched than as one that is closed. Frida agrees, understanding that she has traded one kind of service for another; every report afterwards is entirely true and entirely unhelpful to the man who receives it. Telling it solves nothing. She had imagined the confession as an ending, a debt paid, and finds that saying it aloud only makes it permanently real. She never stops loving Hildegund enough to wish she had not been given the assignment, which she comes to understand is its own kind of unforgivable, since the years she would have to give back to undo it are the only years of her life she can honestly call happy. In the garden behind the old armory she answers Reinolt's one question with The whole of it, and the rosebush blooms the spring after they marry. The manuscript's Cast List calls her a chambermaid; the sheet's “lady's maid” is its own, as are its age of twenty-six, light-brown hair braided under a linen cap, hazel eyes, freckles and a small plain necklace. The whole of it.","i":"images/characters/frida-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ulrich","u":"characters/ulrich.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"King of Hartenmark, Hildegund's maternal uncle · thirty years on the throne when she arrives · a full treasury guarded like a daughter · keeps an unglamorous study and an unremarkable wine","x":"Embraces his niece before questioning her, refuses her an army, and gives her instead the one sentence she needs: learn how kingdoms work. The king of Hartenmark, a kingdom of grain and ledgers with a walled capital of the same name, a granary office, a merchants' hall and a small, perpetually underfunded university, who has spent thirty years guarding a full treasury as other men guard a daughter, never once being seen to need anything. He has not seen his sister's daughter since she was nine years old and missing her front teeth. He embraces Hildegund before he questions her, which is worth more to her than any number her own blood is capable of producing. His council argues six hours about a war that had in the strictest sense already started three months earlier; he listens to all of it without committing to any of it, and ends the debate with the one exchange the chroniclers preserve word for word, addressed to his niece: You want your kingdom back? Yes. Then learn how kingdoms work. It is, she decides years afterwards, the least comforting and most genuinely loving thing anyone has said to her since the night Vahlreth fell. He does as he says. Within a month she has a small windowless room off the treasury, a stool and a clerk, Oderic, told on pain of his position to treat her as he would any apprentice, and the muster horns do not sound in Hartenmark that season or the next. He watches her build a faction from the considerable distance a king affords himself for such observations, and identifies what he feels, with the dry precision he once used to end a war council, as pride rather than alarm, though he is careful, being a king, to feel it privately. When the wine fever will not break he forbids the journey to the hermit outright, Every wall in this city exists to keep her alive, and when Reinolt takes her anyway he sends a dozen guards, on the single condition that if his niece means to die disobeying him she does it surrounded by every sword he can spare. It is his ride to Otwin's own court, five weeks after the Hush, on the pretext of a grain-tariff renewal neither treasury much cares about, that turns the war into an accounting. He speaks to Otwin alone for the better part of an hour and delivers, on his way out of the door, a single sentence, whose wording was Hildegund's: that Hartenmark has decided to recognise his niece's claim before the courts of six neighbouring kingdoms, not as an act of war, which its treasury cannot afford, but as an overdue correction to the historical record, and that a king curious about certain physicians' sealed family accounts should feel free to satisfy it, since Hartenmark's own scholars have begun compiling a rather thorough one. The recognition costs him ink and an afternoon's ride, and he asks not to be thanked for it, since kings who are thanked for doing the arithmetically obvious thing start to believe they have done something more generous than arithmetic. The negotiation that follows takes the better part of a year, considerably longer than a war would have, a point he has spent a decade proving on purpose. On the evening before her crowning she finds him in the unglamorous study where he has watched her learn what he told her on her first night she would need to learn, and says that she spent a very long time certain he had been wrong to refuse her an army and has spent the last twelve months certain of the opposite. He tells her he is glad the arithmetic has finally caught up with her, and pours them both a second cup of the same unremarkable wine he has been serving at that desk for as long as she has known him. The manuscript gives him no appearance. The sheet's short grey hair, grey eyes and short, well-kept grey beard, and the plain dark robes in which it dresses him for state business, are its own. Then learn how kingdoms work.","i":"images/characters/ulrich-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sigward","u":"characters/sigward-0277.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"King of Vahlreth, dead three months when the story opens · Hildegund's father · poisoned so cleanly every faction read the report as proof · mid-forties by his sheet","x":"Dies so cleanly that every faction reads the physician's report as proof of the murder it already believed in, and leaves a law, a daughter and a bell no one has told the boy to stop ringing. King of Vahlreth until he is poisoned, three months before the siege, so cleanly that the court physician's report, cautious, technical and entirely correct, is read afterwards by every faction as proof of whichever murder it has already decided to believe in. He is never seen on the page. What he leaves is a kingdom with forty quiet years behind it, a daughter of seventeen who is not yet anointed, and the Unbroken Blood, the law that voids any ducal claim for as long as a single drop of his line still breathes uncrowned, which makes the girl the one obstacle between Otwin and a throne the law itself has all but written his name into. On the night of the siege the bells of Vahlreth's own House of Vigil are still ringing the pattern kept for a king's death, because no one has told the boy on the rope that the king in question is three months buried. The manuscript is careful about who set the cup. In Chapter Thirteen the poison in Hartenmark's wine comes from a supplier who had “served him faithfully twice before, once in a king's cup and once on a riverbank”, and in Chapter Twenty-Three Otwin's attempts begin with “a riverbank poisoner named Hagen”; the manuscript's own revision note says Hagen is established as Sigward's poisoner, and this page, like his, reads the chapters together. Years later his daughter sits across a plain table from the man who paid and says My father, and nothing after it. Otwin does not defend it and does not deny it, and she enters it in a ledger no one else will read as the one line the appointment will not close. Not the Sigward of Case 1268 (Sigward, a Triarch of the Threefold Crown), who is another man in another kingdom. The manuscript gives this king no appearance; the sheet's mid-forties, golden-blond hair and short beard, crown, blue-and-gold mantle with a fur collar and sword are its own.","i":"images/characters/sigward-0277-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Gero","u":"characters/gero.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Royal guard of Vahlreth, thirty-one years in service · held the second stair · late fifties by his sheet","x":"Holds a stairwell for four minutes and asks for nothing, least of all the three syllables that would have made him leave. An old man by the standards of his own trade, thirty-one years in the royal guard and unremarkable by his own account, who has guarded three kings he liked rather less than he likes this one's daughter. On the night Vahlreth falls he takes the second stair, having decided sometime in the last hour, without troubling to announce it to anyone, that the arithmetic of his life has come out roughly even. He does not so much fight the first man through the door as simply refuse, with everything left in his body, to let him pass. It costs the duke's men four full minutes. It costs Gero everything he has left to spend. Hildegund has the word half formed behind her teeth, three syllables that would have ordered him to come with them and might have cost all four of their lives rather than one; she says nothing, and Gero, who had not been waiting for permission, would not have thanked her for it regardless. Four minutes, the manuscript says, was enough. She does not weep for him where anyone can see, and tells herself it is dignity and not the plainer truth, that a girl raised to be a queen has been trained out of the habit before she was old enough to notice it going. The Archival Log lists him among the costs of the contested interval as a royal guard. The manuscript gives him his thirty-one years and his stair; the sheet's late fifties, close grey hair and beard, pale-blue eyes, old scars, worn mail with a crowned shield and red cloak, and the line it prints beside him, “Time takes the body, not the oath”, are its own. Time takes the body, not the oath.","i":"images/characters/gero-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Adalger","u":"characters/adalger.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Soldier of Vahlreth, clears the passage at the postern · killed at the second river · mid-thirties by his sheet","x":"Dies mid-sentence at the second river, an arrow in the gap of a cuirass that had fit him perfectly for six years. A soldier who comes three steps behind Hildegund at the postern, clearing the passage of anyone too slow to have already fled it. He dies at the second river the way soldiers rarely die in the songs and more often die in fact: unglamorously, mid-sentence, an arrow finding the gap between two plates of a cuirass that had fit him perfectly for six years and had simply, on this one night, not fit him fast enough. He does not finish telling Reinolt which way the ground slopes; Reinolt finds out anyway, a moment later, the hard way. It is Adalger Reinolt has shared a mess table with for six years, and whose fall, with none of the ceremony the songs later give it, leaves him sick in the wet bracken. Hildegund, who did not weep for Gero where anyone could see, weeps for Adalger exactly once, alone behind the shack, for as long as it takes water to boil, and then goes back inside and asks how far it is to her uncle's border. The manuscript gives him a name and a death and no appearance, and lists him in the Archival Log as a soldier. The sheet's mid-thirties, light-brown cropped hair and short beard, grey eyes, plate armour and red cloak with a stag's head are its own; it calls him a royal guard, where the Cast List says soldier.","i":"images/characters/adalger-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Hagen","u":"characters/hagen.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"The Apothecary · one of Otwin's huntsmen · poison as a question of rates · mid-forties by his sheet","x":"Salts an arrow with enough of his own preparation that a girl running for her life will do the tracking for him. A huntsman in Duke Otwin's service whom the men call the Apothecary without much affection, and who understands poison less as a cruelty than as a question of rates. A wound too clean kills too fast to be of use to a hunting party; a wound salted with just enough of his own preparation kills slowly enough that a girl running for her life does the tracking herself, her collapsing body marking a trail no huntsman's eye could improve. He takes no pleasure in this, precisely. He does take a certain professional satisfaction in it, the way a moneylender takes satisfaction in watching compound interest do work that violence alone could never manage half so cleanly. The third arrow at the second river, which finds Hildegund low in the side, is his: a wound built to be survived just long enough to matter, the skin blackening in a ring no clean blade could cause, the blood coming away thin and strange-smelling, the deliberate slowness of a hand that wanted her walking rather than falling for as long as her legs could manage the trick. He is not seen again, but he is not forgotten. The poison in Hartenmark's wine comes, in Chapter Thirteen, from a supplier who had served Otwin faithfully twice before, once in a king's cup and once on a riverbank, and Chapter Twenty-Three, naming Hildegund's first act as queen, calls the first of Otwin's attempts the work of “a riverbank poisoner named Hagen” whose patient cruelty Otwin went on to refine rather than abandon. The manuscript's own revision note says Hagen is established as Sigward's poisoner. The manuscript gives him no appearance; the sheet's mid-forties, dark hair greying at the temples, narrow watchful face, stubble, forest-coloured hunter's leathers, bow and case of vials are its own.","i":"images/characters/hagen-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Gertrud","u":"characters/gertrud.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Miller's widow of Muhlbach · lost two sons in a border skirmish history did not name · mid-fifties by her sheet","x":"Feeds anyone who comes to her door looking the way she imagines her sons might have looked, had either lived long enough to look tired. A miller's widow in Muhlbach, a village of perhaps forty roofs around a mill wheel that has not stopped turning, by any villager's memory, in eleven decades. She lost two sons to a border skirmish history did not think important enough to name, and decided some years ago to feed anyone who arrives at her door looking as she imagines her sons might have looked, had either of them lived long enough to look tired. She takes in the girl, the guard and the hermit without a single question. It is, Hildegund will reflect many years later, the first ordinary evening of her entire life, no herald announcing the courses, no taster, no third of the table reserved for men whose only function was to watch the other two-thirds, and she does not know what to do with her hands until Vara sets a knife and a bowl of unshelled peas in front of her. Two days behind them a larger, less careless patrol of Otwin's men questions her with a courtesy that unsettles her rather more than threats would. It gets the direction it wants, thanks her, pays for the bread it ate and rides on without overturning a chair, because a village that notices it has been robbed talks, and one visited by polite strangers does not. In the alehouses of Muhlbach, where they had actually fed him, the preferred theory of the hermit is that he was nobody at all. The manuscript gives her no appearance; the sheet's mid-fifties, grey hair under a plain wool headscarf, sturdy thick-armed build and apron, and flour worked into the creases of her hands are its own.","i":"images/characters/gertrud-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Oderic","u":"characters/oderic.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Clerk assigned to Hildegund at Hartenmark · told to treat the princess as any apprentice · early forties by his sheet","x":"Is instructed, on pain of his own position, to treat a king's niece exactly as badly as any other apprentice. A clerk whom Ulrich assigns, within a month of her arrival, to Hildegund, with instructions, on pain of his own position, to treat her exactly as badly as he would treat any other apprentice who made an apprentice's ordinary mistakes. What she is given, within the month, is a small windowless room off the treasury, a stool and him. The manuscript gives him nothing more: no speech, no opinion and no appearance, and the page keeps to that. The sheet's early forties, receding dark-brown hair, narrow unsmiling face, plain dark clerk's robe and ink-stained hands are its own; it draws him standing with an armful of books and again bent over a desk with a pen.","i":"images/characters/oderic-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Osgerd","u":"characters/osgerd.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Eldest guild-master of a merchants' hall in Hartenmark · negotiated with two kings and outlived both of them · late sixties by her sheet","x":"Thanks the princess for the grain tariff with a courtesy so complete it amounts to its own kind of insult. The eldest of four guild-masters across the table in a merchants' hall that smells of tallow and wet wool, who with her colleagues has buried enough failed treaties to paper a small chapel, and who has negotiated with two kings and outlived both. Hildegund walks in with what she still considers a perfectly reasonable position on grain tariffs and walks out three hours later having conceded every point that mattered, and one more, out of what she can only later diagnose as pure nervous generosity, that was not even under dispute. Osgerd thanks her afterwards with a courtesy so complete that it amounts to its own kind of insult, and Hildegund understands, walking back to her small room in the fading light, that she has been handled by a professional the way a professional handles an amateur: gently, thoroughly, and without ever once raising her voice. She does not cry, which she is privately rather proud of, and does not eat, which she is less proud of, until Frida finds her at the window past midnight. The manuscript gives her a name, a career and a courtesy. The sheet's late sixties, white hair in a tight coil, pale-grey eyes, jewelled earrings and dark patterned merchant's robes are its own.","i":"images/characters/osgerd-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Baldric","u":"characters/baldric.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Steward of Hartenmark's granary office · liked far more than he was ever audited · mid-fifties by his sheet","x":"A decade of quiet embezzlement, found by a girl who had learned to read a ledger for the gap. The steward of Hartenmark's granary office, whom everyone at court has liked considerably more than they have ever bothered to audit. In her fourth year there Hildegund learns to read a ledger the way her blood once read a burning corridor, for the number that does not sit quite right beside its neighbours, and uncovers a decade of quiet embezzlement. Reinolt, who spent two winters early in their exile sparring in the same practice yard as Baldric's own son, asks her the night before she lays it before the council whether she means to ruin the father quietly or publicly. She chooses public, on the ground that a public ruin teaches the rest of the granary office a lesson a quiet one cannot, and learns afterwards, from Reinolt's silence, that competence and decency are not entries in the same ledger. Baldric's eldest son leaves Hartenmark's service within the month and does not, in nine further years, fully return, a cost that appears on no account she keeps and that she never forgets. The manuscript does not say how his ruin was carried out. The sheet's mid-fifties, grey thinning hair, round genial face and moustache, good unostentatious clerk's robes and ledger under his arm are its own, with a second panel of him kneeling between guards.","i":"images/characters/baldric-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Under-Cook","u":"characters/the-under-cook.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Kitchen servant at Hartenmark's palace · bribed, frightened and, once caught, willing to talk · late twenties by his sheet","x":"Is placed at the north door of a banquet hall, with a knife, to finish whatever the wine fails to. Somebody's under-cook: bribed, frightened and, once caught, entirely willing to name an intermediary who can be traced, by the same patient, unglamorous work Hildegund has spent nine years learning to do herself, precisely as far as the border and not one step further. He stands at the northern door of the spring banquet for the better part of an hour, not moving, not speaking and never letting the door itself out of arm's reach, a man she has registered only the way a person half-hears a clock; when she walks to the window rather than the door, he would have finished, by his own later and extensively documented confession, with a knife whatever the wine failed to finish first. Reinolt chooses not to tell her so that night, judging it something she does not need to carry on top of everything else. At the room's edge a serving girl turns away with an empty tray and an expression arranged to look like nothing at all; the manuscript does not say who she is. The manuscript describes him only by his job and its outcome. The sheet's late twenties, unstyled brown hair and brown eyes, patchy stubble, kitchen apron over a plain tunic, burn scars on the forearms and a knife under the apron are its own.","i":"images/characters/the-under-cook-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Emmerich","u":"characters/emmerich.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Keeper of the Vigil of Ardwen, archivist to Otwin · thirty-one years cataloguing parish rolls, land grants and registries · late sixties by his sheet","x":"Finds the same pale hermit in three documents across four generations, and cannot make any two of them agree. A Keeper of the Vigil of Ardwen who has spent thirty-one years cataloguing Vahlreth's parish rolls, land grants and Vigil registries with a thoroughness his superiors find faintly excessive and his king, once he finally has use for it, finds indispensable. Otwin, who after Frida's report sends for his own most patient archivist, gets his first answer in nine days: a land grant four generations old exempting “the solitary tenant of the northern clearing” from a tithe nobody has ever successfully collected from him. It takes Emmerich longer, and costs him more private unease, to notice that the description attached to that tenant, pale, unaging, remarkably young for a man already established as a fixture of the district, matches item for item the one in a Vigil chapter's disciplinary log two generations earlier, concerning a hermit some overzealous young Keeper once attempted, and failed, to compel into formal worship. It matches again, further back still, in a single surviving line of a tax dispute that predates the founding of Otwin's own dukedom, in which a neighbouring lord complains that the clearing's tenant “paid nothing, aged not at all, and could not be made to answer for either”. Emmerich has built a career on the comforting principle that any sufficiently patient man can eventually reconcile any two documents. He presents his findings with the visible discomfort of a scholar who has found two that refuse every attempt, and offers Otwin two readings: an unusually persistent family tradition of hermits adopting an identical appearance and manner across four documented generations, or something he does not, in his own carefully hedged written words, “possess sufficient framework to properly categorize”. Otwin reads the report a third time at his own desk, by a candle he does not trouble to replace, and keeps it. Together with Rosalind's forty-one days and the physician's account it is what Otwin builds a clean, seductive theory on, and the manuscript says the theory was entirely wrong. The manuscript gives Emmerich his title, his thirty-one years and his documents, and no appearance. The sheet's late sixties, thin white hair, weak pale-blue eyes, thin stooped build, grey-brown Keeper's robe, ink-stained sleeves and satchel of styluses and wax tablets are its own, as is its line that he serves as Vahlreth's court archivist under Otwin.","i":"images/characters/emmerich-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Rosalind","u":"characters/rosalind.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Huntress in Otwin's service · forty-one days in a forest by her own count · mid-thirties by her sheet","x":"Is told to do nothing more provocative than exist near the hermit, and comes back confessing she has stopped being curious. A huntress by trade, unremarkable in every particular that matters to Otwin's purpose, whom he sends because soldiers, he has learned at some cost, are an argument the hermit has no difficulty winning. Her instructions are to do nothing more provocative than exist, at a respectful distance, in the same forest as Vara, for as long as it takes to see something worth reporting. She stays forty-one days by her own count, which she has stopped trusting by the third week. She can describe, with a huntress's precision, every game trail, every deadfall and every bend of the water the hermit walks out to each morning for his shrimp. She cannot produce one coherent account of an entire day in his company that does not seem to be missing pieces she has no memory of losing: a conversation she remembers beginning and cannot remember ending, an afternoon she is certain held more than the two hours her careful notes account for. She goes to Otwin at last not with an observation but a confession. Around the fourth week she began to feel toward the pale hermit exactly the vague, comfortable, faintly embarrassed lack of curiosity a person feels toward a neighbour's fence post, and she needed a full day's ride away from that forest before the feeling thinned out enough for her to notice it had ever been there. It is her report, more than the records, that supplies him with the one fact he lacked: that whatever the hermit is, he has arranged, deliberately or not, to be the kind of thing a mind simply declines to hold onto, so that every account of him is permanently incomplete in ways nobody has a method of measuring. Otwin keeps it beside Emmerich's, and later, still failing to describe the quality of stillness she could not describe, finds it nothing like the man who comes to his door. The Auditor, watching from elsewhere, notes with some unease that a usurper's primitive methods, parchment, memory and a huntress with a notebook, have produced in months a fuller profile of the variable than the Auditor's own nine years. The manuscript gives her no appearance, only her trade, her notes and her forty-one days. The sheet's mid-thirties, dark hair in a single braid, dark-green eyes, weathered skin, muted forest-green cloak, bow, knife and notebook are its own.","i":"images/characters/rosalind-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Erchanbald","u":"characters/erchanbald.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"A great lord of Vahlreth · first of the great houses to kneel to Otwin · early sixties by his sheet","x":"Kneels first, and then, after the flagstone, begins quietly to buy a future in somebody else's currency. The first of Vahlreth's great houses to kneel to Otwin on the very night the old line was presumed extinguished. When the girl everyone had assured him was as good as dead keeps declining to stay that way, he finds himself explaining to his increasingly skeptical sons, four separate times in as many years, why. He does not withdraw his loyalty, not openly and not in any way a court recorder could later point to, but after the flagstone he begins quietly diversifying: a younger son sent to study at Hartenmark's own university under a name that is not quite his own, and a modest but real investment in a Hartenmark grain concern, the kind of small, deniable hedge a careful man makes against a future he has stopped being entirely certain of. Otwin notices all of it, because noticing precisely this is the competence that built his reign, and finds that noticing changes nothing. The manuscript gives him no appearance. The sheet's early sixties, long grey hair and full grey beard, fur-trimmed mantle, sword and banner are its own.","i":"images/characters/erchanbald-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Hush","u":"characters/the-hush.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Contract assassin of the three kingdoms east of the mountains · eleven confirmed contracts, no witness who can describe his face · age and appearance unknown by design","x":"Comes through a window nobody watched, stands over a sleeping woman for nearly a minute, and is beaten by a dream and a scream. A man whose reputation is older than the war he is hired into, known across three kingdoms east of the mountains by a single word rather than a name: not one of his eleven confirmed contracts has produced a witness capable of describing his face, his voice or the means by which he entered a room no door recorded as having opened. He costs, by the careful accounting of Otwin's own treasurer, more than the twelve men at the clearing and the standing border company together, a sum Otwin spends two full years telling himself he will not spend and a third discovering that he will. He comes on an unremarkable night in high summer, through a window three floors above a courtyard no ladder should have reached, and stands over Hildegund's bed for what the night's accounting puts at nearly a full minute before he moves, a minute he will later describe, in the one interview he gives, years afterwards, in a language none of Hartenmark's scholars can agree on the translation of, as the closest he has come in a long career to simple professional admiration. She sleeps, he says, like a woman who has never needed to consider that she might not wake. It is the one misjudgment of his career, and a forgivable one: she has not slept a full night through since the wine, and the fear he mistakes for rest is exactly what the sickness in her blood has been feeding on all summer. A dream, delivered whole and wordless into whatever she is already dreaming, moves her six inches to the left in the half-second the blade would otherwise have found her heart. He does not panic, which is the single skill that has kept him alive across eleven contracts when skill alone has not been enough. He closes one gloved hand around her throat before her eyes have finished opening and reaches with the other for the shorter blade he keeps for exactly this contingency. What eleven contracts have never required him to account for is a half-strangled throat, unpractised at screaming, that produces in the one half-second left to it a sound that travels through two closed doors and down a full flight of stairs. What follows is a good deal less dignified than the songs will make it: six hours of soldiers with torches searching a castle built across four centuries by people who never designed it for a search, a rooftop chase that costs one guardsman a broken ankle and the Hush nothing, and an escape not understood until morning, when a servants' grain chute nobody had thought to guard is found missing one very old, very small and very conveniently placed grating. He is never seen again, not in Hartenmark, not in Vahlreth, not in any of the three kingdoms that had known him well enough to give him a name, which several of Otwin's own intelligencers independently decide is the most unsettling fact of the affair. Vara, sleeping uncommonly soundly half a mile away, hears none of it. The manuscript's own point is that no one can say what he looks like, and the sheet says so too: it states that his appearance is unknown in-universe and that every visual detail on it is invented for reference. Its early-forties figure, short dark hair, dark-brown eyes, olive skin, clean-shaven, in a smoke-grey hooded cloak with twin blades and soft-soled boots, is therefore its own and not a likeness. The sheet also calls the night the fifth and final attempt on her life; the manuscript's own tally, which counts the poisoned arrow, the ford and the clearing as attempts, is seven, and the Hush is the last.","i":"images/characters/the-hush-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Hereditary Marker","u":"characters/the-hereditary-marker.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"The Catalyst, Mythic Class · a thinness in the blood · five confirmed activations · the sheet shows its carrier at seventeen","x":"Strikes through every branch that ends with her dead and leaves her one number. It asks a great fear first, and it cannot be summoned. A dormant condition in Hildegund's line, called by the physician whose sealed account Otwin inherited “a thinness in the blood”: passed down a family in no order the physician could establish, sometimes skipping a generation entirely, and making its sufferer, in moments of great fright, act as though possessed of a foreknowledge she could not possibly hold. In Hildegund it has lain asleep for seventeen years, inherited from a grandfather who died without ever suspecting what slept in him, and wakes in a burning corridor, not because seventeen means anything to it but because that is how long it takes her life to hand it a crisis extreme enough to answer. The Auditor's first working note records it as activating on schedule, “three generations delayed, but within acceptable dormancy variance”. What it does is not a vision. The manuscript is strict about this: she does not see the future, and never will, but feels, in that instant and in every instant afterwards that the sickness chooses to answer, the single number left once every other number has been struck through. It takes a great fear to wake it, it cannot be summoned by wanting, and it never warns her of anything she has not first been given cause to dread. It will not seize a problem she has already answered, as at the ford, where trusting the man ahead has a survival rate of a hundred percent. It needs her dread of stairs to cross the flagstone. It gives her nothing on the eastern road, where fear needs a moment to gather and the wheel allows none. And it does not need her to be listening: on the night of the Hush it reaches her through a dream, as in the three days of fever when she relived four deaths she did not die, and moves her six inches to the left. The account that describes it is Otwin's whole justification: the one other case the physician knew, a lordling whose survival of an assassin's blade bred a decade of rule on the conviction that whatever protected him always would, at the cost of two ruinous wars and a famine. Otwin concludes that the blood protects the body more reliably than it protects the judgment, and that a reckless sovereign is a patient catastrophe. The manuscript's answer, delivered at its end, is that the condition did not make Hildegund careless. She asks what a thing will cost and then asks again, and she ends by training an ordinary mind to do slowly and on purpose something the blood once did in half a heartbeat. The Auditor's closing log counts five confirmed activations and files the case as successful by every metric the Auditor is authorised to apply. It has no body of its own. The sheet shows its carrier, Hildegund at seventeen, in her fugitive dress and in a long court gown, with panels for its triggers and the fall of Vahlreth drawn as the moment it woke. The manuscript's Archival Log gives its class as Mythic.","i":"images/characters/the-hereditary-marker-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Auditor","u":"characters/the-auditor.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Cultivator observer posted to Terrarium 0277–Vahlreth · the voice of the three working notes and the closing log · never makes contact · age unspecified","x":"Cannot close the file, cannot categorise the finding, and keeps finding reasons not to file the report. The Cultivator observer whose unfiled working notes sit between the parts of the case, and whose closing log closes the file. The first note finds the Hereditary Marker activated on schedule and the file, by every standard metric, ready to close, and it is not closing. A variable is present at the activation site that predates the Auditor's own assignment by a margin that cannot be established and appears in no registry the Auditor is cleared to search. It “does not conceal itself”; it simply does not register as a question until one thinks to ask it. Six hours earlier it moved eleven metres in a duration the Auditor's optical logging renders as a single unbroken frame. The Auditor reviews the capture four times and records that there is no category to put the suspicion in, and that an Auditor who cannot categorise a finding has functionally stopped auditing. “I am not entering this in the standard report yet.” “I do not know why.” Nine years on, the claimant has built exactly the kind of soft, informal political capital that should not, on any model, translate into a stable claim to a throne she has not occupied in nearly a decade. The Auditor still cannot close the file. The marker is understood completely; the girl who carries it less well than the marker does, “which I am aware is methodologically backward”, and four times in nine years the Auditor has found a defensible reason to postpone contact protocols appropriate to a “Tier 4 unknown”. “I am aware that this pattern has a name in my own training.” “I am choosing, for the moment, not to apply it to myself.” A third note adds a second party, the usurper with his parchment and his huntress, who has produced a fuller profile of the variable in months than the Auditor in nine years. The variable's effect on human observers resembles induced salience-suppression, which the Auditor could write a training module on. What cannot be explained is why instruments that have no attention to suppress return equally incomplete readings. “A camera does not get bored.” The honest conclusion, unreported, is that the variable may not be assembled from anything in the finite catalogue of physics the Auditor's civilisation and several hundred others have recorded, and that no better instrument may exist. The closing log, headed “Custodian Diagnostic Log” and “Case 0,277”, files the case as successful by every metric the Auditor is authorised to apply: five confirmed activations of the marker; a voluntary, negotiated transfer of power; one precedent, a crown that can be waited out by a six-chancellery silence; recorded costs of one king poisoned and one palace burned, twenty-one soldiers of the usurper's service, two men-at-arms of the claimant's escort, a royal guard, a soldier and a carriage driver, and one standing border company disbanded. The variable's true name has been confirmed and entered nowhere but a private, unfiled line. “A name is not a category.” The Auditor reported the variable to a superior three months before the last entry, not because it had taken any action against the terrarium but because the theoretical risk of so unclassified a variable exceeded what the Auditor judged its own authority to manage, though the interval between confirming the necessity and filing was longer than the calculation alone would explain. The Auditor never initiated contact, while forming on at least three occasions the impression that the variable was already aware of being watched and had elected not to remark on it. Status: “Terrarium closed. Variable open.” No one in the story knows the Auditor exists, although Vara, at the end, asks Hildegund whether she knows of the Observer. The manuscript presents the Auditor only as a voice and a set of instruments, with no body and no pronoun, and this page follows it. The sheet's hairless pale-grey humanoid figure, silver-white eyes without visible pupils, fine geometric pattern at one temple and close-fitted grey observation garment, with the instruments drawn as faint rings of light, are its own. A name is not a category.","i":"images/characters/the-auditor-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The second stair","u":"records/the-second-stair.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Gero, Hildegund","x":"A grey-bearded old guard in scaled mail with a torn red cloak stands braced on a narrow stone stair with a dented shield on his left arm and a long sword thrust out toward a crowd of helmeted soldiers pressing up at the lower left, their raised swords catching an orange glow, more torch-lit helmets receding up the curving stairwell behind him On the night Vahlreth falls Gero takes the second stair and simply refuses to let the first man through the door. It costs the duke's men four full minutes and him everything he has left, while Hildegund, three syllables unsaid behind her teeth, runs on.","i":"images/scenes/the-second-stair-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The corridor at Vahlreth","u":"records/the-corridor-at-vahlreth.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Reinolt, Adalger, Hildegund, The Hereditary Marker","x":"A young woman with long wavy fair hair in a dark patterned gown stands in the foreground of a smoke-filled stone corridor looking back over her shoulder, two helmeted men in dark mail at her back, a torn black banner hanging at the upper left and the ceiling above them bursting into orange fire, a stair climbing behind her to the left, and the towers of a castle and a small crowd glimpsed at the lower right In a burning corridor, with Reinolt at her shoulder and Adalger three steps behind, Hildegund feels the ceiling ahead go orange and the Hereditary Marker wake. It strikes through every branch that ends with her dead and leaves her one number: left.","i":"images/scenes/the-corridor-at-vahlreth-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The second river","u":"records/the-second-river.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Adalger, Hagen, Hildegund, Reinolt","x":"A young woman with long tangled fair hair in a torn grey-white gown presses her hand to a bloodied wound in her side as a helmeted man in dark mail grips her from behind, a soldier lying on his back with an arrow in his chest and one hand outstretched at the left, a dropped torch burning at the lower left, a fast river on the right and dark figures moving among the trees under a full moon At the second river Adalger dies mid-sentence with an arrow in the gap of his cuirass, and a third arrow, Hagen's, finds Hildegund low in the side. Reinolt takes an arrow in the shoulder, and the number turns northeast, toward the hermit in the forest.","i":"images/scenes/the-second-river-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The low table","u":"records/the-low-table.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Vara, Hildegund, Reinolt","x":"A pale white-haired man in a heavy brown hooded robe kneels beside a young fair-haired woman lying on a low table, pressing a clay-coloured paste from a wooden bowl onto a wound at her bared side, bundles of dried herbs hanging from the rafters above, a big fire burning at the right, and a young guard with a bandaged forearm and a sword at his belt standing in the open door at the left In the shack in the forest Vara says one word, In, and closes Hildegund's wound with a paste the colour of river clay. Reinolt will call it magic for the rest of his life. What it was, the manuscript says, was competence: old, unhurried and entirely explicable.","i":"images/scenes/the-low-table-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"A bowl of unshelled peas","u":"records/a-bowl-of-unshelled-peas.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Hildegund, Vara","x":"In a warm timbered kitchen lit by a big hearth fire and a hanging lantern, a young fair-braided woman and a pale white-haired man in a hooded brown robe sit side by side at a table shelling peas into wooden bowls, bundles of herbs hanging above them, a frosted mill wheel visible through the small window behind, and a young guard in dark mail with a sword leaning back in a chair at the right At Gertrud's table in Muhlbach Hildegund has her first ordinary evening and does not know what to do with her hands, until Vara puts a knife and a bowl of unshelled peas in front of her and sets about the same task himself.","i":"images/scenes/a-bowl-of-unshelled-peas-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Twenty men at the ford","u":"records/twenty-men-at-the-ford.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Vara, Reinolt, Hildegund","x":"At the left foreground a bloodied young guard with tousled brown hair and a girl with long fair hair crouch behind a rock watching, while at the centre a white-haired figure in a grey-brown cloak is smeared across the frame in streaks of motion as armoured men in dark mail topple around him into a rushing, sunlit river strewn with fallen soldiers, spears and swords raised at the edges and a dark fir forest behind Twenty men wait at the ford. For the first minutes Vara fights like a very good man with a very good sword beside Reinolt, bleeding from a cut forearm he has not yet noticed; then Vara lets it go, and Hildegund, up on the rocks, watches twenty and two become an arithmetic error.","i":"images/scenes/twenty-men-at-the-ford-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The gate of Hartenmark","u":"records/the-gate-of-hartenmark.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Hildegund, Vara","x":"At dusk under a purple and orange sky, a pale fair-haired man in a long hooded grey-brown cloak walks away to the left with his head bowed and his back to a torch-lit stone gate, while at the right a young woman with long wavy fair hair in a dark gown holds out one hand toward him from the open gate and a helmeted guard with a spear watches, torch-bearing sentries on the wall above and dark fir trees on the far side of the road At Hartenmark's gate the guards open for Hildegund, which no soldier has done since the night Vahlreth fell. Vara stops a spear's length short, says A king's gratitude has a way of becoming a king's certainty, then I would rather remain a rumor, and walks back toward the trees, unhurried.","i":"images/scenes/the-gate-of-hartenmark-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Before the questions","u":"records/before-the-questions.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Ulrich, Hildegund","x":"A grey-haired bearded king in a dark fur-trimmed cloak with gold embroidery holds a fair-haired young woman in a grey-brown cloak against his chest, his cheek on her head, her blood-stained hand clutching his sleeve, a row of grey-bearded councillors in black robes at the left and helmeted spearmen at the right, in a tall stone hall of candelabra and dark banners In his receiving hall Ulrich has not seen his sister's daughter since she was nine and missing her front teeth. He embraces Hildegund before he questions her, a guardsman's blood still under her fingernails, and only then lets the council's six hours begin.","i":"images/scenes/before-the-questions-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Tea before chairs","u":"records/tea-before-chairs.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Vara, Frida, Hildegund, Reinolt","x":"In dappled gold light on a rocky river bank, a white-haired man in a ragged grey-brown cloak crouches over a steaming iron pot on a small fire stirring it with a wooden spoon, a flat stone of salted river shrimp and a basket at his feet, while a fair-braided young woman and a brown-haired young woman in a russet cloak sit laughing on a stump with wooden cups, a teapot beside them, and a bearded man in a dark cloak with a sword stands watching the trees at the right In the rebuilt clearing, nine years on, Vara offers tea before chairs and lays river shrimp on a stone while Frida talks court gossip and Hildegund is nine years old again in every way that has nothing to do with age. Reinolt notices the birds go quiet first.","i":"images/scenes/tea-before-chairs-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Twelve who did not walk back","u":"records/twelve-who-did-not-walk-back.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Frida, Vara, Hildegund","x":"In a sun-shafted forest clearing a pale fair-haired figure in a light shirt is blurred into streaks of motion at the centre as armoured soldiers in dark mail fall around him, two fallen men and a dented helmet lying in the lower foreground beside a smoking campfire, while at the right a fair-haired young woman and a brown-haired young woman in a dark cloak crouch together behind an overturned chair and a shield, more spearmen waiting among the trees behind Twelve of Otwin's men come out of the tree line with no ceremony, and Frida, whose letter brought them, makes one involuntary syllable of horror. Vara does not test the water this time; two of Hildegund's own men-at-arms die, and none of the twelve walks back into the trees.","i":"images/scenes/twelve-who-did-not-walk-back-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"A wrongness in the wine","u":"records/a-wrongness-in-the-wine.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Hildegund, The Under-Cook","x":"A fair-haired young woman in a gold-and-grey gown with a half-drunk glass of red wine in her hand rises from a long candlelit banquet table, her head turned sharply toward a tall window with a church spire beyond it, seated lords and ladies turning to stare, tall heraldic banners above, and at the far end of the hall a serving girl in an apron and a dark-clad man standing in an open doorway At the spring banquet, in the middle of a toast, Hildegund tastes a metallic afterthought under the sweetness, and the room reorders itself: a serving girl turning away, a man at the north door. Walk to the window, not the door.","i":"images/scenes/a-wrongness-in-the-wine-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"The window three floors up","u":"records/the-window-three-floors-up.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"The Hush, Hildegund","x":"In a dark four-poster bedchamber, a hooded figure in a heavy black cloak and black gloves leans over a young woman with tangled fair hair propped on white bedding, one gloved hand closed around her throat as her eyes open and her fingers claw at the air, pale curtains blowing at a tall window with a full moon beyond and a spilled basin lying on the floor at the lower right On a night in high summer the Hush comes through a window no ladder should have reached and stands over Hildegund for nearly a minute. A dream moves her six inches to the left, his glove closes on her throat, and she screams loud enough to carry through two closed doors.","i":"images/scenes/the-window-three-floors-up-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Behind the old armory","u":"records/behind-the-old-armory.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Reinolt, Frida","x":"At sunset in a small walled garden, a young man in a brown leather jerkin with a scar down his bare forearm kneels weeding beside a rosebush with a few red buds, onions growing in the foreground and a basket at his side, while a young woman in a cream head-cloth and brown dress stands with her hands clasped looking down at him against a stone wall hung with garden tools, a castle skyline and a low red sun behind Behind the old armory Reinolt grows onions, mint and a stubborn rosebush that has never bloomed. Three days after her confession Frida finds him there, and he asks whether she loved him for the whole of it or only some smaller, harder-to-locate part.","i":"images/scenes/behind-the-old-armory-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"A cup of hot water","u":"records/a-cup-of-hot-water.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Vara, Otwin","x":"In a candlelit book-lined study, a bearded man with greying hair in a heavy fur cloak and a chain with a medallion sits with his hands clasped, watching, while across the table a pale white-haired man in a grey-green hooded robe sips from a steaming cup held in both hands, a stoppered dark bottle and an untouched goblet standing between them, wax-sealed letters spread on the table in front, stacks of heavy books at either end, an oil lamp at the left and a fire in a hearth behind Vara walks to Otwin's gate alone, declines the wine and drinks a plain cup of hot water as if it were the finer of the two. Otwin, who had prepared for a kingmaker, asks the one question the sealed account taught him to fear: is she careless?","i":"images/scenes/a-cup-of-hot-water-grid.jpg"},{"k":"r","t":"Are you the queen now?","u":"records/are-you-the-queen-now.html","m":"Case 0277 · The Uncounted Variable","d":"Hildegund, Vara, Reinolt","x":"Beside a timber shack hung with dried fish and herbs on a golden river bank, a white-haired man in a patched grey cloak crouches by a small fire with a black kettle on a hook and looks up with a faint smile at a fair-haired woman in a cream gown and grey cloak with a thin gold circlet and a cup in her hands, while a grey-bearded older man in a dark cloak with a sword at his belt stands beside her with a hand pressed over his mouth, shoulders shaking, a castle on the far shore Years on, Hildegund visits Vara at his new river shack, a small circlet she has never got used to on her head. He looks at her a long moment and asks, in entire sincerity, Are you the queen now? Reinolt very nearly does not turn his laugh into a cough.","i":"images/scenes/are-you-the-queen-now-grid.jpg"},{"k":"c","t":"The Dog Is Not in It","u":"books/0113.html","m":"Case 0113 · Awaiting release","d":"Warmth. Sign. Debt. War. Unentered.","x":"Case 0113 Fiction / Fantasy / Historical For four hundred years, the kings of Danjeri have been Weighed onto the throne by a rite built never to give a straight answer: a Recounter recites the candidate’s ancestry from memory, the court watches the smoke and the bull, and the ancestors, being dead, decline to commit. It has always been treated as a feature. Nobody has ever needed it to say no. Then a wandering healer, who is also a field Observer for an ancient civilization that studies how little it takes to bring a stable culture down, carries something into the palace at Kambaso that he was never authorized to carry. He does it for a dying boy. He loses it to a dog. The dog does not die. It does not even grow grey. A kennel hand begins to count its blinks, its breaths, the whorl of fur at its shoulder. A beer-house gives the animal a name, a trader coins a joke, a young Recounter turns the joke into liturgy, and a queen puts a line in the treasury ledger that no clerk will ever dare to stop copying. Meanwhile, down a palace corridor, three wives raise three sons by a ledger nobody writes down, and a second Observer arrives in the salt trade, buys nothing, and begins to do the sums. The Dog Is Not in It is a quiet, cumulative tragedy of succession, grief and bookkeeping: a kennelmaster who only ever wanted to keep a promise he was never meant to understand, a Chief Recounter who knows exactly what his faith can and cannot say, and a rite that has survived four centuries on ambiguity and is about to be asked its first real question. No one in this book is a villain. Almost everyone is careful. The ledger balances anyway."},{"k":"s","t":"Kolo","u":"characters/kolo.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Kennel-hand, then kennelmaster of the palace stable-and-kennel yard · keeper of the dog by Sana's charge · 11 or 12 when first met, 61 when the dog dies, dies at 79","x":"Takes a dog from a weeping healer as a boy, counts its blinks and the arms of the whorl at its withers for fifty years, and buries it under the hill of sandals. A wiry, watchful boy of eleven or twelve when the manuscript first meets him, the household's senior kennel-hand, whose entire domain is the palace's modest stable-and-kennel yard and who takes a proprietary, faintly jealous interest in any animal that crosses into it without his permission. He begins, without admitting it, to save the best table-scraps for a dog that is not, by any household ledger, his to feed. On the morning after Damba dies he is found sitting alone in the empty yard, dry-eyed and furious, with the dog's head across his knees; Sana kneels, tells him that the dog is his now and that he must never let anyone convince him it should be given up, and Kolo remembers for a very long life that the healer's voice cracked on the word never. He counts. On every Uncounted Day, the one day of the year that belongs to no month and no week, he threads a single cowrie onto a leather cord beneath his shirt, from the first winter after the healer leaves; it is at the twenty-second shell that he notices the dog has never grown grey at the muzzle. He counts the arms of the whorl at its withers, eight and thirteen, with a wet thumb, as a man counts the coins in a purse he does not want to find short, and its blinks, eleven to the minute. He was not a man who trusted his own eyes about a thing he loved, so he counted. When a stable mule kicks the dog in the ribs he sits with it through the night and by the third evening finds nothing under his palms but ribs, and learns only that it mends whatever happens to the dog and never what the dog was born with. He dislikes children and princes and distrusts anyone who arrives at his yard with a retinue; when a six-year-old prince with a cloth of honeyed millet cakes turns up he tells the boy that he may carry water, and, against every principle he owns, begins to like Sekou. When the queen's steward has the kennel hand Tuma salt the dog's fish, he notices within the hour, because it blinks eight times to the minute where it has always blinked eleven. He tastes the fish, does the sum (one queen, one kennelmaster, one boy with a coin he cannot spend, and a dog that cannot testify), accuses no one, throws the fish away and from that morning feeds the dog from his own pouch, with fish he has bought himself with his own copper. He keeps one exception, the keg Hawa sends up on every Uncounted Day, which he could not have refused in front of the wardens without announcing that he suspected the woman who sent it. When Fode asks, as sideways as a Recounter knows how, whether a man who loved an animal might not do best to carry it beyond the northern wells, Kolo does not look up from the fish he is sorting: I was told never. Captain Wuro arrests him on a charge of sacrilege, and Sekou's wardens find him in the cellar of the guardhouse with a split lip and the dog asleep across his feet. On the Uncounted Day he adds the fiftieth cowrie to the cord, drinks his horn from Hawa's keg slowly, sitting on the water-jar with the dog across his feet, and is asleep before the wardens on the wall have finished theirs. He wakes late into the first morning of Averin and understands before he has crossed the yard exactly what kind of horn of beer he was given. He sits with the cold body across his knees, counting its blinks, out of habit, until he understands that there are no more. He digs the grave himself, with a kennel spade, in the corner where the dog piled its shoes for half a century, and lays the cord with its fifty cowries beside the body. He outlives it by eighteen years, will not let any of the animals that follow be called by the old name, never learns who laid a small right-footed shoe on top of the hill when the dog had only ever stolen left-footed ones, and dies at seventy-nine, on the Uncounted Day, on the water-jar in the yard, with a dog across his feet. The trader Yandé takes a puppy from that spring's litter; he tells her what the healer once told him. The manuscript gives wiry and watchful at eleven or twelve, lean and watchful past fifty, and the cord; the rest is invented. The sheet's age of 61 agrees with the manuscript's arithmetic, as do its 5 ft 9 in (175 cm), narrow weathered face, cropped grey hair, short grey stubble, dark brown eyes, deep brown leathery skin, patched undyed wrapper and kennel spade, which are its own, as are its panels Watchfulness, Patient Counting, Quiet Suspicion and Grief and its scenes He feeds the dog from his own pouch and The hill where he will one day bury the dog. I was told never","i":"images/characters/kolo-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Fode","u":"characters/fode.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Second-ranked Recounter, then Chief Recounter of Danjeri · carrier of sentences · 43 in Chapter Seven, 50 at the Weighing","x":"Hears a drunk's joke at a beer-house, says nothing at the Weighing, says one perfect sentence at the Second Asking, and does the rest alone, unentered, on the day nothing is. Second-ranked of the Recounters at forty-three, in a plain brown wrapper that does not entirely hide a braided cord, drinking well below his station at the Weighmaster's Table on the theory that a man who said nothing there might be mistaken for a clerk. When a drunk Bantaran caravan-master calls the palace dog the river's long debt, he is the one man in the room who does not laugh: in the grammar of the Kept Account a debt never collected is a dispute, and a dispute left standing across generations is the beginning of every catastrophe the faith was founded to prevent. He tries the phrase silently, as a man tries a tooth he suspects, and finds that it fits the thing far too well. Recounters write nothing down; he remembers all of it, which was worse. On the walk home he recites the line of kings backward, which Recounters do only when afraid. Seven years on he is Chief Recounter, made so two winters earlier on the death of the ancient Nyeleni, and known to King Tiemoko since he was a stammering novice; the dying king sends for him and for the dog. Tell Damba, the king says, I kept the dog fed: he will set it into Damba's line within the week, and say it every day for the rest of his life without understanding that it is the only complete sentence about the dog the Account will ever hold. At the Weighing, when the dog sits on Sekou's left foot and Mansa cries that the long debt is paid, he has two words to say, the account stands or the account is disputed, and says nothing, which is afterward the one thing every party can agree on: the sign was never ratified. At the Second Asking, asked whether the Account would stand upon whatever the dog did, he has invented a third sentence, the one the case's cover title takes: The account stands, he says, as the court begins to roar, The dog is not in it. It is, for a Recounter, a perfect sentence, true in the only sense the faith's grammar allows and entirely incapable of being understood. Sekou's people hear the first half and Alassane's the second. On the sandbar in the last week of Jorren he carries the two parties' sentences forty paces each way, ninety-one times by his own private count, each in the order and with the breath-marks it was spoken with; and when Alassane asks, Is it warm, where it sleeps?, he tells him that it slept on his brother's feet. On the fifth of Mavren he hears of the captains' vote to carry the dog before the army in a litter, recites the kings backward twice without reaching the bargain, and ten days later reaches it. He has rejected removal in order, asked Kolo sideways whether a man who loved an animal might not carry it beyond the northern wells, and been told I was told never; he pays Sira four hundred weights from the Kept Account's own reserve, its only unaudited expenditure in four hundred years, and chooses the Uncounted Day, on which a deed is entered nowhere. The night before, he sits in the kennel yard on the water-jar, and the dog lies down across his bare cold feet. The Archival Log presumes, on the strength of the Chief Recounter's later conduct and of nothing this desk would call evidence, that he was the principal. He outlives the war by eleven years, teaching Mansa to recite a war that contains no dog: fourteen lines, no drug, no sign, no animal and no Uncounted Day. I am teaching you to be quiet about it, he tells her. On the last night of his life he asks her to enter one more deed into his own line, four hundred weights, weighed on the river-stone on the Uncounted Day, and she refuses. The manuscript gives his ages, the braided cord, the brown wrapper, the stammering novice and the breath-marks of the sentences he carries. The sheet's apparent build, spare and slightly stooped at about 173 cm, long grave face with deep vertical brow lines, close-cropped grey hair, short grey beard and deep-set dark brown eyes are its own, as are its panels Grave Counsel, Silent Refusal, Measured Recitation and Solitary Resolve and its scene Carrying the sentences between the tents at the sandbar. The dog is not in it.","i":"images/characters/fode-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Doni","u":"characters/doni.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"The palace dog, carrier of the Immortal Cell · also the Long Debt and the river's change · young-adult condition for nearly fifty years","x":"Warms cold feet, steals only left-footed sandals, never grows grey, and is turned by a kingdom into a mandate, a fraud, a sign, a standard and a target. A lean, sand-colored, middling animal with one ear that folds over and one that does not, a tail that curls across its back like a question nobody has finished asking, and a habit of tilting its head when addressed, which visitors take for intelligence and Kolo knows for a bad left ear. It sleeps on its back with its legs in the air. It steals sandals, never chewing them, only carrying them, one at a time, to a single corner of the yard, where a small hill grows; it greets everyone, from the grain-clerk to the Chief Elder, with the same undignified, full-body joy. Sana named it Doni, a placeholder syllable meaning roughly nothing, and the Archival Log closes on that: the local designation on file is Doni, the translation is nothing, and This desk has checked. It swallows the Immortal Cell from a sealed pouch in Sana's satchel, beside dried meat. As Observer 1,451's Interlude II sets it down, the Cell arrests senescence and repairs damage within limits: it does not restore what was never intact, so the folded ear and the poor hearing on the left are unchanged after more than forty years, and it does not preclude death by catastrophic trauma; heritability is unassessed. Its one visible signature is at the withers, where Kolo counts, by firelight, a whorl that has arranged itself out of a scribble of crossed fur into a double spiral, eight arms one way and thirteen the other. When a stable mule kicks it across the ribs in Sekou's tenth winter it mends by the third evening. It blinks eleven times to the minute, and Kolo has never told a soul that he counts. In the cold of the harmattan it crosses the lean-to where Sekou, a kennel-boy, is sleeping with numb bare feet, turns around twice and lies across them, and by the end of that season sits on his foot the way other dogs sit on hearthstones. It was not devotion, exactly; devotion is an attitude, and this was a habit. It lies across the cold feet of a dying king, Tiemoko, and, at the first Weighing, walks the length of the Ancestor Court through some fifteen hundred people and sits on Sekou's left foot. At the Second Asking it is set down three paces from Alassane, sniffs his hand, with the perfect manners it shows to everyone, and trots the twenty-seven remaining paces up the raked lane to sit on Sekou's foot again. Captain Wuro takes it on a rope as evidence, the first time in fifty years it has worn one, and it goes along delighted, its curled tail waving. On the Uncounted Day of its fiftieth year in the yard it wakes at the step of a stranger, as it always does, rolls over, beats its tail twice against the mat and comes to lick her hand. Sira kills it with a blade, once, and it does not know. Observer 1,451 arrives eleven minutes late, puts a small sandal back on the hill and files the case as moot. Kolo buries it under the hill of sandals, with a cord of fifty cowries beside it. The treasury line for its ration, For the dog of the boy Damba — millet, four measures by the moon; dried fish, six; to continue., goes on being copied for three centuries. The Log's reading of its fifty years is that its sole recorded behavior was the provision of warmth to those it knew when they were cold, and that the institutions around it converted this, in order, into a mandate, a fraud, a sign, a standard, and a target. The manuscript supplies the colour, build, ears, tail, whorl and habits; the sheet's 55 cm at the shoulder, 19 kg and dark brown eyes are its own. Its title strip gives an apparent age of 3 years and an actual age just under 50, and lists the names the Long Debt, the river's change and Grey-Never; the manuscript gives the third name to Hawa's darker beer, not to the dog. Its panels are Joy, Listening, Alert and Trust, and its two scenes are He sits on Sekou's left foot and Led on a rope as evidence. the most reliably warm thing in the terrarium","i":"images/characters/doni-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sana","u":"characters/sana.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Observer 640, the Envoy of Case 0113 · healer and reader of bones, the wandering diviner · apparent age 43 on his sheet","x":"Carries a cell no larger than a date pit into a palace for a dying boy, loses it to his own dog in the time it takes to draw water from a well, and does not reach for a blade. A healer and reader of bones who walks the river towns and caravan camps of Danjeri with a satchel of cowrie shells, sheep knuckles and dried roots, consulted less for his cures, which are competent but unremarkable, than for the unhurried way he listens to a complaint before he touches the patient. That is his whole method: his value to the Corps lies almost wholly in what people tell him while they believe he is only listening. He is Observer 640, three years into a posting meant to last four; in his second year he took in a half-grown, rib-visible stray on the road between Kambaso and the salt-pans. There was no line on any form for companion, unbonded, non-operational, so he did not report it. He named it Doni, a word that meant, in the trade-tongue, roughly nothing at all. Brought to the palace on the word of a caravan-master, he is the ninth physician to look at King Soriba's second son Damba, and he knows from the first examination that nothing local will help. He keeps coming back anyway. He tells his supervising desk that the household holds politically relevant intelligence, which is not a lie and not the reason; the reason is that Damba asks direct questions and expects direct answers. In the month of Ferren he decides, alone in a room above a dyer's shop, to use an Immortal Cell, a single dormant biological instantiation no larger than a date pit, which he requisitioned through a supply irregularity he believed he could justify afterwards. He has written the fiction (a spontaneous remission) and not what comes after it: the Cell would keep a body at the shape of its prime, and he means, so far as he lets himself mean anything, to carry the boy out of Danjeri within a few years. He has miscounted exactly one thing, the dog's own hunger. He leaves the satchel unattended for about as long as it takes to draw water from the courtyard well, and the sealed pouch smells to a dog's nose precisely like the dried meat it was packed beside. Protocol on a compromised specimen is not ambiguous: retrieve the Cell if retrieval is possible, and destroy it, along with its host, if it is not. He runs the calculation in something under four seconds and arrives at the correct answer before his hand has finished moving to where a blade would have been. He does not reach for one. Damba's fever turns in the second week of Garren, too fast for the tea he brought or for the second plan he had assembled, a hollow reed, a goat's bladder and blood drawn from the one creature in Danjeri that now carries the Cell. The boy dies on the ninth day of Garren, with Sana at the bedside for the last two nights. The posting ends nineteen days later, on schedule. He finds the kennel-hand Kolo alone in the empty yard with the dog's head across his knees and tells him that the dog is his now and that he must never let anyone convince him it needs to be given up, and his voice cracks on the word never. He leaves Danjeri three days later and does not look back at the kennel yard. His Interlude I filing closes the case as compromised, uncontained, non-recoverable within operational cost parameters, with an addendum appended without authorization noting that the specimen is a dog and that the desk does not know how to feel about it. Some sixty years on he files a routine request for the status of the closed case, and makes the same request at irregular intervals for several centuries; he keeps a small stone from the Kambaso road on the shelf above each new bed. The Archival Log's last word on him is that the loss was his and the sequence was not: Observer 640: unreviewed; no annotation entered. The second Observer of the case, 1,451, field name Yandé, has no sheet of her own and so no page here. The manuscript gives his cover, his satchel, his listening and the crack in his voice on one word, and no age, face or build. The sheet's apparent age of 43, lean 5 ft 8 in (173 cm) build, medium-deep, sun-weathered skin, dark brown eyes, short black hair greying at the temples, close-trimmed grey-flecked beard, two old root-cutting scars on the left forearm, faded indigo wrapper over an undyed tunic and worn leather sandals are its own, as are its panels Attentive Calm, Grief for Damba, Quiet Resolve and Case Still Open, and its scene He could not save Damba; he gave the dog to Kolo. It draws the dog at his heel as a pale golden animal; the manuscript's Doni is sand-colored. He did not reach for one.","i":"images/characters/sana-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sekou","u":"characters/sekou.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Second prince, Warden of the Road-Stations, then King of Danjeri · the dog's chosen foot · Sekou the Steady · 31 in Chapter Eight, 36 at the Weighing","x":"Is sent to a kennel yard at six to be no one's enemy, has a dog sit on his foot, and becomes the best king the granary-keepers think Danjeri could afford. Binta's son, born two winters after Alassane, and sent at six to Kolo's yard, the only place in the palace where a boy could spend his days being useful to no faction whatever. Kolo, who dislikes children and princes, tells the six-year-old with the honeyed millet cakes that if he wishes to stay he may carry water, and Sekou carries it for two years with such stubborn, uncomplaining exactness that Kolo, against every principle he owns, begins to like him. In the harmattan, when his bare feet go numb in the lean-to where he sleeps, the dog crosses the room one night, turns around twice and lies down across them; by the end of the season it sits on his foot the way other dogs sit on hearthstones. It was not devotion, exactly; devotion is an attitude, and this was a habit. He breaks his arm at twelve, falling from the stable roof after a stray kite, and the first physician to reach him is Awa's own bone-setter. At thirty-one he is Warden of the Road-Stations, the office of wells and caravan shelters, given to him on the principle that no one has ever been assassinated for a well, and every seventh day he walks to the kennel yard in a plain wrapper with a cloth of something to eat, says a few words about nothing, and the dog crosses the yard and sits on his left foot. At the Weighing he stands barefoot in the plain cloth he would have worn to inspect a well, like someone waiting to be told he had come to the wrong place, and when the dog sits on his foot before two thousand people he understands that he had just been made a very great deal of use. That night in the kennel yard he says, It has chosen me, and Kolo answers, It has chosen your foot; he laughs once, the way a man laughs at a bill he cannot pay. When the war comes he is acclaimed in the Ancestor Court by a crowd that would have acclaimed a well, accepts it the way he had once accepted a bucket, because it was in front of him, and for thirty-one months sits on a stool in the plain wrapper and signs what is put in front of him. Each evening, when the day's dead have been reckoned, he walks to the kennel yard and asks Kolo how the dog is. Eleven: the only number in Danjeri, in all those months, that has not got worse. He agrees to the sandbar's hardest clause, that Awa shall not be tried, after consulting his mother, and makes the concession, the largest of his life, in the voice in which he would have agreed to dig a well. He hangs Sory from the old tamarind at dawn on the ninth of Kerren, because a truce-breaker who was not hanged was an invitation; he hears the matter himself and did not enjoy it, and did not pretend to. When the dog is found dead he comes on foot, sits on the water-jar and puts his feet on the earth, and waits. Nothing came to sit on them. It is, the book says, the coldest he has ever been. He tells Kolo he is sorry, and Kolo says that it did not matter to the dog. He is Weighed in the spring, with no dog and a bare foot on the cold red earth, and an unremarkable thread of smoke that bends east after a moment's hesitation. He reigns twenty-six years more, rebuilds the granaries, the wells and, in the eleventh year, the ford of the Second Road, and is remembered as Sekou the Steady, the best king Danjeri could afford. He never orders the dog's name struck from the Account or entered in it. On every Uncounted Day for the rest of his life he walks alone to the kennel yard, sits on the water-jar for an hour and does not ask anything to sit on his feet. The manuscript gives his ages, his plain wrapper, his bare feet and the dog's habit. The sheet's age of 36 at the Weighing, lean 5 ft 10 in (178 cm) build, deep brown skin, close black hair, dark brown eyes and calm square face are its own, as are its panels Steady, Watchful, Duty and Resolve and its scenes Inspecting Wells, The Kennel's Food, The Dog's Chosen Foot and At Dawn on the Ninth of Kerren. Its title strip, The dog's chosen foot, and its line Later King of Danjeri — Sekou the Steady follow the manuscript's Epilogue. Chapter Twelve's narration says that Sekou had, at seven, on a cold night, taught the dog to sit on his foot and that Kolo let him, which does not match Chapter Six, where the dog crosses the lean-to and lies down across his bare feet of its own accord; the page reports both. the best king Danjeri could afford","i":"images/characters/sekou-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Alassane","u":"characters/alassane.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Elder prince of Danjeri, Awa's son · candidate at the Weighing and the Second Asking · offered the title Keeper of the East · 38 years old","x":"Is told from his cradle that he will be king, is outranked by a dog, and dies in the shallows of the river he feared, very nearly at peace. Awa's son, born in the ninth winter after Damba's death: quick, proud, a little afraid of the river he would someday be expected to bargain with, who grows into a man who wants, more than the throne itself, to be the person everyone has always said he would be. He visits the kennel yard perhaps four times in his whole boyhood, on the arm of a nurse, is greeted by the dog with the same joyful sandal-bearing enthusiasm as anyone, offers it a strip of dried fish, has it taken from him politely and watches the dog wander back toward Sekou's foot. He feels, without being able to say why, faintly and permanently outranked by an animal, and puts it out of his mind. At the Weighing he stands barefoot at the northern end of the Ancestor Court, tall and immaculate in a wrapper dyed for the day, wearing the calm of a man who has been rehearsing it since childhood, and when the dog crosses the court and sits on his rival's foot he remembers, with a clarity that turns his stomach, a strip of dried fish and a courteous animal walking away. An hour before dawn he comes to his mother's chamber, for the first time since he was eight, and asks whether it is true that the ancestors have chosen. I do not want to fight, he says, to the floor, the truest thing he would say that year. At the Second Asking he holds out his hand, more afraid than he has ever been, and it shows only in the stillness of his fingers; the dog sniffs it, with the perfect manners it shows to everyone from grain-clerks to kings, and trots the twenty-seven remaining paces up the lane to Sekou's left foot. He leaves Kambaso with his mother, her steward and four of the Sitting Elders, and the country divides along the line her dowry always drew. On the sandbar, in the last week of Jorren in the third winter of the war, he asks for the whole of the east as a second crown; Sekou answers by offering it in trust, as a keepership, which was not the same word. In the ninth hour he puts the term he came to put, in six words that the Chief Recounter Fode carries exactly: that his mother should not be tried. Sekou says it is agreed. Alassane receives it with his eyes shut, and when the Recounter stands between the tents with nothing left to carry he asks, not as a prince but as a man staying on after the business is done, Is it warm, where it sleeps?, and is told that it sleeps on his brother's feet. He is very nearly a happy man on the way back, crossing at the shallows at dusk with a single guard, and he was, for the length of that crossing, at peace. Sory is waiting on the far bank. Alassane dies in the shallows of the river he had feared, and is brought to his mother's tent on a door. The manuscript gives his height as tall, his immaculate dress and his hands that go still when he is afraid; the rest is invented. The sheet's age of 38, 6 ft 1 in (185 cm) broad-shouldered frame, deep brown skin, close-cropped hair, neat chin beard, braided gold wristband and deep-indigo wrapper with a saffron-yellow border are its own, as are its panels Courtly Composure, Fear Held Still, Grief and Resolve at the Shallows and its scene Returning Across the Shallows, captioned He sends his terms through the Chief Recounter, then returns to the shallows. Its title strip calls him the eldest surviving son of Tiemoko and Awa, which implies sons the manuscript does not mention. The manuscript's Chapter Six has the fish remembered after thirty-one years; Chapter Ten has it remembered after thirty. I do not want to fight","i":"images/characters/alassane-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Founding Ancestor","u":"characters/founding-ancestor.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"First ancestor of Danjeri's kings · bargained with the river for the right to cross · unnamed · about 40 on his sheet","x":"Stands on a grey stone at the river's edge and asks the water for passage, at a price no living person can name, and a kingdom's religion is built on keeping the bargain. He never appears in the manuscript, and nothing is said of him beyond a bargain. The kingdom's ruling line traces itself, through a spoken genealogy kept by a hereditary caste of court historians, back to a founding ancestor who had — in the account, at least — bargained with the river itself for the right to cross it safely, a bargain on which the kingdom's whole religious life has since been built. The Ancestor Court beside the palace is where he was said to have stood on the bank and made his bargain with the water, and every king of Danjeri since has had to stand there in his turn while the senior Recounter recites his line back to that moment without pause or error. The bargain keeps turning up as a balance. In the beer-houses of Kambaso it becomes the second theory of the palace dog: that the founding ancestor's bargain had left a balance, that the water had been owed something and had paid it back in the only coin it had. A Bantaran caravan-master's joke, the river's long debt, is a drunk's rendering of the same idea. In Chapter Fourteen the place of the bargain is a flat grey stone at the river's edge a mile below the ford, which the Recounters keep swept and which no one else visits: the faith held that the first ancestor had stood on it to ask the water for passage, and that the water had agreed at a price no living person could name. Fode has stood on it four hundred times. On the fifteenth night of Mavren he reaches it with a plan, and weighs out four hundred weights of gold-dust on it by starlight to Sira. The manuscript gives him no name, age or face, and does not say what the price was. The sheet's title strip, First ancestor of Danjeri's kings, and its age of about 40 are its own, as are the tall, lean build, deep brown skin, close-cropped black hair, short black beard, dark brown eyes, undyed cotton drape and bare feet on grey stone; a line at its foot says it is a mythic reconstruction; appearance invented for consistency. Its caption At the River's Edge, its scene He bargains with the river for safe passage. and its panels Calm Resolve, Cautious Hope, Solemn Bargain and Quiet Relief are the sheet's alone. bargained with the river itself for the right to cross it safely","i":"images/characters/founding-ancestor-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Caravan-Master","u":"characters/caravan-master.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"The caravan-master who recommends Sana to the palace · unnamed · 46 years old on his sheet","x":"Swears loudly and at length in a Kambaso beer-hall that a wandering diviner cured a mule-driver's lung-rot, and so puts a stranger at a dying boy's bedside. One sentence in Chapter Two. King Soriba, who has exhausted every physician his own court can produce, is in the mood to try anything that does not cost him standing, and Sana is brought to the palace on the recommendation of a caravan-master who had once sworn, loudly and at length in a Kambaso beer-hall, that the wandering diviner had cured a mule-driver's lung-rot with nothing but boiled roots and an unhurried bedside manner. The manuscript names neither the man nor the mule-driver and does not say whether the oath was true. A different caravan-master, Yero of Bantara, appears in Chapter Seven and has no sheet of his own; the manuscript does not connect the two. The manuscript gives an oath and a recommendation; no age, face or build. The sheet's age of 46, stocky 5 ft 8 in (173 cm) build, weathered wide face with a broken nose, grey-flecked cropped hair under a headcloth, dark brown sun-lined skin, thick moustache, dusty traveling robe, short knife and coiled mule-whip are its own, as are its panels Boastful, Conviction, Defiance and Earnest Vow and its scenes Loud Oath and He swears Sana cured the mule-driver. the wandering diviner had cured a mule-driver's lung-rot","i":"images/characters/caravan-master-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Soriba","u":"characters/soriba.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"King of Danjeri · father of Tiemoko and Damba · 45 years old when first met on his sheet","x":"Has exhausted every physician his court can produce, tries a wandering healer, and follows his queen to the grave three years after she dies. King of Danjeri, a practical man, who by the winter the manuscript first meets him had by then exhausted every physician his own court could produce for his second son, Damba, and was in the mood to try anything that did not cost him standing to try. That is how Sana reaches the palace: on the word of a caravan-master, as the household's ninth physician. The manuscript gives the king no scene of his own at the bedside. Queen consort Yarissa sits with Sana through Damba's last two nights, and it is she, seventeen days after the burial, who dictates the treasury line for the dog; she lives fourteen years more, never once enters the kennel yard, and dies in the dry season in her sleep without having asked after it. Soriba followed her three years later of a lung complaint his physicians declined to name. His elder son, Tiemoko, who has been sitting in on council mornings since he was thirteen and is now his only surviving son, is Weighed onto the throne in the month of Kerren. The beer-house of Chapter Seven fixes the dog's age against him: by its informal reckoning the dog had been a grown animal when King Soriba died. The manuscript gives Soriba his title, his temper and his illness, and no age, face or build. The sheet's title strip, King of the Soninke, and its age of 45 when first met are its own, with the 5 ft 11 in (180 cm) broad frame, deep brown skin, cropped hair flecked with grey, short grey beard, dark brown eyes, blunt practical hands and plain white-and-indigo court wrapper fastened with one plain gold clasp. It prints a line for him, A tired king carries a heavier crown than he shows., which is not in the manuscript, and its scene, The Council, draws him on a stool before the council, listening as he hires the ninth physician, in the sheet's own words. a practical man","i":"images/characters/soriba-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Damba","u":"characters/damba.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Second son of King Soriba and Queen consort Yarissa · the boy the Cell was for · 9 years old","x":"Asks a healer direct questions, learns that the dog's name means nothing, and dies on the ninth day of Garren with his hand in its fur. Nine years old, small for it, sick since before his youngest memory with a wasting, feverish complaint that recedes for months at a time and returns worse, which leaves him breathless on the palace's outer stair and, by his ninth winter, unable to sit through a full council session without his mother's hand finding the back of his neck to check it for heat. He is not expected to inherit anything; the throne will pass to his brother Tiemoko, four years older, who treats his illness with the mild, distracted kindness of someone who did not yet understand that kindness might be all that was left to offer. Sana is the ninth physician to try. Damba, unlike every adult in that palace, asked Sana direct questions and expected direct answers, and somewhere across a dozen visits stops treating the healer as a professional curiosity and starts treating him as a friend, the unhurried, unimpressed kind a sick child rarely gets, since everyone else who entered his room arrived already grieving him. On Sana's sixth visit the dog comes with him for the first time, and Damba is delighted by it in the undignified way of a child who has been solemn about his own fragility for far longer than any child should have to be. He learns that its name, Doni, means nothing at all, and asks whether nothing could still be a good name; Sana tells him, truthfully for once, that it is probably the best kind. After that the dog comes every time, and sleeps across the foot of his sleeping-mat. In the month of Ferren Sana decides to use the Immortal Cell for him, and loses it to the dog instead. The fever turns in the second week of Garren, faster than anything the illness has done before; Damba dies on the ninth day, a little before the dawn recitation-bell, with his hand loosely curled in the fur at the dog's neck. What survives him is a line and a sentence. The household Recounter adds his name to the spoken account within the hour; seventeen days after he is carried to the burial ground his mother dictates a ration for the dog to continue; the lower town comes to believe the dog is the boy himself, come back to stay; and forty-seven years later his brother, dying, sends a message to him through the Chief Recounter, I kept the dog fed, which the Account still holds in the line of Damba, son of Soriba, who died in his ninth year. The manuscript does not say whether he ever knew what Sana had planned. The manuscript gives his age, smallness, fever, the heat at the back of his neck, the dog on his mat and the hand in its fur. The sheet's 4 ft 1 in (124 cm), thin frame, deep brown skin with an ashen cast, fever-flushed forehead, short tight black curls, large direct dark-brown eyes, soft white cotton and straw mat are its own, as are its panels Fever, Trust, Quiet Joy and Fading Strength, and its scene Ninth Day of Garren — His Hand in the Dog's Fur. asked Sana direct questions and expected direct answers","i":"images/characters/damba-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Tiemoko","u":"characters/tiemoko.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Elder son of King Soriba, then King of Danjeri for thirty years · three wives, two sons he would not name · 60 years old at death","x":"Is Weighed onto the throne in a clear sky, sits silent beside a dog in the small hours, and dies with it on his feet, asking that a dead boy be told he kept it fed. A broad, patient, unremarkable boy of thirteen when the manuscript first meets him, four years older than Damba, already sitting in on his father's council mornings. After Soriba dies he is Weighed onto the throne of Danjeri in the month of Kerren, with the whole court in attendance and the sky over the Ancestor Court obligingly clear: the Weighing of Tiemoko is the one the histories describe at length, the last one anyone would remember as simple: the elders had settled it three years before, there was no rival, and Balla declared the thread of smoke to be acceptance. He takes three wives in a reign that runs thirty years, Awa, Binta and Sata, and has a son by each. A rumour of the lower town, that when his cough kept him wakeful he went down to the kennel yard in the small hours, alone, and sat on an upturned water-jar beside the dog and said nothing to it for an hour at a time, was true, and unknown to everyone who repeated the rest. His cough begins as a small dry thing at the back of the throat and becomes a wet thing in the chest. He declines, with the mild, hopeful stubbornness of a man who had always let other people's accounts balance themselves, to name either of his sons as heir, because the old custom forbade it: the Weighing named kings. He dies on the twelfth day of Iverin, in the fourth hour before dawn, with his feet under a wool blanket that has not managed that winter to warm them. He has not asked for his sons; he has received each for an hour on the third day and said nothing a court scribe could not have written in advance. He asks instead for Fode, whom he has known since Fode was a stammering novice, and for the dog. Kolo brings it up the servants' stair with a cloth over his arm; it climbs onto the bed, turns twice and lies across the old man's cold feet, and Tiemoko, who has not smiled in a month, smiles. He says only one thing more, to Fode: I kept the dog fed, to be told to Damba. He dies with the dog on his feet, the Chief Recounter in the doorway and neither son in the room. The manuscript gives his boyhood, his reign, the cough, the cold feet and the water-jar; it gives no age at death, but its own arithmetic (thirteen at Damba's death, forty-seven years later) makes sixty, which agrees with the sheet. The sheet's 5 ft 11 in (180 cm) broad build, deep brown skin, short grey hair and beard, dark brown eyes, patient face and white-and-indigo royal dress with a gold trim are its own, as are its panels Patience, Royal Resolve, The Wet Cough and Quiet Suffering and its scenes Final Illness and He asks for Fode and the dog. I kept the dog fed","i":"images/characters/tiemoko-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Household Recounter","u":"characters/household-recounter.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Recounter of the royal household · unnamed · 45 years old on his sheet","x":"Begins, within the hour of the boy's death, the formal addition that carries Damba's name into the kingdom's spoken account. One clause in Chapter Four. Damba dies on the ninth day of Garren, a little before the dawn recitation-bell, with his hand curled in the fur at the dog's neck, and the household's Recounter begins, within the hour, the formal genealogical addition that will carry the boy's name and his unremarkable, unfinished nine years forward into the kingdom's spoken account, for as long as anyone in Danjeri kept remembering to say it aloud. The manuscript gives him no name, no words and no further appearance. His work is the kind that the rest of the book leans on. The Kept Account holds that anything entrusted to a written page can be altered by a living hand, so a dead child enters it by being said. Forty-seven years later, when King Tiemoko dies asking that the Chief Recounter tell Damba that he kept the dog fed, the Account's formula for such errands is that a living word to the dead is added, in the plain grammar of a deed, to the dead person's own line. The line the household Recounter began is the one to which Fode adds the sentence, and the book's last paragraphs have every Recounter still reciting it: in the line of Damba, son of Soriba, who died in his ninth year, one sentence that none of them can gloss. The manuscript does not say whether the household Recounter was the man who first set down that line, nor that he lived to hear the sentence added. The manuscript gives the office, the hour and the formal addition; the rest is invented. The sheet's age of 45, deep brown skin, close-cropped black hair, dark brown eyes, clean-shaven composed face and braided cord, with the earth-brown wrapper, are its own, as are its caption Genealogy Recited from Memory, its scene Damba's Name Entered and its panels Dignity, Solemn Grief, Concentration and Formal Resolve. the household's Recounter began, within the hour, the formal genealogical addition","i":"images/characters/household-recounter-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Balla","u":"characters/balla.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Aged Recounter · declares the sign at Tiemoko's Weighing · 78 years old on his sheet","x":"Reads a thread of smoke that hesitated and bent east as acceptance, at the last Weighing anyone remembered as simple. One sentence in Chapter Five, and the sentence carries a coronation. The Weighing of Tiemoko is the one the histories describe at length, because it was the last one anyone would remember as simple. The elders had settled the matter in private three years before, there was no rival, and the sign, when it came, was a thread of smoke from the sacrificial fire that hesitated, bent east, and was declared by the aged Recounter Balla to be acceptance. The court exhaled. He is the only Recounter the manuscript shows declaring a sign for a king. The book sets down the arrangement as it stood: in the four hundred years the Recounters could account for, the Weighing had never once put a question to the ancestors whose answer was not already known to everyone present. Balla's reading costs nothing, because nobody present needs the smoke to say anything but yes. At the next Weighing the smoke rises straight up and then splits, the bull sits down, and the Chief Recounter is Fode, who declares nothing. The manuscript does not say whether Balla was the senior Recounter who recited Tiemoko's line, what rank he held, or what became of him; the Chief Recounter before Fode is Nyeleni, and the manuscript does not connect the two. The manuscript gives his name and his epithet, aged. The sheet's age of 78, dark brown skin with an ashen cast, cropped white hair, sparse white beard, milky-brown half-lidded eyes, thin bent frame, trembling hands, staff and braided shoulder cord are its own. It prints a line for him, The ancestors do not speak loudly. They speak in what remains., which is not in the manuscript. Its scenes, The Acceptance and The Ancestors Accept Tiemoko, draw him before the fire with a thread of smoke rising; its panels are Gravitas, Doubt, Recognition and Quiet Certainty. declared by the aged Recounter Balla to be acceptance","i":"images/characters/balla-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sitting Elders","u":"characters/sitting-elders.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Twelve heads of the kingdom's oldest lineages · the stools along the eastern wall · 52 to 78 on their sheet","x":"Settle every Weighing at dinner, are left for the first time with a rite that will not answer, and find that a sign can be offered to them with its hole already cut. Twelve heads of the kingdom's oldest lineages, who meet in the Ancestor Court on the fifth day of every week, on stools along the eastern wall, and who governed, in every sense that mattered to a farmer or a toll-keeper, everything the king had not the time to govern. They settled Tiemoko's Weighing three years in advance, over a long succession of dinners at which nobody had said the word. They cannot settle the next. Awa's lineage holds four of the twelve stools, Binta's holds, through the gold-weighers' guild, the market's credit, and Tiemoko, with the mild, hopeful stubbornness of a man who had always let other people's accounts balance themselves, will not name either son. When the smoke splits and the bull sits down they are left, for the first time in four hundred years, with the terrible impartiality of a rite that had finally been asked a question and had, courteously, declined to answer it. They meet four times between the Weighing and the Second Asking, and on none of those evenings does anyone say aloud what the meeting is about. No elder is willing to propose smoke or bull, and none to propose nothing. It is Awa's steward Kassa who makes the proposal sound like a courtesy, that the dog be the whole of the Asking, and the elders, who had spent four hundred years learning to find the hole in a sign and had never once been offered a sign with its own hole already cut, agree on the fourth night and put to Fode the single question the rite allows: whether the Account would stand upon whatever the dog did. They also set the dog down three paces from Alassane, on a second proposal of the same steward's that no one examines. When Fode speaks, they rise to speak and are drowned. After the second night of the war four of the twelve leave with Awa, and the court is left with four stools empty; the sandbar terms, in the end, restore them. The manuscript gives their number, stools, day of meeting and the four of Awa's lineage; it names none of them and says nothing of their ages or faces. The sheet's ages of 52 to 78, mostly in their sixties, deep-brown aged skin, white or grey cropped or wrapped hair, mixed beards, white-and-indigo cloth with a gold-thread border, and twelve distinct lineage tokens on cords are its own. So are its panels Gravitas, Quiet Scrutiny, Deliberation and Final Judgment, and its scene Private dinner: settling a Weighing. a sign with its own hole already cut","i":"images/characters/sitting-elders-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Treasury Clerk (the First Entry)","u":"characters/treasury-clerk-first-entry.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Treasury clerk who writes the dog's first ration line · unnamed · 26 years old at the first entry on his sheet","x":"Writes down a queen's dictation without being told why, takes its closing words for padding, and copies the line forward for the whole of a long career. The first entry appears in the household accounts of the palace at Kambaso in the month of Havren, seventeen days after the boy Damba is carried out to the burial ground, in the flat hand of a treasury clerk who had not been told why he was writing it: For the dog of the boy Damba — millet, four measures by the moon; dried fish, six; to continue. Queen Yarissa dictates it herself, dry-eyed, from her sleeping-mat, in the voice she reserves for matters she does not intend to discuss. The clerk takes the closing words for a formula, a piece of the chancery's ordinary padding, and never grasps that a queen in mourning has just bound the royal treasury to an obligation with no term. He lives to be a very old man, and spends the whole of a long career copying the line forward from one ledger to the next without once being asked to justify it. The palace keeps its accounts in a mercantile script borrowed from the caravan trade, scratched onto strips of scraped hide and stitched into long folding books, and the Kept Account, which would not trust an ancestor to ink, has never extended the same suspicion to grain. A dog's ration can therefore be written down by anyone, in any hand, and altered by anyone with a reason to alter it, and nobody in the treasury ever does: the line simply continued, month after month, the longest sentence anyone in Danjeri would ever fail to finish. Forty-two years later the Observer Yandé finds it copied forward without a change of a single word; the Epilogue has no auditor in three centuries querying it. The manuscript does not say whether the clerk who opens the accounts to Yandé in Chapter Eight is the same man; the sheets draw them as two, and the second has a page of his own. The manuscript gives the clerk his office, his flat hand, his incomprehension and his long career. The sheet's age of 26 at the first entry, slim 5 ft 8 in (173 cm) build, narrow careful face, dark brown eyes, deep brown skin, close-cropped black hair, clean-shaven jaw, plain wrapper, ink-stained cuffs and stitched folding hide-books are its own, as are its panels Composure, Attention, Routine and Unquestioning. Its scene, The first line for the dog — copied forward for a long career, draws a dog on the floor beside the table; the manuscript does not put the dog in the room. the longest sentence anyone in Danjeri would ever fail to finish","i":"images/characters/treasury-clerk-first-entry-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Awa","u":"characters/awa.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Queen, first wife of King Tiemoko · daughter of an ancient lineage of the Sitting Elders · keeps two bowls of beans · 59 years old on her sheet","x":"Keeps accounts of what she is owed and what she owes in dried beans, tells her son he will be pensioned, and asks her steward to clear the sign of noise. The first wife, the daughter of one of the Sitting Elders' oldest lineages, married to Tiemoko while he was still a prince, who brings to the marriage a dowry of toll-rights on the eastern road and a temperament the palace describes, with careful neutrality, as exacting. She sits up with her son Alassane through the nightmares of his eighth year, counting his breaths in the dark, because she kept accounts even of that. When Binta's son Sekou breaks his arm at twelve she sends her own bone-setter across the palace at a run, unasked, with instructions to spare nothing: the last uncalculated kindness she would ever do anyone outside her own blood. The beer-house of Chapter Seven says she feeds the dog from her own hand every ninth day for luck, which is false. Her own ledger is two bowls of dried beans beside her sleeping-mat, one for what she is owed and one for what she owes, moved from one to the other every night for forty years and never mentioned to anyone. On the night of the Weighing she does not touch them: The columns were perfectly clear. The first column is long: the eastern toll-rights to the gold-weighers' guild within the year, four of the twelve stools to lineages that never forgave her family for holding them, her brothers' posts at the salt-toll to Binta's nephews, and herself pensioned. The second is the boy. A defeated candidate is not killed in Danjeri; he was permitted to go on, which was the crueler of the two outcomes. She tells Alassane that there remains the Second Asking, which is his by right, and that You will be asked to stand in a court and let an animal look at you; she takes his face in both hands and says that he will not be pitied, he will be pensioned. She was not a wicked woman, the manuscript says, and the histories, which needed her to be, would have to work at it. She tells her steward Kassa to see that the animal is quiet on the day, and never once says the word for what she has asked. At the Second Asking she watches from the gallery as the dog looks at her son, sees the bright dark eyes, and understands that she has failed before she understands why. On the second night of the war she leaves by the eastern gate in a closed litter with Alassane, her steward and four of the twelve elders, the toll-houses of the eastern road already in her pocket. When Alassane is killed in the shallows she is found beside the body, making a low continuous sound with no words in it and counting his breaths, and it was not grief as the songs have it. It was a mechanism failing. Both bowls are overturned and she cannot tell which beans were owed and which paid. The trial is dropped in the peace; she is permitted to live out her days in the house she has been given, where she counted nothing at all and was said, in the last years, to speak of her son as though he were in the next room. The manuscript gives her lineage, dowry, temperament and beans; it gives no age, face or build, and it has her stop blinking for one long moment at the Weighing, which implies that she ordinarily does. The sheet's age of 59, tall, spare, very upright frame, iron-grey plaits under an indigo-and-white head wrap, dark-brown hooded eyes that almost never blink, gold pendants and indigo-and-white dress are its own, as are its panels Authority, Suspicion, Grief and Empty Resolve and its scenes After Alassane and Leaves Kambaso with her son and four elders when war begins. You will be asked to stand in a court and let an animal look at you.","i":"images/characters/awa-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Binta","u":"characters/binta.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Second wife of King Tiemoko · a gold-weigher's daughter · mother of Sekou · 57 years old on her sheet","x":"Places a six-year-old in a kennel yard where no thief would look, and opens the gold-weighers' credit to him without limit and without mention. The second wife, a gold-weigher's daughter, a woman of the market rather than the lineages, who arrives with no tolls at all and a habit of asking what things cost. Raised among scales, she understands at once the arithmetic of her position: a second wife's son has, in the ordinary run of things, exactly one asset, which is to be no one's enemy. She therefore sends Sekou, from the age of six, to the only place in the palace where a boy can be useful to no faction whatever, the kennel yard, with the cold exact calculation of a woman placing a small sum where no thief would think to look and with no idea that it would compound. The beer-house of Chapter Seven says she had the dog weighed against its own weight in millet by a bribed clerk, to see whether it grew heavier with the years, which is false. On the night of the Weighing she does her sums aloud by lamplight with a pair of traveling scales set out as her father taught her, and reaches her total faster than Awa reaches hers, because she never expected the entry to come due: she had placed a boy, not a dog, and a return that exceeded the sum placed by a margin no scale in Danjeri could take is not, to a woman raised among weights, a profit, since whatever produced it could neither be asked what it wanted nor made to account for itself. She sends no word to her son. She sends before dawn one factor of her father's guild with one instruction, that the gold-weighers' credit stand open to the second prince without limit and without mention, the largest loan of her life, and it appears in no book. Before the sandbar the king comes to her at the gold-weighers' compound with the terms he means to offer, laid out the way a customer lays goods on a counter, and she tells him two things: that a peace bought with a king's own credit would hold exactly as long as no one mentioned the price, and that it is the only peace in the market at that figure, and that if he does not buy it someone else will, later, at a higher one, in other people's blood. Forgive it quietly, she tells him, or you will be forgiving it every year: the longest speech she ever made to him, and she does not remind him that she put him in a kennel yard at six. In the Epilogue she keeps the credit open for the rest of his reign, neither of them ever mentioning it, the most accurately kept account in Danjeri, and it was never entered anywhere. The manuscript gives her trade-bred habits, her scales, her loan and her one speech, and no age, face or build. The sheet's age of 57, round shrewd face, grey-streaked plaits beneath a plain white wrap, watchful dark-brown eyes, broad-hipped compact build and brass weight at the throat are its own, as are its caption Pride (Guild-Born) and its panels Concern (A Mother), Calculation (Before the Sandbar) and Resolve (Credit Without Limit). Its three scenes, She counsels Sekou before the sandbar., She opens the gold-weighers' credit to Sekou without limit. and She sends Sekou toward the kennel yard., all draw Sekou as a small boy, which fits only the last: the sandbar and the credit belong to the man of thirty-six to thirty-eight. a peace bought with a king's own credit would hold exactly as long as no one mentioned the price","i":"images/characters/binta-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sata","u":"characters/sata.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Third wife of King Tiemoko · mother of Bakary, sister of Sory · 44 years old on her sheet","x":"Is remembered chiefly as the mother of a boy, and after the boy is killed carries his water-skin to her brother's hanging and does not eat. The third wife, young and cheerful, remembered chiefly as the mother of a boy: Bakary, born fourteen winters after Sekou, when the first two sons were already old enough to have opinions about him. When he is brought home across a gate-shutter with the water-skin still on his shoulder, her cry is heard in the lower town. She would never again eat a meal in company. Her brother Sory, a warden who loved the boy as a second son, hears it too, and goes out into the street with his hands empty and comes back with them full. When Sekou hangs Sory at dawn on the ninth of Kerren from the old tamarind in the gold-weighing yard, Sata asks to be present. She stands at the foot of the tree in the wrapper she has worn since the day they carried Bakary home, with the water-skin in her hands, which Sory had kept on a nail above his sleeping-mat and she took down the night before, and she does not look at her brother's face. She looked at his feet, which hung a little above the market dust, bare, and she looked at them without blinking until a warden cut him down, and then walks home with the water-skin held against her chest as she once held a boy, and does not eat. The manuscript does not say what becomes of her after. The manuscript gives her youth, her cheerfulness, her son and her brother; no age, face or build. The sheet's age of 44, slender 5 ft 5 in (165 cm) frame, medium-deep brown skin, round hollowed face, soft dark-brown eyes, black plaits under a pale ivory wrap and worn plain wrapper are its own, as are its panels Remembered Joy, A Mother, Silent Grief and Endurance and its scenes At Sory's Hanging and She No Longer Eats in Company. Sory has no sheet and so no page here. She would never again eat a meal in company.","i":"images/characters/sata-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bakary","u":"characters/bakary.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Prince, Sata's son, King Tiemoko's youngest · the water-carrier · 22 years old","x":"Comes down from the upper gallery because someone is going to take his brother's dog, offers his water-skin to a captain, and is hit by a spear nobody meant to throw. Sata's son, born fourteen winters after Sekou, the boy who had been born to arrive fourteen years after the rest. On the ninth day of Kerren he is twenty-two, and has never held a spear in anger or been asked to. He comes down from the upper gallery only because he has heard that someone is going to take his brother's dog: Captain Wuro of the palace guard has walked twenty men into the kennel yard, arrested Kolo for sacrilege and taken the dog, on a rope, as evidence. It is a hot dry morning, the wardens have been shouting for an hour, and he has a water-skin over his shoulder, which he is offering to a captain called Mory, who has been shouting longest. A boy on the wall, one of a guard who are nineteen and have not slept, throws a spear at the captain. It misses him by a hand, and hits the water-carrier. Nobody had meant it. That was, afterward, the only part of it anyone could agree on, and it helped no one. They bring him home across a gate-shutter with the water-skin still on his shoulder. By nightfall there is an open line in the ledger of the dead, and in Danjeri only one thing can be done with an open line. His mother Sata never again eats a meal in company; her brother Sory keeps the water-skin on a nail above his sleeping-mat, and she takes it down for his hanging. Mory, who was among the first to reach the palace gate on the morning Bakary died, is the captain who takes off his cap in the yard when he finds Kolo with the dead dog across his knees. In the Epilogue the Recounters' fourteen lines on the war name the deaths of Bakary, of Alassane and of Sory, in that order. The manuscript gives his age, his birth order, the water-skin, the offer and the spear, and no face or build. The sheet's slim, slightly lanky 5 ft 8 in (173 cm) build, round open cheerful face, short uneven black hair, warm dark-brown eyes, smooth deep brown skin, faint dimple in the left cheek, faint moustache and gourd water-skin on a faded strap are its own, as are its panels Cheerful (At ease), Curious (Listening), Concerned (Alarmed) and Peaceful (Accepting fate). Its scene, He kneels, offering his water-skin to a captain, not knowing the spear already flies., has him kneeling, which the manuscript does not; its side notes, Killed by a stray spear while offering his water-skin to a captain, which opens the war and The water-skin, later kept on a nail by his uncle, follow it. Nobody had meant it.","i":"images/characters/bakary-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Hawa","u":"characters/hawa.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Widow of a dismissed crown weigher · keeper of the Weighmaster's Table · brewer of Grey-Never · sixty in Chapter Seven, 67 on her sheet","x":"Waters her beer with a steadiness the street admires, sells a dark brew called Grey-Never by the horn, and sends a keg to the palace kennel every Uncounted Day. The widow of a crown weigher who lost his post, and the owner of the Weighmaster's Table, a low mud-brick beer-house at the lower end of Kambaso's gold-weighing yard whose thatch has been repaired so often that no single straw of it is older than a decade: a broad-armed woman of sixty who watered her millet beer with a steadiness the whole street admired and no one had ever managed to prove. It is at her tables that the palace dog's long life becomes talk, and then theory; that a drunk Bantaran caravan-master calls it the river's long debt, in front of a Recounter in a plain brown wrapper who does not laugh; and that the fishmonger Dembele says what is, by the standards of every age before and since, entirely right, and is forgotten within the week. Her darker brew, sold by the horn and called Grey-Never, outsells her ordinary beer four to one. On every Uncounted Day since it was named she sends a small keg of it up to the palace kennel with her compliments, to be drunk at dusk by the wardens on the kennel wall and the kennelmaster together, the one night of the year on which nothing said or done is entered anywhere. Kolo, who after the business with the fish feeds the dog from his own pouch, keeps this one exception, since he could not have refused it in front of the wardens without announcing aloud that he suspected the woman who sent it. When Tuma flees to her tables on the night of the Second Asking and drinks two horns faster than is wise, she, who has never in thirty years turned a paying customer away, or a rumour either, puts a third horn in his hand and tells him to start again from the beginning. The keg Kolo drinks on the last Uncounted Day has been dosed at her back door by Sira. The manuscript does not say whether Hawa ever knows, and does not say what becomes of her; in the Epilogue her granddaughter's brewery sells a dark ale for two hundred years, and the manuscript does not say it is the same brew. The manuscript gives her trade, her widowhood, her arms, her age in Chapter Seven and her third horn. The sheet's age of 67 follows from the manuscript's sixty, seven years on; its deep brown skin, round creased calm face, grey plaits beneath a wine-red wrap, stained indigo dress and worn leather apron are its own, as are its panels Patient, Stern Resolve, Wry Amusement and Quiet Kindness and its scene The Third Horn for Tuma. a broad-armed woman of sixty who watered her millet beer with a steadiness the whole street admired and no one had ever managed to prove","i":"images/characters/hawa-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Dembele","u":"characters/dembele.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Fishmonger to the palace kennel for thirty years · the one dissenter · 55 years old on his sheet","x":"Sells the palace kennel its dried fish for thirty years, concludes from the invoices that the dog is only a dog with a very good accountant, and is forgotten within the week. One man in the Weighmaster's Table dissents from the theories about the palace dog. He was the fishmonger Dembele, who had sold the palace kennel its dried fish for thirty years, and who had concluded, on the evidence of thirty years of invoices, that the animal was merely a dog with a very good accountant. He says so once, on a slow evening, to the room at large: Nothing that eats fish, he says, has ever been a god. The manuscript's verdict is flat and complete: He was, by the standards of every age before and since, entirely right, and the beer-house forgot him within the week. He has no other scene. The fish he sold is the dried fish of the treasury's ration line, dried fish, six, though the manuscript does not say whether the invoices he held were for that line. It does not say what became of him, or whether he lived to see the dog killed. Hawa's beer-house, where he spoke, forgets him; Kolo later buys the dog's fish with his own copper in the lower market, and the manuscript does not connect the two. The manuscript gives his trade, his thirty years and his sentence. The sheet's age of 55, wiry build, deep brown skin, shaved head, dark brown eyes, short grey goatee, fish-scale flecks on the forearms and tally cords, undyed cloth and worn leather apron are its own, as are its panels Skepticism, Dry Amusement, Defiance and Quiet Resolve, its title strip, Palace Kennel Fishmonger, and its scene At the Weighmaster's Table, which prints his sentence unbroken: Nothing that eats fish has ever been a god. the animal was merely a dog with a very good accountant","i":"images/characters/dembele-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bantaran Horse-Lords","u":"characters/bantaran-horse-lords.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"The horse-lords of Bantara, two weeks' march beyond the Ghelo salt-pans · four thousand riders in Deyren · 22 to 50 on their sheet","x":"Watch the trade roads like herons at a pond, ride in with four thousand riders in the second spring of the war, and do not ride out again. Bantara lies to the east, two weeks' march beyond the Ghelo salt-pans: a kingdom of caravan-towns and horse-lords that had grown fat on the same trade roads Danjeri guarded and had never quite forgiven Danjeri for standing on them. Its merchants come and go at Kambaso as easily as weather and carry away the town's stories with their salt. A Bantaran caravan-master, Yero, names the palace dog; a Bantaran poet makes a song of it. In the second spring of the war the horse-lords move. Bantara's horse-lords, who had been watching the trade roads like herons at a pond, rode in during Deyren with four thousand riders, took the pans, took the ford of the Second Road while they were about it, and did not ride out again. Neither claimant can afford to defend the eastern road's toll-houses, and neither does. The riders are reported afterward to have expressed surprise at how easy it had been. By the second autumn the salt caravans are diverted through Bantaran towns and a measure of millet in Kambaso sells for what a measure of gold-dust cost in Tiemoko's day. When the war exhausts itself, the Bantaran riders at the Ghelo pans had begun to look past the horizon at what the ruined kingdom might still be made to yield. The pans and the ford are not recovered for a generation, and when they were, they were recovered at a price. The histories blame the agents of Bantara for the war; a later century, finding that the horse-lords had in fact done rather well out of it, concludes that they must have started it. The Archival Log is plainer: a neighbouring power that fought neither claimant and took what both had left undefended. The manuscript names no horse-lord, and does not describe a battle. The sheet's ages of 22 to 50, lean long-limbed builds (173 to 185 cm), medium to deep brown wind-burnt skin, braided hair beneath indigo turbans, dark-brown eyes, indigo-stained fingertips, leather charms at the throat and brass bells on the bridles are its own. Its printed line, Four thousand riders. They take the ford and do not ride out again., follows the manuscript; its panels are Vigilance, Grim Resolve, Quiet Command and Before the Charge, and its scenes Full Dress, Mounted — Rear View and The Ghelo Salt Pans — The Second Road. had been watching the trade roads like herons at a pond","i":"images/characters/bantaran-horse-lords-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Wardens of the Road-Stations","u":"characters/wardens-of-the-road-stations.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Sekou's wardens · keepers of the wells and caravan shelters on Danjeri's four roads · about a thousand called up · 20 to 55 on their sheet","x":"A thousand men called up to keep order at the Second Asking, who come up to the palace with staves and sword-belts and no plan, and hold it by the second night of the war. The office is the unglamorous one that sees to the wells and caravan shelters on Danjeri's four roads, which the palace has given to Binta's son Sekou on the principle that no one has ever been assassinated for a well; the wardens are the men who work under it. In the three weeks after the Weighing they drift into Kambaso along the four roads, a thousand men called up by their Warden to keep order at the Second Asking, quartered by the hundred in the beer-houses of the lower town at nobody's expense. When Captain Wuro arrests the kennelmaster and takes the dog on a rope, they come up to the palace in the course of a single morning with staves and sword-belts and no plan, and are joined at the gate by the gold-weighers' guild and by a great many ordinary citizens who have eaten Grey-Never bread for forty years and do not intend to watch it be arrested. When a boy on the wall throws a spear, they are the men on the other side of the gate. By the second night of the war Sekou's wardens held the palace; they find the kennelmaster in the cellar of the guardhouse with a split lip and the dog asleep across his feet. The manuscript uses the word for two bodies of men. The wardens on the kennel wall drink Hawa's Uncounted Day keg with Kolo at dusk, are asleep at their posts when Sira comes over the back wall, and find him at midday with the dead dog across his knees; six of them die when a party of Awa's riders tries the wall in the autumn. It does not say whether they are the same men as the thousand called up from the roads, though the war leaves the palace and its wall in their hands. The manuscript gives their number, their arms and their office; no ages, faces or colouring. The sheet's ages of 20 to 55, mostly in their thirties, deep brown sun-baked skin, dark brown eyes, cropped hair, weathered faces, mixed facial hair, earth-toned wrappers and dark leather sword-belts are its own. Its scenes, Called Up to the Palace, A Thousand Men, No Plan and Drinking with the Kennelmaster on the Uncounted Day, treat the two bodies as one, as do its panels Vigilance, Uncertainty, Weariness and Camaraderie. with staves and sword-belts and no plan","i":"images/characters/wardens-of-the-road-stations-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Treasury Clerk (the Household Accounts)","u":"characters/treasury-clerk-household-accounts.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Treasury clerk who shows Yandé the household accounts · unnamed · 34 years old on his sheet","x":"Lets a trader look at the palace's household accounts for a fistful of cowries and the promise of a better rate on salt. One sentence in Chapter Eight. In the dry season of the forty-second year after Damba's death, the trader Yandé, who is Observer 1,451, has been in Kambaso some weeks and has bought nothing. A treasury clerk, in exchange for a fistful of her cowries and the promise of a better rate on salt, lets her look at the household accounts for the previous year, on the understanding that she is auditing the palace's kitchen supplies, which she is not. She finds the line on the third strip, For the dog of the boy Damba — millet, four measures by the moon; dried fish, six; to continue., copied forward without a change of a single word from a ledger forty-two years old. She reads it three times. The line was not an error. It was not, strictly speaking, anything: a habit, written down. The manuscript gives the clerk a bargain and nothing else: no name, age, words or later appearance, and no account of whether he knows what she is looking for. It does not say whether he is the clerk who wrote the line in the first place, forty-two years earlier, who was to live a very old man; the sheets draw two men, and the first has his own page. The manuscript gives the bargain, the cowries and the salt. The sheet's age of 34, slight build, medium-deep brown skin, dark brown eyes, close-cropped hair, thin moustache, cowries tied at the belt, ink-stained cuffs and muted earth-brown dress are its own, as are its panels Pride (Bargaining), Reluctance, Calculation and Guarded Agreement and its scenes The Bargain and He shows Yandé the household accounts. let her look at the household accounts for the previous year","i":"images/characters/treasury-clerk-household-accounts-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Nyeleni","u":"characters/nyeleni.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Former Chief Recounter · the ancient Nyeleni · died two winters before Tiemoko · about 86 on the sheet","x":"Is named once, as the dead Chief Recounter whose office Fode inherits two winters before the king dies. The manuscript names Nyeleni once, in Chapter Nine, and gives no pronoun. The dying King Tiemoko asks for Fode, who had been made Chief Recounter two winters earlier on the death of the ancient Nyeleni, and whom the king had known since Fode was a stammering novice. That is all: no age, no words, no cause of death, no account of how long the office was held, no appearance. It is enough to date the change, and the book leans on it. The Chief Recounter who stands at the mouth of the Ancestor Court for the Weighing of the two sons, and who carries sentences across the sandbar, and who pays for the dog's death out of the Kept Account's reserve, has held the office for two winters when the king dies. The manuscript does not say whether Nyeleni was the Chief Recounter at Tiemoko's own Weighing, where Balla declares the sign. The sheet's title strip, Former Chief Recounter, Died at about 86, Succeeded by Fode and Dead two winters before Tiemoko, restates the manuscript's one clause and adds the age. Its drawing, a frail man of about 5 ft 5 in (165 cm) with a gaunt serene face, cropped white hair, sparse white beard, dark brown clouded eyes, a plain wrapper, a cord of office and an acacia staff, is the artist's, as are its panels Quiet Authority, Remembrance, Weariness and Final Peace and its scene Beneath the Acacia. on the death of the ancient Nyeleni","i":"images/characters/nyeleni-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bantaran Poet","u":"characters/bantaran-poet.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Poet of Bantara · composer of the song of the palace dog · unnamed · 35 years old on his sheet","x":"Composes a song about the palace dog whose refrain rhymes grey with stay, and sees it sung in caravan camps by men who could not find Kambaso on a map. In the five years between the trader's arrival and the king's death, a Bantaran poet composed a song about it, whose refrain rhymed grey with stay and was sung in caravan camps as far as the northern wells by men who could not have found Kambaso on a map. The dog's fame, which had been a marvel, has become a habit. The poet is named nowhere and speaks nowhere. In Chapter Fourteen the song has a second use: Fode, rejecting the idea of sending the dog over the border, sees a sand-colored animal that does not age in every caravan camp where a Bantaran poet's refrain was already sung, and concludes that a sign that can be sighted can be claimed. The manuscript gives the poet, the song and the refrain; it does not give a line of the song, a name, an age or a face, and does not say whether the poet has seen the dog. The sheet's age of 35, medium-deep brown skin, short braids, trimmed beard, long expressive face, dark brown eyes, thumb callus, indigo embroidered robe and four-string lute are its own. Its line, A song for the campfire; a rhyme the palace cannot keep., is its own too; its panels are Wry Amusement, Listening, Composing and Quiet Pride, and its scene is The Palace Dog's Song, which sets him by a campfire among listeners. a Bantaran poet composed a song about it, whose refrain rhymed grey with stay","i":"images/characters/bantaran-poet-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Mansa","u":"characters/mansa.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Second-rank Recounter · the girl who cried the long debt · 24 at the Weighing","x":"Hears a crowd's gap and fills it with a formula, takes a beer-house joke into the mouth of the faith, and is never able to forgive herself for it. Twenty-four, in the second rank of the Recounters at the Weighing of the two sons, brilliant, a little in love with the faith's own grammar. When the sand-colored dog crosses the Ancestor Court and sits on Sekou's left foot, she hears the sound the crowd is making and understands it as a gap into which a formula wanted to fall. Before any of her seniors can stop her she raises both arms and cries out, in the high liturgical voice, that the long debt was paid: that the river had sent its change home to the rightful hand. The court takes it up before she has finished. It is the folk-theory of the Weighmaster's Table lifted, in a single morning and by a single girl, out of the beer-house and into the mouth of the faith, and it would never again be put back. The Chief Recounter, Fode, does nothing, and the doing is his; the formula is hers. The Epilogue has her as his pupil for the eleven years he outlives the war, who had cried the name at the Weighing and had never entirely forgiven herself for it, learning to recite a war that contained no dog. She sees what he is doing by the third recitation and tells him so: You are teaching me to forget it. On his last night he asks her to enter one further deed into his own line, four hundred weights, weighed on the river-stone on the Uncounted Day, and she refuses, the only time in forty years as a Recounter she disobeys a senior, out of a love she could not have explained to him or to herself. She carries the deed unentered for thirty-one years, teaches it to no one, and it goes into the ground with her. The manuscript does not say what Fode told her of it beyond the sum and the day. The manuscript gives her age, her rank, her mind, her raised arms and the cry; no face, build or colouring. The sheet's deep brown skin, sharp bright face, close-plaited hair under a narrow head cord, slight 5 ft 5 in (165 cm) build and bright dark brown eyes are its own. It prints her cry as The long debt is paid., where the manuscript has it only in reported speech. Its scenes are Fode teaches the war with no dog. and Carries the last deed unentered for 31 years., its caption Raised Arms · Liturgy, its panels Pride, Grief, Refusal and Resolve. You are teaching me to forget it","i":"images/characters/mansa-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Tuma","u":"characters/tuma.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Kennel hand from the lower town · paid in gold to salt the dog's fish · 15 years old","x":"Is paid a coin heavier than anything he has held to season a dog's fish for six nights, and confesses, drunk, to a beer-house. A boy of fifteen from the lower town, the youngest hand in the palace kennel, whose mother's roof leaks and who has not been paid in two years. The queen's steward Kassa puts into his palm a coin heavier than anything he has ever held and tells him that a friend of the palace would be glad if the dog's evening fish were seasoned, for six nights, with a little of something that would help it rest; the twist of folded leaf is handed to him at the kitchen gate by Kassa's servant. Tuma understands that he is being asked to do something wrong, and, more sharply, that it is very small, and that it would pay for a roof. He salts the fish. The dog, who does not know it is being bribed, licks the hand that feeds it, eats, and sleeps very soundly, and is, in the morning, a little slow. Kolo sees a boy's whole guilty spine across the yard and says nothing to him. After the failed Second Asking Tuma flees to the Weighmaster's Table that same night with the last of the coin in his fist, drinks two horns of Grey-Never faster than is wise, and tells the whole room, in the thick, high, tearful voice of a boy who has discovered that he is in something too large for him, that the queen had made him salt the Long Debt's fish. Hawa puts a third horn in his hand and tells him to start again from the beginning. She only wanted it calm, he says. By dawn the story has reached the palace, the market, the road-stations and the gold-weighers' yard, and has acquired improvements: the herb has become poison, the dog has all but died, the queen has stood over the fish herself with a spoon. In the Epilogue Tuma was never charged with anything, and mends the roof, which leaks again within the decade. The manuscript gives fifteen, the lower town, the leaking roof and the guilty spine; the rest is invented. The sheet's thin build of about 5 ft 3 in (160 cm), round frightened face, close-cropped black hair, dark brown eyes, deep brown skin, ragged short wrapper and heavy gold coin are its own, as are its panels Fear, Guilt, Confession and Relief and its scenes Six Nights' Payment, Confession at the Weighmaster's Table and He confesses; he is never charged. She only wanted it calm","i":"images/characters/tuma-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Boy at the Market Gate","u":"characters/boy-at-the-market-gate.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"A boy selling leather goods at the market gate · unnamed · 10 years old on his sheet","x":"Sells a trader the single small leather sandal she cannot say why she wanted, and puts a mystery into a hill of stolen shoes. One clause in Chapter Eleven. On the evening she watches Kassa's servant press a twist of folded leaf into a kennel-boy's hand, Observer 1,451 buys something for the first time in five years, on the way home, from a boy at the market gate: a single small leather sandal, which she could not have said why she wanted. The sandal does more work than the sale. Her Interlude III note reports that her desk is in possession of one small leather sandal and is unable to account for the purchase. On the Uncounted Day, eleven minutes late, she finds the dog dead and walks across the yard to the heap of stolen shoes and puts it back. When Kolo buries the dog there is, on top of the hill, a small right-footed shoe of good leather, laid down as carefully as an offering, though the dog had only ever stolen left-footed ones; for eighteen years he works at who might have returned a shoe the dog never took, and never solves it. The manuscript does not say outright that the shoe on the hill is the one she bought. The manuscript gives a boy at a gate and a sale; no age, face or name. The sheet's age of 10, small wiry build of about 4 ft 6 in (137 cm), quick grinning face, close-shaved hair, dark brown eyes, deep brown skin, bare scabbed knees, ragged earth-brown wrapper and tray of cheap leather goods are its own, as are its panels Quick Grin, Curiosity, Cautious Bargain and Pleased Sale and its scene He sells the small leather sandal to Yandé. a single small leather sandal, which she could not have said why she wanted","i":"images/characters/boy-at-the-market-gate-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kassa","u":"characters/kassa.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Steward to Queen Awa for thirty years · soft-handed · 62 years old on his sheet","x":"Understands, better than his mistress does, exactly what she has asked, and then makes each next step sound like a courtesy. Awa's steward, a soft-handed man who had served her thirty years and who understood, better than she did, exactly what she had asked. When Awa, who never says the word for it, tells him to see that the animal is quiet on the day, he goes to the lower town, to the woman who sells sleep by the pinch, and pays her price in gold; he then finds the youngest hand in the palace kennel, a boy of fifteen named Tuma, whose mother's roof leaks, and puts into his palm a coin heavier than anything the boy has ever held, with a message that a friend of the palace would be glad if the dog's evening fish were seasoned, for six nights, with a little of something that would help it rest. The twist of folded leaf is handed over by his servant at the kitchen gate. At the Sitting Elders' meetings he is the one who made the proposal sound like a courtesy: that the dog be the whole of the Second Asking, since the ancestors had spoken through it once. The elders, who had never been offered a sign with its own hole already cut, agree on the fourth night. At the same meeting he puts forward a second proposal, with the same courtesy, that the dog be set down three paces from Alassane, on the ground that an animal frightened by crowds should not be made to walk far, and no one thinks to examine it. The dog trots twenty-seven paces to Sekou. When the war comes he leaves with his mistress in the closed litter, and in the last of his years, after the dog is killed, he is the voice of a household that no longer had a son, saying that the wardens had murdered their own sign to hide a fraud. The manuscript does not say how he dies. The manuscript gives his office, his hands and his service; no age, face or build. The sheet's age of 62, heavyset 5 ft 7 in (170 cm) soft build, thinning close-cut grey hair, dark brown eyes, smooth round clean-shaven face with a courteous smile, small brass seal and indigo wrapper are its own, as are its panels Courteous Service, Patient Calculation, Quiet Concern and Household Resolve and its scene The Second Asking, captioned Three paces from Alassane. a soft-handed man who had served her thirty years and who understood, better than she did, exactly what she had asked","i":"images/characters/kassa-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Kassa's Servant","u":"characters/kassas-servant.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Hands Tuma the folded leaf at the kitchen gate · unnamed · 28 years old on his sheet","x":"Presses a twist of folded leaf into a kennel-boy's hand while an Observer watches from the kitchen gate. One clause in Chapter Eleven. On the evening the leaf is handed over, Observer 1,451 is standing at the kitchen gate, and sees Kassa's servant handed the boy Tuma a twist of folded leaf. She has an instrument behind her eyes that can read the residue of a plant alkaloid on a human hand at eleven paces, and she reads it. The servant is the third link in a short chain: Awa tells Kassa, Kassa pays the woman who sells sleep by the pinch and puts the coin in the hand of Tuma, and the leaf itself passes through the servant's. The manuscript gives no name, age, words or later appearance, and does not say whether he knows what the leaf is. It is the same evening on which the Observer, going home, buys a small leather sandal from a boy at the market gate. The manuscript gives the gate, the leaf and the handing; the rest is invented. The sheet's age of 28, deep brown skin, close-cropped hair, dark brown eyes, plain closed face with no facial hair, medium build and undyed linen are its own, as is its remark that he is deliberately forgettable. Its panels are Neutral, Watchful, Hesitation and Resolve, and its scenes At the Kitchen Gate and He Hands Tuma the Folded Leaf. Kassa's servant handed the boy Tuma a twist of folded leaf","i":"images/characters/kassas-servant-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Mory","u":"characters/mory.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Captain of Sekou's wardens · the captain the water-skin was offered to · 48 years old on his sheet","x":"Is offered a water-skin by a prince on a hot morning, is missed by a spear by a hand's breadth, and, as the war nears its end, takes his cap off in a kennel yard. A captain of Sekou's wardens, who had been shouting longest on the morning of the ninth of Kerren, when the wardens are quartered in the lower town and have come up to the palace gate with no plan. Bakary, twenty-two, comes down from the upper gallery with a water-skin over his shoulder, because it is a hot dry morning and the wardens have been shouting for an hour, and offers it to him. A boy on the wall throws a spear at the captain. It misses him by a hand, and hits the water-carrier. He appears once more, at the end. When the wardens find Kolo in the yard at midday on the first morning of Averin, Captain Mory, who had been among the first to reach the palace gate on the morning Bakary died, looks at the man and the animal and the yard for as long as it takes to understand what he is seeing, takes his own cap off and goes to fetch the king. The manuscript does not say whether he sits on the captains' council, or what he does in the war between. The manuscript gives his rank, his shouting, his cap and his errand; no age, face or build. The sheet's age of 48, deep brown weathered skin, square face, heavy moustache, broad build, cropped hair under a flat cap, brass whistle on a thong and earth-brown wrapper are its own, as are its panels Command, Suspicion, Grave Duty and Resolve and its scenes Bakary offers the water-skin at the palace gate and Removing His Cap. took his own cap off","i":"images/characters/mory-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"The Spear-Thrower","u":"characters/spear-thrower.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"A boy of the palace guard on the wall · unnamed · 19 years old on his sheet","x":"Throws one spear at a shouting captain, sleepless and two days into being told the ancestors are insulted, and opens the war. The guard on the wall, who were nineteen, and had not slept, and had been told for two days that the ancestors themselves were being insulted, watch Sekou's wardens come with the dry-mouthed fear of young men who have discovered that they are the last thing between a crowd and a door. A boy on the wall threw a spear at the captain. It misses Mory by a hand and hits Bakary, who is offering a water-skin. Nobody had meant it. That was, afterward, the only part of it anyone could agree on, and it helped no one. The manuscript names him nowhere and gives him no words. It says only that he is a boy, and that the guard are nineteen; it does not say what becomes of him. The sheet's age of 19 follows the guard's. Its deep brown skin, dark brown eyes, close-cropped black hair, clean-shaven face, thin build, trembling grip and worn leather are its own; it prints a line, Two days without sleep; two days told the ancestors were being insulted., from the manuscript's description of the guard, and its panels are Exhaustion, Fear, Confusion and Realization. Its scene is He throws at the shouting captain — Bakary is struck instead. A boy on the wall threw a spear at the captain.","i":"images/characters/spear-thrower-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Ghelo Pan-Workers","u":"characters/ghelo-pan-workers.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Two thousand rakers of the Ghelo salt-pans · where nine of every ten measures of the kingdom's salt came from · 16 to 60 on their sheet","x":"Rake the shallow basins by hand, until the pans change hands without a battle. The Ghelo salt-pans lie two weeks east of Kambaso on the rim of the white desert: a shimmering plain of shallow evaporation basins, walled with clay and raked by hand by two thousand pan-workers, from which nine of every ten measures of salt in the kingdom came, and from whose tolls the crown had drawn a third of its revenue for four hundred years. In Deyren of the second spring of the war the Bantaran riders take them. The manuscript says nothing of what happens to the workers: whether they flee, stay, are paid, or work under new masters. Around them the accounts shift: the salt caravans are diverted through Bantaran towns, the sorghum stands unharvested in the flood-plain, and a measure of millet in Kambaso sells for what a measure of gold-dust cost in Tiemoko's day. The manuscript gives them their number and their rakes; it gives no names, ages, faces or later appearance. The sheet's ages of 16 to 60, lean sinewy stooped builds, deep brown salt-crusted skin, cropped or wrapped hair, dark-brown eyes, rake-worn hands, straw hats and clay-grey basins are its own, as is its remark that they work under Bantaran riders after the pans fall. Its panels are Endurance, Exhaustion, Quiet Defiance and Watchful Fear, and its scenes At the Pans, Raking the Basins, Salt-Dried Faces and Under Bantaran Riders. The riders who take the pans have a page of their own. raked by hand by two thousand pan-workers","i":"images/characters/ghelo-pan-workers-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Captains' Council","u":"characters/captains-council.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Council of Sekou's captains · votes over the king's objection · 35 to 55 on their sheet","x":"Votes, over the king's objection, to carry the dog before the army in a litter, and sets a Recounter walking to a grey stone. In the third winter of the war the captains' council votes, over the king's objection, to carry the dog before the army in the spring: a litter, a canopy of indigo, a guard of fifty, so that the Long Debt might lead the host and the enemy might see it. The idea was reached in the east from the other direction, where a party of Awa's riders has already tried once to take the dog. The council counts, in the manuscript's words, only what it could march, which is why Bocar the granary-keeper, who dies on his bench beside an empty bin, is not one of the dead it counts. Fode hears of the vote on the fifth day of Mavren, in the council court, from a scribe, says nothing, goes home and recites the kings backward twice without reaching the bargain. It is the last link in the reasoning that sends him ten days later to the grey stone: the captains mean to carry the dog into the field in a litter of indigo and it will be killed by soldiers or kept. The manuscript does not name or number the captains, say who votes which way, or say whether Mory sits among them; after the dog's death Sekou's captains say that the east did it. The sheet's ages of 35 to 55, deep brown skin, dark brown eyes, cropped hair, hard practical faces, mixed facial hair, sturdy builds, rope-calloused hands and plain wrappers and sword-belts are its own, as are its panels Resolve, Objection, Deliberation and Unity and its scenes The Vote: Carry the Dog Before the Army and The Captains' Vote. It labels the king King Sekou (objection) and draws a dog beside the council table, though the manuscript does not place the dog at the vote. voted to carry the dog before the army in the spring","i":"images/characters/captains-council-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Awa's Riders","u":"characters/awas-riders.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"A party of riders loyal to Awa's household · 22 to 40 on their sheet","x":"Try once, in the autumn, to take the dog from the kennel wall, are driven back at the cost of six wardens' lives, and are met by a dog that wags. Mentioned in a single sentence of Chapter Thirteen-B, as the east's answer to the captains' vote: a party of Awa's riders had already tried once, in the autumn, to take it, and had been driven back from the kennel wall at the cost of six wardens' lives. The dog, it was reported, had wagged at them. Fode counts them in the reasoning that sends him to the grey stone: Awa's riders meant to take the dog, and would try again. The manuscript says nothing of their number, leader or weapons, or whether the party was the east's by order or by zeal. The manuscript gives the party, the autumn and the six wardens. The sheet's ages of 22 to 40, deep brown wind-roughened skin, cropped black hair (some under headcloths), dark brown eyes, hard faces and set jaws, lean riding builds, quirt scars on the forearms, dust-yellow dress and spears and curved swords are its own, as are its panels Watchful, Defiant, Grief and Retreat and its scenes Riders of Awa's Household and Autumn: The Attempt at the Kennel Wall. a party of Awa's riders had already tried once, in the autumn, to take it","i":"images/characters/awas-riders-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bocar","u":"characters/bocar.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Keeper of the lower granary in Kambaso · keeps his accounts in knotted cord · 52 years old","x":"Gives out the last of the millet level, with the flat of his hand, to eleven hundred people who do not push, and sits down beside the empty bin. A heavy, methodical man of fifty-two, the keeper of the lower granary in Kambaso, who has measured out other people's millet for thirty years without once being accused of an undercount. The granary-keepers keep their accounts in knotted cord, one cord to a household, one knot to a measure, and he goes on knotting through the winter of the war long after there is anything in the bin to measure. By the first of Lorren the allowance is a bowl to a household, and by the twentieth a bowl to a street; he gives it out from the same wooden measure, level, with the flat of his hand, to eleven hundred people who stand in a line that bends twice around the granary wall and who do not, he notices, push. He gives out the last of the millet on the third of Mavren. That night he ties the last knot on the cord for his own household, when his daughter, who is nine, stops asking whether the line is moving. On the fourth he sits down on the bench beside the empty bin with the full set of cords across his knees, as though to check a sum, and does not get up. He was not one of the dead the Recounters' verse counted, since he had not died in a field, and he was not one the captains' council counted, since it counted only what it could march. He was one of the thirty thousand. The Recounters' verse gives eleven thousand dead in the field; the granary-keepers' cords, which nobody ever gathered, would have given thirty thousand more by that winter, most of whom died at home. The manuscript gives his age, build in one word, trade, measure and cords. The sheet's broad build, deep brown skin, cropped grey hair, short grey beard, tired dark-brown eyes and undyed cotton dusted with millet are its own, as are its belt cords, one knot per measure, and its worn wooden measure. Its panels are Patience, Weariness, Resolve and Last Ration, and its scene He dies on the bench by the empty bin; the manuscript says he sits and does not get up. He was one of the thirty thousand.","i":"images/characters/bocar-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Bocar's Daughter","u":"characters/bocars-daughter.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Daughter of the lower granary's keeper · unnamed · 9 years old","x":"Stops asking whether the line is moving, and for forty-one years adds a knot to her father's cords every winter for reasons she cannot give either. Nine years old on the night her father, Bocar, ties the last knot on the cord for his own household: the night his daughter stopped asking whether the line was moving. Her father sits down on the bench beside the empty bin the next day and does not get up. She keeps his cords. His daughter would keep the cords for forty-one years, and would be found, when she died, to have added a knot to each of them every winter, for reasons nobody who knew her could have given, and which she could not have given either. The manuscript does not name her, say who raised her, or give her age at death, and does not mention her again. The manuscript gives her age, her question and her forty-one years. The sheet's small thin build of about 4 ft 3 in (130 cm), deep brown skin, solemn round face, large dark-brown eyes, two short plaits tied with twine, missing front tooth and faded earth-brown and ochre wrapper are its own, as are its panels Patience (Waiting), Grief (Silent), Listening (No Questions) and Resolve (Each Winter). Its captions, The Cords, Kept for 41 years and She stops asking if the line is moving; each winter, she adds a knot to her father's cords., follow the manuscript. added a knot to each of them every winter","i":"images/characters/bocars-daughter-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Hawa's Delivery Boy","u":"characters/hawas-delivery-boy.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Carries Hawa's Uncounted Day keg to the palace kennel · unnamed · 14 years old on his sheet","x":"Is met at the back door of the Weighmaster's Table by a poisoner, and is not hurt. One clause in Chapter Fourteen. Hawa has sent a keg of her darkest brew up to the kennel yard on every Uncounted Day since the year the brew was named, and the keg has to be carried; on the twenty-eighth of Mavren Sira had made the delivery boy's acquaintance at the back door of the Weighmaster's Table, where the keg stood waiting. She did not hurt him. She had no need. She doses the keg instead, with a compound she uses on guards, so as not to have to deal with witnesses, in an amount calculated for the lightest man likely to drink from it, who is a lean man of sixty-one. The manuscript does not say whether he ever learns what he carried, and gives him no age, name, face or later appearance. The manuscript gives the boy, the keg and the meeting; everything visible is invented, as the sheet itself notes at its foot. Its age of 14, thin wiry build of about 5 ft 1 in (155 cm), narrow alert face, close-shaven hair, dark brown eyes, deep brown skin, short undyed wrapper, woven head-pad and rope sling are its own, as are its panels Watchful, Unsure, Steady Burden and Quiet Relief. Its scene, At the Kennel's Back Door, sets him at the kennel; the manuscript's one meeting for him is at the back door of the Weighmaster's Table. She did not hurt him.","i":"images/characters/hawas-delivery-boy-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Sira","u":"characters/sira.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Poisoner and killer for hire · the woman the Chief Recounter pays four hundred weights · 46 or so","x":"Kills the dog with a blade, once, because a thing that has survived fifty years of kitchen scraps has earned a fast method, and stands for forty breaths looking at nothing. Middle height, forty-six or so, with the unremarkable, slightly tired face of a person who has spent a career being overlooked and has come to depend on it. She has killed sixty-one people in twenty-two years, fifty-eight of them by poison, in a trade with no guild but exacting manners, and has never once been asked to do it for a reason she could respect. The Chief Recounter Fode finds her, after nine days, through the woman who sells sleep by the pinch, and meets her on the flat grey stone at the fourth hour of the night. She listens. The price of a dog, she says, is a fish; when he says it is not a dog, she says it is four hundred weights, the cost of a small king. She adds a condition that surprises him: no poison. A thing that has survived fifty years of kitchen scraps and river water and a queen's sedative has earned the courtesy of a fast method, and she will use a blade, once, and it will not know. It is the closest she has come in twenty-two years to a scruple, and she dislikes it exactly as much as a professional dislikes a habit she cannot bill for. On the twenty-eighth of Mavren she makes the acquaintance of Hawa's delivery boy at the back door of the Weighmaster's Table and does not hurt him. She doses the Uncounted Day keg with a compound she uses on guards, so as not to have to deal with witnesses, in an amount calculated for the lightest man likely to drink from it, a lean man of sixty-one. It is a considerate dose, and she notes with mild irritation that considerateness has crept into her work. She comes over the back wall a little past the second hour after midnight, waits in the shadow of the lean-to until the wardens' breathing has settled into the rhythm she dosed it for, and crosses the yard. The dog is asleep on its back with one paw chasing a rabbit that had died of old age before she was born. It wakes, as it always does at a step, and beats its tail twice against the mat, and comes to lick her hand. She would say, if anyone had ever asked her, that it was the easiest commission of her career and the only one she had not been able to enjoy, and that the two facts were related. She stands in the yard for some forty breaths afterward, with the blade cleaned and put away, looking at nothing, and goes back over the wall. Observer 1,451, arriving eleven minutes late, sees a figure go over the wall ahead of her. The Archival Log calls her a paid agent whose identity was never established. The manuscript does not say what becomes of her. The manuscript gives her age, her height, her face, her price and her habits; the sheet's medium-brown skin, dark brown eyes, dark plaits beneath a dark wrap, pouches of compounds and charcoal-and-earth dress are its own. Its title strip, Poisoner and killer for hire, and its printed line, A trade with no guild., follow the manuscript. Its panels are Measured Calm, Quiet Suspicion, After the Dose and Empty Stare, and its scenes At the grey stone, she takes the Chief Recounter's commission. and She doses the Uncounted Day keg, crosses the wall, and stands forty breaths looking at nothing. She had killed sixty-one people in twenty-two years","i":"images/characters/sira-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Awa's Nephew","u":"characters/awas-nephew.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Nephew of Queen Awa · put in the eastern seat after the war · unnamed · 33 years old on his sheet","x":"Is put into the eastern seat with the dead man's name struck out, and does not appear again. One clause in Chapter Fifteen. The peace that exhausts the war is not a settlement so much as an exhaustion, and its terms are the terms of the sandbar, more or less, with the dead man's name struck out and a nephew of Awa's put in the eastern seat; the trial is dropped, and Awa is permitted to live out her days in the house she has been given. The seat is the one the sandbar had meant for Alassane, as the wording reads. The manuscript gives the nephew no name, age or words, and does not say whether he fought. The manuscript gives the relationship and the seat. The sheet's age of 33 is its own, and a note on it says Appearance details are invented; no physical description survives in the source. Its stocky 5 ft 9 in (175 cm) build, round mild face with a slightly anxious brow, close-cropped black hair, guarded dark-brown eyes, clean-shaven jaw, toll-key on a cord, brass seal ring on the right hand and indigo dress with a narrow border are its own, as are its panels Guarded Caution, Subdued Grief, Reluctant Resolve and Quiet Uncertainty and its scene Installed in the Eastern Seat. a nephew of Awa's put in the eastern seat","i":"images/characters/awas-nephew-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"s","t":"Hawa's Granddaughter","u":"characters/hawas-granddaughter.html","m":"Case 0113 · The Dog Is Not in It","d":"Brewer, owner of the dark ale's brewery · unnamed · 35 years old on her sheet","x":"Her brewery sells a dark ale for two hundred years under a name that has come to mean only the strong one. One sentence in the Epilogue, in the paragraph on what the war leaves behind as furniture. Hawa's granddaughter's brewery sold a dark ale for two hundred years under a name that had come to mean only the strong one. Hawa is known for her dark brew, Grey-Never, but the manuscript does not say that this is the same ale, does not give its later name, and does not say how the brewery came to her or when. It is one of the small survivals of the dog: in the same paragraph, to pay the long debt comes to mean buying a round for someone one has wronged, and nobody remembers why. The manuscript gives her a grandmother, a brewery and two hundred years; no name, age, face or build. The sheet's age of 35, deep brown skin, dark brown eyes, short plaits beneath an indigo wrap, round face, strong brewing arms and hammered-copper kettle are its own, as are its panels Shrewdness, Labor, Resolve and Stewardship and its scene The Strong One. Hawa's granddaughter's brewery sold a dark ale for two hundred years","i":"images/characters/hawas-granddaughter-thumb.jpg"},{"k":"a","t":"Cultivator","u":"lore/cultivator.html","m":"Archive","d":"The observers who deliver Catalysts to chosen subjects across worlds.","x":"A loose, still-forming collective of offices and field agents — simply “observers” in the earliest records — who select subjects across many worlds and deliver a Catalyst directly into their hands, then spend the rest of that subject's life quietly filing reports on what the world does with it. The name “Cultivator” wasn't settled on until long after the practice began. Their field agents are called Envoys, or Observers. Nearly every case has its own, and — with a single exception on file, an Envoy posted to two cases in succession — no two are the same person: each works through a numbered “deployment archetype” and a disguise suited to the world they enter — a woman on a hilltop, a traveling confectioner, an ordinary old man at the bottom of a hole, a wandering hermit, an ascetic kneeling at a drainage ditch, a court astronomer-priest who keeps a kingdom's calendar from a tower, a shipwreck survivor who has kept the same flooded ruin for a hundred and forty years. Every Envoy on file is listed below. Not every case resolves within a single lifetime: in at least one instance on file, a Catalyst sat untouched in one family's keeping for four centuries before its case ever closed, and the Envoy assigned to it counts eleven centuries of comparable postings behind him. Redundancy is standard practice on others: one Envoy's own field notes cite several thousand comparable seedings, on the reasoning that a single object left in open ground is recovered by its intended finder only slightly more often than it's carried off by a flood, a jackdaw, or a passing child who simply wants it for its color. One Envoy on file sells her entire remaining stock to a single buyer in an ordinary afternoon of trade and is three postings distant before anyone drinks what she sold them. Another looks like a child of twelve, has not aged in four generations, and is forbidden by protocol to do anything about what she logs. A third is recalled from a tower parapet on the one evening he exceeds his instructions, and leaves a lamp burning behind him. Another, a mute stonecutter, dresses both ends of a matched pair of stones, spends thirty-one years as a quarry-hand beside the one that receives, and is asked, in notches drawn in the dust, the one question he may not answer. Another, a rat-catcher with a bait-spoon, breaks open a single clay pot in the broom-scrub of a mountain pass, files three notes, sells nothing, and passes that stonecutter on a quarry road with an exact courtesy. Another, a shrine-keeper whose watch the valley below cannot date, sits beside a spring that copies one man's whole life into the next to drink, and says the same two words to the master who comes to die at it and to the disciple who comes looking for what he left. Another, an Auditor who speaks to no one in the case, watches a marker for more than a decade, keeps finding reasons not to file the one variable that will not fit any category, and closes the terrarium with the variable open. And one, a clockmaker in two successive postings, goes on watching his second subject from a rented room two valleys away for eighteen years after the posting has formally ended. One case has none posted in person at all: a network of probes does the delivering, and an analytical office, not a field agent, files the closing log."},{"k":"a","t":"Catalyst","u":"lore/catalyst.html","m":"Archive","d":"The single object at the center of every case.","x":"The one object a Cultivator puts into a society to see what the society does with it. A Catalyst is rarely dangerous in itself — a jar of candy, a pair of shoes, a sword no one else can lift, a hole in a hillside, a flask that never lets its contents go cold, a traffic cone, an eight-foot electric eel — and it arrives with no explanation and no instructions. What matters is everything that happens after: the miracle someone declares, the heresy someone else does, the pride that takes offense, and the office that quietly writes it all down. Each Catalyst belongs to one case, is placed by one Envoy, and is filed under a number and a class of its own. Some are handed to one person, some are slipped into a court, and one is a hundred-level structure left in a hillside for someone to find, and one is a living animal, released into a flooded ruin and left to be found. Redundancy is standard on some cases: one Catalyst was seeded three times over in the same ditch, so that a single unit lost to a flood or an incurious passerby wouldn't end the case before it began. One Catalyst on file grants nothing but another creature's own perception, on loan for a quarter of an hour at a time, and is never once used by the same hand twice. Another is a buried chamber that rebuilds whatever enters it, exact in everything that can be measured. Another, left beneath a threshing-floor, cuts a circle of clear noon with a ruler's edge out of a sky of ash and holds it open for four hundred and twenty-one days. Another is a pair of stones, one in a shrine and one on a village green, that carries whoever lays a bare palm on the first to the second, and no one ever back. Another is a banded fungus, broken open from one sealed clay pot ahead of a migrating swarm, that turns every animal it takes toward the sun and leaves four hundred feet of cliff hung with the dead. Another is a mountain spring, fed by nothing and drained by nothing, that copies the whole memory and skill of the last person to touch it into the next person who drinks, and overwrites itself each time. Another is a prosthetic left hand with eleven fingers, grafted onto a prince over three nights, that a kingdom spends eighteen years deciding is not a hand at all. Another is not an object but a network: a field of dormant probes in the high air over a kingdom that, on a schedule fixed before they were seeded, lets one village at a time sleep its way up to see the shape of its own world, and gives nothing else. Another, likewise not an object, is a dormant condition in one family's blood that, in the instant of great fright, strikes through every branch that ends in its carrier's death and leaves her the one number remaining, and that no one, the carrier included, can summon by wanting. Every Catalyst on file is listed below."},{"k":"a","t":"The Faith of Ardwen","u":"lore/faith-of-ardwen.html","m":"Archive","d":"A monotheism built on waiting, honest prayer, and a sword no one else can lift.","x":"A monotheism built around a single historical miracle: an honest prayer, answered from the sky. Ardwen — the Unhastening, the Sky-Answered — is worshipped as the one eternal goddess, without rival, consort, or divine family, and asks less of her followers than most faiths do: wait, ask honestly, keep your promises, and act responsibly without pretending to know a god's mind for her. Her clergy are careful to distinguish what is known from what is merely mysterious, and the faith's own maxim is built to resist any shortcut: Ardwen answers the honest, but never on command. Her institutional church, the Vigil of Ardwen, is led by a First Keeper and organized into regional Houses of Vigil, with confession, baptism, marriage, funerals, and pilgrimage among its sacred rites. The faith reveres its founding miracle — and the sword that came with it — as a sacred sign of that day, never as a rival object of worship. Whatever else it is, Valeria is not a god. The founding faith of The Sword of Valeria, where the goddess herself and the relic she left behind both have entries of their own: Ardwen and Valeria. Generations later, in The Stolen Prince War, the Vigil's doctrine of unearned, uncommandable grace is tested to its limit by a dungeon that answers the same chest the same way every single time — a contradiction its own Keeper, Yudith, spends eleven years trying to preach around. In The Purging of Charsianon, a Byzantine-styled river valley keeps the Vigil in a plain House of stone and candles, and a Keeper of the Dorylaion Vigil turns a name for a strange calm — the Doctrine of the Stolen Vessel — into an army. In The Second Hand, a hidden mountain fold called Otsane keeps the Vigil in a plain house with no cathedral, where its Keeper, Amets, gives an exile a new name and an open door, and the cathedral at Ardenwake turns a distant rumor into a question about the Anchorite, the founding wielder and a sword no one else can lift. And in Independent Observation, a single unaffiliated anchorite of the Vigil, the Anchorite of Ardwen, walks from burning to burning through a Threefold kingdom that files her faith among the empty crowns, asks the silence beyond the heavens to let her come, and takes the silence that meets her as an answer, which is the only kind Ardwen gives: never on command. And in Fossa, the Keeper Alazaïs of the House of Vigil at Sant-Sever, asked which of the other two altars the realm owes its deliverance, says only “I do not know,” sends six hundred hands to dig, and feeds the families of both sides when the war comes. And in The Uncounted Variable, the bells of a Vahlreth House of Vigil are still ringing the pattern for a king's death three months after he was buried, a Keeper, Emmerich, finds one hermit across four generations of rolls and registries without the framework to categorise him, the Keeper of Hartenmark's own House answers a council that a girl's survival is “a sign worth remembering rather than a fact worth building policy on”, and the chronicles, in time, settle on a tidier miracle."},{"k":"a","t":"The Threefold Crown","u":"lore/threefold-crown.html","m":"Archive","d":"Three gods, or one god wearing three faces — the Crown has never resolved which.","x":"An old pagan faith built around three supreme gods who may, or may not, be one god wearing three faces — a contradiction its own believers call the Threefold Mystery and have never been in any hurry to resolve. Auron the Father governs heaven, law, kingship, judgment, and oaths; Elara the Mother governs earth, birth, fertility, harvest, and hearth; Solan the Son governs the sun, fire, youth, passion, and sacrifice. A believer may favor one god over the other two without ever quite denying that all three are, somehow, the same throne. The formula recited at its temples leaves the contradiction standing on purpose: Father above. Mother beneath. Son beside us. Three crowns. One Heaven. Ritual-heavy and elaborately hierarchical, with wine treated as sacred and pork freely eaten, the Threefold Crown holds that its own throne is the one true one — a claim that makes its temple politics every bit as susceptible to ambition as any earthly court, and its priesthood just as capable of turning a private grief, or a private embezzlement, into doctrine. Vantashen's state religion in The Stolen Prince War, where Hierarch Doreth turns a private grief into a war he tells himself is a monument, and the guardian at Level 40 is known to delvers as the Trial of Solan. In The Iron Stiletto War, the Order of the Pale Cloth's High Cleric Ambrose declares a pair of shoes heretical on the Crown's own authority. In The Third Grain, a legal claim over a county mill is argued and won entirely inside the Crown's own law, under a courtroom ceiling painted with a judge who has spent thirty years complaining that Auron's three scales were never painted quite level. And in The Stone of the Clean Hand, a hearth-priest's honest report of a stranger's bow to Solan's Fire and refusal of the feast-pig reaches Hierarch Odilo at Castelmaur, who lays the temple's interdict on a whole lane until its idol is cast down. And in The Second Hand, the kingdom of Sarkveti keeps the Crown as its faith the way it keeps its terraces, and Hierarch Demna of Auron's temple in Tavgora rules, on the Doctrine of the Unbroken Hand, that a prince's grafted hand is not his own. And in Independent Observation, the Crown is Aldemark's actual government, its Triarchs Ansovald, Adaltrude and Sigward holding the seats of Auron, Elara and Solan, until a decade of dreams of a round world, and an eleventh selection that falls on their own capital, leave them three answers to one fact and the Crown a faith rather than a government. And in Fossa, the kingdom of Auvarre keeps the Threefold Crown at its temple of Auron in Castelmaur, where Hierarch Odilo weeps sincerely at every response, declares the Accord of Montclar void on the fifth of Iverin, and is believed by eleven of the realm's seventeen barons. And in The Sable Skin, the kingdom of Austrenne keeps the Crown as its law of oaths: the Hierarch Rothari, who has presided over the Ordeal of the Third Stroke on the black oak Altar of Oaths eleven times, finds in the Eleventh Clause of the Third Book that if both blades are sound the cause is not for the altar, and after the Law of the Oath-Blade is repealed declares void the verdict of forsworn that the Landing's Priest gave against the stranger of Case 2231 with the words “He was merely early.” The kingdom's map is filed as the Gazetteer of the Sable Skin. And in The Uncounted Variable, the usurping duke Otwin's house has kept the Crown through four generations of nominal Vigil piety the way other houses keep an unmarried cousin, and on a sleepless night he kneels, out of practice, not to Auron but to the unhastening goddess of the kingdom he took by force."},{"k":"a","t":"The Decree of Luminescence","u":"lore/decree-of-luminescence.html","m":"Archive","d":"A theology of measured light: what is favored is lit, and what is lit has been favored.","x":"The youngest of the faiths on file, promulgated after a kingdom-threatening catastrophe: years of ash-blotted sky broken, at the exact point of famine, by a single standing column of true daylight over the capital. Sol-Invictus, the Unconquered Sun, is worshipped as the one eternal, lending light, and its doctrine is unusually literal for a religion: light is not read as a sign of favor, it is favor, measured out to every soul at birth as a store of borrowed fire and returned, spent or unspent, at death. It is a creditor's theology at bottom, and its own clergy don't pretend otherwise. The doctrine's core article states the idea plainly: Whatever was favored was lit, and whatever was lit had been favored. Its church, the Last Order, is led by a High Pontiff and keeps its own doctrine correctable only by further entry, never by erasure. A claimant to real favor stands before a lens that concentrates true daylight onto them, to see whether they visibly glow; a crown is carried through open flame, to see whether the gold endures. What three centuries of both tests have never quite worked out is how to tell light that's generated from light that's merely, cleverly, reflected. Its founding is the subject of The Decree of Luminescence. Three centuries on, in The Vessel of Unmediated Grace, where King Aethelgard's death without an heir turns the succession into a lit competition, High Pontiff Sarel presides over the Grand Basilica's Great Judgment, and a peddler named Fenn makes an honest living selling pilgrims small shards of warmed river quartz as splinters of the crown. The Anchorite of the Drowned Road sect keeps its own small, tolerated counter-doctrine on the Sun's behalf, practicing His absence rather than His favor. The kingdom's map, drawn under both its names, is filed as the Gazetteer of Illumaria. In Fossa, the Decree's Last Order sits in the realm of Auvarre as the second altar of the Accord of Montclar: its Provost-Examiner Ottavio Brandi turns a ground lens on a cliff of dead grasshoppers, writes “Lit” in the Book, and spends the rest of his life putting the date, the weather and the name of the town at the head of every document that leaves his hand; the charter that calls it to the table is the Accord of Montclar."},{"k":"a","t":"Gazetteer of Illumaria","u":"lore/illumaria.html","m":"Gazetteer","d":"One kingdom under two names: the map of Vallombra at its founding, and of Illumaria long after.","x":"A map of a single kingdom at two moments. Case 2365 knows it as Vallombra, in the years of ash when its kings and its Reckoner found a religion; Case 4555 knows it as Illumaria, long after, when the religion has a Basilica, a High Pontiff and a succession to settle. The map prints both names together, as Vallombra / Illumaria, and sets the March of Verrenhal beside it to the north-west. It is filed here as a gazetteer, not as a subject or a record. The places below are the ones printed on the map, from north to south; each note is drawn from the case files, and a place that no case file yet describes is said to be unfiled rather than guessed at. Vallombra, at the founding of its religion, is the kingdom of The Decree of Luminescence (Case 2365). The same kingdom, long after and under the name Illumaria, is the kingdom of The Vessel of Unmediated Grace (Case 4555). Their shared faith is the Decree of Luminescence. Unknown Lands. Printed along the map's northern, western and eastern margins. No case file describes them. March of Verrenhal. The northern realm of King Ragnvald and his sister Sigrid in Case 2365, who rules it as regent for eleven years after him. Its host comes down a mountain pass into Vallombra in the ash. The map files it as a March. Saltway Gate. The northern gate on the Saltway, where Rufio's wardens turn back the people whose names cannot be found, where Arne's four thousand are met at the low hour, and where Sigrid finds a row of a hundred and forty-one dead with their feet toward the gold. The parley is held here. Saltway Quarter. A parish of the Roll of the Lit. It is the column of three hundred and eleven names that the copyist Lino skips when his wick gutters, and the quarter to which Livia sends forty wagons of grain, unlabelled, on the anniversary of the First Furrow for fifty-one years. Ashduct. The hills in the east. When they breathe and the sun withdraws, Severin extends the year's grace in Case 2365 from three days to a season. Vallombra / Illumaria. The kingdom itself. In Case 2365 it is Vallombra, the realm of King Faustin and then his daughter Livia, Princess of Vallombra and later Queen of Illumaria. In Case 4555 it is Illumaria, the High Realm of King Aethelgard and, in time, of High Sovereign Blakk. Torrecalda. The walled city on the river, drawn with a keep. In Case 2365 it is where the Granary's physician tells Faustin that no room is large enough to keep the sick from the well, and the city whose tanning trade the Tanner's Widow belongs to. Lower Granaries. The grain-houses below Torrecalda. Nina, Piero's sister, is the daughter of the Lower Granary clerk and dies in the granary house in the first winter of the ash. Threshing Round. The Round, where the First Furrow is turned. The Reckoner's tower stands beside it; Livia is crowned there at nine; and it is the ground on which the sun stands at the sixth hour when Severin states his one false figure. Grand Basilica of Sol-Invictus. The seat of the religion the first case founds. In Case 2365 a lens the diameter of a cartwheel is ground to catch the single shaft of sun through its oculus; in Case 4555 the Great Judgment is held here under High Pontiff Sarel, and Observer 902 watches from an alcove in its black stone. Tanners' Row. Marked on the map; no case file describes it yet. Wool Lane. A column on the Roll of the Lit: the next one Lino copies after the Saltway Quarter. Glassmakers' Row. The street of Anselmo, master of the guild that grinds the Sol-Focus Arc by hand for eleven years. Furrow reserve. Marked on the map; no case file describes it yet. Round-bed. Marked on the map; no case file describes it yet. Mud-Reach. Blakk's duchy in Case 4555: nine hundred square miles of drained marsh, standing timber and tenant debt. Illmere Ford. The Standing at Illmere Ford, the engagement that follows Blakk's unread letters of surrender. Astraea's cavalry meets Ignis's raiding column here, and Cassian arrives on the fifth day to find no one left worth conquering. Sarn's Crossing. Where Astraea throws herself between Blakk and Canon Voss's blade, taking a wound that troubles her for the rest of her life. Weirs. The harbour at the river's mouth. Lord Fulvio of the Weirs, one of the sixteen lords of Vallombra, offers his grain-ships in Case 2365 as the Order's sole licensed factor for whatever is built on the strength of the noon."},{"k":"a","t":"Gazetteer of the Iron Stiletto War","u":"lore/iron-stiletto-war.html","m":"Gazetteer","d":"The map of Case 4417: Solis, Ironhold, and the country between them.","x":"The country of Case 4417, drawn from the sea inland: Solis on the coast, Ironhold in the mountains to the north-east, and the forest line and the river between them. It is filed here as a gazetteer, not as a subject or a record. The places below are the ones printed on the map; each note is drawn from the case file, and a place the file never mentions is said to be unfiled rather than guessed at. The country of The Iron Stiletto War (Case 4417). Unknown Lands. Printed along the map's northern and southern margins. No case file describes them. House Vell. Shown as a keep north of Solis. House Vell commits to Solis in secret, and its Lord is the brother of Isolde of Ironhold, a connection that matters more to the war than she first realizes. Ironhold. The mountain country of Duke Aldous and Duchess Beatrice, whose steward Osric has kept its accounts for three dukes and whose Lord of the Western Passes is Fenric. Beatrice's humiliation at the Great Concord is the war's original grievance. Solis. The coastal kingdom of Queen Aurelia, where Vane arrives disguised as a wandering hermit and leaves a pair of chrome heels, and where Chancellor Wren has served three monarchs. The siege reaches its walls at the war's end. Great Hall. Shown beneath Solis's castle. Duke Aldous goes into the war's final confrontation here unarmed and offering terms, and is killed in it. Greywater. The river that runs west to the sea below Solis's castle. No case file names it. The Greywater crossing. Marked with a cross on the river's course. No case file describes it. Stonewic Hills. The hills in the south. No case file describes them. The eastern marches. The country of Baron Corvin, Baron of the Eastern Marches, patient and outwardly neutral while he works every side of the war he helps prolong."},{"k":"a","t":"Gazetteer of the Vessel of the Betrayed Host","u":"lore/vessel-of-the-betrayed-host.html","m":"Gazetteer","d":"The map of Case 4420: Mount Skahr and House Skarth in the far north, the Broken Glacier, and the marches that run south to Sumne.","x":"The country of Case 4420, drawn from Mount Skahr in the far north southward: House Skarth beneath the mountain, the Broken Glacier and the ice line, and the marches that run on to Sumne and the lowlands. It is filed here as a gazetteer, not as a subject or a record. The places below are the ones printed on the map; each note is drawn from the case file, and a place the file never mentions is said to be unfiled rather than guessed at. The country of The Vessel of the Betrayed Host (Case 4420). Mount Skahr. The mountain at the head of the map. House Skarth's legend says its steel flask came down from the dragon who keeps the mountain. Observer 814 came down onto its summit in a black, wingless vessel some four centuries before the war and was folded by the clans into that keeper, the Dragon-Speaker. Yvaine climbs it to ask him for a blessing. House Skarth. Shown as a castle west of the mountain gorge. The house of Baron Kaelen and his twin Yvaine, which has carried one steel flask for four centuries without asking where it came from. Kettish. Drawn as a small keep at the eastern edge of the mountains. Marked on the map; no case file describes it yet. House Varga. Drawn as a keep in the western tundra. Marked on the map; no case file describes it yet. House Tolvin. Drawn as a keep beside House Varga. Marked on the map; no case file describes it yet. Broken Glacier. The ice east of the mountain. Duke Maros's hunt reaches it in the northern winter, and by the fourth week it has cost him every toe on both feet. See the record The Broken Glacier. Cindale. Drawn as a ring of small marks at the centre of the map. A frightened border holdfast that Yvaine spares on her march south. Thornfield. Drawn as a walled town in the south-east. Marked on the map; no case file describes it yet. Sumne. Printed in large letters across the southern country, with a castle on a hill below. The case file says only that Soren, after delivering the recovered flask to Duke Vane, leaves Sumne within the month. The tundra. Printed across the north-west. Duke Maros drags a royal hunt two hundred miles into the northern tundra to answer a stag's head, and Baron Kaelen joins it on guest-right alone. The ice line. Printed across the glacier country in the east. The northern clans mark their dead along it with black-fletched arrows. The northern marches. The country of Duke Maros, Duke of the Northern March and Lord of Karhold Keep. Yvaine ends the war in the hall of his captured keep, where she finds Vane. The map does not mark the keep by name. The western march. Marked on the map; no case file describes it yet. The southern marches. The country of Duke Vane, Duke of the Southern March and Lord of Vane Keep, who wounds Maros with a summer-killed stag's head and then sells him his rescue. The map does not mark the keep by name. The lowlands. Marked on the map; no case file describes it yet."},{"k":"a","t":"Gazetteer of the Sable Skin","u":"lore/sable-skin.html","m":"Gazetteer","d":"The map of Case 4181: Marnholt and its three towns on the river Aure, the long bridge, and, in the corner, the three places Higaide carries with him from Case 2231.","x":"The country of Case 4181, drawn from the mountains and the glacier at its head southward: Marnholt on its cliff, the High Town, the Middle Town and the Low Market below it, the long bridge across the Aure, and the kingdom's name, Austrenne, lettered across the foot. In the upper left the map also draws three places that belong to Case 2231, Castelmaur, burning Vilarnau and Lasserra, because the protagonist of this case, Tomobe no Higaide, carries them with him; the manuscript puts them some six weeks' walk to the south and west of Marnholt. It is filed here as a gazetteer, not as a subject or a record. The places below are the ones the map labels, from north to south; each note is drawn from the case files, and a place that no case file yet describes is said to be unfiled rather than guessed at. Three things the map draws without a label are left out of the list: a long arched bridge with a gate-tower, a forge with a plume of smoke, and a hamlet with salt-pans on the coast. The manuscript has a bridge of nine arches with a Roman gate-tower at its northern head, a Low Forge that puts its smoke straight up, and a Salt Landing of thirty huts and a hundred salt-pans at the river-mouth, but the map does not name them, and this page does not say they are the same. The mountains and the glacier at the head of the map are not in the manuscript at all. One label also differs from the manuscript: the map prints Marnholt on its own at the cliff top and the High Town, Middle Town and Low Market as separate names, while the manuscript makes Marnholt the whole city and the three its parts. The notes below follow the manuscript. The country of The Sable Skin (Case 4181). Its three westernmost places, Castelmaur, Vilarnau and Lasserra, belong to The Stone of the Clean Hand (Case 2231), whose survivor is the protagonist of this one. Castelmaur. Drawn as a castle in the upper left. In Case 2231 the seat of the barony of the same name, in the realm of Auvarre; in this case it is named once, as the barony in which Higaide's village stood. See Case 2231. Vilarnau. Drawn as a burning village. The moot-green where, nineteen years before the opening, Higaide came out of the Stone and was given a bowl by a miller's widow; he lived there three months before a bailiff, a struck child, three men dead in a mill loft and a fire. On the first morning of Averin he finds the Stone gone, a clean rectangular hollow and a little grey dust blowing off it. Case 2231 and Case 0202 both return to the village. Lasserra. Drawn among woods, with a church. The hamlet called the High Ground that the survivors of Vilarnau built in the old chestnut forest on the ridge, where Higaide spends the nineteen years that follow as a charcoal-burner, a well-digger, a mender of ploughs, a teacher of boys with sticks and a very bad poet. In the third week of Brayen he leaves it, going east; his friend Peire receives his goodbye. Marnholt. Drawn apart, as a cliff-top town in the upper right. In the manuscript Marnholt is the city of Austrenne, on the north bank of a bend of the river Aure, with a stone bridge of nine arches and a Roman gate-tower at its northern head; its three parts are the High Town, the Middle Town and the Low Market, and the Hammer-Oath Guild, the King and the Hierarch all sit within it. High Town. Drawn as a walled hill with a tower and a church. The chalk bluff at the head of the city: the King's hall, the cathedral of the Threefold Crown with its three apses and the black oak Altar of Oaths, and the Guildhall of the Hammer-Oath, later the Hall of the Order of the Hundred and Forty-Four, which has no door. Middle Town. The quarter of the Guild's masters and journeymen, below the bluff. By Kerren eleven of its forges have bought oxen, and Sigebert Marl keeps rooms behind its tanneries. Cooper's Row. Drawn as a street in the Middle Town. One of the two streets at whose corner the Three Sheaves stands; a drover from the north sees the foreigner there once at dusk, standing beside a cart as though waiting for it to speak, and names him the Moon-Man. Tanners' Lane. Drawn as a street in the Middle Town. The other street at the corner of the Three Sheaves. Marked on the map; no case file describes it further. Three Sheaves. Drawn with a wheat-sheaf sign. The tavern at the corner of Tanners' Lane and the Cooper's Row, under a sign of three wheat-ears bound with red cord and kept by the widow Waldrada, who waters the beer one part to five and is held to be the most honest woman in Marnholt. It is where the black salt acquires its names and where a king is said to have died of arithmetic. Low Market. The muddy flat beyond the old wall, where the Guild's rent-collector comes at Terce on the ninth of the month. The Low Forge stands at its far edge against the old wall, and the market stays peaceful for ten months of war because both armies buy their hinges and cabbages there. The map draws a forge with smoke at its left; it does not label it. Austrenne. Printed in spaced letters across the foot of the map. The kingdom: a country of rye-fields, steep vineyards and beech forest along the middle reaches of the Aure, ruled by King Theudebald the Careful and then by Queen Adalgund, and divided into the duchy of Tarnwald in the north, the county of Eschwin on the south bank and the city of Marnholt. The map marks none of the three provinces by name."}];
